        Software: Adversaries may gather information about the victim's host software that can be used during targeting. Information about installed software may include a variety of details such as types and versions on specific hosts, as well as the presence of additional components that might be indicative of added defensive protections (ex: antivirus, SIEMs, etc.).  Adversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) (ex: listening ports, server banners, user agent strings) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Adversaries may also compromise sites then include malicious content designed to collect host information from visitors.(Citation: ATT ScanBox) Information about the installed software may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: job postings, network maps, assessment reports, resumes, or purchase invoices). Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593) or [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Develop Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587) or [Obtain Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588)), and/or for initial access (ex: [Supply Chain Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195) or [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133)).
          Use Nmap with version detection (-sV) to identify software versions running on open ports
          Deploy web-based fingerprinting tools like Wappalyzer to identify web technologies and frameworks
          Analyze HTTP headers and server banners using curl or specialized tools
          Search job boards (LinkedIn, Indeed) for technology stack information in job postings
          Monitor SSL/TLS certificates for software version information using tools like sslscan
          Analyze DNS records and WHOIS data for server software information
          Use Shodan or Censys to gather information about exposed services and software versions
          Create phishing emails targeting IT staff requesting software inventory information
          Monitor public code repositories for technology stack information
          Analyze security headers and error messages for software version leaks
          Extract metadata from downloaded files and documents to identify software versions
          Analyze mobile apps from app stores to identify backend technologies and frameworks
          Use cloud-specific tools (AWS Config, Azure Resource Graph) to enumerate cloud services
          Monitor software update servers and CDNs for version information
          Analyze public vulnerability reports and security advisories mentioning the organization
          Scan for specific software signatures using specialized tools (e.g., WPScan for WordPress)
          Monitor software license management portals for exposed information
          Use Google dorks to find exposed configuration files and software documentation
          Analyze browser User-Agent strings from organization's public websites
          Monitor software vendor customer reference lists and case studies
          Monitor software development forums for questions posted by organization's employees
          Analyze package managers and dependency files in public repositories
          Inspect archived versions of websites using Wayback Machine for technology changes
          Monitor paste sites (Pastebin, GitHub Gists) for leaked software configurations
          Analyze email headers from organization's mail servers for software information
          Inspect third-party plugins and integrations on public-facing applications
          Review software vendor's customer portals for exposed deployment information
          Analyze software error pages and stack traces for version information
          Monitor software-specific APIs for version and configuration details
          Inspect software update logs and changelogs exposed on public servers
        Hardware: Adversaries may gather information about the victim's host hardware that can be used during targeting. Information about hardware infrastructure may include a variety of details such as types and versions on specific hosts, as well as the presence of additional components that might be indicative of added defensive protections (ex: card/biometric readers, dedicated encryption hardware, etc.).  Adversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) (ex: hostnames, server banners, user agent strings) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Adversaries may also compromise sites then include malicious content designed to collect host information from visitors.(Citation: ATT ScanBox) Information about the hardware infrastructure may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: job postings, network maps, assessment reports, resumes, or purchase invoices). Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593) or [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Develop Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587) or [Obtain Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588)), and/or initial access (ex: [Compromise Hardware Supply Chain](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/003) or [Hardware Additions](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1200)).
          Use network scanning tools like Nmap with OS fingerprinting to identify hardware specifications
          Analyze public procurement documents and invoices from victim organization's website
          Extract hardware information from HTTP User-Agent strings through compromised websites
