        Server: Adversaries may compromise third-party servers that can be used during targeting. Use of servers allows an adversary to stage, launch, and execute an operation. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize servers for various tasks, including for Command and Control.(Citation: TrendMicro EarthLusca 2022) Instead of purchasing a [Server](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/004) or [Virtual Private Server](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/003), adversaries may compromise third-party servers in support of operations.  Adversaries may also compromise web servers to support watering hole operations, as in [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189), or email servers to support [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) operations.
          Use SQL injection attacks to compromise web servers through vulnerable applications
          Exploit known vulnerabilities in server management interfaces (e.g., cPanel, WebMin)
          Deploy web shells on compromised servers for persistent access
          Compromise email servers through Exchange vulnerabilities (e.g., ProxyLogon, ProxyShell)
          Use automated scanning tools to identify and exploit vulnerable server instances
          Leverage stolen credentials to access administrative interfaces
          Exploit server misconfigurations (e.g., default credentials, open ports)
          Install backdoors on compromised web servers for watering hole attacks
          Modify server configurations to redirect traffic for phishing campaigns
          Use compromised servers as malware distribution points
          Use kernel exploits to gain root access on compromised Linux servers
          Modify server DNS settings to redirect traffic to malicious infrastructure
          Install cryptocurrency miners on compromised servers to monetize access
          Create backdoor admin accounts with legitimate-looking credentials
          Leverage compromised servers for proxy chains to hide malicious traffic
          Exploit vulnerable server backup systems to maintain persistence
          Use compromised servers to host command and control infrastructure
          Deploy rootkits to hide malicious activities on compromised servers
          Exploit trust relationships between servers for lateral movement
          Modify server logging mechanisms to avoid detection
          Exploit container escape vulnerabilities to compromise host servers
          Modify server SSL/TLS certificates to intercept encrypted traffic
          Compromise server load balancers to manipulate traffic distribution
          Exploit vulnerable server virtualization platforms (e.g., VMware, Hyper-V)
          Install malicious modules in popular server applications (Apache, Nginx)
          Leverage compromised server backups for data theft and persistence
          Exploit server automation tools (e.g., Ansible, Chef) for widespread compromise
          Modify server caching mechanisms to serve malicious content
          Use compromised servers to host phishing infrastructure
          Exploit server monitoring systems for network reconnaissance
        DNS Server: Adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers that can be used during targeting. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize DNS traffic for various tasks, including for Command and Control (ex: [Application Layer Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071)). Instead of setting up their own DNS servers, adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers in support of operations.  By compromising DNS servers, adversaries can alter DNS records. Such control can allow for redirection of an organization's traffic, facilitating Collection and Credential Access efforts for the adversary.(Citation: Talos DNSpionage Nov 2018)(Citation: FireEye DNS Hijack 2019)  Additionally, adversaries may leverage such control in conjunction with [Digital Certificates](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/004) to redirect traffic to adversary-controlled infrastructure, mimicking normal trusted network communications.(Citation: FireEye DNS Hijack 2019)(Citation: Crowdstrike DNS Hijack 2019) Adversaries may also be able to silently create subdomains pointed at malicious servers without tipping off the actual owner of the DNS server.(Citation: CiscoAngler)(Citation: Proofpoint Domain Shadowing)
