          Create timing attack implementations for cryptographic systems
        Malware: Adversaries may develop malware and malware components that can be used during targeting. Building malicious software can include the development of payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors (including backdoored images), packers, C2 protocols, and the creation of infected removable media. Adversaries may develop malware to support their operations, creating a means for maintaining control of remote machines, evading defenses, and executing post-compromise behaviors.(Citation: Mandiant APT1)(Citation: Kaspersky Sofacy)(Citation: ActiveMalwareEnergy)(Citation: FBI Flash FIN7 USB)  As with legitimate development efforts, different skill sets may be required for developing malware. The skills needed may be located in-house, or may need to be contracted out. Use of a contractor may be considered an extension of that adversary's malware development capabilities, provided the adversary plays a role in shaping requirements and maintains a degree of exclusivity to the malware.  Some aspects of malware development, such as C2 protocol development, may require adversaries to obtain additional infrastructure. For example, malware developed that will communicate with Twitter for C2, may require use of [Web Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/006).(Citation: FireEye APT29)
          Custom Payload Development: Development of specialized malicious code designed for specific target environments or objectives
          Command and Control Protocol Engineering: Creation of custom protocols for maintaining communication with compromised systems
          Backdoor Implementation: Development of persistent access mechanisms within legitimate software or systems
          Dropper Creation: Building specialized software to deliver and install other malware components
          Anti-Analysis Features: Development of capabilities to prevent reverse engineering and analysis
          Custom Encryption Implementation: Creating proprietary encryption schemes for malware communications and data protection
          Rootkit Development: Building software to hide malware presence and activities from the operating system
          Mobile Malware Development: Creating malicious software specifically targeting mobile platforms
          Firmware Malware Creation: Developing malware that targets system firmware or BIOS
          Modular Malware Framework: Building extensible malware platforms that can be customized with different modules
          Fileless Malware Development: Creating malware that operates primarily in memory without writing to disk
          Supply Chain Implant Development: Building malware specifically designed to compromise software distribution channels
          Virtual Machine Evasion: Developing capabilities to detect and evade virtual machine-based analysis
          Worm Propagation Mechanisms: Creating self-spreading capabilities for autonomous malware distribution
          Ransomware Development: Building specialized encryption and payment handling capabilities for ransomware operations
          Industrial Control System Malware: Developing malware targeting specific industrial protocols and systems
          Browser Extension Malware: Creating malicious browser extensions and related web-based malware
          Bootkit Development: Building malware that infects boot processes for persistence
          Network Protocol Abuse: Developing malware that exploits legitimate network protocols for communication
          Polymorphic Code Generation: Creating self-modifying code to evade signature-based detection
          Cloud Platform Malware: Developing malware specifically designed to operate in and exploit cloud environments
          Container Environment Malware: Creating malicious code targeting container platforms and orchestration systems
          Cross-Platform Malware Development: Building malware capable of operating across multiple operating systems
          IoT Device Malware: Developing specialized malware for Internet of Things devices
          Machine Learning Evasion: Creating malware that can adapt to and evade ML-based detection systems
          Hardware-Assisted Malware: Developing malware that leverages hardware features for persistence or evasion
          API Abuse Development: Creating malware that misuses legitimate APIs for malicious purposes
          Cryptocurrency Mining Malware: Building specialized crypto-mining capabilities
          Data Destruction Malware: Developing wipers and other destructive malware variants
          Lateral Movement Automation: Creating malware specifically designed to automate network spread
          Kernel-Mode Malware: Developing malware that operates at the kernel level for deep system access
          Microcode Malware: Creating malware that targets CPU microcode or low-level hardware instructions
          Hypervisor-Level Malware: Building malware that targets or creates malicious hypervisors
          UEFI/BIOS Malware: Developing malware specifically targeting UEFI/BIOS systems
          Network Stack Malware: Creating malware that operates at the network stack level
          Embedded Systems Malware: Developing malware for specialized embedded systems
          Quantum-Resistant Malware: Building malware with encryption designed to resist quantum computing analysis
          Memory-Only Rootkit: Creating sophisticated rootkits that exist purely in memory
          Trusted Execution Environment Malware: Developing malware that targets or abuses TEEs
          Air-Gap Bypass Malware: Creating specialized malware designed to breach air-gapped systems
        Digital Certificates: Adversaries may create self-signed SSL/TLS certificates that can be used during targeting. SSL/TLS certificates are designed to instill trust. They include information about the key, information about its owner's identity, and the digital signature of an entity that has verified the certificate's contents are correct. If the signature is valid, and the person examining the certificate trusts the signer, then they know they can use that key to communicate with its owner. In the case of self-signing, digital certificates will lack the element of trust associated with the signature of a third-party certificate authority (CA).  Adversaries may create self-signed SSL/TLS certificates that can be used to further their operations, such as encrypting C2 traffic (ex: [Asymmetric Cryptography](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/002) with [Web Protocols](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/001)) or even enabling [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557) if added to the root of trust (i.e. [Install Root Certificate](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/004)).  After creating a digital certificate, an adversary may then install that certificate (see [Install Digital Certificate](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/003)) on infrastructure under their control.
          Use OpenSSL command line tool to generate self-signed certificates with custom parameters
          Utilize PowerShell's New-SelfSignedCertificate cmdlet to create certificates on Windows systems
          Generate wildcard certificates (*.domain.com) to cover multiple subdomains
          Create certificates with extended validity periods beyond standard practices
