          Create fake legal compliance scenarios with time-sensitive document submission requirements
          Deploy supply chain compromise scenarios impersonating trusted third-party service providers
        Spearphishing Attachment: Adversaries may send spearphishing emails with a malicious attachment in an attempt to gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing attachment is a specific variant of spearphishing. Spearphishing attachment is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry. In this scenario, adversaries attach a file to the spearphishing email and usually rely upon [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) to gain execution.(Citation: Unit 42 DarkHydrus July 2018) Spearphishing may also involve social engineering techniques, such as posing as a trusted source.  There are many options for the attachment such as Microsoft Office documents, executables, PDFs, or archived files. Upon opening the attachment (and potentially clicking past protections), the adversary's payload exploits a vulnerability or directly executes on the user's system. The text of the spearphishing email usually tries to give a plausible reason why the file should be opened, and may explain how to bypass system protections in order to do so. The email may also contain instructions on how to decrypt an attachment, such as a zip file password, in order to evade email boundary defenses. Adversaries frequently manipulate file extensions and icons in order to make attached executables appear to be document files, or files exploiting one application appear to be a file for a different one.
          Send macro-enabled Office documents (.doc, .xls) that prompt users to "Enable Content" to execute malicious VBA code
          Attach password-protected ZIP files containing malware to bypass email scanning, including password in email body
          Use double file extensions (e.g., invoice.pdf.exe) and deceptive icons to disguise executable files
          Embed malicious scripts in seemingly benign PDF files that exploit Adobe Reader vulnerabilities
          Use LNK files disguised as documents that execute PowerShell commands when opened
          Attach ISO/IMG disk image files containing malware to bypass Mark-of-the-Web protections
          Send HTML attachments that download and execute malware when opened
          Use RAR files with right-to-left override characters to hide true file extensions
          Embed malicious macros in XLSM files that download additional payloads via BITS transfers
          Use WSF (Windows Script Files) disguised as document files to execute malicious JavaScript/VBScript
          Use CHM (Compiled HTML Help) files containing malicious scripts that execute on open
          Embed malware in DOCM templates that load remote content when opened
          Use OneNote attachments with embedded executables that bypass email filters
          Create malicious XML files that exploit Microsoft Office External Entity (XXE) vulnerabilities
          Use SFX (Self-extracting archives) disguised as legitimate software installers
          Attach malicious PPAM (PowerPoint add-in) files that auto-execute when PowerPoint opens
          Use DotM (Word template) files containing malicious macros that execute on document close
          Create malicious SYLK (Symbolic Link) files that bypass macro security warnings
          Use CAB files containing malicious scripts that execute through rundll32
          Attach HTA (HTML Application) files disguised as documentation or reports
          Embed malicious scripts in MIME HTML (MHT) files that execute via Internet Explorer
