          Create at job through WTS API: at 19:00 "wts.exe -s target -u admin -p pass cmd.exe"
          Use at with Group Policy scheduled tasks: at 20:00 "gpupdate /force && payload.exe"
          Chain at with Windows Event Log: at 21:00 "wevtutil.exe cl System && payload.exe"
      Windows Remote Management: Windows Remote Management (WinRM) is the name of both a Windows service and a protocol that allows a user to interact with a remote system (e.g., run an executable, modify the Registry, modify services). (Citation: Microsoft WinRM) It may be called with the <code>winrm</code> command or by any number of programs such as PowerShell. (Citation: Jacobsen 2014)
        ~NTLM Authentication: Using NTLM authentication mechanism to establish WinRM connections, potentially exploiting legacy authentication weaknesses
        ~Kerberos Authentication: Leveraging Kerberos tickets and authentication for WinRM connections
        ~PowerShell Remoting: Utilizing PowerShell's remoting capabilities through WinRM for command execution
        ~WinRS Command Line: Using the Windows Remote Shell (WinRS) command-line tool to execute commands
        ~Group Policy Configuration: Exploiting Group Policy settings to enable and configure WinRM across multiple systems
        ~Certificate-Based Authentication: Using certificate-based authentication for WinRM connections
        ~HTTP/HTTPS Transport: Utilizing different transport protocols (HTTP on 5985, HTTPS on 5986) for WinRM communication
        ~Custom Endpoint Creation: Creating and using custom WinRM endpoints for persistent access
      Exploitation for Client Execution: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in client applications to execute code. Vulnerabilities can exist in software due to unsecure coding practices that can lead to unanticipated behavior. Adversaries can take advantage of certain vulnerabilities through targeted exploitation for the purpose of arbitrary code execution. Oftentimes the most valuable exploits to an offensive toolkit are those that can be used to obtain code execution on a remote system because they can be used to gain access to that system. Users will expect to see files related to the applications they commonly used to do work, so they are a useful target for exploit research and development because of their high utility.  Several types exist:  ### Browser-based Exploitation  Web browsers are a common target through [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189) and [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002). Endpoint systems may be compromised through normal web browsing or from certain users being targeted by links in spearphishing emails to adversary controlled sites used to exploit the web browser. These often do not require an action by the user for the exploit to be executed.  ### Office Applications  Common office and productivity applications such as Microsoft Office are also targeted through [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566). Malicious files will be transmitted directly as attachments or through links to download them. These require the user to open the document or file for the exploit to run.  ### Common Third-party Applications  Other applications that are commonly seen or are part of the software deployed in a target network may also be used for exploitation. Applications such as Adobe Reader and Flash, which are common in enterprise environments, have been routinely targeted by adversaries attempting to gain access to systems. Depending on the software and nature of the vulnerability, some may be exploited in the browser or require the user to open a file. For instance, some Flash exploits have been delivered as objects within Microsoft Office documents.
        ~Browser Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in web browsers and their components to execute malicious code during web browsing sessions
        ~Office Application Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in office productivity software to execute malicious code when documents are opened
        ~PDF Reader Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in PDF readers to execute malicious code when PDF files are opened
        ~Media Player Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in media players and codecs to execute code when media files are processed
        ~Browser Plugin Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in browser plugins and extensions to execute malicious code
        ~Java Runtime Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Java Runtime Environment to execute malicious code across different platforms
        ~ActiveX Control Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in ActiveX controls to execute code through Internet Explorer
        ~Flash Player Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Adobe Flash Player to execute malicious code
      Scripting: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059) where appropriate.**  Adversaries may use scripts to aid in operations and perform multiple actions that would otherwise be manual. Scripting is useful for speeding up operational tasks and reducing the time required to gain access to critical resources. Some scripting languages may be used to bypass process monitoring mechanisms by directly interacting with the operating system at an API level instead of calling other programs. Common scripting languages for Windows include VBScript and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086) but could also be in the form of command-line batch scripts.  Scripts can be embedded inside Office documents as macros that can be set to execute when files used in [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193) and other types of spearphishing are opened. Malicious embedded macros are an alternative means of execution than software exploitation through [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1203), where adversaries will rely on macros being allowed or that the user will accept to activate them.  Many popular offensive frameworks exist which use forms of scripting for security testers and adversaries alike. Metasploit (Citation: Metasploit_Ref), Veil (Citation: Veil_Ref), and PowerSploit (Citation: Powersploit) are three examples that are popular among penetration testers for exploit and post-compromise operations and include many features for evading defenses. Some adversaries are known to use PowerShell. (Citation: Alperovitch 2014)
        ~PowerShell Scripting: Use of PowerShell for automation, system administration, and post-exploitation tasks
        ~Visual Basic Scripting: Use of VBScript for Windows automation and task execution
        ~Batch Scripting: Use of Windows batch files (.bat, .cmd) for command execution
        ~Office Macro Scripting: Embedding malicious macros in Office documents for execution
        ~Shell Scripting: Use of Unix/Linux shell scripts for system manipulation
        ~JavaScript Scripting: Use of JavaScript outside of browsers for system interaction
        ~Python Scripting: Use of Python scripts for automation and system manipulation
        ~AutoIT/AutoHotkey Scripting: Use of automation-focused scripting languages
        ~Administrative Tool Scripting: Abuse of built-in administrative scripting tools
        ~Cross-Platform Scripting: Scripts designed to work across multiple operating systems
        ~Active Directory Scripting: Use of AD-specific scripting languages and tools (like VBS for ADSI)
        ~Database Scripting: Use of SQL and other database scripting languages for data manipulation
        ~Registry Scripting: Scripts specifically targeting Windows Registry manipulation
        ~COM Scripting: Scripts leveraging Component Object Model for Windows automation
        ~WMI Scripting: Scripts using Windows Management Instrumentation
        ~HTA Scripting: HTML Application scripts combining HTML and scripting languages
        ~Network Device Scripting: Scripts targeting network device configuration and automation
        ~Enterprise Management Scripting: Scripts abusing enterprise management frameworks
        ~SCADA/ICS Scripting: Scripts targeting industrial control systems
        ~Security Tool Scripting: Scripts for security tools and frameworks (e.g., Metasploit, Cobalt Strike)
        ~Scheduled Task Scripting: Scripts executed through task scheduling mechanisms
        ~Container Orchestration Scripting: Scripts targeting container platforms (Docker, Kubernetes)
        ~Virtual Machine Scripting: Scripts for VM management and automation
        ~Remote Management Scripting: Scripts leveraging remote management protocols
        ~Cloud Platform Scripting: Scripts targeting cloud service providers' APIs and automation tools
        ~Configuration Management Scripting: Scripts for automated system configuration tools (Ansible, Puppet, Chef)
        ~Firmware/BIOS Scripting: Low-level system scripting targeting firmware or BIOS
        ~Mobile Device Management Scripting: Scripts targeting MDM systems and mobile devices
        ~Application Installation Scripting: Scripts for software deployment and installation automation
      Container Administration Command: Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment.(Citation: Docker Daemon CLI)(Citation: Kubernetes API)(Citation: Kubernetes Kubelet)  In Docker, adversaries may specify an entrypoint during container deployment that executes a script or command, or they may use a command such as <code>docker exec</code> to execute a command within a running container.(Citation: Docker Entrypoint)(Citation: Docker Exec) In Kubernetes, if an adversary has sufficient permissions, they may gain remote execution in a container in the cluster via interaction with the Kubernetes API server, the kubelet, or by running a command such as <code>kubectl exec</code>.(Citation: Kubectl Exec Get Shell)
        ~Docker Exec Commands: Adversaries may use docker exec to run commands in existing containers
        ~Kubernetes API Server Abuse: Adversaries may exploit the Kubernetes API server to execute commands in pods
        ~Kubelet API Exploitation: Adversaries may directly interact with kubelets to execute commands
        ~Container Entrypoint Manipulation: Adversaries may modify container entrypoints during deployment
        ~Container Runtime Socket Abuse: Adversaries may abuse container runtime sockets for command execution
        ~Container Management API Abuse: Adversaries may exploit container management APIs for remote execution
        ~Container Orchestration CLI Tools: Adversaries may abuse legitimate CLI tools like kubectl for command execution
        ~Container Build Time Command Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious commands during container build process
        ~Container Registry API Abuse: Adversaries may exploit container registry APIs to deploy compromised containers
        ~Container Service Account Abuse: Adversaries may leverage compromised service accounts for command execution
        ~Containerd Runtime Abuse: Adversaries may exploit containerd runtime interfaces for command execution
        ~CRI-O Runtime Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse CRI-O container runtime interfaces
        ~Container Network Interface (CNI) Abuse: Adversaries may exploit CNI plugins for command execution
        ~Container Storage Interface (CSI) Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse storage interfaces for command injection
        ~Container Sidecar Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious sidecar containers for command execution
        ~Container Init Process Abuse: Adversaries may manipulate container init processes
        ~Container Configuration Map Abuse: Adversaries may exploit config maps for command injection
        ~Container Secrets Management Abuse: Adversaries may leverage secrets management interfaces
        ~Cloud Container Service Abuse: Adversaries may exploit cloud-specific container management interfaces (ECS, AKS, GKE)
        ~Container Monitoring Tool Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse monitoring agents and interfaces
        ~Container Admission Controller Bypass: Adversaries may bypass admission controllers to execute commands
        ~Container Resource Quota Manipulation: Adversaries may exploit resource management interfaces
        ~Container Audit Log Manipulation: Adversaries may abuse logging interfaces for command execution
        ~Container Service Mesh Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse service mesh control planes
      Space after Filename: Adversaries can hide a program's true filetype by changing the extension of a file. With certain file types (specifically this does not work with .app extensions), appending a space to the end of a filename will change how the file is processed by the operating system. For example, if there is a Mach-O executable file called evil.bin, when it is double clicked by a user, it will launch Terminal.app and execute. If this file is renamed to evil.txt, then when double clicked by a user, it will launch with the default text editing application (not executing the binary). However, if the file is renamed to "evil.txt " (note the space at the end), then when double clicked by a user, the true file type is determined by the OS and handled appropriately and the binary will be executed (Citation: Mac Backdoors are back).   Adversaries can use this feature to trick users into double clicking benign-looking files of any format and ultimately executing something malicious.
        ~Terminal Command Execution: Using space-after-filename to execute shell commands or scripts through Terminal.app
        ~Application Masquerading: Renaming executable files to appear as document files (e.g., .txt, .doc) with trailing spaces
        ~Custom File Association Abuse: Exploiting file association handling when spaces are present
        ~Batch File Execution: Using spaces after filename to trigger batch file processing
        ~Multiple Space Characters: Adding multiple spaces or special Unicode spaces after filename
        ~Mixed Extension Abuse: Combining multiple extensions with spaces (e.g., "file.txt .exe")
        ~Directory Path Manipulation: Using spaces in directory paths to affect file execution
        ~System Service Execution: Leveraging spaces in service names or paths for execution
      Launchctl: Launchctl controls the macOS launchd process which handles things like launch agents and launch daemons, but can execute other commands or programs itself. Launchctl supports taking subcommands on the command-line, interactively, or even redirected from standard input. By loading or reloading launch agents or launch daemons, adversaries can install persistence or execute changes they made  (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan). Running a command from launchctl is as simple as <code>launchctl submit -l <labelName> -- /Path/to/thing/to/execute "arg" "arg" "arg"</code>. Loading, unloading, or reloading launch agents or launch daemons can require elevated privileges.   Adversaries can abuse this functionality to execute code or even bypass whitelisting if launchctl is an allowed process.
        ~Launch Agent Manipulation: Loading or reloading malicious launch agents through launchctl to execute code or establish persistence
        ~Launch Daemon Manipulation: Abusing launch daemons through launchctl with elevated privileges to execute system-level commands
        ~Direct Command Execution: Using launchctl submit command to directly execute malicious commands or scripts
        ~Standard Input Redirection: Exploiting launchctl's ability to accept commands from standard input for stealthy execution
        ~Interactive Mode Abuse: Leveraging launchctl's interactive mode to execute multiple commands in sequence
        ~Label Manipulation: Creating or modifying service labels to disguise malicious activities
        ~Privilege Escalation via Launchctl: Exploiting launchctl's elevated privilege requirements for certain operations
        ~Configuration Modification: Modifying existing launchctl configurations to execute malicious code
      Graphical User Interface: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) where appropriate.**  The Graphical User Interfaces (GUI) is a common way to interact with an operating system. Adversaries may use a system's GUI during an operation, commonly through a remote interactive session such as [Remote Desktop Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1076), instead of through a [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059), to search for information and execute files via mouse double-click events, the Windows Run command (Citation: Wikipedia Run Command), or other potentially difficult to monitor interactions.
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) Access: Using Microsoft's RDP protocol to gain GUI-based remote access to systems
        ~VNC-based Access: Utilizing Virtual Network Computing protocols for remote GUI manipulation
        ~GUI Automation Tools: Using tools like AutoIT or similar to automate GUI interactions
        ~Run Dialog Execution: Leveraging the Windows Run dialog for command execution
        ~Context Menu Manipulation: Exploiting right-click context menus for execution
        ~Desktop Shortcut Abuse: Using or creating desktop shortcuts for malicious execution
        ~Start Menu Navigation: Accessing and executing programs through the Start Menu interface
        ~System Tray Interactions: Manipulating system tray applications and their interfaces
        ~File Explorer Operations: Using GUI-based file operations for malicious purposes
        ~Dialog Box Manipulation: Exploiting system dialogs and prompts for execution
        ~Control Panel Execution: Accessing and executing programs through Control Panel applets
        ~Task Manager Interface: Using Task Manager's GUI to launch processes
        ~MMC Console Operations: Leveraging Microsoft Management Console interfaces
        ~Scheduled Tasks GUI: Using the Task Scheduler interface to create and execute tasks
        ~Network Connections GUI: Exploiting network configuration interfaces for execution
        ~Alternative Shell Execution: Using alternative desktop environments or shell replacements
        ~System Properties Dialogs: Accessing system tools through properties dialogs
        ~Administrative Tools Menu: Leveraging administrative tools through their GUI interfaces
        ~User Account Control Dialogs: Exploiting UAC prompts and elevation dialogs
        ~Login Screen Interface: Manipulating login screen components and accessibility features
        ~Application Installer Wizards: Leveraging software installation GUI workflows
        ~Notification Center Actions: Using system notifications and action center interfaces
        ~Screen Lock Interface: Exploiting lock screen features and notifications
        ~Accessibility Tools Interface: Abusing accessibility features through their GUI
        ~Windows Settings App: Using modern Windows Settings interface for execution
      InstallUtil: InstallUtil is a command-line utility that allows for installation and uninstallation of resources by executing specific installer components specified in .NET binaries. (Citation: MSDN InstallUtil) InstallUtil is located in the .NET directories on a Windows system: <code>C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v<version>\InstallUtil.exe</code> and <code>C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v<version>\InstallUtil.exe</code>. InstallUtil.exe is digitally signed by Microsoft.  Adversaries may use InstallUtil to proxy execution of code through a trusted Windows utility. InstallUtil may also be used to bypass process whitelisting through use of attributes within the binary that execute the class decorated with the attribute <code>[System.ComponentModel.RunInstaller(true)]</code>. (Citation: LOLBAS Installutil)
        ~Malicious Assembly Execution: Execute malicious code by creating a .NET assembly with RunInstaller attribute and using InstallUtil to run it
        ~Uninstall Method Abuse: Leverage the uninstall method (/u) to execute malicious code while appearing as legitimate uninstallation
        ~Parameter Manipulation: Use various command-line parameters to modify execution behavior and evade detection
        ~Framework Version Targeting: Specifically target different .NET framework versions to exploit version-specific vulnerabilities or behaviors
        ~Assembly Loading Techniques: Different methods of loading malicious assemblies through InstallUtil, including remote loading
        ~Memory-Based Execution: Execute malicious code directly in memory without writing to disk by loading assemblies from byte arrays
        ~Alternative Host Process Abuse: Execute InstallUtil through alternative trusted host processes like PowerShell or MSBuild
        ~Custom Installer Class Implementation: Create specialized installer classes with custom logic for different attack scenarios
        ~Logger Class Abuse: Exploit InstallUtil's logging mechanisms to execute additional code
        ~Cross-Process Injection: Use InstallUtil to facilitate code injection into other processes
      Input Injection: Adversaries may simulate keystrokes on a victim’s computer by various means to perform any type of action on behalf of the user, such as launching the command interpreter using keyboard shortcuts,  typing an inline script to be executed, or interacting directly with a GUI-based application.  These actions can be preprogrammed into adversary tooling or executed through physical devices such as Human Interface Devices (HIDs).  For example, adversaries have used tooling that monitors the Windows message loop to detect when a user visits bank-specific URLs. If detected, the tool then simulates keystrokes to open the developer console or select the address bar, pastes malicious JavaScript from the clipboard, and executes it - enabling manipulation of content within the browser, such as replacing bank account numbers during transactions.(Citation: BleepingComputer BackSwap)(Citation: welivesecurity BackSwap)  Adversaries have also used malicious USB devices to emulate keystrokes that launch PowerShell, leading to the download and execution of malware from adversary-controlled servers.(Citation: BleepingComputer USB)
        ~Software-Based Keystroke Simulation: Adversaries use software tools to programmatically generate and inject keystrokes into applications or system interfaces
        ~Hardware Interface Device Spoofing: Adversaries use malicious USB or other HID devices to simulate keyboard/mouse input
        ~Browser Input Manipulation: Adversaries inject keystrokes specifically to manipulate web browser behavior and content
        ~Automation Framework Abuse: Adversaries leverage legitimate automation frameworks and accessibility features to simulate user input
        ~GUI Element Manipulation: Adversaries simulate mouse clicks and interactions with graphical interface elements
        ~Clipboard Input Injection: Adversaries manipulate clipboard contents and simulate paste operations
        ~Touch Input Simulation: Adversaries simulate touch input events on systems with touchscreen capabilities
        ~Game Controller Spoofing: Adversaries emulate game controller input to execute malicious actions
        ~Remote Input Simulation: Adversaries use remote access tools to simulate input from a distance
        ~Sensor Input Spoofing: Adversaries simulate input from system sensors like accelerometers or light sensors
      CMSTP: The Microsoft Connection Manager Profile Installer (CMSTP.exe) is a command-line program used to install Connection Manager service profiles. (Citation: Microsoft Connection Manager Oct 2009) CMSTP.exe accepts an installation information file (INF) as a parameter and installs a service profile leveraged for remote access connections.  Adversaries may supply CMSTP.exe with INF files infected with malicious commands. (Citation: Twitter CMSTP Usage Jan 2018) Similar to [Regsvr32](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1117) / ”Squiblydoo”, CMSTP.exe may be abused to load and execute DLLs (Citation: MSitPros CMSTP Aug 2017)  and/or COM scriptlets (SCT) from remote servers. (Citation: Twitter CMSTP Jan 2018) (Citation: GitHub Ultimate AppLocker Bypass List) (Citation: Endurant CMSTP July 2018) This execution may also bypass AppLocker and other whitelisting defenses since CMSTP.exe is a legitimate, signed Microsoft application.  CMSTP.exe can also be abused to [Bypass User Account Control](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1088) and execute arbitrary commands from a malicious INF through an auto-elevated COM interface. (Citation: MSitPros CMSTP Aug 2017) (Citation: GitHub Ultimate AppLocker Bypass List) (Citation: Endurant CMSTP July 2018)
        ~Malicious INF File Execution: Adversaries may create specially crafted INF files containing malicious commands to be executed by CMSTP.exe
        ~Remote DLL Loading: Adversaries may abuse CMSTP to load malicious DLLs from remote locations through INF file specifications
        ~COM Scriptlet Execution: Using CMSTP to execute malicious COM scriptlets (SCT files) hosted on remote servers
        ~UAC Bypass via COM Interface: Exploiting CMSTP's auto-elevated COM interface to bypass User Account Control
        ~AppLocker Bypass: Leveraging CMSTP as a signed Microsoft binary to bypass application whitelisting controls
        ~Service Profile Installation: Adversaries may abuse CMSTP's legitimate service profile installation functionality to establish persistence
        ~Network Connection Manipulation: Abusing CMSTP to modify network connection settings and establish malicious connections
        ~Command Line Parameter Abuse: Exploiting various command-line parameters of CMSTP.exe for code execution
        ~Registry Modification: Using CMSTP to make malicious registry changes during profile installation
    persistence: The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold.  Persistence consists of techniques that adversaries use to keep access to systems across restarts, changed credentials, and other interruptions that could cut off their access. Techniques used for persistence include any access, action, or configuration changes that let them maintain their foothold on systems, such as replacing or hijacking legitimate code or adding startup code.
      Server Software Component: Adversaries may abuse legitimate extensible development features of servers to establish persistent access to systems. Enterprise server applications may include features that allow developers to write and install software or scripts to extend the functionality of the main application. Adversaries may install malicious components to extend and abuse server applications.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW)
        vSphere Installation Bundles: Adversaries may abuse vSphere Installation Bundles (VIBs) to establish persistent access to ESXi hypervisors. VIBs are collections of files used for software distribution and virtual system management in VMware environments. Since ESXi uses an in-memory filesystem where changes made to most files are stored in RAM rather than in persistent storage, these modifications are lost after a reboot. However, VIBs can be used to create startup tasks, apply custom firewall rules, or deploy binaries that persist across reboots. Typically, administrators use VIBs for updates and system maintenance.  VIBs can be broken down into three components:(Citation: VMware VIBs)  * VIB payload: a `.vgz` archive containing the directories and files to be created and executed on boot when the VIBs are loaded.   * Signature file: verifies the host acceptance level of a VIB, indicating what testing and validation has been done by VMware or its partners before publication of a VIB. By default, ESXi hosts require a minimum acceptance level of PartnerSupported for VIB installation, meaning the VIB is published by a trusted VMware partner. However, privileged users can change the default acceptance level using the `esxcli` command line interface. Additionally, VIBs are able to be installed regardless of acceptance level by using the <code> esxcli software vib install --force</code> command.  * XML descriptor file: a configuration file containing associated VIB metadata, such as the name of the VIB and its dependencies.    Adversaries may leverage malicious VIB packages to maintain persistent access to ESXi hypervisors, allowing system changes to be executed upon each bootup of ESXi – such as using  `esxcli` to enable firewall rules for backdoor traffic, creating listeners on hard coded ports, and executing backdoors.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence ESXi VIBs 2022) Adversaries may also masquerade their malicious VIB files as PartnerSupported by modifying the XML descriptor file.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence ESXi VIBs 2022)
          Create malicious VIB payload with backdoor binary and modify XML descriptor to fake PartnerSupported status
          Use esxcli software vib install --force command to bypass acceptance level checks
