          Use Windows Resource Protection (WRP) bypass techniques to modify protected termsrv.dll
          Implement periodic DLL content rotation to evade file integrity monitoring
          Modify Terminal Services group policy objects to load custom DLL configurations
          Create persistent scheduled tasks to restore malicious termsrv.dll modifications
          Implement code caves within termsrv.dll to store malicious payload without size change
          Use DLL function stomping to replace unused Terminal Services functions with malicious code
          Leverage Windows Installer to deploy modified termsrv.dll through legitimate update channels
          Create system restore point blocks to prevent recovery of original termsrv.dll
          Implement RDP session multiplexing through modified protocol handlers in termsrv.dll
          Use Microsoft Detours library to implement persistent DLL modifications
          Implement AMSI bypass within modified termsrv.dll to evade security scanning
          Create parallel Terminal Service instances with modified DLL loading sequences
          Use DLL signature cloning to maintain valid digital signatures after modification
          Implement dead drop resolver components in termsrv.dll for covert C2
          Create Terminal Services kernel callbacks for persistent DLL loading
          Use DLL sideloading through Terminal Services application directory
          Implement cross-process injection through Terminal Services subsystems
          Create shadow Terminal Services infrastructure with modified DLL chain
          Use binary padding in termsrv.dll to avoid detection signatures
          Implement self-healing mechanisms through service control manager callbacks
      Startup Items: Per Apple’s documentation, startup items execute during the final phase of the boot process and contain shell scripts or other executable files along with configuration information used by the system to determine the execution order for all startup items (Citation: Startup Items). This is technically a deprecated version (superseded by Launch Daemons), and thus the appropriate folder, <code>/Library/StartupItems</code> isn’t guaranteed to exist on the system by default, but does appear to exist by default on macOS Sierra. A startup item is a directory whose executable and configuration property list (plist), <code>StartupParameters.plist</code>, reside in the top-level directory.   An adversary can create the appropriate folders/files in the StartupItems directory to register their own persistence mechanism (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence). Additionally, since StartupItems run during the bootup phase of macOS, they will run as root. If an adversary is able to modify an existing Startup Item, then they will be able to Privilege Escalate as well.
        ~System Startup Items: Creation or modification of items in /Library/StartupItems for system-wide persistence
        ~User Startup Items: Manipulation of user-specific startup items in ~/Library/StartupItems
        ~StartupParameters.plist Modification: Altering configuration files to modify startup behavior
        ~Startup Item Hijacking: Replacing legitimate startup items with malicious ones
        ~Hidden Startup Items: Concealing malicious startup items using special characters or hidden directories
        ~Legacy Startup Item Abuse: Exploiting deprecated startup mechanisms that may still be present
        ~Startup Item Privilege Escalation: Leveraging startup items to gain elevated permissions
        ~Cross-Platform Startup Items: Creating startup items that work across different macOS versions
      Cloud Application Integration: Adversaries may achieve persistence by leveraging OAuth application integrations in a software-as-a-service environment. Adversaries may create a custom application, add a legitimate application into the environment, or even co-opt an existing integration to achieve malicious ends.(Citation: Push Security SaaS Persistence 2022)(Citation: SaaS Attacks GitHub Evil Twin Integrations)  OAuth is an open standard that allows users to authorize applications to access their information on their behalf. In a SaaS environment such as Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, users may integrate applications to improve their workflow and achieve tasks.    Leveraging application integrations may allow adversaries to persist in an environment – for example, by granting consent to an application from a high-privileged adversary-controlled account in order to maintain access to its data, even in the event of losing access to the account.(Citation: Wiz Midnight Blizzard 2024)(Citation: Microsoft Malicious OAuth Applications 2022)(Citation: Huntress Persistence Microsoft 365 Compromise 2024) In some cases, integrations may remain valid even after the original consenting user account is disabled.(Citation: Push Security Slack Persistence 2023) Application integrations may also allow adversaries to bypass multi-factor authentication requirements through the use of [Application Access Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/001)s. Finally, they may enable persistent [Automated Exfiltration](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1020) over time.(Citation: Synes Cyber Corner Malicious Azure Application 2023)  Creating or adding a new application may require the adversary to create a dedicated [Cloud Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136/003) for the application and assign it [Additional Cloud Roles](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/003) – for example, in Microsoft 365 environments, an application can only access resources via an associated service principal.(Citation: Microsoft Entra ID Service Principals)
        ~Custom Application Creation: Adversaries create malicious applications from scratch and register them with cloud platforms to gain OAuth permissions and maintain persistence
        ~Legitimate Application Abuse: Adversaries leverage existing legitimate applications by modifying their configurations or permissions to maintain unauthorized access
        ~Application Impersonation: Adversaries create applications that mimic legitimate ones to trick users or administrators into granting permissions
        ~Service Principal Manipulation: Adversaries modify service principal configurations and permissions to maintain persistent access to cloud resources
        ~OAuth Token Abuse: Adversaries manipulate OAuth tokens and refresh mechanisms to maintain long-term access to cloud resources
        ~Integration Chain Exploitation: Adversaries abuse chains of integrated applications to maintain access across multiple services and persist even if one integration is discovered
        ~Cross-Platform Integration: Adversaries leverage integrations between different cloud platforms (e.g., Slack to Google Workspace) to establish redundant persistence mechanisms
        ~Delegated Permission Abuse: Adversaries exploit delegated admin permissions and consent mechanisms to maintain persistent access through application integrations
        ~Tenant-Level Integration: Adversaries establish organization-wide integrations that persist beyond individual user account compromises
        ~Integration Configuration Tampering: Adversaries modify integration settings, webhooks, or callback URLs to maintain control even if the original integration is detected
        ~Backup Integration Abuse: Adversaries establish secondary or backup integrations that remain dormant until primary access is lost
        ~Version Control Exploitation: Adversaries manipulate application version controls and update mechanisms to maintain persistence across application updates
        ~Marketplace Application Abuse: Adversaries leverage third-party marketplace applications and their associated trust relationships to maintain persistence
        ~Workflow Automation Exploitation: Adversaries embed persistent access in automated workflows and integrations (e.g., Power Automate, Zapier)
        ~API Gateway Manipulation: Adversaries modify API gateway configurations and routing rules to maintain persistent access to integrated services
      Systemd Service: Systemd services can be used to establish persistence on a Linux system. The systemd service manager is commonly used for managing background daemon processes (also known as services) and other system resources.(Citation: Linux man-pages: systemd January 2014)(Citation: Freedesktop.org Linux systemd 29SEP2018) Systemd is the default initialization (init) system on many Linux distributions starting with Debian 8, Ubuntu 15.04, CentOS 7, RHEL 7, Fedora 15, and replaces legacy init systems including SysVinit and Upstart while remaining backwards compatible with the aforementioned init systems.  Systemd utilizes configuration files known as service units to control how services boot and under what conditions. By default, these unit files are stored in the <code>/etc/systemd/system</code> and <code>/usr/lib/systemd/system</code> directories and have the file extension <code>.service</code>. Each service unit file may contain numerous directives that can execute system commands.   * ExecStart, ExecStartPre, and ExecStartPost directives cover execution of commands when a services is started manually by 'systemctl' or on system start if the service is set to automatically start.  * ExecReload directive covers when a service restarts.  * ExecStop and ExecStopPost directives cover when a service is stopped or manually by 'systemctl'.  Adversaries have used systemd functionality to establish persistent access to victim systems by creating and/or modifying service unit files that cause systemd to execute malicious commands at recurring intervals, such as at system boot.(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019)(Citation: gist Arch package compromise 10JUL2018)(Citation: Arch Linux Package Systemd Compromise BleepingComputer 10JUL2018)(Citation: acroread package compromised Arch Linux Mail 8JUL2018)  While adversaries typically require root privileges to create/modify service unit files in the <code>/etc/systemd/system</code> and <code>/usr/lib/systemd/system</code> directories, low privilege users can create/modify service unit files in directories such as <code>~/.config/systemd/user/</code> to achieve user-level persistence.(Citation: Rapid7 Service Persistence 22JUNE2016)
        ~System-Level Service Units: Adversaries create/modify service units in /etc/systemd/system or /usr/lib/systemd/system to establish system-wide persistence requiring root privileges
        ~User-Level Service Units: Adversaries create/modify service units in ~/.config/systemd/user for user-level persistence without requiring root privileges
        ~Timer-Based Execution: Adversaries use systemd timer units alongside service units to schedule periodic execution of malicious commands
        ~Socket-Based Activation: Adversaries configure systemd socket activation to trigger malicious services when specific network connections are received
        ~Dependency-Based Execution: Adversaries modify service dependencies to ensure their malicious service starts when other legitimate services start
        ~Environment Variable Manipulation: Adversaries abuse service unit environment variables to modify service behavior and maintain persistence
        ~Service Template Exploitation: Adversaries abuse systemd service templates to create multiple instances of malicious services
        ~Service Override Manipulation: Adversaries create override.conf files to modify existing service behavior without changing original unit files
      Port Monitors: A port monitor can be set through the  (Citation: AddMonitor) API call to set a DLL to be loaded at startup. (Citation: AddMonitor) This DLL can be located in <code>C:\Windows\System32</code> and will be loaded by the print spooler service, spoolsv.exe, on boot. The spoolsv.exe process also runs under SYSTEM level permissions. (Citation: Bloxham) Alternatively, an arbitrary DLL can be loaded if permissions allow writing a fully-qualified pathname for that DLL to <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Print\Monitors</code>.   The Registry key contains entries for the following:  * Local Port * Standard TCP/IP Port * USB Monitor * WSD Port  Adversaries can use this technique to load malicious code at startup that will persist on system reboot and execute as SYSTEM.
        ~Registry-Based Port Monitor: Direct modification of HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Print\Monitors to register malicious DLLs
        ~API-Based Port Monitor: Using AddMonitor API to programmatically register port monitor DLLs
        ~Print Spooler Service Manipulation: Targeting spoolsv.exe directly to load malicious port monitors
        ~Custom Port Monitor Protocol: Creating custom port monitor protocols to evade detection
        ~Port Monitor DLL Replacement: Replacing legitimate port monitor DLLs with malicious versions
        ~Network Port Monitor Injection: Exploiting network-based port monitors to establish persistence across networked printers
        ~Print Driver Package Abuse: Incorporating malicious port monitors within legitimate print driver packages
        ~Local Port Monitor Proxy: Creating proxy mechanisms between local and network port monitors
        ~Monitor Configuration Tampering: Modifying existing port monitor configurations rather than adding new ones
        ~Port Monitor Chain Loading: Using legitimate port monitors to chain-load additional malicious monitors
      Login Item: MacOS provides the option to list specific applications to run when a user logs in. These applications run under the logged in user's context, and will be started every time the user logs in. Login items installed using the Service Management Framework are not visible in the System Preferences and can only be removed by the application that created them (Citation: Adding Login Items). Users have direct control over login items installed using a shared file list which are also visible in System Preferences (Citation: Adding Login Items). These login items are stored in the user's <code>~/Library/Preferences/</code> directory in a plist file called <code>com.apple.loginitems.plist</code> (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence). Some of these applications can open visible dialogs to the user, but they don’t all have to since there is an option to ‘Hide’ the window. If an adversary can register their own login item or modified an existing one, then they can use it to execute their code for a persistence mechanism each time the user logs in (Citation: Malware Persistence on OS X) (Citation: OSX.Dok Malware). The API method <code> SMLoginItemSetEnabled </code> can be used to set Login Items, but scripting languages like [AppleScript](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1155) can do this as well  (Citation: Adding Login Items).
        ~Service Management Framework Login Items: Adversaries create hidden login items using SMLoginItemSetEnabled API that are not visible in System Preferences
        ~Shared File List Login Items: Adversaries modify the com.apple.loginitems.plist file directly to add malicious applications to user login items
        ~AppleScript Login Item Creation: Adversaries use AppleScript to programmatically add login items through automation
        ~Login Item Modification: Adversaries modify existing legitimate login items to point to malicious executables
        ~Hidden Window Login Items: Adversaries leverage the 'Hide' window option to make malicious login items run invisibly
        ~System-Wide Login Items: Adversaries target /Library/Preferences/ to create system-wide login items affecting all users
        ~Launch Services Login Items Registration: Adversaries leverage Launch Services API to register login items programmatically
        ~Login Item Inheritance: Adversaries exploit parent-child process relationships to maintain persistence through inherited login items
        ~Login Item Synchronization Abuse: Adversaries exploit iCloud synchronization to propagate malicious login items across devices
      Valid Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of existing accounts as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access, network devices, and remote desktop.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW) Compromised credentials may also grant an adversary increased privilege to specific systems or access to restricted areas of the network. Adversaries may choose not to use malware or tools in conjunction with the legitimate access those credentials provide to make it harder to detect their presence.  In some cases, adversaries may abuse inactive accounts: for example, those belonging to individuals who are no longer part of an organization. Using these accounts may allow the adversary to evade detection, as the original account user will not be present to identify any anomalous activity taking place on their account.(Citation: CISA MFA PrintNightmare)  The overlap of permissions for local, domain, and cloud accounts across a network of systems is of concern because the adversary may be able to pivot across accounts and systems to reach a high level of access (i.e., domain or enterprise administrator) to bypass access controls set within the enterprise.(Citation: TechNet Credential Theft)
        Default Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of a default account as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Default accounts are those that are built-into an OS, such as the Guest or Administrator accounts on Windows systems. Default accounts also include default factory/provider set accounts on other types of systems, software, or devices, including the root user account in AWS, the root user account in ESXi, and the default service account in Kubernetes.(Citation: Microsoft Local Accounts Feb 2019)(Citation: AWS Root User)(Citation: Threat Matrix for Kubernetes)  Default accounts are not limited to client machines; rather, they also include accounts that are preset for equipment such as network devices and computer applications, whether they are internal, open source, or commercial. Appliances that come preset with a username and password combination pose a serious threat to organizations that do not change it post installation, as they are easy targets for an adversary. Similarly, adversaries may also utilize publicly disclosed or stolen [Private Keys](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/004) or credential materials to legitimately connect to remote environments via [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021).(Citation: Metasploit SSH Module)  Default accounts may be created on a system after initial setup by connecting or integrating it with another application. For example, when an ESXi server is connected to a vCenter server, a default privileged account called `vpxuser` is created on the ESXi server. If a threat actor is able to compromise this account’s credentials (for example, via [Exploitation for Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1212) on the vCenter host), they will then have access to the ESXi server.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence VMWare ESXi Zero-Day 2023)(Citation: Pentera vCenter Information Disclosure)
          Operating System Default Accounts: Abuse of built-in system accounts like Administrator, Guest, or root in various operating systems
          Application Service Accounts: Exploitation of default service accounts created during software installation or integration
          Network Device Default Credentials: Use of factory-preset credentials in network appliances and infrastructure devices
          Cloud Platform Default Accounts: Abuse of default administrative accounts in cloud services and platforms
          Database Default Accounts: Exploitation of default database administrator accounts and service accounts
          IoT Device Default Credentials: Use of manufacturer-set default credentials in IoT devices and embedded systems
          Development Environment Defaults: Abuse of default accounts in development tools, build systems, and testing environments
          Virtual Infrastructure Defaults: Exploitation of default accounts in virtualization platforms and management interfaces
          Security Appliance Defaults: Abuse of default administrative accounts in security devices and monitoring systems
          Backup System Defaults: Use of default accounts in backup software and disaster recovery systems
          Container Platform Defaults: Exploitation of default service accounts in container orchestration platforms and registries
          Identity Provider Defaults: Abuse of default accounts in authentication systems and directory services
          Management Console Defaults: Use of default administrative accounts in remote management interfaces and consoles
          Industrial Control System Defaults: Exploitation of default accounts in SCADA systems and industrial equipment
          Telecommunications Equipment Defaults: Abuse of default credentials in telecom infrastructure and equipment
          Embedded Web Server Defaults: Exploitation of default accounts in embedded web servers and interfaces
          Print System Defaults: Abuse of default accounts in printer management systems and print servers
          Legacy System Defaults: Exploitation of default accounts in legacy or obsolete systems that remain connected
          Testing/QA Environment Defaults: Use of default accounts left in testing and quality assurance environments
          Emergency Access Defaults: Abuse of emergency or break-glass accounts configured as defaults
        Local Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of a local account as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Local accounts are those configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration on a single system or service.  Local Accounts may also be abused to elevate privileges and harvest credentials through [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). Password reuse may allow the abuse of local accounts across a set of machines on a network for the purposes of Privilege Escalation and Lateral Movement.
          Use Mimikatz to dump local SAM hashes from Windows systems
          Exploit default administrator account with common password patterns
          Abuse local service accounts by modifying their privileges
          Create new hidden local admin accounts with names mimicking system processes
          Modify local account policies to prevent lockouts and logging
          Extract cached credentials from Local Security Authority Subsystem Service (LSASS)
          Exploit password reuse across local accounts using password spraying
          Leverage built-in Windows commands like 'net user' to enumerate local accounts
          Modify Registry to enable auto-logon for persistence with local accounts
          Use PowerShell scripts to modify local account group memberships
          Modify local account password hash in SAM database directly
          Abuse Windows Credential Editor (WCE) to create tokens from local accounts
          Exploit sticky keys and accessibility features for local account backdoors
          Extract credentials from local Windows credential vault
          Abuse password reset disk creation for local accounts
          Modify local security policy to disable account auditing
          Use third-party password recovery tools to reset local account passwords
          Create secondary local accounts through Guest account exploitation
          Exploit local account password hints for password guessing
          Leverage scheduled tasks with stored local account credentials
          Extract credentials from Windows Credential Manager backup files
          Abuse Windows Easy Transfer for local account migration
          Exploit stored RDP credentials in local account contexts
          Modify local security support providers (SSP) for credential interception
          Abuse Windows backup utilities to extract local account data
          Exploit local account password reset questions
          Leverage local account credential caching in remote access tools
          Modify local account login scripts for persistence
          Abuse Windows To Go feature for local account extraction
          Exploit local account password complexity policy modification
        Cloud Accounts: Valid accounts in cloud environments may allow adversaries to perform actions to achieve Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Cloud accounts are those created and configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration of resources within a cloud service provider or SaaS application. Cloud Accounts can exist solely in the cloud; alternatively, they may be hybrid-joined between on-premises systems and the cloud through syncing or federation with other identity sources such as Windows Active Directory.(Citation: AWS Identity Federation)(Citation: Google Federating GC)(Citation: Microsoft Deploying AD Federation)  Service or user accounts may be targeted by adversaries through [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110), [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), or various other means to gain access to the environment. Federated or synced accounts may be a pathway for the adversary to affect both on-premises systems and cloud environments - for example, by leveraging shared credentials to log onto [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021). High privileged cloud accounts, whether federated, synced, or cloud-only, may also allow pivoting to on-premises environments by leveraging SaaS-based [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) to run commands on hybrid-joined devices.  An adversary may create long lasting [Additional Cloud Credentials](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001) on a compromised cloud account to maintain persistence in the environment. Such credentials may also be used to bypass security controls such as multi-factor authentication.   Cloud accounts may also be able to assume [Temporary Elevated Cloud Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/005) or other privileges through various means within the environment. Misconfigurations in role assignments or role assumption policies may allow an adversary to use these mechanisms to leverage permissions outside the intended scope of the account. Such over privileged accounts may be used to harvest sensitive data from online storage accounts and databases through [Cloud API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/009) or other methods. For example, in Azure environments, adversaries may target Azure Managed Identities, which allow associated Azure resources to request access tokens. By compromising a resource with an attached Managed Identity, such as an Azure VM, adversaries may be able to [Steal Application Access Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528)s to move laterally across the cloud environment.(Citation: SpecterOps Managed Identity 2022)
          Use stolen credentials to create long-term access tokens in AWS IAM
          Exploit Azure Managed Identity token generation on compromised VMs
          Create persistent service principals with high privileges in Azure AD
          Configure AWS cross-account role assumption policies for persistence
