      Kernel Modules and Extensions: Loadable Kernel Modules (or LKMs) are pieces of code that can be loaded and unloaded into the kernel upon demand. They extend the functionality of the kernel without the need to reboot the system. For example, one type of module is the device driver, which allows the kernel to access hardware connected to the system. (Citation: Linux Kernel Programming) When used maliciously, Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) can be a type of kernel-mode [Rootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1014) that run with the highest operating system privilege (Ring 0). (Citation: Linux Kernel Module Programming Guide) Adversaries can use loadable kernel modules to covertly persist on a system and evade defenses. Examples have been found in the wild and there are some open source projects. (Citation: Volatility Phalanx2) (Citation: CrowdStrike Linux Rootkit) (Citation: GitHub Reptile) (Citation: GitHub Diamorphine)  Common features of LKM based rootkits include: hiding itself, selective hiding of files, processes and network activity, as well as log tampering, providing authenticated backdoors and enabling root access to non-privileged users. (Citation: iDefense Rootkit Overview)  Kernel extensions, also called kext, are used for macOS to load functionality onto a system similar to LKMs for Linux. They are loaded and unloaded through <code>kextload</code> and <code>kextunload</code> commands. Several examples have been found where this can be used. (Citation: RSAC 2015 San Francisco Patrick Wardle) (Citation: Synack Secure Kernel Extension Broken) Examples have been found in the wild. (Citation: Securelist Ventir)
        ~Linux Kernel Module Installation: Installation of malicious LKMs on Linux systems using insmod/modprobe to achieve persistence
        ~macOS Kernel Extension Abuse: Exploitation of macOS kext system to load malicious kernel extensions
        ~Driver Registration Manipulation: Modifying driver registration mechanisms to load malicious kernel code
        ~System Call Table Hooking: Using kernel modules to modify system call tables for persistence
        ~Kernel Function Hooking: Implementing hooks on kernel functions through modules to intercept system operations
        ~Hidden Module Implementation: Techniques to hide kernel modules from standard system utilities and security tools
        ~Boot-time Module Loading: Configuration of kernel modules to load automatically during system boot
        ~Module Parameter Manipulation: Abuse of kernel module parameters to maintain malicious functionality
      Web Shell: A Web shell is a Web script that is placed on an openly accessible Web server to allow an adversary to use the Web server as a gateway into a network. A Web shell may provide a set of functions to execute or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the Web server. In addition to a server-side script, a Web shell may have a client interface program that is used to talk to the Web server (see, for example, China Chopper Web shell client). (Citation: Lee 2013)  Web shells may serve as [Redundant Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1108) or as a persistence mechanism in case an adversary's primary access methods are detected and removed.
        ~PHP-based Web Shell: Adversaries deploy web shells using PHP scripts that can execute system commands and provide file management capabilities
        ~ASP/ASPX Web Shell: Web shells targeting Microsoft IIS servers using ASP or ASPX, often providing GUI interfaces and encrypted communications
        ~JSP Web Shell: Java-based web shells targeting Java application servers like Tomcat or JBoss
        ~CGI-based Web Shell: Web shells implemented using Common Gateway Interface scripts in Perl, Python, or other languages
        ~File Upload Exploitation: Adversaries exploit file upload functionality in web applications to deploy web shells
        ~Framework Plugin Injection: Web shells disguised as legitimate plugins or modules for web frameworks
        ~Encoded/Encrypted Web Shell: Web shells using encoding or encryption to evade detection
        ~Memory-Resident Web Shell: Web shells that operate primarily in memory to avoid disk-based detection
        ~Database-Stored Web Shell: Web shells stored in database fields and executed through SQL injection or database functions
        ~Backdoored Library Web Shell: Web shells embedded within modified versions of legitimate web application libraries
        ~One-Time Password Web Shell: Web shells requiring OTP or time-based tokens for access
        ~Web Service Masquerading: Web shells disguised as legitimate web services or APIs
        ~Reverse Proxy Web Shell: Web shells that act as reverse proxies to tunnel traffic through the compromised server
        ~WebSocket-based Web Shell: Web shells using WebSocket protocols for real-time bidirectional communication
        ~Scheduled Task Web Shell: Web shells that maintain persistence through scheduled HTTP requests
        ~Environmental Keyed Web Shell: Web shells that only activate under specific environmental conditions
        ~Multi-Stage Web Shell: Web shells that use multiple components and stages for full functionality
        ~Domain Fronting Web Shell: Web shells utilizing domain fronting techniques for communication
        ~Server-Side Include Web Shell: Web shells implemented through server-side include functionality
        ~Cross-Platform Web Shell: Web shells designed to operate across multiple web server platforms
        ~Self-Modifying Web Shell: Web shells that can modify their own code to evade detection
        ~Fileless Upload Web Shell: Web shells that can upload additional payloads without writing to disk
        ~Database Command Web Shell: Web shells specifically designed for database command execution and manipulation
        ~Service Worker Web Shell: Web shells that leverage service workers for persistence in modern web applications
        ~Cloud Function Web Shell: Web shells deployed as serverless cloud functions
        ~Container Escape Web Shell: Web shells designed to break out of containerized environments
        ~Load Balancer Aware Web Shell: Web shells that maintain persistence across load-balanced servers
        ~API Gateway Web Shell: Web shells that exploit API gateway functionality
        ~Shared Hosting Web Shell: Web shells designed to operate in shared hosting environments
        ~Web Application Firewall (WAF) Bypass Web Shell: Web shells specifically designed to evade WAF detection
      Component Firmware: Some adversaries may employ sophisticated means to compromise computer components and install malicious firmware that will execute adversary code outside of the operating system and main system firmware or BIOS. This technique may be similar to [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1019) but conducted upon other system components that may not have the same capability or level of integrity checking. Malicious device firmware could provide both a persistent level of access to systems despite potential typical failures to maintain access and hard disk re-images, as well as a way to evade host software-based defenses and integrity checks.
        ~Network Card Firmware: Compromise of network interface card firmware to maintain persistence and intercept network traffic at hardware level
        ~Storage Controller Firmware: Manipulation of storage controller firmware to survive system reimaging and maintain covert storage
        ~GPU Firmware: Modification of graphics card firmware to execute malicious code and potentially leverage GPU processing capabilities
        ~USB Controller Firmware: Compromise of USB controller firmware to maintain persistence and intercept USB communications
        ~Keyboard Controller Firmware: Manipulation of keyboard controller firmware for keylogging at hardware level
        ~Sound Card Firmware: Modification of audio device firmware for covert data exfiltration or surveillance
        ~RAID Controller Firmware: Compromise of RAID controller firmware to maintain persistence across storage changes
        ~Embedded Controller Firmware: Manipulation of laptop/desktop embedded controller firmware for system-level control
      Pre-OS Boot: Adversaries may abuse Pre-OS Boot mechanisms as a way to establish persistence on a system. During the booting process of a computer, firmware and various startup services are loaded before the operating system. These programs control flow of execution before the operating system takes control.(Citation: Wikipedia Booting)  Adversaries may overwrite data in boot drivers or firmware such as BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) and The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) to persist on systems at a layer below the operating system. This can be particularly difficult to detect as malware at this level will not be detected by host software-based defenses.
        System Firmware: Adversaries may modify system firmware to persist on systems.The BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) and The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) or Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI) are examples of system firmware that operate as the software interface between the operating system and hardware of a computer.(Citation: Wikipedia BIOS)(Citation: Wikipedia UEFI)(Citation: About UEFI)  System firmware like BIOS and (U)EFI underly the functionality of a computer and may be modified by an adversary to perform or assist in malicious activity. Capabilities exist to overwrite the system firmware, which may give sophisticated adversaries a means to install malicious firmware updates as a means of persistence on a system that may be difficult to detect.
