        Additional Local or Domain Groups: An adversary may add additional local or domain groups to an adversary-controlled account to maintain persistent access to a system or domain.  On Windows, accounts may use the `net localgroup` and `net group` commands to add existing users to local and domain groups.(Citation: Microsoft Net Localgroup)(Citation: Microsoft Net Group) On Linux, adversaries may use the `usermod` command for the same purpose.(Citation: Linux Usermod)  For example, accounts may be added to the local administrators group on Windows devices to maintain elevated privileges. They may also be added to the Remote Desktop Users group, which allows them to leverage [Remote Desktop Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/001) to log into the endpoints in the future.(Citation: Microsoft RDP Logons) On Linux, accounts may be added to the sudoers group, allowing them to persistently leverage [Sudo and Sudo Caching](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/003) for elevated privileges.   In Windows environments, machine accounts may also be added to domain groups. This allows the local SYSTEM account to gain privileges on the domain.(Citation: RootDSE AD Detection 2022)
          Use "net localgroup administrators [username] /add" to add user to local admin group on Windows
          Use "net group 'Domain Admins' [username] /add /domain" to add user to domain admin group
          Execute "usermod -aG sudo [username]" to add user to sudo group on Linux
          Modify /etc/group file directly to add user to privileged groups on Linux
          Use PowerShell Add-LocalGroupMember cmdlet to add users to local groups
          Use Active Directory Administrative Center GUI to add users to domain groups
          Leverage ADSI Edit to modify group memberships directly in AD
          Use "net localgroup 'Remote Desktop Users' [username] /add" for RDP access
          Modify Local Security Policy (secpol.msc) to add users to privileged groups
          Use "dsadd group" command to create and populate new domain groups
          Use Group Policy Management Console to modify Security Groups
          Execute "dsmember" command for bulk group membership modifications
          Create PowerShell script to automate group additions across multiple systems
          Use "wmic useraccount" commands to modify local group memberships
          Leverage Microsoft Management Console (MMC) Active Directory Users and Computers snap-in
          Use Azure AD PowerShell to modify cloud group memberships
          Employ Sysinternals PsTools (psexec) for remote group modifications
          Utilize "net user /domain" for domain user group modifications
          Create batch scripts with multiple group addition commands
          Use LDAP modifications through ADSIEdit for direct group attribute changes
          Use "gpasswd" command for group administration on Linux systems
          Employ LDAP Admin tools for cross-platform group modifications
          Use Microsoft System Center Configuration Manager for enterprise-wide group management
          Modify Kerberos authentication groups through kadmin utility
          Execute Docker group modifications for container persistence
          Use VMware vSphere permissions to modify ESXi host group memberships
          Leverage Ansible playbooks for automated group modifications across systems
          Use OpenLDAP command-line tools for directory service group modifications
          Employ Red Hat Identity Management (IdM) for centralized group administration
          Use "dseditgroup" command on macOS systems for group modifications
          Use "cluster.exe" to modify Windows Server Failover Cluster groups
          Modify SAM database directly using Registry Editor for offline group changes
          Use "ntdsutil" for directory service maintenance operations affecting groups
          Employ Citrix Director for managing groups in virtual desktop environments
          Use Oracle Enterprise Manager for database role group modifications
          Modify groups through Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE)
          Use "dsquery" and "dsmod" for fine-grained AD group operations
          Employ Okta Admin Console for cloud identity group management
          Use AWS IAM console/CLI for AWS directory service group modifications
          Leverage "groupmod" command for system group modifications in Unix
          Use RACF commands for mainframe access control group modifications
          Modify Cisco ISE device administration groups through CLI/GUI
          Use "setfacl" for modifying Access Control Lists on Unix-like systems
          Employ RSA SecurID Authentication Manager for token group management
          Modify groups in backup software like Veeam for persistent access
          Use IBM Security Access Manager for enterprise group modifications
          Modify Network Policy Server (NPS) groups for RADIUS authentication
          Use SAP NetWeaver group management tools for application persistence
          Employ FortiAuthenticator for network access group modifications
          Use BMC Control-M for scheduling system group modifications
      Time Providers: The Windows Time service (W32Time) enables time synchronization across and within domains. (Citation: Microsoft W32Time Feb 2018) W32Time time providers are responsible for retrieving time stamps from hardware/network resources and outputting these values to other network clients. (Citation: Microsoft TimeProvider)  Time providers are implemented as dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) that are registered in the subkeys of  <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\TimeProviders\</code>. (Citation: Microsoft TimeProvider) The time provider manager, directed by the service control manager, loads and starts time providers listed and enabled under this key at system startup and/or whenever parameters are changed. (Citation: Microsoft TimeProvider)  Adversaries may abuse this architecture to establish Persistence, specifically by registering and enabling a malicious DLL as a time provider. Administrator privileges are required for time provider registration, though execution will run in context of the Local Service account. (Citation: Github W32Time Oct 2017)
        ~Malicious Time Provider DLL Registration: Adversaries may register a malicious DLL as a time provider by adding entries to the Windows Registry TimeProviders key
        ~Time Provider Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may modify existing time provider parameters to load malicious code during time synchronization
        ~Time Provider Service Hijacking: Adversaries may replace legitimate time provider DLLs with malicious ones to maintain persistence
        ~Time Synchronization Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may exploit the Network Time Protocol (NTP) communication channel for command and control
        ~Time Provider Chain Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code into the time provider loading chain
        ~Time Provider Privilege Escalation: Adversaries may exploit the Local Service context of time providers to gain elevated privileges
        ~Custom Time Provider Interface: Adversaries may implement custom time provider interfaces to evade detection
        ~Time Provider Recovery Abuse: Adversaries may manipulate time provider backup and recovery mechanisms to maintain persistence
        ~Time Provider State Manipulation: Adversaries may alter system state through malicious time providers to affect dependent services
      Authentication Package: Windows Authentication Package DLLs are loaded by the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. They provide support for multiple logon processes and multiple security protocols to the operating system. (Citation: MSDN Authentication Packages)  Adversaries can use the autostart mechanism provided by LSA Authentication Packages for persistence by placing a reference to a binary in the Windows Registry location <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\</code> with the key value of <code>"Authentication Packages"=<target binary></code>. The binary will then be executed by the system when the authentication packages are loaded.
        ~Custom Authentication Package Installation: Deploy custom-made authentication package DLLs to handle specific authentication scenarios while maintaining persistence
        ~LSA Package Replacement: Replace legitimate authentication packages with malicious ones while preserving original functionality
        ~Authentication Package Injection: Inject malicious code into existing authentication packages
        ~Registry Key Manipulation: Modify registry entries to reference malicious binaries as authentication packages
        ~Security Support Provider Integration: Combine authentication package abuse with SSP manipulation
        ~Package Load Order Manipulation: Alter the order of authentication package loading to ensure malicious package execution
        ~Credential Provider Abuse: Leverage authentication package integration with credential providers
        ~System Process Impersonation: Use authentication packages to impersonate system processes
        ~Authentication Cache Manipulation: Modify authentication caches through malicious packages
        ~Package Parameter Tampering: Manipulate parameters passed to authentication packages
        ~Authentication Package Backup Abuse: Exploit backup/restore mechanisms to maintain persistence through system updates
        ~Debug Feature Exploitation: Abuse authentication package debug features for code execution
        ~Network Authentication Integration: Leverage network authentication components to maintain package persistence
        ~Package Verification Bypass: Circumvent authentication package integrity checks
        ~Package Dependencies Manipulation: Modify dependent libraries or components to maintain persistence
      New Service: When operating systems boot up, they can start programs or applications called services that perform background system functions. (Citation: TechNet Services) A service's configuration information, including the file path to the service's executable, is stored in the Windows Registry.   Adversaries may install a new service that can be configured to execute at startup by using utilities to interact with services or by directly modifying the Registry. The service name may be disguised by using a name from a related operating system or benign software with [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036). Services may be created with administrator privileges but are executed under SYSTEM privileges, so an adversary may also use a service to escalate privileges from administrator to SYSTEM. Adversaries may also directly start services through [Service Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1035).
        ~System Utilities: Adversaries may use built-in system utilities like sc.exe, New-Service cmdlet, or services.msc to create and configure new services
        ~Registry Manipulation: Direct modification of registry keys under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services to create or modify service entries
        ~Third-party Service Managers: Use of third-party tools or software designed for service management to create and configure services
        ~Service Configuration Tampering: Modification of existing service configurations to point to malicious executables
        ~Driver Services: Installation of malicious drivers as services for persistence and privileged execution
        ~Temporary Service Registration: Creating temporary services that are deleted after execution but recreated at system startup
        ~Service DLL Hijacking: Creating services that load malicious DLLs through service DLL search order hijacking
        ~Interactive Service Installation: Creating services that can interact with the desktop for user session access
        ~Recovery Action Services: Configuring service recovery actions to execute malicious code when service failures occur
        ~Clustered Service Installation: Installing services on Windows cluster nodes for distributed persistence
        ~Delayed Start Services: Creating services with delayed start parameters to avoid detection during initial system startup
        ~Service Trigger Installation: Creating services that start based on specific system events or triggers
        ~Virtual Service Installation: Creating services within virtual environments or containers
        ~Service Dependencies Manipulation: Creating services with specifically crafted dependency chains
        ~Kernel Mode Services: Installing services that operate in kernel mode for enhanced privileges
        ~Group Policy Service Deployment: Using Group Policy to deploy and configure services across domains
        ~Service Account Manipulation: Creating services with specifically crafted service accounts
        ~Distributed Service Installation: Creating coordinated services across multiple systems
      Bootkit: A bootkit is a malware variant that modifies the boot sectors of a hard drive, including the Master Boot Record (MBR) and Volume Boot Record (VBR). (Citation: MTrends 2016)  Adversaries may use bootkits to persist on systems at a layer below the operating system, which may make it difficult to perform full remediation unless an organization suspects one was used and can act accordingly.  ### Master Boot Record The MBR is the section of disk that is first loaded after completing hardware initialization by the BIOS. It is the location of the boot loader. An adversary who has raw access to the boot drive may overwrite this area, diverting execution during startup from the normal boot loader to adversary code. (Citation: Lau 2011)  ### Volume Boot Record The MBR passes control of the boot process to the VBR. Similar to the case of MBR, an adversary who has raw access to the boot drive may overwrite the VBR to divert execution during startup to adversary code.
        ~MBR Modification: Directly modify Master Boot Record code to load malicious code before OS boot
        ~VBR Infection: Inject malicious code into Volume Boot Record to intercept boot process
        ~UEFI Bootkit: Target UEFI firmware and boot process instead of traditional BIOS-based approach
        ~Multi-Stage Bootkit: Use multiple stages of boot process modification for enhanced stealth
        ~Firmware-Level Bootkit: Implement persistence at firmware level alongside boot sector modifications
        ~Bootloader Hijacking: Replace or modify legitimate bootloader with malicious version
        ~Hidden Partition Bootkit: Create hidden partition to store malicious boot code
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Implement techniques to circumvent Secure Boot protections
        ~Boot Configuration Data (BCD) Modification: Alter boot configuration to load malicious components
        ~Hardware-Assisted Bootkit: Leverage hardware features for more sophisticated boot process manipulation
        ~TPM-Aware Bootkit: Implement bootkit that can operate despite TPM measurements
        ~Hybrid Bootkit: Combine multiple boot sector modifications for redundant persistence
        ~Boot Guard Evasion: Specifically target Intel Boot Guard protection mechanisms
        ~Legacy Option ROM Bootkit: Target Option ROM loading process for persistence
        ~ESP Partition Bootkit: Target EFI System Partition for bootkit installation
        ~Shadow MBR: Create shadow copy of MBR for stealth and backup persistence
        ~Boot Script Modification: Modify EFI boot scripts to maintain persistence
        ~Bootkit Rootkit Combination: Integrate bootkit with rootkit capabilities
      Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription: Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) can be used to install event filters, providers, consumers, and bindings that execute code when a defined event occurs. Adversaries may use the capabilities of WMI to subscribe to an event and execute arbitrary code when that event occurs, providing persistence on a system. Adversaries may attempt to evade detection of this technique by compiling WMI scripts into Windows Management Object (MOF) files (.mof extension). (Citation: Dell WMI Persistence) Examples of events that may be subscribed to are the wall clock time or the computer's uptime. (Citation: Kazanciyan 2014) Several threat groups have reportedly used this technique to maintain persistence. (Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2015)
        ~Timer-Based Event Filter: Using WMI to create event filters based on system time or intervals to trigger malicious code execution
        ~System State Event Filter: Creating event filters that monitor system state changes like startup, shutdown, or user logon
        ~Process Monitoring Event Filter: Establishing filters to monitor specific process creation or termination events
        ~Hardware Event Filter: Setting up filters to monitor hardware-related events like USB insertions or network adapter changes
        ~Registry Event Filter: Creating filters to monitor registry modifications or specific registry key access
        ~File System Event Filter: Implementing filters to monitor file system changes or specific file access patterns
        ~Service State Event Filter: Establishing filters to monitor service state changes like starts, stops, or modifications
        ~Network Event Filter: Creating filters to monitor network-related events like connection establishments or DNS queries
        ~Security Event Filter: Setting up filters to monitor security-related events like authentication attempts or privilege changes
        ~Custom Query Event Filter: Implementing complex custom WQL queries for specific event monitoring scenarios
        ~ActiveScript Event Consumer: Using script-based event consumers to execute VBScript or JScript code
        ~CommandLine Event Consumer: Utilizing command-line consumers to execute system commands or scripts
        ~Permanent Event Registration: Creating permanent WMI event subscriptions that persist across system restarts
        ~Temporary Event Registration: Implementing temporary event subscriptions that exist only during runtime
        ~Distributed WMI Subscription: Creating WMI subscriptions that work across network boundaries
      Emond: Adversaries may use Event Monitor Daemon (emond) to establish persistence by scheduling malicious commands to run on predictable event triggers. Emond is a [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1160) that accepts events from various services, runs them through a simple rules engine, and takes action. The emond binary at <code>/sbin/emond</code> will load any rules from the <code>/etc/emond.d/rules/</code> directory and take action once an explicitly defined event takes place. The rule files are in the plist format and define the name, event type, and action to take. Some examples of event types include system startup and user authentication. Examples of actions are to run a system command or send an email. The emond service will not launch if there is no file present in the QueueDirectories path <code>/private/var/db/emondClients</code>, specified in the [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1160) configuration file at<code>/System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.emond.plist</code>.(Citation: xorrior emond Jan 2018)(Citation: magnusviri emond Apr 2016)(Citation: sentinelone macos persist Jun 2019)  Adversaries may abuse this service by writing a rule to execute commands when a defined event occurs, such as system start up or user authentication.(Citation: xorrior emond Jan 2018)(Citation: magnusviri emond Apr 2016)(Citation: sentinelone macos persist Jun 2019) Adversaries may also be able to escalate privileges from administrator to root as the emond service is executed with root privileges by the [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1160) service.
        ~System Startup Rules: Creating emond rules that trigger on system boot to establish persistence through system startup events
        ~User Authentication Rules: Implementing rules that execute malicious commands when user authentication events occur
        ~Custom Event Rules: Creating rules for specific custom events to trigger malicious actions
        ~Privilege Escalation via Rule Configuration: Exploiting emond's root privileges by crafting rules that elevate permissions
        ~Email Action Rules: Establishing persistence through rules that send emails containing command output or system information
        ~Queue Directory Manipulation: Manipulating the emondClients directory to ensure service execution
        ~Rule File Masquerading: Disguising malicious rules as legitimate system monitoring configurations
        ~Launch Daemon Configuration Modification: Altering the emond Launch Daemon configuration for persistent access
      Change Default File Association: When a file is opened, the default program used to open the file (also called the file association or handler) is checked. File association selections are stored in the Windows Registry and can be edited by users, administrators, or programs that have Registry access (Citation: Microsoft Change Default Programs) (Citation: Microsoft File Handlers) or by administrators using the built-in assoc utility. (Citation: Microsoft Assoc Oct 2017) Applications can modify the file association for a given file extension to call an arbitrary program when a file with the given extension is opened.  System file associations are listed under <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\.[extension]</code>, for example <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\.txt</code>. The entries point to a handler for that extension located at <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\[handler]</code>. The various commands are then listed as subkeys underneath the shell key at <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\[handler]\shell\[action]\command</code>. For example: * <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\txtfile\shell\open\command</code> * <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\txtfile\shell\print\command</code> * <code>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\txtfile\shell\printto\command</code>  The values of the keys listed are commands that are executed when the handler opens the file extension. Adversaries can modify these values to continually execute arbitrary commands. (Citation: TrendMicro TROJ-FAKEAV OCT 2012)
        ~User Level Association Changes: Modifying file associations in HKEY_CURRENT_USER to maintain persistence while limiting visibility and requiring less privilege
        ~System Level Association Changes: Modifying file associations in HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE to affect all users and maintain broader persistence
        ~Common Extension Hijacking: Targeting commonly used file extensions (.txt, .pdf, .doc) to increase likelihood of execution
        ~URL Protocol Handler Modification: Changing handlers for URL protocols (http://, ftp://) to intercept web-related actions
        ~Script Extension Association: Modifying associations for script files (.js, .vbs, .ps1) to execute malicious interpreters
        ~Backup File Association: Targeting backup file extensions (.bak, .old) to maintain persistence through system recovery processes
        ~Group Policy Association Override: Using Group Policy to enforce malicious file associations across domain systems
        ~Application-Specific Handler Hijacking: Modifying associations for specific applications' custom file types
        ~Command-Line Tool Abuse: Exploiting built-in tools like 'assoc' and 'ftype' for file association changes
        ~Default Program Feature Abuse: Leveraging Windows Default Programs interface for stealthy association changes
      Create or Modify System Process: Adversaries may create or modify system-level processes to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. When operating systems boot up, they can start processes that perform background system functions. On Windows and Linux, these system processes are referred to as services.(Citation: TechNet Services) On macOS, launchd processes known as [Launch Daemon](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/004) and [Launch Agent](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/001) are run to finish system initialization and load user specific parameters.(Citation: AppleDocs Launch Agent Daemons)   Adversaries may install new services, daemons, or agents that can be configured to execute at startup or a repeatable interval in order to establish persistence. Similarly, adversaries may modify existing services, daemons, or agents to achieve the same effect.    Services, daemons, or agents may be created with administrator privileges but executed under root/SYSTEM privileges. Adversaries may leverage this functionality to create or modify system processes in order to escalate privileges.(Citation: OSX Malware Detection)
        Systemd Service: Adversaries may create or modify systemd services to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. Systemd is a system and service manager commonly used for managing background daemon processes (also known as services) and other system resources.(Citation: Linux man-pages: systemd January 2014) Systemd is the default initialization (init) system on many Linux distributions replacing legacy init systems, including SysVinit and Upstart, while remaining backwards compatible.    Systemd utilizes unit configuration files with the `.service` file extension to encode information about a service's process. By default, system level unit files are stored in the `/systemd/system` directory of the root owned directories (`/`). User level unit files are stored in the `/systemd/user` directories of the user owned directories (`$HOME`).(Citation: lambert systemd 2022)   Inside the `.service` unit files, the following directives are used to execute commands:(Citation: freedesktop systemd.service)    * `ExecStart`, `ExecStartPre`, and `ExecStartPost` directives execute when a service is started manually by `systemctl` or on system start if the service is set to automatically start. * `ExecReload` directive executes when a service restarts.  * `ExecStop`, `ExecStopPre`, and `ExecStopPost` directives execute when a service is stopped.    Adversaries have created new service files, altered the commands a `.service` file’s directive executes, and modified the user directive a `.service` file executes as, which could result in privilege escalation. Adversaries may also place symbolic links in these directories, enabling systemd to find these payloads regardless of where they reside on the filesystem.(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019)(Citation: airwalk backdoor unix systems)(Citation: Rapid7 Service Persistence 22JUNE2016)   The `.service` file’s User directive can be used to run service as a specific user, which could result in privilege escalation based on specific user/group permissions.   Systemd services can be created via systemd generators, which support the dynamic generation of unit files. Systemd generators are small executables that run during boot or configuration reloads to dynamically create or modify systemd unit files by converting non-native configurations into services, symlinks, or drop-ins (i.e., [Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037)).(Citation: Elastic Security Labs Linux Persistence 2024)(Citation: Pepe Berba Systemd 2022)
          Create malicious service file in /etc/systemd/system/ with ExecStart pointing to backdoor script
          Modify existing service file's ExecStart directive to include additional malicious commands
          Create symbolic link in /etc/systemd/system/ pointing to malicious service file in hidden location
          Use systemctl enable to configure malicious service for automatic startup
          Create systemd generator script in /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/ to dynamically generate malicious unit files
          Modify service file's User directive to execute as privileged user
          Insert malicious commands in ExecStartPre or ExecStartPost for stealthy execution
          Create user-level service in ~/.config/systemd/user/ for persistence without root privileges
          Modify ExecReload directive to execute malicious payload during service restart
          Use systemd-run command to create transient service unit for temporary persistence
          Modify service dependencies using Requires/Wants directives to ensure malicious service starts with critical services
          Create template service unit (using @.service) to dynamically spawn multiple instances of malicious service
          Use systemd path units to trigger malicious service based on filesystem changes
          Modify service file permissions to prevent detection/modification
          Abuse systemd timer units to schedule periodic execution of malicious service
          Create drop-in configuration files (.conf) in /etc/systemd/system/service.d/ to modify existing services
          Use systemd socket activation to trigger malicious service on network events
          Modify service environment variables through Environment directive for covert configuration
          Implement service state notification through Type=notify for controlled execution
          Create systemd target unit to group and manage multiple malicious services
          Configure Restart=always in service unit to ensure persistence after crashes or kills
          Manipulate service unit's OnFailure directive to trigger backup malicious service
          Use systemd escape sequences in service file to bypass command sanitization
          Modify service's StandardOutput/StandardError to hide malicious activity
          Abuse systemd's Linux Security Module (LSM) integration to bypass security controls
          Configure service sandboxing options (e.g., PrivateNetwork=no) to maintain network access
          Use systemd's DynamicUser feature to create transient users for service execution
          Implement condition checks (ConditionPathExists=) to control service execution
          Abuse systemd's cgroup integration for resource hiding
          Configure KillMode directive to ensure child processes survive service termination
          Use systemd-sysv-generator to create systemd services from legacy init scripts for evasion
          Modify service's ReadWritePaths directive to maintain access to specific filesystem locations
          Configure service's ProtectSystem directive to prevent service removal/modification
          Use systemd's ImportCredential feature to store and access sensitive data
          Implement service file verification bypass through dynamic unit file generation
          Configure service's AssertPathExists to control execution based on attacker's files
          Use systemd's delegation features to maintain control over service management
          Implement service file atomicity bypass through partial writes
          Configure service's RemainAfterExit to maintain persistence after process completion
          Use systemd's transient unit properties to modify service behavior at runtime
        Windows Service: Adversaries may create or modify Windows services to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. When Windows boots up, it starts programs or applications called services that perform background system functions.(Citation: TechNet Services) Windows service configuration information, including the file path to the service's executable or recovery programs/commands, is stored in the Windows Registry.  Adversaries may install a new service or modify an existing service to execute at startup in order to persist on a system. Service configurations can be set or modified using system utilities (such as sc.exe), by directly modifying the Registry, or by interacting directly with the Windows API.   Adversaries may also use services to install and execute malicious drivers. For example, after dropping a driver file (ex: `.sys`) to disk, the payload can be loaded and registered via [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions such as `CreateServiceW()` (or manually via functions such as `ZwLoadDriver()` and `ZwSetValueKey()`), by creating the required service Registry values (i.e. [Modify Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112)), or by using command-line utilities such as `PnPUtil.exe`.(Citation: Symantec W.32 Stuxnet Dossier)(Citation: Crowdstrike DriveSlayer February 2022)(Citation: Unit42 AcidBox June 2020) Adversaries may leverage these drivers as [Rootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1014)s to hide the presence of malicious activity on a system. Adversaries may also load a signed yet vulnerable driver onto a compromised machine (known as "Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver" (BYOVD)) as part of [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068).(Citation: ESET InvisiMole June 2020)(Citation: Unit42 AcidBox June 2020)  Services may be created with administrator privileges but are executed under SYSTEM privileges, so an adversary may also use a service to escalate privileges. Adversaries may also directly start services through [Service Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/002).  To make detection analysis more challenging, malicious services may also incorporate [Masquerade Task or Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/004) (ex: using a service and/or payload name related to a legitimate OS or benign software component). Adversaries may also create ‘hidden’ services (i.e., [Hide Artifacts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564)), for example by using the `sc sdset` command to set service permissions via the Service Descriptor Definition Language (SDDL). This may hide a Windows service from the view of standard service enumeration methods such as `Get-Service`, `sc query`, and `services.exe`.(Citation: SANS 1)(Citation: SANS 2)
          Use sc.exe to create a new service with "sc create [name] binPath= [path]" and set it to auto-start
          Modify existing service binary paths in Registry under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services
          Use CreateServiceW() API call to programmatically create persistent services
          Install malicious driver using PnPUtil.exe /add-driver [path] followed by sc start
          Use sc sdset to modify service SDDL permissions to hide from standard enumeration tools
          Create service with name mimicking legitimate Windows services (e.g., "Windows Update Service")
          Modify service recovery actions to execute malicious payload on service failure
          Use PowerShell New-Service cmdlet to create persistent services
          Register driver through direct Registry manipulation of service parameters
          Modify existing service DLL search order to load malicious DLL
          Use ZwLoadDriver() native API to manually load malicious drivers
          Modify service trigger settings to execute on system events
          Create service with dependencies on legitimate services to ensure execution order
          Use sc config to modify existing service security descriptors
          Create service with custom recovery programs instead of binary paths
          Manipulate service SID settings for privilege escalation
          Use Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) to create/modify services
          Exploit service application directory permissions for DLL planting
          Create service with custom V2 service trigger providers
          Register driver through DeviceTree manipulation
          Use Group Policy to deploy services across domain
          Modify service ImagePath to include command-line arguments
          Create nested services using parent-child relationship
          Exploit shared service processes for DLL injection
          Use service failure actions to create recursive recovery chains
          Modify service account privileges through Local Security Policy
          Create services with custom COM activation settings
          Use remote registry to modify service configurations
          Exploit service application protocol handlers
          Create service with custom ETW providers for monitoring evasion
          Use service.exe to clone legitimate service configurations
          Exploit service snapshot and restore functionality
          Create virtual service instances through isolation
          Modify service registry ACLs to prevent removal
          Use service shared sections for inter-process communication
          Exploit service host grouping policies
          Create service with custom performance counters
          Use service application compartments for isolation
          Modify service hang recovery settings
          Exploit service dependency evaluation order
        Container Service: Adversaries may create or modify container or container cluster management tools that run as daemons, agents, or services on individual hosts. These include software for creating and managing individual containers, such as Docker and Podman, as well as container cluster node-level agents such as kubelet. By modifying these services, an adversary may be able to achieve persistence or escalate their privileges on a host.  For example, by using the `docker run` or `podman run` command with the `restart=always` directive, a container can be configured to persistently restart on the host.(Citation: AquaSec TeamTNT 2023) A user with access to the (rootful) docker command may also be able to escalate their privileges on the host.(Citation: GTFOBins Docker)  In Kubernetes environments, DaemonSets allow an adversary to persistently [Deploy Container](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1610)s on all nodes, including ones added later to the cluster.(Citation: Aquasec Kubernetes Attack 2023)(Citation: Kubernetes DaemonSet) Pods can also be deployed to specific nodes using the `nodeSelector` or `nodeName` fields in the pod spec.(Citation: Kubernetes Assigning Pods to Nodes)(Citation: AppSecco Kubernetes Namespace Breakout 2020)  Note that containers can also be configured to run as [Systemd Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/002)s.(Citation: Podman Systemd)(Citation: Docker Systemd)
          Configure Docker container with "restart=always" flag to ensure persistent execution across host reboots
          Create Kubernetes DaemonSet to deploy malicious containers across all cluster nodes
          Use nodeSelector/nodeName in pod specifications to target specific nodes for container deployment
          Modify kubelet configuration to maintain persistent access to node-level container management
          Exploit rootful Docker socket access to escalate privileges on host system
          Configure container as systemd service unit for persistence
          Modify existing container runtime configurations to inject malicious commands
          Deploy privileged containers with host system mounts for persistent access
          Manipulate container networking configurations for maintaining backdoor access
          Modify container image registry configurations to pull compromised images
          Modify Docker Swarm service configurations for persistent container deployment
          Create persistent volume mounts in container definitions to maintain data across restarts
          Manipulate container checkpoint/restore functionality for persistence
