      Hidden Files and Directories: To prevent normal users from accidentally changing special files on a system, most operating systems have the concept of a ‘hidden’ file. These files don’t show up when a user browses the file system with a GUI or when using normal commands on the command line. Users must explicitly ask to show the hidden files either via a series of Graphical User Interface (GUI) prompts or with command line switches (<code>dir /a</code> for Windows and <code>ls –a</code> for Linux and macOS).  Adversaries can use this to their advantage to hide files and folders anywhere on the system for persistence and evading a typical user or system analysis that does not incorporate investigation of hidden files.  ### Windows  Users can mark specific files as hidden by using the attrib.exe binary. Simply do <code>attrib +h filename</code> to mark a file or folder as hidden. Similarly, the “+s” marks a file as a system file and the “+r” flag marks the file as read only. Like most windows binaries, the attrib.exe binary provides the ability to apply these changes recursively “/S”.  ### Linux/Mac  Users can mark specific files as hidden simply by putting a “.” as the first character in the file or folder name  (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan) (Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware). Files and folder that start with a period, ‘.’, are by default hidden from being viewed in the Finder application and standard command-line utilities like “ls”. Users must specifically change settings to have these files viewable. For command line usages, there is typically a flag to see all files (including hidden ones). To view these files in the Finder Application, the following command must be executed: <code>defaults write com.apple.finder AppleShowAllFiles YES</code>, and then relaunch the Finder Application.  ### Mac  Files on macOS can be marked with the UF_HIDDEN flag which prevents them from being seen in Finder.app, but still allows them to be seen in Terminal.app (Citation: WireLurker). Many applications create these hidden files and folders to store information so that it doesn’t clutter up the user’s workspace. For example, SSH utilities create a .ssh folder that’s hidden and contains the user’s known hosts and keys.
        ~Hidden System Files - Using system file attributes (like +s in Windows) to make files appear as protected system files
        ~Hidden User Files - Using basic hidden file attributes (like +h in Windows or dot prefix in Unix)
        ~Alternate File System Streams - Hiding data in NTFS alternate data streams
        ~Protected Directories - Placing files in system-protected directories that require elevated privileges to access
        ~Hidden File Extensions - Manipulating file extensions to hide true file types
        ~Special Filesystem Locations - Using special folders like $Recycle.bin or System Volume Information
        ~Hidden Symbolic Links - Creating hidden symbolic links to redirect access
        ~Hidden Network Shares - Using hidden shares (ending with $) to store files
        ~Extended Attributes - Using extended file attributes to hide files from normal listings
        ~Hidden Mount Points - Using hidden mount points to conceal directories
        ~Encrypted Containers - Hiding files within encrypted containers that appear as regular files
        ~Archive File Manipulation - Hiding files within specially crafted archive files
        ~Resource Fork Storage - Using macOS resource forks to hide data
        ~Hidden Desktop.ini - Manipulating Windows desktop.ini files to hide content
        ~Hidden Junction Points - Using NTFS junction points to redirect and hide content
        ~Hidden Registry Storage - Storing file content within hidden registry values
        ~Hidden Application Data - Hiding within application-specific storage locations
        ~Steganographic Storage - Hiding files within other legitimate files
        ~Timestomping Hidden Files - Manipulating timestamps to hide files among legitimate system files
        ~Hidden Virtual File Systems - Creating hidden virtual file systems within files
        ~Hidden Partition Storage - Hiding files in unused disk partitions or slack space
        ~Memory-Mapped Persistence - Using memory-mapped files for hiding that persist across reboots
        ~Hidden Bootloader Storage - Hiding files in bootloader-accessible areas
      AppInit DLLs: Dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) that are specified in the AppInit_DLLs value in the Registry keys <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Windows</code> or <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Wow6432Node\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Windows</code> are loaded by user32.dll into every process that loads user32.dll. In practice this is nearly every program, since user32.dll is a very common library. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), these values can be abused to obtain persistence and privilege escalation by causing a malicious DLL to be loaded and run in the context of separate processes on the computer. (Citation: AppInit Registry)  The AppInit DLL functionality is disabled in Windows 8 and later versions when secure boot is enabled. (Citation: AppInit Secure Boot)
        ~Registry Key Modification: Direct modification of AppInit_DLLs registry values to specify malicious DLL paths
        ~DLL Path Manipulation: Using relative or environment variable paths in AppInit_DLLs to load DLLs from alternate locations
        ~Load Order Manipulation: Controlling the order of DLL loading through registry value sequencing
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Methods to bypass the Windows 8+ secure boot restrictions on AppInit DLLs
        ~Multiple DLL Loading: Loading multiple DLLs through semicolon-separated paths in AppInit_DLLs
        ~Registry Value Hiding: Using binary registry values or encoding to obscure malicious DLL paths
        ~DLL Name Masquerading: Using names that mimic legitimate system DLLs to avoid detection
        ~Cross-Architecture Loading: Specifically targeting 32-bit vs 64-bit processes through respective registry keys
        ~LoadAppInit_DLLs Flag Manipulation: Modifying the LoadAppInit_DLLs registry value to ensure DLL loading
        ~System Service Loading: Leveraging system services that load user32.dll to ensure DLL persistence
      Accessibility Features: Windows contains accessibility features that may be launched with a key combination before a user has logged in (for example, when the user is on the Windows logon screen). An adversary can modify the way these programs are launched to get a command prompt or backdoor without logging in to the system.  Two common accessibility programs are <code>C:\Windows\System32\sethc.exe</code>, launched when the shift key is pressed five times and <code>C:\Windows\System32\utilman.exe</code>, launched when the Windows + U key combination is pressed. The sethc.exe program is often referred to as "sticky keys", and has been used by adversaries for unauthenticated access through a remote desktop login screen. (Citation: FireEye Hikit Rootkit)  Depending on the version of Windows, an adversary may take advantage of these features in different ways because of code integrity enhancements. In newer versions of Windows, the replaced binary needs to be digitally signed for x64 systems, the binary must reside in <code>%systemdir%\</code>, and it must be protected by Windows File or Resource Protection (WFP/WRP). (Citation: DEFCON2016 Sticky Keys) The debugger method was likely discovered as a potential workaround because it does not require the corresponding accessibility feature binary to be replaced. Examples for both methods:  For simple binary replacement on Windows XP and later as well as and Windows Server 2003/R2 and later, for example, the program (e.g., <code>C:\Windows\System32\utilman.exe</code>) may be replaced with "cmd.exe" (or another program that provides backdoor access). Subsequently, pressing the appropriate key combination at the login screen while sitting at the keyboard or when connected over [Remote Desktop Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1076) will cause the replaced file to be executed with SYSTEM privileges. (Citation: Tilbury 2014)  For the debugger method on Windows Vista and later as well as Windows Server 2008 and later, for example, a Registry key may be modified that configures "cmd.exe," or another program that provides backdoor access, as a "debugger" for the accessibility program (e.g., "utilman.exe"). After the Registry is modified, pressing the appropriate key combination at the login screen while at the keyboard or when connected with RDP will cause the "debugger" program to be executed with SYSTEM privileges. (Citation: Tilbury 2014)  Other accessibility features exist that may also be leveraged in a similar fashion: (Citation: DEFCON2016 Sticky Keys)  * On-Screen Keyboard: <code>C:\Windows\System32\osk.exe</code> * Magnifier: <code>C:\Windows\System32\Magnify.exe</code> * Narrator: <code>C:\Windows\System32\Narrator.exe</code> * Display Switcher: <code>C:\Windows\System32\DisplaySwitch.exe</code> * App Switcher: <code>C:\Windows\System32\AtBroker.exe</code>
        ~Binary Replacement: Replace accessibility feature executables with malicious programs to gain SYSTEM privileges when triggered at login screen
        ~Debugger Registry Modification: Modify registry to set malicious program as debugger for accessibility features
        ~Sticky Keys Exploitation: Target sethc.exe (Sticky Keys) specifically for persistence through login screen
        ~Utility Manager Exploitation: Target utilman.exe (Utility Manager) specifically for persistence
        ~On-Screen Keyboard Exploitation: Target osk.exe for persistence through login screen
        ~Magnifier Exploitation: Target Magnify.exe for persistence through login screen
        ~Narrator Exploitation: Target Narrator.exe for persistence through login screen
        ~Display Switcher Exploitation: Target DisplaySwitch.exe for persistence
        ~App Switcher Exploitation: Target AtBroker.exe for persistence
        ~Security Control Bypass: Techniques to bypass Windows File/Resource Protection and digital signature requirements for accessibility binaries
        ~Remote Trigger Configuration: Modify accessibility features to respond to remote triggers rather than just local key combinations
        ~Update Resilient Persistence: Methods to maintain accessibility feature persistence across Windows updates
        ~Alternative Trigger Mechanism: Configure custom key combinations or events to trigger compromised accessibility features
      LSASS Driver: The Windows security subsystem is a set of components that manage and enforce the security policy for a computer or domain. The Local Security Authority (LSA) is the main component responsible for local security policy and user authentication. The LSA includes multiple dynamic link libraries (DLLs) associated with various other security functions, all of which run in the context of the LSA Subsystem Service (LSASS) lsass.exe process. (Citation: Microsoft Security Subsystem)  Adversaries may target lsass.exe drivers to obtain execution and/or persistence. By either replacing or adding illegitimate drivers (e.g., [DLL Side-Loading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1073) or [DLL Search Order Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1038)), an adversary can achieve arbitrary code execution triggered by continuous LSA operations.
        ~LSA Authentication Package Installation: Installing malicious authentication packages that get loaded by LSASS during system startup
        ~Security Support Provider Injection: Adding malicious SSP DLLs that get loaded by LSASS for credential handling
        ~Driver Replacement: Replacing legitimate LSASS drivers with malicious versions while maintaining expected functionality
        ~Driver Path Manipulation: Modifying search paths to load malicious drivers before legitimate ones
        ~Custom Driver Development: Creating custom drivers that interface with LSASS legitimately while containing hidden functionality
        ~Driver Signing Bypass: Exploiting driver signing vulnerabilities to load unsigned malicious drivers into LSASS
        ~Memory-Mapped Driver Injection: Loading malicious driver code directly into LSASS memory space
        ~Legitimate Driver Abuse: Exploiting vulnerabilities in legitimate LSASS drivers to achieve persistence
        ~Boot-Time Driver Loading: Manipulating boot configuration to load malicious drivers during early boot process
        ~Driver Stack Manipulation: Inserting malicious drivers into the LSASS driver stack to intercept operations
      System Firmware: The BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) and The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) or Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI) are examples of system firmware that operate as the software interface between the operating system and hardware of a computer. (Citation: Wikipedia BIOS) (Citation: Wikipedia UEFI) (Citation: About UEFI)  System firmware like BIOS and (U)EFI underly the functionality of a computer and may be modified by an adversary to perform or assist in malicious activity. Capabilities exist to overwrite the system firmware, which may give sophisticated adversaries a means to install malicious firmware updates as a means of persistence on a system that may be difficult to detect.
        ~BIOS Modification: Adversaries may modify BIOS firmware to maintain persistence by injecting malicious code or modifying existing BIOS routines
        ~UEFI Boot Manager Manipulation: Attackers may alter UEFI boot manager entries or add malicious boot applications
        ~Option ROM Compromise: Modification of Option ROMs on expansion cards to execute malicious code during system initialization
        ~SMM Code Injection: Injection of malicious code into System Management Mode to maintain stealthy persistence
        ~Firmware Configuration Override: Manipulation of firmware configuration settings to enable malicious functionality
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Techniques to bypass or disable Secure Boot to allow unauthorized firmware modifications
        ~Firmware Update Mechanism Abuse: Exploitation of legitimate firmware update mechanisms to install malicious firmware
        ~ESP Partition Manipulation: Modification of EFI System Partition contents to maintain persistence
        ~Platform Controller Hub Compromise: Targeting the PCH firmware to maintain low-level persistence
        ~ME/BMC Firmware Modification: Compromising Management Engine or Baseboard Management Controller firmware
        ~TPM Firmware Compromise: Manipulation of Trusted Platform Module firmware to bypass security measures
        ~NIC Firmware Modification: Installing persistent malware in network interface card firmware
        ~Storage Controller Firmware: Compromising storage controller firmware for persistence
        ~CPU Microcode Updates: Abuse of CPU microcode update mechanisms
        ~ACPI Table Modification: Altering Advanced Configuration and Power Interface tables for persistence
        ~PCI Firmware Manipulation: Targeting PCI/PCIe device firmware
        ~GPU Firmware Compromise: Modifying graphics card firmware for persistence
        ~Embedded Controller Modification: Targeting laptop/desktop embedded controller firmware
        ~NVRAM Variable Abuse: Manipulation of NVRAM variables to maintain persistence
        ~Bootloader Firmware Compromise: Targeting bootloader-specific firmware components
        ~Keyboard Controller Firmware: Compromising keyboard controller firmware for keylogging and persistence
        ~RAID Controller Firmware: Modifying RAID controller firmware for persistent access
        ~Power Management Firmware: Targeting power management unit firmware
        ~USB Controller Firmware: Compromising USB controller firmware for persistence
        ~Audio Controller Firmware: Modifying audio device firmware for persistence
        ~SATA Controller Firmware: Targeting storage interface controller firmware
        ~Thunderbolt Controller Firmware: Compromising Thunderbolt controller firmware
        ~Memory Controller Firmware: Modifying memory controller firmware
        ~Clock Generator Firmware: Targeting system timing controller firmware
        ~Wireless Controller Firmware: Compromising Wi-Fi/Bluetooth controller firmware
      Create Account: Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020) With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.  Accounts may be created on the local system or within a domain or cloud tenant. In cloud environments, adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific services, which can reduce the chance of detection.
        Local Account: Adversaries may create a local account to maintain access to victim systems. Local accounts are those configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration on a single system or service.   For example, with a sufficient level of access, the Windows <code>net user /add</code> command can be used to create a local account.  In Linux, the `useradd` command can be used, while on macOS systems, the <code>dscl -create</code> command can be used. Local accounts may also be added to network devices, often via common [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as <code>username</code>, to ESXi servers via `esxcli system account add`, or to Kubernetes clusters using the `kubectl` utility.(Citation: cisco_username_cmd)(Citation: Kubernetes Service Accounts Security)  Such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.
          Execute "net user /add username password" followed by "net localgroup administrators username /add" to create privileged Windows local account
          Run "useradd -m -s /bin/bash username" and "usermod -aG sudo username" to create privileged Linux account
          Use "dscl . -create /Users/username" with additional dscl commands to set up macOS account attributes
          Execute "username admin privilege 15 secret password" on Cisco devices via CLI
          Create local ESXi account using "esxcli system account add -i username -p password -c password"
          Use "kubectl create serviceaccount username" followed by role binding for Kubernetes service account
          Create hidden account with $ suffix in Windows to avoid typical account listing
          Add account with UID 0 in Linux for stealth root privileges
          Configure account with auto-login parameters in macOS using defaults write commands
          Create system account in Windows using "net user username password /add /Y && sc create service binPath=executable obj=username"
          Execute "set system login user username authentication plain-text-password password" on Juniper devices
          Create local account on FortiGate using "config system admin; edit username; set password password"
          Use "local-user username password cipher password privilege level 15" on Huawei devices
          Create Windows local account through WMI: "wmic useraccount create name='username',password='password'"
          Add local account on VMware vCenter using "dir-cli user create --account username --password password --first-name fname --last-name lname"
          Create local account in Windows registry directly using reg.exe commands to bypass typical account creation logging
          Add local account on HP iLO using "create /map1/accounts1 username=username password=password"
          Create AIX local account using "mkuser" with specific attributes for system administration
          Add account on Schneider Electric PLC through Unity Pro software
          Create local account on Dell iDRAC using "racadm config -g cfgUserAdmin -o cfgUserAdminUserName -i 2 username"
          Create NetApp storage admin account using "security login create -vserver name -user-or-group-name username -application ssh -authentication-method password"
          Add local SQL Server account using "CREATE LOGIN username WITH PASSWORD='password'; CREATE USER username FOR LOGIN username"
          Create Oracle database local user: "CREATE USER username IDENTIFIED BY password"
          Add local admin account on IBM Storwize using "mkuser -name username -pw password -grp Administrator"
          Create MongoDB local user: "db.createUser({user:'username',pwd:'password',roles:['root']})"
          Add local account on Citrix NetScaler using "add system user username password"
          Create PostgreSQL superuser: "CREATE ROLE username WITH SUPERUSER LOGIN PASSWORD 'password'"
          Add local admin on F5 BIG-IP using "tmsh create auth user username partition-access add { all-partitions { role admin } }"
          Create WebLogic Server local admin: "createUser.sh -u username -p password -g Administrators"
          Add local account on EMC Unity storage using "create user -name username -role administrator -password password"
          Create Palo Alto firewall admin using "set mgt-config users username password"
          Add Veeam backup admin account: "Add-VBRUser -Name username -Password password -Role Administrator"
          Create Nagios XI admin: "htpasswd -b /usr/local/nagios/etc/htpasswd.users username password"
          Add local account on Splunk using "splunk add user username -role admin -password password"
          Create Jenkins local admin through script console: "jenkins.model.Jenkins.instance.securityRealm.createAccount('username','password')"
          Add CheckPoint firewall admin: "add user username password"
          Create Zabbix super admin using SQL: "INSERT INTO users (alias,name,surname,passwd,type)"
          Add local account on Veritas NetBackup using "bpnbat -AddUser username password"
          Create IBM WebSphere admin: "wsadmin.sh -c '$AdminTask createUser -userid username -password password'"
          Add SolarWinds Orion account using "DECLARE @RC int EXEC [SolarWindsOrion].[dbo].[AddAccount]"
          Create Microsoft Exchange admin using "New-Mailbox -UserPrincipalName username -Alias username -Name 'display' -Password password -Database 'DB' -OrganizationalUnit 'OU'"
          Add OpenLDAP admin account: "ldapadd -x -D cn=admin -W -f user.ldif"
          Create Samba file share admin: "smbpasswd -a username" followed by "usermod -aG sambashare username"
          Add Apache Guacamole remote access admin using MySQL: "INSERT INTO guacamole_user (username, password_hash, password_salt)"
          Create AWS S3 service account using AWS CLI: "aws iam create-user --user-name username && aws iam create-access-key"
          Add local admin on RADIUS server: "raduser -a username -p password -g Admin"
          Create Dovecot mail admin: "doveadm user create -p password username"
          Add local account on Kerberos KDC: "kadmin.local -q 'add_principal username'"
          Create FTP server admin using ProFTPD: "ftpasswd --passwd --name=username --uid=2001 --gid=2001 --shell=/bin/false"
          Add Microsoft 365 Exchange Online admin using "New-EOPMailUser" cmdlet
          Create VMware AirWatch admin: "awcli users create --username username --password password --role administrator"
          Add Cisco ASA VPN admin: "username username password password privilege 15"
          Create Okta super admin using API: "POST /api/v1/users with admin privileges"
          Add GitLab root account through rails console: "User.create!(username:'username', password:'password', admin:true)"
          Create SAP system admin: "SU01 transaction to create dialog user with SAP_ALL profile"
          Add WordPress admin using WP-CLI: "wp user create username email@domain.com --role=administrator"
          Create MobileIron admin account through API with system privileges
          Add Pulse Secure VPN admin: "set admin username password password"
          Create Atlassian admin using API: "POST /admin/users with site-admin privileges"
          Add Oracle E-Business Suite system admin: "FNDCPASS utility to create admin user"
          Create SolarWinds NCM admin: "SWQL Studio to execute CreateOrUpdateAccount procedure"
          Add Microsoft Teams admin using Graph API: "POST /users with Teams Administrator role"
          Create Cisco Prime Infrastructure super admin: "ncs-cli admin username password"
          Add local admin on Lenel OnGuard: "System Administration > Users with full system rights"
          Create Slack Workspace Owner using organization management console
          Add Building Management System (BMS) admin on Honeywell Enterprise Buildings Integrator
          Create Kronos system admin through application console
          Add Cisco Meraki dashboard admin: "organization > administrators > add administrator"
          Create local admin on APC InfraStruxure Manager
          Add Siemens Desigo CC building automation system admin account
        Cloud Account: Adversaries may create a cloud account to maintain access to victim systems. With a sufficient level of access, such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that does not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.(Citation: Microsoft O365 Admin Roles)(Citation: Microsoft Support O365 Add Another Admin, October 2019)(Citation: AWS Create IAM User)(Citation: GCP Create Cloud Identity Users)(Citation: Microsoft Azure AD Users)  In addition to user accounts, cloud accounts may be associated with services. Cloud providers handle the concept of service accounts in different ways. In Azure, service accounts include service principals and managed identities, which can be linked to various resources such as OAuth applications, serverless functions, and virtual machines in order to grant those resources permissions to perform various activities in the environment.(Citation: Microsoft Entra ID Service Principals) In GCP, service accounts can also be linked to specific resources, as well as be impersonated by other accounts for [Temporary Elevated Cloud Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/005).(Citation: GCP Service Accounts) While AWS has no specific concept of service accounts, resources can be directly granted permission to assume roles.(Citation: AWS Instance Profiles)(Citation: AWS Lambda Execution Role)  Adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific cloud services, which can reduce the chance of detection.  Once an adversary has created a cloud account, they can then manipulate that account to ensure persistence and allow access to additional resources - for example, by adding [Additional Cloud Credentials](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001) or assigning [Additional Cloud Roles](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/003).
