      BITS Jobs: Adversaries may abuse BITS jobs to persistently execute code and perform various background tasks. Windows Background Intelligent Transfer Service (BITS) is a low-bandwidth, asynchronous file transfer mechanism exposed through [Component Object Model](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559/001) (COM).(Citation: Microsoft COM)(Citation: Microsoft BITS) BITS is commonly used by updaters, messengers, and other applications preferred to operate in the background (using available idle bandwidth) without interrupting other networked applications. File transfer tasks are implemented as BITS jobs, which contain a queue of one or more file operations.  The interface to create and manage BITS jobs is accessible through [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) and the [BITSAdmin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0190) tool.(Citation: Microsoft BITS)(Citation: Microsoft BITSAdmin)  Adversaries may abuse BITS to download (e.g. [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105)), execute, and even clean up after running malicious code (e.g. [Indicator Removal](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070)). BITS tasks are self-contained in the BITS job database, without new files or registry modifications, and often permitted by host firewalls.(Citation: CTU BITS Malware June 2016)(Citation: Mondok Windows PiggyBack BITS May 2007)(Citation: Symantec BITS May 2007) BITS enabled execution may also enable persistence by creating long-standing jobs (the default maximum lifetime is 90 days and extendable) or invoking an arbitrary program when a job completes or errors (including after system reboots).(Citation: PaloAlto UBoatRAT Nov 2017)(Citation: CTU BITS Malware June 2016)  BITS upload functionalities can also be used to perform [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).(Citation: CTU BITS Malware June 2016)
        ~Long-Standing Job Creation: Adversaries create BITS jobs with extended lifetimes (up to 90 days) to maintain persistent access
        ~Job Completion Triggers: Abuse of BITS job completion notifications to execute malicious code
        ~Error Handler Abuse: Exploitation of BITS error handling mechanisms to trigger malicious code execution
        ~PowerShell BITS Management: Using PowerShell cmdlets to create and manage malicious BITS jobs
        ~BITSAdmin Tool Abuse: Leveraging the BITSAdmin utility to create and manage malicious transfers
        ~Download and Execute: Using BITS to download malicious payloads and execute them
        ~Upload Channel: Abuse of BITS upload functionality for data exfiltration
        ~Notification Interface Manipulation: Modifying BITS notification interfaces to maintain persistence
        ~COM Interface Abuse: Exploitation of BITS COM interfaces for custom job creation and management
        ~Clean-up Automation: Using BITS job completion triggers to remove evidence of malicious activities
        ~Job Priority Manipulation: Modifying BITS job priority levels to control execution timing and resource usage
        ~Custom Notification Program: Creating custom notification programs to handle BITS job events
        ~Proxy Configuration Abuse: Manipulating BITS proxy settings to bypass network controls
        ~Credential Storage Abuse: Exploiting BITS credential storage for privilege persistence
        ~Job Chaining: Creating interconnected BITS jobs that trigger each other for complex persistence
      Implant Internal Image: Adversaries may implant cloud or container images with malicious code to establish persistence after gaining access to an environment. Amazon Web Services (AWS) Amazon Machine Images (AMIs), Google Cloud Platform (GCP) Images, and Azure Images as well as popular container runtimes such as Docker can be implanted or backdoored. Unlike [Upload Malware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/001), this technique focuses on adversaries implanting an image in a registry within a victim’s environment. Depending on how the infrastructure is provisioned, this could provide persistent access if the infrastructure provisioning tool is instructed to always use the latest image.(Citation: Rhino Labs Cloud Image Backdoor Technique Sept 2019)  A tool has been developed to facilitate planting backdoors in cloud container images.(Citation: Rhino Labs Cloud Backdoor September 2019) If an adversary has access to a compromised AWS instance, and permissions to list the available container images, they may implant a backdoor such as a [Web Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003).(Citation: Rhino Labs Cloud Image Backdoor Technique Sept 2019)
        ~Modify Base Image Layer: Adversaries modify the base layer of a container image to include malicious code that persists across derived images
        ~Inject Malicious Components: Adversaries add malicious components or dependencies during image build process
        ~Compromise Build Pipeline: Adversaries manipulate the CI/CD pipeline to automatically inject malicious code into images during build
        ~Backdoor Image Configuration: Adversaries modify image configuration files to execute malicious code on container startup
        ~Replace Image Tags: Adversaries replace existing image tags to point to compromised versions while maintaining the same name
        ~Modify Image Metadata: Adversaries alter image metadata to execute malicious commands during container initialization
        ~Cross-Account Image Sharing: Adversaries share compromised images across different cloud accounts to expand persistence
        ~Layer Manipulation: Adversaries add new layers to existing images containing malicious code while preserving original functionality
        ~Runtime Configuration Injection: Adversaries inject malicious runtime configurations that execute during container instantiation
        ~Service Account Manipulation: Adversaries modify service account configurations within images to maintain privileged access
        ~Registry Permission Abuse: Adversaries exploit registry permissions to replace or modify existing trusted images
        ~Image Scan Evasion: Adversaries implement techniques to bypass security scans and vulnerability detection in container registries
        ~Custom Init System Implant: Adversaries modify init systems within images to maintain persistence across container restarts
        ~Layer Cache Poisoning: Adversaries poison layer caches to inject malicious code into images during build process
        ~Image Pull Hook Manipulation: Adversaries modify image pull hooks to execute malicious code during image retrieval
        ~Template Injection: Adversaries inject malicious code into infrastructure-as-code templates that generate images
        ~Image Manifest Modification: Adversaries alter image manifests to include unauthorized layers or configurations
        ~Snapshot Backdooring: Adversaries implant malicious code into VM snapshots used for image creation
        ~Environment Variable Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate environment variables within images to execute malicious code
        ~Image Policy Bypass: Adversaries implement techniques to bypass image security policies and signing requirements
        ~Orchestrator Configuration Poisoning: Adversaries modify orchestrator configurations (like Kubernetes manifests) to deploy compromised images
        ~Multi-Region Image Replication: Adversaries exploit image replication features to propagate compromised images across regions
        ~Image Signing Key Compromise: Adversaries compromise image signing keys to sign and distribute malicious images
        ~Base Image Supply Chain Attack: Adversaries compromise commonly used base images in public repositories
        ~Cross-Platform Image Targeting: Adversaries implant malicious code that targets multiple architecture/platform combinations
        ~Image Layer Versioning Abuse: Adversaries manipulate layer versioning to maintain persistence across image updates
        ~Image Rebuild Trigger Exploitation: Adversaries exploit automated rebuild triggers to reintroduce malicious code
        ~Registry Webhook Manipulation: Adversaries modify registry webhooks to maintain control over image deployment
        ~Image Conversion Tool Compromise: Adversaries exploit image conversion tools to inject malicious code during format conversion
        ~Hybrid Cloud Image Synchronization: Adversaries exploit hybrid cloud image synchronization to propagate compromised images
      Rc.common: During the boot process, macOS executes <code>source /etc/rc.common</code>, which is a shell script containing various utility functions. This file also defines routines for processing command-line arguments and for gathering system settings, and is thus recommended to include in the start of Startup Item Scripts (Citation: Startup Items). In macOS and OS X, this is now a deprecated technique in favor of launch agents and launch daemons, but is currently still used.  Adversaries can use the rc.common file as a way to hide code for persistence that will execute on each reboot as the root user (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence).
        ~Function Hijacking: Modifying existing utility functions within rc.common to include malicious code while maintaining original functionality
        ~Script Injection: Directly inserting malicious scripts or commands at the beginning or end of rc.common
        ~Environment Variable Manipulation: Adding malicious environment variable definitions that affect system-wide behavior
        ~Command Line Argument Poisoning: Modifying argument processing routines to execute malicious code
        ~System Settings Tampering: Inserting code into system settings gathering routines to maintain persistence
        ~Backup File Creation: Creating backup copies of rc.common with malicious code to survive system updates
        ~Conditional Execution: Adding conditional statements that execute malicious code based on system state
        ~Library Loading: Adding malicious library loading commands to rc.common
      Netsh Helper DLL: Netsh.exe (also referred to as Netshell) is a command-line scripting utility used to interact with the network configuration of a system. It contains functionality to add helper DLLs for extending functionality of the utility. (Citation: TechNet Netsh) The paths to registered netsh.exe helper DLLs are entered into the Windows Registry at <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Netsh</code>.  Adversaries can use netsh.exe with helper DLLs to proxy execution of arbitrary code in a persistent manner when netsh.exe is executed automatically with another Persistence technique or if other persistent software is present on the system that executes netsh.exe as part of its normal functionality. Examples include some VPN software that invoke netsh.exe. (Citation: Demaske Netsh Persistence)  Proof of concept code exists to load Cobalt Strike's payload using netsh.exe helper DLLs. (Citation: Github Netsh Helper CS Beacon)
        ~Registry-based Helper Registration: Adversaries directly modify the Windows Registry to register malicious helper DLLs in HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Netsh
        ~VPN Software Abuse: Targeting legitimate VPN software that regularly calls netsh.exe to inject malicious helper DLLs
        ~System Service Exploitation: Leveraging system services that use netsh.exe to load malicious helper DLLs during system startup
        ~Network Configuration Hijacking: Replacing legitimate netsh helper DLLs used for network configuration with malicious versions
        ~Command-Line Installation: Using netsh.exe add helper command to install malicious DLLs through command-line interface
        ~Helper DLL Backup Persistence: Creating backup copies of malicious helper DLLs in alternate locations to re-establish persistence if primary DLL is removed
        ~Network Interface Manipulation: Registering helper DLLs that specifically target network interface configuration functions
        ~Multiple Helper Chain: Installing multiple interconnected helper DLLs that provide redundant persistence and complicate removal
        ~Privilege Escalation via Helper DLL: Leveraging netsh helper DLL loading to escalate privileges during persistence establishment
        ~Proxy Configuration Abuse: Using helper DLLs to manipulate network proxy settings for maintaining persistence and command & control
      Scheduled Task/Job: Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. Utilities exist within all major operating systems to schedule programs or scripts to be executed at a specified date and time. A task can also be scheduled on a remote system, provided the proper authentication is met (ex: RPC and file and printer sharing in Windows environments). Scheduling a task on a remote system typically may require being a member of an admin or otherwise privileged group on the remote system.(Citation: TechNet Task Scheduler Security)  Adversaries may use task scheduling to execute programs at system startup or on a scheduled basis for persistence. These mechanisms can also be abused to run a process under the context of a specified account (such as one with elevated permissions/privileges). Similar to [System Binary Proxy Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1218), adversaries have also abused task scheduling to potentially mask one-time execution under a trusted system process.(Citation: ProofPoint Serpent)
        Scheduled Task: Adversaries may abuse the Windows Task Scheduler to perform task scheduling for initial or recurring execution of malicious code. There are multiple ways to access the Task Scheduler in Windows. The [schtasks](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0111) utility can be run directly on the command line, or the Task Scheduler can be opened through the GUI within the Administrator Tools section of the Control Panel.(Citation: Stack Overflow) In some cases, adversaries have used a .NET wrapper for the Windows Task Scheduler, and alternatively, adversaries have used the Windows netapi32 library and [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047) (WMI) to create a scheduled task. Adversaries may also utilize the Powershell Cmdlet `Invoke-CimMethod`, which leverages WMI class `PS_ScheduledTask` to create a scheduled task via an XML path.(Citation: Red Canary - Atomic Red Team)  An adversary may use Windows Task Scheduler to execute programs at system startup or on a scheduled basis for persistence. The Windows Task Scheduler can also be abused to conduct remote Execution as part of Lateral Movement and/or to run a process under the context of a specified account (such as SYSTEM). Similar to [System Binary Proxy Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1218), adversaries have also abused the Windows Task Scheduler to potentially mask one-time execution under signed/trusted system processes.(Citation: ProofPoint Serpent)  Adversaries may also create "hidden" scheduled tasks (i.e. [Hide Artifacts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564)) that may not be visible to defender tools and manual queries used to enumerate tasks. Specifically, an adversary may hide a task from `schtasks /query` and the Task Scheduler by deleting the associated Security Descriptor (SD) registry value (where deletion of this value must be completed using SYSTEM permissions).(Citation: SigmaHQ)(Citation: Tarrask scheduled task) Adversaries may also employ alternate methods to hide tasks, such as altering the metadata (e.g., `Index` value) within associated registry keys.(Citation: Defending Against Scheduled Task Attacks in Windows Environments)
          Use schtasks.exe command-line utility to create new scheduled tasks for persistence
          Create scheduled tasks through Task Scheduler GUI in Administrator Tools
          Leverage PowerShell Invoke-CimMethod cmdlet with PS_ScheduledTask WMI class to create tasks
          Use .NET TaskScheduler wrapper library to programmatically create tasks
          Employ Windows netapi32 library for scheduled task creation
          Delete Security Descriptor registry value to hide tasks from schtasks /query
