      External Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage external-facing remote services to initially access and/or persist within a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms allow users to connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations. There are often remote service gateways that manage connections and credential authentication for these services. Services such as [Windows Remote Management](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/006) and [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) can also be used externally.(Citation: MacOS VNC software for Remote Desktop)  Access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to use the service is often a requirement, which could be obtained through credential pharming or by obtaining the credentials from users after compromising the enterprise network.(Citation: Volexity Virtual Private Keylogging) Access to remote services may be used as a redundant or persistent access mechanism during an operation.  Access may also be gained through an exposed service that doesn’t require authentication. In containerized environments, this may include an exposed Docker API, Kubernetes API server, kubelet, or web application such as the Kubernetes dashboard.(Citation: Trend Micro Exposed Docker Server)(Citation: Unit 42 Hildegard Malware)
        ~VPN Services: Adversaries may exploit VPN services and gateways to maintain persistent access to networks, including manipulation of VPN configurations or compromised VPN credentials
        ~Container APIs: Adversaries may target exposed container management APIs like Docker API or Kubernetes API server for persistent access to containerized environments
        ~Remote Desktop Services: Adversaries may abuse remote desktop protocols and services like VNC, RDP, or Citrix for persistent access
        ~Cloud Service Management: Adversaries may exploit cloud service management interfaces and APIs for maintaining access to cloud-hosted resources
        ~Remote Management Protocols: Adversaries may leverage protocols like SSH, WinRM, or other remote management services for persistence
        ~Web-Based Administrative Interfaces: Adversaries may target exposed web-based management consoles like Kubernetes dashboard or similar administrative interfaces
        ~Identity Provider Services: Adversaries may compromise identity provider services that manage authentication for remote access
        ~Application Gateway Services: Adversaries may exploit application delivery controllers and gateway services that provide remote access to internal applications
      Setuid and Setgid: When the setuid or setgid bits are set on Linux or macOS for an application, this means that the application will run with the privileges of the owning user or group respectively  (Citation: setuid man page). Normally an application is run in the current user’s context, regardless of which user or group owns the application. There are instances where programs need to be executed in an elevated context to function properly, but the user running them doesn’t need the elevated privileges. Instead of creating an entry in the sudoers file, which must be done by root, any user can specify the setuid or setgid flag to be set for their own applications. These bits are indicated with an "s" instead of an "x" when viewing a file's attributes via <code>ls -l</code>. The <code>chmod</code> program can set these bits with via bitmasking, <code>chmod 4777 [file]</code> or via shorthand naming, <code>chmod u+s [file]</code>.  An adversary can take advantage of this to either do a shell escape or exploit a vulnerability in an application with the setsuid or setgid bits to get code running in a different user’s context. Additionally, adversaries can use this mechanism on their own malware to make sure they're able to execute in elevated contexts in the future  (Citation: OSX Keydnap malware).
        ~Application Exploitation: Targeting existing setuid/setgid applications to exploit vulnerabilities for privilege escalation and persistence
        ~Custom Binary Installation: Creating and installing malicious setuid/setgid binaries in common system directories
        ~Shell Command Wrapper: Creating setuid/setgid shell script wrappers around legitimate commands to intercept execution
        ~Scheduled Task Manipulation: Modifying scheduled tasks to execute with setuid/setgid privileges
        ~Library Hijacking: Exploiting shared library loading in setuid/setgid programs
        ~Configuration File Modification: Altering configurations of setuid/setgid programs to maintain access
        ~Service Binary Replacement: Replacing legitimate service binaries with malicious setuid/setgid versions
        ~Temporary File Manipulation: Exploiting temporary file handling in setuid/setgid programs
        ~Command Line Parameter Injection: Exploiting command line parameter handling in setuid/setgid programs
        ~Environment Variable Abuse: Manipulating environment variables to affect setuid/setgid program behavior
        ~Debug Tool Abuse: Exploiting debugging tools with setuid/setgid permissions for persistence
        ~Backup Utility Manipulation: Modifying backup tools that run with elevated privileges
        ~System Maintenance Script Modification: Altering maintenance scripts that run with setuid/setgid permissions
        ~Recovery Tool Exploitation: Targeting system recovery tools that require elevated privileges
        ~Print Spooler Abuse: Exploiting print system components that run with elevated permissions
        ~Package Manager Manipulation: Abusing package management tools with setuid/setgid bits
        ~Device Management Tool Exploitation: Targeting device management utilities with elevated privileges
        ~Logging Tool Abuse: Exploiting logging utilities that run with elevated permissions
      Launch Agent: Per Apple’s developer documentation, when a user logs in, a per-user launchd process is started which loads the parameters for each launch-on-demand user agent from the property list (plist) files found in <code>/System/Library/LaunchAgents</code>, <code>/Library/LaunchAgents</code>, and <code>$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents</code> (Citation: AppleDocs Launch Agent Daemons) (Citation: OSX Keydnap malware) (Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware). These launch agents have property list files which point to the executables that will be launched (Citation: OSX.Dok Malware).   Adversaries may install a new launch agent that can be configured to execute at login by using launchd or launchctl to load a plist into the appropriate directories  (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan)  (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence). The agent name may be disguised by using a name from a related operating system or benign software. Launch Agents are created with user level privileges and are executed with the privileges of the user when they log in (Citation: OSX Malware Detection) (Citation: OceanLotus for OS X). They can be set up to execute when a specific user logs in (in the specific user’s directory structure) or when any user logs in (which requires administrator privileges).
        ~User-Level Launch Agent: Creating and installing launch agents in user's ~/Library/LaunchAgents directory for user-specific persistence
        ~System-Wide Launch Agent: Installing launch agents in /Library/LaunchAgents for persistence across all users
        ~System Launch Agent Masquerading: Placing malicious launch agents in /System/Library/LaunchAgents disguised as legitimate system components
        ~Launch Agent Property List Modification: Modifying existing legitimate plist files to include malicious commands
        ~Launch Agent Label Spoofing: Creating launch agents with names that mimic legitimate system or application processes
        ~Scheduled Launch Agent: Configuring launch agents with specific scheduling parameters for timed execution
        ~Environmental Trigger Launch Agent: Creating launch agents that activate based on specific system events or conditions
        ~Launch Agent Command Injection: Injecting malicious commands into legitimate launch agent configurations
      Modify Registry: Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry as part of a variety of other techniques to aid in defense evasion, persistence, and execution.  Access to specific areas of the Registry depends on account permissions, with some keys requiring administrator-level access. The built-in Windows command-line utility [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) may be used for local or remote Registry modification.(Citation: Microsoft Reg) Other tools, such as remote access tools, may also contain functionality to interact with the Registry through the Windows API.  The Registry may be modified in order to hide configuration information or malicious payloads via [Obfuscated Files or Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027).(Citation: Unit42 BabyShark Feb 2019)(Citation: Avaddon Ransomware 2021)(Citation: Microsoft BlackCat Jun 2022)(Citation: CISA Russian Gov Critical Infra 2018) The Registry may also be modified to [Impair Defenses](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562), such as by enabling macros for all Microsoft Office products, allowing privilege escalation without alerting the user, increasing the maximum number of allowed outbound requests, and/or modifying systems to store plaintext credentials in memory.(Citation: CISA LockBit 2023)(Citation: Unit42 BabyShark Feb 2019)  The Registry of a remote system may be modified to aid in execution of files as part of lateral movement. It requires the remote Registry service to be running on the target system.(Citation: Microsoft Remote) Often [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) are required, along with access to the remote system's [SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002) for RPC communication.  Finally, Registry modifications may also include actions to hide keys, such as prepending key names with a null character, which will cause an error and/or be ignored when read via [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) or other utilities using the Win32 API.(Citation: Microsoft Reghide NOV 2006) Adversaries may abuse these pseudo-hidden keys to conceal payloads/commands used to maintain persistence.(Citation: TrendMicro POWELIKS AUG 2014)(Citation: SpectorOps Hiding Reg Jul 2017)
        ~Run Keys / Startup Folder: Adversaries modify Registry run keys and startup folder entries to maintain persistence across system restarts
        ~Accessibility Features: Adversaries modify Registry entries related to Windows accessibility features to maintain access
        ~Security Support Provider: Adversaries modify Registry to register malicious security support providers for credential access
        ~Hidden Registry Keys: Adversaries use techniques like null character prefixing to hide malicious Registry entries
        ~AppInit DLLs: Adversaries modify Registry keys to load malicious DLLs into processes
        ~Application Shimming: Adversaries modify Registry entries to implement application compatibility shims
        ~Port Monitors: Adversaries modify Registry entries related to port monitor DLLs
        ~Print Processors: Adversaries modify Registry keys related to print processor loading
        ~Services Registry Permissions Weakness: Adversaries modify Registry permissions of service entries
        ~Terminal Services Registry Modification: Adversaries modify Terminal Services related Registry keys
        ~Authentication Package: Adversaries modify Registry entries to load malicious authentication packages
        ~Browser Extensions: Adversaries modify Registry to install malicious browser extensions
        ~WinLogon Helper DLL: Adversaries modify WinLogon Registry entries to load malicious DLLs during login
        ~Group Policy Modifications: Adversaries modify Registry entries related to Group Policy settings
        ~Windows Defender Registry: Adversaries modify Registry settings to disable security features
        ~Network Protocol Settings: Adversaries modify Registry entries controlling network protocols
        ~COM Object Hijacking: Adversaries modify Registry entries for COM object registration
        ~Active Setup: Adversaries abuse Active Setup Registry entries for persistence
        ~Image File Execution Options: Adversaries modify Registry entries to intercept application execution
        ~Screensaver Settings: Adversaries modify Registry entries controlling screensaver execution
        ~Shell Folders: Adversaries modify user Shell folder Registry settings
        ~User Init: Adversaries modify UserInit Registry values for persistence
        ~PowerShell Profile: Adversaries modify Registry settings related to PowerShell profiles
        ~Windows Error Reporting: Adversaries modify Registry settings for error reporting
        ~Software Policy Settings: Adversaries modify Registry entries controlling software policies
      Modify Existing Service: Windows service configuration information, including the file path to the service's executable or recovery programs/commands, is stored in the Registry. Service configurations can be modified using utilities such as sc.exe and [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075).  Adversaries can modify an existing service to persist malware on a system by using system utilities or by using custom tools to interact with the Windows API. Use of existing services is a type of [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036) that may make detection analysis more challenging. Modifying existing services may interrupt their functionality or may enable services that are disabled or otherwise not commonly used.  Adversaries may also intentionally corrupt or kill services to execute malicious recovery programs/commands. (Citation: Twitter Service Recovery Nov 2017) (Citation: Microsoft Service Recovery Feb 2013)
        ~Service Executable Modification: Replace or modify the service's executable file path in the Registry to point to malicious code
        ~Service Configuration Changes: Alter service parameters like startup type, dependencies, or account privileges
        ~Recovery Action Manipulation: Modify service recovery actions to execute malicious commands when service fails
        ~Service DLL Hijacking: Modify service DLL paths or load order to execute malicious DLLs
        ~Service Registry Permissions: Modify permissions on service Registry keys to maintain persistence
        ~Service Trigger Modification: Alter service trigger settings to execute under specific conditions
        ~Service Account Manipulation: Change service account to one controlled by adversary
        ~Service Description Modification: Hide malicious changes by modifying service descriptions
      Shortcut Modification: Shortcuts or symbolic links are ways of referencing other files or programs that will be opened or executed when the shortcut is clicked or executed by a system startup process. Adversaries could use shortcuts to execute their tools for persistence. They may create a new shortcut as a means of indirection that may use [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036) to look like a legitimate program. Adversaries could also edit the target path or entirely replace an existing shortcut so their tools will be executed instead of the intended legitimate program.
        ~Desktop Shortcuts: Adversaries modify desktop shortcuts to maintain persistence by altering shortcuts visible to users on the desktop
        ~Startup Folder Shortcuts: Adversaries place or modify shortcuts in Windows startup folders to achieve persistence across system reboots
        ~Start Menu Shortcuts: Adversaries modify shortcuts in the Start Menu to intercept normal program execution
        ~Command-Line Arguments Modification: Adversaries add or modify command-line arguments in shortcuts to execute additional malicious code
        ~Icon Manipulation: Adversaries modify shortcut icons to masquerade as legitimate programs while pointing to malicious executables
        ~System Symbolic Links: Adversaries create or modify system-level symbolic links to maintain persistence in Unix-like systems
        ~Application Shortcuts: Adversaries modify shortcuts associated with commonly used applications to intercept execution
        ~URL Shortcuts: Adversaries modify URL shortcuts to redirect users to malicious websites or execute malicious code
        ~Network Shortcuts: Adversaries modify shortcuts pointing to network resources to intercept network connections
        ~Quick Launch Shortcuts: Adversaries modify shortcuts in the Quick Launch bar or taskbar for persistent access
        ~Scheduled Task Shortcuts: Adversaries modify shortcuts used in scheduled tasks to maintain persistence through task execution
        ~File Association Shortcuts: Adversaries modify default program shortcuts used for file type associations
        ~Special Folder Redirection: Adversaries modify shortcuts in Windows special folders (like SendTo, Recent) for persistence
        ~Environmental Variable Injection: Adversaries modify shortcuts to include environment variables that resolve to malicious paths
      Redundant Access: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Create Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136), [Web Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003), and [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133) where appropriate.**  Adversaries may use more than one remote access tool with varying command and control protocols or credentialed access to remote services so they can maintain access if an access mechanism is detected or mitigated.   If one type of tool is detected and blocked or removed as a response but the organization did not gain a full understanding of the adversary's tools and access, then the adversary will be able to retain access to the network. Adversaries may also attempt to gain access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to use [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133) such as external VPNs as a way to maintain access despite interruptions to remote access tools deployed within a target network.(Citation: Mandiant APT1) Adversaries may also retain access through cloud-based infrastructure and applications.  Use of a [Web Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1100) is one such way to maintain access to a network through an externally accessible Web server.
        ~Multiple Remote Access Tools: Use of multiple different RATs with distinct protocols and communication methods
        ~Distributed C2 Infrastructure: Maintaining multiple command and control servers and protocols
        ~Redundant Credentials: Creating and maintaining multiple sets of valid credentials across different systems
        ~Cloud Service Redundancy: Establishing multiple access points through different cloud services
        ~Protocol Diversity: Using multiple network protocols for maintaining access
        ~Backup Communication Channels: Implementing alternative communication methods
        ~Multi-Platform Persistence: Installing persistence mechanisms across different operating systems
        ~Service Redundancy: Implementing multiple persistent services that provide remote access
        ~Network Path Diversity: Establishing multiple network paths for maintaining access
        ~Authentication Mechanism Diversity: Using multiple authentication methods for access
      Plist Modification: Property list (plist) files contain all of the information that macOS and OS X uses to configure applications and services. These files are UTF-8 encoded and formatted like XML documents via a series of keys surrounded by < >. They detail when programs should execute, file paths to the executables, program arguments, required OS permissions, and many others. plists are located in certain locations depending on their purpose such as <code>/Library/Preferences</code> (which execute with elevated privileges) and <code>~/Library/Preferences</code> (which execute with a user's privileges).  Adversaries can modify these plist files to point to their own code, can use them to execute their code in the context of another user, bypass whitelisting procedures, or even use them as a persistence mechanism. (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan)
        ~Login Item Modification: Adversaries modify login item plists in ~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginitems.plist to execute malicious code when a user logs in
        ~Launch Daemon Modification: Adversaries modify system-wide launch daemon plists in /Library/LaunchDaemons/ to execute malicious code with root privileges
        ~Launch Agent Modification: Adversaries modify user-specific launch agent plists in ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ to execute malicious code with user privileges
        ~Application Support Modification: Adversaries modify application support plists to hijack legitimate application configurations and execute malicious code
        ~System Preferences Modification: Adversaries modify system preferences plists to alter system behavior and maintain persistence
        ~Startup Item Modification: Adversaries modify startup item plists in /Library/StartupItems/ to execute malicious code during system boot
        ~Safari Extension Modification: Adversaries modify Safari extension plists to load malicious browser extensions
        ~Screensaver Modification: Adversaries modify screensaver preference plists to execute malicious code when the screensaver activates
        ~Spotlight Import Modification: Adversaries modify Spotlight importer plists to execute malicious code during file indexing operations
        ~Printer Preference Modification: Adversaries modify printer preference plists to maintain persistence through print spooler services
        ~iCloud Integration Modification: Adversaries modify iCloud-related plists to persist through cloud synchronization processes
        ~Dock Preferences Modification: Adversaries modify Dock preference plists to execute malicious code through dock items
        ~Network Configuration Modification: Adversaries modify network configuration plists to maintain persistence through network service operations
      Re-opened Applications: Starting in Mac OS X 10.7 (Lion), users can specify certain applications to be re-opened when a user reboots their machine. While this is usually done via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) on an app-by-app basis, there are property list files (plist) that contain this information as well located at <code>~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow.plist</code> and <code>~/Library/Preferences/ByHost/com.apple.loginwindow.* .plist</code>.   An adversary can modify one of these files directly to include a link to their malicious executable to provide a persistence mechanism each time the user reboots their machine (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence).
        ~Manual Plist Modification: Direct editing of loginwindow.plist files to add malicious applications to the re-open list
        ~GUI Manipulation: Exploiting the System Preferences interface to register malicious apps for re-opening
        ~ByHost Plist Exploitation: Targeting machine-specific ByHost plist files for hardware-specific persistence
        ~Login Items Injection: Adding entries to the user's login items through the re-opened applications mechanism
        ~State Manipulation: Modifying the application state data to force re-opening of malicious apps
        ~Session State Hijacking: Manipulating saved application state data to inject malicious code during session restoration
        ~System Migration Abuse: Exploiting the migration of re-opened applications settings during system transfers or upgrades
        ~Privilege Escalation via Re-open: Configuring privileged applications to re-open with elevated permissions
        ~Backup State Manipulation: Modifying Time Machine or system backup data to persist re-opened application settings
      Office Application Startup: Adversaries may leverage Microsoft Office-based applications for persistence between startups. Microsoft Office is a fairly common application suite on Windows-based operating systems within an enterprise network. There are multiple mechanisms that can be used with Office for persistence when an Office-based application is started; this can include the use of Office Template Macros and add-ins.  A variety of features have been discovered in Outlook that can be abused to obtain persistence, such as Outlook rules, forms, and Home Page.(Citation: SensePost Ruler GitHub) These persistence mechanisms can work within Outlook or be used through Office 365.(Citation: TechNet O365 Outlook Rules)
        Outlook Rules: Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Outlook rules to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Outlook rules allow a user to define automated behavior to manage email messages. A benign rule might, for example, automatically move an email to a particular folder in Outlook if it contains specific words from a specific sender. Malicious Outlook rules can be created that can trigger code execution when an adversary sends a specifically crafted email to that user.(Citation: SilentBreak Outlook Rules)  Once malicious rules have been added to the user’s mailbox, they will be loaded when Outlook is started. Malicious rules will execute when an adversary sends a specifically crafted email to the user.(Citation: SilentBreak Outlook Rules)
          Create rule to execute a script when email with specific subject line is received
          Configure rule to run specific application when email from particular sender arrives
          Set up rule to invoke PowerShell command when email containing specific keywords is received
