          Create cross-tenant persistence mechanisms in multi-tenant environments
        Office Test: Adversaries may abuse the Microsoft Office "Office Test" Registry key to obtain persistence on a compromised system. An Office Test Registry location exists that allows a user to specify an arbitrary DLL that will be executed every time an Office application is started. This Registry key is thought to be used by Microsoft to load DLLs for testing and debugging purposes while developing Office applications. This Registry key is not created by default during an Office installation.(Citation: Hexacorn Office Test)(Citation: Palo Alto Office Test Sofacy)  There exist user and global Registry keys for the Office Test feature, such as:  * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Office test\Special\Perf</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Office test\Special\Perf</code>  Adversaries may add this Registry key and specify a malicious DLL that will be executed whenever an Office application, such as Word or Excel, is started.
          Create malicious DLL and add Registry key HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Office test\Special\Perf using reg.exe
          Deploy DLL to shared location and modify HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Office test\Special\Perf using PowerShell
          Use COM hijacking in malicious DLL to intercept Office application launches
          Create DLL that hooks into multiple Office applications (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) simultaneously
          Implement DLL that maintains persistence across Office version updates
          Use Registry modification tools like AutoRuns to establish Office Test persistence
          Create self-updating DLL that downloads new payloads when Office applications start
          Implement stealthy DLL that mimics legitimate Office DLL names
          Use WMI to monitor and maintain Office Test Registry keys
          Deploy DLL with anti-debugging features to evade detection
          Implement DLL that persists through Office repair/reinstallation by monitoring installer events
          Create backup Registry entries in multiple Office Test paths for resilience
          Use scheduled tasks to periodically verify and restore Office Test Registry modifications
          Implement DLL that spreads Office Test persistence to other user profiles on the system
          Create encoded/encrypted DLL payload to evade static analysis
          Use Office Test DLL to establish additional persistence mechanisms
          Implement DLL that validates integrity of persistence chain before execution
          Create DLL with fallback C2 communication methods
          Use Office Test persistence to deploy fileless payloads
          Implement DLL that maintains persistence across Office 365 cloud profile syncs
        Office Template Macros: Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Office templates to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Microsoft Office contains templates that are part of common Office applications and are used to customize styles. The base templates within the application are used each time an application starts. (Citation: Microsoft Change Normal Template)  Office Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) macros (Citation: MSDN VBA in Office) can be inserted into the base template and used to execute code when the respective Office application starts in order to obtain persistence. Examples for both Word and Excel have been discovered and published. By default, Word has a Normal.dotm template created that can be modified to include a malicious macro. Excel does not have a template file created by default, but one can be added that will automatically be loaded.(Citation: enigma0x3 normal.dotm)(Citation: Hexacorn Office Template Macros) Shared templates may also be stored and pulled from remote locations.(Citation: GlobalDotName Jun 2019)   Word Normal.dotm location:<br> <code>C:\Users\&lt;username&gt;\AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Templates\Normal.dotm</code>  Excel Personal.xlsb location:<br> <code>C:\Users\&lt;username&gt;\AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Excel\XLSTART\PERSONAL.XLSB</code>  Adversaries may also change the location of the base template to point to their own by hijacking the application's search order, e.g. Word 2016 will first look for Normal.dotm under <code>C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Office\root\Office16\</code>, or by modifying the GlobalDotName registry key. By modifying the GlobalDotName registry key an adversary can specify an arbitrary location, file name, and file extension to use for the template that will be loaded on application startup. To abuse GlobalDotName, adversaries may first need to register the template as a trusted document or place it in a trusted location.(Citation: GlobalDotName Jun 2019)   An adversary may need to enable macros to execute unrestricted depending on the system or enterprise security policy on use of macros.
          Default Template Modification: Adversaries modify existing default templates like Normal.dotm or create new ones like Personal.xlsb to include malicious macros that execute on application startup
          Template Search Order Hijacking: Adversaries place malicious templates in priority search locations to ensure their template is loaded before legitimate ones
          Remote Template Injection: Adversaries configure Office applications to load templates from remote network locations they control
          GlobalDotName Registry Manipulation: Adversaries modify registry keys to specify custom template locations and names
          Template Trust Exploitation: Adversaries abuse template trust mechanisms by placing malicious templates in trusted locations or registering them as trusted documents
          Shared Workbook Templates: Adversaries abuse shared workbook templates in Excel to maintain persistence across multiple users
          PowerPoint Template Modification: Adversaries modify PowerPoint template files (potx/potm) to execute malicious code on presentation startup
          Template Protection Bypass: Adversaries use various methods to bypass template protection mechanisms and macro security settings
          Enterprise Template Distribution: Adversaries compromise centrally managed organizational templates to affect multiple users
        Outlook Forms: Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Outlook forms to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Outlook forms are used as templates for presentation and functionality in Outlook messages. Custom Outlook forms can be created that will execute code when a specifically crafted email is sent by an adversary utilizing the same custom Outlook form.(Citation: SensePost Outlook Forms)  Once malicious forms have been added to the user’s mailbox, they will be loaded when Outlook is started. Malicious forms will execute when an adversary sends a specifically crafted email to the user.(Citation: SensePost Outlook Forms)
