      Compromise Host Software Binary: Adversaries may modify host software binaries to establish persistent access to systems. Software binaries/executables provide a wide range of system commands or services, programs, and libraries. Common software binaries are SSH clients, FTP clients, email clients, web browsers, and many other user or server applications.  Adversaries may establish persistence though modifications to host software binaries. For example, an adversary may replace or otherwise infect a legitimate application binary (or support files) with a backdoor. Since these binaries may be routinely executed by applications or the user, the adversary can leverage this for persistent access to the host. An adversary may also modify a software binary such as an SSH client in order to persistently collect credentials during logins (i.e., [Modify Authentication Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556)).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)  An adversary may also modify an existing binary by patching in malicious functionality (e.g., IAT Hooking/Entry point patching)(Citation: Unit42 Banking Trojans Hooking 2022) prior to the binary’s legitimate execution. For example, an adversary may modify the entry point of a binary to point to malicious code patched in by the adversary before resuming normal execution flow.(Citation: ESET FontOnLake Analysis 2021)  After modifying a binary, an adversary may attempt to [Impair Defenses](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562) by preventing it from updating (e.g., via the `yum-versionlock` command or `versionlock.list` file in Linux systems that use the yum package manager).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)
        ~Binary Replacement: Replace legitimate binaries with malicious versions while maintaining original functionality
        ~Binary Patching: Modify existing binaries by inserting malicious code or changing execution flow
        ~Support File Modification: Alter configuration files, libraries, or dependencies used by legitimate binaries
        ~SSH Client Modification: Target SSH client binaries to capture credentials and maintain access
        ~Web Browser Modification: Alter browser binaries to establish persistence and collect data
        ~FTP Client Modification: Modify FTP client binaries for credential collection and persistent access
        ~Email Client Modification: Target email client binaries for persistence and data collection
        ~System Utility Modification: Alter common system utilities for persistent access
        ~Update Prevention: Block binary updates to maintain compromised versions
        ~Entry Point Manipulation: Modify binary entry points to execute malicious code before legitimate functionality
        ~DLL/Shared Library Modification: Alter dynamic libraries used by multiple applications
        ~Registry Binary Modification: Target Windows registry-referenced binaries and helpers
        ~Boot Process Binary Modification: Modify binaries involved in system boot sequence
        ~Application Plugin Modification: Alter plugin/extension binaries used by applications
        ~Driver Binary Modification: Compromise device driver binaries for persistence
        ~Code Signing Certificate Abuse: Use stolen certificates to sign modified binaries
        ~Import Table Modification: Alter binary import tables to redirect function calls
        ~Resource Section Modification: Modify binary resource sections to include malicious content
        ~Runtime Patching: Modify binaries during runtime execution
        ~Backup Binary Modification: Alter backup copies of system binaries
        ~Container Image Binary Modification: Modify binaries within container images
        ~Network Service Binary Modification: Target network service daemon binaries
        ~Database Binary Modification: Alter database engine and client binaries
        ~Security Tool Binary Modification: Compromise security software binaries
        ~Firmware Update Binary Modification: Target firmware update utilities
        ~Scripting Engine Binary Modification: Modify script interpreter binaries
        ~Package Manager Binary Modification: Compromise software installation tools
        ~Virtual Machine Binary Modification: Target hypervisor and VM management tools
        ~API Service Binary Modification: Modify API endpoint service binaries
        ~Configuration Tool Binary Modification: Alter system configuration utilities
      Service Registry Permissions Weakness: Windows stores local service configuration information in the Registry under <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services</code>. The information stored under a service's Registry keys can be manipulated to modify a service's execution parameters through tools such as the service controller, sc.exe, [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086), or [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075). Access to Registry keys is controlled through Access Control Lists and permissions. (Citation: MSDN Registry Key Security)  If the permissions for users and groups are not properly set and allow access to the Registry keys for a service, then adversaries can change the service binPath/ImagePath to point to a different executable under their control. When the service starts or is restarted, then the adversary-controlled program will execute, allowing the adversary to gain persistence and/or privilege escalation to the account context the service is set to execute under (local/domain account, SYSTEM, LocalService, or NetworkService).  Adversaries may also alter Registry keys associated with service failure parameters (such as <code>FailureCommand</code>) that may be executed in an elevated context anytime the service fails or is intentionally corrupted.(Citation: TrustedSignal Service Failure)(Citation: Twitter Service Recovery Nov 2017)
        ~Service Binary Path Modification: Modifying the binPath/ImagePath registry value to point to malicious executables
        ~Service Failure Command Exploitation: Manipulating FailureCommand parameters to execute malicious commands on service failure
        ~Service Account Modification: Changing service account registry values to escalate privileges
        ~Service DLL Hijacking: Modifying DLL path registry entries to load malicious libraries
        ~Service Start Parameters Manipulation: Altering service start parameters to include malicious arguments
        ~Recovery Actions Abuse: Modifying service recovery actions to execute arbitrary commands
        ~Service Dependencies Exploitation: Manipulating service dependency registry entries
        ~Service Security Descriptor Modification: Altering service security descriptors to maintain access
        ~Trigger Parameters Abuse: Exploiting service trigger parameters for persistence
        ~Service Group Manipulation: Modifying service group assignments for coordinated execution
        ~Service Start Delay Manipulation: Modifying service start delay parameters to control execution timing
        ~Service State Control Abuse: Manipulating service state control bits to affect service behavior
        ~Service Description Payload Injection: Embedding malicious content in service description fields
        ~Service Tag Manipulation: Modifying service tag parameters to affect load ordering
        ~Service Error Control Exploitation: Manipulating error control settings to prevent service recovery
      Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder: Adversaries may achieve persistence by adding a program to a startup folder or referencing it with a Registry run key. Adding an entry to the "run keys" in the Registry or startup folder will cause the program referenced to be executed when a user logs in. (Citation: Microsoft Run Key) These programs will be executed under the context of the user and will have the account's associated permissions level.  Placing a program within a startup folder will cause that program to execute when a user logs in. There is a startup folder location for individual user accounts as well as a system-wide startup folder that will be checked regardless of which user account logs in.  The startup folder path for the current user is: * <code>C:\Users\[Username]\AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup</code> The startup folder path for all users is: * <code>C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\StartUp</code>  The following run keys are created by default on Windows systems: * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code> * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce</code>  The <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx</code> is also available but is not created by default on Windows Vista and newer. Registry run key entries can reference programs directly or list them as a dependency. (Citation: Microsoft RunOnceEx APR 2018) For example, it is possible to load a DLL at logon using a "Depend" key with RunOnceEx: <code>reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001\Depend /v 1 /d "C:\temp\evil[.]dll"</code> (Citation: Oddvar Moe RunOnceEx Mar 2018)  The following Registry keys can be used to set startup folder items for persistence: * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\User Shell Folders</code> * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\Shell Folders</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\Shell Folders</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\User Shell Folders</code>  The following Registry keys can control automatic startup of services during boot: * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServicesOnce</code> * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServicesOnce</code> * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServices</code> * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServices</code>  Using policy settings to specify startup programs creates corresponding values in either of two Registry keys: * <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\Run</code> * <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\Run</code>  The Winlogon key controls actions that occur when a user logs on to a computer running Windows 7. Most of these actions are under the control of the operating system, but you can also add custom actions here. The <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon\Userinit</code> and <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon\Shell</code> subkeys can automatically launch programs.  Programs listed in the load value of the registry key <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Windows</code> run when any user logs on.  By default, the multistring BootExecute value of the registry key <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager</code> is set to autocheck autochk *. This value causes Windows, at startup, to check the file-system integrity of the hard disks if the system has been shut down abnormally. Adversaries can add other programs or processes to this registry value which will automatically launch at boot.  Adversaries can use these configuration locations to execute malware, such as remote access tools, to maintain persistence through system reboots. Adversaries may also use [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036) to make the Registry entries look as if they are associated with legitimate programs.
        ~User Run Keys: Abuse of HKEY_CURRENT_USER run keys and user-specific startup folders for per-user persistence
        ~System Run Keys: Abuse of HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE run keys and system-wide startup folders for system-wide persistence
        ~RunOnceEx Dependencies: Use of RunOnceEx registry keys to load DLLs and other dependencies at startup
        ~Shell Folder Manipulation: Modification of Shell Folders registry keys to redirect startup folder locations
        ~Boot Services Registration: Abuse of RunServices and RunServicesOnce keys for service-based persistence
        ~Policy-Based Startup: Use of policy-based registry keys to enforce program execution at startup
        ~Winlogon Userinit Abuse: Manipulation of Winlogon registry keys to execute programs during user logon
        ~Session Manager Manipulation: Modification of Session Manager BootExecute values for boot-time execution
      Exclusive Control: Adversaries who successfully compromise a system may attempt to maintain persistence by “closing the door” behind them  – in other words, by preventing other threat actors from initially accessing or maintaining a foothold on the same system.   For example, adversaries may patch a vulnerable, compromised system(Citation: Mandiant-iab-control)(Citation: CERT AT Fortinent Ransomware 2025) to prevent other threat actors from leveraging that vulnerability in the future. They may “close the door” in other ways, such as disabling vulnerable services(Citation: sophos-multiple-attackers), stripping privileges from accounts(Citation: aquasec-postgres-processes), or removing other malware already on the compromised device.(Citation: fsecure-netsky)  Hindering other threat actors may allow an adversary to maintain sole access to a compromised system or network. This prevents the threat actor from needing to compete with or even being removed themselves by other threat actors. It also reduces the “noise” in the environment, lowering the possibility of being caught and evicted by defenders. Finally, in the case of [Resource Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1496), leveraging a compromised device’s full power allows the threat actor to maximize profit.(Citation: sophos-multiple-attackers)
        ~Vulnerability Patching: Adversaries patch known vulnerabilities after exploitation to prevent other actors from using the same entry points
        ~Service Manipulation: Adversaries disable or modify vulnerable services to prevent exploitation by other actors
        ~Account Privilege Modification: Adversaries strip or modify privileges from existing accounts to limit access
        ~Competing Malware Removal: Adversaries identify and remove malware from other threat actors
        ~Network Access Control: Adversaries modify firewall rules or network configurations to restrict unauthorized access
        ~Security Tool Manipulation: Adversaries modify or disable security tools to prevent detection while blocking other threats
        ~Port Closure: Adversaries close unnecessary or vulnerable ports to prevent other actors from gaining access
        ~Authentication Hardening: Adversaries implement stronger authentication mechanisms after compromise to maintain exclusive access
        ~System Hardening: Adversaries apply system hardening measures to block common attack vectors while maintaining their access
        ~Backdoor Protection: Adversaries implement access controls around their own backdoors to prevent discovery or use by others
        ~Resource Locking: Adversaries lock or restrict access to critical system resources to prevent other actors from utilizing them
        ~Process Injection Prevention: Adversaries implement measures to prevent other malware from performing process injection
        ~Active Defense Mechanisms: Adversaries deploy monitoring tools to detect and automatically respond to other threat actors
        ~Infrastructure Reconfiguration: Adversaries modify infrastructure components like DNS or proxy settings to control access paths
        ~Kernel Module Protection: Adversaries implement kernel-level protections to prevent other malicious kernel modules from loading
        ~Persistence Mechanism Protection: Adversaries implement measures to protect their own persistence mechanisms from modification or removal
        ~Recovery Prevention: Adversaries modify system recovery options to prevent other actors from restoring the system to a clean state
        ~Configuration Lockdown: Adversaries implement strict configuration controls to prevent unauthorized changes to their established settings
      PowerShell Profile: Adversaries may gain persistence and elevate privileges in certain situations by abusing [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086) profiles. A PowerShell profile  (<code>profile.ps1</code>) is a script that runs when PowerShell starts and can be used as a logon script to customize user environments. PowerShell supports several profiles depending on the user or host program. For example, there can be different profiles for PowerShell host programs such as the PowerShell console, PowerShell ISE or Visual Studio Code. An administrator can also configure a profile that applies to all users and host programs on the local computer. (Citation: Microsoft About Profiles)   Adversaries may modify these profiles to include arbitrary commands, functions, modules, and/or PowerShell drives to gain persistence. Every time a user opens a PowerShell session the modified script will be executed unless the <code>-NoProfile</code> flag is used when it is launched. (Citation: ESET Turla PowerShell May 2019)   An adversary may also be able to escalate privileges if a script in a PowerShell profile is loaded and executed by an account with higher privileges, such as a domain administrator. (Citation: Wits End and Shady PowerShell Profiles)
        ~Current User Profile Modification: Adversaries modify the current user's PowerShell profile ($PROFILE.CurrentUserCurrentHost) to maintain persistence in the context of the compromised user.
        ~All Users Profile Modification: Adversaries modify the all users PowerShell profile to maintain persistence across all users on the system.
        ~Host-Specific Profile Abuse: Adversaries target specific PowerShell host application profiles (like ISE or VS Code) to maintain persistence when those specific environments are used.
        ~Remote Profile Injection: Adversaries modify PowerShell profiles to load malicious code from remote locations or network shares.
        ~Module Auto-Loading: Adversaries abuse PowerShell profile's module auto-loading functionality to load malicious modules during profile execution.
        ~Profile Inheritance Abuse: Adversaries exploit PowerShell's profile inheritance hierarchy to ensure persistence across multiple profile types and locations.
        ~Conditional Profile Execution: Adversaries implement environment-aware code in profiles that only executes under specific conditions to evade detection.
        ~Profile Backup Persistence: Adversaries maintain copies of malicious profiles in backup locations that get restored through legitimate profile backup/recovery mechanisms.
        ~Cross-Session Profile Sharing: Adversaries modify shared profile components that affect both local and remote PowerShell sessions.
        ~Profile Extension Abuse: Adversaries abuse PowerShell profile extensions and custom profile formats to hide malicious code.
      Launch Daemon: Per Apple’s developer documentation, when macOS and OS X boot up, launchd is run to finish system initialization. This process loads the parameters for each launch-on-demand system-level daemon from the property list (plist) files found in <code>/System/Library/LaunchDaemons</code> and <code>/Library/LaunchDaemons</code> (Citation: AppleDocs Launch Agent Daemons). These LaunchDaemons have property list files which point to the executables that will be launched (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence).   Adversaries may install a new launch daemon that can be configured to execute at startup by using launchd or launchctl to load a plist into the appropriate directories (Citation: OSX Malware Detection). The daemon name may be disguised by using a name from a related operating system or benign software  (Citation: WireLurker). Launch Daemons may be created with administrator privileges, but are executed under root privileges, so an adversary may also use a service to escalate privileges from administrator to root.   The plist file permissions must be root:wheel, but the script or program that it points to has no such requirement. So, it is possible for poor configurations to allow an adversary to modify a current Launch Daemon’s executable and gain persistence or Privilege Escalation.
        ~Create New Launch Daemon: Creating and installing a new malicious Launch Daemon plist file in /Library/LaunchDaemons
        ~Modify Existing Launch Daemon: Modifying the executable path or parameters of an existing legitimate Launch Daemon
        ~Launch Daemon Name Masquerading: Using names that mimic legitimate system or application daemons to avoid detection
        ~Privilege Elevation via Launch Daemon: Exploiting Launch Daemon execution privileges to escalate from admin to root
        ~Launch Daemon Property Manipulation: Modifying Launch Daemon properties like RunAtLoad, KeepAlive, or StartInterval
        ~Launch Daemon Binary Replacement: Replacing the target executable of a legitimate Launch Daemon while maintaining the original plist
        ~Launch Daemon Command Injection: Injecting malicious commands into Launch Daemon program arguments
        ~Cross-Directory Launch Daemon: Exploiting alternative Launch Daemon directories or symbolic links
    privilege-escalation: The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions.  Privilege Escalation consists of techniques that adversaries use to gain higher-level permissions on a system or network. Adversaries can often enter and explore a network with unprivileged access but require elevated permissions to follow through on their objectives. Common approaches are to take advantage of system weaknesses, misconfigurations, and vulnerabilities. Examples of elevated access include:   * SYSTEM/root level * local administrator * user account with admin-like access  * user accounts with access to specific system or perform specific function  These techniques often overlap with Persistence techniques, as OS features that let an adversary persist can execute in an elevated context.
      Sudo: The sudoers file, <code>/etc/sudoers</code>, describes which users can run which commands and from which terminals. This also describes which commands users can run as other users or groups. This provides the idea of least privilege such that users are running in their lowest possible permissions for most of the time and only elevate to other users or permissions as needed, typically by prompting for a password. However, the sudoers file can also specify when to not prompt users for passwords with a line like <code>user1 ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL</code> (Citation: OSX.Dok Malware).   Adversaries can take advantage of these configurations to execute commands as other users or spawn processes with higher privileges. You must have elevated privileges to edit this file though.
        ~Sudo Configuration Modification: Adversaries modify /etc/sudoers to grant additional privileges or remove password requirements
        ~Sudo Version Exploitation: Adversaries exploit known vulnerabilities in specific sudo versions to bypass restrictions
        ~Sudo Token Manipulation: Adversaries reuse or hijack existing sudo tokens to execute commands without re-authentication
        ~Sudo Command Injection: Adversaries exploit poorly configured sudo rules that allow command injection
        ~Sudo Environment Variable Abuse: Adversaries manipulate environment variables that persist through sudo execution
        ~Sudo Log Manipulation: Adversaries modify or delete sudo logs to hide privilege escalation activities
        ~Sudo Plugin Abuse: Adversaries exploit custom sudo plugins or their configuration to gain elevated access
        ~Sudo Timestamp Exploitation: Adversaries manipulate sudo timestamp files to extend or bypass authentication timeouts
      Startup Items: Per Apple’s documentation, startup items execute during the final phase of the boot process and contain shell scripts or other executable files along with configuration information used by the system to determine the execution order for all startup items (Citation: Startup Items). This is technically a deprecated version (superseded by Launch Daemons), and thus the appropriate folder, <code>/Library/StartupItems</code> isn’t guaranteed to exist on the system by default, but does appear to exist by default on macOS Sierra. A startup item is a directory whose executable and configuration property list (plist), <code>StartupParameters.plist</code>, reside in the top-level directory.   An adversary can create the appropriate folders/files in the StartupItems directory to register their own persistence mechanism (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence). Additionally, since StartupItems run during the bootup phase of macOS, they will run as root. If an adversary is able to modify an existing Startup Item, then they will be able to Privilege Escalate as well.
        ~Create New StartupItem: Adversary creates a new directory in /Library/StartupItems with malicious executable and StartupParameters.plist
        ~Modify Existing StartupItem: Adversary modifies existing legitimate startup item's executable or configuration files
        ~System StartupItems: Adversary places malicious code in system-level startup items at /System/Library/StartupItems
        ~User StartupItems: Adversary creates or modifies user-specific startup items in ~/Library/StartupItems
        ~StartupParameters.plist Manipulation: Adversary modifies the execution order or dependencies in StartupParameters.plist
        ~RequiredServices Parameter Abuse: Adversary manipulates the RequiredServices parameter in StartupParameters.plist to ensure execution before security services
        ~Provides Parameter Exploitation: Adversary uses the Provides parameter to masquerade as critical system services
        ~StartupItem Shell Script Injection: Adversary injects malicious code into legitimate startup item shell scripts
        ~Cross-Version StartupItem Abuse: Adversary exploits differences in how various macOS versions handle startup items
        ~Hidden StartupItem Installation: Adversary uses hidden directories or alternate data streams to conceal malicious startup items
      Exploitation for Privilege Escalation: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Security constructs such as permission levels will often hinder access to information and use of certain techniques, so adversaries will likely need to perform privilege escalation to include use of software exploitation to circumvent those restrictions.  When initially gaining access to a system, an adversary may be operating within a lower privileged process which will prevent them from accessing certain resources on the system. Vulnerabilities may exist, usually in operating system components and software commonly running at higher permissions, that can be exploited to gain higher levels of access on the system. This could enable someone to move from unprivileged or user level permissions to SYSTEM or root permissions depending on the component that is vulnerable. This could also enable an adversary to move from a virtualized environment, such as within a virtual machine or container, onto the underlying host. This may be a necessary step for an adversary compromising an endpoint system that has been properly configured and limits other privilege escalation methods.  Adversaries may bring a signed vulnerable driver onto a compromised machine so that they can exploit the vulnerability to execute code in kernel mode. This process is sometimes referred to as Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver (BYOVD).(Citation: ESET InvisiMole June 2020)(Citation: Unit42 AcidBox June 2020) Adversaries may include the vulnerable driver with files delivered during Initial Access or download it to a compromised system via [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105) or [Lateral Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1570).
        ~Kernel Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in the operating system kernel to gain elevated privileges through memory corruption, buffer overflows, or other kernel-level flaws
        ~Driver Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in legitimate signed drivers (BYOVD) or malicious drivers to execute code in kernel mode
        ~Service Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in system services or daemons running with elevated privileges
        ~Container Escape Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities to break out of containerized environments into the host system
        ~Hypervisor Escape Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities to escape from virtual machines to the underlying hypervisor
        ~Application Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in privileged applications to gain elevated access
        ~Race Condition Exploitation: Exploitation of time-of-check to time-of-use vulnerabilities in privileged operations
        ~Memory Protection Bypass: Exploitation of memory protection mechanisms to execute privileged code
      Extra Window Memory Injection: Before creating a window, graphical Windows-based processes must prescribe to or register a windows class, which stipulate appearance and behavior (via windows procedures, which are functions that handle input/output of data). (Citation: Microsoft Window Classes) Registration of new windows classes can include a request for up to 40 bytes of extra window memory (EWM) to be appended to the allocated memory of each instance of that class. This EWM is intended to store data specific to that window and has specific application programming interface (API) functions to set and get its value. (Citation: Microsoft GetWindowLong function) (Citation: Microsoft SetWindowLong function)  Although small, the EWM is large enough to store a 32-bit pointer and is often used to point to a windows procedure. Malware may possibly utilize this memory location in part of an attack chain that includes writing code to shared sections of the process’s memory, placing a pointer to the code in EWM, then invoking execution by returning execution control to the address in the process’s EWM.  Execution granted through EWM injection may take place in the address space of a separate live process. Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), this may allow access to both the target process's memory and possibly elevated privileges. Writing payloads to shared sections also avoids the use of highly monitored API calls such as WriteProcessMemory and CreateRemoteThread. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) More sophisticated malware samples may also potentially bypass protection mechanisms such as data execution prevention (DEP) by triggering a combination of windows procedures and other system functions that will rewrite the malicious payload inside an executable portion of the target process. (Citation: MalwareTech Power Loader Aug 2013) (Citation: WeLiveSecurity Gapz and Redyms Mar 2013)
        ~Shared Section Code Injection: Writing malicious code to shared sections of process memory to avoid detection and monitoring of common API calls
        ~Window Procedure Pointer Manipulation: Placing pointers to malicious code in EWM and manipulating window procedures to execute it
        ~DEP Bypass via Window Procedure Chaining: Chaining multiple window procedures to rewrite payload in executable memory regions
        ~GUI Process Targeting: Specifically targeting graphical processes that handle window messages and user interface elements
        ~EWM Size Extension Abuse: Exploiting the 40-byte EWM allocation to store and execute malicious code pointers
        ~Window Class Registration Hijacking: Intercepting and modifying window class registration to inject malicious code
        ~Message-Triggered Execution: Using window messages to trigger execution of injected code through EWM
        ~Privilege Elevation via Shared Window Memory: Exploiting shared window memory spaces to execute code with elevated privileges
      Parent PID Spoofing: Adversaries may spoof the parent process identifier (PPID) of a new process to evade process-monitoring defenses or to elevate privileges. New processes are typically spawned directly from their parent, or calling, process unless explicitly specified. One way of explicitly assigning the PPID of a new process is via the <code>CreateProcess</code> API call, which supports a parameter that defines the PPID to use.(Citation: DidierStevens SelectMyParent Nov 2009) This functionality is used by Windows features such as User Account Control (UAC) to correctly set the PPID after a requested elevated process is spawned by SYSTEM (typically via <code>svchost.exe</code> or <code>consent.exe</code>) rather than the current user context.(Citation: Microsoft UAC Nov 2018)  Adversaries may abuse these mechanisms to evade defenses, such as those blocking processes spawning directly from Office documents, and analysis targeting unusual/potentially malicious parent-child process relationships, such as spoofing the PPID of [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086)/[Rundll32](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1085) to be <code>explorer.exe</code> rather than an Office document delivered as part of [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193).(Citation: CounterCept PPID Spoofing Dec 2018) This spoofing could be executed via VBA [Scripting](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1064) within a malicious Office document or any code that can perform [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106).(Citation: CTD PPID Spoofing Macro Mar 2019)(Citation: CounterCept PPID Spoofing Dec 2018)  Explicitly assigning the PPID may also enable [Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0004) (given appropriate access rights to the parent process). For example, an adversary in a privileged user context (i.e. administrator) may spawn a new process and assign the parent as a process running as SYSTEM (such as <code>lsass.exe</code>), causing the new process to be elevated via the inherited access token.(Citation: XPNSec PPID Nov 2017)
        ~Windows API Spoofing: Using CreateProcess API and related Windows APIs to explicitly set parent process IDs for new processes
        ~Process Token Manipulation: Combining PPID spoofing with token manipulation to inherit elevated privileges from system processes
        ~UAC Bypass: Exploiting User Account Control mechanisms by spoofing trusted parent processes
        ~Office Document PPID Spoofing: Using VBA macros and scripts within Office documents to perform PPID spoofing
        ~Command Line PPID Spoofing: Using command-line tools and utilities to modify process parentage
        ~System Process Impersonation: Spoofing critical system processes like lsass.exe or svchost.exe as parents
        ~Cross-Session PPID Spoofing: Spoofing parent processes across different Windows sessions
        ~Service Process Spoofing: Impersonating legitimate Windows service processes as parents
      Port Monitors: A port monitor can be set through the  (Citation: AddMonitor) API call to set a DLL to be loaded at startup. (Citation: AddMonitor) This DLL can be located in <code>C:\Windows\System32</code> and will be loaded by the print spooler service, spoolsv.exe, on boot. The spoolsv.exe process also runs under SYSTEM level permissions. (Citation: Bloxham) Alternatively, an arbitrary DLL can be loaded if permissions allow writing a fully-qualified pathname for that DLL to <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Print\Monitors</code>.   The Registry key contains entries for the following:  * Local Port * Standard TCP/IP Port * USB Monitor * WSD Port  Adversaries can use this technique to load malicious code at startup that will persist on system reboot and execute as SYSTEM.
        ~AddMonitor API Abuse: Exploitation of the AddMonitor API to load malicious DLLs through the print spooler service
        ~Registry Key Manipulation: Direct modification of port monitor registry entries to load unauthorized DLLs
        ~Print Spooler Service Hijacking: Targeting the spoolsv.exe process to execute malicious code with SYSTEM privileges
        ~Custom Port Monitor Installation: Creating and registering malicious custom port monitors
        ~Port Monitor DLL Replacement: Replacing legitimate port monitor DLLs with malicious versions
        ~TCP/IP Port Monitor Abuse: Exploiting Standard TCP/IP port monitor configurations for remote code execution
        ~USB Port Monitor Manipulation: Targeting USB printer port monitor configurations for privilege escalation
        ~WSD Port Monitor Exploitation: Leveraging Web Services for Devices (WSD) port monitor for malicious purposes
        ~Local Port Monitor Configuration Abuse: Manipulating local port monitor settings to execute unauthorized code
        ~Port Monitor Configuration File Tampering: Modifying port monitor configuration files to maintain persistence
      Valid Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of existing accounts as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access, network devices, and remote desktop.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW) Compromised credentials may also grant an adversary increased privilege to specific systems or access to restricted areas of the network. Adversaries may choose not to use malware or tools in conjunction with the legitimate access those credentials provide to make it harder to detect their presence.  In some cases, adversaries may abuse inactive accounts: for example, those belonging to individuals who are no longer part of an organization. Using these accounts may allow the adversary to evade detection, as the original account user will not be present to identify any anomalous activity taking place on their account.(Citation: CISA MFA PrintNightmare)  The overlap of permissions for local, domain, and cloud accounts across a network of systems is of concern because the adversary may be able to pivot across accounts and systems to reach a high level of access (i.e., domain or enterprise administrator) to bypass access controls set within the enterprise.(Citation: TechNet Credential Theft)
        Default Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of a default account as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Default accounts are those that are built-into an OS, such as the Guest or Administrator accounts on Windows systems. Default accounts also include default factory/provider set accounts on other types of systems, software, or devices, including the root user account in AWS, the root user account in ESXi, and the default service account in Kubernetes.(Citation: Microsoft Local Accounts Feb 2019)(Citation: AWS Root User)(Citation: Threat Matrix for Kubernetes)  Default accounts are not limited to client machines; rather, they also include accounts that are preset for equipment such as network devices and computer applications, whether they are internal, open source, or commercial. Appliances that come preset with a username and password combination pose a serious threat to organizations that do not change it post installation, as they are easy targets for an adversary. Similarly, adversaries may also utilize publicly disclosed or stolen [Private Keys](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/004) or credential materials to legitimately connect to remote environments via [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021).(Citation: Metasploit SSH Module)  Default accounts may be created on a system after initial setup by connecting or integrating it with another application. For example, when an ESXi server is connected to a vCenter server, a default privileged account called `vpxuser` is created on the ESXi server. If a threat actor is able to compromise this account’s credentials (for example, via [Exploitation for Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1212) on the vCenter host), they will then have access to the ESXi server.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence VMWare ESXi Zero-Day 2023)(Citation: Pentera vCenter Information Disclosure)
          Operating System Default Accounts: Abuse of built-in OS accounts like Administrator, Guest, or root accounts on various operating systems
          Network Device Default Accounts: Exploitation of factory-set credentials on network appliances, routers, and switches
          Cloud Service Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts in cloud environments like AWS root, Azure admin, or GCP service accounts
          Application Default Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials in commercial or open-source applications
          Database Default Accounts: Abuse of default database accounts like 'sa' in SQL Server or 'system' in Oracle
          IoT Device Default Accounts: Exploitation of factory-set credentials on IoT devices and embedded systems
          Container Platform Default Accounts: Abuse of default service accounts in container orchestration platforms like Kubernetes
          Virtual Infrastructure Default Accounts: Exploitation of default accounts in virtualization platforms like ESXi's vpxuser
          Web Application Default Accounts: Abuse of default admin or test accounts in web applications and CMS platforms
          Integration Service Accounts: Exploitation of automatically created default accounts during system integration or federation
          Embedded System Default Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials in industrial control systems (ICS) and SCADA systems
          Development Environment Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts in IDE servers, build systems, and development tools
          Backup System Default Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials in backup software and disaster recovery systems
          Security Tool Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts in security appliances, firewalls, and monitoring tools
          Printer and Peripheral Default Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials on network printers and connected peripherals
          Management Interface Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts in BMC, IPMI, and other management interfaces
          Telecommunications System Default Accounts: Exploitation of default accounts in PBX systems, VoIP platforms, and telephony equipment
          Legacy System Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts in legacy or obsolete systems that remain connected to networks
          Physical Security System Default Accounts: Exploitation of default accounts in access control systems, surveillance cameras, and building automation
          Testing/QA Environment Default Accounts: Abuse of default accounts left in testing, staging, or QA environments
          Remote Access Tool Default Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials in remote access software and VPN appliances
        Local Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of a local account as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Local accounts are those configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration on a single system or service.  Local Accounts may also be abused to elevate privileges and harvest credentials through [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). Password reuse may allow the abuse of local accounts across a set of machines on a network for the purposes of Privilege Escalation and Lateral Movement.
          Use Mimikatz sekurlsa::logonpasswords to extract plaintext passwords from LSASS memory
          Exploit Local Administrator password reuse using tools like CrackMapExec
          Access SAM database through Volume Shadow Copy to extract local account hashes
          Abuse built-in Administrator account (RID 500) after enabling it through net user administrator /active:yes
          Exploit service accounts with autologon credentials stored in registry (HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon)
          Use PowerSploit's Get-UnattendedInstallFile to find credentials in unattended installation files
          Extract credentials from Windows Credential Manager using vaultcmd or cmdkey
          Exploit local account passwords stored in Group Policy Preferences (GPP) files
          Use Pass-the-Hash attacks with local account NTLM hashes
          Abuse local system backup operator accounts for privilege escalation
          Use reg.exe to query HKLM\SAM after gaining SYSTEM privileges
          Exploit local account passwords stored in IIS application pool configurations
          Extract credentials from stored RDP connection files (.rdp)
          Use WDigest registry modification to force plaintext password storage
          Exploit local SQLServer service account privileges
          Access local account details through scheduled task credentials
          Query local security policy for accounts with auto-elevation privileges
          Extract credentials from stored PSExec connections
          Abuse local virtual service accounts (VSAs) for privilege escalation
          Exploit printer operator accounts for privilege escalation via printer driver installation
          Exploit local account credentials cached in third-party backup software
          Access emergency management service accounts used for system recovery
          Extract credentials from stored Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) configurations
          Abuse local account password reset functionality through security questions
          Exploit credentials stored in software deployment tools' local caches
          Access local account tokens from suspended or crashed application dumps
          Extract credentials from local Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio saved connections
          Abuse help desk remote assistance accounts and stored credentials
          Exploit local smart card authentication cached credentials
          Access credentials stored in Windows Hello local authentication data
        Cloud Accounts: Valid accounts in cloud environments may allow adversaries to perform actions to achieve Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion. Cloud accounts are those created and configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration of resources within a cloud service provider or SaaS application. Cloud Accounts can exist solely in the cloud; alternatively, they may be hybrid-joined between on-premises systems and the cloud through syncing or federation with other identity sources such as Windows Active Directory.(Citation: AWS Identity Federation)(Citation: Google Federating GC)(Citation: Microsoft Deploying AD Federation)  Service or user accounts may be targeted by adversaries through [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110), [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), or various other means to gain access to the environment. Federated or synced accounts may be a pathway for the adversary to affect both on-premises systems and cloud environments - for example, by leveraging shared credentials to log onto [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021). High privileged cloud accounts, whether federated, synced, or cloud-only, may also allow pivoting to on-premises environments by leveraging SaaS-based [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) to run commands on hybrid-joined devices.  An adversary may create long lasting [Additional Cloud Credentials](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001) on a compromised cloud account to maintain persistence in the environment. Such credentials may also be used to bypass security controls such as multi-factor authentication.   Cloud accounts may also be able to assume [Temporary Elevated Cloud Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/005) or other privileges through various means within the environment. Misconfigurations in role assignments or role assumption policies may allow an adversary to use these mechanisms to leverage permissions outside the intended scope of the account. Such over privileged accounts may be used to harvest sensitive data from online storage accounts and databases through [Cloud API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/009) or other methods. For example, in Azure environments, adversaries may target Azure Managed Identities, which allow associated Azure resources to request access tokens. By compromising a resource with an attached Managed Identity, such as an Azure VM, adversaries may be able to [Steal Application Access Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528)s to move laterally across the cloud environment.(Citation: SpecterOps Managed Identity 2022)
          Exploit Azure Managed Identity by compromising a VM with attached identity to obtain access tokens for privilege escalation
          Use AWS IAM role assumption chaining to escalate privileges through misconfigured trust relationships
          Exploit Google Cloud Platform service accounts through workload identity federation misconfigurations
          Leverage Azure AD Connect sync account compromise to modify directory synchronization rules
          Exploit misconfigured AWS cross-account role trust policies to assume roles in other accounts
          Use cloud shell/cloud console access to bypass IP-based restrictions and MFA requirements
          Exploit misconfigured SAML federation settings to forge assertions and elevate privileges
          Create persistent privileged service principals in Azure AD through automated application registration
          Leverage AWS instance metadata service to obtain temporary credentials with expanded permissions
          Exploit misconfigured Google Cloud IAM conditions to bypass time-based or resource-based restrictions
          Exploit AWS EC2 instance connect to gain privileged access to instances without SSH keys
          Leverage Azure Automation Accounts with managed identities for privilege escalation
          Abuse GCP Cloud Functions' default service account permissions
          Exploit misconfigured OAuth2 delegated permissions in Microsoft 365 applications
          Use Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM) elevation request automation
          Chain AWS STS token permissions with misconfigured resource-based policies
          Exploit misconfigured Kubernetes service account token mounts in cloud environments
          Leverage cloud provider backup service roles for privilege escalation
          Exploit cloud identity federation through compromised third-party identity providers
          Abuse cloud-managed PKI services to forge privileged credentials
          Exploit cloud load balancer managed identities for privilege escalation
          Abuse cloud database service account permissions through misconfigured connection strings
          Leverage cloud logging service roles to access sensitive audit data and credentials
          Exploit CI/CD pipeline service accounts with overly permissive cloud permissions
          Abuse cloud key management service role assignments for privilege escalation
          Exploit misconfigured cloud DNS service accounts to redirect traffic and capture credentials
          Leverage cloud WAF service roles to intercept and modify traffic
          Abuse cloud storage gateway service accounts for cross-environment access
          Exploit cloud API gateway service roles for privilege escalation
          Leverage misconfigured cloud container registry permissions for supply chain attacks
          Exploit cloud event hub/event grid service identities for privilege escalation
          Abuse cloud security center/security hub service accounts to disable detections
          Leverage cloud machine learning service identities to access sensitive data
          Exploit cloud notification service roles to intercept security alerts
          Abuse cloud queue service identities for cross-account message injection
          Exploit cloud stream analytics service accounts for data access
          Leverage misconfigured cloud CDN service identities for traffic manipulation
          Abuse cloud firewall service accounts to modify security rules
          Exploit cloud batch processing service identities for compute resource access
          Leverage cloud cache service accounts for data extraction
          Exploit cloud integration service connectors to access connected systems
          Abuse marketplace service publisher accounts for malicious app deployment
          Leverage cloud cost management service roles to access billing data and resources
          Exploit cloud site recovery service accounts for cross-region access
          Abuse cloud service broker identities to provision privileged resources
          Exploit cloud migration service accounts to access legacy systems
          Leverage cloud advisor/recommendation service roles to enumerate weaknesses
          Abuse cloud support service accounts to escalate tickets to admin access
          Exploit cloud resource health service identities to trigger automated responses
          Leverage cloud policy service accounts to modify security controls
        Domain Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of a domain account as a means of gaining Initial Access, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, or Defense Evasion.(Citation: TechNet Credential Theft) Domain accounts are those managed by Active Directory Domain Services where access and permissions are configured across systems and services that are part of that domain. Domain accounts can cover users, administrators, and services.(Citation: Microsoft AD Accounts)  Adversaries may compromise domain accounts, some with a high level of privileges, through various means such as [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) or password reuse, allowing access to privileged resources of the domain.
          Use Mimikatz DCSync to extract domain controller account hashes
          Exploit LDAP configuration to perform domain replication
          Utilize Golden Ticket attack by compromising the krbtgt account
          Leverage Silver Ticket attack against specific service accounts
          Exploit Active Directory Integrated DNS for credential access
          Use Kerberoasting against Service Principal Names (SPNs)
          Perform AS-REP Roasting against accounts with "Do not require Kerberos preauthentication"
          Exploit domain trust relationships through SID History injection
          Use BloodHound to map and exploit domain account relationships
          Leverage Pass-the-Hash attacks with domain account NTLM hashes
          Use PowerView/ADFind for domain account enumeration and privilege mapping
          Exploit domain account delegation settings through constrained/unconstrained delegation
          Create Shadow Copies of NTDS.dit for offline credential extraction
          Leverage Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to deploy scheduled tasks with domain credentials
          Use DCOM objects with domain account impersonation
          Exploit Active Directory Certificate Services for domain account privilege escalation
          Perform NTLM relay attacks to capture and reuse domain credentials
          Modify domain account ACLs to establish backdoor access rights
          Exploit domain controller backup files for credential extraction
          Use domain account credentials to establish Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) persistence
          Exploit DSRM (Directory Services Restore Mode) account for domain control
          Use LDAP pass-back attacks to capture domain credentials
          Leverage domain account permissions for DnsAdmins privilege escalation
          Exploit domain controller system services using SeBackupPrivilege
          Perform resource-based constrained delegation attacks
          Use domain accounts to establish Enterprise Security Group persistence
          Exploit domain account permissions through Exchange server privileges
          Leverage domain account MSL (Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool) for credential access
          Use domain controller cloning for credential extraction
          Exploit domain account permissions through ADCS ESC8 attack
          Exploit Azure AD Connect sync accounts for hybrid identity attacks
          Use domain account permissions to manipulate LAPS configurations
          Leverage Print Spooler service for domain account privilege escalation
          Exploit domain account permissions through AdminSDHolder modifications
          Use skeleton key malware for domain-wide credential abuse
          Exploit domain account permissions through MS-EFSRPC protocol
          Leverage domain account permissions for DFS (Distributed File System) abuse
          Use domain accounts to establish persistence through Security Support Provider
          Exploit domain account permissions through Windows Time Service
          Leverage domain account permissions for DPAPI domain backup key extraction
      Web Shell: A Web shell is a Web script that is placed on an openly accessible Web server to allow an adversary to use the Web server as a gateway into a network. A Web shell may provide a set of functions to execute or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the Web server. In addition to a server-side script, a Web shell may have a client interface program that is used to talk to the Web server (see, for example, China Chopper Web shell client). (Citation: Lee 2013)  Web shells may serve as [Redundant Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1108) or as a persistence mechanism in case an adversary's primary access methods are detected and removed.
        ~Command Execution Interface: Web shell that provides command-line access to execute system commands with elevated privileges
        ~File Management Interface: Web shell that enables file system operations with elevated permissions
        ~Database Management: Web shell that provides direct access to database systems with elevated privileges
        ~Network Proxy: Web shell that acts as a proxy to access internal network resources with elevated permissions
        ~Encoded Payload: Web shell that uses encoded or encrypted commands to evade detection while executing privileged operations
        ~Framework-Based: Web shell that exploits web application frameworks to gain elevated access
        ~Memory-Resident: Web shell that operates entirely in memory to avoid disk-based detection
        ~Multi-Stage Deployment: Web shell that uses multiple stages to establish elevated access
        ~API Interface: Web shell that leverages system APIs to perform privileged operations
        ~Scheduled Task Interface: Web shell that creates and manages scheduled tasks with elevated privileges
        ~Service Account Hijacking: Web shell that targets and operates under service account contexts for privilege elevation
        ~Custom Protocol: Web shell that implements custom communication protocols to interact with elevated processes
        ~Registry Manipulation: Web shell that leverages registry modifications for privilege escalation
        ~Token Manipulation: Web shell that performs token stealing or manipulation for elevated access
        ~DLL Injection Interface: Web shell that facilitates DLL injection for privilege escalation
        ~Certificate-Based: Web shell that exploits certificate authorities or PKI for elevated access
        ~Process Injection Interface: Web shell that enables process injection capabilities
        ~Kernel Mode Operations: Web shell that interfaces with kernel-mode operations
        ~Authentication Bypass Interface: Web shell that implements methods to bypass authentication mechanisms
        ~Log Manipulation Interface: Web shell that can modify or delete system logs to hide privileged operations
        ~Named Pipe Interface: Web shell that utilizes named pipes for privileged communications
        ~COM Object Abuse: Web shell that manipulates COM objects for privilege escalation
        ~Driver Interface: Web shell that interacts with device drivers for elevated privileges
        ~Credential Cache Access: Web shell that accesses and exploits cached credentials
        ~Inter-Process Communication: Web shell that exploits IPC mechanisms for privilege escalation
      Image File Execution Options Injection: Image File Execution Options (IFEO) enable a developer to attach a debugger to an application. When a process is created, a debugger present in an application’s IFEO will be prepended to the application’s name, effectively launching the new process under the debugger (e.g., “C:\dbg\ntsd.exe -g  notepad.exe”). (Citation: Microsoft Dev Blog IFEO Mar 2010)  IFEOs can be set directly via the Registry or in Global Flags via the GFlags tool. (Citation: Microsoft GFlags Mar 2017) IFEOs are represented as <code>Debugger</code> values in the Registry under <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE{\Wow6432Node}\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\<executable></code> where <code><executable></code> is the binary on which the debugger is attached. (Citation: Microsoft Dev Blog IFEO Mar 2010)  IFEOs can also enable an arbitrary monitor program to be launched when a specified program silently exits (i.e. is prematurely terminated by itself or a second, non kernel-mode process). (Citation: Microsoft Silent Process Exit NOV 2017) (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018) Similar to debuggers, silent exit monitoring can be enabled through GFlags and/or by directly modifying IEFO and silent process exit Registry values in <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\</code>. (Citation: Microsoft Silent Process Exit NOV 2017) (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018)  An example where the evil.exe process is started when notepad.exe exits: (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018)  * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\notepad.exe" /v GlobalFlag /t REG_DWORD /d 512</code> * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\notepad.exe" /v ReportingMode /t REG_DWORD /d 1</code> * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\notepad.exe" /v MonitorProcess /d "C:\temp\evil.exe"</code>  Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), these values may be abused to obtain persistence and privilege escalation by causing a malicious executable to be loaded and run in the context of separate processes on the computer. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) Installing IFEO mechanisms may also provide Persistence via continuous invocation.  Malware may also use IFEO for Defense Evasion by registering invalid debuggers that redirect and effectively disable various system and security applications. (Citation: FSecure Hupigon) (Citation: Symantec Ushedix June 2008)
        ~Debugger Registry Manipulation: Direct modification of IFEO debugger registry values to attach malicious debuggers to target applications
        ~Global Flags Configuration: Using GFlags tool to set IFEO parameters and attach debuggers
        ~Silent Exit Monitoring: Configuring silent process exit monitoring to execute malicious code when targeted processes terminate
        ~System Binary Targeting: Specifically targeting system binaries and security tools for IFEO manipulation
        ~WOW64 Registry Exploitation: Targeting both 32-bit and 64-bit processes through specific registry paths
        ~Debugger Path Manipulation: Modifying debugger path to point to malicious executables while maintaining legitimate debugger names
        ~Security Application Redirection: Specifically targeting security applications to redirect their execution
        ~Monitor Process Chaining: Configuring multiple monitor processes in sequence for complex execution chains
        ~Application-Specific Flag Manipulation: Targeting specific application behaviors through custom Global Flag configurations
        ~Cross-Process IFEO Inheritance: Exploiting parent-child process relationships in IFEO configurations
      Hooking: Windows processes often leverage application programming interface (API) functions to perform tasks that require reusable system resources. Windows API functions are typically stored in dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) as exported functions.   Hooking involves redirecting calls to these functions and can be implemented via:  * **Hooks procedures**, which intercept and execute designated code in response to events such as messages, keystrokes, and mouse inputs. (Citation: Microsoft Hook Overview) (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) * **Import address table (IAT) hooking**, which use modifications to a process’s IAT, where pointers to imported API functions are stored. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) (Citation: Adlice Software IAT Hooks Oct 2014) (Citation: MWRInfoSecurity Dynamic Hooking 2015) * **Inline hooking**, which overwrites the first bytes in an API function to redirect code flow. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) (Citation: HighTech Bridge Inline Hooking Sept 2011) (Citation: MWRInfoSecurity Dynamic Hooking 2015)  Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), adversaries may use hooking to load and execute malicious code within the context of another process, masking the execution while also allowing access to the process's memory and possibly elevated privileges. Installing hooking mechanisms may also provide Persistence via continuous invocation when the functions are called through normal use.  Malicious hooking mechanisms may also capture API calls that include parameters that reveal user authentication credentials for Credential Access. (Citation: Microsoft TrojanSpy:Win32/Ursnif.gen!I Sept 2017)  Hooking is commonly utilized by [Rootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1014)s to conceal files, processes, Registry keys, and other objects in order to hide malware and associated behaviors. (Citation: Symantec Windows Rootkits)
        ~API Hooks Procedure: Intercepting system events and messages using Windows Hook API (SetWindowsHookEx) to execute malicious code when specific events occur
        ~IAT Hooking: Modifying the Import Address Table to redirect API calls to malicious code by replacing function pointers
        ~Inline Function Hooking: Overwriting the first bytes of legitimate API functions with jump instructions to redirect execution flow
        ~Kernel Callback Function Hooking: Modifying kernel callback tables to intercept system-level events and execute malicious code
        ~SSDT Hooking: Modifying the System Service Descriptor Table to intercept system calls and redirect them to malicious code
        ~Message Hook Chaining: Installing multiple hooks in sequence to intercept and modify communication between applications
        ~Driver Function Hooking: Modifying function pointers in device driver structures to intercept I/O operations
        ~COM Interface Hooking: Intercepting COM object method calls by modifying virtual function tables
        ~Exception Handler Hooking: Modifying exception handler chains to execute malicious code during exception processing
        ~DLL Load Order Hooking: Manipulating DLL search order to load malicious libraries before legitimate ones
        ~Global Input Hooks: Intercepting all keyboard and mouse input across the system by hooking the Windows input subsystem
        ~Shell API Hooking: Intercepting Windows Shell API calls to modify file system and UI operations
        ~Security Package Hooks: Modifying authentication package callbacks to intercept credential validation
        ~RPC Function Hooking: Intercepting Remote Procedure Call functions to modify network communication
        ~Graphics Driver Hooks: Modifying display driver function tables to intercept rendering operations
        ~Registry Callback Hooking: Intercepting registry operations by hooking registry callback routines
        ~File System Minifilter Hooking: Modifying file system filter callbacks to intercept file operations
        ~Network Filter Hooks: Installing network filter hooks to intercept and modify network traffic
        ~Service Control Handler Hooking: Modifying service control handler functions to intercept service state changes
        ~Desktop Window Manager Hooks: Intercepting DWM callbacks to modify window rendering and composition
      Account Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate accounts to maintain and/or elevate access to victim systems. Account manipulation may consist of any action that preserves or modifies adversary access to a compromised account, such as modifying credentials or permission groups.(Citation: FireEye SMOKEDHAM June 2021) These actions could also include account activity designed to subvert security policies, such as performing iterative password updates to bypass password duration policies and preserve the life of compromised credentials.   In order to create or manipulate accounts, the adversary must already have sufficient permissions on systems or the domain. However, account manipulation may also lead to privilege escalation where modifications grant access to additional roles, permissions, or higher-privileged [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).
        Additional Cloud Roles: An adversary may add additional roles or permissions to an adversary-controlled cloud account to maintain persistent access to a tenant. For example, adversaries may update IAM policies in cloud-based environments or add a new global administrator in Office 365 environments.(Citation: AWS IAM Policies and Permissions)(Citation: Google Cloud IAM Policies)(Citation: Microsoft Support O365 Add Another Admin, October 2019)(Citation: Microsoft O365 Admin Roles) With sufficient permissions, a compromised account can gain almost unlimited access to data and settings (including the ability to reset the passwords of other admins).(Citation: Expel AWS Attacker) (Citation: Microsoft O365 Admin Roles)   This account modification may immediately follow [Create Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136) or other malicious account activity. Adversaries may also modify existing [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) that they have compromised. This could lead to privilege escalation, particularly if the roles added allow for lateral movement to additional accounts.  For example, in AWS environments, an adversary with appropriate permissions may be able to use the <code>CreatePolicyVersion</code> API to define a new version of an IAM policy or the <code>AttachUserPolicy</code> API to attach an IAM policy with additional or distinct permissions to a compromised user account.(Citation: Rhino Security Labs AWS Privilege Escalation)  In some cases, adversaries may add roles to adversary-controlled accounts outside the victim cloud tenant. This allows these external accounts to perform actions inside the victim tenant without requiring the adversary to [Create Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136) or modify a victim-owned account.(Citation: Invictus IR DangerDev 2024)
