        Kernel Modules and Extensions: Adversaries may modify the kernel to automatically execute programs on system boot. Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) are pieces of code that can be loaded and unloaded into the kernel upon demand. They extend the functionality of the kernel without the need to reboot the system. For example, one type of module is the device driver, which allows the kernel to access hardware connected to the system.(Citation: Linux Kernel Programming)   When used maliciously, LKMs can be a type of kernel-mode [Rootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1014) that run with the highest operating system privilege (Ring 0).(Citation: Linux Kernel Module Programming Guide) Common features of LKM based rootkits include: hiding itself, selective hiding of files, processes and network activity, as well as log tampering, providing authenticated backdoors, and enabling root access to non-privileged users.(Citation: iDefense Rootkit Overview)  Kernel extensions, also called kext, are used in macOS to load functionality onto a system similar to LKMs for Linux. Since the kernel is responsible for enforcing security and the kernel extensions run as apart of the kernel, kexts are not governed by macOS security policies. Kexts are loaded and unloaded through <code>kextload</code> and <code>kextunload</code> commands. Kexts need to be signed with a developer ID that is granted privileges by Apple allowing it to sign Kernel extensions. Developers without these privileges may still sign kexts but they will not load unless SIP is disabled. If SIP is enabled, the kext signature is verified before being added to the AuxKC.(Citation: System and kernel extensions in macOS)  Since macOS Catalina 10.15, kernel extensions have been deprecated in favor of System Extensions. However, kexts are still allowed as "Legacy System Extensions" since there is no System Extension for Kernel Programming Interfaces.(Citation: Apple Kernel Extension Deprecation)  Adversaries can use LKMs and kexts to conduct [Persistence](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0003) and/or [Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0004) on a system. Examples have been found in the wild, and there are some relevant open source projects as well.(Citation: Volatility Phalanx2)(Citation: CrowdStrike Linux Rootkit)(Citation: GitHub Reptile)(Citation: GitHub Diamorphine)(Citation: RSAC 2015 San Francisco Patrick Wardle)(Citation: Synack Secure Kernel Extension Broken)(Citation: Securelist Ventir)(Citation: Trend Micro Skidmap)
          Linux Kernel Module Loading: Adversaries may load malicious kernel modules through insmod/modprobe to gain elevated privileges and maintain persistence
          MacOS Kext Installation: Adversaries may abuse kextload to install malicious kernel extensions, potentially bypassing System Integrity Protection
          Driver Manipulation: Adversaries may modify or replace legitimate device drivers with malicious ones to gain kernel-level access
          Rootkit Installation: Adversaries may install kernel-mode rootkits that specifically hide processes, files, and network connections
          System Call Table Hooking: Adversaries may modify the system call table through kernel modules to intercept and modify system operations
          Kernel Symbol Table Manipulation: Adversaries may alter kernel symbol tables to redirect kernel functions to malicious code
          Virtual File System Hijacking: Adversaries may use kernel modules to manipulate the VFS layer to hide files and directories
          Network Stack Manipulation: Adversaries may inject modules that modify network stack operations to hide network activity
          Memory Mapped Device Exploitation: Adversaries may use kernel modules to directly access hardware and memory-mapped devices
          Legacy System Extension Abuse: Adversaries may exploit deprecated kernel extension support in modern macOS versions
          Kernel Module Signature Bypass: Adversaries may bypass kernel module signing requirements through various exploits or by disabling signature verification
          Kernel Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may modify kernel parameters through modules to disable security features
          Kernel Debug Interface Abuse: Adversaries may exploit kernel debugging interfaces to load unauthorized modules
          Boot Configuration Modification: Adversaries may modify boot configurations to load malicious kernel modules during early boot
          Inter-Process Communication (IPC) Hijacking: Adversaries may use kernel modules to intercept and modify IPC mechanisms
          Kernel Timer Manipulation: Adversaries may inject modules that manipulate kernel timers to execute privileged code periodically
          Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) Modification: Adversaries may inject modules that modify the HAL to control hardware access
          Kernel Memory Allocation Hijacking: Adversaries may use modules to manipulate kernel memory allocation for privilege escalation
          Secure Boot Bypass: Adversaries may use specially crafted kernel modules to bypass Secure Boot protections
          UEFI Module Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code into UEFI modules that load during kernel initialization
          Hypervisor Interaction: Adversaries may use kernel modules to interact with or compromise hypervisor functionality
          Kernel Patch Protection Bypass: Adversaries may use techniques to bypass kernel patch protection mechanisms like PatchGuard
          Direct Memory Access (DMA) Exploitation: Adversaries may use kernel modules to exploit DMA capabilities for privilege escalation
        Security Support Provider: Adversaries may abuse security support providers (SSPs) to execute DLLs when the system boots. Windows SSP DLLs are loaded into the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. Once loaded into the LSA, SSP DLLs have access to encrypted and plaintext passwords that are stored in Windows, such as any logged-on user's Domain password or smart card PINs.  The SSP configuration is stored in two Registry keys: <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\Security Packages</code> and <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\OSConfig\Security Packages</code>. An adversary may modify these Registry keys to add new SSPs, which will be loaded the next time the system boots, or when the AddSecurityPackage Windows API function is called.(Citation: Graeber 2014)
