        Polymorphic Code: Adversaries may utilize polymorphic code (also known as metamorphic or mutating code) to evade detection. Polymorphic code is a type of software capable of changing its runtime footprint during code execution.(Citation: polymorphic-blackberry) With each execution of the software, the code is mutated into a different version of itself that achieves the same purpose or objective as the original. This functionality enables the malware to evade traditional signature-based defenses, such as antivirus and antimalware tools.(Citation: polymorphic-sentinelone)  Other obfuscation techniques can be used in conjunction with polymorphic code to accomplish the intended effects, including using mutation engines to conduct actions such as [Software Packing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/002), [Command Obfuscation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/010), or [Encrypted/Encoded File](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/013).(Citation: polymorphic-linkedin)(Citation: polymorphic-medium)
          Use metamorphic engines like MetaPHOR to generate different variants of malware code while maintaining functionality
          Implement instruction substitution by replacing operations with equivalent ones (e.g., ADD with SUB of negative value)
          Deploy code permutation techniques to randomly reorder independent instructions
          Use register reassignment to change which registers are used in each iteration
          Implement dead code insertion to add non-functional instructions that change the code signature
          Use control flow randomization to alter the execution path while maintaining logic
          Employ API obfuscation by dynamically resolving Windows API calls at runtime
          Use self-modifying code techniques to alter the code during execution
          Implement garbage code insertion with conditional jumps that never execute
          Use polymorphic XOR encryption with randomly generated keys for code sections
          Use code transposition to randomly reorganize code blocks with jump instructions
          Implement polymorphic engine frameworks like NGVCK (Next Generation Virus Creation Kit)
          Use multi-layer encryption with varying algorithms and keys per instance
          Implement polymorphic shellcode generation using tools like Hyperion
          Use runtime packers with polymorphic unpacking routines
          Implement code virtualization to translate native code into custom bytecode
          Use polymorphic encoders like Shikata Ga Nai in Metasploit
          Implement instruction splitting to break single instructions into multiple equivalent ones
          Use polymorphic header generation for executable files
          Implement entropy-based code mutation using statistical analysis
          Use code integration techniques to blend polymorphic code with legitimate system functions
