      Software Packing: Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory.  Utilities used to perform software packing are called packers. Example packers are MPRESS and UPX. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, (Citation: Wikipedia Exe Compression) but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.  Adversaries may use virtual machine software protection as a form of software packing to protect their code. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run. A virtual machine is then called to run this code.(Citation: ESET FinFisher Jan 2018)
        ~Commercial Packers: Use of legitimate commercial packing tools like VMProtect, Themida, or ASProtect to compress and protect executables
        ~Custom Packing Routines: Development and use of proprietary packing algorithms to avoid detection of known packer signatures
        ~Virtual Machine Protection: Implementation of custom virtual machines to interpret protected code
        ~Multi-Layer Packing: Application of multiple packing techniques in sequence to create complex unpacking routines
        ~Polymorphic Packing: Dynamic modification of the unpacking routine with each instance
        ~Runtime Compression: On-the-fly packing and unpacking of code during execution
        ~Anti-Debug Packing: Integration of anti-debugging features within the packing routine
        ~Memory-Only Unpacking: Techniques that unpack code directly into memory without writing to disk
        ~Encrypted Packing: Use of encryption in addition to compression to protect packed code
        ~API Obfuscation Packing: Combination of API hiding techniques with traditional packing
        ~Hardware-Assisted Packing: Leveraging CPU-specific features for unpacking routines
        ~Format-Specific Packing: Specialized packing techniques for specific file formats (PE, ELF, Mach-O)
        ~Resource Section Packing: Selective packing of resource sections while leaving other parts unchanged
        ~Import Table Compression: Specific focus on compressing and obfuscating import tables
        ~Section Splitting: Breaking code into multiple sections before packing to complicate analysis
        ~Environment-Aware Unpacking: Packing routines that check for specific environment conditions before unpacking
        ~Time-Based Packing: Incorporation of time-based triggers in unpacking routines
        ~Network-Dependent Packing: Requiring network resources or remote components for unpacking
        ~Nested Container Packing: Using multiple container formats to wrap packed content
        ~Selective Section Packing: Packing only specific portions of code to maintain some visible functions
        ~Cross-Platform Packing: Techniques specifically designed for multi-platform executable packing
        ~Metamorphic Packing: Packing routines that completely change their structure between iterations
        ~Sandbox-Aware Packing: Packing mechanisms that detect and behave differently in analysis environments
        ~Delta Packing: Packing that only stores changes between versions to minimize signatures
        ~Just-In-Time Packing: Dynamic packing of code just before execution
        ~Component-Based Packing: Breaking executable into components that are packed separately
        ~Context-Dependent Packing: Packing that varies based on system context or configuration
        ~Fileless Packing: Packing techniques that operate entirely in memory without touching disk
        ~Code-Flow Packing: Techniques that specifically obfuscate program flow during unpacking
        ~Staged Packing: Multiple-stage unpacking process with intermediate transformations
      Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: An adversary may attempt to modify a cloud account's compute service infrastructure to evade defenses. A modification to the compute service infrastructure can include the creation, deletion, or modification of one or more components such as compute instances, virtual machines, and snapshots.  Permissions gained from the modification of infrastructure components may bypass restrictions that prevent access to existing infrastructure. Modifying infrastructure components may also allow an adversary to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence.(Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2020)
        Create Snapshot: An adversary may create a snapshot or data backup within a cloud account to evade defenses. A snapshot is a point-in-time copy of an existing cloud compute component such as a virtual machine (VM), virtual hard drive, or volume. An adversary may leverage permissions to create a snapshot in order to bypass restrictions that prevent access to existing compute service infrastructure, unlike in [Revert Cloud Instance](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/004) where an adversary may revert to a snapshot to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence.  An adversary may [Create Cloud Instance](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/002), mount one or more created snapshots to that instance, and then apply a policy that allows the adversary access to the created instance, such as a firewall policy that allows them inbound and outbound SSH access.(Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2020)
          Use AWS EC2 CreateSnapshot API to create EBS volume snapshots and share them across accounts
