      DLL Search Order Hijacking: Windows systems use a common method to look for required DLLs to load into a program. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Search) Adversaries may take advantage of the Windows DLL search order and programs that ambiguously specify DLLs to gain privilege escalation and persistence.   Adversaries may perform DLL preloading, also called binary planting attacks, (Citation: OWASP Binary Planting) by placing a malicious DLL with the same name as an ambiguously specified DLL in a location that Windows searches before the legitimate DLL. Often this location is the current working directory of the program. Remote DLL preloading attacks occur when a program sets its current directory to a remote location such as a Web share before loading a DLL. (Citation: Microsoft 2269637) Adversaries may use this behavior to cause the program to load a malicious DLL.   Adversaries may also directly modify the way a program loads DLLs by replacing an existing DLL or modifying a .manifest or .local redirection file, directory, or junction to cause the program to load a different DLL to maintain persistence or privilege escalation. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Redirection) (Citation: Microsoft Manifests) (Citation: Mandiant Search Order)  If a search order-vulnerable program is configured to run at a higher privilege level, then the adversary-controlled DLL that is loaded will also be executed at the higher level. In this case, the technique could be used for privilege escalation from user to administrator or SYSTEM or from administrator to SYSTEM, depending on the program.  Programs that fall victim to path hijacking may appear to behave normally because malicious DLLs may be configured to also load the legitimate DLLs they were meant to replace.
        ~Current Directory DLL Placement: Placing malicious DLL in the application's current working directory to exploit default search behavior
        ~System Directory Manipulation: Placing malicious DLLs in Windows system directories that are searched before legitimate DLL locations
        ~Application Directory Hijacking: Installing malicious DLLs in the application's installation directory
        ~Network Share DLL Planting: Exploiting remote DLL loading by placing malicious DLLs on network shares
        ~Manifest File Modification: Altering application manifests to redirect DLL loading paths
        ~Local Redirection File Abuse: Creating or modifying .local files to redirect DLL loading
        ~Junction Point Manipulation: Using directory junctions to redirect DLL searches
        ~Known DLL Cache Exploitation: Targeting DLLs that are normally loaded from the Known DLL cache
        ~Side-by-Side Assembly Abuse: Manipulating SxS assemblies to force alternate DLL loading
        ~Application Configuration Exploitation: Modifying application-specific configuration files that influence DLL search paths
        ~Environment Variable Path Manipulation: Modifying PATH or other environment variables to control DLL search paths
        ~DLL Load Time Exploitation: Targeting DLLs during specific application states or load sequences
        ~Dynamic Link Library Dependencies: Exploiting secondary DLL dependencies to chain multiple DLL loads
        ~Application Extension DLL Hijacking: Targeting application plugin or extension loading mechanisms
        ~Portable Application DLL Hijacking: Exploiting portable applications that don't use absolute paths
        ~Third-Party Update Service Abuse: Targeting third-party application update services' DLL loading behavior
        ~Safe DLL Search Mode Bypass: Techniques to circumvent Windows Safe DLL Search Mode
        ~DLL Loading Deferral Abuse: Exploiting delayed DLL loading mechanisms
      Build Image on Host: Adversaries may build a container image directly on a host to bypass defenses that monitor for the retrieval of malicious images from a public registry. A remote <code>build</code> request may be sent to the Docker API that includes a Dockerfile that pulls a vanilla base image, such as alpine, from a public or local registry and then builds a custom image upon it.(Citation: Docker Build Image)  An adversary may take advantage of that <code>build</code> API to build a custom image on the host that includes malware downloaded from their C2 server, and then they may utilize [Deploy Container](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1610) using that custom image.(Citation: Aqua Build Images on Hosts)(Citation: Aqua Security Cloud Native Threat Report June 2021) If the base image is pulled from a public registry, defenses will likely not detect the image as malicious since it’s a vanilla image. If the base image already resides in a local registry, the pull may be considered even less suspicious since the image is already in the environment.
        ~Local Registry Base Image: Using trusted local registry base images to avoid external pulls and detection
        ~Multi-stage Build Obfuscation: Using multi-stage builds to hide malicious components
        ~Build Context Manipulation: Manipulating the build context to include malicious files
        ~Dynamic Malware Injection: Injecting malware during build time through RUN commands
        ~Base Image Layer Modification: Modifying base image layers to include malicious content
        ~Build Cache Abuse: Exploiting build cache to hide malicious components
        ~Environment Variable Injection: Using build arguments and environment variables to inject malicious code
        ~Custom Build Script Execution: Executing malicious scripts during image build process
        ~Layer Compression Manipulation: Using layer compression to hide malicious content
        ~Build Hook Abuse: Exploiting build hooks to include unauthorized components
        ~Build Time Dependency Substitution: Replacing legitimate dependencies with malicious ones during build
        ~Cross-Build Kit Exploitation: Exploiting different build kits (BuildKit, classic builder) to evade detection
        ~Temporary Build Container Abuse: Manipulating intermediate build containers for malicious purposes
        ~Build Network Isolation Bypass: Exploiting network access during build to fetch malicious content
        ~Build Plugin Abuse: Exploiting build plugins and extensions to include unauthorized components
        ~Build Artifact Poisoning: Manipulating intermediate build artifacts to include malicious content
        ~Build Log Manipulation: Modifying build logs to hide malicious activities
        ~Resource Constraint Bypass: Exploiting resource limits during build to hide malicious activities
        ~Build Context Symlink Abuse: Using symbolic links to access unauthorized files during build
      Revert Cloud Instance: An adversary may revert changes made to a cloud instance after they have performed malicious activities in attempt to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence. In highly virtualized environments, such as cloud-based infrastructure, this may be accomplished by restoring virtual machine (VM) or data storage snapshots through the cloud management dashboard or cloud APIs.  Another variation of this technique is to utilize temporary storage attached to the compute instance. Most cloud providers provide various types of storage including persistent, local, and/or ephemeral, with the ephemeral types often reset upon stop/restart of the VM.(Citation: Tech Republic - Restore AWS Snapshots)(Citation: Google - Restore Cloud Snapshot)
        ~VM Snapshot Restoration: Adversaries restore VM snapshots to a previous clean state to remove evidence of compromise while maintaining access
        ~Ephemeral Storage Reset: Adversaries leverage ephemeral storage properties to automatically clear evidence upon instance restart
        ~Cloud Volume Reversion: Adversaries revert cloud storage volumes to previous snapshots to eliminate forensic artifacts
        ~Instance Replacement: Adversaries terminate compromised instances and launch new ones from clean AMIs while maintaining persistence
        ~Backup State Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate cloud backup systems to restore instances to specific clean states
        ~Automated Reversion Scheduling: Adversaries configure automated schedules to regularly revert instances to clean states while maintaining persistence
        ~Cross-Region Snapshot Transfer: Adversaries copy and restore snapshots across regions to obscure origin and evade regional monitoring
        ~Container Image Rollback: Adversaries revert container instances to clean images while preserving malicious configurations
        ~Serverless Function Version Control: Adversaries manipulate function versions and aliases to revert to clean states while maintaining backdoors
        ~Template-Based Recreation: Adversaries use infrastructure-as-code templates to recreate clean instances while preserving malicious configurations
        ~Hybrid Cloud Reversion: Adversaries coordinate reversion across on-premises and cloud environments to maintain consistency while removing evidence
        ~Database Instance Rollback: Adversaries leverage database backup and restore features to revert database instances while preserving access
        ~Load Balancer State Reset: Adversaries manipulate load balancer configurations during instance reversion to maintain access through service discovery
        ~Managed Service Reversion: Adversaries exploit managed service rollback features to revert configurations while maintaining compromised states
        ~Multi-Account Snapshot Chaining: Adversaries chain snapshot operations across multiple accounts to complicate forensics and maintain access
        ~Edge Location Cache Reset: Adversaries manipulate edge location caches and configurations during reversion to maintain distributed access points
        ~API Gateway Version Rollback: Adversaries revert API gateway configurations while preserving malicious routing rules
        ~Service Mesh State Manipulation: Adversaries coordinate service mesh configuration reversions while maintaining compromised service discovery
        ~Cloud Storage Class Migration: Adversaries leverage storage class transitions during reversion to complicate forensics and maintain persistence
        ~Orchestration Platform Rollback: Adversaries manipulate container orchestration platforms to revert while preserving malicious configurations
      Modify Authentication Process: Adversaries may modify authentication mechanisms and processes to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. The authentication process is handled by mechanisms, such as the Local Security Authentication Server (LSASS) process and the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) on Windows, pluggable authentication modules (PAM) on Unix-based systems, and authorization plugins on MacOS systems, responsible for gathering, storing, and validating credentials. By modifying an authentication process, an adversary may be able to authenticate to a service or system without using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).  Adversaries may maliciously modify a part of this process to either reveal credentials or bypass authentication mechanisms. Compromised credentials or access may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access and remote desktop.
        Pluggable Authentication Modules: Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is <code>pam_unix.so</code>, which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code>.(Citation: Apple PAM)(Citation: Man Pam_Unix)(Citation: Red Hat PAM)  Adversaries may modify components of the PAM system to create backdoors. PAM components, such as <code>pam_unix.so</code>, can be patched to accept arbitrary adversary supplied values as legitimate credentials.(Citation: PAM Backdoor)  Malicious modifications to the PAM system may also be abused to steal credentials. Adversaries may infect PAM resources with code to harvest user credentials, since the values exchanged with PAM components may be plain-text since PAM does not store passwords.(Citation: PAM Creds)(Citation: Apple PAM)
