          Use Regasm to register and execute malicious COM objects remotely
          Use InstallUtil to register Regsvcs/Regasm assemblies indirectly
          Implement custom error handlers in COM assemblies to hide execution failures
          Use Assembly.Load() within Regsvcs/Regasm execution for dynamic loading
          Create decoy COM registration while executing malicious code
          Leverage .NET framework version differences for compatibility evasion
          Use strong-named assemblies with Regsvcs/Regasm for enhanced legitimacy
          Implement delayed execution through COM registration callbacks
          Use manifest-free assemblies to avoid detection
          Chain multiple COM registrations for redundant execution paths
          Implement registry manipulation during COM registration process
          Use alternative paths like Windows Side-by-Side (WinSxS) for Regsvcs/Regasm execution
          Implement custom logging to track registration success while hiding malicious activity
          Leverage Global Assembly Cache (GAC) for persistent COM registration
          Execute through Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) subscription events
          Use command-line switches like /silent and /codebase for stealthy execution
          Implement COM registration through scheduled tasks
          Use assembly binding redirects to target specific .NET versions
          Create self-removing assemblies after COM registration
          Implement DLL search order hijacking with Regsvcs dependencies
          Use registration-free COM activation to avoid registry entries
          Use DCOM for remote Regsvcs/Regasm execution across network
          Implement COM+ catalog manipulation during registration
          Use application domains for isolated execution within Regsvcs process
          Create COM+ partitions to separate malicious components
          Leverage ClickOnce deployment for Regsvcs/Regasm payload delivery
          Implement custom marshaling for COM object communication
