      Password Filter DLL: Windows password filters are password policy enforcement mechanisms for both domain and local accounts. Filters are implemented as dynamic link libraries (DLLs) containing a method to validate potential passwords against password policies. Filter DLLs can be positioned on local computers for local accounts and/or domain controllers for domain accounts.  Before registering new passwords in the Security Accounts Manager (SAM), the Local Security Authority (LSA) requests validation from each registered filter. Any potential changes cannot take effect until every registered filter acknowledges validation.  Adversaries can register malicious password filters to harvest credentials from local computers and/or entire domains. To perform proper validation, filters must receive plain-text credentials from the LSA. A malicious password filter would receive these plain-text credentials every time a password request is made. (Citation: Carnal Ownage Password Filters Sept 2013)
        ~Domain Controller Deployment: Installing malicious password filters on domain controllers to intercept domain-wide password changes
        ~Local Machine Deployment: Installing malicious password filters on individual workstations to capture local account passwords
        ~Custom DLL Implementation: Creating custom password filter DLLs that mimic legitimate password policy enforcement while secretly harvesting credentials
        ~Filter Chain Injection: Inserting malicious filters into existing password filter chains without replacing existing filters
        ~Memory-Based Exfiltration: Using memory-based techniques to store and extract captured credentials from the password filter
        ~Network-Based Exfiltration: Implementing network communication capabilities in the filter to immediately transmit captured credentials
        ~File-Based Exfiltration: Writing captured credentials to hidden files for later collection
        ~Registry Persistence: Using registry modifications to ensure the malicious filter loads on system restart
      Private Keys: Private cryptographic keys and certificates are used for authentication, encryption/decryption, and digital signatures. (Citation: Wikipedia Public Key Crypto)  Adversaries may gather private keys from compromised systems for use in authenticating to [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) like SSH or for use in decrypting other collected files such as email. Common key and certificate file extensions include: .key, .pgp, .gpg, .ppk., .p12, .pem, .pfx, .cer, .p7b, .asc. Adversaries may also look in common key directories, such as <code>~/.ssh</code> for SSH keys on * nix-based systems or <code>C:\Users\(username)\.ssh\</code> on Windows.  Private keys should require a password or passphrase for operation, so an adversary may also use [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056) for keylogging or attempt to [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110) the passphrase off-line.  Adversary tools have been discovered that search compromised systems for file extensions relating to cryptographic keys and certificates. (Citation: Kaspersky Careto) (Citation: Palo Alto Prince of Persia)
        ~SSH Key Extraction: Targeting SSH private keys from user directories and known locations on various operating systems
        ~Code Signing Certificate Theft: Focusing on stealing code signing certificates and their associated private keys
        ~SSL/TLS Certificate Collection: Gathering SSL/TLS private keys from web servers and application configurations
        ~Cloud Service Key Extraction: Targeting cloud service provider credential files and API keys
        ~Smart Card/HSM Key Extraction: Attempting to extract keys from hardware security modules and smart cards
        ~Backup Location Scanning: Searching system and application backups for stored private keys
        ~Memory Dumping for Keys: Extracting private keys from process memory during active use
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Parsing application and service configurations for embedded private keys
        ~Registry Key Extraction: Targeting Windows registry locations containing stored certificates and private keys
        ~Version Control Repository Mining: Searching for accidentally committed private keys in source code repositories
        ~VPN Configuration Extraction: Targeting private keys stored in VPN configurations and connection profiles
        ~Database Credential Store Analysis: Extracting keys from database management systems' credential stores
        ~Mobile Device Certificate Collection: Gathering private keys from mobile device certificate stores and keychain
        ~Email Client Certificate Extraction: Targeting email client certificate stores and S/MIME private keys
        ~Network Device Configuration Mining: Extracting private keys from network device configurations and trusted store
        ~Containerization Platform Keys: Targeting private keys in container orchestration platforms and registries
        ~Identity Provider Certificate Collection: Gathering private keys from identity federation services and SSO systems
        ~Encrypted Volume Key Extraction: Targeting keys used for full disk encryption or encrypted containers
        ~IoT Device Key Extraction: Targeting private keys stored in IoT devices and their management systems
        ~Payment System Certificate Collection: Gathering private keys from payment processing systems and POS terminals
        ~Build System Credential Extraction: Targeting private keys in CI/CD pipelines and build automation systems
        ~Legacy System Key Collection: Extracting keys from legacy systems and protocols that may have weaker protection
        ~Embedded System Key Extraction: Targeting keys stored in firmware and embedded device configurations
        ~Testing Environment Key Collection: Gathering private keys from test environments and staging systems
      Kerberoasting: Service principal names (SPNs) are used to uniquely identify each instance of a Windows service. To enable authentication, Kerberos requires that SPNs be associated with at least one service logon account (an account specifically tasked with running a service (Citation: Microsoft Detecting Kerberoasting Feb 2018)). (Citation: Microsoft SPN) (Citation: Microsoft SetSPN) (Citation: SANS Attacking Kerberos Nov 2014) (Citation: Harmj0y Kerberoast Nov 2016)  Adversaries possessing a valid Kerberos ticket-granting ticket (TGT) may request one or more Kerberos ticket-granting service (TGS) service tickets for any SPN from a domain controller (DC). (Citation: Empire InvokeKerberoast Oct 2016) (Citation: AdSecurity Cracking Kerberos Dec 2015) Portions of these tickets may be encrypted with the RC4 algorithm, meaning the Kerberos 5 TGS-REP etype 23 hash of the service account associated with the SPN is used as the private key and is thus vulnerable to offline [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110) attacks that may expose plaintext credentials. (Citation: AdSecurity Cracking Kerberos Dec 2015) (Citation: Empire InvokeKerberoast Oct 2016) (Citation: Harmj0y Kerberoast Nov 2016)  This same attack could be executed using service tickets captured from network traffic. (Citation: AdSecurity Cracking Kerberos Dec 2015)  Cracked hashes may enable Persistence, Privilege Escalation, and  Lateral Movement via access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078). (Citation: SANS Attacking Kerberos Nov 2014)
        ~Service Account Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate service accounts with SPNs to identify potential targets for Kerberoasting attacks
        ~TGS Request Abuse: Adversaries request TGS tickets for discovered SPNs using valid TGT to obtain service ticket hashes
        ~Network Traffic Capture: Adversaries capture Kerberos TGS traffic from the network to extract service tickets for offline cracking
        ~Offline Hash Cracking: Adversaries perform offline brute-force attacks against captured RC4-encrypted service ticket hashes
        ~Targeted Service Account Attack: Adversaries specifically target high-privilege service accounts that are likely to have weak passwords
        ~Automated Ticket Harvesting: Adversaries use automated tools to continuously harvest and queue service tickets for multiple SPNs
        ~RC4 Downgrade: Adversaries force the use of weaker RC4 encryption for service tickets instead of stronger algorithms
        ~Service Account Password Spraying: Adversaries combine Kerberoasting with password spraying against identified service accounts
        ~Distributed Ticket Cracking: Adversaries distribute the cracking of harvested tickets across multiple systems to speed up the process
        ~SPN Manipulation: Adversaries create or modify SPNs to force target accounts to be Kerberoastable
      Multi-Factor Authentication Interception: Adversaries may target multi-factor authentication (MFA) mechanisms, (i.e., smart cards, token generators, etc.) to gain access to credentials that can be used to access systems, services, and network resources. Use of MFA is recommended and provides a higher level of security than usernames and passwords alone, but organizations should be aware of techniques that could be used to intercept and bypass these security mechanisms.   If a smart card is used for multi-factor authentication, then a keylogger will need to be used to obtain the password associated with a smart card during normal use. With both an inserted card and access to the smart card password, an adversary can connect to a network resource using the infected system to proxy the authentication with the inserted hardware token. (Citation: Mandiant M Trends 2011)  Adversaries may also employ a keylogger to similarly target other hardware tokens, such as RSA SecurID. Capturing token input (including a user's personal identification code) may provide temporary access (i.e. replay the one-time passcode until the next value rollover) as well as possibly enabling adversaries to reliably predict future authentication values (given access to both the algorithm and any seed values used to generate appended temporary codes). (Citation: GCN RSA June 2011)  Other methods of MFA may be intercepted and used by an adversary to authenticate. It is common for one-time codes to be sent via out-of-band communications (email, SMS). If the device and/or service is not secured, then it may be vulnerable to interception. Service providers can also be targeted: for example, an adversary may compromise an SMS messaging service in order to steal MFA codes sent to users’ phones.(Citation: Okta Scatter Swine 2022)
        ~Smart Card Interception: Adversaries may use keyloggers and proxies to intercept smart card authentication, capturing both the physical token presence and associated passwords
        ~Hardware Token Capture: Adversaries may target hardware-based tokens like RSA SecurID to capture PINs and temporary codes
        ~SMS Code Interception: Adversaries may intercept SMS-based MFA codes through compromised telecommunications infrastructure or SS7 vulnerabilities
        ~Email Code Interception: Adversaries may intercept email-based MFA codes through email account compromise or man-in-the-middle attacks
        ~Service Provider Compromise: Adversaries may target MFA service providers directly to steal authentication codes or bypass verification
        ~Token Replay: Adversaries may capture and replay one-time passwords or temporary codes within their validity window
        ~Token Generation Prediction: Adversaries may attempt to predict future token values by analyzing the generation algorithm and seed values
        ~MFA Session Hijacking: Adversaries may intercept and hijack active MFA sessions through man-in-the-middle attacks
        ~Mobile Authenticator Compromise: Adversaries may compromise mobile devices to intercept codes from authentication apps like Google Authenticator or Authy
        ~Browser-Based MFA Interception: Adversaries may use malicious browser extensions or inject code to capture MFA inputs during web authentication
      Cloud Instance Metadata API: Adversaries may attempt to access the Cloud Instance Metadata API to collect credentials and other sensitive data.  Most cloud service providers support a Cloud Instance Metadata API which is a service provided to running virtual instances that allows applications to access information about the running virtual instance. Available information generally includes name, security group, and additional metadata including sensitive data such as credentials and UserData scripts that may contain additional secrets. The Instance Metadata API is provided as a convenience to assist in managing applications and is accessible by anyone who can access the instance.(Citation: AWS Instance Metadata API)  If adversaries have a presence on the running virtual instance, they may query the Instance Metadata API directly to identify credentials that grant access to additional resources. Additionally, attackers may exploit a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability in a public facing web proxy that allows the attacker to gain access to the sensitive information via a request to the Instance Metadata API.(Citation: RedLock Instance Metadata API 2018)  The de facto standard across cloud service providers is to host the Instance Metadata API at <code>http[:]//169.254.169.254</code>.
        ~Direct Instance Query: Adversaries with access to a cloud instance directly query the metadata service endpoint to retrieve credentials and instance information
        ~SSRF Exploitation: Adversaries exploit SSRF vulnerabilities in web applications to make requests to the metadata service from within the cloud environment
        ~Token Extraction: Adversaries specifically target temporary security tokens and access keys stored in instance metadata
        ~IMDSv1 Access: Adversaries exploit the less secure version 1 of Instance Metadata Service that doesn't require token-based authentication
        ~Container Escape: Adversaries exploit container misconfigurations to access host metadata service endpoints
        ~Proxy Service Abuse: Adversaries exploit misconfigured proxy services that don't properly restrict metadata service access
        ~Network Traffic Hijacking: Adversaries use ARP spoofing or similar techniques to intercept metadata service requests
        ~Role Assumption Abuse: Adversaries specifically target instance role credentials to assume higher-privileged roles
        ~Metadata Service Enumeration: Adversaries systematically enumerate all available metadata endpoints to discover sensitive information
        ~Cross-Instance Access: Adversaries exploit misconfigurations to access metadata of other instances from a compromised instance
      Brute Force: Adversaries may use brute force techniques to gain access to accounts when passwords are unknown or when password hashes are obtained.(Citation: TrendMicro Pawn Storm Dec 2020) Without knowledge of the password for an account or set of accounts, an adversary may systematically guess the password using a repetitive or iterative mechanism.(Citation: Dragos Crashoverride 2018) Brute forcing passwords can take place via interaction with a service that will check the validity of those credentials or offline against previously acquired credential data, such as password hashes.  Brute forcing credentials may take place at various points during a breach. For example, adversaries may attempt to brute force access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) within a victim environment leveraging knowledge gathered from other post-compromise behaviors such as [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003), [Account Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1087), or [Password Policy Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1201). Adversaries may also combine brute forcing activity with behaviors such as [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133) as part of Initial Access.
        Password Spraying: Adversaries may use a single or small list of commonly used passwords against many different accounts to attempt to acquire valid account credentials. Password spraying uses one password (e.g. 'Password01'), or a small list of commonly used passwords, that may match the complexity policy of the domain. Logins are attempted with that password against many different accounts on a network to avoid account lockouts that would normally occur when brute forcing a single account with many passwords. (Citation: BlackHillsInfosec Password Spraying)  Typically, management services over commonly used ports are used when password spraying. Commonly targeted services include the following:  * SSH (22/TCP) * Telnet (23/TCP) * FTP (21/TCP) * NetBIOS / SMB / Samba (139/TCP & 445/TCP) * LDAP (389/TCP) * Kerberos (88/TCP) * RDP / Terminal Services (3389/TCP) * HTTP/HTTP Management Services (80/TCP & 443/TCP) * MSSQL (1433/TCP) * Oracle (1521/TCP) * MySQL (3306/TCP) * VNC (5900/TCP)  In addition to management services, adversaries may "target single sign-on (SSO) and cloud-based applications utilizing federated authentication protocols," as well as externally facing email applications, such as Office 365.(Citation: US-CERT TA18-068A 2018)  In default environments, LDAP and Kerberos connection attempts are less likely to trigger events over SMB, which creates Windows "logon failure" event ID 4625.
