      Credentials in Files: Adversaries may search local file systems and remote file shares for files containing passwords. These can be files created by users to store their own credentials, shared credential stores for a group of individuals, configuration files containing passwords for a system or service, or source code/binary files containing embedded passwords.  It is possible to extract passwords from backups or saved virtual machines through [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). (Citation: CG 2014) Passwords may also be obtained from Group Policy Preferences stored on the Windows Domain Controller. (Citation: SRD GPP)  In cloud environments, authenticated user credentials are often stored in local configuration and credential files. In some cases, these files can be copied and reused on another machine or the contents can be read and then used to authenticate without needing to copy any files. (Citation: Specter Ops - Cloud Credential Storage)
        ~Group Policy Preferences Files: Search for and extract credentials stored in Group Policy Preference files on domain controllers
        ~Cloud Service Configuration Files: Target cloud service configuration files like .aws/credentials, azure.json, or gcloud configs
        ~Database Connection Files: Access credentials stored in database configuration files, connection strings, and setup scripts
        ~Application Configuration Files: Extract credentials from web.config, app.config, and similar application configuration files
        ~Source Code and Scripts: Search through source code, scripts, and configuration management files for hardcoded credentials
        ~Browser Password Storage: Access saved credentials from browser password stores and profile directories
        ~Backup Archives: Extract credentials from system backups, disk images, and archived configurations
        ~Registry Export Files: Parse exported registry hives and backup files containing stored credentials
        ~Mobile App Data Files: Extract credentials from mobile application data storage and configuration files
        ~SSH/Certificate Files: Access private keys, certificates, and SSH configuration files containing authentication material
        ~Collaboration Platform Files: Extract credentials from chat logs, team collaboration tools, and messaging platform storage
        ~Virtual Machine Configuration Files: Access credentials stored in VM configuration files, snapshots, and management consoles
        ~Network Device Configs: Extract credentials from network device configuration backups and exported configs
        ~Log and Debug Files: Parse application logs, debug output, and crash dumps for exposed credentials
        ~Office Documents: Search through spreadsheets, documents, and other office files containing stored credentials
        ~Password Manager Files: Access stored credentials in password manager databases and backup files
        ~Container Configuration Files: Extract credentials from container definition files, Docker configs, and orchestration manifests
        ~Email Storage Files: Access credentials stored in email archives, PST files, and local mail storage
        ~IoT Device Storage: Access credentials from IoT device firmware, configuration backups, and local storage
        ~Legacy System Files: Extract credentials from legacy system configuration files and stored procedures
        ~Test Environment Configs: Access credentials in test environment setups, QA configurations, and staging environments
        ~Security Tool Databases: Extract credentials from security tool databases, scan results, and assessment reports
        ~Firmware Images: Access credentials embedded in firmware images, BIOS configurations, and embedded system storage
        ~CI/CD Pipeline Files: Extract credentials from build configurations, deployment scripts, and pipeline definitions
        ~Remote Access Files: Access credentials stored in VPN configurations, remote desktop connection files, and remote access tools
      Modify Authentication Process: Adversaries may modify authentication mechanisms and processes to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. The authentication process is handled by mechanisms, such as the Local Security Authentication Server (LSASS) process and the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) on Windows, pluggable authentication modules (PAM) on Unix-based systems, and authorization plugins on MacOS systems, responsible for gathering, storing, and validating credentials. By modifying an authentication process, an adversary may be able to authenticate to a service or system without using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).  Adversaries may maliciously modify a part of this process to either reveal credentials or bypass authentication mechanisms. Compromised credentials or access may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access and remote desktop.
        Pluggable Authentication Modules: Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is <code>pam_unix.so</code>, which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code>.(Citation: Apple PAM)(Citation: Man Pam_Unix)(Citation: Red Hat PAM)  Adversaries may modify components of the PAM system to create backdoors. PAM components, such as <code>pam_unix.so</code>, can be patched to accept arbitrary adversary supplied values as legitimate credentials.(Citation: PAM Backdoor)  Malicious modifications to the PAM system may also be abused to steal credentials. Adversaries may infect PAM resources with code to harvest user credentials, since the values exchanged with PAM components may be plain-text since PAM does not store passwords.(Citation: PAM Creds)(Citation: Apple PAM)
