        Bash History: Adversaries may search the bash command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials. Bash keeps track of the commands users type on the command-line with the "history" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user’s <code>.bash_history</code> file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: <code>~/.bash_history</code>. Typically, this file keeps track of the user’s last 500 commands. Users often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Adversaries can abuse this by looking through the file for potential credentials. (Citation: External to DA, the OS X Way)
          Use 'cat ~/.bash_history | grep -i "passwd\|password\|user\|username\|login\|credential"' to search for common credential-related terms
          Execute 'find / -name ".bash_history" -type f 2>/dev/null' to locate all bash history files across the system
          Create a custom script to parse .bash_history files and extract commands containing common credential patterns
          Use 'strings ~/.bash_history' to analyze the content of potentially corrupt history files
          Check for credentials in history files of service accounts and system users in addition to regular user accounts
          Monitor real-time bash history updates using 'tail -f ~/.bash_history'
          Search for specific patterns related to cloud service credentials (e.g., AWS keys, API tokens)
          Analyze bash history files from backup locations and archived user directories
          Use tools like "history-tools" to parse and analyze bash history files in bulk
          Extract and decode base64-encoded strings from history files that might contain encoded credentials
          Use 'history | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq' to extract unique commands that might reveal credential patterns
          Check for HISTFILE environment variable modifications that might point to alternate history file locations
          Analyze compressed or rotated history files (e.g., .bash_history.gz, .bash_history.old)
          Parse bash history timestamps when HISTTIMEFORMAT is enabled to correlate credential entries with specific events
          Use 'grep -r "export" ~/.bash_history' to find exported environment variables containing credentials
