      Exploitation for Credential Access: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to collect credentials. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code.   Credentialing and authentication mechanisms may be targeted for exploitation by adversaries as a means to gain access to useful credentials or circumvent the process to gain authenticated access to systems. One example of this is `MS14-068`, which targets Kerberos and can be used to forge Kerberos tickets using domain user permissions.(Citation: Technet MS14-068)(Citation: ADSecurity Detecting Forged Tickets) Another example of this is replay attacks, in which the adversary intercepts data packets sent between parties and then later replays these packets. If services don't properly validate authentication requests, these replayed packets may allow an adversary to impersonate one of the parties and gain unauthorized access or privileges.(Citation: Bugcrowd Replay Attack)(Citation: Comparitech Replay Attack)(Citation: Microsoft Midnight Blizzard Replay Attack)  Such exploitation has been demonstrated in cloud environments as well. For example, adversaries have exploited vulnerabilities in public cloud infrastructure that allowed for unintended authentication token creation and renewal.(Citation: Storm-0558 techniques for unauthorized email access)  Exploitation for credential access may also result in Privilege Escalation depending on the process targeted or credentials obtained.
        ~Kerberos Ticket Forgery: Exploitation of Kerberos authentication system vulnerabilities to forge tickets and gain unauthorized access to resources
        ~Authentication Token Manipulation: Exploiting flaws in token generation, validation, or renewal processes to create or modify authentication tokens
        ~Protocol Replay Attacks: Intercepting and replaying authentication packets to bypass security controls
        ~Cloud Identity Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in cloud identity and access management services
        ~Authentication Bypass: Exploiting implementation flaws to circumvent authentication mechanisms entirely
        ~Session Token Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in session management to hijack or forge session tokens
        ~Certificate Authority Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in certificate authorities or PKI infrastructure
        ~Authentication Protocol Downgrade: Forcing systems to use weaker or vulnerable authentication protocols
        ~Password Hash Extraction: Exploiting vulnerabilities to directly access or extract password hashes
        ~Multi-Factor Authentication Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in MFA implementation to bypass additional authentication factors
        ~Web Authentication Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in web-based authentication mechanisms like SQL injection or parameter manipulation
        ~Directory Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in LDAP, Active Directory, or similar directory services
        ~Credential Cache Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in credential caching mechanisms or stored credentials
        ~SSO Implementation Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Single Sign-On implementations or federation protocols
        ~Password Reset Flow Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in password reset mechanisms or recovery processes
        ~API Authentication Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in API authentication implementations
        ~OAuth Flow Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in OAuth implementation or token handling
        ~SAML Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in SAML protocol implementation or XML processing
        ~Hardware Security Module Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in HSM implementation or communication
        ~Trusted Platform Module Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in TPM-based authentication
        ~Mobile Authentication Bypass: Targeting vulnerabilities specific to mobile authentication mechanisms
        ~Biometric Authentication Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in biometric authentication systems
        ~Smart Card Authentication Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in smart card authentication systems
        ~Embedded System Authentication Bypass: Exploiting authentication vulnerabilities in embedded or IoT devices
      Forced Authentication: Adversaries may gather credential material by invoking or forcing a user to automatically provide authentication information through a mechanism in which they can intercept.  The Server Message Block (SMB) protocol is commonly used in Windows networks for authentication and communication between systems for access to resources and file sharing. When a Windows system attempts to connect to an SMB resource it will automatically attempt to authenticate and send credential information for the current user to the remote system. (Citation: Wikipedia Server Message Block) This behavior is typical in enterprise environments so that users do not need to enter credentials to access network resources.  Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) is also typically used by Windows systems as a backup protocol when SMB is blocked or fails. WebDAV is an extension of HTTP and will typically operate over TCP ports 80 and 443. (Citation: Didier Stevens WebDAV Traffic) (Citation: Microsoft Managing WebDAV Security)  Adversaries may take advantage of this behavior to gain access to user account hashes through forced SMB/WebDAV authentication. An adversary can send an attachment to a user through spearphishing that contains a resource link to an external server controlled by the adversary (i.e. [Template Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1221)), or place a specially crafted file on navigation path for privileged accounts (e.g. .SCF file placed on desktop) or on a publicly accessible share to be accessed by victim(s). When the user's system accesses the untrusted resource it will attempt authentication and send information, including the user's hashed credentials, over SMB to the adversary controlled server. (Citation: GitHub Hashjacking) With access to the credential hash, an adversary can perform off-line [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110) cracking to gain access to plaintext credentials. (Citation: Cylance Redirect to SMB)  There are several different ways this can occur. (Citation: Osanda Stealing NetNTLM Hashes) Some specifics from in-the-wild use include:  * A spearphishing attachment containing a document with a resource that is automatically loaded when the document is opened (i.e. [Template Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1221)). The document can include, for example, a request similar to <code>file[:]//[remote address]/Normal.dotm</code> to trigger the SMB request. (Citation: US-CERT APT Energy Oct 2017) * A modified .LNK or .SCF file with the icon filename pointing to an external reference such as <code>\\[remote address]\pic.png</code> that will force the system to load the resource when the icon is rendered to repeatedly gather credentials. (Citation: US-CERT APT Energy Oct 2017)
        ~SMB Relay Authentication: Forcing authentication through SMB protocol by creating malicious SMB shares or links that trigger automatic authentication attempts
        ~WebDAV Authentication: Exploiting WebDAV protocol to force authentication when SMB is blocked or fails
        ~Template Injection Authentication: Using document templates with external references to force authentication when documents are opened
        ~Shell Link File Authentication: Using modified .LNK files with external icon references to trigger authentication
        ~Shortcut Command File Authentication: Leveraging .SCF files with malicious external references to force credential exposure
        ~UNC Path Injection: Embedding Universal Naming Convention paths in documents or files to trigger automatic authentication
        ~HTML Resource Authentication: Using HTML elements that reference external SMB resources to capture authentication attempts
        ~Office Document Authentication: Exploiting Microsoft Office features that automatically connect to external resources
    discovery: The adversary is trying to figure out your environment.  Discovery consists of techniques an adversary may use to gain knowledge about the system and internal network. These techniques help adversaries observe the environment and orient themselves before deciding how to act. They also allow adversaries to explore what they can control and what’s around their entry point in order to discover how it could benefit their current objective. Native operating system tools are often used toward this post-compromise information-gathering objective.
      Cloud Service Dashboard: An adversary may use a cloud service dashboard GUI with stolen credentials to gain useful information from an operational cloud environment, such as specific services, resources, and features. For example, the GCP Command Center can be used to view all assets, review findings of potential security risks, and run additional queries, such as finding public IP addresses and open ports.(Citation: Google Command Center Dashboard)  Depending on the configuration of the environment, an adversary may be able to enumerate more information via the graphical dashboard than an API. This also allows the adversary to gain information without manually making any API requests.
        ~AWS Console Access: Use of AWS Management Console to discover EC2 instances, S3 buckets, IAM users/roles, and other AWS resources
        ~GCP Console Navigation: Leveraging Google Cloud Console to enumerate projects, services, and security configurations
        ~Azure Portal Reconnaissance: Using Azure Portal to discover virtual machines, storage accounts, and network resources
        ~Oracle Cloud Infrastructure Console: Utilizing OCI Console to identify compute instances, storage, and networking components
        ~Alibaba Cloud Console: Accessing Alibaba Cloud's management console to discover ECS instances and other cloud resources
        ~Kubernetes Dashboard: Using the Kubernetes web UI to discover container deployments, pods, and cluster configurations
        ~Cloud Monitoring Dashboards: Accessing monitoring interfaces like CloudWatch, Stackdriver, or Azure Monitor to gather system metrics and logs
        ~Cloud Security Center: Using security-focused dashboards like AWS Security Hub or Microsoft Defender for Cloud to discover security posture
        ~Multi-Cloud Management Platforms: Leveraging unified cloud management interfaces like CloudHealth or CloudCheckr to discover resources across multiple providers
        ~Service-Specific Consoles: Accessing specialized service dashboards like AWS EKS console, GCP Cloud Run console, or Azure Container Registry interface
      Password Policy Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to access detailed information about the password policy used within an enterprise network or cloud environment. Password policies are a way to enforce complex passwords that are difficult to guess or crack through [Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110). This information may help the adversary to create a list of common passwords and launch dictionary and/or brute force attacks which adheres to the policy (e.g. if the minimum password length should be 8, then not trying passwords such as 'pass123'; not checking for more than 3-4 passwords per account if the lockout is set to 6 as to not lock out accounts).  Password policies can be set and discovered on Windows, Linux, and macOS systems via various command shell utilities such as <code>net accounts (/domain)</code>, <code>Get-ADDefaultDomainPasswordPolicy</code>, <code>chage -l <username></code>, <code>cat /etc/pam.d/common-password</code>, and <code>pwpolicy getaccountpolicies</code> (Citation: Superuser Linux Password Policies) (Citation: Jamf User Password Policies). Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to discover password policy information (e.g. <code>show aaa</code>, <code>show aaa common-criteria policy all</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)  Password policies can be discovered in cloud environments using available APIs such as <code>GetAccountPasswordPolicy</code> in AWS (Citation: AWS GetPasswordPolicy).
        ~Local System Discovery: Adversaries may query local system settings to discover password policies using built-in commands and configuration files specific to the operating system.
        ~Domain Controller Discovery: Adversaries may query domain controllers to obtain domain-wide password policy settings using Active Directory management tools and commands.
        ~Cloud Service Discovery: Adversaries may use cloud service provider APIs and management interfaces to discover password policy settings in cloud environments.
        ~Network Device Discovery: Adversaries may use network device command-line interfaces to discover password policies configured on network infrastructure.
        ~Group Policy Discovery: Adversaries may examine Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to identify password policy settings applied across the domain.
        ~Application Configuration Discovery: Adversaries may examine application configuration files and settings to discover application-specific password policies.
        ~Directory Service Discovery: Adversaries may query non-AD directory services (like LDAP, OpenLDAP) to discover enterprise password policies.
        ~Password Manager Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to identify and examine password management systems to understand enterprise password requirements and policies.
        ~Authentication Service Discovery: Adversaries may probe authentication services (like RADIUS, TACACS+) to discover password policy configurations.
      Query Registry: Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software.  The Registry contains a significant amount of information about the operating system, configuration, software, and security.(Citation: Wikipedia Windows Registry) Information can easily be queried using the [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) utility, though other means to access the Registry exist. Some of the information may help adversaries to further their operation within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.
        ~System Information Enumeration: Query registry keys containing system information, hardware details, and OS configuration
        ~Software Installation Discovery: Query registry keys to identify installed software, versions, and installation paths
        ~Security Configuration Analysis: Query registry keys related to security settings, policies, and configurations
        ~User Profile Enumeration: Query registry keys to gather information about user profiles, settings, and preferences
        ~Service Configuration Discovery: Query registry keys to identify installed services, their configurations, and startup parameters
        ~Network Configuration Analysis: Query registry keys containing network settings, interfaces, and saved connections
        ~Autorun Entry Discovery: Query registry keys to identify programs configured to run automatically at startup
        ~Application Settings Reconnaissance: Query registry keys storing application-specific settings and configurations
        ~Security Product Discovery: Query registry keys to identify installed security products and their configurations
        ~Credential Storage Location Discovery: Query registry keys to locate stored credentials and authentication data
        ~Recent Activity Tracking: Query registry keys containing user activity history, recently opened files, and MRU lists
        ~Peripheral Device History: Query registry keys to identify previously connected USB devices and hardware peripherals
        ~Remote Desktop Configuration Discovery: Query registry keys related to RDP settings, saved connections, and configurations
        ~Windows Feature State Discovery: Query registry keys to identify enabled/disabled Windows features and components
        ~COM Object Registration Discovery: Query registry keys to enumerate registered COM objects and their configurations
        ~Mapped Drive Discovery: Query registry keys to identify network drive mappings and persistent connections
        ~Time Zone Settings Analysis: Query registry keys to determine system time configuration and geographical indicators
        ~System Backup Configuration: Query registry keys related to backup settings and recovery configurations
        ~Windows Update History: Query registry keys containing update history and patch levels
      System Service Discovery: Adversaries may try to gather information about registered local system services. Adversaries may obtain information about services using tools as well as OS utility commands such as <code>sc query</code>, <code>tasklist /svc</code>, <code>systemctl --type=service</code>, and <code>net start</code>.  Adversaries may use the information from [System Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1007) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.
        ~Windows Service Enumeration: Using native Windows commands and APIs to enumerate services, including sc.exe, Get-Service PowerShell cmdlet, and WMI queries
        ~Unix/Linux Service Enumeration: Using systemctl, service, init.d, and related commands to discover services on Unix-like systems
        ~Remote Service Enumeration: Querying service information from remote systems using network protocols and remote administration tools
        ~Registry-Based Service Discovery: Enumerating services by directly querying Windows registry locations like HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services
        ~Process-Service Correlation: Discovering services by analyzing running processes and their relationships to system services
        ~Container Service Discovery: Enumerating services within containerized environments using container-specific tools and commands
        ~Cloud Service Discovery: Identifying and enumerating cloud service endpoints and configurations
        ~Service Configuration Analysis: Analyzing service configuration files and settings to understand service dependencies and capabilities
      File and Directory Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate files and directories or may search in specific locations of a host or network share for certain information within a file system. Adversaries may use the information from [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Many command shell utilities can be used to obtain this information. Examples include <code>dir</code>, <code>tree</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>find</code>, and <code>locate</code>.(Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) Custom tools may also be used to gather file and directory information and interact with the [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106). Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather file and directory information (e.g. <code>dir</code>, <code>show flash</code>, and/or <code>nvram</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)  Some files and directories may require elevated or specific user permissions to access.
        ~Local System Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate files and directories on local systems using built-in commands and utilities to discover potential targets and gather system information.
        ~Network Share Discovery: Adversaries search for and enumerate files and directories on network shares and mapped drives to find sensitive information across the network.
        ~Automated Directory Scanning: Adversaries use automated tools and scripts to systematically scan directories and create file listings for later analysis.
        ~Hidden File Discovery: Adversaries specifically search for hidden files and directories that may contain sensitive information or system configurations.
        ~Configuration File Discovery: Adversaries focus on finding configuration files, registry files, and other system settings files.
        ~User Directory Enumeration: Adversaries specifically target user directories and profiles to find personal or sensitive information.
        ~Application Data Discovery: Adversaries search for application-specific data directories and files containing valuable information.
        ~System Backup Discovery: Adversaries look for system backup files and directories that might contain sensitive historical data.
        ~Cloud Storage Discovery: Adversaries enumerate cloud storage sync directories and cached content on local systems.
        ~Recursive Directory Traversal: Adversaries perform deep recursive scanning of directory structures to map out the complete file system hierarchy.
        ~Network Device Storage Discovery: Adversaries specifically target network device storage like flash memory and NVRAM to enumerate device configurations and firmware.
        ~Permission-Based Enumeration: Adversaries systematically discover files and directories based on access permissions to identify security boundaries and privilege requirements.
        ~Timestamp-Based Discovery: Adversaries enumerate files and directories based on creation, modification, or access times to identify recent activity or changes.
        ~File Extension Filtering: Adversaries systematically search for specific file extensions associated with valuable data types (e.g., .doc, .pdf, .key).
        ~Size-Based Discovery: Adversaries enumerate files based on size thresholds to identify potentially important documents or databases.
        ~Protocol-Specific Enumeration: Adversaries use specific protocols (like SMB, FTP, WebDAV) to discover files and directories through different network protocols and interfaces.
        ~Memory-Mapped File Discovery: Adversaries search for memory-mapped files and related file system structures to identify sensitive data in memory.
        ~Alternate Data Stream Discovery: Adversaries specifically enumerate NTFS alternate data streams to find hidden content and metadata.
        ~Volume Shadow Copy Enumeration: Adversaries discover and enumerate Volume Shadow Copy files to find historical versions of sensitive files.
      Network Sniffing: Adversaries may passively sniff network traffic to capture information about an environment, including authentication material passed over the network. Network sniffing refers to using the network interface on a system to monitor or capture information sent over a wired or wireless connection. An adversary may place a network interface into promiscuous mode to passively access data in transit over the network, or use span ports to capture a larger amount of data.  Data captured via this technique may include user credentials, especially those sent over an insecure, unencrypted protocol. Techniques for name service resolution poisoning, such as [LLMNR/NBT-NS Poisoning and SMB Relay](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/001), can also be used to capture credentials to websites, proxies, and internal systems by redirecting traffic to an adversary.  Network sniffing may reveal configuration details, such as running services, version numbers, and other network characteristics (e.g. IP addresses, hostnames, VLAN IDs) necessary for subsequent [Lateral Movement](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0008) and/or [Defense Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0005) activities. Adversaries may likely also utilize network sniffing during [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557) (AiTM) to passively gain additional knowledge about the environment.  In cloud-based environments, adversaries may still be able to use traffic mirroring services to sniff network traffic from virtual machines. For example, AWS Traffic Mirroring, GCP Packet Mirroring, and Azure vTap allow users to define specified instances to collect traffic from and specified targets to send collected traffic to.(Citation: AWS Traffic Mirroring)(Citation: GCP Packet Mirroring)(Citation: Azure Virtual Network TAP) Often, much of this traffic will be in cleartext due to the use of TLS termination at the load balancer level to reduce the strain of encrypting and decrypting traffic.(Citation: Rhino Security Labs AWS VPC Traffic Mirroring)(Citation: SpecterOps AWS Traffic Mirroring) The adversary can then use exfiltration techniques such as Transfer Data to Cloud Account in order to access the sniffed traffic.(Citation: Rhino Security Labs AWS VPC Traffic Mirroring)  On network devices, adversaries may perform network captures using [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `monitor capture`.(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: capture_embedded_packet_on_software)
        ~Local Interface Sniffing: Adversaries may configure network interfaces in promiscuous mode to capture local network traffic, including unencrypted credentials and network configuration details.
        ~Cloud Traffic Mirroring: Adversaries may leverage cloud platform traffic mirroring services to capture network traffic from virtual machines and cloud resources.
        ~Network Device Capture: Adversaries may use built-in capture capabilities on network devices like routers and switches to collect traffic passing through these devices.
        ~Wireless Network Sniffing: Adversaries may capture wireless network traffic to intercept communications and gather information about wireless networks and connected devices.
        ~Protocol-Specific Monitoring: Adversaries may focus on capturing specific protocol traffic (like HTTP, FTP, SMTP) to gather targeted information about services and credentials.
        ~SPAN Port Capture: Adversaries may utilize Switch Port Analyzer (SPAN) or port mirroring configurations to capture network traffic from multiple sources simultaneously.
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Sniffing: Adversaries may position themselves between network endpoints to intercept and capture traffic through techniques like ARP poisoning or DNS spoofing.
        ~Service Proxy Interception: Adversaries may deploy or compromise network proxies to intercept and capture traffic between clients and services.
        ~Load Balancer Sniffing: Adversaries may target load balancers where TLS termination occurs to capture decrypted traffic before re-encryption.
      Cloud Service Discovery: An adversary may attempt to enumerate the cloud services running on a system after gaining access. These methods can differ from platform-as-a-service (PaaS), to infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS), or software-as-a-service (SaaS). Many services exist throughout the various cloud providers and can include Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD), Lambda Functions, Entra ID, etc. They may also include security services, such as AWS GuardDuty and Microsoft Defender for Cloud, and logging services, such as AWS CloudTrail and Google Cloud Audit Logs.  Adversaries may attempt to discover information about the services enabled throughout the environment. Azure tools and APIs, such as the Microsoft Graph API and Azure Resource Manager API, can enumerate resources and services, including applications, management groups, resources and policy definitions, and their relationships that are accessible by an identity.(Citation: Azure - Resource Manager API)(Citation: Azure AD Graph API)  For example, Stormspotter is an open source tool for enumerating and constructing a graph for Azure resources and services, and Pacu is an open source AWS exploitation framework that supports several methods for discovering cloud services.(Citation: Azure - Stormspotter)(Citation: GitHub Pacu)  Adversaries may use the information gained to shape follow-on behaviors, such as targeting data or credentials from enumerated services or evading identified defenses through [Disable or Modify Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001) or [Disable or Modify Cloud Logs](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/008).
        ~Azure Service Enumeration: Use of Azure-specific APIs and tools like Microsoft Graph API, Azure Resource Manager API, and Stormspotter to discover Azure cloud services, resources, and relationships
        ~AWS Service Enumeration: Use of AWS APIs, CLI tools, and frameworks like Pacu to discover AWS services, Lambda functions, and security configurations
        ~Google Cloud Service Enumeration: Use of Google Cloud APIs, gcloud CLI, and other tools to discover GCP services and resources
        ~Cloud Security Service Discovery: Specifically targeting and enumerating cloud security services like GuardDuty, Microsoft Defender for Cloud, and Cloud Audit Logs
        ~Cloud Storage Service Discovery: Enumeration of storage services like S3 buckets, Azure Blob Storage, and Google Cloud Storage
        ~Cloud Identity Service Discovery: Targeting identity and access management services like AWS IAM, Azure AD, and Google Cloud IAM
        ~Cloud Computing Service Discovery: Enumeration of compute services like EC2, Azure VMs, and Google Compute Engine
        ~Cloud Database Service Discovery: Discovery of database services like RDS, Azure SQL, and Cloud SQL
        ~Cloud Network Service Discovery: Enumeration of network configurations, VPCs, subnets, and routing
        ~Cloud DevOps Service Discovery: Targeting CI/CD pipelines, container registries, and deployment services
        ~Cloud Organization Discovery: Enumeration of organization-wide resources, management groups, and hierarchical structures across cloud environments
        ~Cloud Service Permission Mapping: Discovery of service-to-service permissions, roles, and trust relationships
        ~Cloud Marketplace Service Discovery: Enumeration of third-party services and applications deployed through cloud marketplaces
        ~Automated Cloud Service Scanning: Use of automated tools and scripts for continuous or scheduled service discovery
        ~Cloud Service Configuration Discovery: Detailed enumeration of service configurations, settings, and parameters
        ~Cloud Service Endpoint Discovery: Mapping of service endpoints, APIs, and interconnections
        ~Cloud Service Version Discovery: Identification of service versions, updates, and patch levels
        ~Cross-Cloud Service Discovery: Enumeration of multi-cloud deployments and hybrid cloud services
      Browser Information Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate information about browsers to learn more about compromised environments. Data saved by browsers (such as bookmarks, accounts, and browsing history) may reveal a variety of personal information about users (e.g., banking sites, relationships/interests, social media, etc.) as well as details about internal network resources such as servers, tools/dashboards, or other related infrastructure.(Citation: Kaspersky Autofill)  Browser information may also highlight additional targets after an adversary has access to valid credentials, especially [Credentials In Files](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/001) associated with logins cached by a browser.  Specific storage locations vary based on platform and/or application, but browser information is typically stored in local files and databases (e.g., `%APPDATA%/Google/Chrome`).(Citation: Chrome Roaming Profiles)
        ~Browser Cache Analysis: Adversaries examine browser cache files to extract recently viewed content, images, and web resources that could reveal user activity and internal resources.
        ~Browser Cookie Extraction: Adversaries target browser cookie stores to obtain session tokens, authentication data, and user preferences.
        ~Browser History Enumeration: Adversaries analyze browsing history databases to map user behavior, identify internal resources, and discover potential targets.
        ~Browser Extension Analysis: Adversaries examine installed browser extensions to identify potential security weaknesses and gather information about user tools and services.
        ~Saved Password Extraction: Adversaries target browser password stores to obtain cached login credentials for various services.
        ~Browser Bookmark Analysis: Adversaries examine browser bookmarks to identify frequently accessed resources and internal systems.
        ~Form Data Collection: Adversaries gather autocomplete data and form entries to obtain personal information and commonly used values.
        ~Browser Profile Configuration Analysis: Adversaries examine browser profile settings and configurations to understand security settings and identify potential vulnerabilities.
      Software Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of software and software versions that are installed on a system or in a cloud environment. Adversaries may use the information from [Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Such software may be deployed widely across the environment for configuration management or security reasons, such as [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072), and may allow adversaries broad access to infect devices or move laterally.  Adversaries may attempt to enumerate software for a variety of reasons, such as figuring out what security measures are present or if the compromised system has a version of software that is vulnerable to [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068).
        Security Software Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of security software, configurations, defensive tools, and sensors that are installed on a system or in a cloud environment. This may include things such as cloud monitoring agents and anti-virus. Adversaries may use the information from [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Example commands that can be used to obtain security software information are [netsh](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0108), <code>reg query</code> with [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075), <code>dir</code> with [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106), and [Tasklist](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0057), but other indicators of discovery behavior may be more specific to the type of software or security system the adversary is looking for. It is becoming more common to see macOS malware perform checks for LittleSnitch and KnockKnock software.  Adversaries may also utilize the [Cloud API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/009) to discover cloud-native security software installed on compute infrastructure, such as the AWS CloudWatch agent, Azure VM Agent, and Google Cloud Monitor agent. These agents  may collect  metrics and logs from the VM, which may be centrally aggregated in a cloud-based monitoring platform.
          Use "wmic product get name" to list installed security products on Windows systems
          Query registry keys under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall to identify security software
          Check running processes via "tasklist /svc" to identify active security services
          Use PowerShell Get-MpComputerStatus to check Windows Defender status
          Execute "aws ec2 describe-instances" to identify CloudWatch agent configuration
          Run "az vm extension list" to discover Azure security extensions
          Use "gcloud compute instances describe" to list monitoring agents
          Check for LittleSnitch presence in /Library/Little Snitch on macOS
          Enumerate security tools using System_Profiler on macOS
          Query Windows Security Center through WMI to list security products
          Run "sc query" to list Windows security services and their states
          Use "ps aux | grep" to identify security processes on Linux systems
          Query Docker to list security plugins and runtime protection tools
          Check Kubernetes cluster for security operators and admission controllers
          Use "systemctl list-units" to identify security-related systemd services
          Enumerate ESXi host security settings via PowerCLI
          Check Group Policy Objects (gpresult /r) for security software deployment
          Query WMI for third-party firewall products (root\SecurityCenter2)
          Use osqueryi to enumerate security tools across platforms
          Check launchctl list for security agents on macOS
          Use "netstat -ano" to identify ports used by security monitoring tools
          Query Active Directory for security software distribution groups
          Enumerate SIEM agents and log forwarding configurations
          Check for Mobile Device Management (MDM) profiles and certificates
          Use SNMP walks to discover network security appliances
          Inspect container runtime security policies and AppArmor/SELinux profiles
          Query endpoint DLP agent configurations
          Check for EDR telemetry components in kernel modules
          Enumerate security-related environment variables across systems
          Use PowerShell Get-Service to list third-party security service dependencies
          Query TPM status and security chip configurations
          Enumerate hardware security module (HSM) connections
          Check for security-related browser extensions and policies
          Inspect vulnerability scanner agents and configurations
          Query cloud workload protection platform (CWPP) agents
          Check for application control and whitelisting tools
          Enumerate secure boot configurations and certificates
          Query security compliance and baseline assessment tools
          Inspect software-defined perimeter (SDP) client configurations
          Check for presence of privileged access management (PAM) agents
          Enumerate security token service (STS) configurations
          Query data loss prevention (DLP) policies and rules
          Check for file integrity monitoring (FIM) agents
          Inspect database activity monitoring (DAM) configurations
          Query identity and access management (IAM) agents
          Check for encryption key management service agents
          Enumerate security information management (SIM) collectors
          Query backup software security components
          Check for web application firewall (WAF) agents
          Inspect zero trust network access (ZTNA) client configurations
      Network Share Discovery: Adversaries may look for folders and drives shared on remote systems as a means of identifying sources of information to gather as a precursor for Collection and to identify potential systems of interest for Lateral Movement. Networks often contain shared network drives and folders that enable users to access file directories on various systems across a network.   File sharing over a Windows network occurs over the SMB protocol. (Citation: Wikipedia Shared Resource) (Citation: TechNet Shared Folder) [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) can be used to query a remote system for available shared drives using the <code>net view \\\\remotesystem</code> command. It can also be used to query shared drives on the local system using <code>net share</code>. For macOS, the <code>sharing -l</code> command lists all shared points used for smb services.
        ~Windows Admin Shares: Discovery of default administrative shares (C$, ADMIN$, etc.) using built-in Windows commands and tools
        ~SMB Share Enumeration: Using SMB-specific tools and protocols to discover network shares
        ~NFS Share Discovery: Enumeration of Network File System (NFS) shares on Unix/Linux systems
        ~Cloud Storage Mapping: Discovery of cloud-based shared storage solutions like OneDrive, SharePoint
        ~PowerShell Share Discovery: Using PowerShell cmdlets and scripts to enumerate network shares
        ~Network Mapping Tools: Using specialized tools like SharphoundT or BloodHound for share discovery
        ~Remote Registry Enumeration: Querying remote registry for shared folder information
        ~DFS Share Discovery: Enumeration of Distributed File System shares in Active Directory environments
        ~Cross-Platform Share Discovery: Using cross-platform tools to discover shares across different OS types
        ~Automated Share Scanning: Using automated scripts and tools for bulk share discovery
        ~NetBIOS Discovery: Using NetBIOS protocols and tools to enumerate network shares
        ~FTP Share Discovery: Enumerating FTP servers and shared directories
        ~Enterprise NAS Discovery: Discovering Network Attached Storage devices and their shares
        ~WebDAV Share Discovery: Identifying and enumerating WebDAV shares
        ~iSCSI Target Discovery: Discovering iSCSI storage targets and shared volumes
        ~AFP Share Discovery: Enumerating Apple Filing Protocol shares in Mac environments
        ~Group Policy Share Mapping: Discovering shares through Group Policy configurations
        ~Network Protocol Analysis: Using packet capture tools to identify share access patterns
        ~Virtualization Share Discovery: Enumerating shared storage in virtual environments (VMware, Hyper-V)
        ~Container Storage Discovery: Identifying shared volumes in container orchestration platforms
        ~CIFS Share Discovery: Common Internet File System specific enumeration
        ~Industrial Network Share Discovery: Discovering shares in ICS/SCADA environments
        ~Embedded Systems Share Discovery: Identifying shares on embedded and IoT devices
      System Network Configuration Discovery: Adversaries may look for details about the network configuration and settings, such as IP and/or MAC addresses, of systems they access or through information discovery of remote systems. Several operating system administration utilities exist that can be used to gather this information. Examples include [Arp](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0099), [ipconfig](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0100)/[ifconfig](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0101), [nbtstat](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0102), and [route](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0103).  Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather information about configurations and settings, such as IP addresses of configured interfaces and static/dynamic routes (e.g. <code>show ip route</code>, <code>show ip interface</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: Mandiant APT41 Global Intrusion ) On ESXi, adversaries may leverage esxcli to gather network configuration information. For example, the command `esxcli network nic list` will retrieve the MAC address, while `esxcli network ip interface ipv4 get` will retrieve the local IPv4 address.(Citation: Trellix Rnasomhouse 2024)  Adversaries may use the information from [System Network Configuration Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1016) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including determining certain access within the target network and what actions to do next.
        Internet Connection Discovery: Adversaries may check for Internet connectivity on compromised systems. This may be performed during automated discovery and can be accomplished in numerous ways such as using [Ping](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0097), <code>tracert</code>, and GET requests to websites.  Adversaries may use the results and responses from these requests to determine if the system is capable of communicating with their C2 servers before attempting to connect to them. The results may also be used to identify routes, redirectors, and proxy servers.
          Execute "ping 8.8.8.8" or "ping google.com" to verify external connectivity
          Use tracert/traceroute to map network path to common websites
          Send HTTP GET requests to well-known sites like google.com or microsoft.com
          Query DNS servers using nslookup or dig commands
          Check proxy settings in Internet Explorer/Edge using reg query
          Use PowerShell Invoke-WebRequest to test web connectivity
          Check network interface configurations using ipconfig/ifconfig
          Use curl or wget to test HTTP/HTTPS connectivity
          Execute netstat -n to identify existing internet connections
          Use built-in connection test tools like Test-NetConnection in PowerShell
          Test connectivity to common ports (80, 443, 53) using nc/netcat
          Check cloud service reachability (aws.amazon.com, azure.microsoft.com)
          Use telnet to test specific port connectivity
          Execute whois queries to verify external DNS resolution
          Check proxy auto-config (PAC) file accessibility
          Test ICMP echo requests through specific interfaces
          Use PowerShell Test-Connection with different protocols
          Verify NTP server connectivity for time synchronization
          Check SSL/TLS connectivity using openssl client
          Use socket-based connection tests in Python/PowerShell scripts
          Check VPN interface status using "netsh interface show interface"
          Test bandwidth using speedtest-cli or curl download tests
          Verify outbound SMTP connectivity to detect email restrictions
          Use pathping to test network latency and packet loss
          Check proxy bypass lists using reg query HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings
          Test IPv6 connectivity using ping6 or traceroute6
          Query network location awareness service status
          Check system proxy configuration in /etc/profile or /etc/environment
          Test DNS over HTTPS (DoH) connectivity
          Verify WebSocket connectivity for real-time communications
          Check Windows Network List Manager for network category (Public/Private/Domain)
          Test FTP connectivity using built-in FTP client
          Verify UDP connectivity through common ports (53, 123)
          Query Windows Firewall status and rules affecting connectivity
          Check system hosts file for network routing modifications
          Test connection through SOCKS proxy servers
          Verify IPsec tunnel status and connectivity
          Check network connection sharing status
          Test loopback interface for local network restrictions
          Query network QoS settings and restrictions
        Wi-Fi Discovery: Adversaries may search for information about Wi-Fi networks, such as network names and passwords, on compromised systems. Adversaries may use Wi-Fi information as part of [Account Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1087), [Remote System Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1018), and other discovery or [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0006) activity to support both ongoing and future campaigns.  Adversaries may collect various types of information about Wi-Fi networks from hosts. For example, on Windows names and passwords of all Wi-Fi networks a device has previously connected to may be available through `netsh wlan show profiles` to enumerate Wi-Fi names and then `netsh wlan show profile “Wi-Fi name” key=clear` to show a Wi-Fi network’s corresponding password.(Citation: BleepingComputer Agent Tesla steal wifi passwords)(Citation: Malware Bytes New AgentTesla variant steals WiFi credentials)(Citation: Check Point APT35 CharmPower January 2022) Additionally, names and other details of locally reachable Wi-Fi networks can be discovered using calls to `wlanAPI.dll` [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions.(Citation: Binary Defense Emotes Wi-Fi Spreader)  On Linux, names and passwords of all Wi-Fi-networks a device has previously connected to may be available in files under ` /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/`.(Citation: Wi-Fi Password of All Connected Networks in Windows/Linux) On macOS, the password of a known Wi-Fi may be identified with ` security find-generic-password -wa wifiname` (requires admin username/password).(Citation: Find Wi-Fi Password on Mac)
          Use netsh commands on Windows to list saved Wi-Fi profiles and extract cleartext passwords
          Query wlanAPI.dll functions to enumerate nearby Wi-Fi networks on Windows
          Read NetworkManager configuration files on Linux to extract saved Wi-Fi credentials
          Use security command on macOS to retrieve Wi-Fi passwords from Keychain
          Utilize wireless network scanner tools like inSSIDer or NetSpot to discover nearby networks
          Extract Wi-Fi passwords from browser-saved credentials
          Access Group Policy Preferences on Windows domain-joined machines for Wi-Fi configurations
          Parse Wi-Fi connection logs from system event logs
          Query Windows Registry for wireless network information
          Extract Wi-Fi pre-shared keys from wireless service configuration files
          Extract Wi-Fi configurations from mobile device backups (iOS/Android)
          Query enterprise wireless controllers via SNMP for network information
          Analyze wireless profile XML files in ProgramData\Microsoft\Wlansvc\Profiles
          Parse WPA Supplicant configuration files on Linux/Unix systems
          Use PowerShell scripts to automate Wi-Fi profile extraction
          Dump wireless credentials from LSASS memory
          Extract enterprise Wi-Fi certificates from certificate stores
          Parse wireless settings from mobile device MDM configurations
          Analyze Wi-Fi Direct peer information and connection history
          Query wireless interface driver configurations for hidden networks
          Extract Wi-Fi credentials from IoT device configurations
          Query cloud-managed wireless systems (Meraki, Aruba) via APIs
          Analyze wireless settings in virtual machine configuration files
          Extract Wi-Fi passwords from network configuration backups
          Parse wireless settings from mobile app cached data
          Analyze Wi-Fi configurations in containerized environments
          Extract credentials from wireless debugging logs
          Query embedded device firmware for wireless configurations
      Log Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate system and service logs to find useful data. These logs may highlight various types of valuable insights for an adversary, such as user authentication records ([Account Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1087)), security or vulnerable software ([Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518)), or hosts within a compromised network ([Remote System Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1018)).  Host binaries may be leveraged to collect system logs. Examples include using `wevtutil.exe` or [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) on Windows to access and/or export security event information.(Citation: WithSecure Lazarus-NoPineapple Threat Intel Report 2023)(Citation: Cadet Blizzard emerges as novel threat actor) In cloud environments, adversaries may leverage utilities such as the Azure VM Agent’s `CollectGuestLogs.exe` to collect security logs from cloud hosted infrastructure.(Citation: SIM Swapping and Abuse of the Microsoft Azure Serial Console)  Adversaries may also target centralized logging infrastructure such as SIEMs. Logs may also be bulk exported and sent to adversary-controlled infrastructure for offline analysis.  In addition to gaining a better understanding of the environment, adversaries may also monitor logs in real time to track incident response procedures. This may allow them to adjust their techniques in order to maintain persistence or evade defenses.(Citation: Permiso GUI-Vil 2023)
        ~Windows Event Log Enumeration: Adversaries specifically target Windows Event logs using native tools like wevtutil.exe, PowerShell cmdlets, or WMI queries to gather security, system, and application logs.
        ~Linux System Log Enumeration: Adversaries focus on Linux-specific logs such as /var/log files, syslog, auth.log, and journal logs using tools like journalctl or direct file access.
        ~Cloud Platform Logging: Adversaries target cloud-specific logging mechanisms such as AWS CloudTrail, Azure Monitor logs, or Google Cloud Logging.
        ~SIEM Log Collection: Adversaries specifically target centralized logging infrastructure to access aggregated log data from multiple sources.
        ~Real-time Log Monitoring: Adversaries establish persistent access to log streams for continuous monitoring of system activities and security responses.
        ~Authentication Log Analysis: Adversaries focus specifically on authentication-related logs to gather user credentials and access patterns.
        ~Application-specific Log Collection: Adversaries target logs from specific applications or services like web servers, databases, or email servers.
        ~Network Device Log Enumeration: Adversaries collect and analyze logs from network devices such as firewalls, routers, and switches.
      Container and Resource Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to discover containers and other resources that are available within a containers environment. Other resources may include images, deployments, pods, nodes, and other information such as the status of a cluster.  These resources can be viewed within web applications such as the Kubernetes dashboard or can be queried via the Docker and Kubernetes APIs.(Citation: Docker API)(Citation: Kubernetes API) In Docker, logs may leak information about the environment, such as the environment’s configuration, which services are available, and what cloud provider the victim may be utilizing. The discovery of these resources may inform an adversary’s next steps in the environment, such as how to perform lateral movement and which methods to utilize for execution.
        ~API Enumeration: Adversaries may use Docker and Kubernetes APIs to list containers, pods, services, and other resources
        ~Dashboard Access: Adversaries may access container management dashboards like Kubernetes Dashboard to discover resources
        ~Container Log Analysis: Adversaries may analyze container logs to discover environment details and configurations
        ~Container Registry Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate container registries to discover available images and repositories
        ~Network Service Discovery: Adversaries may scan for container-specific network services and exposed ports
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Adversaries may examine container configuration files to understand the environment
        ~Node Inspection: Adversaries may query node information to understand cluster architecture and resources
        ~Volume Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate mounted volumes and storage resources
        ~Namespace Enumeration: Adversaries may list and analyze Kubernetes namespaces to understand environment segmentation
        ~Service Account Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate service accounts used by containers and pods
        ~Secrets Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate secrets, config maps, and credentials stored in container environments
        ~Orchestration Tool Discovery: Adversaries may discover container orchestration tools and their configurations
        ~Container Metadata Inspection: Adversaries may analyze container metadata to understand relationships and dependencies
        ~Resource Quota Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate resource limits and quotas to understand environment constraints
      Debugger Evasion: Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid debuggers. Debuggers are typically used by defenders to trace and/or analyze the execution of potential malware payloads.(Citation: ProcessHacker Github)  Debugger evasion may include changing behaviors based on the results of the checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a debugged environment. Similar to [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497), if the adversary detects a debugger, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for debugger artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads.  Specific checks will vary based on the target and/or adversary. On Windows, this may involve [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) function calls such as <code>IsDebuggerPresent()</code> and <code> NtQueryInformationProcess()</code>, or manually checking the <code>BeingDebugged</code> flag of the Process Environment Block (PEB). On Linux, this may involve querying `/proc/self/status` for the `TracerPID` field, which indicates whether or not the process is being traced by dynamic analysis tools.(Citation: Cado Security P2PInfect 2023)(Citation: Positive Technologies Hellhounds 2023) Other checks for debugging artifacts may also seek to enumerate hardware breakpoints, interrupt assembly opcodes, time checks, or measurements if exceptions are raised in the current process (assuming a present debugger would “swallow” or handle the potential error).(Citation: hasherezade debug)(Citation: AlKhaser Debug)(Citation: vxunderground debug)  Malware may also leverage Structured Exception Handling (SEH) to detect debuggers by throwing an exception and detecting whether the process is suspended. SEH handles both hardware and software expectations, providing control over the exceptions including support for debugging. If a debugger is present, the program’s control will be transferred to the debugger, and the execution of the code will be suspended. If the debugger is not present, control will be transferred to the SEH handler, which will automatically handle the exception and allow the program’s execution to continue.(Citation: Apriorit)  Adversaries may use the information learned from these debugger checks during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors. Debuggers can also be evaded by detaching the process or flooding debug logs with meaningless data via messages produced by looping [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) function calls such as <code>OutputDebugStringW()</code>.(Citation: wardle evilquest partii)(Citation: Checkpoint Dridex Jan 2021)
        ~API Function Checks: Detection through native API calls like IsDebuggerPresent() and NtQueryInformationProcess() to identify debugger presence
        ~Process Environment Analysis: Checking process-specific flags and structures like PEB BeingDebugged flag or Linux TracerPID
        ~Hardware Breakpoint Detection: Examining debug registers and hardware breakpoint artifacts
        ~Exception-Based Detection: Using SEH and exception handling to determine debugger presence
        ~Timing Analysis: Measuring execution time differences that indicate debugger presence
        ~Debug Output Flooding: Overwhelming debug logs with excessive output to impair analysis
        ~Process Memory Inspection: Scanning for debugger-related memory patterns and artifacts
        ~Debug Flag Manipulation: Modifying debug flags and markers to confuse analysis tools
        ~Anti-Step Execution: Implementing techniques to prevent step-by-step debugging
        ~Debug Port Detection: Checking for open debug ports and communication channels
      Peripheral Device Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to gather information about attached peripheral devices and components connected to a computer system.(Citation: Peripheral Discovery Linux)(Citation: Peripheral Discovery macOS) Peripheral devices could include auxiliary resources that support a variety of functionalities such as keyboards, printers, cameras, smart card readers, or removable storage. The information may be used to enhance their awareness of the system and network environment or may be used for further actions.
        ~USB Device Enumeration: Adversaries may specifically target USB device discovery to identify connected USB peripherals, their capabilities, and potential security weaknesses.
        ~Printer Discovery: Adversaries may focus on discovering network and locally connected printers to identify potential data exfiltration routes or vulnerable devices.
        ~Storage Device Detection: Adversaries may enumerate attached storage devices including external hard drives, USB drives, and card readers to identify potential data sources.
        ~Hardware Interface Scanning: Adversaries may scan various hardware interfaces (PCI, PCIe, USB, etc.) to identify connected peripheral devices and their characteristics.
        ~Driver-based Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate installed device drivers to identify connected peripherals and their capabilities.
        ~Biometric Device Detection: Adversaries may enumerate biometric authentication devices like fingerprint readers or facial recognition cameras to understand security mechanisms.
        ~Network Peripheral Discovery: Adversaries may specifically scan for network-attached peripherals using protocols like SNMP, UPnP, or Bonjour.
        ~Input Device Enumeration: Adversaries may focus on discovering input devices like keyboards, mice, or touchscreens to identify potential keylogging targets.
        ~Media Device Detection: Adversaries may enumerate audio/video devices like webcams, microphones, or speakers for potential surveillance opportunities.
        ~Smart Card Reader Discovery: Adversaries may specifically search for smart card readers to identify potential authentication bypass targets.
      Cloud Storage Object Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate objects in cloud storage infrastructure. Adversaries may use this information during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including requesting all or specific objects from cloud storage.  Similar to [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) on a local host, after identifying available storage services (i.e. [Cloud Infrastructure Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1580)) adversaries may access the contents/objects stored in cloud infrastructure.  Cloud service providers offer APIs allowing users to enumerate objects stored within cloud storage. Examples include ListObjectsV2 in AWS (Citation: ListObjectsV2) and List Blobs in Azure(Citation: List Blobs) .
        ~API-Based Enumeration: Adversaries use cloud provider APIs directly to list objects, including ListObjectsV2 (AWS), List Blobs (Azure), and GCS List Objects (GCP)
        ~CLI Tool Usage: Adversaries leverage command-line interface tools like AWS CLI, Azure CLI, or gsutil to enumerate storage objects
        ~SDK Implementation: Adversaries develop custom scripts using cloud provider SDKs to programmatically discover and list storage objects
        ~Authenticated Enumeration: Adversaries use compromised credentials or stolen access tokens to perform authorized enumeration of storage objects
        ~Public Access Discovery: Adversaries scan for and enumerate publicly accessible storage objects without authentication
      System Location Discovery:  Adversaries may gather information in an attempt to calculate the geographical location of a victim host. Adversaries may use the information from [System Location Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1614) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Adversaries may attempt to infer the location of a system using various system checks, such as time zone, keyboard layout, and/or language settings.(Citation: FBI Ragnar Locker 2020)(Citation: Sophos Geolocation 2016)(Citation: Bleepingcomputer RAT malware 2020) Windows API functions such as <code>GetLocaleInfoW</code> can also be used to determine the locale of the host.(Citation: FBI Ragnar Locker 2020) In cloud environments, an instance's availability zone may also be discovered by accessing the instance metadata service from the instance.(Citation: AWS Instance Identity Documents)(Citation: Microsoft Azure Instance Metadata 2021)  Adversaries may also attempt to infer the location of a victim host using IP addressing, such as via online geolocation IP-lookup services.(Citation: Securelist Trasparent Tribe 2020)(Citation: Sophos Geolocation 2016)
        System Language Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to gather information about the system language of a victim in order to infer the geographical location of that host. This information may be used to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether the adversary infects the target and/or attempts specific actions. This decision may be employed by malware developers and operators to reduce their risk of attracting the attention of specific law enforcement agencies or prosecution/scrutiny from other entities.(Citation: Malware System Language Check)  There are various sources of data an adversary could use to infer system language, such as system defaults and keyboard layouts. Specific checks will vary based on the target and/or adversary, but may involve behaviors such as [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) and calls to [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions.(Citation: CrowdStrike Ryuk January 2019)   For example, on a Windows system adversaries may attempt to infer the language of a system by querying the registry key <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Nls\Language</code> or parsing the outputs of Windows API functions <code>GetUserDefaultUILanguage</code>, <code>GetSystemDefaultUILanguage</code>, <code>GetKeyboardLayoutList</code> and <code>GetUserDefaultLangID</code>.(Citation: Darkside Ransomware Cybereason)(Citation: Securelist JSWorm)(Citation: SecureList SynAck Doppelgänging May 2018)  On a macOS or Linux system, adversaries may query <code>locale</code> to retrieve the value of the <code>$LANG</code> environment variable.
          Windows Registry Language Query: Adversaries query Windows registry keys like HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Nls\Language to determine system language settings
          Windows API Language Functions: Adversaries use Windows API functions such as GetUserDefaultUILanguage and GetSystemDefaultUILanguage to discover language settings
          Linux Environment Variables: Adversaries query environment variables like $LANG and locale settings on Linux systems
          MacOS Language Settings: Adversaries access MacOS system preferences and language configuration files
          Keyboard Layout Analysis: Adversaries examine keyboard layout settings to infer system language and region
          Browser Language Settings: Adversaries inspect browser configurations and accept-language headers
          Regional Format Settings: Adversaries analyze system regional formats including date, time, and number formats
          User Profile Language: Adversaries examine user profile settings for configured languages and locales
      Application Window Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of open application windows. Window listings could convey information about how the system is used.(Citation: Prevailion DarkWatchman 2021) For example, information about application windows could be used identify potential data to collect as well as identifying security tooling ([Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001)) to evade.(Citation: ESET Grandoreiro April 2020)  Adversaries typically abuse system features for this type of enumeration. For example, they may gather information through native system features such as [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059) commands and [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions.
        ~Windows API Enumeration: Uses Windows-specific APIs like EnumWindows() and GetWindowText() to list active windows and their titles
        ~X11 Window Enumeration: Uses X11 window system commands and utilities on Linux systems to list window information
        ~MacOS Window Enumeration: Leverages Apple Script and native MacOS APIs to discover application windows
        ~Command Line Tools: Uses built-in OS commands like 'tasklist' (Windows) or 'ps' (Unix) to list running applications
        ~GUI Automation Tools: Employs automation frameworks to enumerate window properties and states
        ~Background Window Discovery: Specifically targets hidden or minimized windows that might contain sensitive information
        ~Window Property Analysis: Focuses on gathering detailed window attributes beyond just titles (size, position, process owner)
        ~Remote Window Discovery: Attempts to enumerate windows on remote systems through remote administration tools
      Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Adversaries may use several methods to accomplish [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) such as checking for security monitoring tools (e.g., Sysinternals, Wireshark, etc.) or other system artifacts associated with analysis or virtualization. Adversaries may also check for legitimate user activity to help determine if it is in an analysis environment. Additional methods include use of sleep timers or loops within malware code to avoid operating within a temporary sandbox.(Citation: Unit 42 Pirpi July 2015)
        System Checks: Adversaries may employ various system checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Specific checks will vary based on the target and/or adversary, but may involve behaviors such as [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047), [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001), [System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1082), and [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) to obtain system information and search for VME artifacts. Adversaries may search for VME artifacts in memory, processes, file system, hardware, and/or the Registry. Adversaries may use scripting to automate these checks  into one script and then have the program exit if it determines the system to be a virtual environment.   Checks could include generic system properties such as host/domain name and samples of network traffic. Adversaries may also check the network adapters addresses, CPU core count, and available memory/drive size. Once executed, malware may also use [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) to check if it was saved in a folder or file with unexpected or even analysis-related naming artifacts such as `malware`, `sample`, or `hash`.  Other common checks may enumerate services running that are unique to these applications, installed programs on the system, manufacturer/product fields for strings relating to virtual machine applications, and VME-specific hardware/processor instructions.(Citation: McAfee Virtual Jan 2017) In applications like VMWare, adversaries can also use a special I/O port to send commands and receive output.    Hardware checks, such as the presence of the fan, temperature, and audio devices, could also be used to gather evidence that can be indicative a virtual environment. Adversaries may also query for specific readings from these devices.(Citation: Unit 42 OilRig Sept 2018)
          Use WMI queries to check system manufacturer and model for VM-specific strings like "VMware" or "VirtualBox"
          Query registry keys like HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services for VM-related services and drivers
          Check for VM-specific processes like vmtoolsd.exe, VBoxService.exe using Process enumeration
          Examine hardware characteristics through CPUID instruction to detect virtualization features
          Monitor system performance metrics (CPU timing, memory access patterns) for sandbox behavior
          Inspect network adapter MAC addresses for known VM vendor prefixes
          Check for presence and properties of standard hardware devices (sound card, thermal sensors)
          Search for analysis tool artifacts in file system paths and process names
          Use PowerShell Get-WmiObject to enumerate system properties for VM indicators
          Query I/O ports specific to VM platforms (like VMware backdoor I/O port)
          Scan directory paths for analysis-related keywords like "sandbox", "analysis", "malware"
          Check memory for common sandbox debugging/analysis tools signatures
          Measure execution time between instructions to detect debugging
          Compare system uptime with process creation time to detect fresh sandbox instances
          Check screen resolution and display settings for VM default configurations
          Verify presence and count of USB devices and ports
          Examine system language and regional settings for sandbox defaults
          Create automated PowerShell script combining multiple checks with weighted scoring
          Monitor disk size and free space for typical sandbox configurations
          Check for user activity patterns and recently used files to detect inactive sandboxes
          Query SMART disk attributes to detect virtual storage characteristics
          Check for presence and behavior of DMA (Direct Memory Access) capabilities
          Monitor CPU temperature fluctuations for abnormal patterns
          Test graphics card capabilities and acceleration features
          Examine BIOS/UEFI version strings and characteristics
          Check for presence and properties of motherboard sensors
          Verify system bus characteristics and connected devices
          Test for presence of VM-specific instruction set extensions
          Monitor memory page fault patterns for sandbox behavior
          Check ACPI tables for virtualization indicators
          Monitor DNS resolution behavior for sandbox-specific patterns
          Check for presence of common sandbox network restrictions
          Test for debugger-specific API behaviors and breakpoints
          Verify system call patterns for sandbox-specific anomalies
          Check for presence of vendor-specific analysis tools (FireEye, Cuckoo, etc.)
          Monitor inter-process communication patterns
          Test access to privileged system resources
          Check for sandbox-specific memory page permissions
          Verify hardware virtualization extensions availability
          Test for presence of common sandbox communication channels
        Time Based Evasion: Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include enumerating time-based properties, such as uptime or the system clock, as well as the use of timers or other triggers to avoid a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox, specifically those that are automated or only operate for a limited amount of time.  Adversaries may employ various time-based evasions, such as delaying malware functionality upon initial execution using programmatic sleep commands or native system scheduling functionality (ex: [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053)). Delays may also be based on waiting for specific victim conditions to be met (ex: system time, events, etc.) or employ scheduled [Multi-Stage Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1104) to avoid analysis and scrutiny.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Benign commands or other operations may also be used to delay malware execution. Loops or otherwise needless repetitions of commands, such as [Ping](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0097)s, may be used to delay malware execution and potentially exceed time thresholds of automated analysis environments.(Citation: Revil Independence Day)(Citation: Netskope Nitol) Another variation, commonly referred to as API hammering, involves making various calls to [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) functions in order to delay execution (while also potentially overloading analysis environments with junk data).(Citation: Joe Sec Nymaim)(Citation: Joe Sec Trickbot)  Adversaries may also use time as a metric to detect sandboxes and analysis environments, particularly those that attempt to manipulate time mechanisms to simulate longer elapses of time. For example, an adversary may be able to identify a sandbox accelerating time by sampling and calculating the expected value for an environment's timestamp before and after execution of a sleep function.(Citation: ISACA Malware Tricks)
          Use GetTickCount API calls to measure time intervals and detect time acceleration
          Implement Sleep(10000) followed by GetTickCount to compare expected vs actual time elapsed
          Create scheduled tasks that execute malware components at specific future timestamps
          Execute continuous ping commands with -n parameter to create delays
          Implement recursive loops calling GetSystemTime repeatedly to create delays
          Check system uptime using GetTickCount64 to detect newly created VMs
          Use NtDelayExecution API calls in tight loops for API hammering
          Compare multiple time sources (system time, NTP servers, online time services) to detect time manipulation
          Monitor LastBootUpTime WMI property to identify sandbox environments
          Create nested loops of benign arithmetic operations to introduce delays
          Create Windows Event triggers that delay malware execution until specific system events occur
          Use QueryPerformanceCounter for high-precision timing comparisons
          Monitor user idle time through GetLastInputInfo to detect sandbox environments
          Implement delays based on CPU cycle counting using RDTSC instruction
          Create multi-stage payloads with time-gated communication channels
          Check timestamp differences between file system and system clock
          Use WaitForSingleObject with specific timeout values to create precise delays
          Monitor process creation times of common system processes to detect time manipulation
          Implement TCP timing-based checks against external time servers
          Use Windows performance counters to measure actual time intervals
          Compare file modification timestamps across system directories to detect time inconsistencies
          Use memory page access timing measurements to detect debugger presence
          Implement JavaScript timing checks when executing in browser contexts
          Create Windows job objects with time limitations to control execution timing
          Monitor DNS cache TTL values to detect time manipulation
          Use Windows thread quantum timing measurements to detect virtualization
          Compare disk I/O operation timing patterns to baseline measurements
          Implement COM object creation timing checks for environment detection
          Use Windows power state transition timing to detect sandboxes
          Monitor Windows service start time patterns to identify analysis environments
          Compare CPU frequency scaling timing patterns to detect virtualization
          Use kernel driver timing measurements to detect analysis environments
          Monitor GPU operation timing for virtualization detection
          Correlate network adapter timing characteristics with system time
          Compare DLL loading times across multiple processes
          Monitor USB device enumeration timing patterns
          Use ACPI timer measurements for environment detection
          Compare audio subsystem timing characteristics
          Monitor registry key modification timestamp patterns
          Implement cross-process message queue timing measurements
        User Activity Based Checks: Adversaries may employ various user activity checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)  Adversaries may search for user activity on the host based on variables such as the speed/frequency of mouse movements and clicks (Citation: Sans Virtual Jan 2016) , browser history, cache, bookmarks, or number of files in common directories such as home or the desktop. Other methods may rely on specific user interaction with the system before the malicious code is activated, such as waiting for a document to close before activating a macro (Citation: Unit 42 Sofacy Nov 2018) or waiting for a user to double click on an embedded image to activate.(Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017)
          Monitor mouse cursor position changes and movement frequency over time to detect automated environments
          Check browser history size and access timestamps to identify fresh sandbox environments
          Count number of files in Desktop, Downloads, and Documents folders to detect clean VM environments
          Verify existence and age of browser cookies and cache files
          Implement delays that require specific user actions like document closing before executing payload
          Monitor keyboard input frequency and patterns to distinguish human vs automated interaction
          Check for recently opened documents in Office applications
          Verify presence of browser bookmarks and multiple browser profiles
          Wait for specific user interactions like double-clicking or right-clicking before activation
          Analyze timestamps of recently accessed files in user directories to detect system age
          Monitor running applications and their window focus duration to detect real user behavior
          Check system uptime and user login history patterns
          Verify presence and usage patterns of common applications like email clients and messaging apps
          Analyze clipboard history and content for typical user activity
          Check for presence and usage of personalized settings (wallpapers, themes, screen savers)
          Monitor network activity patterns during typical user active hours
          Check for presence of multiple user-created file types (documents, images, downloads)
          Verify existence of user-specific application configurations and preferences
          Monitor system idle time and screen saver activation patterns
          Check for presence of user-installed applications beyond default system apps
          Check for recurring calendar appointments and email patterns in Outlook/email clients
          Monitor USB device connection/disconnection patterns typical of human users
          Analyze printer and scanner usage history
          Check for presence and usage of accessibility features like screen readers or magnifiers
          Monitor audio device usage patterns (microphone, speakers)
          Verify presence of personalized keyboard shortcuts and macros
          Check for presence of user-specific language and regional settings
          Analyze power management patterns (sleep/wake cycles)
          Monitor virtual desktop usage and window arrangement patterns
          Check for presence and usage of cloud storage sync clients
          Check for presence and activity patterns of instant messaging applications
          Monitor media player usage and playlist history
          Verify presence of mobile device sync data (iPhone/Android backups)
          Check for user-configured security settings and preferences
          Monitor gaming platform clients and save data
          Analyze frequency and patterns of system notifications interaction
          Check for presence of password manager usage and vault files
          Monitor video conferencing tool usage patterns
          Analyze VPN client connection patterns and configurations
          Check for presence of personal finance software and usage patterns
      System Owner/User Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system. They may do this, for example, by retrieving account usernames or by using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). The information may be collected in a number of different ways using other Discovery techniques, because user and username details are prevalent throughout a system and include running process ownership, file/directory ownership, session information, and system logs. Adversaries may use the information from [System Owner/User Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1033) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Various utilities and commands may acquire this information, including <code>whoami</code>. In macOS and Linux, the currently logged in user can be identified with <code>w</code> and <code>who</code>. On macOS the <code>dscl . list /Users | grep -v '_'</code> command can also be used to enumerate user accounts. Environment variables, such as <code>%USERNAME%</code> and <code>$USER</code>, may also be used to access this information.  On network devices, [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `show users` and `show ssh` can be used to display users currently logged into the device.(Citation: show_ssh_users_cmd_cisco)(Citation: US-CERT TA18-106A Network Infrastructure Devices 2018)
        ~Local System Commands: Adversaries use built-in system commands like whoami, w, who, and environment variables to identify current users and system owners
        ~Active Directory Queries: Adversaries query Active Directory to enumerate users, their properties, and relationships
        ~Network Session Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate network sessions to identify logged-in users across systems
        ~Process Ownership Analysis: Adversaries examine running processes and their ownership to identify active users
        ~File System Metadata Analysis: Adversaries analyze file and directory ownership/permissions to identify users
        ~System Logs Analysis: Adversaries parse system logs to identify user activities and patterns
        ~Remote System Queries: Adversaries use remote administration tools to query user information from networked systems
        ~Registry Analysis: Adversaries examine Windows Registry for user profile information and login history
        ~Network Device User Enumeration: Adversaries use device-specific commands to list current and configured users on network devices
        ~Cloud Service Account Discovery: Adversaries query cloud service APIs to enumerate user accounts and permissions
        ~Credential Manager Analysis: Adversaries examine stored credentials and credential managers to identify user accounts
        ~Service Account Enumeration: Adversaries specifically target discovery of service accounts and their privileges
        ~Terminal Services User Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate users through Terminal Services/RDP session queries
        ~Container Environment User Discovery: Adversaries identify users and service accounts in containerized environments
        ~Mobile Device Management (MDM) Queries: Adversaries query MDM solutions to discover user associations and device ownership
      Remote System Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of other systems by IP address, hostname, or other logical identifier on a network that may be used for Lateral Movement from the current system. Functionality could exist within remote access tools to enable this, but utilities available on the operating system could also be used such as  [Ping](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0097), <code>net view</code> using [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039), or, on ESXi servers, `esxcli network diag ping`.  Adversaries may also analyze data from local host files (ex: <code>C:\Windows\System32\Drivers\etc\hosts</code> or <code>/etc/hosts</code>) or other passive means (such as local [Arp](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0099) cache entries) in order to discover the presence of remote systems in an environment.  Adversaries may also target discovery of network infrastructure as well as leverage [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands on network devices to gather detailed information about systems within a network (e.g. <code>show cdp neighbors</code>, <code>show arp</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: CISA AR21-126A FIVEHANDS May 2021)
        ~Network Scanning: Adversaries may use network scanning tools and protocols (like ICMP, TCP/UDP port scans) to actively discover remote systems on the network
        ~Host File Analysis: Adversaries may analyze local host files and DNS configurations to discover remote systems
        ~ARP Cache Enumeration: Adversaries may examine ARP cache entries to identify recently contacted systems
        ~Network Device CLI Exploitation: Adversaries may leverage network device command line interfaces to gather information about connected systems
        ~Service Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate network services and protocols to identify remote systems
        ~SNMP Enumeration: Adversaries may use SNMP queries to discover network devices and systems
        ~NetBIOS Discovery: Adversaries may use NetBIOS name resolution to identify systems and shares
        ~DNS Zone Transfer: Adversaries may attempt DNS zone transfers to enumerate hosts in a domain
        ~Active Directory Enumeration: Adversaries may query Active Directory to discover domain-joined systems
        ~Network Share Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate network shares to discover remote systems
        ~Cloud Instance Enumeration: Adversaries may query cloud provider APIs and metadata services to discover other instances and resources
        ~Hypervisor Guest Discovery: Adversaries may use virtualization platform tools to enumerate guest systems and VMs
        ~Wireless Network Scanning: Adversaries may perform wireless network scanning to discover devices on wireless networks
        ~ICS Asset Discovery: Adversaries may use industrial protocol scanning to discover ICS/SCADA devices and systems
        ~Container Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate container instances and orchestration platforms to identify running containers
        ~Remote Management Protocol Discovery: Adversaries may scan for systems exposing remote management protocols like RDP, SSH, or WMI
        ~Network Topology Discovery: Adversaries may use routing protocols and network mapping to understand network topology and discover systems
        ~Multicast/Broadcast Discovery: Adversaries may use broadcast and multicast protocols to discover systems responding to specific services
      Domain Trust Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to gather information on domain trust relationships that may be used to identify lateral movement opportunities in Windows multi-domain/forest environments. Domain trusts provide a mechanism for a domain to allow access to resources based on the authentication procedures of another domain.(Citation: Microsoft Trusts) Domain trusts allow the users of the trusted domain to access resources in the trusting domain. The information discovered may help the adversary conduct [SID-History Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/005), [Pass the Ticket](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/003), and [Kerberoasting](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/003).(Citation: AdSecurity Forging Trust Tickets)(Citation: Harmj0y Domain Trusts) Domain trusts can be enumerated using the `DSEnumerateDomainTrusts()` Win32 API call, .NET methods, and LDAP.(Citation: Harmj0y Domain Trusts) The Windows utility [Nltest](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0359) is known to be used by adversaries to enumerate domain trusts.(Citation: Microsoft Operation Wilysupply)
        ~Windows API Enumeration: Adversaries may use native Windows APIs like DSEnumerateDomainTrusts() to discover domain trust relationships, including both inbound and outbound trusts.
        ~LDAP Query Enumeration: Adversaries may use LDAP queries to enumerate trust relationships by querying domain controllers for trust-related attributes and objects.
        ~PowerShell-Based Discovery: Adversaries may use PowerShell cmdlets and scripts to enumerate domain trusts, leveraging built-in Active Directory modules.
        ~Network Protocol Analysis: Adversaries may monitor network traffic to identify trust relationships through authentication attempts and resource access patterns.
        ~Trust Type Enumeration: Adversaries may specifically enumerate different types of trusts (forest, external, realm) to identify potential paths for lateral movement.
        ~Trust Attribute Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate specific trust attributes like transitivity and trust direction to understand potential attack paths.
        ~Forest Trust Discovery: Adversaries may focus on discovering forest-level trusts to identify potential paths between different Active Directory forests.
        ~Command-Line Tool Usage: Adversaries may use built-in Windows command-line tools like nltest, netdom, or dsquery to enumerate trust relationships.
      System Network Connections Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of network connections to or from the compromised system they are currently accessing or from remote systems by querying for information over the network.   An adversary who gains access to a system that is part of a cloud-based environment may map out Virtual Private Clouds or Virtual Networks in order to determine what systems and services are connected. The actions performed are likely the same types of discovery techniques depending on the operating system, but the resulting information may include details about the networked cloud environment relevant to the adversary's goals. Cloud providers may have different ways in which their virtual networks operate.(Citation: Amazon AWS VPC Guide)(Citation: Microsoft Azure Virtual Network Overview)(Citation: Google VPC Overview) Similarly, adversaries who gain access to network devices may also perform similar discovery activities to gather information about connected systems and services.  Utilities and commands that acquire this information include [netstat](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0104), "net use," and "net session" with [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039). In Mac and Linux, [netstat](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0104) and <code>lsof</code> can be used to list current connections. <code>who -a</code> and <code>w</code> can be used to show which users are currently logged in, similar to "net session". Additionally, built-in features native to network devices and [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) may be used (e.g. <code>show ip sockets</code>, <code>show tcp brief</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A) On ESXi servers, the command `esxi network ip connection list` can be used to list active network connections.(Citation: Sygnia ESXi Ransomware 2025)
        ~Windows Network Commands: Adversaries use native Windows commands like netstat, net use, and net session to enumerate network connections and sessions
        ~Unix-like System Commands: Adversaries leverage Unix/Linux commands such as netstat, lsof, who, and w to discover network connections
        ~Cloud Network Mapping: Adversaries enumerate Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) and virtual networks in cloud environments using provider-specific tools and APIs
        ~Network Device Commands: Adversaries use network device CLI commands and features to list active connections and network status
        ~Virtualization Platform Commands: Adversaries utilize hypervisor-specific commands like ESXi network commands to discover virtual network connections
        ~PowerShell Network Enumeration: Adversaries use PowerShell cmdlets to query network connection information
        ~Third-party Network Monitoring Tools: Adversaries leverage installed network monitoring and analysis tools to gather connection information
        ~API-based Network Discovery: Adversaries query system APIs and network interfaces programmatically to enumerate connections
        ~Container Network Discovery: Adversaries enumerate container network configurations and connections in containerized environments
        ~Remote System Connection Enumeration: Adversaries query remote systems to map network connections across the environment
        ~Memory Analysis Tools: Adversaries use memory analysis tools to extract active network connection information from system memory
        ~Network Log Analysis: Adversaries analyze system and network logs to discover historical connection patterns
        ~Protocol-Specific Enumeration: Adversaries use protocol-specific tools to enumerate connections (e.g., SMB sessions, RDP connections)
        ~Service Discovery Integration: Adversaries combine network connection discovery with service discovery to map service-to-connection relationships
        ~DNS Query Analysis: Adversaries analyze DNS queries and cache to map network connections and communication patterns
        ~Registry Network Information: Adversaries query Windows Registry to discover network connection history and configurations
        ~Proxy Connection Discovery: Adversaries enumerate proxy server connections and configurations
        ~VPN Connection Enumeration: Adversaries discover VPN and tunnel interfaces and their connections
        ~SDN Controller Discovery: Adversaries query SDN controllers and interfaces to map network connections
        ~Network Security Device Enumeration: Adversaries query firewalls, IDS/IPS, and other security devices for connection information
      System Information Discovery: An adversary may attempt to get detailed information about the operating system and hardware, including version, patches, hotfixes, service packs, and architecture. Adversaries may use the information from [System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1082) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  Tools such as [Systeminfo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0096) can be used to gather detailed system information. If running with privileged access, a breakdown of system data can be gathered through the <code>systemsetup</code> configuration tool on macOS. As an example, adversaries with user-level access can execute the <code>df -aH</code> command to obtain currently mounted disks and associated freely available space. Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather detailed system information (e.g. <code>show version</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A) On ESXi servers, threat actors may gather system information from various esxcli utilities, such as `system hostname get`, `system version get`, and `storage filesystem list` (to list storage volumes).(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)(Citation: Varonis)  Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) cloud providers such as AWS, GCP, and Azure allow access to instance and virtual machine information via APIs. Successful authenticated API calls can return data such as the operating system platform and status of a particular instance or the model view of a virtual machine.(Citation: Amazon Describe Instance)(Citation: Google Instances Resource)(Citation: Microsoft Virutal Machine API)  [System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1082) combined with information gathered from other forms of discovery and reconnaissance can drive payload development and concealment.(Citation: OSX.FairyTale)(Citation: 20 macOS Common Tools and Techniques)
        ~Operating System Commands: Using native OS commands and utilities like 'systeminfo', 'uname', 'ver' to gather system details
        ~Registry Query: Querying Windows registry to obtain system information and configurations
        ~API Queries: Using operating system APIs to programmatically gather system information
        ~Cloud Instance Metadata: Accessing cloud provider metadata services to gather instance information
        ~Virtualization Platform Inspection: Gathering information about virtualization environments and hypervisors
        ~Hardware Component Enumeration: Discovering detailed hardware specifications and capabilities
        ~Network Device Queries: Using device-specific commands to gather system information from network equipment
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Reading system configuration files to gather system details
        ~Management Interface Queries: Using management interfaces like WMI to collect system information
        ~Container Environment Discovery: Gathering information about container runtime and configurations
        ~Security Product Discovery: Gathering information about installed security software and configurations
        ~System Backup Inspection: Identifying backup systems and configurations
        ~System Language Discovery: Determining system locale, language, and regional settings
        ~Resource Usage Analysis: Gathering information about system resource utilization and capacity
        ~System Service Discovery: Enumerating running services and daemons
        ~Boot Configuration Analysis: Gathering information about system boot configuration and startup
        ~Storage System Analysis: Discovering information about storage subsystems and configurations
        ~System Update History: Gathering information about installed updates and patches
      Process Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get information about running processes on a system. Information obtained could be used to gain an understanding of common software/applications running on systems within the network. Administrator or otherwise elevated access may provide better process details. Adversaries may use the information from [Process Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1057) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.  In Windows environments, adversaries could obtain details on running processes using the [Tasklist](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0057) utility via [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) or <code>Get-Process</code> via [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001). Information about processes can also be extracted from the output of [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) calls such as <code>CreateToolhelp32Snapshot</code>. In Mac and Linux, this is accomplished with the <code>ps</code> command. Adversaries may also opt to enumerate processes via `/proc`. ESXi also supports use of the `ps` command, as well as `esxcli system process list`.(Citation: Sygnia ESXi Ransomware 2025)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)  On network devices, [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `show processes` can be used to display current running processes.(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: show_processes_cisco_cmd)
        ~Windows Process Discovery: Using native Windows tools like tasklist, Get-Process, and WMI queries to enumerate running processes
        ~Unix-like Process Discovery: Using commands like ps, top, and /proc filesystem inspection on Unix-based systems
        ~Cloud Environment Process Discovery: Enumerating processes in cloud workloads and containers using platform-specific tools
        ~Network Device Process Discovery: Using device-specific commands to list processes on network appliances
        ~Virtualization Platform Process Discovery: Examining processes in hypervisors and virtual machines using platform tools
        ~Process Attribute Discovery: Focusing on gathering detailed process attributes like ownership, privileges, and relationships
        ~Remote Process Discovery: Discovering processes on remote systems through various protocols and tools
        ~Memory-Based Process Discovery: Analyzing process information through direct memory inspection
        ~Security Tool Process Discovery: Leveraging security tools and EDR solutions to enumerate processes
        ~Registry-Based Process Discovery: Examining Windows Registry for process-related information and autorun entries
        ~API-Based Process Discovery: Using specialized APIs and frameworks beyond native system calls
        ~Mobile Platform Process Discovery: Enumerating processes on mobile operating systems like iOS and Android
        ~IoT Device Process Discovery: Discovering processes on IoT and embedded devices
      Account Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of valid accounts, usernames, or email addresses on a system or within a compromised environment. This information can help adversaries determine which accounts exist, which can aid in follow-on behavior such as brute-forcing, spear-phishing attacks, or account takeovers (e.g., [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078)).  Adversaries may use several methods to enumerate accounts, including abuse of existing tools, built-in commands, and potential misconfigurations that leak account names and roles or permissions in the targeted environment.  For examples, cloud environments typically provide easily accessible interfaces to obtain user lists.(Citation: AWS List Users)(Citation: Google Cloud - IAM Servie Accounts List API) On hosts, adversaries can use default [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) and other command line functionality to identify accounts. Information about email addresses and accounts may also be extracted by searching an infected system’s files.
        Domain Account: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of domain accounts. This information can help adversaries determine which domain accounts exist to aid in follow-on behavior such as targeting specific accounts which possess particular privileges.  Commands such as <code>net user /domain</code> and <code>net group /domain</code> of the [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility, <code>dscacheutil -q group</code> on macOS, and <code>ldapsearch</code> on Linux can list domain users and groups. [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) cmdlets including <code>Get-ADUser</code> and <code>Get-ADGroupMember</code> may enumerate members of Active Directory groups.(Citation: CrowdStrike StellarParticle January 2022)
