      Group Policy Discovery: Adversaries may gather information on Group Policy settings to identify paths for privilege escalation, security measures applied within a domain, and to discover patterns in domain objects that can be manipulated or used to blend in the environment. Group Policy allows for centralized management of user and computer settings in Active Directory (AD). Group policy objects (GPOs) are containers for group policy settings made up of files stored within a predictable network path `\<DOMAIN>\SYSVOL\<DOMAIN>\Policies\`.(Citation: TechNet Group Policy Basics)(Citation: ADSecurity GPO Persistence 2016)  Adversaries may use commands such as <code>gpresult</code> or various publicly available PowerShell functions, such as <code>Get-DomainGPO</code> and <code>Get-DomainGPOLocalGroup</code>, to gather information on Group Policy settings.(Citation: Microsoft gpresult)(Citation: Github PowerShell Empire) Adversaries may use this information to shape follow-on behaviors, including determining potential attack paths within the target network as well as opportunities to manipulate Group Policy settings (i.e. [Domain or Tenant Policy Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1484)) for their benefit.
        ~Command Line Tools: Adversaries may use built-in Windows command line tools like gpresult, gpresult /r, gpresult /z to enumerate Group Policy settings and their effects on a system
        ~PowerShell Enumeration: Adversaries may use PowerShell cmdlets and scripts to query Group Policy information, including Get-GPO, Get-GPOReport, and custom PowerShell frameworks
        ~SYSVOL Access: Adversaries may directly access and browse the SYSVOL share to enumerate Group Policy-related files and settings stored in the default Group Policy file share
        ~Remote Registry Queries: Adversaries may query the registry of remote systems to gather information about applied Group Policy settings and their effects
        ~Group Policy Management Console (GPMC) Abuse: Adversaries may abuse legitimate Group Policy management tools to enumerate policy settings and configurations
        ~Network Protocol Analysis: Adversaries may monitor network traffic for Group Policy-related communications between domain controllers and clients to gather information about policy distribution and settings
        ~LDAP Queries: Adversaries may use LDAP queries to enumerate Group Policy objects and their attributes stored in Active Directory
        ~Group Policy Caching Analysis: Adversaries may examine local Group Policy caches and temporary storage to understand previously applied policies
        ~WMI-Based Enumeration: Adversaries may leverage Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) queries to gather information about applied Group Policy settings
        ~Third-Party Security Tool Abuse: Adversaries may abuse legitimate third-party Group Policy assessment and management tools to gather policy information
      Permission Groups Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to discover group and permission settings. This information can help adversaries determine which user accounts and groups are available, the membership of users in particular groups, and which users and groups have elevated permissions.  Adversaries may attempt to discover group permission settings in many different ways. This data may provide the adversary with information about the compromised environment that can be used in follow-on activity and targeting.(Citation: CrowdStrike BloodHound April 2018)
        Local Groups: Adversaries may attempt to find local system groups and permission settings. The knowledge of local system permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as the users found within the local administrators group.  Commands such as <code>net localgroup</code> of the [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility, <code>dscl . -list /Groups</code> on macOS, and <code>groups</code> on Linux can list local groups.
          Use "net localgroup" command on Windows to list all local groups and their members
          Use "Get-LocalGroup" PowerShell cmdlet to enumerate local security groups
          Execute "dscl . -list /Groups" and "dscacheutil -q group" commands on macOS systems
          Run "cat /etc/group" or "getent group" commands on Linux systems to view group information
          Query Windows Security Account Manager (SAM) database using tools like PsLoggedOn
          Use WMI queries like "Win32_Group" to enumerate local groups programmatically
          Leverage Active Directory PowerShell module with Get-ADGroup for domain-joined systems
          Use "groups" command on Unix-like systems to list group membership for current user
          Execute "id -Gn" command on Linux/Unix to display group names for specified user
          Use "lusrmgr.msc" GUI tool on Windows to browse local groups interactively
          Use "wmic group list full" command to get detailed group information
          Query Windows Registry key "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList" for user profiles
          Use Sysinternals' AccessEnum tool to view group permissions on files and registry
          Execute "net localgroup administrators" to specifically target admin group membership
          Use BloodHound's built-in local group collection methods
          Query group information through Windows Management Instrumentation Command-line (WMIC)
          Use "dir /q" command to view file ownership and group permissions
          Execute PowerView's Get-NetLocalGroup function for detailed group enumeration
          Use "whoami /groups" to view current user's group memberships
          Query Active Directory LDAP for local group information using AdFind tool
          Use "dsquery group" command to enumerate domain local groups
          Execute VBScript to enumerate groups through ADSI interfaces
          Use "gpresult /R" to view group policy-based memberships
          Query Windows Security Event logs for group membership changes (Event ID 4735, 4737)
          Use PsLogList to analyze security logs for group-related events
          Execute WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux) group enumeration commands from Windows
          Use "net view /domain" to discover domain groups that map to local groups
          Query Azure AD group membership using Azure CLI or PowerShell
          Use AWS IAM CLI commands to list local equivalent groups in EC2 instances
          Execute GCP Cloud Identity commands to enumerate project-level groups
          Use "nltest /domain_trusts" to enumerate groups through domain trust relationships
          Query Volume Shadow Copy Service (VSS) snapshots for historical group information
          Use Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) for group enumeration
          Execute "csvde -f groups.csv" to export group information to CSV
          Use System Restore points to analyze group changes over time
          Query Terminal Services/Remote Desktop Services for session-specific group memberships
          Use "dsac.exe" (Directory Services Administrative Center) for detailed group analysis
          Extract group information from system backup files using Windows Server Backup
          Use "auditpol" to view group-related audit policies and logged events
          Query Microsoft Management Console (MMC) snap-ins programmatically for group information
          Use "PsExec -s" to run group enumeration commands in SYSTEM context
          Query SCCM/Microsoft Endpoint Configuration Manager for group information
          Use Docker container inspection commands to view group mappings
          Extract group information from RADIUS server configurations
          Query LDAP-based authentication systems (OpenLDAP, FreeIPA) for group data
          Use Kubernetes RBAC commands to view group bindings in cluster environments
          Query group information through Microsoft Intune management interface
          Use "RunAs" with different contexts to view group permissions
          Extract group information from Windows Defender Security Center
          Query group mappings in Network Policy Server (NPS) configurations
          Use VMware vSphere PowerCLI to enumerate guest OS group information
          Query Hyper-V host for guest OS group details using virtual machine management tools
          Use NetBIOS queries to enumerate legacy Windows group information
          Extract group information from Microsoft SQL Server security principals
          Query Exchange Server PowerShell for distribution group to security group mappings
          Use Process Monitor to track group permission checks in real-time
          Extract group information from IIS web server configurations
          Query SharePoint service accounts and group mappings
          Use Citrix Virtual Apps and Desktops PowerShell to enumerate session groups
          Extract group information from Windows Server Failover Cluster configurations
        Domain Groups: Adversaries may attempt to find domain-level groups and permission settings. The knowledge of domain-level permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as domain administrators.  Commands such as <code>net group /domain</code> of the [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility,  <code>dscacheutil -q group</code> on macOS, and <code>ldapsearch</code> on Linux can list domain-level groups.
          Use "net group /domain" command to enumerate all domain groups
          Query Active Directory using PowerShell Get-ADGroup cmdlet
          Use BloodHound to map domain group relationships and permissions
          Execute LDAP queries with ldapsearch to enumerate domain groups
          Use AdFind tool to query domain group information
          Run PowerView's Get-NetGroup function to discover domain groups
          Query domain controller with dsquery group command
          Use Mimikatz to extract domain group information from memory
          Execute WMI queries to gather domain group membership details
          Use macOS dscacheutil and dscl commands for domain group enumeration
          Use "getent group" command on Linux systems to enumerate domain groups
          Query domain groups using PowerShell's DirectoryServices.DirectorySearcher
          Use Active Directory Administrative Center (GUI) to browse group information
          Execute remote PowerShell commands through WinRM for group enumeration
          Use gpresult /R to identify group policy assignments and relationships
          Query domain groups through ADSI (ADSISearcher)
          Use SharpHound collector for automated domain group mapping
          Execute wbinfo -g command on Linux to list domain groups via Samba
          Use ADExplorer from Sysinternals for interactive group enumeration
          Query domain groups using dsget group command-line tool
          Use ADSI Edit (adsiedit.msc) for low-level directory service access
          Query domain groups using Rubeus with alternate credentials
          Enumerate groups through LDAP authentication coercion
          Use Azure AD PowerShell modules for hybrid/cloud domain groups
          Execute SMB-based group enumeration through named pipes
          Use Empire's powerview.ps1 modules for group enumeration
          Query domain groups through DNS SRV records
          Use Kerbrute for Kerberos-based group enumeration
          Execute CrackMapExec for network-wide group discovery
          Use Impacket's samrdump.py for remote group enumeration
          Query domain groups through NTLM authentication sessions
          Use DCSync technique to replicate domain group information
          Enumerate groups through cross-forest trusts using PowerShell
          Query Read-Only Domain Controller (RODC) for group information
          Use NTDS.dit extraction for offline group enumeration
          Query Global Catalog servers for universal group membership
          Enumerate groups through DsGetDcName API calls
          Use LDAP referrals to discover groups across domains
          Query backup Domain Controllers for group information
          Use replication metadata to track group membership changes
        Cloud Groups: Adversaries may attempt to find cloud groups and permission settings. The knowledge of cloud permission groups can help adversaries determine the particular roles of users and groups within an environment, as well as which users are associated with a particular group.  With authenticated access there are several tools that can be used to find permissions groups. The <code>Get-MsolRole</code> PowerShell cmdlet can be used to obtain roles and permissions groups for Exchange and Office 365 accounts (Citation: Microsoft Msolrole)(Citation: GitHub Raindance).  Azure CLI (AZ CLI) and the Google Cloud Identity Provider API also provide interfaces to obtain permissions groups. The command <code>az ad user get-member-groups</code> will list groups associated to a user account for Azure while the API endpoint <code>GET https://cloudidentity.googleapis.com/v1/groups</code> lists group resources available to a user for Google.(Citation: Microsoft AZ CLI)(Citation: Black Hills Red Teaming MS AD Azure, 2018)(Citation: Google Cloud Identity API Documentation) In AWS, the commands `ListRolePolicies` and `ListAttachedRolePolicies` allow users to enumerate the policies attached to a role.(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 Compromised Cloud Compute Credentials 2022)  Adversaries may attempt to list ACLs for objects to determine the owner and other accounts with access to the object, for example, via the AWS <code>GetBucketAcl</code> API (Citation: AWS Get Bucket ACL). Using this information an adversary can target accounts with permissions to a given object or leverage accounts they have already compromised to access the object.
          Use Get-MsolRole PowerShell cmdlet to enumerate Office 365 and Exchange Online role assignments and group memberships
          Execute az ad user get-member-groups through Azure CLI to list group memberships for Azure AD users
          Query Google Cloud Identity Provider API endpoint GET /v1/groups to list available groups
          Use AWS CLI commands ListRolePolicies and ListAttachedRolePolicies to enumerate IAM role policies
          Call AWS GetBucketAcl API to enumerate S3 bucket access control lists
          Use Azure Portal's Azure Active Directory interface to browse group memberships and permissions
          Leverage Google Cloud Console IAM & Admin interface to view roles and group memberships
          Query AWS IAM API GetAccountAuthorizationDetails to get comprehensive permissions data
          Use Azure PowerShell Get-AzRoleAssignment to list role assignments
          Execute Google Cloud gcloud projects get-iam-policy command to list IAM policies
          Use ScoutSuite to automatically enumerate permissions across AWS, Azure, and GCP
          Execute Prowler security assessment tool for AWS permission analysis
          Use CloudMapper to visualize AWS IAM relationships and permissions
          Query Azure Graph API for detailed group membership analysis
          Use AWS CloudSploit for automated permission and security configuration scanning
          Execute Steampipe for querying cloud infrastructure permissions across providers
          Use PMapper tool to analyze AWS IAM permission paths and trust relationships
          Deploy CloudQuery to extract and analyze permissions across cloud providers
          Use Cartography to map relationships between cloud identities and resources
          Execute Principal Mapper (PMapper) to identify privilege escalation paths in AWS
          Use Azure AD PowerShell Get-AzureADGroupMember for nested group membership enumeration
          Execute AWS IAM Access Analyzer for identifying resource access across accounts
          Use GCP Asset Inventory API to discover IAM policies across organization
          Query AWS Organizations API ListAccounts and ListPolicies for multi-account permission analysis
          Use Azure Managed Identity PowerShell commands to enumerate system-assigned identities
          Execute CloudFox for automated cloud attack path discovery
          Use AWS Organizations API to enumerate Service Control Policies (SCPs)
          Deploy Azure Resource Graph queries for permission relationship mapping
          Use Google Cloud Asset Inventory to export IAM policies organization-wide
          Execute BloodHound Azure AD Collector for permission path analysis
          Use kubectl auth can-i for Kubernetes RBAC permission checking
          Query AWS STS GetCallerIdentity for assumed role and federation information
          Use Azure AD Connect PowerShell to enumerate hybrid identity permissions
          Execute Cloud Workload Identity federation configuration analysis
          Use AWS IAM SimulatePrincipalPolicy API for permission simulation
          Query SAML provider configurations across cloud platforms
          Use Azure Managed Identity MSI_ENDPOINT for container workload identity analysis
          Execute AWS IAM GetServiceLastAccessedDetails for service usage permissions
          Use GCP Workload Identity Federation discovery commands
          Query cloud provider tags and resource labels for permission inheritance
          Use AWS STS GetSessionToken API to analyze temporary credentials
          Query Azure Function App managed identity permissions
          Execute AWS Lambda list-function-event-invoke-configs for permission triggers
          Use Cloud SQL IAM policy analysis commands
          Query Azure Storage Account Shared Access Signatures (SAS)
          Execute AWS API Gateway get-authorizers for API permission configuration
          Use GCP Cloud Functions getIamPolicy for serverless permissions
          Query AWS RDS IAM authentication settings
          Use Azure Key Vault access policy enumeration
          Execute Cloud CDN origin access identity permissions analysis
      Security Software Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of security software, configurations, defensive tools, and sensors that are installed on the system. This may include things such as local firewall rules and anti-virus. Adversaries may use the information from [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1063) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.   ### Windows  Example commands that can be used to obtain security software information are [netsh](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0108), <code>reg query</code> with [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075), <code>dir</code> with [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106), and [Tasklist](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0057), but other indicators of discovery behavior may be more specific to the type of software or security system the adversary is looking for.  ### Mac  It's becoming more common to see macOS malware perform checks for LittleSnitch and KnockKnock software.
        ~Registry Query: Adversaries query Windows registry to identify installed security products and their configurations
        ~Process Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate running processes to identify security software
        ~File System Search: Adversaries search for known installation paths and files of security products
        ~Network Service Discovery: Adversaries query network services to identify security monitoring tools
        ~Command Line Interface: Adversaries use CLI tools to gather information about security software
        ~API Monitoring: Adversaries monitor API calls to detect security software hooks
        ~System Management Tools: Adversaries leverage system management tools to identify security products
        ~Security Event Log Analysis: Adversaries analyze security event logs to identify active security solutions
        ~Driver Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate system drivers to identify security product components
        ~Service Query: Adversaries query system services to identify security-related services
        ~Cloud Security Console Discovery: Adversaries query cloud environments to identify security tools and configurations
        ~Container Security Tool Discovery: Adversaries identify container security solutions and their configurations
        ~Virtualization Security Discovery: Adversaries detect security tools specific to virtual environments
        ~EDR/XDR Discovery: Adversaries specifically probe for endpoint detection and response solutions
        ~MDM Solution Discovery: Adversaries identify mobile device management and security solutions
      System Time Discovery: An adversary may gather the system time and/or time zone settings from a local or remote system. The system time is set and stored by services, such as the Windows Time Service on Windows or <code>systemsetup</code> on macOS.(Citation: MSDN System Time)(Citation: Technet Windows Time Service)(Citation: systemsetup mac time) These time settings may also be synchronized between systems and services in an enterprise network, typically accomplished with a network time server within a domain.(Citation: Mac Time Sync)(Citation: linux system time)  System time information may be gathered in a number of ways, such as with [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) on Windows by performing <code>net time \\hostname</code> to gather the system time on a remote system. The victim's time zone may also be inferred from the current system time or gathered by using <code>w32tm /tz</code>.(Citation: Technet Windows Time Service) In addition, adversaries can discover device uptime through functions such as <code>GetTickCount()</code> to determine how long it has been since the system booted up.(Citation: Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion)  On network devices, [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `show clock detail` can be used to see the current time configuration.(Citation: show_clock_detail_cisco_cmd) On ESXi servers, `esxcli system clock get` can be used for the same purpose.  In addition, system calls – such as <code>time()</code> – have been used to collect the current time on Linux devices.(Citation: MAGNET GOBLIN) On macOS systems, adversaries may use commands such as <code>systemsetup -gettimezone</code> or <code>timeIntervalSinceNow</code> to gather current time zone information or current date and time.(Citation: System Information Discovery Technique)(Citation: ESET DazzleSpy Jan 2022)  This information could be useful for performing other techniques, such as executing a file with a [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053)(Citation: RSA EU12 They're Inside), or to discover locality information based on time zone to assist in victim targeting (i.e. [System Location Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1614)). Adversaries may also use knowledge of system time as part of a time bomb, or delaying execution until a specified date/time.(Citation: AnyRun TimeBomb)
        ~Local System Time Query: Adversaries directly query the local system's time settings using native OS commands and APIs
        ~Remote System Time Query: Adversaries gather time information from remote systems using network protocols and remote administration tools
        ~Network Time Protocol Query: Adversaries interact with NTP servers and services to gather time synchronization information
        ~Time Zone Configuration Discovery: Adversaries specifically target time zone settings to infer geographical location
        ~System Uptime Discovery: Adversaries focus on gathering system uptime information rather than current time
        ~Virtual Machine Time Query: Adversaries specifically target virtualization platforms to gather time information
        ~Network Device Time Query: Adversaries target network infrastructure devices to gather their time settings
        ~Domain Time Synchronization Discovery: Adversaries examine domain time synchronization settings and relationships
      Cloud Infrastructure Discovery: An adversary may attempt to discover infrastructure and resources that are available within an infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) environment. This includes compute service resources such as instances, virtual machines, and snapshots as well as resources of other services including the storage and database services.  Cloud providers offer methods such as APIs and commands issued through CLIs to serve information about infrastructure. For example, AWS provides a <code>DescribeInstances</code> API within the Amazon EC2 API that can return information about one or more instances within an account, the <code>ListBuckets</code> API that returns a list of all buckets owned by the authenticated sender of the request, the <code>HeadBucket</code> API to determine a bucket’s existence along with access permissions of the request sender, or the <code>GetPublicAccessBlock</code> API to retrieve access block configuration for a bucket.(Citation: Amazon Describe Instance)(Citation: Amazon Describe Instances API)(Citation: AWS Get Public Access Block)(Citation: AWS Head Bucket) Similarly, GCP's Cloud SDK CLI provides the <code>gcloud compute instances list</code> command to list all Google Compute Engine instances in a project (Citation: Google Compute Instances), and Azure's CLI command <code>az vm list</code> lists details of virtual machines.(Citation: Microsoft AZ CLI) In addition to API commands, adversaries can utilize open source tools to discover cloud storage infrastructure through [Wordlist Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595/003).(Citation: Malwarebytes OSINT Leaky Buckets - Hioureas)  An adversary may enumerate resources using a compromised user's access keys to determine which are available to that user.(Citation: Expel IO Evil in AWS) The discovery of these available resources may help adversaries determine their next steps in the Cloud environment, such as establishing Persistence.(Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2020)An adversary may also use this information to change the configuration to make the bucket publicly accessible, allowing data to be accessed without authentication. Adversaries have also may use infrastructure discovery APIs such as <code>DescribeDBInstances</code> to determine size, owner, permissions, and network ACLs of database resources. (Citation: AWS Describe DB Instances) Adversaries can use this information to determine the potential value of databases and discover the requirements to access them. Unlike in [Cloud Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1526), this technique focuses on the discovery of components of the provided services rather than the services themselves.
        ~API-Based Discovery: Adversaries may use cloud provider APIs to enumerate infrastructure components, permissions, and configurations
        ~CLI Tool Enumeration: Adversaries may leverage cloud provider CLI tools to list and inspect cloud resources
        ~Storage Resource Discovery: Adversaries may specifically target discovery of storage resources like buckets, blobs, and file shares
        ~Database Resource Discovery: Adversaries may focus on discovering database instances, configurations, and access controls
        ~Network Configuration Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate network settings, security groups, and access controls
        ~Instance Metadata Discovery: Adversaries may query instance metadata services to gather information about cloud resources
        ~Resource Permission Mapping: Adversaries may enumerate IAM roles and permissions associated with discovered resources
        ~Container Resource Discovery: Adversaries may discover container registries, clusters, and orchestration platforms
        ~Serverless Function Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate serverless functions and their configurations
        ~Cross-Account Resource Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to discover resources across multiple linked cloud accounts
        ~Monitoring Service Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate cloud monitoring, logging, and alerting configurations
        ~Backup Infrastructure Discovery: Adversaries may discover backup vaults, snapshot policies, and disaster recovery configurations
        ~Billing Resource Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate billing information, resource usage, and cost allocation tags
        ~Service Endpoint Mapping: Adversaries may discover internal service endpoints, load balancers, and API gateways
        ~Geographic Resource Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate resources across different regions and availability zones
        ~Identity Provider Discovery: Adversaries may discover configured identity providers and federation settings
        ~Key Management Service Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate encryption keys, certificates, and secrets management services
        ~Cache Service Discovery: Adversaries may discover caching layers, content delivery configurations, and edge locations
        ~Queue Service Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate message queues, pub/sub topics, and event buses
        ~DNS Configuration Discovery: Adversaries may discover DNS settings, zones, and routing policies
        ~Security Service Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate cloud security services, WAF rules, and threat detection configurations
        ~Integration Service Discovery: Adversaries may discover API integration services, middleware, and connection points
        ~Resource Organization Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate resource groups, tags, and organizational structures
        ~CI/CD Infrastructure Discovery: Adversaries may discover build pipelines, artifact repositories, and deployment configurations
        ~Data Pipeline Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate ETL services, data lakes, and analytics infrastructure
        ~Resource Template Discovery: Adversaries may discover infrastructure-as-code templates and deployment manifests
        ~Auto-Scaling Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate auto-scaling groups, policies, and configurations
        ~Resource Dependency Discovery: Adversaries may map dependencies between cloud resources and services
        ~Service Mesh Discovery: Adversaries may discover service mesh configurations, proxies, and routing rules
        ~Resource Policy Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate resource-based policies and service control policies
      Network Service Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts and local network infrastructure devices, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Common methods to acquire this information include port, vulnerability, and/or wordlist scans using tools that are brought onto a system.(Citation: CISA AR21-126A FIVEHANDS May 2021)     Within cloud environments, adversaries may attempt to discover services running on other cloud hosts. Additionally, if the cloud environment is connected to a on-premises environment, adversaries may be able to identify services running on non-cloud systems as well.  Within macOS environments, adversaries may use the native Bonjour application to discover services running on other macOS hosts within a network. The Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon automatically registers and advertises a host’s registered services on the network. For example, adversaries can use a mDNS query (such as <code>dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .</code>) to find other systems broadcasting the ssh service.(Citation: apple doco bonjour description)(Citation: macOS APT Activity Bradley)
        ~Port Scanning: Use of tools and protocols to systematically scan network ports to identify running services and their versions
        ~DNS Service Discovery: Leveraging DNS protocols and services like mDNS, LLMNR, or NetBIOS to discover network services
        ~SNMP Scanning: Using SNMP protocols to query network devices and enumerate running services
        ~Cloud Service API Enumeration: Querying cloud provider APIs to discover available services and endpoints
        ~Service Broadcasting Monitoring: Passive monitoring of service advertisements and broadcasts on the network
        ~Network Protocol Analysis: Deep packet inspection and protocol-specific probing to identify services
        ~Vulnerability Scanner Usage: Deployment of vulnerability scanners to identify services and their versions
        ~Service Configuration Mining: Extracting service information from configuration files and system interfaces
        ~Network Proxy Enumeration: Discovery of services through network proxies and load balancers
        ~Service Discovery Protocol Abuse: Exploiting service discovery protocols like UPnP or WS-Discovery
        ~Network Management System Querying: Exploiting network management systems and monitoring tools to discover services
        ~Industrial Protocol Scanning: Using specialized protocols like Modbus, BACnet, or S7comm to discover industrial services
        ~Authentication Service Enumeration: Specifically targeting authentication services like Kerberos, LDAP, or RADIUS
        ~Legacy Protocol Discovery: Using older protocols like FTP, Telnet, or RPC to identify legacy services
        ~Service Correlation Analysis: Combining multiple discovery methods to map service dependencies and relationships
      Device Driver Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to enumerate local device drivers on a victim host. Information about device drivers may highlight various insights that shape follow-on behaviors, such as the function/purpose of the host, present security tools (i.e. [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001)) or other defenses (e.g., [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497)), as well as potential exploitable vulnerabilities (e.g., [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068)).  Many OS utilities may provide information about local device drivers, such as `driverquery.exe` and the `EnumDeviceDrivers()` API function on Windows.(Citation: Microsoft Driverquery)(Citation: Microsoft EnumDeviceDrivers) Information about device drivers (as well as associated services, i.e., [System Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1007)) may also be available in the Registry.(Citation: Microsoft Registry Drivers)  On Linux/macOS, device drivers (in the form of kernel modules) may be visible within `/dev` or using utilities such as `lsmod` and `modinfo`.(Citation: Linux Kernel Programming)(Citation: lsmod man)(Citation: modinfo man)
        ~Windows Registry Enumeration: Adversaries query Windows Registry to identify installed drivers and their configurations
        ~API-Based Discovery: Use of system APIs like EnumDeviceDrivers() and NtQuerySystemInformation() to list drivers
        ~Command-Line Utilities: Using built-in OS commands like driverquery, sc, powershell Get-WindowsDriver
        ~Linux Kernel Module Enumeration: Using commands like lsmod, modinfo, and accessing /proc/modules
        ~Driver Stack Inspection: Examining driver stack relationships and dependencies
        ~Security Driver Targeting: Specifically focusing on discovering security product drivers
        ~Hardware Interface Enumeration: Examining PnP devices and associated drivers
        ~Service-Based Discovery: Enumerating drivers through their associated services
      Virtual Machine Discovery: An adversary may attempt to enumerate running virtual machines (VMs) after gaining access to a host or hypervisor. For example, adversaries may enumerate a list of VMs on an ESXi hypervisor using a [Hypervisor CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/012) such as `esxcli` or `vim-cmd` (e.g. `esxcli vm process list or vim-cmd vmsvc/getallvms`).(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)(Citation: TrendMicro Play) Adversaries may also directly leverage a graphical user interface, such as VMware vCenter, in order to view virtual machines on a host.   Adversaries may use the information from [Virtual Machine Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1673) during discovery to shape follow-on behaviors. Subsequently discovered VMs may be leveraged for follow-on activities such as [Service Stop](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1489) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486).(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)
        ~Command Line Interface Discovery: Use of hypervisor CLI tools like esxcli, vim-cmd, or PowerCLI to enumerate VMs
        ~Management Console Discovery: Use of GUI-based management tools like vCenter, Hyper-V Manager to identify VMs
        ~API-based Discovery: Leveraging management APIs and SDKs to programmatically discover VMs
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Examining VM configuration files and directories to identify VM instances
        ~Network Service Discovery: Scanning for VM management ports and services to identify virtualization infrastructure
        ~Process Analysis: Examining host processes and services to identify running VM instances
        ~Memory Analysis: Analyzing host memory to identify VM-related artifacts and running instances
        ~Storage Infrastructure Analysis: Examining shared storage systems and datastores for VM disk files
        ~Resource Utilization Analysis: Monitoring system resources to identify VM workloads
        ~Snapshot Analysis: Examining VM snapshot metadata and files to discover VM instances
        ~Registry Analysis: Examining system registry and configuration databases for virtualization components
        ~Log-based Discovery: Analyzing system and application logs to identify VM-related activities
        ~Backup Infrastructure Analysis: Examining backup systems and repositories to discover VM inventory
        ~Container Platform Analysis: Investigating container orchestration platforms that may manage VMs
    lateral-movement: The adversary is trying to move through your environment.  Lateral Movement consists of techniques that adversaries use to enter and control remote systems on a network. Following through on their primary objective often requires exploring the network to find their target and subsequently gaining access to it. Reaching their objective often involves pivoting through multiple systems and accounts to gain. Adversaries might install their own remote access tools to accomplish Lateral Movement or use legitimate credentials with native network and operating system tools, which may be stealthier.
      Application Deployment Software: Adversaries may deploy malicious software to systems within a network using application deployment systems employed by enterprise administrators. The permissions required for this action vary by system configuration; local credentials may be sufficient with direct access to the deployment server, or specific domain credentials may be required. However, the system may require an administrative account to log in or to perform software deployment.  Access to a network-wide or enterprise-wide software deployment system enables an adversary to have remote code execution on all systems that are connected to such a system. The access may be used to laterally move to systems, gather information, or cause a specific effect, such as wiping the hard drives on all endpoints.
        ~SCCM/Configuration Manager: Abuse of Microsoft System Center Configuration Manager to deploy malicious packages across an enterprise
        ~PDQ Deploy: Exploitation of PDQ Deploy software to push malicious executables to managed endpoints
        ~Altiris Deployment Solution: Misuse of Symantec's deployment solution for unauthorized software distribution
        ~IBM BigFix: Leveraging BigFix platform to deploy malicious payloads across managed systems
        ~LANDesk Management Suite: Abuse of LANDesk's software distribution capabilities
        ~Citrix Provisioning Services: Exploitation of Citrix PVS for malicious software deployment
        ~Dell KACE Systems Management: Abuse of KACE appliance for unauthorized software distribution
        ~ManageEngine Desktop Central: Leveraging Desktop Central's software deployment features
        ~Jamf Pro: Exploitation of Jamf Pro for deploying malicious packages to macOS systems
        ~Group Policy Software Installation: Abuse of GPO software installation policies for malware deployment
        ~Puppet Enterprise: Abuse of Puppet automation platform to deploy malicious configurations and software
        ~Chef Automate: Exploitation of Chef infrastructure automation for unauthorized deployments
        ~Ansible Tower: Leveraging RedHat Ansible automation platform for malicious playbook execution
        ~Jenkins CI/CD: Abuse of Jenkins deployment pipelines to distribute malware
        ~Kubernetes Deployment Controllers: Exploitation of K8s deployment mechanisms to spread malicious containers
        ~VMware vSphere Update Manager: Abuse of VMware's update and patch deployment capabilities
        ~Ivanti Endpoint Manager: Leveraging Ivanti's software deployment features
        ~Quest KACE Cloud MDM: Abuse of cloud-based mobile device management for malware distribution
        ~Microsoft Intune: Exploitation of Intune's cloud-based deployment capabilities
        ~SolarWinds Deployment Tools: Abuse of SolarWinds software distribution mechanisms
        ~Blackboard Learn Deployment: Abuse of educational institution deployment mechanisms
        ~FOG Project: Exploitation of open-source computer cloning and management solution
        ~Endpoint Central: Abuse of ManageEngine's unified endpoint management and deployment
        ~Baramundi Management Suite: Leveraging Baramundi's software deployment capabilities
