        ~SCADA Software Deployment: Leveraging industrial control system deployment capabilities
        ~IoT Device Management Platforms: Exploitation of IoT fleet management deployment
        ~Mobile Network Element Manager: Abuse of telco infrastructure deployment systems
        ~ATM Software Deployment Tools: Exploitation of ATM fleet management systems
        ~Digital Signage Management: Abuse of digital display deployment systems
        ~Building Management Systems: Leveraging BMS software deployment capabilities
        ~Embedded Device Update Systems: Exploitation of embedded system firmware deployment
      SSH Hijacking: Secure Shell (SSH) is a standard means of remote access on Linux and macOS systems. It allows a user to connect to another system via an encrypted tunnel, commonly authenticating through a password, certificate or the use of an asymmetric encryption key pair.  In order to move laterally from a compromised host, adversaries may take advantage of trust relationships established with other systems via public key authentication in active SSH sessions by hijacking an existing connection to another system. This may occur through compromising the SSH agent itself or by having access to the agent's socket. If an adversary is able to obtain root access, then hijacking SSH sessions is likely trivial. (Citation: Slideshare Abusing SSH) (Citation: SSHjack Blackhat) (Citation: Clockwork SSH Agent Hijacking) Compromising the SSH agent also provides access to intercept SSH credentials. (Citation: Welivesecurity Ebury SSH)  [SSH Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1184) differs from use of [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) because it injects into an existing SSH session rather than creating a new session using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).
        ~SSH Agent Compromise: Adversaries directly compromise the SSH agent process to intercept and hijack existing SSH sessions, potentially by injecting malicious code or replacing the agent binary.
        ~SSH Socket Hijacking: Attackers access and manipulate the SSH agent socket file to take control of existing SSH connections, often by exploiting file permissions or symbolic links.
        ~Root-Level Session Takeover: Using elevated privileges to directly manipulate SSH session data in memory or on disk when root access is obtained.
        ~SSH Agent Forwarding Abuse: Exploiting SSH agent forwarding functionality to hijack forwarded authentication credentials and establish unauthorized connections.
        ~SSH Config Manipulation: Modifying SSH configuration files to redirect or intercept SSH sessions through malicious proxies or alternative authentication methods.
      Shared Webroot: **This technique has been deprecated and should no longer be used.**  Adversaries may add malicious content to an internally accessible website through an open network file share that contains the website's webroot or Web content directory (Citation: Microsoft Web Root OCT 2016) (Citation: Apache Server 2018) and then browse to that content with a Web browser to cause the server to execute the malicious content. The malicious content will typically run under the context and permissions of the Web server process, often resulting in local system or administrative privileges, depending on how the Web server is configured.  This mechanism of shared access and remote execution could be used for lateral movement to the system running the Web server. For example, a Web server running PHP with an open network share could allow an adversary to upload a remote access tool and PHP script to execute the RAT on the system running the Web server when a specific page is visited. (Citation: Webroot PHP 2011)
        ~PHP File Upload: Upload malicious PHP files to shared webroot directories to achieve remote code execution through web requests
        ~ASP/ASPX Exploitation: Leverage ASP.NET web applications by placing malicious ASP/ASPX files in shared directories
        ~CGI Script Injection: Upload malicious CGI scripts to execute commands on the web server
        ~Web Config Manipulation: Modify web server configuration files in shared directories to enable malicious module loading
        ~Web Shell Deployment: Place web shells in shared webroot locations for persistent access
        ~Static Resource Poisoning: Inject malicious code into shared static resources like JavaScript files
        ~Server-Side Include Abuse: Exploit server-side include functionality through shared file access
        ~Virtual Directory Manipulation: Create or modify virtual directory configurations to execute malicious content
      Exploitation of Remote Services: Adversaries may exploit remote services to gain unauthorized access to internal systems once inside of a network. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for lateral movement to enable access to a remote system.  An adversary may need to determine if the remote system is in a vulnerable state, which may be done through [Network Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1046) or other Discovery methods looking for common, vulnerable software that may be deployed in the network, the lack of certain patches that may indicate vulnerabilities,  or security software that may be used to detect or contain remote exploitation. Servers are likely a high value target for lateral movement exploitation, but endpoint systems may also be at risk if they provide an advantage or access to additional resources.  There are several well-known vulnerabilities that exist in common services such as SMB(Citation: CIS Multiple SMB Vulnerabilities) and RDP(Citation: NVD CVE-2017-0176) as well as applications that may be used within internal networks such as MySQL(Citation: NVD CVE-2016-6662) and web server services.(Citation: NVD CVE-2014-7169)(Citation: Ars Technica VMWare Code Execution Vulnerability 2021) Additionally, there have been a number of vulnerabilities in VMware vCenter installations, which may enable threat actors to move laterally from the compromised vCenter server to virtual machines or even to ESXi hypervisors.(Citation: Broadcom VMSA-2024-0019)  Depending on the permissions level of the vulnerable remote service an adversary may achieve [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068) as a result of lateral movement exploitation as well.
        ~SMB Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Server Message Block (SMB) protocol implementations to move laterally within networks
        ~RDP Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) services to gain unauthorized access to remote systems
        ~Database Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in database services like MySQL to compromise database servers and move laterally
        ~Web Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in web servers and applications to gain access to internal systems
        ~Virtualization Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in virtualization management interfaces like VMware vCenter to compromise virtual infrastructure
        ~Container Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in container orchestration and management services to move between containerized environments
        ~Directory Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in directory services like Active Directory to gain broader network access
        ~Remote Management Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in remote management tools and protocols to gain unauthorized system access
        ~File Transfer Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in file transfer services to compromise systems and move laterally
        ~Remote Access Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in VPN and other remote access services to gain network entry points
        ~Print Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in print spooler and other printing services to move laterally within networks
        ~Email Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in internal email servers and services to gain unauthorized access
        ~Network Management Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in network monitoring and management tools to compromise systems
        ~Authentication Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in authentication services and protocols beyond directory services
        ~Application Deployment Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in software deployment and update services to spread across networks
        ~Time Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in network time protocol (NTP) services and time synchronization mechanisms
        ~DNS Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Domain Name System (DNS) services to compromise systems and move laterally
        ~Collaboration Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in enterprise collaboration platforms and their components
        ~ICS Protocol Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in industrial control system protocols and services to move within operational technology networks
      Remote Service Session Hijacking: Adversaries may take control of preexisting sessions with remote services to move laterally in an environment. Users may use valid credentials to log into a service specifically designed to accept remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and RDP. When a user logs into a service, a session will be established that will allow them to maintain a continuous interaction with that service.  Adversaries may commandeer these sessions to carry out actions on remote systems. [Remote Service Session Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1563) differs from use of [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) because it hijacks an existing session rather than creating a new session using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).(Citation: RDP Hijacking Medium)(Citation: Breach Post-mortem SSH Hijack)
        RDP Hijacking: Adversaries may hijack a legitimate user’s remote desktop session to move laterally within an environment. Remote desktop is a common feature in operating systems. It allows a user to log into an interactive session with a system desktop graphical user interface on a remote system. Microsoft refers to its implementation of the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) as Remote Desktop Services (RDS).(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services)  Adversaries may perform RDP session hijacking which involves stealing a legitimate user's remote session. Typically, a user is notified when someone else is trying to steal their session. With System permissions and using Terminal Services Console, `c:\windows\system32\tscon.exe [session number to be stolen]`, an adversary can hijack a session without the need for credentials or prompts to the user.(Citation: RDP Hijacking Korznikov) This can be done remotely or locally and with active or disconnected sessions.(Citation: RDP Hijacking Medium) It can also lead to [Remote System Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1018) and Privilege Escalation by stealing a Domain Admin or higher privileged account session. All of this can be done by using native Windows commands, but it has also been added as a feature in red teaming tools.(Citation: Kali Redsnarf)
          Use tscon.exe with System privileges to hijack active RDP session: `tscon.exe <session_number> /dest:<sessionname>`
