      Replication Through Removable Media: Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.  Mobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).
        ~USB Drive Autorun Manipulation: Adversaries modify autorun.inf files on USB drives to automatically execute malware when connected
        ~Mobile Device USB Mounting: Exploitation of mobile devices appearing as USB storage to deliver malware to target systems
        ~Malicious Firmware Modification: Modification of removable media firmware to persist and spread malware
        ~File Name Spoofing: Renaming malware to appear as legitimate files commonly found on removable media
        ~Charging Cable Exploitation: Use of modified USB charging cables that contain malicious capabilities
        ~Removable Media Pre-infection: Compromise of systems used to format or initialize removable media
        ~Air-Gap Bypass via Physical Media: Specific techniques targeting air-gapped systems through removable media
        ~Multi-Stage Payload Delivery: Use of staged malware delivery through removable media to evade detection
        ~Cross-Platform Media Infection: Techniques for infecting multiple operating systems via single removable media
        ~Supply Chain Media Compromise: Manipulation of removable media during manufacturing or distribution
        ~Optical Media Autorun: Exploitation of autorun features specific to CD/DVD/Blu-ray media
        ~External Hard Drive Firmware Attack: Specific techniques targeting external HDD/SSD firmware
        ~Memory Card Manipulation: Exploitation of memory cards used in cameras, phones, and other devices
        ~Industrial Protocol Media: Targeting specialized industrial removable media formats and protocols
        ~Legacy Port Exploitation: Techniques specific to legacy removable media interfaces (parallel, serial ports)
        ~Printer Storage Media: Exploitation of removable storage in multifunction printers and copiers
        ~Backup Media Compromise: Targeting tape drives and other backup media
        ~Hardware Key Emulation: Malicious devices that emulate security dongles or hardware keys
      Internal Spearphishing: After they already have access to accounts or systems within the environment, adversaries may use internal spearphishing to gain access to additional information or compromise other users within the same organization. Internal spearphishing is multi-staged campaign where a legitimate account is initially compromised either by controlling the user's device or by compromising the account credentials of the user. Adversaries may then attempt to take advantage of the trusted internal account to increase the likelihood of tricking more victims into falling for phish attempts, often incorporating [Impersonation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1656).(Citation: Trend Micro - Int SP)  For example, adversaries may leverage [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/001) or [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002) as part of internal spearphishing to deliver a payload or redirect to an external site to capture credentials through [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056) on sites that mimic login interfaces.  Adversaries may also leverage internal chat apps, such as Microsoft Teams, to spread malicious content or engage users in attempts to capture sensitive information and/or credentials.(Citation: Int SP - chat apps)
        ~Internal Email Spoofing: Adversaries send phishing emails using compromised internal email accounts or by spoofing internal email addresses to appear legitimate to recipients within the organization.
        ~Chat Application Abuse: Adversaries leverage internal chat platforms (Teams, Slack, etc.) to send malicious links or files to targets using compromised accounts.
        ~Intranet Forum/Portal Exploitation: Adversaries post malicious content on internal forums, SharePoint sites, or other collaboration platforms using compromised credentials.
        ~Calendar Invitation Phishing: Adversaries send malicious meeting invites or calendar items containing harmful links or attachments using compromised accounts.
        ~IT Support Impersonation: Adversaries pose as internal IT support staff using compromised accounts to request sensitive information or credentials.
        ~Executive Impersonation: Adversaries use compromised executive accounts or impersonate executives to increase the likelihood of success through authority bias.
        ~Project-Based Targeting: Adversaries use knowledge of internal projects and workflows to craft targeted phishing attempts relevant to ongoing work.
        ~Department-Specific Campaigns: Adversaries target specific departments (HR, Finance, etc.) with contextually relevant phishing content using internal knowledge.
        ~Internal Training Simulation: Adversaries disguise phishing attempts as legitimate security awareness training or compliance exercises.
        ~Document Collaboration Phishing: Adversaries use internal document sharing and collaboration features to share malicious files or links.
        ~Help Desk Ticket Exploitation: Adversaries create or manipulate internal help desk tickets to deliver malicious content or request sensitive information.
        ~Internal Survey/Form Phishing: Adversaries use compromised accounts to distribute malicious internal surveys or forms requesting sensitive information.
        ~Internal File Share Notifications: Adversaries abuse file sharing notification systems to send malicious links disguised as legitimate file access requests.
        ~Printer/Scanner Notification Abuse: Adversaries send fake printer or scanner notifications containing malicious links or attachments.
        ~Internal Newsletter Compromise: Adversaries hijack or spoof internal newsletter distributions to deliver malicious content.
        ~Employee Resource Portal Abuse: Adversaries leverage compromised HR or employee resource portals to distribute malicious content.
        ~Internal Application Update Phishing: Adversaries pose as system administrators sending fake internal application update notifications.
        ~Workflow Automation Abuse: Adversaries exploit automated workflow notifications (like approval requests) to deliver phishing content.
      Pass the Ticket: Pass the ticket (PtT) is a method of authenticating to a system using Kerberos tickets without having access to an account's password. Kerberos authentication can be used as the first step to lateral movement to a remote system.  In this technique, valid Kerberos tickets for [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) are captured by [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). A user's service tickets or ticket granting ticket (TGT) may be obtained, depending on the level of access. A service ticket allows for access to a particular resource, whereas a TGT can be used to request service tickets from the Ticket Granting Service (TGS) to access any resource the user has privileges to access. (Citation: ADSecurity AD Kerberos Attacks) (Citation: GentilKiwi Pass the Ticket)  Silver Tickets can be obtained for services that use Kerberos as an authentication mechanism and are used to generate tickets to access that particular resource and the system that hosts the resource (e.g., SharePoint). (Citation: ADSecurity AD Kerberos Attacks)  Golden Tickets can be obtained for the domain using the Key Distribution Service account KRBTGT account NTLM hash, which enables generation of TGTs for any account in Active Directory. (Citation: Campbell 2014)
        ~Silver Ticket: Creation and use of forged service tickets for specific services using service account NTLM hashes
        ~Golden Ticket: Creation and use of forged TGTs using the KRBTGT account hash
        ~TGT Harvesting: Extracting and reusing legitimate Ticket Granting Tickets from memory
        ~Service Ticket Harvesting: Collecting and reusing service tickets from legitimate sessions
        ~Pass-the-Key: Using extracted AES keys or NTLM hashes to request new tickets
        ~Ticket Modification: Altering existing tickets to escalate privileges or extend validity periods
        ~Cross-Realm Ticket Abuse: Exploiting trust relationships between different Kerberos realms
        ~Diamond Ticket: Creating specially crafted tickets that blend legitimate and forged elements
      Pass the Hash: Pass the hash (PtH) is a method of authenticating as a user without having access to the user's cleartext password. This method bypasses standard authentication steps that require a cleartext password, moving directly into the portion of the authentication that uses the password hash. In this technique, valid password hashes for the account being used are captured using a Credential Access technique. Captured hashes are used with PtH to authenticate as that user. Once authenticated, PtH may be used to perform actions on local or remote systems.   Windows 7 and higher with KB2871997 require valid domain user credentials or RID 500 administrator hashes. (Citation: NSA Spotting)
        ~NTLM Hash Relay: Using captured NTLM hashes to authenticate to other systems by relaying the authentication request
        ~Kerberos Hash Transmission: Utilizing captured Kerberos ticket hashes for authentication across domain systems
        ~Local Account Hash Exploitation: Using local administrator account hashes for lateral movement across workstations
        ~Domain Cache Credential Hash: Leveraging cached domain credential hashes stored on compromised systems
        ~Service Account Hash Abuse: Exploiting service account password hashes for persistent lateral access
        ~RID 500 Administrator Hash: Specifically targeting and using the built-in administrator account hash
        ~Remote Service Session Hash: Using harvested hashes to authenticate to remote services like WMI or PSExec
        ~Network Share Authentication: Leveraging captured hashes to access network shares and resources
      Component Object Model and Distributed COM: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Distributed Component Object Model](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/003) and [Component Object Model](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559/001).**  Adversaries may use the Windows Component Object Model (COM) and Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM) for local code execution or to execute on remote systems as part of lateral movement.   COM is a component of the native Windows application programming interface (API) that enables interaction between software objects, or executable code that implements one or more interfaces.(Citation: Fireeye Hunting COM June 2019) Through COM, a client object can call methods of server objects, which are typically Dynamic Link Libraries (DLL) or executables (EXE).(Citation: Microsoft COM) DCOM is transparent middleware that extends the functionality of Component Object Model (COM) (Citation: Microsoft COM) beyond a local computer using remote procedure call (RPC) technology.(Citation: Fireeye Hunting COM June 2019)  Permissions to interact with local and remote server COM objects are specified by access control lists (ACL) in the Registry. (Citation: Microsoft COM ACL)(Citation: Microsoft Process Wide Com Keys)(Citation: Microsoft System Wide Com Keys) By default, only Administrators may remotely activate and launch COM objects through DCOM.  Adversaries may abuse COM for local command and/or payload execution. Various COM interfaces are exposed that can be abused to invoke arbitrary execution via a variety of programming languages such as C, C++, Java, and VBScript.(Citation: Microsoft COM) Specific COM objects also exists to directly perform functions beyond code execution, such as creating a [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053), fileless download/execution, and other adversary behaviors such as Privilege Escalation and Persistence.(Citation: Fireeye Hunting COM June 2019)(Citation: ProjectZero File Write EoP Apr 2018)  Adversaries may use DCOM for lateral movement. Through DCOM, adversaries operating in the context of an appropriately privileged user can remotely obtain arbitrary and even direct shellcode execution through Office applications (Citation: Enigma Outlook DCOM Lateral Movement Nov 2017) as well as other Windows objects that contain insecure methods.(Citation: Enigma MMC20 COM Jan 2017)(Citation: Enigma DCOM Lateral Movement Jan 2017) DCOM can also execute macros in existing documents (Citation: Enigma Excel DCOM Sept 2017) and may also invoke [Dynamic Data Exchange](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1173) (DDE) execution directly through a COM created instance of a Microsoft Office application (Citation: Cyberreason DCOM DDE Lateral Movement Nov 2017), bypassing the need for a malicious document.
        ~Office Application COM Hijacking: Adversaries may abuse COM objects in Microsoft Office applications for code execution and lateral movement
        ~MMC20 Application COM Exploitation: Adversaries may target the MMC20 application COM object for remote code execution
        ~Registry COM Hijacking: Adversaries may modify COM registry entries to intercept legitimate COM object calls
        ~DCOM Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may directly manipulate DCOM protocol for remote execution
        ~COM Service Exploitation: Adversaries may target COM-enabled services for privilege escalation and persistence
        ~Excel DCOM Macro Execution: Adversaries may leverage Excel's DCOM capabilities to execute malicious macros remotely
        ~Outlook DCOM Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse Outlook's DCOM interfaces for code execution
        ~COM Object Creation Abuse: Adversaries may create malicious COM objects to establish persistence
        ~DCOM Shell Execution: Adversaries may use DCOM to directly execute shell commands on remote systems
        ~COM Interface Method Hijacking: Adversaries may hijack COM interface methods to intercept and modify functionality
        ~COM Authentication Bypass: Adversaries may exploit COM authentication mechanisms to gain unauthorized access
        ~System Service DLL COM Abuse: Adversaries may target COM-enabled system service DLLs for persistence
        ~COM Launch Permissions Modification: Adversaries may modify COM launch permissions to enable unauthorized access
        ~DCOM Network Protocol Tunneling: Adversaries may tunnel other protocols through DCOM for covert communication
        ~COM Object Memory Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate COM object memory to execute arbitrary code
        ~Scheduled Task COM Hijacking: Adversaries may abuse COM objects used by Windows Task Scheduler
        ~WMI COM Interface Abuse: Adversaries may exploit WMI's COM interfaces for system manipulation
        ~COM Data Binding Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse COM data binding mechanisms to execute code
      Windows Remote Management: Windows Remote Management (WinRM) is the name of both a Windows service and a protocol that allows a user to interact with a remote system (e.g., run an executable, modify the Registry, modify services). (Citation: Microsoft WinRM) It may be called with the <code>winrm</code> command or by any number of programs such as PowerShell. (Citation: Jacobsen 2014)
        ~Interactive Session: Adversaries may use WinRM to establish interactive PowerShell sessions with remote systems for command execution and system management
        ~Script-Based Execution: Adversaries may use WinRM to execute scripts and commands remotely without establishing an interactive session
        ~Credential-Based Access: Adversaries may use stolen or compromised credentials to authenticate to WinRM services on remote systems
        ~Group Policy Modification: Adversaries may modify Group Policy settings to enable and configure WinRM on target systems
        ~Port Forwarding: Adversaries may use port forwarding techniques to tunnel WinRM traffic through intermediary systems
        ~Service Configuration Modification: Adversaries may modify WinRM service configurations to maintain persistence and enable remote access
        ~HTTP/HTTPS Transport: Adversaries may leverage both HTTP and HTTPS transport options available in WinRM for remote system access
        ~WS-Management Protocol: Adversaries may exploit the underlying WS-Management protocol implementation for custom remote management operations
      Taint Shared Content:  Adversaries may deliver payloads to remote systems by adding content to shared storage locations, such as network drives or internal code repositories. Content stored on network drives or in other shared locations may be tainted by adding malicious programs, scripts, or exploit code to otherwise valid files. Once a user opens the shared tainted content, the malicious portion can be executed to run the adversary's code on a remote system. Adversaries may use tainted shared content to move laterally.  A directory share pivot is a variation on this technique that uses several other techniques to propagate malware when users access a shared network directory. It uses [Shortcut Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/009) of directory .LNK files that use [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036) to look like the real directories, which are hidden through [Hidden Files and Directories](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/001). The malicious .LNK-based directories have an embedded command that executes the hidden malware file in the directory and then opens the real intended directory so that the user's expected action still occurs. When used with frequently used network directories, the technique may result in frequent reinfections and broad access to systems and potentially to new and higher privileged accounts. (Citation: Retwin Directory Share Pivot)  Adversaries may also compromise shared network directories through binary infections by appending or prepending its code to the healthy binary on the shared network directory. The malware may modify the original entry point (OEP) of the healthy binary to ensure that it is executed before the legitimate code. The infection could continue to spread via the newly infected file when it is executed by a remote system. These infections may target both binary and non-binary formats that end with extensions including, but not limited to, .EXE, .DLL, .SCR, .BAT, and/or .VBS.
        ~Directory Share Pivot: Uses modified .LNK files and hidden malware to create malicious directory structures that execute code when accessed
        ~Binary Infection: Modifies legitimate binaries in shared locations by appending or prepending malicious code and altering the original entry point
        ~Network Share Manipulation: Targets shared network drives and folders with malicious content
        ~Code Repository Poisoning: Injects malicious code into shared code repositories and development resources
        ~Script Injection: Adds malicious scripts to shared script files or configuration files
        ~Shortcut File Manipulation: Modifies shortcut files to execute malicious code while maintaining apparent legitimate functionality
        ~Configuration File Tampering: Modifies shared configuration files to include malicious commands or scripts
        ~Document Template Poisoning: Injects malicious content into shared document templates or forms
      Windows Admin Shares: Windows systems have hidden network shares that are accessible only to administrators and provide the ability for remote file copy and other administrative functions. Example network shares include <code>C$</code>, <code>ADMIN$</code>, and <code>IPC$</code>.   Adversaries may use this technique in conjunction with administrator-level [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to remotely access a networked system over server message block (SMB) (Citation: Wikipedia SMB) to interact with systems using remote procedure calls (RPCs), (Citation: TechNet RPC) transfer files, and run transferred binaries through remote Execution. Example execution techniques that rely on authenticated sessions over SMB/RPC are [Scheduled Task/Job](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053), [Service Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1035), and [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047). Adversaries can also use NTLM hashes to access administrator shares on systems with [Pass the Hash](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1075) and certain configuration and patch levels. (Citation: Microsoft Admin Shares)  The [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility can be used to connect to Windows admin shares on remote systems using <code>net use</code> commands with valid credentials. (Citation: Technet Net Use)
        ~Remote Service Access: Using admin shares to connect to and manipulate remote services through RPC calls over SMB
        ~File System Operations: Leveraging admin shares for remote file system access, copying malware, and data theft
        ~ADMIN$ Exploitation: Specifically targeting the ADMIN$ share for system-level access and remote administration
        ~IPC$ Share Abuse: Exploiting the Inter-Process Communication share for enumeration and remote procedure calls
        ~C$ Drive Access: Utilizing the default C$ share for direct access to the system drive and file operations
        ~Hidden Share Discovery: Techniques for discovering and accessing non-standard administrative shares
        ~Share Permission Abuse: Exploiting misconfigured share permissions for unauthorized access
        ~Authentication Bypass: Methods to bypass authentication requirements for admin shares
      Lateral Tool Transfer: Adversaries may transfer tools or other files between systems in a compromised environment. Once brought into the victim environment (i.e., [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105)) files may then be copied from one system to another to stage adversary tools or other files over the course of an operation.  Adversaries may copy files between internal victim systems to support lateral movement using inherent file sharing protocols such as file sharing over [SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002) to connected network shares or with authenticated connections via [Remote Desktop Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/001).(Citation: Unit42 LockerGoga 2019)  Files can also be transferred using native or otherwise present tools on the victim system, such as scp, rsync, curl, sftp, and [ftp](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0095). In some cases, adversaries may be able to leverage [Web Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102)s such as Dropbox or OneDrive to copy files from one machine to another via shared, automatically synced folders.(Citation: Dropbox Malware Sync)
        ~SMB File Transfer: Adversaries leverage Server Message Block protocol to transfer files between Windows systems using shared folders and admin shares
        ~Remote Desktop File Transfer: Adversaries use RDP's built-in clipboard and drive sharing features to transfer files between systems
        ~Secure Copy Protocol Transfer: Adversaries utilize SCP for secure file transfers between Unix-like systems
        ~FTP/SFTP Transfer: Adversaries employ FTP or SFTP protocols for file transfers using built-in or installed clients
        ~Cloud Storage Sync: Adversaries abuse cloud storage services like Dropbox or OneDrive for file transfers via shared folders
        ~Network Share Mount: Adversaries mount remote network shares to transfer files between systems
        ~Web Browser Download: Adversaries host files on compromised internal web servers for distribution to other systems
        ~Rsync Transfer: Adversaries use rsync for efficient file synchronization between Unix-like systems
        ~PowerShell File Transfer: Adversaries leverage PowerShell commands and modules for file transfers
        ~WMI File Transfer: Adversaries use Windows Management Instrumentation for file transfers between Windows systems
        ~BITS Transfer: Adversaries abuse Background Intelligent Transfer Service for stealthy file transfers
        ~NFS Transfer: Adversaries utilize Network File System protocol in Unix/Linux environments
        ~Memory-Mapped Transfer: Adversaries transfer files through memory-mapped regions across processes or systems
        ~SQL Server Link Transfer: Adversaries leverage SQL Server database links to transfer files between instances
        ~Exchange Server Transfer: Adversaries abuse Exchange Server features to move files between systems
        ~Distributed File System Transfer: Adversaries leverage DFS replication for file transfers
        ~Group Policy Transfer: Adversaries abuse Group Policy file distribution mechanisms
        ~Print Spooler Transfer: Adversaries exploit print spooler functionality to transfer files
        ~Container Volume Transfer: Adversaries abuse container volume mounts to transfer files between containers and hosts
        ~Hypervisor Transfer: Adversaries leverage hypervisor features to transfer files between VMs
        ~ICMP Tunneling Transfer: Adversaries tunnel file transfers through ICMP protocol
        ~DNS Transfer: Adversaries abuse DNS protocol for covert file transfers between systems
        ~Backup Software Transfer: Adversaries abuse backup software's distribution mechanisms
        ~Distributed Cache Transfer: Adversaries leverage distributed cache systems (like Redis, Memcached) to transfer files
        ~Configuration Management Transfer: Adversaries abuse configuration management tools (like Ansible, Chef) for file distribution
    collection: The adversary is trying to gather data of interest to their goal.  Collection consists of techniques adversaries may use to gather information and the sources information is collected from that are relevant to following through on the adversary's objectives. Frequently, the next goal after collecting data is to either steal (exfiltrate) the data or to use the data to gain more information about the target environment. Common target sources include various drive types, browsers, audio, video, and email. Common collection methods include capturing screenshots and keyboard input.
      Input Capture: Adversaries may use methods of capturing user input to obtain credentials or collect information. During normal system usage, users often provide credentials to various different locations, such as login pages/portals or system dialog boxes. Input capture mechanisms may be transparent to the user (e.g. [Credential API Hooking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/004)) or rely on deceiving the user into providing input into what they believe to be a genuine service (e.g. [Web Portal Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/003)).
        Keylogging: Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials as the user types them. Keylogging is likely to be used to acquire credentials for new access opportunities when [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) efforts are not effective, and may require an adversary to intercept keystrokes on a system for a substantial period of time before credentials can be successfully captured. In order to increase the likelihood of capturing credentials quickly, an adversary may also perform actions such as clearing browser cookies to force users to reauthenticate to systems.(Citation: Talos Kimsuky Nov 2021)  Keylogging is the most prevalent type of input capture, with many different ways of intercepting keystrokes.(Citation: Adventures of a Keystroke) Some methods include:  * Hooking API callbacks used for processing keystrokes. Unlike [Credential API Hooking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/004), this focuses solely on API functions intended for processing keystroke data. * Reading raw keystroke data from the hardware buffer. * Windows Registry modifications. * Custom drivers. * [Modify System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601) may provide adversaries with hooks into the operating system of network devices to read raw keystrokes for login sessions.(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)
