          Extract email backups from Windows File History
          Parse Outlook Web App cached data from browser profiles
          Use Volatility framework to extract email artifacts from memory dumps
          Access Evolution email client local storage on WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux)
      Data from Network Shared Drive: Adversaries may search network shares on computers they have compromised to find files of interest. Sensitive data can be collected from remote systems via shared network drives (host shared directory, network file server, etc.) that are accessible from the current system prior to Exfiltration. Interactive command shells may be in use, and common functionality within [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) may be used to gather information.
        ~SMB Share Enumeration: Adversaries may use SMB protocol to enumerate and access network shares, using built-in Windows commands or tools to list and access available shares.
        ~NFS Mount Point Access: Adversaries may target Network File System (NFS) mount points in Unix/Linux environments to collect data from shared directories.
        ~WebDAV Share Collection: Adversaries may access WebDAV-enabled shares to collect files through HTTP/HTTPS protocols.
        ~DFS Share Targeting: Adversaries may specifically target Distributed File System (DFS) shares to access data distributed across multiple servers.
        ~Administrative Share Access: Adversaries may focus on collecting data from default administrative shares (C$, ADMIN$) when they have appropriate privileges.
        ~Cloud Storage Sync Folders: Adversaries may target synchronized cloud storage folders (like OneDrive, Dropbox) that appear as network shares.
        ~Automated Share Crawling: Adversaries may use automated tools to recursively crawl through accessible network shares to identify and collect data.
        ~Shadow Copy Access: Adversaries may access Volume Shadow Copies through network shares to collect historical versions of files.
      Browser Session Hijacking: Adversaries may take advantage of security vulnerabilities and inherent functionality in browser software to change content, modify user-behaviors, and intercept information as part of various browser session hijacking techniques.(Citation: Wikipedia Man in the Browser)  A specific example is when an adversary injects software into a browser that allows them to inherit cookies, HTTP sessions, and SSL client certificates of a user then use the browser as a way to pivot into an authenticated intranet.(Citation: Cobalt Strike Browser Pivot)(Citation: ICEBRG Chrome Extensions) Executing browser-based behaviors such as pivoting may require specific process permissions, such as <code>SeDebugPrivilege</code> and/or high-integrity/administrator rights.  Another example involves pivoting browser traffic from the adversary's browser through the user's browser by setting up a proxy which will redirect web traffic. This does not alter the user's traffic in any way, and the proxy connection can be severed as soon as the browser is closed. The adversary assumes the security context of whichever browser process the proxy is injected into. Browsers typically create a new process for each tab that is opened and permissions and certificates are separated accordingly. With these permissions, an adversary could potentially browse to any resource on an intranet, such as [Sharepoint](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1213/002) or webmail, that is accessible through the browser and which the browser has sufficient permissions. Browser pivoting may also bypass security provided by 2-factor authentication.(Citation: cobaltstrike manual)
        ~Man-in-the-Browser Malware: Use of malware specifically designed to intercept and modify browser communications in real-time while residing in the browser process
        ~Cookie Theft and Replay: Extraction and reuse of authentication cookies to impersonate legitimate users' sessions
        ~Browser Extension Abuse: Exploitation of browser extensions or injection of malicious extensions to gain access to browser sessions
        ~SSL/TLS Session Hijacking: Interception and manipulation of SSL/TLS sessions through certificate manipulation or downgrade attacks
        ~Proxy Injection: Implementation of malicious proxies to intercept and redirect browser traffic
        ~Browser Process Injection: Direct injection of malicious code into browser processes to inherit security contexts
        ~Session Token Manipulation: Modification or forging of session tokens to gain unauthorized access
        ~Browser API Abuse: Exploitation of browser APIs to access sensitive session data and functionality
        ~Browser Helper Object Exploitation: Use of browser helper objects to intercept and modify browser behavior
        ~Cross-Process Cookie Theft: Extraction of cookie data from browser processes through cross-process techniques
        ~HTML5 Storage Exploitation: Manipulation of HTML5 localStorage and sessionStorage to hijack persistent sessions
        ~Browser Cache Poisoning: Modification of cached browser content to maintain unauthorized access
        ~Session Fixation: Forcing a user's session identifier to a known value
        ~Cross-Browser Session Synchronization: Exploitation of browser sync features to propagate hijacked sessions
        ~Browser Password Manager Abuse: Leveraging stored credentials in browser password managers
        ~Browser Debugging Protocol Abuse: Exploitation of browser debugging interfaces for session manipulation
        ~Service Worker Hijacking: Manipulation of service workers to intercept and modify browser requests
        ~WebSocket Session Interception: Hijacking WebSocket connections to maintain persistent access
        ~Browser Isolation Bypass: Techniques to bypass browser sandbox and isolation mechanisms
        ~Origin Policy Manipulation: Exploitation of Same-Origin Policy implementations and cross-origin resource sharing
        ~Browser Memory Manipulation: Direct manipulation of browser memory to extract or modify session data
        ~Browser State Manipulation: Exploitation of browser state management features
        ~Browser Protocol Handler Abuse: Manipulation of custom protocol handlers for session hijacking
        ~Browser Extension Permission Abuse: Exploitation of legitimate extension permissions to access session data
      Data from Removable Media: Adversaries may search connected removable media on computers they have compromised to find files of interest. Sensitive data can be collected from any removable media (optical disk drive, USB memory, etc.) connected to the compromised system prior to Exfiltration. Interactive command shells may be in use, and common functionality within [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) may be used to gather information.   Some adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119) on removable media.
        ~Manual File Exploration: Adversaries manually browse and search through removable media directories to identify and collect files of interest
        ~Automated Media Scanning: Use of scripts or tools to automatically scan and inventory files on removable media when connected
        ~File Type Targeting: Specifically searching for and collecting certain file types or extensions from removable media
        ~Hidden File Access: Accessing and collecting hidden files and alternate data streams from removable media
        ~Deleted File Recovery: Using file recovery tools to retrieve deleted files from removable media
        ~Media Device Fingerprinting: Collecting metadata and system information about the removable media device itself
        ~Scheduled Collection: Setting up scheduled tasks to monitor and collect from removable media when connected
        ~Memory Scraping: Extracting data from removable media while it's being accessed in memory
        ~Firmware Access: Collecting data by accessing the firmware or controller of removable media devices
        ~Encrypted Media Access: Techniques for accessing and collecting data from encrypted removable media
        ~Media Format Recognition: Techniques to identify and handle different formats of removable media (FAT32, NTFS, exFAT, etc.)
        ~Autorun Exploitation: Leveraging autorun features of removable media to trigger collection
        ~Write Protection Bypass: Methods to collect data from write-protected removable media
        ~Historical Connection Analysis: Collecting information about previously connected removable media from system logs
        ~Cross-Platform Collection: Specialized techniques for collecting from media formatted for different operating systems
      Data from Configuration Repository: Adversaries may collect data related to managed devices from configuration repositories. Configuration repositories are used by management systems in order to configure, manage, and control data on remote systems. Configuration repositories may also facilitate remote access and administration of devices.  Adversaries may target these repositories in order to collect large quantities of sensitive system administration data. Data from configuration repositories may be exposed by various protocols and software and can store a wide variety of data, much of which may align with adversary Discovery objectives.(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: US-CERT TA17-156A SNMP Abuse 2017)
        SNMP (MIB Dump): Adversaries may target the Management Information Base (MIB) to collect and/or mine valuable information in a network managed using Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  The MIB is a configuration repository that stores variable information accessible via SNMP in the form of object identifiers (OID). Each OID identifies a variable that can be read or set and permits active management tasks, such as configuration changes, through remote modification of these variables. SNMP can give administrators great insight in their systems, such as, system information, description of hardware, physical location, and software packages(Citation: SANS Information Security Reading Room Securing SNMP Securing SNMP). The MIB may also contain device operational information, including running configuration, routing table, and interface details.  Adversaries may use SNMP queries to collect MIB content directly from SNMP-managed devices in order to collect network information that allows the adversary to build network maps and facilitate future targeted exploitation.(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)
          Use snmpwalk tool with public community string to dump entire MIB tree
          Deploy custom SNMP scanning script using pysnmp to extract specific OIDs
          Utilize Nmap's SNMP scripts (snmp-brute, snmp-info) to enumerate SNMP information
          Execute SNMP queries using Net-SNMP tools targeting system description OIDs
          Exploit SNMPv1/v2c weak community strings using brute force tools like onesixtyone
          Use Metasploit's auxiliary/scanner/snmp modules for automated MIB enumeration
          Deploy SNMP MIB browser tools like iReasoning to explore and extract MIB data
          Create custom SNMP polling scripts to periodically collect configuration changes
