          Use SNMP::Info Perl module for custom MIB parsing and extraction
          Implement RMON probe techniques for historical MIB data collection
          Use WhatsUp Gold's SNMP Smart Monitor for adaptive polling
          Deploy SNMPCollector for high-performance, scalable MIB data gathering
        Network Device Configuration Dump: Adversaries may access network configuration files to collect sensitive data about the device and the network. The network configuration is a file containing parameters that determine the operation of the device. The device typically stores an in-memory copy of the configuration while operating, and a separate configuration on non-volatile storage to load after device reset. Adversaries can inspect the configuration files to reveal information about the target network and its layout, the network device and its software, or identifying legitimate accounts and credentials for later use.  Adversaries can use common management tools and protocols, such as Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) and Smart Install (SMI), to access network configuration files.(Citation: US-CERT TA18-106A Network Infrastructure Devices 2018)(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks) These tools may be used to query specific data from a configuration repository or configure the device to export the configuration for later analysis.
          Use SNMP GET requests with default community strings to retrieve configuration data
          Exploit Cisco Smart Install protocol to dump device configurations
          Use TFTP to download configuration files after enabling configuration export
          Leverage SNMP write access to modify device configuration for data exfiltration
          Execute memory dump commands through CLI access to extract running configurations
          Use vendor-specific tools like Cisco Network Configuration Manager for bulk extraction
          Exploit vulnerable management interfaces to download startup configurations
          Deploy network scanning tools to identify and query devices supporting config extraction
          Use protocol-specific MIBs to enumerate and extract configuration sections
          Leverage automated configuration backup systems to access stored device configs
          Exploit NETCONF/YANG interfaces to extract configuration data
          Use console port access with default credentials to dump configurations
          Leverage CDP/LLDP information disclosure to identify and target devices
          Extract configurations through compromised network management stations (NMS)
