          Extract sensitive data from repository analytics and metrics dashboards
          Leverage repository webhooks to capture push/commit notifications
          Access code snippet sharing features and internal paste bins
          Extract data from issue tracking systems and pull request discussions
          Access package registry credentials and private package feeds
          Exploit repository search features to find sensitive patterns
          Access repository plugin configurations and integration settings
          Extract data from code review comments and inline discussions
          Access development environment configurations stored with projects
          Extract sensitive data from automated code quality scan results
          Access repository statistics and contributor activity logs
          Exploit repository synchronization tools and git submodules
          Extract data from code signing and verification systems
          Access deployment key configurations and SSH key stores
          Extract sensitive information from code dependency files
          Access repository backup configuration files
          Extract data from repository event logs and audit trails
          Access development environment variables stored in repository settings
          Extract sensitive information from repository access tokens and PATs
          Access archived repositories and legacy code storage
          Extract data from repository organization settings and team structures
          Access repository migration tools and import/export logs
          Extract sensitive data from repository maintenance scripts
          Access repository metrics collection systems
          Extract data from code repository caching layers
          Access repository disaster recovery configurations
          Extract data from repository load balancers and proxies
          Access repository database dumps and backups
          Extract sensitive information from repository monitoring systems
      Data from Local System: Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, local databases, or virtual machine files, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration.  Adversaries may do this using a [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059), such as [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) as well as a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008), which have functionality to interact with the file system to gather information.(Citation: show_run_config_cmd_cisco) Adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119) on the local system.
        ~System Configuration Files: Adversaries may target system configuration files containing sensitive settings, credentials, and system information
        ~Browser Data: Collection of browser history, saved passwords, cookies, and cached data from local browser storage
        ~Local Database Files: Targeting database files containing business data, user information, or application data
        ~Virtual Machine Disk Files: Extraction of data from virtual machine disk files, snapshots, and configuration files
        ~Application Logs: Collection of application logs containing sensitive information, error messages, and user activity
        ~User Profile Data: Gathering data from user profile directories including documents, downloads, and application data
        ~Registry Data: Extraction of sensitive information stored in Windows Registry hives
        ~Backup Files: Targeting local backup files, system restore points, and archived data
        ~Source Code Repositories: Collection of local source code repositories, development files, and associated credentials
        ~Credential Stores: Targeting local credential stores, key chains, and password vaults
        ~Memory Dump Files: Collection of crash dumps, page files, and hibernation files containing system memory data
        ~Printer Spool Files: Targeting printer spool directories for document reconstruction
        ~Temporary File Locations: Gathering data from temporary directories used by applications and the system
        ~System Journal Files: Collection of system journals, event logs, and audit files
        ~Encrypted Container Files: Targeting encrypted volumes, BitLocker files, and other encrypted containers
        ~Remote Storage Cache: Collection of cached data from remote storage systems like OneDrive or Dropbox
        ~Application Data Files: Targeting application-specific data files containing configurations and user data
        ~Email Client Files: Collection of local email storage files, attachments, and cached messages
        ~Certificate Stores: Gathering digital certificates, private keys, and related security artifacts
        ~Shadow Copy Data: Accessing Volume Shadow Copy files for historical data collection
        ~Alternate Data Streams: Collection of hidden data stored in NTFS alternate data streams
        ~Swap Files: Targeting system swap files and page files for sensitive data recovery
        ~Recycle Bin Data: Recovery of deleted files and metadata from system trash/recycle locations
        ~System Restore Points: Accessing historical system state data from restore points
        ~Diagnostic Data: Collection of system diagnostic files, crash reports, and debugging information
        ~Firmware Configuration: Gathering data from firmware configuration storage and UEFI variables
        ~Mounted Network Shares: Accessing locally mounted network shares and mapped drives
        ~Installation Logs: Collection of software installation logs and cached installers
        ~Kernel Dump Files: Targeting kernel memory dumps and system crash data
        ~Hidden Partitions: Accessing recovery partitions and other hidden system partitions
      Data from Cloud Storage: Adversaries may access data from cloud storage.  Many IaaS providers offer solutions for online data object storage such as Amazon S3, Azure Storage, and Google Cloud Storage. Similarly, SaaS enterprise platforms such as Office 365 and Google Workspace provide cloud-based document storage to users through services such as OneDrive and Google Drive, while SaaS application providers such as Slack, Confluence, Salesforce, and Dropbox may provide cloud storage solutions as a peripheral or primary use case of their platform.   In some cases, as with IaaS-based cloud storage, there exists no overarching application (such as SQL or Elasticsearch) with which to interact with the stored objects: instead, data from these solutions is retrieved directly though the [Cloud API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/009). In SaaS applications, adversaries may be able to collect this data directly from APIs or backend cloud storage objects, rather than through their front-end application or interface (i.e., [Data from Information Repositories](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1213)).   Adversaries may collect sensitive data from these cloud storage solutions. Providers typically offer security guides to help end users configure systems, though misconfigurations are a common problem.(Citation: Amazon S3 Security, 2019)(Citation: Microsoft Azure Storage Security, 2019)(Citation: Google Cloud Storage Best Practices, 2019) There have been numerous incidents where cloud storage has been improperly secured, typically by unintentionally allowing public access to unauthenticated users, overly-broad access by all users, or even access for any anonymous person outside the control of the Identity Access Management system without even needing basic user permissions.  This open access may expose various types of sensitive data, such as credit cards, personally identifiable information, or medical records.(Citation: Trend Micro S3 Exposed PII, 2017)(Citation: Wired Magecart S3 Buckets, 2019)(Citation: HIPAA Journal S3 Breach, 2017)(Citation: Rclone-mega-extortion_05_2021)  Adversaries may also obtain then abuse leaked credentials from source repositories, logs, or other means as a way to gain access to cloud storage objects.
        ~Public Access Exploitation: Adversaries exploit misconfigured cloud storage permissions that allow public access without authentication
        ~Credential Abuse: Adversaries use stolen or leaked credentials to access cloud storage services
        ~API Token Theft: Adversaries steal and abuse API tokens or keys to access cloud storage
        ~IAM Misconfiguration Abuse: Adversaries exploit overly permissive IAM policies or roles
        ~Cross-Account Access: Adversaries leverage misconfigured cross-account access permissions
        ~Service Account Compromise: Adversaries compromise and abuse service account credentials
        ~Bucket Enumeration: Adversaries systematically discover and access cloud storage buckets
        ~Metadata Analysis: Adversaries collect and analyze cloud storage metadata to identify sensitive data
        ~Version History Access: Adversaries access previous versions of cloud storage objects
        ~Direct URL Access: Adversaries bypass application controls by accessing storage URLs directly
        ~Storage Gateway Compromise: Adversaries exploit on-premises storage gateway appliances that connect to cloud storage
        ~Replication Rule Abuse: Adversaries manipulate storage replication settings to access data copies
        ~Third-Party Integration Abuse: Adversaries exploit trusted third-party applications with cloud storage access
        ~Storage Event Trigger Exploitation: Adversaries abuse storage event notifications and triggers
        ~Temporary URL Generation: Adversaries generate and exploit temporary access URLs
        ~Cross-Region Replication Access: Adversaries access data through misconfigured cross-region replicas
        ~Storage Policy Modification: Adversaries modify storage access policies to gain persistent access
        ~Storage Lifecycle Exploitation: Adversaries access data by manipulating object lifecycle rules and archived content
        ~Cross-Service Authentication Abuse: Adversaries exploit trusted service-to-service authentication mechanisms
        ~Sync Client Compromise: Adversaries target cloud storage synchronization clients on endpoints
        ~Storage Network Protocol Abuse: Adversaries exploit cloud storage network protocols (like S3's REST API)
        ~Archived Data Access: Adversaries access and exfiltrate data from cloud archive storage tiers
      Screen Capture: Adversaries may attempt to take screen captures of the desktop to gather information over the course of an operation. Screen capturing functionality may be included as a feature of a remote access tool used in post-compromise operations. Taking a screenshot is also typically possible through native utilities or API calls, such as <code>CopyFromScreen</code>, <code>xwd</code>, or <code>screencapture</code>.(Citation: CopyFromScreen .NET)(Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware)
        ~API-Based Capture: Adversaries may use operating system APIs like Windows GDI, DirectX, or macOS CoreGraphics to directly capture screen content programmatically
        ~Native Utility Abuse: Adversaries may abuse built-in screenshot utilities like PrintScreen, Snipping Tool, or screencapture to avoid detection
        ~Remote Access Tool Integration: Adversaries may use screen capture capabilities built into RATs and post-exploitation frameworks
        ~Periodic Capture: Adversaries may implement automated, scheduled screen captures at regular intervals to monitor user activity
        ~Window-Specific Capture: Adversaries may target specific application windows or dialog boxes rather than capturing the entire screen
        ~Event-Triggered Capture: Adversaries may capture screens in response to specific user actions like keyboard input, mouse clicks, or application launches
        ~Driver-Level Capture: Adversaries may implement screen capture through display driver manipulation or virtual display drivers
        ~Virtual Desktop Capture: Adversaries may specifically target virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI) environments using specialized methods
        ~Clipboard-Integrated Capture: Adversaries may capture screens by intercepting or monitoring system clipboard operations when users copy/paste screen content
        ~Memory-Based Capture: Adversaries may directly read screen buffer memory to capture screen content while avoiding traditional screen capture APIs
      Clipboard Data: Adversaries may collect data stored in the clipboard from users copying information within or between applications.   For example, on Windows adversaries can access clipboard data by using <code>clip.exe</code> or <code>Get-Clipboard</code>.(Citation: MSDN Clipboard)(Citation: clip_win_server)(Citation: CISA_AA21_200B) Additionally, adversaries may monitor then replace users’ clipboard with their data (e.g., [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002)).(Citation: mining_ruby_reversinglabs)  macOS and Linux also have commands, such as <code>pbpaste</code>, to grab clipboard contents.(Citation: Operating with EmPyre)
        ~System Clipboard Monitoring: Continuous monitoring of system clipboard through API hooks or system calls to capture all copied data
        ~Cloud Clipboard Interception: Targeting synchronized clipboard features in cloud services and virtual desktop environments
        ~Remote Session Clipboard Capture: Intercepting clipboard data during remote desktop or terminal sessions
        ~Kernel-level Clipboard Access: Using kernel-mode drivers or exploits to directly access clipboard memory
        ~Cross-Application Clipboard Tracking: Specifically monitoring clipboard transfers between different applications or security contexts
        ~Clipboard Format Targeting: Selectively capturing specific clipboard data formats (text, images, files)
        ~Programmatic API Access: Using legitimate programming interfaces and commands to access clipboard contents
        ~Clipboard Chain Manipulation: Intercepting and modifying clipboard chain to maintain persistent access
      Video Capture: An adversary can leverage a computer's peripheral devices (e.g., integrated cameras or webcams) or applications (e.g., video call services) to capture video recordings for the purpose of gathering information. Images may also be captured from devices or applications, potentially in specified intervals, in lieu of video files.  Malware or scripts may be used to interact with the devices through an available API provided by the operating system or an application to capture video or images. Video or image files may be written to disk and exfiltrated later. This technique differs from [Screen Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1113) due to use of specific devices or applications for video recording rather than capturing the victim's screen.  In macOS, there are a few different malware samples that record the user's webcam such as FruitFly and Proton. (Citation: objective-see 2017 review)
        ~Webcam Hijacking: Unauthorized access and control of integrated or external webcams to capture video without user awareness
        ~Video Conference Interception: Exploitation of video conferencing applications to capture ongoing calls or stored recordings
        ~Mobile Device Camera Access: Unauthorized access to mobile device cameras through malicious apps or exploits
        ~Scheduled Image Capture: Periodic capture of still images at predetermined intervals using compromised devices
        ~API-Based Recording: Leveraging system or application APIs to programmatically control video recording devices
        ~Driver-Level Manipulation: Direct manipulation of device drivers to bypass security controls and capture video
        ~Remote Activation: Triggering video capture through remote commands or conditions
        ~Application Session Recording: Capturing video during specific application usage or user sessions
      Audio Capture: An adversary can leverage a computer's peripheral devices (e.g., microphones and webcams) or applications (e.g., voice and video call services) to capture audio recordings for the purpose of listening into sensitive conversations to gather information.(Citation: ESET Attor Oct 2019)  Malware or scripts may be used to interact with the devices through an available API provided by the operating system or an application to capture audio. Audio files may be written to disk and exfiltrated later.
        ~Hardware Microphone Access: Directly accessing and controlling physical microphone devices through system APIs or driver interfaces
        ~Communication App Hijacking: Intercepting or manipulating voice/video call applications to capture audio streams
        ~Audio Driver Manipulation: Modifying or hooking audio drivers to intercept audio data at the system level
        ~Scheduled Recording: Implementing timed or scheduled audio capture based on specific triggers or time windows
        ~Live Streaming Capture: Capturing audio in real-time and streaming it directly to command and control servers
        ~Background Process Recording: Running persistent background processes that continuously monitor and record audio
        ~Meeting Software Exploitation: Specifically targeting virtual meeting software to capture conference calls and online meetings
        ~Audio Buffer Interception: Intercepting audio data from system sound buffers or mixing devices
        ~Smart Device Audio Capture: Targeting smart home devices or IoT devices with audio capabilities
        ~System Sound Capture: Recording system audio output instead of microphone input
        ~Voice Activity Detection: Using voice detection algorithms to selectively record only when speech is present
        ~Acoustic Side Channel Recording: Capturing audio to extract information from device sounds like keyboard clicks or printer noise
        ~Audio Compression Techniques: Using specific compression methods to optimize storage of captured audio
        ~Multi-Channel Recording: Capturing and correlating audio from multiple input sources simultaneously
        ~Selective Frequency Capture: Recording specific frequency ranges to target particular types of audio signals
        ~Audio Triggering: Starting recording based on specific audio patterns or keyword detection
        ~Environmental Audio Analysis: Capturing ambient sounds to gather information about the physical environment
        ~Audio Device Enumeration: Systematically identifying and targeting specific audio devices in the system
        ~Audio Watermark Extraction: Capturing audio specifically to extract hidden watermarks or steganographic content
        ~Cross-Device Audio Bridging: Leveraging audio capture across multiple connected devices to expand collection range
        ~Audio Cache Exploitation: Targeting temporary audio buffers and cache files from applications
        ~Conference Room Targeting: Specifically targeting audio systems in meeting rooms and conference facilities
        ~Acoustic Emission Analysis: Capturing high-frequency sounds from electronic devices for information gathering
    command-and-control: The adversary is trying to communicate with compromised systems to control them.  Command and Control consists of techniques that adversaries may use to communicate with systems under their control within a victim network. Adversaries commonly attempt to mimic normal, expected traffic to avoid detection. There are many ways an adversary can establish command and control with various levels of stealth depending on the victim’s network structure and defenses.
      Ingress Tool Transfer: Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from an external system into a compromised environment. Tools or files may be copied from an external adversary-controlled system to the victim network through the command and control channel or through alternate protocols such as [ftp](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0095). Once present, adversaries may also transfer/spread tools between victim devices within a compromised environment (i.e. [Lateral Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1570)).   On Windows, adversaries may use various utilities to download tools, such as `copy`, `finger`, [certutil](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0160), and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) commands such as <code>IEX(New-Object Net.WebClient).downloadString()</code> and <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code>. On Linux and macOS systems, a variety of utilities also exist, such as `curl`, `scp`, `sftp`, `tftp`, `rsync`, `finger`, and `wget`.(Citation: t1105_lolbas)  A number of these tools, such as `wget`, `curl`, and `scp`, also exist on ESXi. After downloading a file, a threat actor may attempt to verify its integrity by checking its hash value (e.g., via `certutil -hashfile`).(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence COSCMICENERGY 2023)  Adversaries may also abuse installers and package managers, such as `yum` or `winget`, to download tools to victim hosts. Adversaries have also abused file application features, such as the Windows `search-ms` protocol handler, to deliver malicious files to victims through remote file searches invoked by [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) (typically after interacting with [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) lures).(Citation: T1105: Trellix_search-ms)  Files can also be transferred using various [Web Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102)s as well as native or otherwise present tools on the victim system.(Citation: PTSecurity Cobalt Dec 2016) In some cases, adversaries may be able to leverage services that sync between a web-based and an on-premises client, such as Dropbox or OneDrive, to transfer files onto victim systems. For example, by compromising a cloud account and logging into the service's web portal, an adversary may be able to trigger an automatic syncing process that transfers the file onto the victim's machine.(Citation: Dropbox Malware Sync)
        ~Native OS Command Transfer: Adversaries may use built-in OS commands and utilities like PowerShell, curl, wget, and certutil to download and transfer tools
        ~Package Manager Abuse: Adversaries may abuse legitimate package managers and installers like yum, winget, or apt to download malicious tools
        ~Cloud Storage Sync: Adversaries may abuse cloud storage sync clients like Dropbox or OneDrive to transfer files by compromising cloud accounts
        ~Protocol Handler Abuse: Adversaries may abuse protocol handlers like search-ms to deliver malicious files through remote file searches
        ~Web Service Transfer: Adversaries may leverage web services and APIs to transfer tools through HTTP/HTTPS channels
        ~Standard File Transfer Protocols: Adversaries may use standard file transfer protocols like FTP, SFTP, TFTP, or SCP for tool transfer
        ~File Integrity Verification: Adversaries may employ hash checking and verification mechanisms to ensure transferred tools are intact
        ~Raw Socket Transfer: Adversaries may implement custom socket-based file transfer mechanisms over TCP/IP
        ~Alternative Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may abuse non-standard protocols or services like finger for tool transfer
      Encrypted Channel: Adversaries may employ an encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files.
        Symmetric Cryptography: Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4.
          Use RC4 stream cipher with hardcoded keys in malware to encrypt C2 traffic
          Implement AES-256 encryption with key derived from system-specific parameters
          Deploy custom malware using Blowfish algorithm with keys embedded in configuration files
          Utilize 3DES encryption with keys rotated based on timestamp
          Implement XOR-based encryption with rolling keys generated from system metadata
          Use AES-CBC mode with PKCS7 padding and predetermined initialization vectors
          Deploy malware using multiple symmetric algorithms in sequence (encryption cascade)
          Implement custom key scheduling algorithm with AES for periodic key rotation
          Use embedded DES keys with custom key whitening techniques
          Implement symmetric encryption with key obfuscation through environmental keying
          Store encryption keys in registry values split across multiple keys
          Generate symmetric keys using system-specific hardware identifiers
          Implement time-based key generation using system uptime as seed
          Use symmetric encryption with key fragments distributed across multiple files
          Deploy custom key derivation functions based on system locale and settings
          Implement symmetric encryption with keys stored in alternate data streams
          Use network adapter MAC address as basis for key generation
          Implement key extraction from specific memory regions
          Use symmetric encryption with keys derived from disk serial numbers
          Deploy polymorphic key generation based on running processes
          Implement symmetric encryption within DNS tunnel payload encoding
          Use memory-mapped encryption keys with process hollowing
          Deploy symmetric encryption with custom Base64 variants for transport
          Implement key generation based on geolocation data
          Use symmetric encryption with nested container formats
          Implement key derivation from domain-specific parameters
          Deploy encryption with chunked data and varying block sizes
          Use symmetric encryption with custom padding oracle techniques
          Implement key generation based on Active Directory attributes
          Deploy symmetric encryption with network timing-based key synchronization
          Implement symmetric encryption with keys derived from cloud instance metadata
          Use steganographic techniques to hide encrypted C2 traffic in image files
          Deploy symmetric encryption with automated fallback algorithms
          Implement key recovery through distributed backup channels
          Use symmetric encryption with container orchestration parameters
          Deploy encryption with built-in error correction codes
          Implement key generation based on virtual machine parameters
          Use symmetric encryption within legitimate protocol headers
          Deploy encryption with load balancer specific implementations
          Implement key rotation based on cloud service API responses
          Implement symmetric encryption within SQL query parameters
          Use encryption keys derived from application configuration files
          Deploy cross-platform key generation using common system attributes
          Implement symmetric encryption within browser extension storage
          Use encryption with keys stored in cached application data
          Deploy symmetric encryption within PDF metadata fields
          Implement key generation based on installed software versions
          Use encryption within Microsoft Office macro streams
          Deploy symmetric encryption using printer spooler services
          Implement key generation based on installed font metrics
          Implement symmetric encryption using IoT device sensor data for keys
          Use encryption keys derived from mobile device identifiers
          Deploy symmetric encryption within embedded system firmware
          Implement key generation using multi-factor authentication tokens
          Use encryption with keys derived from smart card reader data
          Deploy symmetric encryption within UEFI variables
          Implement key generation based on biometric sensor data
          Use encryption with keys derived from USB device identifiers
          Deploy symmetric encryption using TPM-stored keys
          Implement key generation based on bluetooth device parameters
        Asymmetric Cryptography: Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private. Due to how the keys are generated, the sender encrypts data with the receiver’s public key and the receiver decrypts the data with their private key. This ensures that only the intended recipient can read the encrypted data. Common public key encryption algorithms include RSA and ElGamal.  For efficiency, many protocols (including SSL/TLS) use symmetric cryptography once a connection is established, but use asymmetric cryptography to establish or transmit a key. As such, these protocols are classified as [Asymmetric Cryptography](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/002).
          Use RSA encryption with 2048-bit keys in custom C2 protocol implementation
          Implement ElGamal encryption for securing C2 traffic between implant and server
          Leverage existing SSL/TLS libraries with custom certificates for C2 communication
          Deploy custom malware using embedded RSA public keys for initial key exchange
          Utilize PGP/GPG for encrypting C2 payloads and commands
          Implement hybrid cryptosystem using RSA for key exchange and AES for bulk data
          Use ECC (Elliptic Curve Cryptography) based protocols for lightweight C2 channels
          Create custom key generation and distribution mechanism using X.509 certificates
          Employ Diffie-Hellman key exchange for establishing secure C2 sessions
          Use quantum-resistant algorithms like NTRU for future-proofing C2 channels
          Implement asymmetric encryption in PowerShell Empire using built-in .NET crypto APIs
          Use Java Cryptography Architecture (JCA) for cross-platform C2 implementations
          Deploy Metasploit's asymmetric encryption modules for staged payloads
          Implement SSH-based C2 channel using public key authentication
          Use Windows CryptoAPI with embedded certificates for C2 traffic
          Create DNS tunneling with RSA-encrypted payloads in TXT records
          Implement ECDSA-based signing for C2 message authentication
          Use S/MIME protocol for email-based C2 communications
          Deploy HTTP/2 with custom TLS implementation for C2 traffic
          Implement QUIC protocol with custom certificate handling for C2
          Use AWS KMS for key management and asymmetric encryption in cloud-based C2
          Implement Android Keystore System for mobile C2 client encryption
          Use TPM/HSM hardware for secure key storage and asymmetric operations
          Embed encrypted C2 traffic in digital images using RSA-encrypted steganography
          Deploy iOS Keychain-based asymmetric encryption for mobile C2 clients
          Implement PKCS#11 for hardware token-based C2 encryption
          Use smart card-based asymmetric encryption for C2 authentication
          Implement lightweight asymmetric encryption for IoT device C2 (NTRU-based)
          Use blockchain-based PKI for distributed C2 key management
          Implement post-quantum cryptography using SPHINCS+ for future-proof C2
      Domain Generation Algorithms: Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Unit 42 DGA Feb 2019)  DGAs can take the form of apparently random or “gibberish” strings (ex: istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru) when they construct domain names by generating each letter. Alternatively, some DGAs employ whole words as the unit by concatenating words together instead of letters (ex: cityjulydish.net). Many DGAs are time-based, generating a different domain for each time period (hourly, daily, monthly, etc). Others incorporate a seed value as well to make predicting future domains more difficult for defenders.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: Akamai DGA Mitigation)  Adversaries may use DGAs for the purpose of [Fallback Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008). When contact is lost with the primary command and control server malware may employ a DGA as a means to reestablishing command and control.(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)(Citation: ESET Sednit 2017 Activity)
        ~Character-Based Generation: Generates domain names by assembling random or algorithmically generated characters (e.g., istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru)
        ~Word-Based Generation: Creates domains by concatenating dictionary words together (e.g., cityjulydish.net)
        ~Time-Based Generation: Generates different domains based on time periods (hourly, daily, monthly)
        ~Seeded Generation: Uses seed values combined with algorithms to generate domains
        ~Fallback Channel DGA: Implements DGA specifically as a backup communication method
        ~Arithmetic-Based Generation: Uses mathematical functions and operations to generate domain names
        ~Hash-Based Generation: Employs cryptographic hash functions to generate deterministic domain names
        ~TLD Rotation: Cycles through different top-level domains while maintaining similar base domains
        ~External Data Seeding: Incorporates external data sources (like news headlines or stock prices) to generate domains
      Traffic Signaling: Adversaries may use traffic signaling to hide open ports or other malicious functionality used for persistence or command and control. Traffic signaling involves the use of a magic value or sequence that must be sent to a system to trigger a special response, such as opening a closed port or executing a malicious task. This may take the form of sending a series of packets with certain characteristics before a port will be opened that the adversary can use for command and control. Usually this series of packets consists of attempted connections to a predefined sequence of closed ports (i.e. [Port Knocking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1205/001)), but can involve unusual flags, specific strings, or other unique characteristics. After the sequence is completed, opening a port may be accomplished by the host-based firewall, but could also be implemented by custom software.  Adversaries may also communicate with an already open port, but the service listening on that port will only respond to commands or trigger other malicious functionality if passed the appropriate magic value(s).  The observation of the signal packets to trigger the communication can be conducted through different methods. One means, originally implemented by Cd00r (Citation: Hartrell cd00r 2002), is to use the libpcap libraries to sniff for the packets in question. Another method leverages raw sockets, which enables the malware to use ports that are already open for use by other programs.  On network devices, adversaries may use crafted packets to enable [Network Device Authentication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/004) for standard services offered by the device such as telnet.  Such signaling may also be used to open a closed service port such as telnet, or to trigger module modification of malware implants on the device, adding, removing, or changing malicious capabilities.  Adversaries may use crafted packets to attempt to connect to one or more (open or closed) ports, but may also attempt to connect to a router interface, broadcast, and network address IP on the same port in order to achieve their goals and objectives.(Citation: Cisco Synful Knock Evolution)(Citation: Mandiant - Synful Knock)(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)  To enable this traffic signaling on embedded devices, adversaries must first achieve and leverage [Patch System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/001) due to the monolithic nature of the architecture.  Adversaries may also use the Wake-on-LAN feature to turn on powered off systems. Wake-on-LAN is a hardware feature that allows a powered down system to be powered on, or woken up, by sending a magic packet to it. Once the system is powered on, it may become a target for lateral movement.(Citation: Bleeping Computer - Ryuk WoL)(Citation: AMD Magic Packet)
        Socket Filters: Adversaries may attach filters to a network socket to monitor then activate backdoors used for persistence or command and control. With elevated permissions, adversaries can use features such as the `libpcap` library to open sockets and install filters to allow or disallow certain types of data to come through the socket. The filter may apply to all traffic passing through the specified network interface (or every interface if not specified). When the network interface receives a packet matching the filter criteria, additional actions can be triggered on the host, such as activation of a reverse shell.  To establish a connection, an adversary sends a crafted packet to the targeted host that matches the installed filter criteria.(Citation: haking9 libpcap network sniffing) Adversaries have used these socket filters to trigger the installation of implants, conduct ping backs, and to invoke command shells. Communication with these socket filters may also be used in conjunction with [Protocol Tunneling](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1572).(Citation: exatrack bpf filters passive backdoors)(Citation: Leonardo Turla Penquin May 2020)  Filters can be installed on any Unix-like platform with `libpcap` installed or on Windows hosts using `Winpcap`.  Adversaries may use either `libpcap` with `pcap_setfilter` or the standard library function `setsockopt` with `SO_ATTACH_FILTER` options. Since the socket connection is not active until the packet is received, this behavior may be difficult to detect due to the lack of activity on a host, low CPU overhead, and limited visibility into raw socket usage.
          Use libpcap with pcap_setfilter to create BPF filters that trigger on specific TCP flags or packet sequences
          Implement Winpcap-based filters on Windows systems to monitor network interfaces for specific packet patterns
          Use setsockopt with SO_ATTACH_FILTER to attach custom BPF programs to raw sockets
          Create socket filters that respond to ICMP echo requests with specific payloads
          Deploy filters that monitor for UDP packets with magic values in specific payload positions
          Install packet filters that trigger on specially crafted TCP SYN packets with specific sequence numbers
          Configure socket filters to detect and respond to custom DNS queries containing encoded commands
