      Multiband Communication: **This technique has been deprecated and should no longer be used.**  Some adversaries may split communications between different protocols. There could be one protocol for inbound command and control and another for outbound data, allowing it to bypass certain firewall restrictions. The split could also be random to simply avoid data threshold alerts on any one communication.
        ~Protocol Splitting: Separating C2 traffic between different network protocols (e.g., HTTP for commands, DNS for data exfiltration)
        ~Port Hopping: Dynamically switching between different ports while maintaining separate communication channels
        ~Service Multiplexing: Using multiple legitimate services simultaneously (e.g., combining social media APIs with cloud storage)
        ~Traffic Distribution: Spreading C2 traffic across multiple channels based on data type or size
        ~Protocol Tunneling Combination: Using multiple protocol tunneling techniques simultaneously
        ~Scheduled Channel Rotation: Time-based switching between different communication protocols
        ~Fallback Channels: Implementing separate primary and backup communication channels using different protocols
        ~Data Stream Splitting: Fragmenting data across multiple protocols to avoid size-based detection
        ~Protocol-specific Encoding: Using different encoding schemes for different communication channels
        ~Cross-Protocol Synchronization: Coordinating communication across multiple protocols using timing or markers
        ~Asymmetric Protocol Usage: Using different protocols for upstream vs downstream communication
        ~Geographic Protocol Distribution: Distributing C2 traffic across different protocols based on geographic location
        ~Bandwidth-Based Channel Selection: Selecting protocols based on available bandwidth and network conditions
        ~Protocol Blending: Mixing legitimate and malicious traffic across multiple protocols simultaneously
        ~Cross-Domain Channel Splitting: Using different protocols for internal vs external network communication
      Data Encoding: Adversaries may encode data to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system. Use of data encoding may adhere to existing protocol specifications and includes use of ASCII, Unicode, Base64, MIME, or other binary-to-text and character encoding systems.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding) (Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.
        Standard Encoding: Adversaries may encode data with a standard data encoding system to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system that adheres to existing protocol specifications. Common data encoding schemes include ASCII, Unicode, hexadecimal, Base64, and MIME.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding)(Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.
