      Communication Through Removable Media: Adversaries can perform command and control between compromised hosts on potentially disconnected networks using removable media to transfer commands from system to system.(Citation: ESET Sednit USBStealer 2014) Both systems would need to be compromised, with the likelihood that an Internet-connected system was compromised first and the second through lateral movement by [Replication Through Removable Media](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1091). Commands and files would be relayed from the disconnected system to the Internet-connected system to which the adversary has direct access.
        ~USB Storage Devices: Using USB drives to transfer command files and receive response data between systems
        ~External Hard Drives: Leveraging external HDDs for larger command sets and data transfers
        ~Memory Card Transfer: Using SD cards or other memory cards for covert command transfer
        ~Automated File Monitoring: Implementing file watchers to detect and process new commands
        ~Custom Protocol Implementation: Using specialized protocols for command encoding on removable media
        ~File-based Command Queue: Implementing command queuing systems through file structures
        ~Encrypted Storage Channel: Using encrypted containers or files on removable media
        ~Timestamped Command Execution: Using file timestamps for command scheduling
        ~Hidden File Transfer: Using hidden files or alternate data streams for command transfer
        ~Multi-stage Command Relay: Using multiple removable media devices in sequence for complex command chains
        ~Optical Media Transfer: Using CD/DVD/Blu-ray discs for command transfer in environments with strict USB controls
        ~Firmware-Based Storage: Embedding commands in device firmware for stealthy transfer
        ~Partition Table Manipulation: Hiding commands in unused partition spaces
        ~File Attribute Encoding: Using file metadata and attributes to encode commands
        ~Cross-Format Command Storage: Embedding commands across multiple file formats
        ~Scheduled Media Rotation: Implementing timed media swapping for regular command updates
        ~Response Collection System: Specialized mechanisms for gathering command execution results
        ~Dead Drop Protocol: Implementing specific drop points and collection schedules
        ~Error-Tolerant Transfer: Implementing redundancy and verification in command transfer
        ~Multi-System Relay Chain: Coordinating commands across multiple air-gapped systems
      Commonly Used Port: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Non-Standard Port](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1571) where appropriate.**  Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend with normal network activity to avoid more detailed inspection. They may use commonly open ports such as  * TCP:80 (HTTP) * TCP:443 (HTTPS) * TCP:25 (SMTP) * TCP/UDP:53 (DNS)  They may use the protocol associated with the port or a completely different protocol.   For connections that occur internally within an enclave (such as those between a proxy or pivot node and other nodes), examples of common ports are   * TCP/UDP:135 (RPC) * TCP/UDP:22 (SSH) * TCP/UDP:3389 (RDP)
        ~Web Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may abuse HTTP/HTTPS ports (80/443) by tunneling C2 traffic through these commonly allowed protocols
        ~DNS Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may abuse DNS port 53 for command and control by encoding commands in DNS queries and responses
        ~Email Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may abuse SMTP port 25 to send commands disguised as email traffic
        ~Internal Service Port Abuse: Adversaries may abuse internal service ports like RDP (3389) or SSH (22) for C2 traffic
        ~Protocol Tunneling: Adversaries may tunnel non-standard protocols through commonly used ports to evade detection
        ~Standard Service Impersonation: Adversaries may make their C2 traffic appear as legitimate service traffic on standard ports
        ~Mixed Protocol Usage: Adversaries may alternate between different common ports to avoid detection
        ~Protocol Header Manipulation: Adversaries may modify protocol headers while using standard ports to bypass deep packet inspection
      Proxy: Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications to a command and control server to avoid direct connections to their infrastructure. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including [HTRAN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0040), ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap. (Citation: Trend Micro APT Attack Tools) Adversaries use these types of proxies to manage command and control communications, reduce the number of simultaneous outbound network connections, provide resiliency in the face of connection loss, or to ride over existing trusted communications paths between victims to avoid suspicion. Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to further disguise the source of malicious traffic.  Adversaries can also take advantage of routing schemes in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) to proxy command and control traffic.
        Domain Fronting: Adversaries may take advantage of routing schemes in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and other services which host multiple domains to obfuscate the intended destination of HTTPS traffic or traffic tunneled through HTTPS. (Citation: Fifield Blocking Resistent Communication through domain fronting 2015) Domain fronting involves using different domain names in the SNI field of the TLS header and the Host field of the HTTP header. If both domains are served from the same CDN, then the CDN may route to the address specified in the HTTP header after unwrapping the TLS header. A variation of the the technique, "domainless" fronting, utilizes a SNI field that is left blank; this may allow the fronting to work even when the CDN attempts to validate that the SNI and HTTP Host fields match (if the blank SNI fields are ignored).  For example, if domain-x and domain-y are customers of the same CDN, it is possible to place domain-x in the TLS header and domain-y in the HTTP header. Traffic will appear to be going to domain-x, however the CDN may route it to domain-y.
