      Non-Standard Port: Adversaries may communicate using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088(Citation: Symantec Elfin Mar 2019) or port 587(Citation: Fortinet Agent Tesla April 2018) as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data.  Adversaries may also make changes to victim systems to abuse non-standard ports. For example, Registry keys and other configuration settings can be used to modify protocol and port pairings.(Citation: change_rdp_port_conti)
        ~Web Protocol Abuse: Using HTTP/HTTPS protocols over uncommon ports (e.g., 8080, 8443) to evade network monitoring
        ~Remote Access Protocol Modification: Changing default ports for remote access services like RDP, SSH, or VNC
        ~DNS Port Variation: Using DNS protocol over non-standard ports instead of default port 53
        ~Mail Protocol Misuse: Utilizing SMTP/IMAP/POP3 protocols over non-standard ports
        ~Dynamic Port Selection: Implementing rotating port numbers for C2 communications
        ~Port Forwarding Configuration: Modifying system settings to redirect traffic through non-standard ports
        ~Application Layer Protocol Redirection: Tunneling application protocols through unexpected ports
        ~Custom Protocol Implementation: Using custom protocols over arbitrary ports
      Standard Cryptographic Protocol: Adversaries may explicitly employ a known encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if necessary secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files.
        ~Symmetric Key Encryption: Adversaries may use standard symmetric encryption algorithms (e.g., AES, DES) to encrypt C2 traffic using pre-shared keys
        ~Public Key Cryptography: Adversaries may implement asymmetric encryption protocols (e.g., RSA, ECC) for secure key exchange and C2 communication
        ~TLS/SSL Implementation: Adversaries may implement custom TLS/SSL stacks to encrypt C2 traffic while appearing as legitimate HTTPS traffic
        ~Block Cipher Modes: Adversaries may utilize various block cipher modes (CBC, ECB, CTR) to encrypt C2 communications
        ~Stream Ciphers: Adversaries may implement stream cipher algorithms (e.g., RC4, ChaCha20) for real-time encryption of C2 traffic
        ~Hash-based Encryption: Adversaries may use cryptographic hash functions (e.g., MD5, SHA) in combination with encryption for message authentication
        ~Hybrid Cryptosystems: Adversaries may combine multiple cryptographic protocols to create complex encryption schemes for C2 traffic
        ~Custom Protocol Encryption: Adversaries may implement custom variations of standard cryptographic protocols with modified parameters
      Custom Command and Control Protocol: Adversaries may communicate using a custom command and control protocol instead of encapsulating commands/data in an existing [Application Layer Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071). Implementations include mimicking well-known protocols or developing custom protocols (including raw sockets) on top of fundamental protocols provided by TCP/IP/another standard network stack.
        ~Protocol Mimicry: Adversaries design custom protocols to mimic characteristics of legitimate protocols like HTTP, DNS, or SMTP to avoid detection
        ~Binary Protocol Implementation: Adversaries implement binary-based custom protocols for efficient command transmission and reduced network footprint
        ~Custom Encoding Schemes: Adversaries use unique data encoding methods within custom protocols to obscure command and control traffic
        ~Stateful Protocol Design: Adversaries implement state-tracking mechanisms in custom protocols to maintain persistent control connections
        ~Custom Packet Structures: Adversaries design unique packet formats and headers for their command and control communications
        ~Raw Socket Communications: Adversaries bypass standard protocol stacks by implementing direct socket-level communications
        ~Time-Based Protocol Patterns: Adversaries implement timing-based patterns in their custom protocols to control traffic flow and avoid detection
        ~Multi-Layer Protocol Design: Adversaries implement protocols that operate across multiple network layers simultaneously
        ~Protocol Mutation: Adversaries design protocols that can dynamically change their characteristics based on network conditions or detection attempts
        ~Hybrid Protocol Implementation: Adversaries combine elements of multiple standard protocols to create unique communication channels
        ~Custom Error Recovery: Adversaries implement proprietary error detection and recovery mechanisms to ensure reliable command execution
        ~Protocol Fragmentation: Adversaries split communications across multiple channels or protocols to avoid detection
        ~Covert Signaling: Adversaries embed control signals within seemingly benign protocol behaviors
        ~Custom Authentication Schemes: Adversaries implement unique authentication mechanisms within their protocols to prevent unauthorized access
        ~Traffic Optimization: Adversaries design protocols with minimal overhead and compressed commands to reduce network footprint
      Custom Cryptographic Protocol: Adversaries may use a custom cryptographic protocol or algorithm to hide command and control traffic. A simple scheme, such as XOR-ing the plaintext with a fixed key, will produce a very weak ciphertext.  Custom encryption schemes may vary in sophistication. Analysis and reverse engineering of malware samples may be enough to discover the algorithm and encryption key used.  Some adversaries may also attempt to implement their own version of a well-known cryptographic algorithm instead of using a known implementation library, which may lead to unintentional errors. (Citation: F-Secure Cosmicduke)
        ~XOR-based Encryption: Use of simple XOR operations with static or dynamic keys to encode C2 traffic
        ~Custom Block Cipher Implementation: Adversary-developed implementations of block cipher algorithms with custom modifications
        ~Proprietary Stream Cipher: Custom streaming encryption protocols for real-time C2 communication
        ~Modified Standard Algorithms: Altered versions of known cryptographic algorithms like modified AES or RC4
        ~Time-based Key Rotation: Custom protocols using time-synchronized key generation and rotation
        ~Position-dependent Encryption: Encryption schemes where the algorithm changes based on data position or packet sequence
        ~Multi-layer Custom Encoding: Combination of multiple custom encoding and encryption layers
        ~Hardware-based Custom Protocol: Cryptographic protocols designed to leverage specific hardware characteristics
        ~Environmental Keying: Encryption schemes using environment-specific information as key material
        ~Polymorphic Encryption: Protocols that modify their encryption method based on various triggers
        ~Substitution-Based Protocol: Custom character or byte substitution schemes with dynamic mapping tables
        ~Protocol-Embedded Cryptography: Custom encryption embedded within legitimate protocol fields
        ~Mathematical Transform Protocol: Use of custom mathematical functions (non-standard algebra) for encryption
        ~Timing-Based Synchronization: Protocols using precise timing patterns for key generation and validation
        ~Custom Key Exchange Implementation: Proprietary methods for initial key exchange and session establishment
        ~State-Machine Encryption: Encryption schemes based on complex state machines and transitions
        ~Data Segmentation Protocol: Custom splitting and recombining of encrypted data with proprietary algorithms
        ~Checksum-Integrated Encryption: Custom protocols incorporating checksum-based encryption methods
        ~Context-Aware Protocol: Encryption that adapts based on network conditions or system state
        ~Hybrid Custom Protocol: Combination of multiple custom cryptographic methods with dynamic selection
        ~Error-Resilient Protocol: Custom encryption with built-in error detection and recovery mechanisms
        ~Custom Padding Scheme: Proprietary padding methods to obscure message lengths and patterns
        ~Anti-Analysis Protocol: Encryption schemes designed to resist reverse engineering and analysis
        ~Protocol Mutation: Encryption protocols that regularly modify their structure and behavior
        ~Format-Preserving Protocol: Custom encryption that maintains specific data formats while encrypting
        ~Decoy-Based Protocol: Encryption schemes incorporating deliberate decoy data or operations
        ~Side-Channel Aware Protocol: Custom cryptographic implementations designed to minimize side-channel leakage
        ~Memory-Optimized Protocol: Custom lightweight encryption designed for minimal memory footprint
        ~Compression-Integrated Protocol: Custom encryption combined with proprietary compression schemes
        ~Quantum-Resistant Custom Protocol: Custom implementations attempting to resist quantum computing attacks
        ~OS-Specific Protocol: Custom cryptographic implementations optimized for specific operating systems
        ~Network-Adaptive Protocol: Encryption schemes that adapt to network conditions and restrictions
        ~Authentication-Integrated Protocol: Custom protocols combining encryption with authentication mechanisms
        ~Fragmentation-Aware Protocol: Custom encryption designed for efficient fragmentation and reassembly
        ~Traffic-Pattern Masking: Protocols designed to mimic legitimate traffic patterns while encrypting
        ~Cross-Platform Protocol: Custom encryption designed to work consistently across different platforms
        ~Protocol-Switching: Dynamic switching between different custom encryption schemes based on detection
        ~Resource-Triggered Protocol: Encryption that adapts based on available system resources
        ~Custom Header Protocol: Encryption schemes with custom protocol headers for enhanced obfuscation
        ~Fallback-Enabled Protocol: Custom encryption with built-in fallback mechanisms for reliability
      Web Service: Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for relaying data to/from a compromised system. Popular websites, cloud services, and social media acting as a mechanism for C2 may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google, Microsoft, or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise.(Citation: Broadcom BirdyClient Microsoft Graph API 2024) Web service providers commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.  Use of Web services may also protect back-end C2 infrastructure from discovery through malware binary analysis while also enabling operational resiliency (since this infrastructure may be dynamically changed).
        One-Way Communication: Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for sending commands to a compromised system without receiving return output over the Web service channel. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems may opt to send the output from those commands back over a different C2 channel, including to another distinct Web service. Alternatively, compromised systems may return no output at all in cases where adversaries want to send instructions to systems and do not want a response.  Popular websites and social media acting as a mechanism for C2 may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise. Web service providers commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.
