      Hide Infrastructure: Adversaries may manipulate network traffic in order to hide and evade detection of their C2 infrastructure. This can be accomplished in various ways including by identifying and filtering traffic from defensive tools,(Citation: TA571) masking malicious domains to obfuscate the true destination from both automated scanning tools and security researchers,(Citation: Schema-abuse)(Citation: Facad1ng)(Citation: Browser-updates) and otherwise hiding malicious artifacts to delay discovery and prolong the effectiveness of adversary infrastructure that could otherwise be identified, blocked, or taken down entirely.  C2 networks may include the use of [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090) or VPNs to disguise IP addresses, which can allow adversaries to blend in with normal network traffic and bypass conditional access policies or anti-abuse protections. For example, an adversary may use a virtual private cloud to spoof their IP address to closer align with a victim's IP address ranges. This may also bypass security measures relying on geolocation of the source IP address.(Citation: sysdig)(Citation: Orange Residential Proxies)  Adversaries may also attempt to filter network traffic in order to evade defensive tools in numerous ways, including blocking/redirecting common incident responder or security appliance user agents.(Citation: mod_rewrite)(Citation: SocGholish-update) Filtering traffic based on IP and geo-fencing may also avoid automated sandboxing or researcher activity (i.e., [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497)).(Citation: TA571)(Citation: mod_rewrite)  Hiding C2 infrastructure may also be supported by [Resource Development](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0042) activities such as [Acquire Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583) and [Compromise Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584). For example, using widely trusted hosting services or domains such as prominent URL shortening providers or marketing services for C2 networks may enable adversaries to present benign content that later redirects victims to malicious web pages or infrastructure once specific conditions are met.(Citation: StarBlizzard)(Citation: QR-cofense)
        ~Traffic Filtering: Adversaries filter and manipulate network traffic to block or redirect security tools and researcher access
        ~Domain Fronting: Adversaries leverage trusted domains and CDNs to mask C2 traffic destination
        ~Dynamic DNS: Adversaries use dynamic DNS services to frequently change C2 infrastructure addressing
        ~Geographic IP Filtering: Adversaries implement geo-fencing to restrict access based on source IP location
        ~Legitimate Service Abuse: Adversaries leverage trusted services like URL shorteners or marketing platforms to hide C2 infrastructure
        ~IP Address Spoofing: Adversaries manipulate source IP addresses to match target environment ranges
        ~Protocol Masquerading: Adversaries disguise C2 traffic as legitimate application protocols
        ~Fast Flux DNS: Adversaries rapidly rotate IP addresses associated with C2 domain names
        ~Domain Generation: Adversaries use algorithms to generate domain names dynamically for C2 communication
        ~Traffic Redirection: Adversaries implement conditional redirects based on client characteristics
        ~Dead Drop Resolvers: Adversaries use legitimate web services as dead drop resolvers to store C2 configurations
        ~Traffic Padding: Adversaries add random data or adjust timing patterns to mask C2 communications
        ~Infrastructure Compartmentalization: Adversaries separate C2 infrastructure components to minimize detection
        ~Cloud Service Blending: Adversaries leverage cloud service APIs to blend C2 traffic with legitimate cloud traffic
        ~Multi-Stage Proxying: Adversaries use multiple layers of proxies to obscure the true C2 server location
        ~Domain Trust Abuse: Adversaries exploit domain trust relationships to hide C2 traffic
        ~Certificate Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate SSL/TLS certificates to make C2 traffic appear legitimate
        ~Content Delivery Network Abuse: Adversaries leverage CDN features to hide malicious content delivery
        ~DNS Tunneling: Adversaries encode C2 traffic within DNS queries and responses
        ~Social Media Channel Abuse: Adversaries use social media platforms as C2 channels
        ~Steganographic Communication: Adversaries hide C2 traffic within legitimate files or media
        ~Custom Protocol Implementation: Adversaries implement custom protocols to avoid detection
        ~Split DNS: Adversaries use split DNS configurations to serve different responses based on query source
      Multi-hop Proxy: To disguise the source of malicious traffic, adversaries may chain together multiple proxies. Typically, a defender will be able to identify the last proxy traffic traversed before it enters their network; the defender may or may not be able to identify any previous proxies before the last-hop proxy. This technique makes identifying the original source of the malicious traffic even more difficult by requiring the defender to trace malicious traffic through several proxies to identify its source.
        ~Domain Fronting Proxy Chains: Using multiple layers of domain fronting through CDNs and cloud services to mask C2 traffic
        ~Tor Network Routing: Utilizing The Onion Router network as part of the proxy chain for anonymization
        ~VPN Service Chaining: Chaining multiple commercial VPN services together to obscure traffic origin
        ~SOCKS Proxy Chains: Creating chains of SOCKS proxies through compromised hosts or rented infrastructure
        ~HTTP Proxy Layering: Using multiple HTTP proxies in sequence with different authentication methods
        ~Peer Relay Network: Using a network of compromised peers as proxy nodes for traffic routing
        ~Cloud Service Proxy Chain: Leveraging multiple cloud service providers as proxy hops
        ~Mixed Protocol Chain: Combining different proxy protocols (HTTP, SOCKS, SSH) in the same chain
        ~Dynamic Proxy Rotation: Automatically rotating through different proxy configurations
        ~Residential Proxy Networks: Using compromised home routers or IoT devices as proxy nodes
        ~DNS Proxy Chain: Using multiple DNS servers as proxy hops for command and control
        ~Blockchain Network Proxying: Leveraging blockchain networks and nodes for traffic routing
        ~IPv6 Tunnel Chain: Using chained IPv6 tunnels through multiple endpoints
        ~SSH Tunnel Chain: Creating nested SSH tunnels through multiple compromised hosts
        ~Messaging Service Proxy: Using multiple messaging services as proxy relays
        ~Geographic Traffic Routing: Deliberately routing traffic through specific countries or regions
        ~Container Registry Proxy: Using container registries and repositories as proxy points
        ~WebSocket Proxy Chain: Chaining multiple WebSocket connections through different servers
        ~ICMP Tunnel Chain: Using multiple ICMP tunneling relays for proxy communication
        ~Smart Contract Relay: Using smart contract platforms as intermediate proxy points
        ~Content Delivery Network Chain: Chaining multiple CDN providers as proxy hops
        ~Gaming Service Proxy: Using gaming services and platforms as proxy intermediaries
        ~MQTT Broker Chain: Using chains of MQTT brokers as proxy relays
      Content Injection: Adversaries may gain access and continuously communicate with victims by injecting malicious content into systems through online network traffic. Rather than luring victims to malicious payloads hosted on a compromised website (i.e., [Drive-by Target](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/004) followed by [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189)), adversaries may initially access victims through compromised data-transfer channels where they can manipulate traffic and/or inject their own content. These compromised online network channels may also be used to deliver additional payloads (i.e., [Ingress Tool Transfer](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105)) and other data to already compromised systems.(Citation: ESET MoustachedBouncer)  Adversaries may inject content to victim systems in various ways, including:  * From the middle, where the adversary is in-between legitimate online client-server communications (**Note:** this is similar but distinct from [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557), which describes AiTM activity solely within an enterprise environment) (Citation: Kaspersky Encyclopedia MiTM) * From the side, where malicious content is injected and races to the client as a fake response to requests of a legitimate online server (Citation: Kaspersky ManOnTheSide)  Content injection is often the result of compromised upstream communication channels, for example at the level of an internet service provider (ISP) as is the case with "lawful interception."(Citation: Kaspersky ManOnTheSide)(Citation: ESET MoustachedBouncer)(Citation: EFF China GitHub Attack)
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Injection: Adversaries position themselves between legitimate client-server communications to intercept and modify traffic, injecting malicious content into the data stream.
        ~Man-on-the-Side Injection: Adversaries monitor traffic and quickly respond with malicious content that races with legitimate server responses.
        ~ISP-Level Injection: Adversaries compromise or leverage access to Internet Service Provider infrastructure to inject content into network traffic at scale.
        ~DNS Response Injection: Adversaries inject malicious DNS responses to redirect victims to controlled infrastructure.
        ~HTTP Response Injection: Adversaries inject malicious content into HTTP responses before they reach the client.
        ~JavaScript Injection: Adversaries inject malicious JavaScript code into web traffic to establish persistent communication channels.
        ~Protocol-Specific Injection: Adversaries inject content into specific protocols (e.g., SMTP, FTP) to maintain command and control.
        ~CDN Injection: Adversaries compromise or abuse Content Delivery Networks to inject malicious content into legitimate web traffic.
      Data Obfuscation: Adversaries may obfuscate command and control traffic to make it more difficult to detect.(Citation: Bitdefender FunnyDream Campaign November 2020) Command and control (C2) communications are hidden (but not necessarily encrypted) in an attempt to make the content more difficult to discover or decipher and to make the communication less conspicuous and hide commands from being seen. This encompasses many methods, such as adding junk data to protocol traffic, using steganography, or impersonating legitimate protocols.
        Protocol or Service Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate protocols or web service traffic to disguise command and control activity and thwart analysis efforts. By impersonating legitimate protocols or web services, adversaries can make their command and control traffic blend in with legitimate network traffic.    Adversaries may impersonate a fake SSL/TLS handshake to make it look like subsequent traffic is SSL/TLS encrypted, potentially interfering with some security tooling, or to make the traffic look like it is related with a trusted entity.   Adversaries may also leverage legitimate protocols to impersonate expected web traffic or trusted services. For example, adversaries may manipulate HTTP headers, URI endpoints, SSL certificates, and transmitted data to disguise C2 communications or mimic legitimate services such as Gmail, Google Drive, and Yahoo Messenger.(Citation: ESET Okrum July 2019)(Citation: Malleable-C2-U42)
          Modify HTTP User-Agent strings to match legitimate browsers and include common header fields to mimic normal web traffic
          Generate fake SSL certificates using tools like OpenSSL with common trusted CA names to appear legitimate
          Configure C2 traffic to use standard ports (80/443) and format payloads to look like normal HTTP/HTTPS requests
          Use Cobalt Strike Malleable C2 profiles to make traffic appear as legitimate services like Office365 or Amazon AWS
          Implement custom C2 channels that mimic DNS queries using tools like dnscat2
          Format C2 traffic to match Gmail API patterns including authentic-looking email headers and base64 encoded content
          Structure C2 beacons to match CDN traffic patterns with appropriate cache-control headers and content-types
          Use domain fronting techniques through legitimate cloud services to mask C2 infrastructure
