        Junk Data: Adversaries may add junk data to protocols used for command and control to make detection more difficult.(Citation: FireEye SUNBURST Backdoor December 2020) By adding random or meaningless data to the protocols used for command and control, adversaries can prevent trivial methods for decoding, deciphering, or otherwise analyzing the traffic. Examples may include appending/prepending data with junk characters or writing junk characters between significant characters.
          Append random Base64-encoded strings to HTTP request parameters to mimic legitimate web traffic
          Insert null bytes or random whitespace characters between command strings
          Add misleading DNS TXT records with garbage data alongside legitimate C2 records
          Implement random padding in TCP payloads to vary packet sizes
          Use Unicode homoglyphs and zero-width characters in HTTP headers
          Inject fake JSON/XML elements with random data in API-like communications
          Add commented-out garbage code in HTML/JavaScript responses
          Randomize packet lengths by adding variable-length padding
          Mix legitimate and junk HTTP cookies in requests
          Embed C2 data within fake error messages padded with random strings
          Add random email headers and MIME boundaries in SMTP-based C2
