          Hide C2 traffic in certificate fields and extensions
          Implement steganography in WMF/EMF image format metadata
      Protocol Tunneling: Adversaries may tunnel network communications to and from a victim system within a separate protocol to avoid detection/network filtering and/or enable access to otherwise unreachable systems. Tunneling involves explicitly encapsulating a protocol within another. This behavior may conceal malicious traffic by blending in with existing traffic and/or provide an outer layer of encryption (similar to a VPN). Tunneling could also enable routing of network packets that would otherwise not reach their intended destination, such as SMB, RDP, or other traffic that would be filtered by network appliances or not routed over the Internet.   There are various means to encapsulate a protocol within another protocol. For example, adversaries may perform SSH tunneling (also known as SSH port forwarding), which involves forwarding arbitrary data over an encrypted SSH tunnel.(Citation: SSH Tunneling)(Citation: Sygnia Abyss Locker 2025)   [Protocol Tunneling](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1572) may also be abused by adversaries during [Dynamic Resolution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568). Known as DNS over HTTPS (DoH), queries to resolve C2 infrastructure may be encapsulated within encrypted HTTPS packets.(Citation: BleepingComp Godlua JUL19)   Adversaries may also leverage [Protocol Tunneling](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1572) in conjunction with [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090) and/or [Protocol or Service Impersonation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/003) to further conceal C2 communications and infrastructure.
        ~SSH Tunneling: Adversaries use SSH port forwarding to tunnel traffic through encrypted SSH connections, allowing them to bypass firewalls and hide malicious traffic within legitimate SSH sessions.
        ~DNS Tunneling: Adversaries encode command and control traffic within DNS queries and responses, leveraging DNS protocol to exfiltrate data or maintain C2 communications through typically allowed protocols.
        ~HTTPS Tunneling: Adversaries encapsulate non-HTTP protocols within HTTPS traffic to bypass security controls and hide malicious traffic within legitimate-looking encrypted web traffic.
        ~ICMP Tunneling: Adversaries embed command and control traffic within ICMP echo requests and replies to avoid detection, as ICMP is often allowed through firewalls.
        ~TCP/UDP Protocol Wrapping: Adversaries wrap prohibited protocols within allowed TCP or UDP streams to bypass protocol-based filtering and network segmentation.
        ~WebSocket Tunneling: Adversaries leverage WebSocket protocol to establish persistent, bidirectional tunnels through web infrastructure, often bypassing HTTP proxy inspection.
        ~IPv6 Tunneling: Adversaries encapsulate IPv4 traffic within IPv6 packets or vice versa to bypass network controls that may only monitor one IP version.
        ~RDP Tunneling: Adversaries tunnel other protocols through RDP virtual channels, leveraging legitimate remote desktop sessions to hide malicious traffic.
        ~SMB Tunneling: Adversaries abuse SMB protocol to tunnel command and control traffic, especially in internal networks where SMB is commonly allowed.
        ~HTTP/3 and QUIC Tunneling: Adversaries leverage newer transport protocols like QUIC to tunnel traffic, taking advantage of UDP-based encryption and multiplexing capabilities.
    exfiltration: The adversary is trying to steal data.  Exfiltration consists of techniques that adversaries may use to steal data from your network. Once they’ve collected data, adversaries often package it to avoid detection while removing it. This can include compression and encryption. Techniques for getting data out of a target network typically include transferring it over their command and control channel or an alternate channel and may also include putting size limits on the transmission.
      Transfer Data to Cloud Account: Adversaries may exfiltrate data by transferring the data, including through sharing/syncing and creating backups of cloud environments, to another cloud account they control on the same service.  A defender who is monitoring for large transfers to outside the cloud environment through normal file transfers or over command and control channels may not be watching for data transfers to another account within the same cloud provider. Such transfers may utilize existing cloud provider APIs and the internal address space of the cloud provider to blend into normal traffic or avoid data transfers over external network interfaces.(Citation: TLDRSec AWS Attacks)  Adversaries may also use cloud-native mechanisms to share victim data with adversary-controlled cloud accounts, such as creating anonymous file sharing links or, in Azure, a shared access signature (SAS) URI.(Citation: Microsoft Azure Storage Shared Access Signature)  Incidents have been observed where adversaries have created backups of cloud instances and transferred them to separate accounts.(Citation: DOJ GRU Indictment Jul 2018)
        ~AWS S3 Cross-Account Access: Use of AWS S3 bucket policies and IAM roles to enable data transfer between accounts
        ~Azure Blob Storage Sharing: Creation and use of SAS tokens for controlled access to storage accounts
        ~Cloud Storage Synchronization: Leveraging native sync features to mirror data between accounts
        ~Instance Snapshot Transfer: Creating and sharing VM/instance snapshots across accounts
        ~Cloud Backup Exfiltration: Using cloud backup services to transfer data between accounts
        ~Anonymous Access Links: Generation of temporary public access URLs for data transfer
        ~Service Principal Delegation: Using delegated access permissions to transfer data between tenants
        ~Storage Account Replication: Configuring cross-account storage replication features
        ~Cloud Database Export: Exporting database contents to another account's storage
        ~Container Registry Transfer: Moving container images between accounts
        ~Cloud Drive Sharing: Using cloud storage services like Google Drive, OneDrive for Business to share data
        ~Cross-Region Replication: Configuring replication to adversary-controlled regions
        ~Managed Identity Abuse: Exploiting managed identities to access resources across accounts
        ~Federation Trust Exploitation: Using established federation trust relationships for cross-account access
        ~Cloud Function Transfer: Using serverless functions to move data between accounts
        ~API Gateway Proxying: Leveraging API gateways to relay data between accounts
        ~Cloud Storage Mount Points: Mounting storage volumes across accounts
        ~Service Mesh Transfer: Exploiting service mesh configurations for cross-account data movement
        ~Marketplace Service Abuse: Exploiting shared marketplace services to transfer data between accounts
        ~Cloud Logging Transfer: Abusing logging services to send data to adversary-controlled accounts
        ~Development Environment Sync: Using cloud IDE and development environment sync features
        ~Cross-Account Pipeline Abuse: Exploiting CI/CD pipelines for data transfer
        ~Cloud Cache Service Transfer: Using distributed cache services to move data between accounts
        ~Shared Resource Exploitation: Leveraging shared resource pools between accounts
      Automated Exfiltration: Adversaries may exfiltrate data, such as sensitive documents, through the use of automated processing after being gathered during Collection.(Citation: ESET Gamaredon June 2020)   When automated exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) and [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).
        Traffic Duplication: Adversaries may leverage traffic mirroring in order to automate data exfiltration over compromised infrastructure. Traffic mirroring is a native feature for some devices, often used for network analysis. For example, devices may be configured to forward network traffic to one or more destinations for analysis by a network analyzer or other monitoring device. (Citation: Cisco Traffic Mirroring)(Citation: Juniper Traffic Mirroring)  Adversaries may abuse traffic mirroring to mirror or redirect network traffic through other infrastructure they control. Malicious modifications to network devices to enable traffic redirection may be possible through [ROMMONkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/004) or [Patch System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/001).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)  Many cloud-based environments also support traffic mirroring. For example, AWS Traffic Mirroring, GCP Packet Mirroring, and Azure vTap allow users to define specified instances to collect traffic from and specified targets to send collected traffic to.(Citation: AWS Traffic Mirroring)(Citation: GCP Packet Mirroring)(Citation: Azure Virtual Network TAP)  Adversaries may use traffic duplication in conjunction with [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040), [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056), or [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557) depending on the goals and objectives of the adversary.
          Configure Cisco SPAN/RSPAN to mirror traffic to attacker-controlled collector
          Set up AWS VPC Traffic Mirroring with compromised target/destination instances
          Implement GCP Packet Mirroring between victim and attacker-controlled instances
          Deploy Azure vTAP to redirect traffic to malicious monitoring endpoints
          Modify network device firmware via ROMMONkit to enable covert traffic duplication
          Use Juniper port mirroring features to duplicate traffic to remote collection points
          Patch network device system images to include persistent traffic mirroring capabilities
          Configure switch port analyzers to duplicate traffic to unauthorized capture points
          Set up transparent traffic duplication through compromised network TAP devices
          Install malicious network monitoring tools that leverage native mirroring APIs
          Configure VMware vSphere distributed switch port mirroring
          Abuse Wireshark remote capture interfaces for traffic duplication
          Manipulate OpenFlow controllers to duplicate traffic in SDN environments
          Deploy transparent network bridges with mirroring capabilities
          Set up ERSPAN (Encapsulated Remote SPAN) tunnels for traffic duplication
          Configure VSS (Virtual Switch System) mirroring on virtual networks
          Implement VXLAN-based traffic mirroring in overlay networks
          Use network packet brokers for unauthorized traffic duplication
      Data Encrypted: Data is encrypted before being exfiltrated in order to hide the information that is being exfiltrated from detection or to make the exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender. The encryption is performed by a utility, programming library, or custom algorithm on the data itself and is considered separate from any encryption performed by the command and control or file transfer protocol. Common file archive formats that can encrypt files are RAR and zip.  Other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) and [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048)
        ~Symmetric Encryption: Using symmetric encryption algorithms (like AES) with shared keys to encrypt data before exfiltration
        ~Public Key Encryption: Using asymmetric encryption with public/private key pairs to secure data
        ~Custom Encryption Algorithms: Implementation of proprietary or custom encryption methods to avoid detection
        ~Password-Protected Archives: Creating password-protected compressed archives using utilities like RAR or ZIP
        ~File-Level Encryption: Encrypting individual files using built-in OS encryption features or third-party tools
        ~Volume Encryption: Encrypting entire volumes or containers before exfiltrating their contents
        ~Steganography: Hiding encrypted data within other file formats to make exfiltration less detectable
        ~Hardware-Based Encryption: Using hardware encryption modules or TPM for data encryption
        ~Memory-Based Encryption: Encrypting data in memory before writing to disk or exfiltrating
        ~Multi-Layer Encryption: Using multiple encryption methods or nested encrypted containers
        ~One-Time Pad Encryption: Using randomly generated one-time pads for perfect secrecy
        ~Format-Preserving Encryption: Encrypting data while maintaining the original data format
        ~Stream Encryption: Encrypting data streams on-the-fly during exfiltration
        ~Key Rotation Encryption: Using rotating encryption keys to complicate detection and analysis
        ~Time-Based Encryption: Using time-based keys or algorithms for encryption
        ~Split-Key Encryption: Distributing encryption keys across multiple locations or systems
        ~Environment-Keyed Encryption: Using system-specific information as encryption keys
        ~Polymorphic Encryption: Changing encryption methods or parameters dynamically
        ~Protocol-Embedded Encryption: Encrypting data within existing protocol structures
        ~Database Field Encryption: Selectively encrypting specific database fields or records
        ~Blockchain-Based Encryption: Using blockchain technology for encryption and key management
        ~Quantum-Resistant Encryption: Using encryption algorithms designed to resist quantum computing attacks
        ~Homomorphic Encryption: Encrypting data while maintaining ability to perform operations on it
        ~Recovery-Enabled Encryption: Including recovery mechanisms within encrypted data
      Scheduled Transfer: Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability.  When scheduled exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) or [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).
        ~Business Hours Alignment: Schedule exfiltration during normal business hours when network traffic is high to blend with legitimate activities
        ~Off-Hours Transfer: Schedule data transfers during off-hours when monitoring might be reduced
        ~Network Load-Based: Time exfiltration based on network load conditions to avoid triggering performance-based alerts
        ~System State Dependent: Schedule transfers based on specific system states like CPU usage or memory availability
        ~Periodic Micro-Transfers: Schedule small, regular transfers instead of bulk exfiltration to avoid volume-based detection
        ~Event-Triggered Transfer: Schedule exfiltration based on specific system or user events (like user logoffs or system backups)
        ~Geographic Time Zone Alignment: Schedule transfers to align with time zones of legitimate business partners or branch offices
        ~Calendar-Based Scheduling: Coordinate transfers with predictable organizational events or meetings
        ~Maintenance Window Alignment: Schedule transfers during known system maintenance windows
        ~Automated Task Scheduling: Use built-in task scheduling mechanisms (like cron jobs or Windows Task Scheduler) for persistent scheduling
      Exfiltration Over C2 Channel: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an existing command and control channel. Stolen data is encoded into the normal communications channel using the same protocol as command and control communications.
        ~Protocol Tunneling: Adversaries tunnel stolen data within legitimate protocol traffic (HTTP, DNS, ICMP) used by the C2 channel
        ~Steganographic Encoding: Adversaries hide stolen data within seemingly benign C2 traffic using steganography techniques
        ~Chunked Transfer: Adversaries split large data into smaller chunks to avoid detection and transfer limits
        ~Custom Protocol Encoding: Adversaries use proprietary or custom protocols to encode stolen data within C2 traffic
        ~Base64 Wrapping: Adversaries encode stolen data using Base64 or similar encoding schemes within C2 communications
        ~Encrypted Payload: Adversaries encrypt stolen data before sending it through the C2 channel
        ~Header Manipulation: Adversaries hide data within modified protocol headers of C2 traffic
        ~Timing-Based Exfiltration: Adversaries use timing variations in C2 traffic to encode stolen data
        ~Compression-Based: Adversaries compress stolen data to reduce size and avoid detection during C2 transfer
        ~Multiplexed Streams: Adversaries interleave stolen data with legitimate C2 traffic across multiple streams
        ~Session Cookie Manipulation: Adversaries embed stolen data within modified session cookies used by the C2 channel
        ~Error Message Encoding: Adversaries hide data within fake error messages or response codes in C2 traffic
        ~Fragmented Encoding: Adversaries split data across multiple non-sequential C2 messages to evade detection
        ~Polymorphic Encoding: Adversaries dynamically change encoding patterns across C2 sessions
        ~Traffic Pattern Mimicry: Adversaries adjust exfiltration timing and volume to match legitimate C2 patterns
        ~Metadata Field Abuse: Adversaries hide data within metadata fields of C2 protocols
        ~Multi-Stage Transfer: Adversaries use multiple C2 channels in sequence to transfer different parts of the data
        ~Protocol Switching: Adversaries alternate between different protocols within the same C2 channel for exfiltration
      Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over a different protocol than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.    Alternate protocols include FTP, SMTP, HTTP/S, DNS, SMB, or any other network protocol not being used as the main command and control channel. Adversaries may also opt to encrypt and/or obfuscate these alternate channels.   [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048) can be done using various common operating system utilities such as [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039)/SMB or FTP.(Citation: Palo Alto OilRig Oct 2016) On macOS and Linux <code>curl</code> may be used to invoke protocols such as HTTP/S or FTP/S to exfiltrate data from a system.(Citation: 20 macOS Common Tools and Techniques)  Many IaaS and SaaS platforms (such as Microsoft Exchange, Microsoft SharePoint, GitHub, and AWS S3) support the direct download of files, emails, source code, and other sensitive information via the web console or [Cloud API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/009).
        Exfiltration Over Asymmetric Encrypted Non-C2 Protocol: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an asymmetrically encrypted network protocol other than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.   Asymmetric encryption algorithms are those that use different keys on each end of the channel. Also known as public-key cryptography, this requires pairs of cryptographic keys that can encrypt/decrypt data from the corresponding key. Each end of the communication channels requires a private key (only in the procession of that entity) and the public key of the other entity. The public keys of each entity are exchanged before encrypted communications begin.   Network protocols that use asymmetric encryption (such as HTTPS/TLS/SSL) often utilize symmetric encryption once keys are exchanged. Adversaries may opt to use these encrypted mechanisms that are baked into a protocol.
