      Data Transfer Size Limits: An adversary may exfiltrate data in fixed size chunks instead of whole files or limit packet sizes below certain thresholds. This approach may be used to avoid triggering network data transfer threshold alerts.
        ~Fixed-size Chunking: Breaking data into predetermined fixed-size chunks before transmission
        ~Network Protocol Size Limits: Limiting packet sizes to match common protocol standards (e.g., DNS, HTTP)
        ~Bandwidth-aware Transfer: Dynamically adjusting transfer sizes based on network bandwidth monitoring
        ~Time-based Segmentation: Splitting data transfers across time intervals with size limits per interval
        ~Protocol-specific Fragmentation: Using protocol-level fragmentation to control transfer sizes
        ~Custom Transfer Windows: Implementing custom windows for controlled data transfer sizes
        ~Header Size Manipulation: Adjusting packet header sizes to stay within specific thresholds
        ~Buffer-based Limitations: Using buffer size restrictions to control data transfer amounts
        ~Stream Size Control: Implementing streaming with controlled segment sizes
        ~Adaptive Size Limiting: Dynamically adjusting transfer sizes based on network monitoring patterns
        ~Compression-based Sizing: Applying compression before transfer to meet size limits
        ~Multi-Channel Distribution: Distributing data across multiple channels with individual size limits
        ~Queue-based Transfer: Using message queues with size restrictions for controlled transfer
        ~Threshold-aware Splitting: Splitting data based on known network monitoring thresholds
        ~Size-based Encoding: Using specific encoding schemes to control transfer sizes
      Data Compressed: An adversary may compress data (e.g., sensitive documents) that is collected prior to exfiltration in order to make it portable and minimize the amount of data sent over the network. The compression is done separately from the exfiltration channel and is performed using a custom program or algorithm, or a more common compression library or utility such as 7zip, RAR, ZIP, or zlib.
        ~Archive Formats: Adversaries compress data using common archive formats like ZIP, RAR, 7z to avoid detection and reduce file size
        ~Custom Compression: Adversaries develop and use custom compression algorithms to evade detection and bypass security controls
        ~Embedded Compression: Adversaries leverage built-in compression capabilities within file formats (like Office documents) or protocols
        ~Staged Compression: Adversaries compress data in multiple stages or layers to further reduce size and complicate analysis
        ~Selective Compression: Adversaries compress specific file types or data categories based on sensitivity or size requirements
        ~Memory-based Compression: Adversaries compress data directly in memory before writing to disk or transmitting
        ~Stream Compression: Adversaries compress data as a continuous stream rather than discrete files
        ~Hardware-accelerated Compression: Adversaries utilize hardware capabilities for faster compression of large datasets
      Exfiltration Over Other Network Medium: Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over a different network medium than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, the exfiltration may occur, for example, over a WiFi connection, modem, cellular data connection, Bluetooth, or another radio frequency (RF) channel.  Adversaries may choose to do this if they have sufficient access or proximity, and the connection might not be secured or defended as well as the primary Internet-connected channel because it is not routed through the same enterprise network.
        Exfiltration Over Bluetooth: Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over Bluetooth rather than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, an adversary may opt to exfiltrate data using a Bluetooth communication channel.  Adversaries may choose to do this if they have sufficient access and proximity. Bluetooth connections might not be secured or defended as well as the primary Internet-connected channel because it is not routed through the same enterprise network.
