    impact: The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, or destroy your systems and data.   Impact consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt availability or compromise integrity by manipulating business and operational processes. Techniques used for impact can include destroying or tampering with data. In some cases, business processes can look fine, but may have been altered to benefit the adversaries’ goals. These techniques might be used by adversaries to follow through on their end goal or to provide cover for a confidentiality breach.
      Stored Data Manipulation: Adversaries may insert, delete, or manipulate data at rest in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity.(Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018)(Citation: DOJ Lazarus Sony 2018) By manipulating stored data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making.   Stored data could include a variety of file formats, such as Office files, databases, stored emails, and custom file formats. The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the type of data as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system that would typically be gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign in order to have the desired impact.
        ~Database Record Manipulation: Adversaries modify database records to alter business data, financial records, or audit logs
        ~File Content Alteration: Adversaries modify contents of files like documents, spreadsheets, or configuration files
        ~Email Archive Tampering: Adversaries manipulate stored email archives to remove evidence or plant false information
        ~Log File Manipulation: Adversaries modify system and application logs to hide their activities or create false trails
        ~Backup Data Corruption: Adversaries tamper with backup files to prevent system recovery or hide previous activities
        ~Configuration File Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored configuration files to alter system behavior
        ~Source Code Manipulation: Adversaries modify source code repositories or compiled code to insert malicious logic
        ~Accounting Data Manipulation: Adversaries alter financial records, transaction logs, or accounting databases
        ~Identity Store Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored identity information like user accounts, permissions, or certificates
        ~Metadata Manipulation: Adversaries alter file metadata, timestamps, or attributes to hide activities or create confusion
        ~Blockchain Data Manipulation: Adversaries modify distributed ledger data to alter transaction history or smart contracts
        ~Cloud Storage Manipulation: Adversaries tamper with data stored in cloud storage services or object stores
        ~Industrial Control System Data Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored process parameters, setpoints, or calibration data
        ~Medical Record Manipulation: Adversaries alter patient records, diagnostic data, or treatment information
        ~Version Control History Manipulation: Adversaries modify repository history, commits, or tags to hide malicious changes
        ~Machine Learning Model Manipulation: Adversaries alter stored ML models or training data to affect system behavior
        ~Geographic Information System Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored maps, coordinates, or spatial data
        ~Digital Certificate Store Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored certificates or certificate trust relationships
        ~Registry Data Manipulation: Adversaries modify Windows Registry or similar system databases
        ~Virtual Machine Image Manipulation: Adversaries modify stored VM images, snapshots, or templates
        ~Firmware Image Manipulation: Adversaries alter stored firmware images or updates before deployment
        ~Embedded Device Storage Manipulation: Adversaries modify data stored in IoT or embedded device memory
        ~Time Series Data Manipulation: Adversaries alter historical sensor data, metrics, or monitoring information
        ~Document Management System Manipulation: Adversaries modify content in enterprise document management systems
        ~Audio/Video Archive Manipulation: Adversaries alter stored multimedia content or its metadata
        ~Backup Catalog Manipulation: Adversaries modify backup indexes or catalogs to prevent specific data recovery
      Data Manipulation: Adversaries may insert, delete, or manipulate data in order to influence external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data.(Citation: Sygnia Elephant Beetle Jan 2022) By manipulating data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making.  The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the target application and process as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system that would typically be gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign in order to have the desired impact.
        Transmitted Data Manipulation: Adversaries may alter data en route to storage or other systems in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data.(Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018)(Citation: DOJ Lazarus Sony 2018) By manipulating transmitted data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making.  Manipulation may be possible over a network connection or between system processes where there is an opportunity deploy a tool that will intercept and change information. The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the target transmission mechanism as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system that would typically be gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign in order to have the desired impact.
          Use ARP spoofing with Ettercap to intercept and modify network traffic between hosts
          Deploy SSL/TLS MITM proxy to modify HTTPS traffic using tools like mitmproxy
          Inject malicious code into network packets using packet manipulation tools like Scapy
          Modify DNS responses to redirect traffic through adversary-controlled proxy
          Use process injection to hook network API calls and modify data before transmission
          Implement TCP session hijacking to inject modified packets into existing connections
          Deploy malicious network drivers to intercept and modify network stack data
