        Application or System Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities that can cause an application or system to crash and deny availability to users. (Citation: Sucuri BIND9 August 2015) Some systems may automatically restart critical applications and services when crashes occur, but they can likely be re-exploited to cause a persistent denial of service (DoS) condition.  Adversaries may exploit known or zero-day vulnerabilities to crash applications and/or systems, which may also lead to dependent applications and/or systems to be in a DoS condition. Crashed or restarted applications or systems may also have other effects such as [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485), [Firmware Corruption](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1495), [Service Stop](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1489) etc. which may further cause a DoS condition and deny availability to critical information, applications and/or systems.
          Exploit BIND9 DNS server vulnerabilities (CVE-2015-5477) to crash DNS services
          Use buffer overflow exploits against web servers like Apache to cause system crashes
          Exploit memory corruption vulnerabilities in critical system services
          Target known vulnerabilities in database management systems (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL) to cause service failures
          Exploit race conditions in application threading to cause system hangs
          Use malformed packet attacks against network service daemons
          Deploy recursive function calls to exhaust application stack memory
          Exploit integer overflow vulnerabilities in system APIs
          Target known vulnerabilities in container runtimes to crash containerized applications
          Use format string attacks against legacy applications to cause crashes
          Exploit kernel vulnerabilities to trigger system panics and crashes
          Target Java Virtual Machine memory management bugs to crash enterprise applications
          Use SSL/TLS protocol implementation vulnerabilities to crash security services
          Exploit Windows RPC service vulnerabilities to crash system services
          Target embedded device firmware through buffer overflow exploits
          Exploit memory leaks in long-running service applications
          Use malformed input to crash XML parsers in web services
          Target vulnerabilities in load balancer software to disrupt service availability
          Exploit authentication service vulnerabilities to prevent system access
          Use heap spray attacks against browser engines to crash web applications
          Exploit vulnerabilities in message queue systems (RabbitMQ, Apache Kafka) to crash messaging infrastructure
          Target shared library dependencies to cause cascading application failures
          Exploit vulnerabilities in logging services (log4j) to crash application servers
          Use malformed requests to crash API gateway services
          Target memory management in Node.js event loop to crash JavaScript applications
          Exploit vulnerabilities in caching services (Redis, Memcached) to disrupt application performance
          Target session management components in web frameworks to cause application crashes
          Exploit vulnerabilities in service mesh infrastructure (Istio, Linkerd)
          Use malformed GraphQL queries to crash API servers
          Target container orchestration systems (Kubernetes) through component vulnerabilities
          Exploit vulnerabilities in MQTT brokers to crash IoT device communication
          Target real-time operating system (RTOS) components to crash critical systems
          Exploit vulnerabilities in VoIP servers (Asterisk, FreeSWITCH) to disrupt communication services
          Use malformed gRPC messages to crash microservice communications
          Target time-series database engines (InfluxDB, Prometheus) to crash monitoring systems
          Exploit vulnerabilities in stream processing engines (Apache Flink, Storm)
          Target WebSocket server implementations to crash real-time web applications
          Exploit vulnerabilities in service discovery systems (Consul, etcd)
          Use malformed LDAP queries to crash directory services
          Target CI/CD pipeline components (Jenkins, GitLab runners) to disrupt development operations
          Exploit vulnerabilities in blockchain nodes to crash distributed ledger networks
          Target SCADA protocol implementations to crash industrial control systems
          Exploit vulnerabilities in payment gateway services to disrupt financial transactions
          Use malformed DICOM protocol messages to crash medical imaging systems
          Target electronic health record (EHR) system components to disrupt healthcare services
          Exploit vulnerabilities in trading platform middleware to crash financial systems
          Target building management system (BMS) protocols to disrupt facility operations
          Exploit vulnerabilities in smart grid communication protocols
          Use malformed CANbus messages to crash automotive systems
          Target air traffic control system components through protocol vulnerabilities
      Network Denial of Service: Adversaries may perform Network Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of targeted resources to users. Network DoS can be performed by exhausting the network bandwidth services rely on. Example resources include specific websites, email services, DNS, and web-based applications. Adversaries have been observed conducting network DoS attacks for political purposes(Citation: FireEye OpPoisonedHandover February 2016) and to support other malicious activities, including distraction(Citation: FSISAC FraudNetDoS September 2012), hacktivism, and extortion.(Citation: Symantec DDoS October 2014)  A Network DoS will occur when the bandwidth capacity of the network connection to a system is exhausted due to the volume of malicious traffic directed at the resource or the network connections and network devices the resource relies on. For example, an adversary may send 10Gbps of traffic to a server that is hosted by a network with a 1Gbps connection to the internet. This traffic can be generated by a single system or multiple systems spread across the internet, which is commonly referred to as a distributed DoS (DDoS).  To perform Network DoS attacks several aspects apply to multiple methods, including IP address spoofing, and botnets.  Adversaries may use the original IP address of an attacking system, or spoof the source IP address to make the attack traffic more difficult to trace back to the attacking system or to enable reflection. This can increase the difficulty defenders have in defending against the attack by reducing or eliminating the effectiveness of filtering by the source address on network defense devices.  For DoS attacks targeting the hosting system directly, see [Endpoint Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499).
        Direct Network Flood: Adversaries may attempt to cause a denial of service (DoS) by directly sending a high-volume of network traffic to a target. This DoS attack may also reduce the availability and functionality of the targeted system(s) and network. [Direct Network Flood](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498/001)s are when one or more systems are used to send a high-volume of network packets towards the targeted service's network. Almost any network protocol may be used for flooding. Stateless protocols such as UDP or ICMP are commonly used but stateful protocols such as TCP can be used as well.  Botnets are commonly used to conduct network flooding attacks against networks and services. Large botnets can generate a significant amount of traffic from systems spread across the global Internet. Adversaries may have the resources to build out and control their own botnet infrastructure or may rent time on an existing botnet to conduct an attack. In some of the worst cases for distributed DoS (DDoS), so many systems are used to generate the flood that each one only needs to send out a small amount of traffic to produce enough volume to saturate the target network. In such circumstances, distinguishing DDoS traffic from legitimate clients becomes exceedingly difficult. Botnets have been used in some of the most high-profile DDoS flooding attacks, such as the 2012 series of incidents that targeted major US banks.(Citation: USNYAG IranianBotnet March 2016)
          Use LOIC (Low Orbit Ion Cannon) to generate UDP flood attacks targeting specific IP addresses and ports
          Deploy Mirai botnet variants to coordinate large-scale TCP SYN floods from compromised IoT devices
          Utilize HOIC (High Orbit Ion Cannon) for HTTP GET/POST flood attacks against web servers
          Execute ICMP flood attacks using custom Python scripts with scapy library
          Employ hping3 tool to generate TCP SYN flood attacks with spoofed source IPs
          Configure botnet C2 infrastructure using IRC channels to coordinate DDoS attacks
          Use UDP amplification through misconfigured DNS servers with spoofed source addresses
          Deploy BlackEnergy malware for coordinated volumetric DDoS attacks
          Implement network stress testing tools like Slowloris for sustained TCP connection floods
          Utilize distributed attack platforms like HULK (HTTP Unbearable Load King) for HTTP flooding
          Leverage AWS EC2 instances with spoofed IPs to launch distributed TCP floods
          Use Tsunami UDP flooder with randomized packet payloads
          Deploy Trinity DDoS tool for mixed protocol flooding attacks
          Implement XOIC for targeted application layer floods
          Use Dirt Jumper toolkit for coordinated volumetric attacks
          Deploy Golden Eye HTTP flood generator targeting application weak points
          Utilize Pandora DDoS framework for multi-vector attacks
          Configure RUDY (R-U-Dead-Yet) for low-and-slow HTTP POST floods
          Use Xerxes tool for establishing multiple SSL connections
          Deploy DDOSIM-Layer7 for application layer protocol floods
          Use Impulse toolkit for multi-protocol DDoS from mobile devices
          Deploy Ufonet botnet framework with volunteer zombie networks
          Implement DAVOSET (DDoS attacks via other sites execution tool) for reflected floods
          Use Memcrashed tool for memcached amplification attacks
          Deploy Saddam DDoS tool for amplified DNS/NTP/SNMP floods
          Configure LORIS toolkit for Apache server targeted attacks
          Use Saphyra for multi-threaded HTTP POST flood attacks
          Deploy THC-SSL-DOS for SSL/TLS resource exhaustion
          Implement Torshammer for anonymous Tor-based HTTP floods
          Use PyLoris for advanced slow HTTP attack vectors
        Reflection Amplification: Adversaries may attempt to cause a denial of service (DoS) by reflecting a high-volume of network traffic to a target. This type of Network DoS takes advantage of a third-party server intermediary that hosts and will respond to a given spoofed source IP address. This third-party server is commonly termed a reflector. An adversary accomplishes a reflection attack by sending packets to reflectors with the spoofed address of the victim. Similar to Direct Network Floods, more than one system may be used to conduct the attack, or a botnet may be used. Likewise, one or more reflectors may be used to focus traffic on the target.(Citation: Cloudflare ReflectionDoS May 2017) This Network DoS attack may also reduce the availability and functionality of the targeted system(s) and network.  Reflection attacks often take advantage of protocols with larger responses than requests in order to amplify their traffic, commonly known as a Reflection Amplification attack. Adversaries may be able to generate an increase in volume of attack traffic that is several orders of magnitude greater than the requests sent to the amplifiers. The extent of this increase will depending upon many variables, such as the protocol in question, the technique used, and the amplifying servers that actually produce the amplification in attack volume. Two prominent protocols that have enabled Reflection Amplification Floods are DNS(Citation: Cloudflare DNSamplficationDoS) and NTP(Citation: Cloudflare NTPamplifciationDoS), though the use of several others in the wild have been documented.(Citation: Arbor AnnualDoSreport Jan 2018)  In particular, the memcache protocol showed itself to be a powerful protocol, with amplification sizes up to 51,200 times the requesting packet.(Citation: Cloudflare Memcrashed Feb 2018)
          Use DNS reflection by sending DNS queries with spoofed source IP to open DNS resolvers requesting ANY records
          Exploit NTP servers using monlist command to generate amplified traffic towards target
          Leverage memcached servers by sending spoofed UDP requests to exposed memcached instances
          Utilize SSDP protocol by sending spoofed M-SEARCH requests to IoT devices
          Exploit SNMP servers by sending GetBulk requests with spoofed source IP
          Use CharGEN protocol to generate amplified traffic by sending small requests to exposed CharGEN services
          Abuse CLDAP servers by sending search requests with spoofed source IP
          Exploit exposed TFTP servers by requesting large files with spoofed source IP
          Use RIPv1 routing protocol to generate amplified responses
