      Service Stop: Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services or processes can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018)(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster)   Adversaries may accomplish this by disabling individual services of high importance to an organization, such as <code>MSExchangeIS</code>, which will make Exchange content inaccessible.(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) In some cases, adversaries may stop or disable many or all services to render systems unusable.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018) Services or processes may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services or processes in order to conduct [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486) on the data stores of services like Exchange and SQL Server, or on virtual machines hosted on ESXi infrastructure.(Citation: SecureWorks WannaCry Analysis)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)
        ~Operating System Services: Targeting core Windows/Linux services using native service management commands and tools
        ~Application Services: Disrupting services of business-critical applications like databases, web servers, and email systems
        ~Security Services: Specifically targeting security-related services such as antivirus, EDR, and monitoring tools
        ~Cloud Service Disruption: Stopping cloud-based services and workloads through management APIs or interfaces
        ~Container Services: Targeting container orchestration services and individual container instances
        ~Virtualization Services: Disrupting hypervisor and virtual machine management services
        ~Network Services: Stopping critical network services like DNS, DHCP, and directory services
        ~Backup Services: Targeting backup software services and scheduled backup jobs
        ~Industrial Control System Services: Disrupting services specific to ICS/SCADA environments
        ~Cluster Services: Targeting high-availability cluster services and failover mechanisms
        ~Service Dependencies: Targeting upstream service dependencies to cause cascading failures
        ~Registry-Based Service Manipulation: Modifying service configurations in the registry to prevent starts
        ~Scheduled Task Services: Disrupting services that rely on scheduled task mechanisms
        ~Device Driver Services: Targeting device-related services to disable hardware functionality
        ~Remote Service Administration: Using remote service management tools and protocols
        ~Service Account Manipulation: Altering service account permissions or credentials
        ~Boot Services: Targeting services that initialize during system startup
        ~Temporary Service Suspension: Temporarily pausing services rather than stopping them completely
  MITRE-Mobile
    network-effects: The adversary is trying to intercept or manipulate network traffic to or from a device.  This category refers to network-based techniques that an adversary may be able to use to fulfill his or her objectives without access to the mobile device itself. These include techniques to intercept or manipulate network traffic to and from the mobile device.
      Eavesdrop on Insecure Network Communication: If network traffic between the mobile device and remote servers is unencrypted or is encrypted in an insecure manner, then an adversary positioned on the network can eavesdrop on communication.(Citation: mHealth)
        ~Man-in-the-Middle on Public WiFi: Adversaries may position themselves between mobile devices and WiFi access points to intercept unencrypted or weakly encrypted communications in public locations
        ~SSL/TLS Downgrade Attacks: Adversaries may force mobile devices to use older, vulnerable versions of SSL/TLS protocols to enable interception of supposedly secure communications
        ~Cellular Network Interception: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in cellular network protocols to intercept unencrypted or weakly encrypted mobile communications
        ~Bluetooth Sniffing: Adversaries may capture and analyze unencrypted Bluetooth communications between mobile devices and peripherals
        ~DNS Traffic Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor unencrypted DNS queries to gather information about mobile device communications and user behavior
        ~Application Layer Protocol Interception: Adversaries may target insecure application layer protocols used by mobile apps to intercept sensitive data
        ~VPN Connection Monitoring: Adversaries may intercept unencrypted or improperly configured VPN connections from mobile devices
        ~IoT Protocol Interception: Adversaries may target insecure IoT communication protocols used between mobile devices and IoT devices
      Downgrade to Insecure Protocols: An adversary could cause the mobile device to use less secure protocols, for example by jamming frequencies used by newer protocols such as LTE and only allowing older protocols such as GSM to communicate(Citation: NIST-SP800187). Use of less secure protocols may make communication easier to eavesdrop upon or manipulate.
        ~GSM Fallback Attack: Force devices to fall back to GSM by jamming or interfering with LTE/3G frequencies
        ~Protocol Negotiation Manipulation: Intercept and modify protocol negotiation messages to force less secure versions
        ~IMSI Catcher Operation: Deploy fake base stations that only support legacy protocols to force downgrades
        ~SSL/TLS Version Downgrade: Force mobile apps to use older, vulnerable SSL/TLS versions
        ~Wi-Fi Protocol Downgrade: Force devices to connect using older, less secure Wi-Fi protocols like WEP
        ~Bluetooth Version Downgrade: Exploit Bluetooth protocol negotiation to force older, less secure versions
        ~VoLTE to Circuit-Switched Fallback: Force voice calls to fall back to less secure circuit-switched networks
        ~DNS Protocol Downgrade: Force DNS queries to use unencrypted protocols instead of DNS-over-HTTPS/TLS
      Exploit SS7 to Track Device Location: An adversary could exploit signaling system vulnerabilities to track the location of mobile devices. (Citation: Engel-SS7) (Citation: Engel-SS7-2008) (Citation: 3GPP-Security) (Citation: Positive-SS7) (Citation: CSRIC5-WG10-FinalReport)
        ~SendRoutingInfoForSM Abuse: Exploiting the MAP SendRoutingInfoForSM operation to query HLR for device location information
        ~AnyTimeInterrogation Exploitation: Using ATI messages to directly request location information from the network
        ~provideSublocationInfo Manipulation: Abusing location update procedures to track device movements
        ~UpdateLocation Spoofing: Impersonating legitimate network elements to receive location updates
        ~CAMEL Protocol Abuse: Exploiting CAMEL services to gather location data
        ~PSI Query Exploitation: Abusing Provide Subscriber Info queries to gather location data
        ~Check IMEI Abuse: Exploiting equipment identity checks to determine device location
        ~Inter-carrier Routing Manipulation: Exploiting cross-carrier SS7 connections to track devices across networks
        ~Forward-To-Number Abuse: Using call forwarding related messages to determine subscriber location
        ~MAP InsertSubscriberData Exploitation: Manipulating subscriber data messages to enable tracking
      Rogue Cellular Base Station: An adversary could set up a rogue cellular base station and then use it to eavesdrop on or manipulate cellular device communication. A compromised cellular femtocell could be used to carry out this technique(Citation: Computerworld-Femtocell).
        ~IMSI Catcher Deployment: Using fake base stations specifically designed to capture International Mobile Subscriber Identity numbers from nearby devices
        ~Downgrade Attack: Forcing devices to connect using older, less secure cellular protocols (like 2G) by masquerading as the only available network
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Interception: Positioning rogue base station between target devices and legitimate cellular networks to intercept communications
        ~Femtocell Compromise: Exploiting legitimate femtocells by modifying their firmware or configuration to create a rogue access point
        ~Signal Jamming and Replacement: Jamming legitimate cellular signals while providing a stronger rogue signal to force connections
        ~Selective Service Blocking: Configuring rogue base station to selectively block or degrade service for specific devices or subscribers
        ~Location Tracking: Using multiple synchronized rogue base stations to triangulate and track target device locations
        ~Authentication Bypass: Exploiting cellular authentication protocols to bypass security mechanisms and force device connections
        ~Network Configuration Manipulation: Modifying network parameters to affect device behavior and connection preferences
        ~Cellular Broadcast Abuse: Exploiting cellular broadcast channels to send malicious or spoofed messages to connected devices
      Rogue Wi-Fi Access Points: An adversary could set up unauthorized Wi-Fi access points or compromise existing access points and, if the device connects to them, carry out network-based attacks such as eavesdropping on or modifying network communication(Citation: NIST-SP800153)(Citation: Kaspersky-DarkHotel).
        ~Evil Twin AP: Creating a replica of legitimate access point with same SSID and stronger signal to force devices to connect
        ~Captive Portal Spoofing: Setting up fake login pages on rogue APs to harvest credentials
        ~KARMA Attack: Exploiting probe requests to impersonate networks devices previously connected to
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Configuration: Setting up rogue AP with network position to intercept and relay traffic
        ~DNS Manipulation: Modifying DNS responses through rogue AP to redirect traffic
        ~SSL Stripping: Downgrading HTTPS connections to HTTP through rogue AP
        ~Rogue AP Persistence: Maintaining long-term presence by compromising existing infrastructure
        ~Mobile Hotspot Impersonation: Creating rogue APs that mimic legitimate mobile hotspot names
        ~Public WiFi Masquerading: Setting up rogue APs in public locations with common free WiFi names
        ~Corporate SSID Spoofing: Specifically targeting corporate devices by cloning enterprise network names
        ~WPA3 Downgrade: Forcing devices to connect using weaker WPA2 or WPA protocols
        ~Beacon Flood Attack: Broadcasting multiple SSIDs to overwhelm device scanning and force reconnection
        ~Client Blacklisting: Blocking legitimate AP connections to force connection to rogue AP
        ~Preferred Network Abuse: Exploiting devices' preferred network lists to force connections
        ~Hidden SSID Spoofing: Impersonating hidden enterprise networks to target specific organizations
        ~Band Switching Attack: Forcing devices to switch between 2.4GHz and 5GHz to preferred malicious AP
        ~Geofenced Targeting: Deploying rogue APs in specific locations known to be frequented by targets
        ~Signal Strength Manipulation: Using directional antennas to provide stronger signals than legitimate APs
        ~Auto-Connect Exploitation: Abusing devices' automatic connection features to force connections
        ~Network Bridge Hijacking: Compromising legitimate network bridges to insert rogue AP functionality
        ~Rogue RADIUS Server: Deploying fake authentication servers for enterprise wireless networks
        ~Zero Handshake Exploitation: Bypassing normal wireless authentication processes
        ~Multi-Channel AP Operation: Operating rogue AP across multiple channels to increase success rate
        ~Selective Client Targeting: Configuring rogue AP to only respond to specific device MAC addresses
        ~Roaming Protocol Abuse: Exploiting wireless roaming protocols to hijack connections
        ~AP Fingerprint Cloning: Precisely replicating all characteristics of legitimate access points
        ~Split-Tunnel Hijacking: Exploiting split-tunnel VPN configurations through rogue APs
        ~MDM Bypass: Targeting mobile device management wireless security controls
        ~Wireless IDS Evasion: Using timing and protocol variations to avoid detection
        ~Cellular-to-WiFi Forcing: Exploiting automatic cellular-to-WiFi switching features
      Manipulate Device Communication: If network traffic between the mobile device and a remote server is not securely protected, then an attacker positioned on the network may be able to manipulate network communication without being detected. For example, FireEye researchers found in 2014 that 68% of the top 1,000 free applications in the Google Play Store had at least one Transport Layer Security (TLS) implementation vulnerability potentially opening the applications' network traffic to adversary-in-the-middle attacks (Citation: FireEye-SSL).
        ~SSL/TLS Downgrade: Force device to use weaker or broken SSL/TLS versions allowing easier interception
        ~Certificate Spoofing: Present fake certificates to intercept encrypted communications
        ~DNS Hijacking: Redirect device traffic by manipulating DNS responses
        ~Proxy Manipulation: Insert malicious proxy settings to route traffic through attacker-controlled servers
        ~Protocol Downgrade: Force communications to fall back to less secure protocol versions
        ~WiFi SSID Spoofing: Create fake access points mimicking trusted networks
        ~API Response Manipulation: Modify server responses to inject malicious content
        ~Session Token Manipulation: Intercept and modify session tokens to hijack communications
        ~Traffic Injection: Insert malicious packets into legitimate communication streams
        ~Cache Poisoning: Manipulate cached DNS or other protocol data to redirect traffic
        ~Cellular Protocol Exploitation: Manipulate cellular network protocols (2G/3G/4G/5G) to intercept communications
        ~Content Length Manipulation: Modify packet length fields to cause parsing errors or buffer overflows
        ~Protocol Fragment Injection: Insert malicious fragments into segmented network traffic
        ~Update Server Spoofing: Impersonate legitimate update servers to deliver malicious content
        ~SMS Gateway Manipulation: Intercept and modify SMS communications through gateway vulnerabilities
        ~Push Notification Hijacking: Manipulate push notification traffic to inject malicious messages
        ~VPN Connection Tampering: Exploit VPN protocol vulnerabilities to redirect or intercept traffic
        ~Broadcast Traffic Manipulation: Modify broadcast messages to affect multiple devices
        ~Protocol State Manipulation: Exploit protocol state machines to force unintended behaviors
        ~Network Time Protocol (NTP) Spoofing: Manipulate device time synchronization to affect security mechanisms
        ~Mobile Carrier APN Manipulation: Exploit carrier Access Point Name settings to redirect traffic
        ~Cloud Sync Traffic Manipulation: Intercept and modify device-to-cloud synchronization data
        ~App-to-App Traffic Manipulation: Exploit inter-application communication channels
        ~OAuth Flow Manipulation: Intercept and modify OAuth authentication traffic
        ~Device Enrollment Protocol Manipulation: Tamper with MDM/enterprise enrollment traffic
        ~Bluetooth Traffic Manipulation: Intercept and modify Bluetooth communication channels
        ~Backup Traffic Manipulation: Intercept and modify device backup/restore communications
        ~Mobile Payment Protocol Manipulation: Tamper with mobile payment transaction traffic
        ~Enterprise SSO Traffic Manipulation: Manipulate single sign-on protocol communications
        ~Contact Sync Protocol Manipulation: Intercept and modify contact synchronization traffic
      Exploit SS7 to Redirect Phone Calls/SMS: An adversary could exploit signaling system vulnerabilities to redirect calls or text messages (SMS) to a phone number under the attacker's control. The adversary could then act as an adversary-in-the-middle to intercept or manipulate the communication. (Citation: Engel-SS7) (Citation: Engel-SS7-2008) (Citation: 3GPP-Security) (Citation: Positive-SS7) (Citation: CSRIC5-WG10-FinalReport) Interception of SMS messages could enable adversaries to obtain authentication codes used for multi-factor authentication(Citation: TheRegister-SS7).
        ~Location Update Spoofing: Exploit SS7 location update messages to trick the network into thinking the target device is in a different location, forcing traffic routing through attacker-controlled nodes
        ~HLR Query Manipulation: Manipulate Home Location Register queries to modify routing information for specific subscribers
        ~CAMEL Profile Exploitation: Exploit Customized Applications for Mobile Enhanced Logic profiles to redirect call handling
        ~MAP Forward-to-Number Attack: Use Mobile Application Part protocol to modify forwarding settings for target numbers
        ~SMS Home Routing Bypass: Exploit vulnerabilities in SMS home routing to intercept messages before they reach their intended destination
        ~ISUP Parameter Tampering: Manipulate ISDN User Part parameters to reroute call signaling
        ~MSC/VLR Impersonation: Impersonate Mobile Switching Center or Visitor Location Register to intercept communications
        ~Authentication Vector Theft: Exploit SS7 to steal authentication vectors from the target's home network
        ~SCCP Global Title Manipulation: Modify Signaling Connection Control Part addressing to redirect messages
        ~Mobility Management Exploitation: Manipulate mobility management messages to force target device registration through malicious nodes
    remote-service-effects: The adversary is trying to control or monitor the device using remote services.  This category refers to techniques involving remote services, such as vendor-provided cloud services (e.g. Google Drive, Google Find My Device, or Apple iCloud), or enterprise mobility management (EMM)/mobile device management (MDM) services that an adversary may be able to use to fulfill his or her objectives without access to the mobile device itself.
      Obtain Device Cloud Backups: An adversary who is able to obtain unauthorized access to or misuse authorized access to cloud backup services (e.g. Google's Android backup service or Apple's iCloud) could use that access to obtain sensitive data stored in device backups. For example, the Elcomsoft Phone Breaker product advertises the ability to retrieve iOS backup data from Apple's iCloud (Citation: Elcomsoft-EPPB). Elcomsoft also describes (Citation: Elcomsoft-WhatsApp) obtaining WhatsApp communication histories from backups stored in iCloud.
        ~Credential Stuffing: Using previously compromised credentials to attempt access to cloud backup services
        ~OAuth Token Theft: Stealing or manipulating OAuth tokens to gain unauthorized access to cloud backup services
        ~Social Engineering for Access: Tricking users or support staff into granting access to cloud backup accounts
        ~MFA Bypass: Exploiting weaknesses in multi-factor authentication to access cloud backup services
        ~Session Hijacking: Intercepting and exploiting active backup service sessions
        ~API Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in cloud backup service APIs
        ~Backup Encryption Bypass: Circumventing encryption mechanisms protecting cloud backups
        ~Service Configuration Abuse: Exploiting misconfigured backup service settings
        ~Recovery Method Abuse: Misusing account recovery mechanisms to gain access to backups
        ~Third-Party App Access: Leveraging compromised third-party applications with backup service access
        ~Legal Authority Abuse: Misusing legal requests or warrants to compel cloud providers to provide backup access
        ~Cloud Provider Employee Impersonation: Posing as cloud service provider staff to gain administrative access
        ~Backup Sync Interception: Intercepting backup data during synchronization between device and cloud
        ~Backup Restoration Manipulation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in the backup restoration process
        ~Cloud Infrastructure Attack: Compromising the underlying cloud infrastructure hosting backups
        ~Backup Migration Exploitation: Attacking backup data during migration between services or regions
        ~Device Enrollment Abuse: Exploiting device enrollment processes to gain backup access
        ~Vendor Portal Compromise: Attacking vendor management portals that control backup services
        ~Automated Backup Trigger Abuse: Exploiting automatic backup scheduling mechanisms
        ~Cross-Device Backup Poisoning: Manipulating cross-device backup sharing features
        ~Backup Metadata Exploitation: Using metadata analysis to target specific backup data
        ~Retention Policy Manipulation: Exploiting backup retention and archival policies
        ~Differential Backup Attack: Targeting incremental or differential backup mechanisms
        ~Legacy Backup Format Exploitation: Attacking outdated backup format vulnerabilities
      Remotely Track Device Without Authorization: An adversary who is able to obtain unauthorized access to or misuse authorized access to cloud services (e.g. Google's Android Device Manager or Apple iCloud's Find my iPhone) or to an enterprise mobility management (EMM) / mobile device management (MDM) server console could use that access to track mobile devices.(Citation: Krebs-Location)
        ~Cloud Device Location Services: Abuse of consumer cloud services like Find My Device/iPhone to track device location without device owner's knowledge
        ~EMM/MDM Console Abuse: Unauthorized use of enterprise device management systems to track managed devices
        ~Account Takeover: Compromising cloud service accounts through credential theft or social engineering to access device tracking features
        ~Authorized Access Misuse: Legitimate users (like IT admins) exceeding their authority to track devices for non-business purposes
        ~Third-Party Tracking Apps: Exploitation of third-party device tracking applications or services that have device location access
        ~Network Service Provider Abuse: Unauthorized access to or manipulation of cellular network provider systems to track device location
        ~Device Backup Service Exploitation: Abuse of cloud backup services to extract location history and tracking information
        ~Remote Configuration Manipulation: Unauthorized modification of device settings to enable tracking features
        ~Social Engineering for Access: Using social engineering to gain access to tracking capabilities through help desk or support channels
        ~Vendor Portal Compromise: Exploitation of device manufacturer or service provider portals that provide device tracking capabilities
      Remotely Wipe Data Without Authorization: An adversary who is able to obtain unauthorized access to or misuse authorized access to cloud services (e.g. Google's Android Device Manager or Apple iCloud's Find my iPhone) or to an EMM console could use that access to wipe enrolled devices (Citation: Honan-Hacking).
        ~Cloud Service Account Compromise: Adversary compromises credentials for cloud service accounts (like iCloud or Google Account) to access remote wipe functionality
        ~EMM Console Abuse: Adversary exploits vulnerabilities or misconfigurations in EMM/MDM console to initiate unauthorized device wipes
        ~Social Engineering for Account Access: Adversary uses social engineering to gain access to accounts with remote wipe capabilities
        ~API Token Theft: Adversary steals or compromises API tokens used for device management services
        ~Insider Threat Misuse: Authorized administrator abuses legitimate access to EMM/MDM systems to maliciously wipe devices
        ~Session Hijacking: Adversary hijacks active sessions of cloud service or EMM console administrators
        ~Third-Party Integration Exploitation: Adversary exploits third-party services integrated with device management platforms
        ~Password Reset Abuse: Adversary manipulates password reset mechanisms to gain control of accounts with remote wipe capabilities
    initial-access: The adversary is trying to get into your device.  The initial access tactic represents the vectors adversaries use to gain an initial foothold onto a mobile device.
      Deliver Malicious App via Other Means: Malicious applications are a common attack vector used by adversaries to gain a presence on mobile devices. This technique describes installing a malicious application on targeted mobile devices without involving an authorized app store (e.g., Google Play Store or Apple App Store). Adversaries may wish to avoid placing malicious applications in an authorized app store due to increased potential risk of detection or other reasons. However, mobile devices often are configured to allow application installation only from an authorized app store which would prevent this technique from working.  Delivery methods for the malicious application include:  * [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/001) - Including the mobile app package as an attachment to an email message. * [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002) - Including a link to the mobile app package within an email, text message (e.g. SMS, iMessage, Hangouts, WhatsApp, etc.), web site, QR code, or other means. * Third-Party App Store - Installed from a third-party app store (as opposed to an authorized app store that the device implicitly trusts as part of its default behavior), which may not apply the same level of scrutiny to apps as applied by an authorized app store.(Citation: IBTimes-ThirdParty)(Citation: TrendMicro-RootingMalware)(Citation: TrendMicro-FlappyBird)  Some Android malware comes with functionality to install additional applications, either automatically or when the adversary instructs it to.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)
        ~Third-Party App Store Distribution: Adversaries distribute malicious apps through unofficial app stores that may have less stringent security controls
        ~Direct APK/IPA Download: Adversaries host malicious app packages on their controlled servers for direct download
        ~USB Side-Loading: Adversaries use physical access or social engineering to install malicious apps via USB connection
        ~Fake App Update: Adversaries trick users into installing malicious apps by masquerading them as legitimate app updates
        ~Social Media Distribution: Adversaries use social media platforms to distribute links to malicious apps
        ~Compromised Developer Account: Adversaries compromise legitimate developer accounts to distribute apps through alternative channels
        ~Peer-to-Peer Network Distribution: Adversaries distribute malicious apps through P2P file-sharing networks
        ~Instant Messaging Distribution: Adversaries use messaging apps to distribute malicious app packages
        ~Drive-by Download: Adversaries exploit browser vulnerabilities to trigger automatic downloads of malicious apps
        ~QR Code Distribution: Adversaries use QR codes placed in physical locations or digital media to direct users to malicious app downloads
        ~Enterprise MDM Abuse: Adversaries compromise or abuse Mobile Device Management systems to distribute malicious apps to enterprise devices
        ~App Bundling: Adversaries bundle malicious apps with legitimate software packages or utilities
        ~Near Field Communication (NFC): Adversaries use NFC tags to initiate malicious app downloads when devices are in proximity
        ~SMS/MMS Distribution: Adversaries use SMS or MMS messages to distribute malicious app links or packages
        ~Bluetooth Distribution: Adversaries use Bluetooth file transfer to deliver malicious apps to nearby devices
        ~Custom App Store: Adversaries create fake custom app stores masquerading as legitimate enterprise or specialized app repositories
        ~Modified Backup Restoration: Adversaries modify device backups to include malicious apps that get installed during restoration
        ~App Cloning: Adversaries use app cloning tools to create and distribute modified versions of legitimate apps
      Deliver Malicious App via Authorized App Store: Malicious applications are a common attack vector used by adversaries to gain a presence on mobile devices. Mobile devices often are configured to allow application installation only from an authorized app store (e.g., Google Play Store or Apple App Store). An adversary may seek to place a malicious application in an authorized app store, enabling the application to be installed onto targeted devices.  App stores typically require developer registration and use vetting techniques to identify malicious applications. Adversaries may use these techniques against app store defenses:  * [Download New Code at Runtime](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1407) * [Obfuscated Files or Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1406)  Adversaries may also seek to evade vetting by placing code in a malicious application to detect whether it is running in an app analysis environment and, if so, avoid performing malicious actions while under analysis. (Citation: Petsas) (Citation: Oberheide-Bouncer) (Citation: Percoco-Bouncer) (Citation: Wang)  Adversaries may also use fake identities, payment cards, etc., to create developer accounts to publish malicious applications to app stores. (Citation: Oberheide-Bouncer)  Adversaries may also use control of a target's Google account to use the Google Play Store's remote installation capability to install apps onto the Android devices associated with the Google account. (Citation: Oberheide-RemoteInstall) (Citation: Konoth) (Only applications that are available for download through the Google Play Store can be remotely installed using this technique.)
        ~Anti-Analysis Detection: Implementing code to detect and behave differently in analysis environments to evade app store security checks
        ~Fake Developer Accounts: Creating fraudulent developer accounts using stolen or fake identities to publish malicious apps
        ~Remote Installation Abuse: Exploiting remote installation features of app stores after compromising user accounts
        ~Malicious Code Injection at Runtime: Publishing seemingly benign apps that download and execute malicious code after installation
        ~Component Masquerading: Disguising malicious functionality as legitimate app components to bypass security reviews
        ~Update-Based Exploitation: Publishing legitimate apps initially, then introducing malicious functionality through updates
        ~Library Manipulation: Including modified versions of legitimate libraries that contain hidden malicious functionality
        ~Permission Abuse: Requesting legitimate permissions but using them for malicious purposes
        ~Cross-Store Publishing: Publishing across multiple authorized stores to increase chances of successful deployment
        ~Repackaged App Distribution: Modifying legitimate apps with malicious code and redistributing through official channels
        ~App Name Spoofing: Using names/branding similar to legitimate apps to trick users into installing malicious versions
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Compromising legitimate developer accounts or build systems to distribute malicious versions
        ~Region-Specific Publishing: Targeting specific geographical regions to avoid detection by limiting exposure
        ~Time-Based Deployment: Controlling malicious behavior activation based on time delays after publication
        ~Policy Loophole Exploitation: Exploiting specific app store policy gaps or inconsistencies
        ~Beta Testing Abuse: Exploiting beta testing programs to distribute malicious apps to initial targets
        ~In-App Purchase Exploitation: Using legitimate payment systems to trigger malicious functionality
        ~Review Manipulation: Using fake reviews and ratings to increase app credibility and installation rates
        ~Family Account Abuse: Exploiting family sharing features to spread malicious apps across linked accounts
      Lockscreen Bypass: An adversary with physical access to a mobile device may seek to bypass the device’s lockscreen. Several methods exist to accomplish this, including:  * Biometric spoofing: If biometric authentication is used, an adversary could attempt to spoof a mobile device’s biometric authentication mechanism. Both iOS and Android partly mitigate this attack by requiring the device’s passcode rather than biometrics to unlock the device after every device restart, and after a set or random amount of time.(Citation: SRLabs-Fingerprint)(Citation: TheSun-FaceID) * Unlock code bypass: An adversary could attempt to brute-force or otherwise guess the lockscreen passcode (typically a PIN or password), including physically observing (“shoulder surfing”) the device owner’s use of the lockscreen passcode. Mobile OS vendors partly mitigate this by implementing incremental backoff timers after a set number of failed unlock attempts, as well as a configurable full device wipe after several failed unlock attempts. * Vulnerability exploit: Techniques have been periodically demonstrated that exploit mobile devices to bypass the lockscreen. The vulnerabilities are generally patched by the device or OS vendor once disclosed.(Citation: Wired-AndroidBypass)(Citation: Kaspersky-iOSBypass)
        ~Biometric Spoofing: Adversaries may attempt to bypass biometric authentication by creating artificial fingerprints, face models, or other biometric data to fool the device's sensors
        ~PIN/Pattern Observation: Adversaries may observe and record the device owner entering their unlock code through direct observation or camera surveillance
        ~Brute Force Attack: Adversaries may systematically attempt different combinations of PINs or patterns to unlock the device
        ~Cold Boot Attack: Adversaries may attempt to recover encryption keys from memory by quickly cooling the device and transferring its memory contents
        ~USB Debugging Exploit: Adversaries may leverage enabled USB debugging features to bypass the lockscreen through ADB or similar tools
        ~Emergency Call Interface Exploit: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in emergency call interfaces that remain accessible while the device is locked
        ~Screen Overlay Attack: Adversaries may use screen overlay techniques to capture unlock credentials when the user attempts to unlock the device
        ~Factory Reset Bypass: Adversaries may attempt to bypass factory reset protection mechanisms to gain access to a locked device
      Phishing: Adversaries may send malicious content to users in order to gain access to their mobile devices. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Adversaries can conduct both non-targeted phishing, such as in mass malware spam campaigns, as well as more targeted phishing tailored for a specific individual, company, or industry, known as “spearphishing”.  Phishing often involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a trusted source, as well as evasion techniques, such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages.  Mobile phishing may take various forms. For example, adversaries may send emails containing malicious attachments or links, typically to deliver and then execute malicious code on victim devices. Phishing may also be conducted via third-party services, like social media platforms.    Mobile devices are a particularly attractive target for adversaries executing phishing campaigns.  Due to their smaller form factor than traditional desktop endpoints, users may not be able to notice minor differences between genuine and phishing websites. Further, mobile devices have additional sensors and radios that allow adversaries to execute phishing attempts over several different vectors, such as:   - SMS messages: Adversaries may send SMS messages (known as “smishing”) from compromised devices to potential targets to convince the target to, for example, install malware, navigate to a specific website, or enable certain insecure configurations on their device. - Quick Response (QR) Codes: Adversaries may use QR codes (known as “quishing”) to redirect users to a phishing website. For example, an adversary could replace a legitimate public QR Code with one that leads to a different destination, such as a phishing website. A malicious QR code could also be delivered via other means, such as SMS or email. In the latter case, an adversary could utilize a malicious QR code in an email to pivot from the user’s desktop computer to their mobile device. - Phone Calls: Adversaries may call victims (known as “vishing”) to persuade them to perform an action, such as providing login credentials or navigating to a malicious website. This could also be used as a technique to perform the initial access on a mobile device, but then pivot to a computer/other network by having the victim perform an action on a desktop computer.
        ~SMS-based Phishing (Smishing): Adversaries send deceptive SMS messages containing malicious links or instructions to trick users into compromising their mobile devices
        ~QR Code Phishing (Quishing): Adversaries leverage malicious or modified QR codes to direct users to phishing websites or trigger malicious actions
        ~Voice Phishing (Vishing): Adversaries use phone calls to socially engineer victims into performing harmful actions on their mobile devices
        ~Email-based Mobile Phishing: Adversaries send specially crafted emails exploiting mobile email clients' limitations and UI constraints
        ~Social Media Platform Phishing: Adversaries leverage social media apps and messages to deliver phishing content
        ~In-App Message Phishing: Adversaries exploit messaging apps and their notification systems to deliver phishing content
        ~Mobile Browser-Specific Phishing: Adversaries create websites specifically designed to exploit mobile browser limitations and UI differences
        ~Cross-Platform Phishing: Adversaries design phishing campaigns that coordinate across mobile and desktop platforms to increase effectiveness
        ~Mobile App Store Phishing: Adversaries create fake app store pages or ads that mimic legitimate ones to distribute malicious apps
        ~Push Notification Phishing: Adversaries abuse push notification systems to deliver phishing content or trigger user actions
        ~NFC-based Phishing: Adversaries use malicious NFC tags in physical locations to trigger phishing attacks when users scan them
        ~Bluetooth Phishing: Adversaries exploit Bluetooth connectivity to deliver phishing content through nearby device notifications or pairing requests
        ~Mobile Game Phishing: Adversaries use in-game messages and rewards in mobile games to deliver phishing content
        ~Mobile Payment Phishing: Adversaries create fake payment confirmations or requests through mobile payment apps or SMS
        ~Location-based Phishing: Adversaries deliver contextual phishing attacks based on user's physical location or proximity to specific places
        ~Mobile Calendar Phishing: Adversaries send malicious calendar invites that appear legitimate to deliver phishing content
        ~Mobile Keyboard Phishing: Adversaries use malicious third-party keyboard apps to capture credentials during phishing attacks
        ~Digital Assistant Phishing: Adversaries exploit voice assistants and their integration with mobile devices to deliver phishing content
        ~Mobile Authentication App Phishing: Adversaries create fake authentication app notifications or requests to steal credentials
        ~Mobile QR Scanner App Phishing: Adversaries create malicious QR scanner apps that manipulate scanned content to deliver phishing attacks
      Install Insecure or Malicious Configuration: An adversary could attempt to install insecure or malicious configuration settings on the mobile device, through means such as phishing emails or text messages either directly containing the configuration settings as an attachment, or containing a web link to the configuration settings. The device user may be tricked into installing the configuration settings through social engineering techniques (Citation: Symantec-iOSProfile).  For example, an unwanted Certification Authority (CA) certificate could be placed in the device's trusted certificate store, increasing the device's susceptibility to adversary-in-the-middle network attacks seeking to eavesdrop on or manipulate the device's network communication ([Eavesdrop on Insecure Network Communication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1439) and [Manipulate Device Communication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1463)).  On iOS, malicious Configuration Profiles could contain unwanted Certification Authority (CA) certificates or other insecure settings such as unwanted proxy server or VPN settings to route the device's network traffic through an adversary's system. The device could also potentially be enrolled into a malicious Mobile Device Management (MDM) system (Citation: Talos-MDM).
        ~Malicious CA Certificate Installation: Installing unauthorized Certificate Authority certificates to enable man-in-the-middle attacks
        ~Malicious MDM Enrollment: Tricking users into enrolling devices in unauthorized Mobile Device Management systems
        ~Malicious VPN Configuration: Installing rogue VPN profiles to redirect traffic through attacker-controlled servers
        ~Malicious Proxy Settings: Modifying proxy configurations to route traffic through malicious intermediaries
        ~Email Configuration Manipulation: Modifying email settings to enable message interception or manipulation
        ~Device Restriction Bypass: Installing configurations that disable security features or restrictions
        ~Enterprise App Trust Configuration: Adding unauthorized enterprise developer certificates
        ~System-level Settings Modification: Changing critical system settings through configuration profiles
        ~Network Configuration Manipulation: Modifying WiFi or cellular network settings for traffic interception
        ~App Installation Policy Modification: Altering policies controlling which apps can be installed
        ~Authentication Settings Manipulation: Modifying device authentication requirements or trusted identities
        ~Automated Configuration Deployment: Using automated tools or scripts to deploy malicious configurations
        ~Security Policy Override: Installing configurations that override built-in security policies
        ~Backup Configuration Manipulation: Modifying backup settings to expose data
        ~Device Monitoring Configuration: Installing configurations that enable unauthorized monitoring
        ~App Permission Policy Modification: Altering default app permissions through configuration
        ~Device Encryption Configuration: Modifying encryption settings to weaken security
        ~Remote Access Configuration: Installing configurations that enable unauthorized remote access
      Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.  Supply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:  * Manipulation of development tools * Manipulation of a development environment * Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private) * Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies * Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms * Compromised/infected system images * Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions * Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors * Shipment interdiction  While supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, attackers looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels. Targeting may be specific to a desired victim set or malicious software may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.  Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency, specifically with the widespread usage of third-party advertising libraries.(Citation: Grace-Advertisement)(Citation: NowSecure-RemoteCode)
        Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools: Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Applications often depend on external software to function properly. Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Grace-Advertisement)
          Inject malicious code into popular mobile advertising SDKs to affect multiple apps using the library
          Compromise mobile app build tools to insert backdoors during compilation
          Upload malicious versions of common mobile dependencies to package repositories
          Modify mobile development IDE plugins to include malicious functionality
          Insert malicious code into shared UI component libraries used by mobile apps
          Compromise continuous integration/deployment pipelines for mobile app builds
          Manipulate mobile analytics SDKs to collect unauthorized data
          Insert backdoors into mobile payment processing libraries
          Modify mobile game engine dependencies to include malicious code
          Compromise mobile app testing frameworks to inject malicious payloads
          Compromise cloud-based mobile app building services to inject malicious code during remote builds
          Insert malicious code into cross-platform frameworks like React Native or Flutter dependencies
          Modify mobile app security scanning tools to ignore specific malicious patterns
          Compromise mobile app localization libraries to execute region-specific attacks
          Insert backdoors into mobile database ORM libraries
          Manipulate mobile app crash reporting tools to exfiltrate sensitive data
          Compromise mobile authentication library dependencies
          Modify mobile app networking libraries to intercept traffic
          Insert malicious code into mobile app template generators
          Compromise mobile app optimization libraries used during build process
        Compromise Software Supply Chain: Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Supply chain compromise of software can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update/distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.
          Inject malicious code into legitimate mobile apps through compromised development tools like Android Studio or Xcode
          Upload trojanized versions of popular apps to third-party app stores
          Compromise mobile app update servers to distribute modified app versions
          Insert malicious code into widely-used mobile advertising SDKs
          Modify mobile app source code repositories during the CI/CD pipeline
          Manipulate shared development certificates and provisioning profiles
          Compromise build systems to inject malware during app compilation
          Replace legitimate app binaries with modified versions before app store submission
          Inject malicious code into third-party payment or analytics SDKs
          Compromise developer accounts to upload modified versions of legitimate apps
          Compromise enterprise mobile device management (MDM) systems to distribute modified apps
          Manipulate React Native or Flutter framework dependencies during app building
          Exploit iOS TestFlight beta distribution to spread compromised apps
          Modify Android Gradle plugin or dependencies during build process
          Compromise mobile app code signing certificates and keys
          Insert malicious code into mobile game engine plugins (Unity, Unreal)
          Manipulate shared Cocoapods or npm packages used in mobile apps
          Compromise enterprise app store infrastructure
          Modify mobile app obfuscation tools to insert backdoors
          Tamper with mobile app automated testing frameworks to inject malicious code
          Compromise cloud-based mobile app building services (e.g., PhoneGap Build)
          Manipulate mobile app protection/wrapping services used by enterprises
          Insert malicious code through compromised app localization services
          Compromise mobile app performance monitoring SDK distribution
          Manipulate mobile app crash reporting tools and services
          Exploit mobile app A/B testing framework distribution
          Compromise mobile app backup services to modify stored apps
          Manipulate mobile app store optimization tools to inject malicious code
          Insert backdoors through compromised mobile app security scanning services
          Compromise mobile app analytics platforms to distribute modified SDKs
          Compromise mobile app debugging tool distribution channels
          Manipulate in-app purchase SDK distribution systems
          Compromise mobile app CDN providers to serve modified resources
          Insert malicious code through compromised remote config services
          Manipulate mobile deep linking service SDKs
          Compromise mobile app profiling tool distributions
          Insert backdoors through compromised mobile payment gateway SDKs
          Manipulate mobile app feature flag service integrations
          Compromise mobile app authentication service SDKs
          Exploit compromised mobile app content management systems
        Compromise Hardware Supply Chain: Adversaries may manipulate hardware components in products prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. By modifying hardware or firmware in the supply chain, adversaries can insert a backdoor into consumer networks that may be difficult to detect and give the adversary a high degree of control over the system.
          Insert malicious chips or circuits into mobile device motherboards during manufacturing
          Modify baseband processors to include backdoor capabilities
          Replace genuine battery management circuits with compromised versions containing malware
          Tamper with cellular modem firmware during production
          Modify touch screen controller firmware to capture user input
          Install compromised secure element chips in mobile payment systems
          Replace authentic fingerprint sensor modules with malicious versions
          Intercept device shipments to install hardware keyloggers
          Modify camera module firmware to enable covert surveillance
          Install compromised GPS modules that can report false location data
          Modify WiFi/Bluetooth modules to enable unauthorized network access
          Replace genuine NFC controllers with compromised versions for payment interception
          Install compromised eMMC/UFS storage controllers with hidden partitions
          Modify voltage regulators to enable fault injection attacks
          Replace genuine RF amplifiers with versions capable of unauthorized transmission
          Tamper with hardware random number generators to weaken device encryption
          Modify USB controller firmware to enable data exfiltration
          Install compromised audio codecs for ambient recording
          Replace genuine temperature sensors to enable thermal attacks
          Modify power management ICs to enable side-channel attacks
      Masquerade as Legitimate Application: An adversary could distribute developed malware by masquerading the malware as a legitimate application. This can be done in two different ways: by embedding the malware in a legitimate application, or by pretending to be a legitimate application.  Embedding the malware in a legitimate application is done by downloading the application, disassembling it, adding the malicious code, and then re-assembling it.(Citation: Zhou) The app would appear to be the original app, but would contain additional malicious functionality. The adversary could then publish the malicious application to app stores or use another delivery method.  Pretending to be a legitimate application relies heavily on lack of scrutinization by the user. Typically, a malicious app pretending to be a legitimate one will have many similar details as the legitimate one, such as name, icon, and description.(Citation: Palo Alto HenBox)  Malicious applications may also masquerade as legitimate applications when requesting access to the accessibility service in order to appear as legitimate to the user, increasing the likelihood that the access will be granted.
        ~Repackaging Legitimate Apps: Adversaries modify legitimate applications by adding malicious code through decompilation, modification, and repackaging
        ~Clone Application Interface: Adversaries create applications that closely mimic the visual appearance and branding of legitimate apps
        ~Fake Store Publishing: Adversaries publish masqueraded malware through unofficial or compromised app stores
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Adversaries masquerade malware as legitimate accessibility services or apps requiring accessibility permissions
        ~SDK Impersonation: Adversaries disguise malware as legitimate software development kits or plugins
        ~System App Masquerading: Adversaries make their malware appear as system applications or utilities
        ~Popular App Impersonation: Adversaries create fake versions of trending or popular applications
        ~Banking App Spoofing: Adversaries create malware that mimics legitimate banking or financial applications
      Exploitation for Initial Access: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities to gain initial access to a mobile device.   This can be accomplished in a variety of ways. Vulnerabilities may be present in the applications, the services, the underlying operating system, or the kernel itself. Several well-known mobile device exploits exist, including FORCEDENTRY, StageFright, and BlueBorne. Furthermore, some exploits may be possible to exploit without any user interaction (i.e. zero-click exploits, see [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1658)), making them particularly dangerous. Mobile operating system vendors are typically very quick to patch such critical bugs, ensuring only a small window where they can be exploited.
        ~Zero-Click Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities that require no user interaction, often targeting messaging apps, cellular protocols, or background services
        ~Application Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in third-party or system applications to gain access to the device
        ~Operating System Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries target vulnerabilities in the mobile OS core components or system services
        ~Kernel Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in the mobile device's kernel to gain privileged access
        ~Bluetooth Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Bluetooth implementation or protocols to gain access
        ~Cellular Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in cellular protocols (2G/3G/4G/5G) or baseband processors
        ~WebKit Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in the WebKit browser engine to gain access through malicious web content
        ~File Format Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in how the device processes specific file formats
        ~Hardware Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries exploit hardware-level vulnerabilities in mobile device components
        ~Supply Chain Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities introduced through compromised device firmware or pre-installed software
        ~NFC Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Near Field Communication protocols or implementation to gain access when in close proximity
        ~Wi-Fi Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in Wi-Fi implementation, WPA protocols, or wireless drivers
        ~SMS/MMS Processing Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in how devices process text or multimedia messages
        ~Mobile Device Management Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in MDM solutions to gain access to managed devices
        ~SIM Card Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in SIM card implementation or communication protocols
        ~Mobile Radio Interface Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in radio interface layer (RIL) or modem firmware
        ~Bootloader Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in bootloader implementation to gain persistent access
        ~Secure Element Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in secure elements used for payment or authentication
        ~Mobile Peripheral Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in peripheral device drivers or connection protocols
        ~App Store Delivery Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in app store delivery mechanisms or verification processes
      Drive-By Compromise: Adversaries may gain access to a system through a user visiting a website over the normal course of browsing. With this technique, the user's web browser is typically targeted for exploitation, but adversaries may also use compromised websites for non-exploitation behavior such as acquiring an [Application Access Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/001).  Multiple ways of delivering exploit code to a browser exist, including:  * A legitimate website is compromised where adversaries have injected some form of malicious code such as JavaScript, iFrames, and cross-site scripting. * Malicious ads are paid for and served through legitimate ad providers. * Built-in web application interfaces are leveraged for the insertion of any other kind of object that can be used to display web content or contain a script that executes on the visiting client (e.g. forum posts, comments, and other user controllable web content).  Often the website used by an adversary is one visited by a specific community, such as government, a particular industry, or region, where the goal is to compromise a specific user or set of users based on a shared interest. This kind of targeted attack is referred to a strategic web compromise or watering hole attack. There are several known examples of this occurring.(Citation: Lookout-StealthMango)  Typical drive-by compromise process:  1. A user visits a website that is used to host the adversary controlled content. 2. Scripts automatically execute, typically searching versions of the browser and plugins for a potentially vulnerable version.      * The user may be required to assist in this process by enabling scripting or active website components and ignoring warning dialog boxes. 3. Upon finding a vulnerable version, exploit code is delivered to the browser. 4. If exploitation is successful, then it will give the adversary code execution on the user's system unless other protections are in place.     * In some cases a second visit to the website after the initial scan is required before exploit code is delivered.
        ~Malicious Ad Networks: Exploitation through compromised mobile advertising networks that deliver malicious code to mobile browsers
        ~Mobile Browser Vulnerabilities: Targeting specific vulnerabilities in mobile browsers like Safari on iOS or Chrome on Android
        ~Mobile App WebView Exploitation: Compromising WebView components within mobile apps that render web content
        ~Social Media Link Manipulation: Using shortened or obfuscated URLs on social media platforms to direct mobile users to malicious sites
        ~Mobile-Specific Watering Holes: Targeting mobile-optimized websites frequently visited by specific user groups
        ~Progressive Web App Abuse: Exploiting Progressive Web Apps to maintain persistence after initial compromise
        ~QR Code Redirection: Using QR codes to direct mobile users to malicious websites
        ~Mobile Browser Extension Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in mobile browser extensions or plugins
        ~Certificate Spoofing: Using fake or compromised certificates to make malicious sites appear legitimate on mobile browsers
        ~In-App Browser Hijacking: Compromising in-app browsers used by social media or messaging apps
        ~SMS/MMS Link Delivery: Using text messages to deliver malicious links targeting mobile browsers
        ~Mobile Network Operator Injection: Exploiting mobile carrier networks to inject malicious content into web traffic
        ~App Store Description Links: Embedding malicious links in app store descriptions or developer websites
        ~Cross-App URI Scheme Abuse: Exploiting custom URL schemes to trigger malicious web content loading
        ~Mobile Deep Link Manipulation: Abusing deep linking mechanisms to direct users to compromised web content
        ~AMP Cache Poisoning: Compromising Accelerated Mobile Pages caches to deliver malicious content
        ~Mobile Browser Sync Exploitation: Leveraging browser sync features to propagate attacks across devices
        ~Mobile Payment Gateway Injection: Compromising mobile payment processing web interfaces
      Exploit via Radio Interfaces: The mobile device may be targeted for exploitation through its interface to cellular networks or other radio interfaces.  ### Baseband Vulnerability Exploitation  A message sent over a radio interface (typically cellular, but potentially Bluetooth, GPS, NFC, Wi-Fi(Citation: ProjectZero-BroadcomWiFi) or other) to the mobile device could exploit a vulnerability in code running on the device(Citation: Register-BaseStation)(Citation: Weinmann-Baseband).  ### Malicious SMS Message  An SMS message could contain content designed to exploit vulnerabilities in the SMS parser on the receiving device(Citation: Forbes-iPhoneSMS). An SMS message could also contain a link to a web site containing malicious content designed to exploit the device web browser. Vulnerable SIM cards may be remotely exploited and reprogrammed via SMS messages(Citation: SRLabs-SIMCard).
        ~Cellular Baseband Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in cellular modem firmware or baseband processors through malformed cellular network messages
        ~Bluetooth Protocol Attacks: Targeting vulnerabilities in Bluetooth stack implementation or pairing mechanisms
        ~WiFi Frame Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in WiFi drivers or protocol implementations through malformed frames
        ~NFC Reader Vulnerabilities: Targeting vulnerabilities in NFC controller firmware or protocol stack
        ~SMS Parser Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SMS message parsing components
        ~SIM Card Remote Attack: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SIM card software or configuration via radio interfaces
        ~GPS Signal Spoofing: Exploiting vulnerabilities in GPS receiver firmware or signal processing
        ~Radio Interface Fuzzing: Systematically testing radio interfaces with malformed input to find vulnerabilities
      Replication Through Removable Media: Adversaries may move onto devices by exploiting or copying malware to devices connected via USB. In the case of Lateral Movement, adversaries may utilize the physical connection of a device to a compromised or malicious charging station or PC to bypass application store requirements and install malicious applications directly.(Citation: Lau-Mactans) In the case of Initial Access, adversaries may attempt to exploit the device via the connection to gain access to data stored on the device.(Citation: Krebs-JuiceJacking) Examples of this include:    * Exploiting insecure bootloaders in a Nexus 6 or 6P device over USB and gaining the ability to perform actions including intercepting phone calls, intercepting network traffic, and obtaining the device physical location.(Citation: IBM-NexusUSB)  * Exploiting weakly-enforced security boundaries in Android devices such as the Google Pixel 2 over USB.(Citation: GoogleProjectZero-OATmeal)  * Products from Cellebrite and Grayshift purportedly that can exploit some iOS devices using physical access to the data port to unlock the passcode.(Citation: Computerworld-iPhoneCracking)
        ~USB Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in USB protocol implementations to gain unauthorized access or execute malicious code
        ~Malicious Charging Stations: Adversaries use compromised charging stations to deliver malware or exploit vulnerabilities during device charging
        ~Bootloader Exploitation: Adversaries target insecure bootloaders to gain system-level access when devices are connected via USB
        ~Direct Application Installation: Adversaries bypass app store restrictions by installing malicious apps through USB connections
        ~Data Port Access: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in device data ports to extract data or bypass security measures
        ~Mobile Device Management Abuse: Adversaries leverage legitimate MDM protocols over USB to deploy malicious configurations
        ~Firmware Modification: Adversaries modify device firmware through physical access to implement persistent backdoors
        ~Recovery Mode Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in device recovery modes accessible via USB
      Application Versioning: An adversary may push an update to a previously benign application to add malicious code. This can be accomplished by pushing an initially benign, functional application to a trusted application store, such as the Google Play Store or the Apple App Store. This allows the adversary to establish a trusted userbase that may grant permissions to the application prior to the introduction of malicious code. Then, an application update could be pushed to introduce malicious code.(Citation: android_app_breaking_bad)  This technique could also be accomplished by compromising a developer’s account. This would allow an adversary to take advantage of an existing userbase without having to establish the userbase themselves.
        ~Developer Account Takeover: Adversaries compromise legitimate developer accounts through credential theft, social engineering, or exploiting account recovery processes to push malicious updates
        ~Gradual Malware Introduction: Adversaries incrementally introduce malicious functionality across multiple updates to avoid detection
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries compromise third-party libraries or dependencies used by the application to introduce malicious code
        ~Beta Channel Abuse: Adversaries use beta/testing channels to initially distribute malicious updates to a smaller audience
        ~Update Server Compromise: Adversaries compromise self-hosted update servers to distribute malicious updates outside official app stores
        ~SDK Injection: Adversaries introduce malicious code through seemingly legitimate SDK updates or additions
        ~Dynamic Code Loading: Adversaries use legitimate update mechanisms to download and execute malicious code after installation
        ~Permission Escalation Updates: Adversaries push updates that request additional permissions to enable malicious functionality
      SIM Card Swap: Adversaries may gain access to mobile devices through transfers or swaps from victims’ phone numbers to adversary-controlled SIM cards and mobile devices.(Citation: ATT SIM Swap Scams)(Citation: Verizon SIM Swapping)   The typical process is as follows:    1. Adversaries will first gather information about victims through [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1660), social engineering, data breaches, or other avenues.  2. Adversaries will then impersonate victims as they contact mobile carriers to request for the SIM swaps. For example, adversaries would provide victims’ name and address to mobile carriers; once authenticated, adversaries would request for victims’ phone numbers to be transferred to adversary-controlled SIM cards.   3. Once completed, victims will lose mobile data, such as text messages and phone calls, on their mobile devices. In turn, adversaries will receive mobile data that was intended for the victims.    Adversaries may use the intercepted SMS messages to log into online accounts that use SMS-based authentication. Specifically, adversaries may use SMS-based authentication to log into banking and/or cryptocurrency accounts, then transfer funds to adversary-controlled wallets.
        ~Social Engineering Call Center: Adversaries may manipulate mobile carrier call center employees through social engineering to authorize SIM swaps
        ~Insider Threat: Adversaries may collude with mobile carrier employees who have access to SIM card management systems
        ~Online Portal Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in carrier's online account management portals to initiate SIM swaps
        ~Identity Document Forgery: Adversaries may use forged identity documents to convince carriers to perform SIM swaps in physical stores
        ~Account Takeover: Adversaries may compromise victims' existing mobile carrier accounts to authorize SIM swaps
        ~Port-Out Fraud: Adversaries may transfer numbers to different carriers to bypass security measures at the original carrier
        ~Emergency Transfer Abuse: Adversaries may exploit emergency SIM replacement procedures meant for legitimate customers
        ~Bulk SIM Swap: Adversaries may perform multiple simultaneous SIM swaps to overwhelm security monitoring systems
    execution: The adversary is trying to run malicious code.  Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a mobile device. Techniques that run malicious code are often paired with techniques from all other tactics to achieve broader goals, like exploring a network or stealing data.
      Native API: Adversaries may use Android’s Native Development Kit (NDK) to write native functions that can achieve execution of binaries or functions. Like system calls on a traditional desktop operating system, native code achieves execution on a lower level than normal Android SDK calls.  The NDK allows developers to write native code in C or C++ that is compiled directly to machine code, avoiding all intermediate languages and steps in compilation that higher level languages, like Java, typically have. The Java Native Interface (JNI) is the component that allows Java functions in the Android app to call functions in a native library.(Citation: Google NDK Getting Started)  Adversaries may also choose to use native functions to execute malicious code since native actions are typically much more difficult to analyze than standard, non-native behaviors.(Citation: MITRE App Vetting Effectiveness)
        ~JNI Function Calls: Use of Java Native Interface to directly call native functions from Java code, potentially bypassing security controls
        ~Native Library Loading: Dynamic loading of malicious native libraries through System.loadLibrary() or similar methods
        ~Native Code Obfuscation: Implementation of obfuscated native code to hide malicious functionality from analysis
        ~Native Memory Manipulation: Direct manipulation of process memory using native code to modify app behavior
        ~Native System Call Interface: Direct system calls through native code to bypass Android API restrictions
        ~Native Code Encryption: Use of encrypted native code segments that are decrypted at runtime
        ~Native Code Injection: Injection of native code into other processes or memory spaces
        ~Native Binary Execution: Direct execution of compiled native binaries through native code
        ~Native IPC Communication: Use of native code for inter-process communication to evade monitoring
        ~Native Code Anti-Analysis: Implementation of anti-debugging and anti-analysis techniques in native code
        ~Native Hardware Access: Direct access to device hardware components through native code bypassing Android API restrictions
        ~Native Code Privilege Exploitation: Exploitation of privilege escalation vulnerabilities through native code
        ~Cross-Architecture Native Code: Implementation of native code for multiple CPU architectures to ensure broader device compatibility
        ~Native Shared Memory Access: Use of native code to access and manipulate shared memory segments
        ~Native Code Dynamic Generation: Runtime generation or modification of native code to evade detection
      Command-Line Interface: Adversaries may use built-in command-line interfaces to interact with the device and execute commands. Android provides a bash shell that can be interacted with over the Android Debug Bridge (ADB) or programmatically using Java’s `Runtime` package. On iOS, adversaries can interact with the underlying runtime shell if the device has been jailbroken.  If the device has been rooted or jailbroken, adversaries may locate and invoke a superuser binary to elevate their privileges and interact with the system as the root user. This dangerous level of permissions allows the adversary to run special commands and modify protected system files.
        ~ADB Shell Access: Adversaries may connect to Android devices through ADB to execute shell commands remotely, either over USB or wireless connections
        ~Terminal Emulator Apps: Adversaries may install and use terminal emulator applications to access the device's command line interface locally
        ~Runtime Command Execution: Adversaries may use programmatic methods like Java's Runtime.exec() or ProcessBuilder to execute shell commands
        ~Jailbroken iOS Shell: Adversaries may leverage Cydia or similar package managers on jailbroken iOS devices to access command line tools
        ~Superuser Command Execution: Adversaries may use su or sudo commands on rooted/jailbroken devices to execute commands with elevated privileges
        ~Background Service Shell: Adversaries may create persistent background services that maintain shell access to the device
        ~Remote Shell Backdoor: Adversaries may implement remote shell capabilities in malicious apps to maintain CLI access
      Scheduled Task/Job: Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. On Android and iOS, APIs and libraries exist to facilitate scheduling tasks to execute at a specified date, time, or interval.  On Android, the `WorkManager` API allows asynchronous tasks to be scheduled with the system. `WorkManager` was introduced to unify task scheduling on Android, using `JobScheduler`, `GcmNetworkManager`, and `AlarmManager` internally. `WorkManager` offers a lot of flexibility for scheduling, including periodically, one time, or constraint-based (e.g. only when the device is charging).(Citation: Android WorkManager)  On iOS, the `NSBackgroundActivityScheduler` API allows asynchronous tasks to be scheduled with the system. The tasks can be scheduled to be repeating or non-repeating, however, the system chooses when the tasks will be executed. The app can choose the interval for repeating tasks, or the delay between scheduling and execution for one-time tasks.(Citation: Apple NSBackgroundActivityScheduler)
        ~Android WorkManager Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Android's WorkManager API to schedule malicious tasks that persist across app restarts and system reboots, leveraging its unified scheduling capabilities.
        ~Android JobScheduler Exploitation: Adversaries may directly exploit Android's JobScheduler to schedule background jobs that execute malicious code under specific conditions or intervals.
        ~Android AlarmManager Misuse: Adversaries may misuse Android's AlarmManager to schedule precise or inexact alarms that trigger malicious code execution at specific times.
        ~iOS Background Task Framework Abuse: Adversaries may abuse iOS's background task framework to schedule and execute malicious code while the app is in the background.
        ~iOS Background Fetch Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit iOS's background fetch capability to periodically execute malicious code under the guise of content updates.
        ~Constraint-Based Task Scheduling: Adversaries may schedule tasks to execute only under specific device conditions (charging, idle, network available) to evade detection.
        ~Periodic Task Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple scheduled tasks together to maintain persistence and execute complex attack sequences.
        ~System Update Task Masquerading: Adversaries may disguise malicious scheduled tasks as system update or maintenance operations.
      Broadcast Receivers: An intent is a message passed between Android application or system components. Applications can register to receive broadcast intents at runtime, which are system-wide intents delivered to each app when certain events happen on the device, such as network changes or the user unlocking the screen. Malicious applications can then trigger certain actions within the app based on which broadcast intent was received.  Further, malicious applications can register for intents broadcasted by other applications in addition to the Android system itself. This allows the malware to respond based on actions in other applications. This behavior typically indicates a more intimate knowledge, or potentially the targeting of specific devices, users, or applications.  In Android 8 (API level 26), broadcast intent behavior was changed, limiting the implicit intents that applications can register for in the manifest. In most cases, applications that register through the manifest will no longer receive the broadcasts. Now, applications must register context-specific broadcast receivers while the user is actively using the app.(Citation: Android Changes to System Broadcasts)
        ~System Event Monitoring: Register receivers for system events like boot completion, network changes, or battery status to trigger malicious code
        ~Application Intent Interception: Monitor and intercept broadcast intents from specific target applications
        ~Dynamic Runtime Registration: Register broadcast receivers during runtime to evade static analysis and manifest restrictions
        ~Screen State Monitoring: Listen for screen on/off and unlock events to time malicious activities
        ~Network State Abuse: Monitor network connectivity changes to coordinate command and control communications
        ~SMS/Call Monitoring: Register for telephony-related broadcasts to intercept communications
        ~Package Installation Tracking: Monitor for app install/uninstall events to detect security tools
        ~Battery State Exploitation: Use battery state broadcasts to optimize malicious activities timing
        ~Time Zone Changes: Monitor timezone/time changes to coordinate timed attacks
        ~Storage Events: Listen for external storage mounting/unmounting to access sensitive data
        ~User Presence Detection: Monitor user presence and device idle state broadcasts to time malicious activities
        ~Bluetooth State Monitoring: Listen for Bluetooth connection events to track device proximity and potential targets
        ~Camera Status Tracking: Monitor camera usage broadcasts to perform surveillance activities
        ~System Update Events: Listen for system update notifications to adjust malware behavior
        ~Language Change Monitoring: Track system locale changes for targeted attacks
        ~Power Connection Events: Monitor power connection/disconnection for persistence
        ~Device Admin Events: Listen for device administrator changes to detect security controls
        ~Clipboard Monitoring: Register for clipboard change broadcasts to steal sensitive data
        ~Account Sync Events: Monitor account synchronization broadcasts for data theft
        ~Airplane Mode Changes: Track connectivity mode changes for evasion
        ~Permission Change Monitoring: Track runtime permission changes to detect security posture
        ~Inter-App Communication Chaining: Chain multiple broadcast receivers across apps for complex attacks
        ~Media Scanner Events: Monitor media scanning broadcasts to detect new files
        ~Background Restriction Events: Monitor changes in background execution policies
        ~Input Method Changes: Track keyboard and input method changes for keylogging
        ~USB Connection Events: Monitor USB connection states for data exfiltration
        ~Audio State Monitoring: Track audio state changes for environmental awareness
        ~Notification Events: Intercept notification broadcasts for information gathering
        ~Widget Update Events: Monitor home screen widget updates for persistence
        ~Debug State Changes: Track debugging state changes for anti-analysis
        ~Enterprise Policy Changes: Monitor enterprise policy broadcasts for organizational context
        ~Vendor Update Events: Track vendor-specific system update broadcasts
        ~Work Profile Events: Monitor work profile state changes in enterprise environments
        ~Task Scheduling Events: Listen for alarm and job scheduler broadcasts
        ~Package Verification Events: Monitor package verification state changes
        ~Device Encryption Events: Track device encryption state changes
        ~Credential Change Events: Monitor credential storage modifications
        ~VPN Connection Events: Track VPN state changes for network awareness
        ~Remote Control Events: Monitor remote control service broadcasts
        ~Backup State Events: Track backup service state changes
      Command and Scripting Interpreter: Adversaries may abuse command and script interpreters to execute commands, scripts, or binaries. These interfaces and languages provide ways of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many different platforms. Most systems come with some built-in command-line interface and scripting capabilities, for example, Android is a UNIX-like OS and includes a basic [Unix Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1623/001) that can be accessed via the Android Debug Bridge (ADB) or Java’s `Runtime` package.  Adversaries may abuse these technologies in various ways as a means of executing arbitrary commands. Commands and scripts can be embedded in [Initial Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0027) payloads delivered to victims as lure documents or as secondary payloads downloaded from an existing C2. Adversaries may also execute commands through interactive terminals/shells.
        Unix Shell: Adversaries may abuse Unix shell commands and scripts for execution. Unix shells are the underlying command prompts on Android and iOS devices. Unix shells can control every aspect of a system, with certain commands requiring elevated privileges that are only accessible if the device has been rooted or jailbroken.   Unix shells also support scripts that enable sequential execution of commands as well as other typical programming operations such as conditionals and loops. Common uses of shell scripts include long or repetitive tasks, or the need to run the same set of commands on multiple systems.   Adversaries may abuse Unix shells to execute various commands or payloads. Interactive shells may be accessed through command and control channels or during lateral movement such as with SSH. Adversaries may also leverage shell scripts to deliver and execute multiple commands on victims or as part of payloads used for persistence.   If the device has been rooted or jailbroken, adversaries may locate and invoke a superuser binary to elevate their privileges and interact with the system as the root user. This dangerous level of permissions allows the adversary to run special commands and modify protected system files.
          Use ADB shell to execute commands on Android devices through USB debugging or wireless debugging connection
          Create malicious shell scripts that establish reverse shells through netcat or similar tools
          Deploy shell scripts that modify system files to maintain persistence after device reboot
          Execute 'su' command after device rooting to gain root privileges and modify system settings
          Use shell commands to disable SELinux or other security mechanisms on rooted devices
          Create scheduled tasks (crontabs) through shell access for persistence
          Leverage shell access to modify Android manifest files to elevate app privileges
          Use shell commands to extract sensitive data from system directories
          Deploy shell scripts that modify network configurations to redirect traffic
          Execute shell commands to disable or bypass mobile device management (MDM) restrictions
          Use shell commands to modify system partition and remount as read-write
          Execute shell scripts to intercept and modify SMS messages through system files
          Use shell commands to kill security-related processes and services
          Deploy scripts to monitor and log user input through event devices
          Execute shell commands to access and exfiltrate cached application data
          Use shell to modify hosts file for DNS spoofing and traffic redirection
          Deploy scripts to access and control device sensors (camera, microphone, GPS)
          Execute shell commands to modify system certificates for SSL interception
          Use shell to create hidden files and directories for malware storage
          Deploy scripts to monitor and intercept clipboard content
          Use shell commands to inject code into running applications through ptrace
          Execute scripts to modify app backup files and restore modified versions
          Use shell to manipulate system property files for behavior modification
          Deploy scripts to create and hide backdoor user accounts
          Execute shell commands to modify bootloader configuration
          Use shell to extract encryption keys from memory dumps
          Deploy scripts to modify system update mechanisms
          Execute shell commands to bypass factory reset protection
          Use shell to modify system sounds and notifications for social engineering
          Deploy scripts to create hidden VPN tunnels for traffic interception
      Exploitation for Client Execution: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in client applications to execute code. Vulnerabilities can exist in software due to insecure coding practices that can lead to unanticipated behavior. Adversaries may take advantage of certain vulnerabilities through targeted exploitation for the purpose of arbitrary code execution. Oftentimes the most valuable exploits to an offensive toolkit are those that can be used to obtain code execution on a remote system because they can be used to gain access to that system. Users will expect to see files related to the applications they commonly used to do work, so they are a useful target for exploit research and development because of their high utility.   Adversaries may use device-based zero-click exploits for code execution. These exploits are powerful because there is no user interaction required for code execution.   ### SMS/iMessage Delivery    SMS and iMessage in iOS are common targets through [Drive-By Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1456), [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1660), etc. Adversaries may use embed malicious links, files, etc. in SMS messages or iMessages. Mobile devices may be compromised through one-click exploits, where the victim must interact with a text message, or zero-click exploits, where no user interaction is required.   ### AirDrop   Unique to iOS, AirDrop is a network protocol that allows iOS users to transfer files between iOS devices. Before patches from Apple were released, on iOS 13.4 and earlier, adversaries may force the Apple Wireless Direct Link (AWDL) interface to activate, then exploit a buffer overflow to gain access to the device and run as root without interaction from the user.
        ~SMS/MMS Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SMS/MMS processing components to execute code without or with minimal user interaction
        ~Messaging App Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in popular messaging applications like WhatsApp, Telegram, or Signal
        ~Browser Engine Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in mobile browser engines like WebKit or Blink
        ~PDF Reader Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in built-in or third-party PDF viewers
        ~Media Player Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in media processing libraries and players
        ~Bluetooth Protocol Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Bluetooth implementation and services
        ~WiFi Stack Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in WiFi drivers and networking stack
        ~File Format Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in document viewers and file processors
        ~App Store Client Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in the platform's app store client
        ~System Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in system-level services and daemons
        ~Calendar App Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in calendar apps and appointment handling
        ~Email Client Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in native and third-party email clients
        ~NFC Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Near Field Communication services
        ~QR Code Scanner Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in built-in or third-party QR code scanners
        ~Voice Assistant Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in voice command processing systems
        ~Mobile Payment App Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in payment processing apps and services
        ~Game Engine Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in popular mobile game engines
        ~Contact Manager Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in contact management systems
        ~Input Method Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in keyboard apps and input methods
        ~Camera API Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in camera processing and related APIs
        ~Cloud Sync Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in cloud storage and synchronization clients
        ~MDM Client Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in Mobile Device Management clients
        ~Biometric Authentication Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in fingerprint, face recognition, or other biometric systems
        ~Push Notification Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in push notification handling services
        ~WebView Component Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in embedded web views and hybrid app frameworks
        ~Clipboard Manager Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in clipboard management systems
        ~Background Task Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in background process handling
        ~Share Sheet Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in the system share functionality
        ~Authentication Framework Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in platform authentication services
        ~App Extension Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in app extensions and plugins
        ~AR/VR Component Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in augmented and virtual reality frameworks
        ~Health Kit Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in health and fitness data processing
        ~Digital Wallet Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in digital wallet and credential storage systems
        ~App Sandbox Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in application sandboxing mechanisms
        ~Social Media Framework Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in built-in social media integration
        ~Location Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in location tracking and geofencing
        ~Screen Recording Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in screen capture and recording features
        ~Deep Link Handler Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in URL scheme handlers and deep linking
        ~Accessibility Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in accessibility features and services
        ~Device Backup Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in backup and restore functionality
    persistence:  The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold.  Persistence is any access, action, or configuration change to a mobile device that gives an attacker a persistent presence on the device. Attackers often will need to maintain access to mobile devices through interruptions such as device reboots and potentially even factory data resets.
      Compromise Client Software Binary: Adversaries may modify system software binaries to establish persistent access to devices. System software binaries are used by the underlying operating system and users over adb or terminal emulators.   Adversaries may make modifications to client software binaries to carry out malicious tasks when those binaries are executed. For example, malware may come with a pre-compiled malicious binary intended to overwrite the genuine one on the device. Since these binaries may be routinely executed by the system or user, the adversary can leverage this for persistent access to the device.
        ~System App Binary Modification: Adversaries may modify system application binaries to maintain persistence, targeting core OS applications that run with elevated privileges.
        ~Command Line Tool Replacement: Adversaries may replace legitimate command-line tools and utilities used in terminal emulators with malicious versions.
        ~ADB Binary Manipulation: Adversaries may modify Android Debug Bridge binaries to maintain access and execute malicious commands when ADB is used.
        ~Boot Process Binary Modification: Adversaries may modify binaries involved in the device boot process to ensure persistence across reboots.
        ~Package Manager Binary Compromise: Adversaries may modify package manager binaries to intercept or manipulate app installations.
        ~Runtime Binary Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code into running system binaries during runtime.
        ~Backup/Restore Binary Tampering: Adversaries may modify backup and restore utilities to maintain persistence across device resets.
        ~System Update Binary Compromise: Adversaries may modify system update mechanisms to maintain persistence across OS updates.
      Code Injection: Adversaries may use code injection attacks to implant arbitrary code into the address space of a running application. Code is then executed or interpreted by that application. Adversaries utilizing this technique may exploit capabilities to load code in at runtime through dynamic libraries.  With root access, `ptrace` can be used to target specific applications and load shared libraries into its process memory.(Citation: Shunix Code Injection Mar 2016)(Citation: Fadeev Code Injection Aug 2018) By injecting code, an adversary may be able to gain access to higher permissions held by the targeted application by executing as the targeted application. In addition, the adversary may be able to evade detection or enable persistent access to a system under the guise of the application’s process.(Citation: Google Triada June 2019)
        ~Dynamic Library Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code by forcing an application to load compromised dynamic libraries during runtime, leveraging Android's dynamic loading capabilities or iOS dynamic frameworks.
        ~Process Memory Modification: Adversaries may directly modify process memory of running applications using tools like ptrace to inject malicious code segments.
        ~Framework Abuse: Adversaries may exploit mobile framework features like Android Runtime or iOS dynamic frameworks to inject code into legitimate applications.
        ~System Call Hooking: Adversaries may inject code by intercepting and modifying system calls made by legitimate applications.
        ~Native Code Injection: Adversaries may inject native code (ARM/x86) directly into processes to bypass security mechanisms and maintain persistence.
        ~WebView Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious JavaScript or native bridge code into WebView components used by mobile applications to execute arbitrary code.
        ~Component Hijacking: Adversaries may inject code by exploiting exposed application components like Activities, Services, or BroadcastReceivers in Android applications.
        ~IPC Channel Exploitation: Adversaries may inject code through manipulated inter-process communication channels like Android Binder or iOS XPC.
        ~Cross-Platform Framework Exploitation: Adversaries may inject code by exploiting vulnerabilities in cross-platform frameworks like React Native or Flutter.
      Hijack Execution Flow: Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way operating systems run applications. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence since this hijacked execution may reoccur over time.   There are many ways an adversary may hijack the flow of execution. A primary way is by manipulating how the operating system locates programs to be executed. How the operating system locates libraries to be used by a program can also be intercepted. Locations where the operating system looks for programs or resources, such as file directories, could also be poisoned to include malicious payloads.
        System Runtime API Hijacking: Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way an operating system runs applications. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence since this hijacked execution may reoccur at later points in time.    On Android, adversaries may overwrite the standard OS API library with a malicious alternative to hook into core functions to achieve persistence. By doing this, the adversary’s code will be executed every time the overwritten API function is called by an app on the infected device.
          Replace libc.so with malicious version to intercept system calls
          Use Cydia Substrate framework to hook into Android Runtime (ART) APIs
          Modify Android's linker to load malicious shared libraries
          Install Xposed Framework modules to intercept API calls
          Inject code into zygote process to affect all newly created apps
          Replace system's libdvm.so to intercept Dalvik VM calls
          Modify system's SELinux policies to allow API interception
          Use function hooking frameworks like ADBI (Android Dynamic Binary Instrumentation)
          Replace system's libart.so to intercept ART runtime calls
          Modify Android's global symbol table to redirect API calls
          Modify Android's linker cache (linker.cache) to redirect library loading
          Patch system's libandroid_runtime.so to intercept JNI bridges
          Use Frida framework for dynamic instrumentation and API hooking
          Replace system's libbinder.so to intercept IPC communications
          Modify init.rc to load compromised libraries during boot
          Use LSPosed framework for system API modification
          Replace system's liblog.so to intercept logging and monitoring
          Modify Android's boot.oat to inject code into all apps
          Use VirtualXposed for non-root API hooking
          Replace system's libutils.so for low-level function interception
          Modify vendor-specific HAL libraries for persistent API hooks
          Replace system's libsurfaceflinger.so to intercept graphics subsystem
          Use EdXposed framework for system-wide API modification
          Patch Android's native bridge implementation for ARM/x86 translation
          Modify system's audioflinger for audio subsystem interception
          Use RootCloak framework to hide root status while hooking
          Replace system's libinput.so to intercept input events
          Inject hooks into Android's shared memory segments
          Modify system's libsensorservice.so for sensor data interception
          Use Pine framework for non-root method hooking
          Replace system's libnetd.so to intercept network operations
          Modify Android's Package Manager service for app loading control
          Use LSPatch for converting system apps into hook hosts
          Patch system's mount service for storage operation interception
          Replace system's libwebviewchromium.so for web content hooks
          Modify system's MediaServer for media processing interception
          Use SandHook framework for ART runtime modification
          Replace system's location service for location data interception
          Modify system's telephony libraries for call/SMS interception
          Use FastHook for high-performance API hooking
      Scheduled Task/Job: Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. On Android and iOS, APIs and libraries exist to facilitate scheduling tasks to execute at a specified date, time, or interval.  On Android, the `WorkManager` API allows asynchronous tasks to be scheduled with the system. `WorkManager` was introduced to unify task scheduling on Android, using `JobScheduler`, `GcmNetworkManager`, and `AlarmManager` internally. `WorkManager` offers a lot of flexibility for scheduling, including periodically, one time, or constraint-based (e.g. only when the device is charging).(Citation: Android WorkManager)  On iOS, the `NSBackgroundActivityScheduler` API allows asynchronous tasks to be scheduled with the system. The tasks can be scheduled to be repeating or non-repeating, however, the system chooses when the tasks will be executed. The app can choose the interval for repeating tasks, or the delay between scheduling and execution for one-time tasks.(Citation: Apple NSBackgroundActivityScheduler)
        ~Android WorkManager Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Android's WorkManager API to schedule malicious background tasks that persist across device reboots and system constraints.
        ~iOS Background Task Scheduling: Adversaries may exploit iOS's NSBackgroundActivityScheduler to maintain persistence through scheduled background activities.
        ~Android AlarmManager Exploitation: Adversaries may directly utilize Android's AlarmManager to schedule recurring malicious tasks that wake up the device.
        ~Android JobScheduler Misuse: Adversaries may abuse Android's JobScheduler API to schedule jobs that execute under specific device conditions.
        ~iOS Background Fetch: Adversaries may abuse iOS background fetch capabilities to maintain periodic execution of malicious code.
        ~System Boot Receivers: Adversaries may register broadcast receivers for system boot events to restart malicious scheduled tasks after device restart.
        ~Periodic Work Requests: Adversaries may configure periodic work requests that execute at defined intervals to maintain persistence.
        ~Chained Task Scheduling: Adversaries may chain multiple scheduled tasks together to ensure redundant persistence mechanisms.
      Broadcast Receivers: An intent is a message passed between Android application or system components. Applications can register to receive broadcast intents at runtime, which are system-wide intents delivered to each app when certain events happen on the device, such as network changes or the user unlocking the screen. Malicious applications can then trigger certain actions within the app based on which broadcast intent was received.  Further, malicious applications can register for intents broadcasted by other applications in addition to the Android system itself. This allows the malware to respond based on actions in other applications. This behavior typically indicates a more intimate knowledge, or potentially the targeting of specific devices, users, or applications.  In Android 8 (API level 26), broadcast intent behavior was changed, limiting the implicit intents that applications can register for in the manifest. In most cases, applications that register through the manifest will no longer receive the broadcasts. Now, applications must register context-specific broadcast receivers while the user is actively using the app.(Citation: Android Changes to System Broadcasts)
        ~System Event Monitoring: Register receivers for system events like BOOT_COMPLETED, BATTERY_CHANGED, or CONNECTIVITY_CHANGE to persist after device restarts
        ~Application Activity Monitoring: Monitor other app activities through custom broadcast intents to trigger malicious behavior
        ~Dynamic Registration: Use context.registerReceiver() at runtime to bypass manifest restrictions
        ~Manifest Registration: Register receivers in AndroidManifest.xml for backwards compatibility on older Android versions
        ~Screen State Monitoring: Monitor screen on/off and unlock events to trigger malicious activities
        ~Package Management Events: Monitor app installation/removal events to maintain awareness of security apps
        ~SMS/Call Monitoring: Register for SMS and call-related broadcasts to intercept communications
        ~Location Change Monitoring: Track device location changes through broadcast receivers
      Foreground Persistence: Adversaries may abuse Android's `startForeground()` API method to maintain continuous sensor access. Beginning in Android 9, idle applications running in the background no longer have access to device sensors, such as the camera, microphone, and gyroscope.(Citation: Android-SensorsOverview) Applications can retain sensor access by running in the foreground, using Android’s `startForeground()` API method. This informs the system that the user is actively interacting with the application, and it should not be killed. The only requirement to start a foreground service is showing a persistent notification to the user.(Citation: Android-ForegroundServices)  Malicious applications may abuse the `startForeground()` API method to continue running in the foreground, while presenting a notification to the user pretending to be a genuine application. This would allow unhindered access to the device’s sensors, assuming permission has been previously granted.(Citation: BlackHat Sutter Android Foreground 2019)  Malicious applications may also abuse the `startForeground()` API to inform the Android system that the user is actively interacting with the application, thus preventing it from being killed by the low memory killer.(Citation: TrendMicro-Yellow Camera)
        ~Notification Spoofing: Adversaries may create deceptive notifications mimicking legitimate system or popular app notifications to hide the foreground service
        ~Sensor Access Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple sensor access requests to maintain continuous access even if individual permissions are revoked
        ~Service Resurrection: Adversaries may implement service auto-restart mechanisms when the foreground service is killed
        ~Low Memory Killer Evasion: Adversaries may manipulate process priority and resource usage to avoid being terminated by the system's low memory killer
        ~Permission Persistence: Adversaries may exploit permission caching mechanisms to maintain sensor access even after permission model changes
        ~Service Binding Abuse: Adversaries may bind their malicious service to legitimate system services to prevent termination
        ~Task Affinity Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate task affinity to make their foreground service appear as part of legitimate app tasks
        ~Fake User Interaction: Adversaries may simulate user interactions to maintain foreground status
        ~Component Hijacking: Adversaries may hijack components of legitimate apps to maintain their foreground service
        ~Priority Inflation: Adversaries may artificially inflate their service priority by creating dependencies with critical system services
        ~Broadcast Receiver Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple broadcast receivers to restart foreground services after system events
        ~WorkManager Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Android's WorkManager API to schedule persistent foreground tasks
        ~Process State Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate process states to appear as essential foreground processes
        ~System Overlay Abuse: Adversaries may use system overlay windows to maintain foreground status while hiding malicious activity
        ~Power Management Bypass: Adversaries may exploit power management features to prevent service suspension during device idle states
      Event Triggered Execution: Adversaries may establish persistence using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Mobile operating systems have means to subscribe to events such as receiving an SMS message, device boot completion, or other device activities.   Adversaries may abuse these mechanisms as a means of maintaining persistent access to a victim via automatically and repeatedly executing malicious code. After gaining access to a victim’s system, adversaries may create or modify event triggers to point to malicious content that will be executed whenever the event trigger is invoked.
        Broadcast Receivers: Adversaries may establish persistence using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Mobile operating systems have means to subscribe to events such as receiving an SMS message, device boot completion, or other device activities.   An intent is a message passed between Android applications or system components. Applications can register to receive broadcast intents at runtime, which are system-wide intents delivered to each app when certain events happen on the device, such as network changes or the user unlocking the screen. Malicious applications can then trigger certain actions within the app based on which broadcast intent was received.   In addition to Android system intents, malicious applications can register for intents broadcasted by other applications. This allows the malware to respond based on actions in other applications. This behavior typically indicates a more intimate knowledge, or potentially the targeting of specific devices, users, or applications.   In Android 8 (API level 26), broadcast intent behavior was changed, limiting the implicit intents that applications can register for in the manifest. In most cases, applications that register through the manifest will no longer receive the broadcasts. Now, applications must register context-specific broadcast receivers while the user is actively using the app.(Citation: Android Changes to System Broadcasts)
