      Compromise Application Executable: Adversaries may modify applications installed on a device to establish persistent access to a victim. These malicious modifications can be used to make legitimate applications carry out adversary tasks when these applications are in use.  There are multiple ways an adversary can inject malicious code into applications. One method is by taking advantages of device vulnerabilities, the most well-known being Janus, an Android vulnerability that allows adversaries to add extra bytes to APK (application) and DEX (executable) files without affecting the file's signature. By being able to add arbitrary bytes to valid applications, attackers can seamlessly inject code into genuine executables without the user's knowledge.(Citation: Guardsquare Janus)  Adversaries may also rebuild applications to include malicious modifications. This can be achieved by decompiling the genuine application, merging it with the malicious code, and recompiling it.(Citation: CheckPoint Agent Smith)  Adversaries may also take action to conceal modifications to application executables and bypass user consent. These actions include altering modifications to appear as an update or exploiting vulnerabilities that allow activities of the malicious application to run inside a system application.(Citation: CheckPoint Agent Smith)
        ~Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities like Janus to modify APK/DEX files while preserving signatures, allowing injection of malicious code into legitimate applications without detection
        ~Decompile and Repackage: Adversaries decompile legitimate applications, inject malicious code, then recompile and resign the application
        ~Update Masquerading: Adversaries disguise compromised applications as legitimate updates to bypass user suspicion and consent mechanisms
        ~System App Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities to run malicious code within system applications, gaining elevated privileges and persistence
        ~Resource Injection: Adversaries modify application resources and configuration files to alter application behavior without changing the main executable
        ~Dynamic Code Loading: Adversaries exploit legitimate dynamic code loading features to inject malicious code into running applications
        ~Library Modification: Adversaries modify shared libraries or dependencies used by legitimate applications to inject malicious functionality
        ~Sandbox Manipulation: Adversaries alter application sandbox configurations to enable persistent malicious behavior
        ~Framework Compromise: Adversaries modify development frameworks, SDKs, or third-party components used by legitimate applications
      Modify System Partition: If an adversary can escalate privileges, he or she may be able to use those privileges to place malicious code in the device system partition, where it may persist after device resets and may not be easily removed by the device user.  Many Android devices provide the ability to unlock the bootloader for development purposes. An unlocked bootloader may provide the ability for an adversary to modify the system partition. Even if the bootloader is locked, it may be possible for an adversary to escalate privileges and then modify the system partition.
        ~Bootloader Unlock Exploitation: Exploiting device bootloader unlock mechanisms to gain write access to system partition
        ~System Update Package Injection: Creating and installing malicious system update packages to modify system files
        ~Root Privilege Abuse: Using root privileges obtained through vulnerability exploitation to modify system files
        ~Recovery Mode Manipulation: Modifying system partition through custom recovery environments
        ~System Mount Remount: Remounting system partition as read-write to make modifications
        ~System File Replacement: Replacing critical system files with malicious versions while maintaining system functionality
        ~Init Script Modification: Adding or modifying init scripts to achieve persistence across reboots
        ~Vendor Partition Tampering: Modifying vendor-specific partitions that are part of the system
      Modify Cached Executable Code: ART (the Android Runtime) compiles optimized code on the device itself to improve performance. An adversary may be able to use escalated privileges to modify the cached code in order to hide malicious behavior. Since the code is compiled on the device, it may not receive the same level of integrity checks that are provided to code running in the system partition.(Citation: Sabanal-ART)
        ~Modify OAT Files: Adversaries directly modify OAT (One AheadOf Time) compiled files in the /data/dalvik-cache directory to inject malicious code
        ~Runtime Memory Patching: Attackers patch the in-memory representation of compiled code during runtime execution
        ~DEX to OAT Manipulation: Adversaries intercept and modify code during the DEX to OAT compilation process
        ~Cache Poisoning: Attackers replace legitimate cached code files with malicious versions during the optimization process
        ~JIT Cache Modification: Adversaries target the Just-In-Time compilation cache to inject malicious code into frequently executed methods
        ~Profile-Guided Optimization Abuse: Attackers manipulate ART's PGO data to force recompilation with malicious modifications
        ~Boot Image Modification: Adversaries modify the ART boot image containing pre-compiled code for core libraries
        ~Interpreter Bridge Manipulation: Attackers modify the bridge between interpreted and compiled code to execute malicious logic
        ~Class Loader Cache Poisoning: Adversaries target the class loader cache to inject malicious code during class resolution
        ~Quick Code Cache Tampering: Attackers modify the quick code cache used for frequently accessed methods
      Modify Trusted Execution Environment: If an adversary can escalate privileges, he or she may be able to use those privileges to place malicious code in the device's Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) or other similar isolated execution environment where the code can evade detection, may persist after device resets, and may not be removable by the device user. Running code within the TEE may provide an adversary with the ability to monitor or tamper with overall device behavior.(Citation: Roth-Rootkits)
        ~TEE Firmware Modification: Adversaries may modify the firmware of the TEE to maintain persistence and execute malicious code in the secure environment
        ~Secure Boot Tampering: Adversaries may modify secure boot components within the TEE to ensure malicious code loads during device initialization
        ~TrustZone Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in ARM TrustZone implementation to inject and execute malicious code
        ~Secure Element Compromise: Adversaries may target secure elements within the TEE to maintain persistence and intercept sensitive operations
        ~TEE Driver Manipulation: Adversaries may modify or replace TEE drivers to maintain persistent access to secure functions
        ~Trusted Application Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious trusted applications into the TEE to maintain persistence
        ~TEE Memory Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate TEE memory regions to inject and execute malicious code
        ~Secure Monitor Modification: Adversaries may modify secure monitor code to maintain control over TEE operations
      Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence. Initialization scripts are part of the underlying operating system and are not accessible to the user unless the device has been rooted or jailbroken.
        ~RC Scripts Modification: Adversaries may modify RC (Run Commands) scripts on rooted Android devices to maintain persistence across reboots
        ~Launch Daemon Manipulation: On jailbroken iOS devices, adversaries may create or modify launch daemons to execute malicious code during system startup
        ~Init.d Script Injection: Attackers may inject malicious code into init.d scripts on rooted Android devices to ensure execution during boot sequence
        ~System Properties Modification: Adversaries may alter system properties files to modify boot behavior and maintain persistence
        ~Startup Service Creation: Creation of malicious system services that automatically start during device initialization
        ~Recovery Script Modification: Adversaries may modify recovery mode scripts to maintain persistence even after factory reset
        ~Vendor Init Scripts: Modification of vendor-specific initialization scripts that run during device startup
        ~SELinux Policy Scripts: Adversaries may modify SELinux policy loading scripts to maintain persistence and elevate privileges during boot
        ~Shell Profile Injection: Modification of shell profile scripts that execute during system initialization on rooted/jailbroken devices
        ~Boot Animation Scripts: Adversaries may hide malicious code in boot animation scripts that execute during device startup
    privilege-escalation:  The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions.  Privilege escalation includes techniques that allow an attacker to obtain a higher level of permissions on the mobile device. Attackers may enter the mobile device with very limited privileges and may be required to take advantage of a device weakness to obtain higher privileges necessary to successfully carry out their mission objectives.
      Code Injection: Adversaries may use code injection attacks to implant arbitrary code into the address space of a running application. Code is then executed or interpreted by that application. Adversaries utilizing this technique may exploit capabilities to load code in at runtime through dynamic libraries.  With root access, `ptrace` can be used to target specific applications and load shared libraries into its process memory.(Citation: Shunix Code Injection Mar 2016)(Citation: Fadeev Code Injection Aug 2018) By injecting code, an adversary may be able to gain access to higher permissions held by the targeted application by executing as the targeted application. In addition, the adversary may be able to evade detection or enable persistent access to a system under the guise of the application’s process.(Citation: Google Triada June 2019)
        ~Dynamic Library Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code through dynamic library loading mechanisms, exploiting the mobile platform's ability to load libraries at runtime to execute arbitrary code within target processes.
        ~Process Memory Modification: Adversaries may directly modify process memory to inject and execute malicious code, often using system calls or debugging interfaces like ptrace.
        ~Framework Manipulation: Adversaries may exploit mobile framework vulnerabilities to inject code into application contexts, such as manipulating Java/Android Runtime or Objective-C runtime.
        ~System Process Injection: Adversaries may specifically target system processes with elevated privileges to inject code and gain higher permissions.
        ~App Component Hijacking: Adversaries may inject code by exploiting inter-component communication mechanisms in mobile apps to execute malicious code in the context of privileged components.
        ~Runtime Method Hooking: Adversaries may inject code by hooking method calls in the mobile runtime environment, intercepting and modifying application behavior to execute malicious code.
        ~Native Code Bridge Exploitation: Adversaries may inject code through compromised native bridges between different execution environments (e.g., JavaScript to native code bridges).
        ~Shared Memory Injection: Adversaries may leverage shared memory segments to inject code into privileged processes, exploiting mobile OS IPC mechanisms.
        ~Boot Process Injection: Adversaries may inject code during the mobile device boot process to gain elevated privileges, targeting initialization sequences.
        ~Resource Loading Manipulation: Adversaries may inject code by manipulating how applications load external resources, intercepting legitimate resource loading to execute malicious code.
      Device Administrator Permissions: Adversaries may request device administrator permissions to perform malicious actions.  By abusing the device administration API, adversaries can perform several nefarious actions, such as resetting the device’s password for [Device Lockout](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1446), factory resetting the device to [Delete Device Data](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1447) and any traces of the malware, disabling all of the device’s cameras, or make it more difficult to uninstall the app.(Citation: Android DeviceAdminInfo)  Device administrators must be approved by the user at runtime, with a system popup showing which of the actions have been requested by the app. In conjunction with other techniques, such as [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), an app can programmatically grant itself administrator permissions without any user input.
        ~Automated Permission Request: Adversaries may use automated scripts or malware to repeatedly request device administrator permissions until granted
        ~Social Engineering Prompts: Adversaries may use deceptive prompts or messages to convince users to grant device administrator permissions
        ~System Update Masquerading: Adversaries may disguise malicious apps as system updates requiring administrator permissions
        ~API Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in the device administration API to elevate privileges without user consent
        ~Permission Chain Abuse: Adversaries may chain multiple permission requests to gradually escalate to device administrator status
        ~Legitimate App Impersonation: Adversaries may clone or mimic legitimate apps that commonly request administrator permissions
        ~Background Permission Escalation: Adversaries may wait for specific system states or conditions to request permissions with minimal user notice
        ~Update-Based Elevation: Adversaries may introduce administrator permission requests through app updates after establishing initial trust
      Process Injection: Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses or even elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via process injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process.   Both Android and iOS have no legitimate way to achieve process injection. The only way this is possible is by abusing existing root access or exploiting a vulnerability.
        Ptrace System Calls: Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via ptrace (process trace) system calls in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Ptrace system call injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process.    Ptrace system call injection involves attaching to and modifying a running process. The ptrace system call enables a debugging process to observe and control another process (and each individual thread), including changing memory and register values.(Citation: PTRACE man) Ptrace system call injection is commonly performed by writing arbitrary code into a running process (e.g., by using `malloc`) then invoking that memory with `PTRACE_SETREGS` to set the register containing the next instruction to execute. Ptrace system call injection can also be done with `PTRACE_POKETEXT`/`PTRACE_POKEDATA`, which copy data to a specific address in the target process's memory (e.g., the current address of the next instruction).(Citation: PTRACE man)(Citation: Medium Ptrace JUL 2018)    Ptrace system call injection may not be possible when targeting processes with high-privileges, and on some systems those that are non-child processes.(Citation: BH Linux Inject)    Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via ptrace system call injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process.
          Use PTRACE_ATTACH to attach to target process followed by PTRACE_SETREGS to modify registers for code execution
          Employ PTRACE_POKETEXT to directly write shellcode into process memory space
          Utilize custom ptrace wrapper tools to automate process injection
          Inject shared libraries into running processes using ptrace and dlopen
          Use ptrace to modify return addresses in stack frames to redirect execution
          Implement memory allocation in target process using ptrace and mmap syscalls
          Deploy ptrace-based rootkits that hook system calls in running processes
          Use ptrace to inject code into privileged system services for privilege escalation
          Modify process memory protection flags using ptrace to bypass W^X restrictions
          Create debugging backdoors using persistent ptrace attachments
          Use PTRACE_SYSCALL to intercept and modify system calls in target process
          Implement ptrace-based anti-debugging by detecting and blocking other debugger attachments
          Chain multiple PTRACE_PEEK operations to read and verify memory before injection
          Use ptrace to modify process credentials (uid/gid) in process memory
          Implement ptrace-based system call hooking for keystroke logging
          Create ptrace-based process hollowing by stopping thread execution and replacing code segments
          Use ptrace to inject code into short-lived child processes during fork operations
          Deploy ptrace for direct kernel memory manipulation through process interfaces
          Use ptrace to inject code into suspended processes during thread creation
          Implement ptrace-based signal handler manipulation for stealth execution
          Deploy ptrace to modify process environment variables for library load path hijacking
          Use PTRACE_SINGLESTEP for fine-grained execution control and anti-analysis
          Create ptrace-based watchpoints to monitor and intercept memory access
          Implement self-debugging techniques using ptrace to prevent other attachments
          Use ptrace to modify process names and command line arguments for disguise
      Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Adversaries may circumvent mechanisms designed to control elevated privileges to gain higher-level permissions. Most modern systems contain native elevation control mechanisms that are intended to limit privileges that a user can gain on a machine. Authorization has to be granted to specific users in order to perform tasks that are designated as higher risk. An adversary can use several methods to take advantage of built-in control mechanisms in order to escalate privileges on a system.
        Device Administrator Permissions: Adversaries may abuse Android’s device administration API to obtain a higher degree of control over the device. By abusing the API, adversaries can perform several nefarious actions, such as resetting the device’s password for [Endpoint Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1642), factory resetting the device for [File Deletion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1630/002) and to delete any traces of the malware, disabling all the device’s cameras, or to make it more difficult to uninstall the app.  Device administrators must be approved by the user at runtime, with a system popup showing which actions have been requested by the app. In conjunction with other techniques, such as [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), an app can programmatically grant itself administrator permissions without any user input.
          Use Android Debug Bridge (ADB) to programmatically send touch events that approve device admin permissions
          Implement DeviceAdminReceiver class with elevated privileges and request all available admin permissions
          Create malicious app that tricks users by masquerading as system update/security tool requiring admin rights
          Exploit accessibility services to automatically click through admin permission dialogs
          Use reflection to bypass DeviceAdminReceiver restrictions and gain additional privileges
          Deploy automated scripts to inject touch events via /dev/input/event* interface
          Abuse Android's Device Owner provisioning to gain persistent admin access
          Create overlay attacks to hide legitimate permission prompts while granting admin rights
          Implement background services that repeatedly request admin permissions until granted
          Use Android Enterprise APIs to bypass normal admin permission restrictions
          Implement backup/restore functionality to preserve admin rights across device resets
          Create scheduled tasks that monitor and re-enable admin privileges if removed
          Chain multiple DevicePolicyManager API calls to maximize privilege scope
          Use package name spoofing to masquerade as legitimate system admin apps
          Implement dead-man switch that triggers device wipe if admin rights are removed
          Abuse device owner provisioning during initial device setup
          Exploit MDM enrollment processes to gain admin privileges
          Use split APK technique to hide admin permission requests in dynamic modules
          Chain WebView exploits with admin permission requests
          Implement failsafe admin rights restoration via broadcast receivers
          Use work profile creation to silently enable admin permissions
          Exploit device OEM-specific admin permission implementations
          Chain Knox API vulnerabilities with admin permission requests
          Abuse Android Zero-Touch enrollment process
          Implement multi-stage admin rights elevation through system app updates
          Use device policy controller spoofing in managed environments
          Create fake system update UI to force admin permission approval
          Exploit Android for Work provisioning flows
          Chain USB debugging enablement with admin permission requests
          Abuse Android Enterprise QR code provisioning
          Exploit Samsung KNOX container creation for admin escalation
          Chain Android Factory Reset Protection bypass with admin requests
          Abuse Android Device Owner migration protocols
          Exploit multi-user profile switching for admin persistence
          Use Android Enterprise API deprecation inconsistencies
          Chain Android Verified Boot bypass with admin elevation
          Exploit managed configuration deployment weaknesses
          Abuse Android provisioning mode time windows
          Chain Android SafetyNet attestation bypass with admin requests
          Exploit Android corporate-owned device enrollment flows
          Exploit device setup wizard timing for silent admin activation
          Chain bootloader unlocking with admin permission persistence
          Abuse Android EMM API version inconsistencies
          Exploit device encryption state changes for admin elevation
          Chain backup transport service with admin permission requests
          Abuse Android device provisioning app vulnerabilities
          Exploit managed profile creation race conditions
          Chain TEE (Trusted Execution Environment) bypass with admin elevation
          Abuse Android provisioning mode timeout windows
          Exploit cross-profile intent filters for admin persistence
      Exploit TEE Vulnerability: A malicious app or other attack vector could be used to exploit vulnerabilities in code running within the Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) (Citation: Thomas-TrustZone). The adversary could then obtain privileges held by the TEE potentially including the ability to access cryptographic keys or other sensitive data (Citation: QualcommKeyMaster). Escalated operating system privileges may be first required in order to have the ability to attack the TEE (Citation: EkbergTEE). If not, privileges within the TEE can potentially be used to exploit the operating system (Citation: laginimaineb-TEE).
        ~Memory Corruption in TEE: Exploiting buffer overflows, use-after-free, or other memory corruption vulnerabilities in TEE code to gain unauthorized access or privileges
        ~Secure Monitor Call Abuse: Exploiting vulnerabilities in the interface between normal world and secure world through SMC instruction manipulation
        ~Cryptographic Implementation Flaws: Targeting weaknesses in cryptographic implementations within TEE to extract sensitive keys or data
        ~Shared Memory Attacks: Exploiting shared memory regions between normal world and secure world to leak or manipulate protected data
        ~TEE Driver Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in TEE device drivers to gain elevated privileges
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in secure boot verification within TEE to load unauthorized code
        ~Side-Channel Analysis: Using timing, power, or electromagnetic analysis to extract sensitive information from TEE operations
        ~TEE Configuration Attacks: Exploiting misconfigurations or design flaws in TEE setup and initialization
      Exploitation for Privilege Escalation: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in order to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in an application, service, within the operating system software, or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Security constructions, such as permission levels, will often hinder access to information and use of certain techniques. Adversaries will likely need to perform privilege escalation to include use of software exploitation to circumvent those restrictions.   When initially gaining access to a device, an adversary may be operating within a lower privileged process which will prevent them from accessing certain resources on the system. Vulnerabilities may exist, usually in operating system components and applications running at higher permissions, that can be exploited to gain higher levels of access on the system. This could enable someone to move from unprivileged or user- level permission to root permissions depending on the component that is vulnerable.
        ~Kernel Vulnerability Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in the mobile device's kernel to gain root access
        ~Application Sandbox Escape: Exploiting vulnerabilities in application sandboxing mechanisms to break out of restricted environments
        ~System Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in privileged system services to gain elevated permissions
        ~Permission Model Bypass: Exploiting flaws in the OS permission model to gain unauthorized privileges
        ~Bootloader Exploitation: Leveraging vulnerabilities in bootloader components to achieve persistent root access
        ~Device Driver Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in device drivers that run with elevated privileges
        ~Memory Corruption: Exploiting memory corruption vulnerabilities in privileged processes
        ~IPC Channel Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in inter-process communication mechanisms
        ~Trust Zone Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in secure execution environments
        ~WebView Privilege Escalation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in WebView components running with elevated privileges
        ~Native Library Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in native libraries loaded by privileged processes
        ~OTA Update Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in over-the-air update mechanisms
        ~Hardware Peripheral Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in hardware peripheral interfaces and their drivers
        ~SELinux Policy Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SELinux policy enforcement
        ~Vendor HAL Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Hardware Abstraction Layer implementations
        ~Root Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in pre-installed root service applications
        ~Package Manager Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in system package management components
        ~File System Mount Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in privileged file system mounting operations
        ~Baseband Processor Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in cellular baseband processors to gain system privileges
        ~Debug Interface Exploitation: Targeting enabled or partially disabled debug interfaces for privilege escalation
        ~Hypervisor Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in mobile virtualization layers
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in secure boot implementation
        ~Platform Security Feature Bypass: Targeting implementation flaws in platform-specific security features
        ~MDM Component Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in Mobile Device Management components
    defense-evasion:  The adversary is trying to avoid being detected.  Defense evasion consists of techniques an adversary may use to evade detection or avoid other defenses. Sometimes these actions are the same as or variations of techniques in other categories that have the added benefit of subverting a particular defense or mitigation. Defense evasion may be considered a set of attributes the adversary applies to all other phases of the operation.
      User Evasion: Adversaries may attempt to avoid detection by hiding malicious behavior from the user. By doing this, an adversary’s modifications would most likely remain installed on the device for longer, allowing the adversary to continue to operate on that device.   While there are many ways this can be accomplished, one method is by using the device’s sensors. By utilizing the various motion sensors on a device, such as accelerometer or gyroscope, an application could detect that the device is being interacted with. That way, the application could continue to run while the device is not in use but cease operating while the user is using the device, hiding anything that would indicate malicious activity was ongoing. Accessing the sensors in this way does not require any permissions from the user, so it would be completely transparent.
        ~Sensor-based Activity Detection: Using device sensors (accelerometer, gyroscope, etc.) to detect user activity and pause malicious operations when the device is in use
        ~Background Process Masking: Disguising malicious processes as legitimate system processes or hiding them from process lists
        ~UI Element Hiding: Dynamically hiding malicious UI elements when user interaction is detected
        ~Screen Overlay Evasion: Using screen overlays to mask malicious activity while appearing legitimate to users
        ~Silent Mode Operation: Operating only when device is in silent/do not disturb mode to avoid user awareness
        ~Sleep Mode Exploitation: Executing malicious activities only when device screen is off or in sleep mode
        ~Resource Usage Throttling: Limiting resource consumption during user activity to avoid detection through performance degradation
        ~Notification Suppression: Preventing system notifications that might alert users to malicious activity
        ~App Icon Hiding: Removing app icons from launcher while maintaining background operations
        ~Time-based Execution: Scheduling malicious activities during typical non-use periods
        ~Storage Access Masking: Performing file operations only when other apps are accessing storage to blend in with legitimate activity
        ~Network Traffic Blending: Timing malicious network communications to coincide with legitimate app usage
        ~Battery-aware Operation: Executing operations only during charging to avoid noticeable battery drain
        ~System Update Mimicry: Disguising malicious activities as system update processes
        ~Input Method Monitoring: Detecting keyboard/input method usage to pause operations during active user interaction
        ~Proximity Sensor Abuse: Using proximity sensor data to detect when device is in pocket/face-down
        ~Audio Activity Detection: Monitoring microphone or audio output to determine user presence
        ~Connectivity-based Timing: Executing actions only during specific network conditions to avoid user notice
        ~Device Motion Pattern Analysis: Using complex motion patterns to identify genuine user interaction versus automated movement
        ~Screen State Monitoring: Detecting screen on/off states and lock status to time malicious activities
        ~App Usage Pattern Analysis: Monitoring which apps are active to time malicious activities during specific app usage contexts
        ~Device Unlock Pattern: Operating only during specific unlock patterns or authentication methods
        ~Ambient Light Detection: Using light sensor to determine environmental context and user activity
        ~Location-based Triggering: Executing operations based on device location or movement patterns
        ~Cellular Signal Monitoring: Timing operations based on signal strength changes to detect user movement
        ~Device Temperature Analysis: Using temperature sensors to detect active device usage
        ~Bluetooth Device Detection: Monitoring connected Bluetooth devices to infer user activity
        ~System Load Analysis: Monitoring system load to blend activities during high-activity periods
        ~Memory Pressure Detection: Executing operations only during specific memory availability conditions
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Using accessibility services to detect user interactions while appearing legitimate
        ~Virtual Environment Detection: Detecting if device is running in emulator/test environment to avoid analysis
        ~Debug Mode Evasion: Detecting and behaving differently when device is in developer/debug mode
        ~Multi-User Profile Detection: Adjusting behavior based on active user profile on device
        ~Device Administrator Abuse: Using device admin features to persist while appearing as system functionality
        ~Gesture Recognition Evasion: Monitoring complex touch gestures to identify real user interaction
        ~System Language Adaptation: Modifying behavior based on system language/locale settings
        ~Vendor-specific Feature Abuse: Exploiting manufacturer-specific features for evasion
        ~Biometric State Monitoring: Detecting biometric authentication events to time operations
        ~Power Save Mode Detection: Adapting behavior when power saving features are enabled
        ~Security App Detection: Identifying and adapting behavior when security apps are present
      Code Injection: Adversaries may use code injection attacks to implant arbitrary code into the address space of a running application. Code is then executed or interpreted by that application. Adversaries utilizing this technique may exploit capabilities to load code in at runtime through dynamic libraries.  With root access, `ptrace` can be used to target specific applications and load shared libraries into its process memory.(Citation: Shunix Code Injection Mar 2016)(Citation: Fadeev Code Injection Aug 2018) By injecting code, an adversary may be able to gain access to higher permissions held by the targeted application by executing as the targeted application. In addition, the adversary may be able to evade detection or enable persistent access to a system under the guise of the application’s process.(Citation: Google Triada June 2019)
        ~Dynamic Library Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code by forcing an application to load compromised dynamic libraries at runtime.
        ~Process Memory Modification: Adversaries may directly modify the memory space of running processes to inject and execute malicious code.
        ~Method Hooking: Adversaries may hook into legitimate application methods to intercept and modify their behavior during runtime.
        ~Native Code Injection: Adversaries may inject native code (ARM/x86) into processes to bypass security controls and execute privileged operations.
        ~JNI Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Java Native Interface to inject malicious native code into Java-based Android applications.
        ~Runtime Method Swizzling: Adversaries may use method swizzling in iOS to replace legitimate method implementations with malicious ones at runtime.
        ~Framework Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate mobile framework components to inject code into protected processes.
        ~WebView JavaScript Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious JavaScript code into WebView components to compromise app functionality.
      Execution Guardrails: Adversaries may use execution guardrails to constrain execution or actions based on adversary supplied and environment specific conditions that are expected to be present on the target. Guardrails ensure that a payload only executes against an intended target and reduces collateral damage from an adversary’s campaign. Values an adversary can provide about a target system or environment to use as guardrails may include environment information such as location.(Citation: SWB Exodus March 2019)  Guardrails can be used to prevent exposure of capabilities in environments that are not intended to be compromised or operated within. This use of guardrails is distinct from typical [System Checks](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633/001). While use of [System Checks](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633/001) may involve checking for known sandbox values and continuing with execution only if there is no match, the use of guardrails will involve checking for an expected target-specific value and only continuing with execution if there is such a match.
        Geofencing: Adversaries may use a device’s geographical location to limit certain malicious behaviors. For example, malware operators may limit the distribution of a second stage payload to certain geographic regions.(Citation: Lookout eSurv)  [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627/001) is accomplished by persuading the user to grant the application permission to access location services. The application can then collect, process, and exfiltrate the device’s location to perform location-based actions, such as ceasing malicious behavior or showing region-specific advertisements.   One method to accomplish [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627/001) on Android is to use the built-in Geofencing API to automatically trigger certain behaviors when the device enters or exits a specified radius around a geographical location. Similar to  other [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627/001) methods, this requires that the user has granted the `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` and `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` permissions. The latter is only required if the application targets Android 10 (API level 29) or higher. However, Android 11 introduced additional permission controls that may restrict background location collection based on user permission choices at runtime. These additional controls include "Allow only while using the app", which will effectively prohibit background location collection.    Similarly, on iOS, developers can use built-in APIs to setup and execute geofencing. Depending on the use case, the app will either need to call `requestWhenInUseAuthorization()` or `requestAlwaysAuthorization()`, depending on when access to the location services is required. Similar to Android, users also have the option to limit when the application can access the device’s location, including one-time use and only when the application is running in the foreground.    [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627/001) can be used to prevent exposure of capabilities in environments that are not intended to be compromised or operated within. For example, location data could be used to limit malware spread and/or capabilities, which could also potentially evade application analysis environments (ex: malware analysis outside of the target geographic area). Other malicious usages could include showing language-specific input prompts and/or advertisements.
