          Track camera usage state to avoid detection during active user photography/video
          Monitor device orientation changes frequency to determine usage patterns
        Suppress Application Icon: A malicious application could suppress its icon from being displayed to the user in the application launcher. This hides the fact that it is installed, and can make it more difficult for the user to uninstall the application. Hiding the application's icon programmatically does not require any special permissions.   This behavior has been seen in the BankBot/Spy Banker family of malware.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: sunny-stolen-credentials)(Citation: bankbot-spybanker)   Beginning in Android 10, changes were introduced to inhibit malicious applications’ ability to hide their icon. If an app is a system app, requests no permissions, or does not have a launcher activity, the application’s icon will be fully hidden. Further, if the device is fully managed or the application is in a work profile, the icon will be fully hidden. Otherwise, a synthesized activity is shown, which is a launcher icon that represents the app’s details page in the system settings. If the user clicks the synthesized activity in the launcher, they are taken to the application’s details page in the system settings.(Citation: Android 10 Limitations to Hiding App Icons)(Citation: LauncherApps getActivityList)
          Use PackageManager.setComponentEnabledSetting() to disable the launcher activity
          Modify AndroidManifest.xml to remove android.intent.category.LAUNCHER from main activity
          Implement empty/blank activity as main launcher to appear non-functional
          Register as device administrator to prevent uninstallation while hidden
          Use android:enabled="false" in activity declaration to prevent icon display
          Create dummy package name mimicking system apps to avoid detection
          Exploit work profile APIs to force icon hiding in Android 10+
          Use system app privileges to ensure complete icon suppression
          Implement dynamic component enabling/disabling to show/hide icon on demand
          Abuse accessibility services to programmatically hide launcher icons
          Exploit vendor-specific launcher APIs (e.g., MIUI, EMUI) to hide icons
          Use split APK mechanism to separate icon resources from main package
          Replace app icon with transparent PNG to appear invisible
          Modify launcher database directly on rooted devices
          Use overlay attacks to mask application presence in launcher
          Implement time-based icon suppression to evade periodic checks
          Abuse third-party launcher plugins for icon hiding
          Create alias activities with missing icons as decoys
          Use runtime resource modification to remove icon assets
          Exploit backup/restore mechanisms to manipulate icon visibility
        Conceal Multimedia Files: Adversaries may attempt to hide multimedia files from the user. By doing so, adversaries may conceal captured files, such as pictures, videos and/or screenshots, then later exfiltrate those files.    Specific to Android devices, if the `.nomedia` file is present in a folder, multimedia files in that folder will not be visible to the user in the Gallery application. Additionally, other applications are asked not to scan the folder with the `.nomedia` file, effectively making the folder appear invisible to the user.    This technique is often used by stalkerware and spyware applications.
          Create .nomedia file in camera output directory to hide captured photos
          Place .nomedia file in WhatsApp media folders to conceal downloaded content
          Programmatically create hidden directories with .nomedia files using Android APIs
          Modify media store database to exclude specific directories from scanning
          Use root access to modify system media scanner configuration
          Implement custom content provider to intercept media queries
          Rename media files with non-standard extensions to bypass gallery apps
          Use Android Storage Access Framework to create hidden directories
          Modify file timestamps to prevent media indexing
          Use native code to manipulate media store database entries
          Use iOS private directories to store media files outside of Photos app access
          Encrypt media files and store with non-media extensions
          Fragment media files across multiple hidden directories
          Modify media file headers to prevent proper identification
          Use system backup directories to hide media content
          Implement custom media codec to prevent standard players from opening files
          Utilize device-specific hidden partitions for media storage
          Exploit third-party gallery apps' cache directories
          Use temporary directories with rapid deletion/recreation cycles
          Implement steganography to hide media within apparent normal files
          Abuse Android Work Profile isolation to hide media in enterprise container
          Exploit cloud backup apps' local cache directories
          Use system clipboard storage locations for temporary media hiding
          Modify media file EXIF data to bypass content analysis
          Implement virtual file system within app private storage
          Use WebView cache directories to store concealed media
          Exploit shared storage vulnerabilities in dual-app systems
          Hide media in system crash dump directories
          Use app-specific backup locations to store media files
          Implement RAM-based temporary media storage with persistence
          Exploit vendor-specific secure folder features (e.g., Samsung Secure Folder)
          Use SD card hidden partition techniques
          Leverage Android multi-user feature to hide media in secondary user space
          Hide media in system update package directories
          Exploit app widget storage locations
          Use device health monitoring directories for storage
          Hide media in system accessibility cache
          Exploit instant app storage locations
          Use system keyboard cache directories
          Leverage system advertisement cache locations
      Foreground Persistence: Adversaries may abuse Android's `startForeground()` API method to maintain continuous sensor access. Beginning in Android 9, idle applications running in the background no longer have access to device sensors, such as the camera, microphone, and gyroscope.(Citation: Android-SensorsOverview) Applications can retain sensor access by running in the foreground, using Android’s `startForeground()` API method. This informs the system that the user is actively interacting with the application, and it should not be killed. The only requirement to start a foreground service is showing a persistent notification to the user.(Citation: Android-ForegroundServices)  Malicious applications may abuse the `startForeground()` API method to continue running in the foreground, while presenting a notification to the user pretending to be a genuine application. This would allow unhindered access to the device’s sensors, assuming permission has been previously granted.(Citation: BlackHat Sutter Android Foreground 2019)  Malicious applications may also abuse the `startForeground()` API to inform the Android system that the user is actively interacting with the application, thus preventing it from being killed by the low memory killer.(Citation: TrendMicro-Yellow Camera)
        ~Notification Spoofing: Adversaries may create fake notifications that mimic legitimate system or popular app notifications to deceive users about the running foreground service
        ~Camera Access Persistence: Adversaries may specifically maintain persistent camera access by continuously running a foreground service with spoofed camera-related notifications
        ~Microphone Access Persistence: Adversaries may maintain persistent microphone access through foreground services while masking the activity as audio-related legitimate functions
        ~Location Tracking Persistence: Adversaries may abuse foreground services to maintain continuous GPS and location sensor access while masquerading as navigation or fitness apps
        ~Service Chaining: Adversaries may chain multiple foreground services together to maintain persistence even if one service is terminated
        ~Resource Usage Masking: Adversaries may manipulate CPU and memory usage patterns of the foreground service to appear as a legitimate background process
        ~Auto-restart Mechanisms: Adversaries may implement multiple triggers to automatically restart the foreground service if it gets terminated
        ~System Event Hijacking: Adversaries may tie the foreground service to legitimate system events (like screen on/off, battery changes) to appear more legitimate
        ~Split Service Technique: Adversaries may split malicious functionality across multiple minimal foreground services to reduce suspicion and resource usage patterns
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Adversaries may combine foreground persistence with accessibility services to maintain privileged access while appearing legitimate
      Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors after checking for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware’s behavior to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the payload. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping further payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.   Adversaries may use several methods to accomplish [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633) such as checking for system artifacts associated with analysis or virtualization. Adversaries may also check for legitimate user activity to help determine if it is in an analysis environment.
        System Checks: Adversaries may employ various system checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behavior after checking for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual environment or sandbox. If the adversary detects a virtual environment, they may alter their malware’s behavior to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for virtualization artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads.   Checks could include generic system properties such as host/domain name and samples of network traffic. Adversaries may also check the network adapters addresses, CPU core count, and available memory/drive size.   Hardware checks, such as the presence of motion sensors, could also be used to gather evidence that can be indicative a virtual environment. Adversaries may also query for specific readings from these devices.
          Check for emulator-specific phone numbers like "1-555-521-5554"
          Query motion sensor data patterns to detect abnormal/constant values indicating emulation
          Verify presence of common Android emulator files like "/sys/qemu_trace"
          Compare device fingerprints against known emulator MAC addresses
          Check CPU temperature variations which are often missing in emulators
          Monitor GPS location changes to detect static/unrealistic patterns
          Examine battery state changes for unrealistic patterns
          Test for presence of common debugging tools like "frida" or "substrate"
          Compare device model/manufacturer against known emulator strings
          Check memory size against typical emulator configurations
          Check for consistent network latency patterns that might indicate sandboxing
          Verify presence of typical mobile carrier APN settings
          Test storage I/O speeds which are often faster in emulators
          Check for presence of real SIM card properties
          Examine build.prop values for emulator-specific settings
          Test camera API responses for emulator patterns
          Verify Bluetooth adapter properties and capabilities
          Check for realistic app installation history
          Test microphone input patterns for artificial responses
          Examine system uptime and boot time patterns
          Check for touch screen event consistency and patterns
          Verify presence of typical mobile keyboard cache data
          Test SMS/MMS functionality completeness
          Examine mobile radio state transitions
          Check for consistent power management patterns
          Verify presence and behavior of mobile DRM services
          Test NFC hardware capabilities and responses
          Check for realistic app permission grant history
          Verify presence of typical mobile vendor services
          Test biometric sensor API responses
      Suppress Application Icon: A malicious application could suppress its icon from being displayed to the user in the application launcher to hide the fact that it is installed, and to make it more difficult for the user to uninstall the application. Hiding the application's icon programmatically does not require any special permissions.  This behavior has been seen in the BankBot/Spy Banker family of malware.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: sunny-stolen-credentials)(Citation: bankbot-spybanker)
        ~Android Package Manager Manipulation: Modify AndroidManifest.xml to remove the app from launcher using android.intent.category.LAUNCHER category
        ~Component Disabling: Dynamically disable activity components that would show icons
        ~Empty Icon Resource: Set empty or transparent icon resources to make the app invisible
        ~Hidden App Category: Register app under system or hidden categories to prevent launcher display
        ~Alias Activity Manipulation: Create and manipulate activity aliases to control icon visibility
        ~System UI Masking: Use system UI flags to hide app presence in recent apps list
        ~Alternative Launch Methods: Configure app to launch via other means while hiding main icon
      Download New Code at Runtime: Adversaries may download and execute dynamic code not included in the original application package after installation. This technique is primarily used to evade static analysis checks and pre-publication scans in official app stores. In some cases, more advanced dynamic or behavioral analysis techniques could detect this behavior. However, in conjunction with [Execution Guardrails](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627) techniques, detecting malicious code downloaded after installation could be difficult.  On Android, dynamic code could include native code, Dalvik code, or JavaScript code that utilizes Android WebView’s `JavascriptInterface` capability.   On iOS, dynamic code could be downloaded and executed through 3rd party libraries such as JSPatch. (Citation: FireEye-JSPatch)
        ~Android Dynamic Dex Loading: Download and load additional Dalvik executable code at runtime using DexClassLoader
        ~Android Native Library Loading: Download and load native libraries (.so files) dynamically using System.load()
        ~Android WebView JavaScript Interface: Execute downloaded JavaScript code through Android WebView's JavascriptInterface
        ~iOS Dynamic Framework Loading: Download and load additional frameworks at runtime
        ~iOS JavaScript Bridge: Execute downloaded JavaScript code through WebKit bridges
        ~Remote Configuration Loading: Download and interpret configuration files that contain executable code
        ~Dynamic Plugin Architecture: Load new functionality through a plugin system after installation
        ~Encrypted Code Downloads: Download encrypted code modules that are decrypted at runtime
        ~Code Injection via WebView: Inject and execute code through mobile browser components
        ~Hot Module Replacement: Replace existing code modules with new versions during runtime
        ~Cloud Storage Code Retrieval: Download code modules from cloud storage services like AWS S3 or Google Cloud Storage
        ~P2P Code Distribution: Retrieve code updates through peer-to-peer networks
        ~Custom Protocol Implementation: Use custom protocols for code delivery to bypass standard HTTP monitoring
        ~Dynamic Script Interpretation: Download and execute interpreted languages like Python or Lua
        ~Memory-Only Execution: Download code directly into memory without persistent storage
        ~Content Delivery Network (CDN) Usage: Leverage CDNs to distribute and download code updates
        ~Code Streaming: Download and execute code in chunks or streams rather than complete files
        ~Inter-Process Code Transfer: Download code in one process and transfer to another for execution
        ~Time-Based Code Retrieval: Download different code variants based on timing or scheduling
        ~Split Code Assembly: Download code fragments from multiple sources and assemble at runtime
        ~DNS Tunneling Code Delivery: Use DNS queries and responses to transfer code fragments
        ~Code Polymorphism: Download different variations of the same code functionality
        ~Social Media Channel Retrieval: Extract code from social media platforms' content or messages
        ~SMS-Based Code Retrieval: Download code triggered by SMS messages
        ~Push Notification Delivery: Use push notification services to trigger code downloads
        ~Deep Link Code Loading: Leverage mobile deep links to initiate code downloads
        ~Bluetooth Code Transfer: Download code through Bluetooth connections
        ~NFC-Based Code Retrieval: Use NFC communications for code transfer
        ~Geofenced Code Distribution: Download different code based on device location
        ~QR Code Triggered Downloads: Initiate code downloads through QR code scanning
      Hooking: Adversaries may utilize hooking to hide the presence of artifacts associated with their behaviors to evade detection. Hooking can be used to modify return values or data structures of system APIs and function calls. This process typically involves using 3rd party root frameworks, such as Xposed or Magisk, with either a system exploit or pre-existing root access. By including custom modules for root frameworks, adversaries can hook system APIs and alter the return value and/or system data structures to alter functionality/visibility of various aspects of the system.
        ~System API Hooking: Modifying system API calls to hide malicious activities or alter system behavior using frameworks like Xposed
        ~Runtime Method Hooking: Intercepting and modifying method calls during application runtime to bypass security checks
        ~Native Library Hooking: Hooking native libraries to intercept low-level system calls and modify their behavior
        ~Security Framework Hooking: Targeting security-related APIs to bypass built-in security mechanisms
        ~Anti-Debug Detection Hooking: Modifying debug detection mechanisms to prevent analysis
        ~Root Detection Bypass: Hooking root detection APIs to hide root status from applications
        ~SSL/TLS Validation Hooking: Intercepting certificate validation calls to bypass SSL pinning
        ~Storage Access Hooking: Modifying storage access APIs to hide malicious files or data
        ~Process Monitoring Hooking: Altering process monitoring APIs to hide malicious processes
        ~Permission Check Hooking: Bypassing permission checks by hooking permission validation APIs
        ~UI Component Hooking: Modifying UI-related APIs to hide malicious overlays or prevent security alerts
        ~Biometric Validation Hooking: Intercepting biometric authentication APIs to bypass security checks
        ~Package Manager Hooking: Modifying package manager calls to hide malicious app components
        ~Sensor Data Hooking: Intercepting sensor APIs to provide fake data and hide malicious behavior
        ~IPC Mechanism Hooking: Modifying inter-process communication APIs to hide malicious data exchange
        ~Memory Protection Hooking: Bypassing memory protection mechanisms through API hooking
        ~App Lifecycle Hooking: Intercepting application lifecycle methods to hide malicious activities
        ~Network Stack Hooking: Modifying network stack APIs to hide suspicious traffic patterns
        ~Keychain/Credential Storage Hooking: Intercepting secure storage APIs to bypass credential protection
        ~Device Admin Hooking: Modifying device administration APIs to bypass enterprise security policies
        ~Sandbox Isolation Hooking: Bypassing sandbox containment through API hooking
        ~Logging Framework Hooking: Modifying system logging APIs to prevent malicious activity recording
        ~SELinux Policy Hooking: Intercepting SELinux policy enforcement to bypass security constraints
        ~Secure Boot Validation Hooking: Modifying boot validation checks to hide system modifications
        ~Container Isolation Hooking: Bypassing app container isolation through API interception
      Modify System Partition: If an adversary can escalate privileges, he or she may be able to use those privileges to place malicious code in the device system partition, where it may persist after device resets and may not be easily removed by the device user.  Many Android devices provide the ability to unlock the bootloader for development purposes. An unlocked bootloader may provide the ability for an adversary to modify the system partition. Even if the bootloader is locked, it may be possible for an adversary to escalate privileges and then modify the system partition.
        ~Bootloader Unlock: Exploit device bootloader unlocking mechanisms to gain write access to system partition
        ~Root Exploitation: Use root exploits to gain privileged access for system partition modification
        ~System Update Tampering: Intercept and modify system updates to inject malicious code into system partition
        ~Recovery Mode Manipulation: Abuse recovery mode features to modify system partition contents
        ~Custom ROM Installation: Replace legitimate system partition with modified custom ROM
        ~System File Injection: Insert malicious files directly into system partition through elevated privileges
        ~Partition Table Modification: Alter partition tables to gain unauthorized access to system partition
        ~OTA Update Chain: Compromise over-the-air update mechanism to modify system partition
        ~Fastboot Interface Abuse: Exploit fastboot interface vulnerabilities to write to system partition
        ~System Mount Manipulation: Modify mount points and permissions to gain write access to system partition
        ~Firmware Update Manipulation: Modify firmware update packages to inject malicious code into system partition
        ~Debug Interface Exploitation: Use hardware debug interfaces (like JTAG) to directly modify system partition
        ~Backup/Restore Chain: Compromise backup/restore mechanisms to inject malicious content into system partition
        ~EDL Mode Abuse: Exploit Emergency Download Mode to modify system partition
        ~A/B Partition Switching: Manipulate A/B system partition switching mechanism for persistence
      Obfuscated Files or Information: Adversaries may attempt to make a payload or file difficult to discover or analyze by encrypting, encoding, or otherwise obfuscating its contents on the device or in transit. This is common behavior that can be used across different platforms and the network to evade defenses.    Payloads may be compressed, archived, or encrypted in order to avoid detection. These payloads may be used during Initial Access or later to mitigate detection. Portions of files can also be encoded to hide the plaintext strings that would otherwise help defenders with discovery. Payloads may also be split into separate, seemingly benign files that only reveal malicious functionality when reassembled.(Citation: Microsoft MalLockerB)
        Software Packing: Adversaries may perform software packing to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory.   Utilities used to perform software packing are called packers. An example packer is FTT. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.
          Use UPX packer to compress Android APK files while maintaining their functionality
          Apply ProGuard for Android app code obfuscation and size reduction
          Implement custom encryption routines in native code (JNI) to pack Android app components
          Use DexGuard commercial packer for advanced Android app protection
          Employ multi-layer packing combining compression and encryption for iOS apps
          Implement dynamic library loading with packed contents in iOS applications
          Use third-party commercial packers like Qihoo 360 for Android apps
          Apply custom ARM assembly-level packing techniques for iOS executables
          Implement time-based unpacking triggers to evade dynamic analysis
          Use string encryption and resource packing tools like StringFog
          Use Bangcle packer for Android app protection and anti-reverse engineering
          Implement LLVM-based obfuscation with custom packing routines
          Apply Alibaba's ADAM packer for Android applications
          Use TencentProtector for comprehensive app packing and protection
          Implement segmented packing with runtime decryption for iOS apps
          Use APKProtect for Android app packing and protection
          Apply ShieldSquare packer with anti-tampering features
          Implement memory-only unpacking routines using mmap/mprotect
          Use ijiami packer for Chinese market distribution
          Apply multi-stage unpacking with environmental checks
          Use SecNeo packer with virtualization-based protection
          Implement selective packing of native libraries with custom headers
          Apply AppSolid cloud-based packing service
          Use Baidu Protected App Service for app packing
          Implement real-time integrity checking with packed verification code
          Use NQ Shield packer with anti-root detection
          Apply dynamic code loading with encrypted DEX files
          Implement native code packing with anti-hooking protection
          Use AppGuard with whitebox cryptography protection
          Apply shell-script based packing for app resources
        Steganography: Adversaries may use steganography techniques in order to prevent the detection of hidden information. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digital media such as images, audio tracks, video clips, or text files.
          Use LSB (Least Significant Bit) steganography to embed malicious code within image files stored in mobile gallery apps
          Encode payload within metadata fields of mobile media files like EXIF data in photos
