          Hide payload in mobile app cache files disguised as legitimate cached content
          Embed malicious code in custom mobile wallpapers or themes
          Use video steganography in short mobile video clips or stories
          Hide data in QR codes that appear legitimate but contain additional encoded layers
          Hide data within mobile app backup files disguised as legitimate backup content
          Embed malicious code in mobile device screenshot artifacts
          Use steganography in mobile keyboard custom themes or layouts
          Conceal data within mobile contact card files (vCard format)
          Hide payloads in mobile device diagnostic logs or crash reports
          Embed data in mobile calendar event attachments
          Use steganography in mobile app widget graphics
          Hide data within mobile device health/fitness tracking data files
          Conceal payloads in mobile device system sounds or haptic feedback patterns
          Embed malicious content in mobile game assets or save files
          Hide data within mobile device motion sensor reading patterns
          Embed payloads in mobile app push notification payload structures
          Conceal data in mobile device clipboard history data
          Use steganography in mobile instant messaging sticker packs during transit
          Hide malicious code in mobile app deep link parameters
          Embed data within mobile device voice assistant custom commands
          Conceal payloads in mobile app advertisement cache files
          Hide data in mobile device sharing sheet metadata
          Use steganography in mobile payment QR code additional data fields
          Embed malicious content in mobile app runtime configuration files
          Hide data within mobile secure enclave storage area metadata
          Embed payloads in mobile app permission request dialogs
          Conceal data in mobile device biometric authentication templates
          Use steganography in mobile app intent/URL scheme parameters
          Hide malicious code in mobile device trusted anchor certificates
          Embed data within mobile app sandbox isolation boundaries
          Conceal payloads in mobile device keychain entries
          Hide data in mobile app shared storage provider metadata
          Use steganography in mobile device enterprise profile data
          Embed malicious content in mobile app WebView cache structures
      Abuse Accessibility Features: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417), [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), and [Input Prompt](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1411) where appropriate.**  A malicious app could abuse Android's accessibility features to capture sensitive data or perform other malicious actions.(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android to emulate a user's clicks, for example to steal money from a user's bank account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: banking-trojans-google-play)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android devices to evade defenses by repeatedly clicking the "Back" button when a targeted app manager or mobile security app is launched, or when strings suggesting uninstallation are detected in the foreground. This effectively prevents the malicious application from being uninstalled.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)
        ~Back Button Manipulation: Abuse accessibility services to programmatically trigger back button presses to prevent uninstallation or escape security screens
        ~Click Emulation: Use accessibility services to simulate user touch inputs and clicks to perform unauthorized actions
        ~Screen Content Monitoring: Leverage accessibility services to read screen content and capture sensitive information
        ~Gesture Injection: Abuse accessibility features to inject complex touch gestures and navigation patterns
        ~Accessibility Service Persistence: Maintain persistence by repeatedly re-enabling disabled accessibility services
        ~UI Element Manipulation: Modify or interact with UI elements through accessibility APIs to bypass security controls
        ~Keyboard Event Interception: Capture keyboard events through accessibility services to collect sensitive data
        ~Dialog Dismissal: Automatically dismiss security warnings or consent dialogs using accessibility features
        ~Screen Reader Abuse: Exploit screen reader functionality to gather information about on-screen content
        ~Accessibility Settings Modification: Manipulate accessibility-related device settings to maintain control
        ~Voice Command Interception: Abuse accessibility services to intercept and manipulate voice commands or TalkBack features
        ~Accessibility Service Chaining: Chain multiple accessibility services together to perform complex unauthorized operations
        ~Overlay Injection: Use accessibility features to create and manipulate overlay windows to hide malicious activity
        ~Auto-fill Abuse: Exploit accessibility features to manipulate auto-fill functionality and capture credentials
        ~Notification Access Abuse: Leverage accessibility services to read and interact with system notifications
      Uninstall Malicious Application: Adversaries may include functionality in malware that uninstalls the malicious application from the device. This can be achieved by:  * Abusing device owner permissions to perform silent uninstallation using device owner API calls. * Abusing root permissions to delete files from the filesystem. * Abusing the accessibility service. This requires an intent be sent to the system to request uninstallation, and then abusing the accessibility service to click the proper places on the screen to confirm uninstallation.
        ~Device Owner API Abuse: Adversaries abuse device administrator or device owner APIs to silently uninstall applications without user interaction or notification
        ~Root Permission Exploitation: Adversaries leverage root access to directly remove application files and associated data from protected system directories
        ~Accessibility Service Manipulation: Adversaries abuse accessibility services to programmatically interact with uninstallation dialogs and confirm application removal
        ~Package Manager Exploitation: Adversaries directly interface with the package manager to force application removal while bypassing normal uninstallation procedures
        ~File System Cleanup: Adversaries manually delete application files, cache, and data directories to remove traces of malicious activity
        ~Silent Background Uninstallation: Adversaries implement background services that monitor for specific conditions and trigger automatic self-uninstallation
        ~Intent Interception: Adversaries intercept and modify system intents related to application uninstallation to hide the process from users and security tools
      Install Insecure or Malicious Configuration: An adversary could attempt to install insecure or malicious configuration settings on the mobile device, through means such as phishing emails or text messages either directly containing the configuration settings as an attachment, or containing a web link to the configuration settings. The device user may be tricked into installing the configuration settings through social engineering techniques (Citation: Symantec-iOSProfile).  For example, an unwanted Certification Authority (CA) certificate could be placed in the device's trusted certificate store, increasing the device's susceptibility to adversary-in-the-middle network attacks seeking to eavesdrop on or manipulate the device's network communication ([Eavesdrop on Insecure Network Communication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1439) and [Manipulate Device Communication](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1463)).  On iOS, malicious Configuration Profiles could contain unwanted Certification Authority (CA) certificates or other insecure settings such as unwanted proxy server or VPN settings to route the device's network traffic through an adversary's system. The device could also potentially be enrolled into a malicious Mobile Device Management (MDM) system (Citation: Talos-MDM).
        ~Malicious CA Certificate Installation: Installing unauthorized Certificate Authority certificates to enable man-in-the-middle attacks and compromise secure communications
        ~Malicious MDM Enrollment: Tricking users into enrolling their devices in unauthorized Mobile Device Management systems
        ~Malicious VPN Configuration: Installing compromised VPN settings to route traffic through adversary-controlled servers
        ~Malicious Proxy Settings: Configuring devices to use malicious proxy servers for network traffic interception
        ~Insecure System Settings: Modifying system-level security settings to weaken device security posture
        ~Configuration Profile Abuse: Exploiting iOS configuration profiles to deploy malicious settings
        ~Android Enterprise Policy Manipulation: Abusing Android enterprise policy features to deploy harmful configurations
        ~Network Configuration Tampering: Modifying network-related settings to enable traffic monitoring or redirection
      Geofencing: Adversaries may use a device’s geographical location to limit certain malicious behaviors. For example, malware operators may limit the distribution of a second stage payload to certain geographic regions.(Citation: Lookout eSurv)  [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1581) is accomplished by persuading the user to grant the application permission to access location services. The application can then collect, process, and exfiltrate the device’s location to perform location-based actions, such as ceasing malicious behavior or showing region-specific advertisements.  One method to accomplish [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1581) on Android is to use the built-in Geofencing API to automatically trigger certain behaviors when the device enters or exits a specified radius around a geographical location. Similar to other [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1581) methods, this requires that the user has granted the `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` and `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` permissions. The latter is only required if the application targets Android 10 (API level 29) or higher. However, Android 11 introduced additional permission controls that may restrict background location collection based on user permission choices at runtime. These additional controls include “Allow only while using the app”, which will effectively prohibit background location collection.(Citation: Android Geofencing API)  Similarly, on iOS, developers can use built-in APIs to setup and execute geofencing. Depending on the use case, the app will either need to call `requestWhenInUseAuthorization()` or `requestAlwaysAuthorization()`, depending on when access to the location services is required. Similar to Android, users also have the option to limit when the application can access the device’s location, including one-time use and only when the application is running in the foreground.(Citation: Apple Location Services)  [Geofencing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1581) can be used to prevent exposure of capabilities in environments that are not intended to be compromised or operated within. For example, location data could be used to limit malware spread and/or capabilities, which could also potentially evade application analysis environments (ex: malware analysis outside of the target geographic area). Other malicious usages could include showing language-specific [Input Prompt](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1411)s and/or advertisements.
        ~GPS-based Restrictions: Using GPS coordinates to determine device location and restrict malware behavior
        ~Network Location-based Restrictions: Using cellular network or WiFi location data to determine device location
        ~Time Zone Verification: Using device time zone settings to infer location and restrict behavior
        ~Region-specific App Store Validation: Checking app store region settings to validate target location
        ~IP Geolocation Verification: Using IP address geolocation to verify target region
        ~Language Settings Detection: Using device language and regional settings as location indicators
        ~Cellular Carrier Verification: Checking mobile carrier information to validate target region
        ~Location Permission Management: Managing and manipulating location permission requests to avoid suspicion
        ~Dynamic Location Monitoring: Continuously monitoring location changes to adapt malicious behavior
        ~Location Spoofing Detection: Implementing checks to detect and respond to location spoofing attempts
        ~Location History Analysis: Using device location history to validate long-term presence in target region
        ~Geofence Polygon Mapping: Using complex geographical boundaries instead of simple radius-based restrictions
        ~Platform-specific Location Services: Leveraging unique location APIs of different mobile platforms
        ~Location Data Correlation: Cross-referencing multiple location indicators to improve accuracy
        ~Location-based Feature Activation: Gradually enabling features based on confirmed location patterns
      Subvert Trust Controls: Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted applications. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust. Examples of such features include: an app being allowed to run because it is signed by a valid code signing certificate; an OS prompt alerting the user that an app came from an untrusted source; or getting an indication that you are about to connect to an untrusted site. The method adversaries use will depend on the specific mechanism they seek to subvert.
        Code Signing Policy Modification: Adversaries may modify code signing policies to enable execution of applications signed with unofficial or unknown keys. Code signing provides a level of authenticity on an app from a developer, guaranteeing that the program has not been tampered with and comes from an official source. Security controls can include enforcement mechanisms to ensure that only valid, signed code can be run on a device.   Mobile devices generally enable these security controls by default, such as preventing the installation of unknown applications on Android. Adversaries may modify these policies in a number of ways, including [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) or malicious configuration profiles.
          Enable "Developer Options" and "USB Debugging" on Android to allow installation of unsigned apps
          Install malicious MDM profiles on iOS devices to override code signing requirements
          Use Android Debug Bridge (ADB) commands to modify device_policy_manager settings
          Exploit enterprise distribution certificates to sign malicious apps with seemingly legitimate credentials
          Deploy fake CA certificates to trust unauthorized signing authorities
          Modify Android manifest settings to bypass signature verification
          Use iOS enterprise certificate abuse to distribute unauthorized apps
          Inject malicious code into legitimately signed apps while preserving original signatures
          Exploit Android rooting tools to disable signature verification
          Create custom Android device administrator policies to modify security settings
          Modify Android SELinux policies to bypass signature verification checks
          Exploit iOS profile installation via Safari to deploy unauthorized configuration profiles
          Use Android package manager (pm) commands to disable signature verification
          Create custom provisioning profiles with modified entitlements on iOS
          Leverage Android Enterprise APIs to modify security policies across device fleet
          Exploit iOS simulator environment to test and deploy unsigned applications
          Modify Android boot verification to allow custom-signed system images
          Use enterprise app sideloading features to bypass normal installation restrictions
          Deploy compromised certificate authority in corporate PKI infrastructure
          Modify Android vendor-specific security policies through system properties
          Exploit Knox security policies on Samsung devices to modify signature verification
          Manipulate iOS device supervision settings through Apple Configurator
          Use Android OEM unlocking to modify system signature verification
          Exploit mobile device management (MDM) API vulnerabilities to bypass signing requirements
          Modify Android verified boot parameters to allow custom signatures
          Deploy compromised intermediate certificates in mobile device trust store
          Exploit iOS developer enterprise program for unauthorized app distribution
          Modify Android platform keys through custom ROM installation
          Manipulate mobile device firmware update signatures
          Exploit mobile carrier APN settings to deploy modified security policies
          Exploit Android for Work profile isolation to bypass signature verification
          Modify iOS TestFlight distribution to bypass App Store signing requirements
          Use Android emulator environment to test and deploy modified signing policies
          Manipulate corporate VPN profiles to deploy modified trust settings
          Exploit dual-space/parallel-apps features to bypass signature verification
          Modify device enrollment program (DEP) settings for iOS devices
          Exploit carrier-branded Android device policy modifications
          Use Android Enterprise Dedicated device provisioning to modify signing policies
          Manipulate cross-signed certificates in mobile device trust chains
          Exploit mobile device virtualization layer security policies
      Delete Device Data: Adversaries may wipe a device or delete individual files in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. An application must have administrator access to fully wipe the device, while individual files may not require special permissions to delete depending on their storage location. (Citation: Android DevicePolicyManager 2019)  Stored data could include a variety of file formats, such as Office files, databases, stored emails, and custom file formats. The impact file deletion will have depends on the type of data as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary, but can include deleting update files to evade detection or deleting attacker-specified files for impact.
        ~Full Device Wipe: Adversaries may use device administration APIs to perform a complete factory reset of the device to remove all user data and applications
        ~Selective File Deletion: Adversaries may delete specific files or directories to hide traces of malicious activity while maintaining device functionality
        ~Cache Clearing: Adversaries may clear application caches and temporary files to remove evidence of their activities
        ~Media File Removal: Adversaries may specifically target and delete media files (photos, videos, audio) to prevent data recovery
        ~App Data Deletion: Adversaries may delete application-specific data and databases to disrupt functionality or hide traces
        ~System Log Deletion: Adversaries may delete system logs and diagnostic files to remove evidence of their activities
        ~Secure File Shredding: Adversaries may use secure deletion techniques that overwrite data multiple times to prevent forensic recovery
        ~Background Data Cleanup: Adversaries may delete background application data and cached content periodically to avoid detection
      Masquerade as Legitimate Application: An adversary could distribute developed malware by masquerading the malware as a legitimate application. This can be done in two different ways: by embedding the malware in a legitimate application, or by pretending to be a legitimate application.  Embedding the malware in a legitimate application is done by downloading the application, disassembling it, adding the malicious code, and then re-assembling it.(Citation: Zhou) The app would appear to be the original app, but would contain additional malicious functionality. The adversary could then publish the malicious application to app stores or use another delivery method.  Pretending to be a legitimate application relies heavily on lack of scrutinization by the user. Typically, a malicious app pretending to be a legitimate one will have many similar details as the legitimate one, such as name, icon, and description.(Citation: Palo Alto HenBox)  Malicious applications may also masquerade as legitimate applications when requesting access to the accessibility service in order to appear as legitimate to the user, increasing the likelihood that the access will be granted.
        ~Repackaging Legitimate Apps: Modifying legitimate applications by decompiling, adding malicious code, and recompiling them while maintaining original appearance and functionality
        ~Icon and Name Spoofing: Creating malicious apps that mimic the visual identity of legitimate apps through similar or identical icons and names
        ~Store Description Mimicry: Copying or closely imitating the app store descriptions, screenshots, and metadata of legitimate applications
        ~Accessibility Service Masquerading: Disguising malicious apps as legitimate accessibility services or tools
        ~System App Impersonation: Creating apps that appear to be legitimate system applications or utilities
        ~SDK Spoofing: Masquerading malicious code as legitimate third-party SDKs or development frameworks
        ~Popular App Cloning: Creating exact copies of popular applications with embedded malicious functionality
        ~Update Masquerading: Presenting malicious apps as updates or plugins for legitimate applications
      Evade Analysis Environment: Malicious applications may attempt to detect their operating environment prior to fully executing their payloads. These checks are often used to ensure the application is not running within an analysis environment such as a sandbox used for application vetting, security research, or reverse engineering.  Adversaries may use many different checks such as physical sensors, location, and system properties to fingerprint emulators and sandbox environments.(Citation: Talos Gustuff Apr 2019)(Citation: ThreatFabric Cerberus)(Citation: Xiao-ZergHelper)(Citation: Cyberscoop Evade Analysis January 2019) Adversaries may access `android.os.SystemProperties` via Java reflection to obtain specific system information.(Citation: Github Anti-emulator) Standard values such as phone number, IMEI, IMSI, device IDs, and device drivers may be checked against default signatures of common sandboxes.(Citation: Sophos Anti-emulation)
        ~Physical Sensor Detection: Checks for presence and behavior of device sensors (accelerometer, gyroscope, etc.) that are often improperly emulated in analysis environments
        ~Location Verification: Validates device location data and GPS functionality which may be absent or static in emulators
        ~System Property Analysis: Examines system properties, build parameters, and device characteristics for emulator signatures
        ~Hardware Component Verification: Checks for presence and responses from real hardware components often missing in emulators
        ~Performance Analysis: Measures execution timing and performance characteristics that differ between real devices and emulated environments
        ~Network Environment Analysis: Examines network configurations and connectivity patterns typical of analysis environments
        ~Device ID Verification: Validates device identifiers (IMEI, IMSI, phone numbers) against known emulator defaults
        ~API Response Analysis: Monitors responses from system APIs that might behave differently in analysis environments
        ~File System Analysis: Checks for artifacts and directory structures common to analysis environments
        ~Emulator-Specific Detection: Looks for specific artifacts or behaviors unique to common Android emulators
        ~User Behavior Analysis: Detects artificial or automated user interactions typical of analysis environments
        ~Debug Flag Detection: Identifies debugging flags and developer options that might indicate analysis
        ~Virtual Environment Detection: Checks for indicators of virtualization or containerization
        ~Runtime Library Analysis: Examines loaded libraries and runtime environment characteristics
        ~Memory Analysis: Checks memory patterns and allocations that differ in analysis environments
        ~Process Analysis: Monitors running processes and services for analysis tools
        ~Time Manipulation Detection: Identifies attempts to manipulate device time common in analysis
        ~Root Detection: Checks for root indicators that might suggest analysis environment
        ~Debugger Detection: Identifies attached debuggers or analysis tools
        ~Instrumentation Detection: Detects code instrumentation and hooks used for analysis
        ~Storage Analysis: Examines storage characteristics and patterns that differ in analysis environments
        ~Dynamic Code Loading Detection: Identifies restrictions on dynamic code execution in analysis environments
        ~Security Product Detection: Checks for presence of security analysis tools and monitoring software
        ~Resource Quota Analysis: Verifies resource limitations that might indicate sandboxed environment
        ~Permission Analysis: Examines permission patterns and restrictions typical of analysis environments
        ~Certificate Validation: Checks for analysis environment certificates and signing patterns
        ~UI Layer Analysis: Detects artificial or automated UI layers common in analysis tools
        ~System Call Analysis: Monitors system call patterns that differ in analysis environments
        ~Inter-Process Communication Analysis: Examines IPC patterns typical of analysis environments
        ~Background Service Detection: Identifies analysis-related background services and monitors
      Input Injection: A malicious application can inject input to the user interface to mimic user interaction through the abuse of Android's accessibility APIs.  [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) can be achieved using any of the following methods:  * Mimicking user clicks on the screen, for example to steal money from a user's PayPal account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa) * Injecting global actions, such as `GLOBAL_ACTION_BACK` (programatically mimicking a physical back button press), to trigger actions on behalf of the user.(Citation: Talos Gustuff Apr 2019) * Inserting input into text fields on behalf of the user. This method is used legitimately to auto-fill text fields by applications such as password managers.(Citation: bitwarden autofill logins)
        ~Touch Event Injection: Inject artificial touch events to simulate screen taps and gestures using accessibility or input simulation APIs
        ~Text Field Manipulation: Programmatically insert or modify text in input fields without user interaction
        ~Global Action Simulation: Inject system-level actions like back button, home button, or recent apps navigation
        ~Gesture Pattern Injection: Simulate complex touch patterns and gestures like swipes, pinches, or custom patterns
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Misuse legitimate accessibility services to perform unauthorized input operations
        ~UI Automation Framework Exploitation: Abuse test automation frameworks to inject input events
        ~Input Method Editor (IME) Manipulation: Exploit custom keyboard or IME functionality to inject unauthorized input
        ~Sensor Input Simulation: Inject fake sensor data to trigger motion-based or sensor-dependent actions
      Device Lockout: An adversary may seek to lock the legitimate user out of the device, for example to inhibit user interaction or to obtain a ransom payment.  On Android versions prior to 7, apps can abuse Device Administrator access to reset the device lock passcode to prevent the user from unlocking the device. After Android 7, only device or profile owners (e.g. MDMs) can reset the device’s passcode.(Citation: Android resetPassword)  On iOS devices, this technique does not work because mobile device management servers can only remove the screen lock passcode, they cannot set a new passcode. However, on jailbroken devices, malware has been discovered that can lock the user out of the device.(Citation: Xiao-KeyRaider)
        ~Android Device Administrator Abuse: Exploiting Device Administrator privileges on Android devices prior to version 7 to forcibly reset device passcodes
        ~MDM Profile Manipulation: Abusing Mobile Device Management profiles to modify device lock settings
        ~System UI Overlay: Creating persistent overlays that prevent access to device unlock interfaces
        ~Bootloader Lock Manipulation: Modifying bootloader states to prevent normal device startup and access
        ~Jailbreak Lock Exploitation: Leveraging jailbroken device vulnerabilities to implement device locks
        ~Encryption-Based Lockout: Encrypting device contents and withholding decryption keys
        ~Biometric System Interference: Tampering with biometric authentication systems to prevent legitimate unlocking
        ~Recovery Mode Prevention: Blocking access to device recovery modes to maintain lockout state
      Impair Defenses: Adversaries may maliciously modify components of a victim environment in order to hinder or disable defensive mechanisms. This not only involves impairing preventative defenses, such as anti-virus, but also detection capabilities that defenders can use to audit activity and identify malicious behavior. This may span both native defenses as well as supplemental capabilities installed by users or mobile endpoint administrators.
        Disable or Modify Tools: Adversaries may disable security tools to avoid potential detection of their tools and activities. This can take the form of disabling security software, modifying SELinux configuration, or other methods to interfere with security tools scanning or reporting information. This is typically done by abusing device administrator permissions or using system exploits to gain root access to the device to modify protected system files.
          Use root exploits like DirtyCow to modify SELinux policy files and disable security enforcement
          Abuse Android Device Administrator API to programmatically disable security applications
          Modify system partition to remove or corrupt antivirus application components
          Use package manager commands to forcefully disable security applications without user interaction
          Exploit vulnerabilities in MDM solutions to bypass security policy enforcement
          Modify system files to prevent security tools from starting during boot
          Use root access to modify application sandbox permissions and bypass security restrictions
          Inject code into security application processes to disable their monitoring capabilities
          Modify system logging configurations to prevent security tools from receiving events
          Use kernel exploits to disable security module loading at runtime
          Exploit Samsung KNOX vulnerabilities to disable security container isolation
          Modify Android manifest files to revoke security tool permissions
          Use ADB commands with elevated privileges to disable security services
          Manipulate Android Work Profile policies to bypass enterprise security controls
          Exploit security tool auto-update mechanisms to inject malicious components
          Modify security tool configuration files to whitelist malicious activities
          Use symbolic links to redirect security tool resources to invalid locations
          Exploit shared library loading to inject modified security components
          Manipulate Android Security Provider to disable certificate validation
          Use intent hijacking to intercept and block security tool communications
        Prevent Application Removal: Adversaries may abuse the Android device administration API to prevent the user from uninstalling a target application. In earlier versions of Android, device administrator applications needed their administration capabilities explicitly deactivated by the user before the application could be uninstalled. This was later updated so the user could deactivate and uninstall the administrator application in one step.  Adversaries may also abuse the device accessibility APIs to prevent removal. This set of APIs allows the application to perform certain actions on behalf of the user and programmatically determine what is being shown on the screen. The malicious application could monitor the device screen for certain modals (e.g., the confirmation modal to uninstall an application) and inject screen input or a back button tap to close the modal. For example, Android's `performGlobalAction(int)` API could be utilized to prevent the user from removing the malicious application from the device after installation. If the user wants to uninstall the malicious application, two cases may occur, both preventing the user from removing the application.  * Case 1: If the integer argument passed to the API call is `2` or `GLOBAL_ACTION_HOME`, the malicious application may direct the user to the home screen from settings screen   * Case 2: If the integer argument passed to the API call is `1` or `GLOBAL_ACTION_BACK`, the malicious application may emulate the back press event
          Request Device Admin privileges during installation and use DeviceAdminReceiver to block uninstallation attempts
          Implement AccessibilityService to monitor Settings screens and inject GLOBAL_ACTION_HOME when uninstall dialog appears
          Use AccessibilityService to detect and automatically dismiss uninstall confirmation dialogs
          Register multiple device admin components to require multiple deactivation steps
          Implement screen overlay to block access to application settings and uninstall buttons
          Monitor package removal broadcasts and restart the application before uninstallation completes
          Create persistent background service to re-enable admin rights if disabled
          Use AccessibilityService to programmatically navigate away from Device Administrator settings
          Implement multiple accessibility services that monitor and protect each other from being disabled
          Use device policy manager to disable settings access that could lead to app removal
          Abuse Android Enterprise APIs to enforce app as required work profile application
          Register as device owner using TestDPC to prevent removal
          Exploit vendor-specific MDM APIs (e.g., Samsung KNOX) to lock application
          Use JobScheduler API to persistently monitor and re-enable protection mechanisms
          Create multiple alias activities that reinstall the app if removal is attempted
          Abuse Package Visibility APIs to detect and interfere with system package manager
          Implement screen pinning to prevent access to settings/uninstall functions
          Use root privileges (if available) to modify system settings and protect against removal
          Register as system app through privilege escalation to prevent normal uninstallation
          Create dependencies with other installed apps to prevent individual removal
          Implement fake system update UI that activates when uninstall attempted
          Create fake security warning screens claiming removal would compromise device
          Register as device's default launcher to maintain control over UI navigation
          Abuse Android backup mechanisms to restore app after removal
          Exploit shared user ID mechanism to gain elevated permissions
          Use dynamic code loading to hide removal prevention logic from initial scan
          Implement cross-app communication channels to restore removed components
          Register multiple broadcast receivers to monitor and respond to removal attempts
          Use native code (NDK) to implement harder-to-detect removal prevention
          Abuse Android's account authentication system to prevent removal of "system account" linked app
          Exploit vendor-specific gesture controls to intercept uninstall actions
          Abuse split APK mechanism to distribute removal prevention components
          Use WorkManager API to schedule periodic protection checks across reboots
          Implement custom IME (Input Method Editor) to intercept uninstall confirmations
          Abuse Android's notification system to prevent access to uninstall notifications
          Create timing-based race conditions during uninstall process
          Exploit Android's sharing framework to maintain persistence
          Use foreground service priority to prevent system from killing protection components
          Implement custom WebView component to intercept browser-based uninstall attempts
          Abuse Android's VPN API to monitor and block uninstall-related network traffic
        Device Lockout: An adversary may seek to inhibit user interaction by locking the legitimate user out of the device. This is typically accomplished by requesting device administrator permissions and then locking the screen using `DevicePolicyManager.lockNow()`. Other novel techniques for locking the user out of the device have been observed, such as showing a persistent overlay, using carefully crafted “call” notification screens, and locking HTML pages in the foreground. These techniques can be very difficult to get around, and typically require booting the device into safe mode to uninstall the malware.(Citation: Microsoft MalLockerB)(Citation: Talos GPlayed)(Citation: securelist rotexy 2018)  Prior to Android 7, device administrators were able to reset the device lock passcode to prevent the user from unlocking the device. The release of Android 7 introduced updates that only allow device or profile owners (e.g. MDMs) to reset the device’s passcode.(Citation: Android resetPassword)
          Request device admin permissions through social engineering prompts masquerading as system updates
          Use DevicePolicyManager.lockNow() API call after gaining device admin rights
          Create full-screen overlay with FLAG_NOT_TOUCHABLE and FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE flags
          Implement fake incoming call screen that cannot be dismissed
          Force browser to load persistent HTML page with JavaScript preventing navigation
          Exploit legacy Android (<7) resetPassword() API to change device passcode
          Create multiple overlapping transparent activities to block user interaction
          Abuse accessibility services to prevent user from accessing settings
          Use persistent notifications with maximum priority that cannot be dismissed
          Chain multiple pop-up windows that reappear when dismissed
          Abuse Android Device Owner provisioning to gain enhanced lockout capabilities
          Exploit Android Work Profile to create locked managed profile
          Use WindowManager to create system-alert-window overlays with maximum z-index
          Register multiple broadcast receivers to intercept and block unlock attempts
          Implement device admin activation persistence through device policy controller
          Create overlays that specifically target security settings and uninstall screens
          Chain together multiple activity lifecycle callbacks to prevent task switching
          Abuse toast messages with infinite duration to create partial screen blocks
          Implement foreground service with persistent notification that reinstates lockout
          Register screen-off receivers to immediately reactivate lockout after unlock attempts
          Exploit vendor-specific MDM APIs on Samsung Knox platform for enhanced lockout
          Abuse Android backup/restore mechanism to persist lockout after factory reset
          Encrypt user data and combine with screen lock to create ransomware-style lockout
          Block physical buttons through input event injection
          Manipulate display brightness and auto-rotation to enhance lockout effectiveness
          Abuse picture-in-picture mode to create persistent floating windows
          Chain VPN service with lockout to prevent network-based removal
          Exploit Android Enterprise features to enforce device restrictions
          Create fake biometric prompt overlays that trigger lockout on authentication attempts
          Implement watchdog service to monitor and restart killed lockout components
      Modify Trusted Execution Environment: If an adversary can escalate privileges, he or she may be able to use those privileges to place malicious code in the device's Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) or other similar isolated execution environment where the code can evade detection, may persist after device resets, and may not be removable by the device user. Running code within the TEE may provide an adversary with the ability to monitor or tamper with overall device behavior.(Citation: Roth-Rootkits)
        ~TEE Firmware Modification: Modifying the firmware of the TEE to introduce malicious code or backdoors
        ~Secure Boot Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in secure boot process to load unauthorized code into TEE
        ~Memory Protection Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities to bypass TEE memory isolation mechanisms
        ~TEE API Abuse: Misusing legitimate TEE APIs to execute malicious operations
        ~Secure Monitor Compromise: Tampering with secure monitor code that manages transitions between normal and secure worlds
        ~eFuse Manipulation: Modifying hardware fuses that control TEE security settings
        ~TrustZone Cache Attacks: Exploiting shared cache between secure and non-secure worlds
        ~Secure Storage Tampering: Compromising secure storage mechanisms used by TEE
        ~TEE Driver Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in TEE device drivers
        ~Rollback Protection Bypass: Circumventing version control mechanisms that prevent downgrade attacks
        ~Side-Channel Timing Attacks: Exploiting timing differences in TEE operations to extract sensitive information
        ~TEE Debug Interface Abuse: Exploiting debug interfaces left enabled in production devices
        ~Secure World Privilege Escalation: Exploiting vulnerabilities to escalate privileges within the TEE
        ~TEE Communication Channel Hijacking: Intercepting or manipulating communication between normal world and TEE
        ~Cryptographic Implementation Attacks: Exploiting weaknesses in TEE cryptographic implementations
        ~TEE Resource Exhaustion: Depleting TEE resources to cause denial of service or bypass security checks
        ~Trusted Application Manipulation: Modifying or replacing legitimate trusted applications within TEE
        ~TEE Configuration Tampering: Modifying security-critical TEE configuration parameters
        ~TEE Power State Attacks: Exploiting power state transitions to bypass security controls
        ~Secure Interrupt Handler Exploitation: Manipulating secure interrupt handlers to gain unauthorized access
        ~TEE Initialization Sequence Attacks: Exploiting vulnerabilities during TEE initialization
        ~Hardware Fault Injection: Using physical fault injection techniques to compromise TEE security
        ~Cross-World Data Leakage: Exploiting shared resources between secure and normal worlds to leak data
      Virtualization Solution: Adversaries may carry out malicious operations using virtualization solutions to escape from Android sandboxes and to avoid detection. Android uses sandboxes to separate resources and code execution between applications and the operating system.(Citation: Android Application Sandbox) There are a few virtualization solutions available on Android, such as the Android Virtualization Framework (AVF).(Citation: Android AVF Overview)       Through virtualization solutions, adversaries may execute malicious operations without user knowledge. For example, adversaries may mimic a legitimate banking application’s functionalities in a virtual environment, thanks to the virtualization solution, while malicious code captures credentials.
        ~AVF Exploitation: Abuse Android Virtualization Framework to run malicious code in isolated environments while appearing legitimate
        ~Virtual Machine Overlay: Create virtual environments that overlay legitimate apps to intercept user interactions
        ~Sandbox Escape via Virtualization: Leverage virtualization capabilities to break out of application sandboxes
        ~Hidden Virtual Instances: Run multiple virtual instances of malicious apps while concealing their presence
        ~Virtual Environment Mimicry: Create virtual environments that closely mimic legitimate app behaviors to avoid detection
        ~Cross-VM Data Theft: Extract sensitive data across virtual machine boundaries
        ~Virtual Resource Manipulation: Manipulate virtual resource allocation to hide malicious activities
        ~VM-based Credential Harvesting: Use virtualized environments to capture credentials while masquerading as legitimate apps
      Masquerading: Adversaries may attempt to manipulate features of their artifacts to make them appear legitimate or benign to users and/or security tools. Masquerading occurs when the name, location, or appearance of an object, legitimate or malicious, is manipulated or abused for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may include manipulating file metadata, tricking users into misidentifying the file type, and giving legitimate task or service names.  Renaming abusable system utilities to evade security monitoring is also a form of [Masquerading](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1655)
        Match Legitimate Name or Location: Adversaries may match or approximate the name or location of legitimate files or resources when naming/placing them. This is done for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may be done by giving artifacts the name and icon of a legitimate, trusted application (i.e., Settings), or using a package name that matches legitimate, trusted applications (i.e., `com.google.android.gm`).   Adversaries may also use the same icon of the file or application they are trying to mimic.
          Create malware with package name "com.android.settings" to masquerade as system Settings app
          Clone legitimate banking app's icon and package structure to create lookalike malicious app
          Use Android Asset Studio to recreate exact copies of legitimate app icons
          Place malicious APK files in /system/app directory to appear as pre-installed system apps
          Copy legitimate app's manifest file to replicate permissions and package structure
          Use dex2jar and apktool to decompile legitimate apps and copy their resources
          Register similar-looking Unicode domain names for phishing app distribution
          Create malware using common system process names like "system_update" or "security_patch"
          Modify APK metadata to match legitimate app's version info and certificates
          Use homograph attacks in app names to create visually identical package names
          Use localization files to display legitimate app names in different languages while hiding malicious code
          Modify file timestamps to match legitimate system app installation dates
          Create fake app store listings with identical branding and screenshots as legitimate apps
          Use legitimate app's signing key hash in metadata while using a different actual signature
          Place malicious code in cache directories used by legitimate apps
          Abuse Android's split APK mechanism to hide malicious code in seemingly legitimate app bundles
          Create lookalike widgets that mimic legitimate app functionality
          Use similar package names across different vendor namespaces (com.samsung.* vs com.sec.*)
          Exploit enterprise deployment certificates to appear as legitimate corporate apps
          Clone legitimate app's data directory structure to maintain persistence
          Abuse iOS URL schemes to intercept legitimate app deep links
          Create lookalike iOS configuration profiles mimicking MDM solutions
          Use legitimate app's background fetch identifiers to mask malicious activities
          Clone iOS app extensions to appear as legitimate sharing or widget components
          Modify .plist files to display legitimate app names in system settings
          Abuse iOS universal links to redirect to malicious components
          Create fake iOS app clips masquerading as legitimate quick-access features
          Use legitimate app's keychain access groups in entitlements
          Clone notification service extension identifiers of legitimate apps
          Masquerade as system framework bundles using similar bundle identifiers
      Application Versioning: An adversary may push an update to a previously benign application to add malicious code. This can be accomplished by pushing an initially benign, functional application to a trusted application store, such as the Google Play Store or the Apple App Store. This allows the adversary to establish a trusted userbase that may grant permissions to the application prior to the introduction of malicious code. Then, an application update could be pushed to introduce malicious code.(Citation: android_app_breaking_bad)  This technique could also be accomplished by compromising a developer’s account. This would allow an adversary to take advantage of an existing userbase without having to establish the userbase themselves.
        ~Play Store Update Manipulation: Exploiting Google Play Store's update mechanism to push malicious code updates to existing benign applications
        ~App Store Certificate Abuse: Misusing iOS developer certificates to push malicious updates through Apple's App Store
        ~Developer Account Compromise: Taking over legitimate developer accounts through credential theft or social engineering to push malicious updates
        ~Gradual Permission Escalation: Incrementally requesting additional permissions through updates to avoid suspicion
        ~Dynamic Code Loading: Using update mechanisms to load malicious code modules after installation
        ~Third-party Store Abuse: Exploiting alternative app stores with weaker security controls to distribute malicious updates
        ~Update Server Compromise: Manipulating application update servers to distribute compromised versions
        ~Version Number Spoofing: Manipulating version numbers to force updates or bypass version checks
    credential-access: The adversary is trying to steal account names, passwords, or other secrets that enable access to resources.  Credential access represents techniques that can be used by adversaries to obtain access to or control over passwords, tokens, cryptographic keys, or other values that could be used by an adversary to gain unauthorized access to resources. Credential access allows the adversary to assume the identity of an account, with all of that account's permissions on the system and network, and makes it harder for defenders to detect the adversary. With sufficient access within a network, an adversary can create accounts for later use within the environment.
      Input Capture: Adversaries may use methods of capturing user input to obtain credentials or collect information. During normal device usage, users often provide credentials to various locations, such as login pages/portals or system dialog boxes. Input capture mechanisms may be transparent to the user (e.g. [Keylogging](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417/001)) or rely on deceiving the user into providing input into what they believe to be a genuine application prompt (e.g. [GUI Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417/002)).
        Keylogging: Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials or other information from the user as the user types them.  Some methods of keylogging include:  * Masquerading as a legitimate third-party keyboard to record user keystrokes.(Citation: Zeltser-Keyboard) On both Android and iOS, users must explicitly authorize the use of third-party keyboard apps. Users should be advised to use extreme caution before granting this authorization when it is requested. * Abusing accessibility features. On Android, adversaries may abuse accessibility features to record keystrokes by registering an `AccessibilityService` class, overriding the `onAccessibilityEvent` method, and listening for the `AccessibilityEvent.TYPE_VIEW_TEXT_CHANGED` event type. The event object passed into the function will contain the data that the user typed.  *Additional methods of keylogging may be possible if root access is available.
          Deploy malicious keyboard app "SwiftKey Clone" masquerading as legitimate SwiftKey keyboard to capture keystrokes
          Create malicious keyboard app that requests minimal permissions initially but later requests keyboard access
          Implement AccessibilityService named "AndroidAssist" to monitor TYPE_VIEW_TEXT_CHANGED events
          Create background service that registers accessibility listener for all text input fields
          Use root access to hook into input method framework (IMF) system service
          Install modified system keyboard through root exploit to capture keystrokes
          Deploy keyboard app that exfiltrates logged keystrokes through encrypted C2 channel
          Create accessibility service masquerading as screen reader for visually impaired
          Hook Gboard process using root access to intercept keystrokes before encryption
          Modify Android Input Method Editor (IME) system files with root access to log all keyboard inputs
          Deploy enterprise-signed keyboard through MDM to bypass App Store restrictions
          Create keyboard app that offers unique language support to entice installation
          Bundle keylogger within legitimate-looking productivity app using iOS custom keyboard extension
          Abuse iOS TextField delegation methods through jailbreak to capture keystrokes
          Deploy keyboard that only activates logging after passing App Store review
          Create accessibility service that specifically targets password fields and secure input
          Use work profile on Android to deploy "corporate" keyboard with logging capabilities
          Implement selective keylogging that only captures specific apps' inputs to avoid detection
          Create keyboard that stores logs in encrypted container until exfiltration opportunity
          Deploy keyboard that uses OCR on keyboard area to capture keystrokes as backup method
          Implement kernel-level hooking on rooted devices to intercept input events before they reach IME
          Create keyboard that splits logging functionality across multiple processes to evade detection
          Deploy keyboard that only logs during specific time windows to avoid detection
          Use Android Debug Bridge (ADB) on rooted devices to capture input events directly
          Modify vendor-specific keyboard frameworks (Samsung, Huawei) to implement logging
          Create keyboard that uses side-channel monitoring of touch events as backup logging method
          Implement distributed logging across multiple accessibility services to avoid detection
          Use machine learning to selectively capture high-value keystrokes while ignoring others
          Deploy keyboard that piggybacks on legitimate keyboard's prediction engine to capture input
          Create hybrid keyboard that switches between accessibility and direct input monitoring based on context
          Abuse cloud-sync features of legitimate keyboards to exfiltrate keystroke data
          Exploit keyboard backup/restore functionality to retrieve logged keystrokes
          Use screen overlay to create invisible input capture layer above keyboard
          Implement keyboard that exploits input method switching to maintain persistence
          Create keyboard that leverages predictive text databases for passive logging
          Deploy keyboard that captures input through gesture typing motion tracking
          Exploit keyboard auto-correction database to reconstruct typed text
          Use keyboard theme engine modifications to implement hidden logging
          Implement cross-device keyboard sync feature to expand logging scope
          Create keyboard that exploits shared clipboard monitoring for additional capture
        GUI Input Capture: Adversaries may mimic common operating system GUI components to prompt users for sensitive information with a seemingly legitimate prompt. The operating system and installed applications often have legitimate needs to prompt the user for sensitive information such as account credentials, bank account information, or Personally Identifiable Information (PII). Compared to traditional PCs, the constrained display size of mobile devices may impair the ability to provide users with contextual information, making users more susceptible to this technique’s use.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)  There are several approaches adversaries may use to mimic this functionality. Adversaries may impersonate the identity of a legitimate application (e.g. use the same application name and/or icon) and, when installed on the device, may prompt the user for sensitive information.(Citation: eset-finance) Adversaries may also send fake device notifications to the user that may trigger the display of an input prompt when clicked.(Citation: Group IB Gustuff Mar 2019)   Additionally, adversaries may display a prompt on top of a running, legitimate application to trick users into entering sensitive information into a malicious application rather than the legitimate application. Typically, adversaries need to know when the targeted application and the individual activity within the targeted application is running in the foreground to display the prompt at the proper time. Adversaries can abuse Android’s accessibility features to determine which application is currently in the foreground.(Citation: ThreatFabric Cerberus) Two known approaches to displaying a prompt include:  * Adversaries start a new activity on top of a running legitimate application.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)(Citation: Hassell-ExploitingAndroid) Android 10 places new restrictions on the ability for an application to start a new activity on top of another application, which may make it more difficult for adversaries to utilize this technique.(Citation: Android Background) * Adversaries create an application overlay window on top of a running legitimate application. Applications must hold the `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission to create overlay windows. This permission is handled differently than typical Android permissions and, at least under certain conditions, is automatically granted to applications installed from the Google Play Store.(Citation: Cloak and Dagger)(Citation: NowSecure Android Overlay)(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility) The `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission and its associated ability to create application overlay windows are expected to be deprecated in a future release of Android in favor of a new API.(Citation: XDA Bubbles)
