      Network Traffic Capture or Redirection: An adversary may capture network traffic to and from the device to obtain credentials or other sensitive data, or redirect network traffic to flow through an adversary-controlled gateway to do the same.  A malicious app could register itself as a VPN client on Android or iOS to gain access to network packets. However, on both platforms, the user must grant consent to the app to act as a VPN client, and on iOS the app requires a special entitlement that must be granted by Apple.  Alternatively, if a malicious app is able to escalate operating system privileges, it may be able to use those privileges to gain access to network traffic.  An adversary could redirect network traffic to an adversary-controlled gateway by establishing a VPN connection or by manipulating the device's proxy settings. For example, Skycure (Citation: Skycure-Profiles) describes the ability to redirect network traffic by installing a malicious iOS Configuration Profile.  If applications encrypt their network traffic, sensitive data may not be accessible to an adversary, depending on the point of capture.
        ~Malicious VPN Client: Register application as VPN client to intercept network traffic by requesting user consent for VPN permissions
        ~Proxy Settings Manipulation: Modify device proxy settings to redirect traffic through adversary-controlled servers
        ~Configuration Profile Abuse: Install malicious configuration profiles to redirect network traffic or establish malicious VPN connections
        ~Root/Jailbreak Traffic Capture: Leverage elevated privileges on rooted/jailbroken devices to capture network traffic
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Gateway: Set up malicious network gateway to intercept traffic from targeted devices
        ~DNS Hijacking: Modify DNS settings to redirect traffic through adversary-controlled servers
        ~SSL/TLS Interception: Install malicious certificates to intercept encrypted traffic
        ~ARP Spoofing: Use ARP spoofing techniques to intercept local network traffic
      Access Notifications: Adversaries may collect data within notifications sent by the operating system or other applications. Notifications may contain sensitive data such as one-time authentication codes sent over SMS, email, or other mediums. In the case of Credential Access, adversaries may attempt to intercept one-time code sent to the device. Adversaries can also dismiss notifications to prevent the user from noticing that the notification has arrived and can trigger action buttons contained within notifications.(Citation: ESET 2FA Bypass)
        ~Notification Listener Service Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Android's NotificationListenerService to intercept notifications containing sensitive information
        ~Accessibility Service Exploitation: Malware may abuse accessibility services to read or dismiss notifications containing authentication codes
        ~Overlay Attack on Notifications: Adversaries may use screen overlays to intercept or modify how notifications are displayed
        ~Notification API Hooking: Attackers may hook into notification-related APIs to intercept or modify notification content
        ~Background Service Monitoring: Malware may run background services to continuously monitor and capture incoming notifications
        ~Notification Database Access: Adversaries may attempt to directly access notification storage or databases
        ~Screenshot Capture of Notifications: Malware may take screenshots when notifications appear to capture sensitive information
        ~Notification Center Manipulation: Attackers may manipulate the notification center to hide or modify legitimate notifications
        ~Push Notification Token Theft: Adversaries may steal push notification tokens to intercept notifications before they reach the device
        ~Notification Settings Modification: Malware may modify notification settings to redirect or duplicate notifications
        ~Cloud Notification Sync Interception: Adversaries may exploit notification syncing between devices through cloud services to capture sensitive information
        ~Notification Extension Abuse: Malware may abuse notification extension frameworks to intercept rich notifications
        ~MDM Notification Redirection: Attackers may abuse Mobile Device Management systems to redirect notifications
        ~Custom Notification Channel Exploitation: Adversaries may target custom notification channels used by enterprise apps
        ~Notification Backup Extraction: Malware may extract notification data from device backups or logs
        ~Silent Notification Interception: Adversaries may capture silent/background notifications containing sensitive data
        ~Notification Group Access: Attackers may exploit notification grouping features to access bundled sensitive information
      Steal Application Access Token: Adversaries can steal user application access tokens as a means of acquiring credentials to access remote systems and resources. This can occur through social engineering or URI hijacking and typically requires user action to grant access, such as through a system “Open With” dialogue.    Application access tokens are used to make authorized API requests on behalf of a user and are commonly used as a way to access resources in cloud-based applications and software-as-a-service (SaaS).(Citation: Auth0 - Why You Should Always Use Access Tokens to Secure APIs Sept 2019) OAuth is one commonly implemented framework used to issue tokens to users for access to systems. An application desiring access to cloud-based services or protected APIs can gain entry through OAuth 2.0 using a variety of authorization protocols. An example of a commonly-used sequence is Microsoft's Authorization Code Grant flow.(Citation: Microsoft Identity Platform Protocols May 2019)(Citation: Microsoft - OAuth Code Authorization flow - June 2019) An OAuth access token enables a third-party application to interact with resources containing user data in the ways requested without requiring user credentials.
        URI Hijacking: Adversaries may register Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) to intercept sensitive data.   Applications regularly register URIs with the operating system to act as a response handler for various actions, such as logging into an app using an external account via single sign-on. This allows redirections to that specific URI to be intercepted by the application. If an adversary were to register for a URI that was already in use by a genuine application, the adversary may be able to intercept data intended for the genuine application or perform a phishing attack against the genuine application. Intercepted data may include OAuth authorization codes or tokens that could be used by the adversary to gain access to protected resources.(Citation: Trend Micro iOS URL Hijacking)(Citation: IETF-PKCE)
          Register a malicious app with similar URI scheme to target app (e.g., "legitapp://" vs "legittapp://") to hijack OAuth redirects
          Create lookalike app with identical URI scheme before legitimate app is published to claim the scheme first
          Exploit case-sensitive URI handling in mobile OS to register variant of legitimate URI scheme
          Deploy phishing app that registers to handle common SSO authentication URI schemes
          Modify mobile app manifest to declare intent filters matching target app's authentication URIs
          Register custom URL schemes that match common OAuth redirect URIs used by target services
          Exploit URI scheme conflicts in mobile OS to override legitimate app's URI handler
          Create malware that monitors and intercepts URL launches for targeted authentication flows
          Register broad URI patterns using wildcards to capture various authentication callbacks
          Deploy repackaged version of legitimate app with modified URI handlers to intercept tokens
          Exploit deep linking vulnerabilities in Android apps by registering matching app links
          Use automated tools to scan and identify unregistered URI schemes of popular apps
          Deploy fake browser extensions that intercept OAuth redirect URIs
          Modify system hosts file to redirect OAuth callback domains to attacker-controlled server
          Create universal links on iOS that override app-registered schemes
          Register URI handlers for commonly mistyped versions of legitimate authentication endpoints
          Implement dynamic URI scheme generation to bypass scheme blocklists
          Create malicious app that claims multiple related URI schemes to increase success rate
      Capture SMS Messages: A malicious application could capture sensitive data sent via SMS, including authentication credentials. SMS is frequently used to transmit codes used for multi-factor authentication.  On Android, a malicious application must request and obtain permission (either at app install time or run time) in order to receive SMS messages. Alternatively, a malicious application could attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to bypass the permission requirement.  On iOS, applications cannot access SMS messages in normal operation, so an adversary would need to attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to potentially be able to access SMS messages.
        ~Android Runtime Permission Abuse: Exploiting Android's runtime permission system to gain SMS access by deceiving users into granting permissions
        ~SMS Gateway Interception: Intercepting SMS messages by compromising or spoofing SMS gateway services
        ~SIM Card Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SIM cards to intercept SMS messages before they reach the device
        ~OS Privilege Escalation: Exploiting operating system vulnerabilities to gain unauthorized access to SMS messages
        ~SMS Backup Extraction: Accessing SMS messages through backup files or cloud synchronization services
        ~Malicious Keyboard Implementation: Using malicious keyboard apps to capture SMS messages as they are viewed
        ~SMS Forwarding Configuration: Configuring SMS forwarding rules through compromised device settings
        ~Network Provider Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in mobile network provider infrastructure to intercept SMS
        ~SMS Database Access: Directly accessing SMS database files through elevated privileges
        ~Message Notification Sniffing: Capturing SMS content through notification system exploitation
        ~Fake SMS App Overlay: Using malicious overlay screens to capture SMS content when legitimate SMS apps are opened
        ~MDM Profile Abuse: Exploiting Mobile Device Management profiles to gain SMS access
        ~SMS Sync App Exploitation: Compromising third-party SMS sync applications that have legitimate SMS access
        ~Alternative Messaging App Hijacking: Exploiting multi-platform messaging apps that have SMS capabilities
        ~Recovery Mode Exploitation: Accessing SMS messages through device recovery mode vulnerabilities
        ~Carrier Message Portal Exploitation: Accessing SMS through compromised carrier web portals or apps
        ~Dual-SIM Interception: Exploiting dual-SIM functionality to capture messages intended for the primary SIM
        ~Emergency Message System Abuse: Exploiting emergency message handling systems to intercept SMS
        ~SMS-to-Email Bridge Exploitation: Compromising services that forward SMS to email
      URI Hijacking: Adversaries may register Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) to intercept sensitive data.  Applications regularly register URIs with the operating system to act as a response handler for various actions, such as logging into an app using an external account via single sign-on. This allows redirections to that specific URI to be intercepted by the application. If a malicious application were to register for a URI that was already in use by a genuine application, the malicious application may be able to intercept data intended for the genuine application or perform a phishing attack against the genuine application. Intercepted data may include OAuth authorization codes or tokens that could be used by the malicious application to gain access to resources.(Citation: Trend Micro iOS URL Hijacking)(Citation: IETF-PKCE)
        ~OAuth Flow Interception: Adversaries register URIs to intercept OAuth authentication flows, capturing authorization codes and tokens during the redirect process
        ~Custom Scheme Hijacking: Adversaries register custom URL schemes that conflict with legitimate apps to intercept deep links and associated credentials
        ~Universal Links Spoofing: Adversaries manipulate Apple's Universal Links or Android App Links to redirect users to malicious apps instead of legitimate ones
        ~SSO Credential Capture: Adversaries intercept Single Sign-On (SSO) redirects by registering competing URI handlers
        ~Deep Link Manipulation: Adversaries exploit deep linking mechanisms to intercept app-to-app communication containing authentication data
        ~Login Form Injection: Adversaries register URIs that trigger malicious login forms overlaying legitimate apps to capture credentials
        ~Browser URI Handler Exploitation: Adversaries exploit browser-to-app communication channels to intercept credentials during web authentication flows
        ~Intent Filter Manipulation: Adversaries modify Android intent filters to intercept URIs meant for legitimate apps
        ~Associated Domain Hijacking: Adversaries exploit expired or misconfigured associated domains to take control of URI handling
        ~URI Parameter Tampering: Adversaries modify URI parameters during interception to manipulate authentication flows while maintaining appearance of legitimacy
      Access Sensitive Data in Device Logs: On versions of Android prior to 4.1, an adversary may use a malicious application that holds the READ_LOGS permission to obtain private keys, passwords, other credentials, or other sensitive data stored in the device's system log. On Android 4.1 and later, an adversary would need to attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to be able to access the log.
        ~System Log Access via READ_LOGS Permission: Exploiting pre-Android 4.1 systems by requesting READ_LOGS permission to directly access system logs containing credentials and sensitive data
        ~Logcat Command Exploitation: Using debug bridge connections to execute logcat commands and extract log data containing sensitive information
        ~Log File Directory Access: Directly accessing log file directories after achieving root access through privilege escalation
        ~Custom Logger Interception: Intercepting and capturing data from custom application loggers that may contain sensitive information
        ~Crash Log Analysis: Extracting sensitive data from crash logs that might contain memory dumps with credentials
        ~Debug Log Exploitation: Accessing debug logs that may contain detailed system information and credentials during development or testing modes
      Credentials from Password Store: Adversaries may search common password storage locations to obtain user credentials. Passwords can be stored in several places on a device, depending on the operating system or application holding the credentials. There are also specific applications that store passwords to make it easier for users to manage and maintain. Once credentials are obtained, they can be used to perform lateral movement and access restricted information.
        Keychain: Adversaries may collect keychain data from an iOS device to acquire credentials. Keychains are the built-in way for iOS to keep track of users' passwords and credentials for many services and features such as Wi-Fi passwords, websites, secure notes, certificates, private keys, and VPN credentials.   On the device, the keychain database is stored outside of application sandboxes to prevent unauthorized access to the raw data. Standard iOS APIs allow applications access to their own keychain contained within the database. By utilizing a privilege escalation exploit or existing root access, adversaries can access the entire encrypted database.(Citation: Apple Keychain Services)(Citation: Elcomsoft Decrypt Keychain)
