      Clipboard Data: Adversaries may abuse clipboard manager APIs to obtain sensitive information copied to the device clipboard. For example, passwords being copied and pasted from a password manager application could be captured by a malicious application installed on the device.(Citation: Fahl-Clipboard)      On Android, applications can use the `ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener()` API to register as a listener and monitor the clipboard for changes. However, starting in Android 10, this can only be used if the application is in the foreground, or is set as the device’s default input method editor (IME).(Citation: Github Capture Clipboard 2019)(Citation: Android 10 Privacy Changes)      On iOS, this can be accomplished by accessing the `UIPasteboard.general.string` field. However, starting in iOS 14, upon accessing the clipboard, the user will be shown a system notification if the accessed text originated in a different application. For example, if the user copies the text of an iMessage from the Messages application, the notification will read “application_name has pasted from Messages” when the text was pasted in a different application.(Citation: UIPPasteboard)
        ~Android Clipboard Monitoring: Adversaries may exploit Android's ClipboardManager API to monitor clipboard changes, particularly targeting devices running versions prior to Android 10 where background monitoring was possible.
        ~iOS Clipboard Access: Adversaries may access iOS clipboard data through UIPasteboard, potentially capturing sensitive information despite notification systems introduced in iOS 14.
        ~Input Method Editor (IME) Abuse: Adversaries may create malicious keyboard apps that register as the default IME to maintain persistent clipboard access on Android devices.
        ~Cross-App Clipboard Interception: Adversaries may exploit clipboard sharing between apps to capture data when it's transferred between trusted applications.
        ~Clipboard History Extraction: Adversaries may attempt to access clipboard history or cached clipboard data stored by the mobile operating system.
        ~Cloud Clipboard Sync Exploitation: Adversaries may intercept clipboard data by targeting cloud synchronization features that share clipboard content across devices.
        ~Third-Party Clipboard Manager Abuse: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in third-party clipboard management apps to gain access to stored clipboard history and content.
        ~Enterprise MDM Clipboard Access: Adversaries may abuse Mobile Device Management (MDM) policies and configurations to gain unauthorized access to clipboard data in enterprise environments.
        ~Clipboard Buffer Overflow Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit buffer overflow vulnerabilities in clipboard handling code to gain access to clipboard data or achieve privilege escalation.
      Abuse Accessibility Features: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417), [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), and [Input Prompt](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1411) where appropriate.**  A malicious app could abuse Android's accessibility features to capture sensitive data or perform other malicious actions.(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android to emulate a user's clicks, for example to steal money from a user's bank account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: banking-trojans-google-play)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android devices to evade defenses by repeatedly clicking the "Back" button when a targeted app manager or mobile security app is launched, or when strings suggesting uninstallation are detected in the foreground. This effectively prevents the malicious application from being uninstalled.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)
        ~Screen Content Capture: Abuse accessibility services to capture text and content displayed on screen, including sensitive information from other apps
        ~Keyboard Event Interception: Monitor and record keyboard inputs through accessibility services to capture credentials
        ~Click Event Simulation: Programmatically generate tap/click events to interact with banking or payment apps
        ~Back Button Automation: Automatically trigger back button presses to prevent security app usage or uninstallation
        ~Dialog Dismissal: Automatically dismiss security warnings or prompts using accessibility services
        ~Form Auto-fill Interception: Capture form data when auto-fill services are used to enter credentials
        ~Gesture Recording: Monitor and record gesture-based authentication patterns through accessibility services
        ~Clipboard Monitoring: Use accessibility services to monitor and capture clipboard content containing sensitive data
      Exploit TEE Vulnerability: A malicious app or other attack vector could be used to exploit vulnerabilities in code running within the Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) (Citation: Thomas-TrustZone). The adversary could then obtain privileges held by the TEE potentially including the ability to access cryptographic keys or other sensitive data (Citation: QualcommKeyMaster). Escalated operating system privileges may be first required in order to have the ability to attack the TEE (Citation: EkbergTEE). If not, privileges within the TEE can potentially be used to exploit the operating system (Citation: laginimaineb-TEE).
        ~Buffer Overflow in TEE Components: Exploit memory corruption vulnerabilities in TEE trusted applications or core components
        ~Secure Monitor Call Abuse: Exploit vulnerabilities in the interface between normal world and secure world
        ~Cryptographic Implementation Flaws: Target weaknesses in cryptographic implementations within TEE
        ~Trusted Application Exploitation: Attack vulnerabilities in vendor-specific trusted applications
        ~Side-Channel Attacks: Use timing, power analysis, or other side-channel methods to extract secrets from TEE
        ~TEE Boot Chain Compromise: Target vulnerabilities in TEE boot process or secure boot implementation
        ~Shared Memory Attacks: Exploit vulnerabilities in shared memory mechanisms between normal world and secure world
        ~Hardware Protocol Exploitation: Target vulnerabilities in hardware protocols used by TEE
      Input Prompt: The operating system and installed applications often have legitimate needs to prompt the user for sensitive information such as account credentials, bank account information, or Personally Identifiable Information (PII). Adversaries may mimic this functionality to prompt users for sensitive information.  Compared to traditional PCs, the constrained display size of mobile devices may impair the ability to provide users with contextual information, making users more susceptible to this technique’s use.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)  Specific approaches to this technique include:  ### Impersonate the identity of a legitimate application  A malicious application could impersonate the identity of a legitimate application (e.g. use the same application name and/or icon) and get installed on the device. The malicious app could then prompt the user for sensitive information.(Citation: eset-finance)  ### Display a prompt on top of a running legitimate application  A malicious application could display a prompt on top of a running legitimate application to trick users into entering sensitive information into the malicious application rather than the legitimate application. Typically, the malicious application would need to know when the targeted application (and individual activity within the targeted application) is running in the foreground, so that the malicious application knows when to display its prompt. Android 5.0 and 5.1.1, respectively, increased the difficulty of determining the current foreground application through modifications to the `ActivityManager` API.(Citation: Android-getRunningTasks)(Citation: StackOverflow-getRunningAppProcesses). A malicious application can still abuse Android’s accessibility features to determine which application is currently in the foreground.(Citation: ThreatFabric Cerberus) Approaches to display a prompt include:  * A malicious application could start a new activity on top of a running legitimate application.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)(Citation: Hassell-ExploitingAndroid) Android 10 places new restrictions on the ability for an application to start a new activity on top of another application, which may make it more difficult for adversaries to utilize this technique.(Citation: Android Background) * A malicious application could create an application overlay window on top of a running legitimate application. Applications must hold the `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission to create overlay windows. This permission is handled differently than typical Android permissions, and at least under certain conditions is automatically granted to applications installed from the Google Play Store.(Citation: Cloak and Dagger)(Citation: NowSecure Android Overlay)(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility) The `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission and its associated ability to create application overlay windows are expected to be deprecated in a future release of Android in favor of a new API.(Citation: XDA Bubbles)  ### Fake device notifications  A malicious application could send fake device notifications to the user. Clicking on the device notification could trigger the malicious application to display an input prompt.(Citation: Group IB Gustuff Mar 2019)
        ~Application Identity Spoofing - Mimicking legitimate app names, icons, and branding to deceive users into trusting malicious prompts
        ~Overlay Attack - Creating visual overlays on top of legitimate applications to intercept user input
        ~Notification Manipulation - Abusing system notifications to trigger malicious input prompts
        ~System Dialog Impersonation - Replicating system-level dialogs and permission requests
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse - Exploiting accessibility features to detect app states and inject prompts
        ~Activity Hijacking - Intercepting or replacing legitimate app activities with malicious ones
        ~Screen Lock Impersonation - Mimicking device lock screens to collect credentials
        ~Browser Popup Abuse - Creating fake browser-based authentication or payment prompts
        ~SMS Verification Spoofing - Impersonating SMS verification code request dialogs
        ~App Store Prompt Spoofing - Mimicking app store payment or authentication dialogs
        ~Biometric Prompt Spoofing - Mimicking system biometric authentication dialogs
        ~Network Connection Prompts - Fake WiFi or Bluetooth connection/authentication dialogs
        ~App Update Prompts - Malicious prompts masquerading as legitimate app update requests
        ~Cloud Backup Prompts - Fake cloud storage authentication or backup restoration dialogs
        ~Device Administrator Prompts - Spoofing device admin permission requests
        ~Security Alert Prompts - Fake security warning or antivirus alert dialogs
        ~Account Sync Prompts - Impersonating account synchronization requests
        ~In-App Purchase Spoofing - Fake payment or subscription dialog manipulation
        ~VPN Configuration Prompts - Fake VPN setup or connection authentication dialogs
        ~Certificate Installation Prompts - Spoofing security certificate installation dialogs
        ~Mobile Device Management Prompts - Impersonating enterprise MDM enrollment dialogs
        ~Password Auto-fill Prompts - Manipulating system password manager prompts
        ~App Permission Spoofing - Fake permission request dialogs for sensitive device features
        ~Emergency Alert Spoofing - Impersonating system emergency notification prompts
        ~File Access Prompts - Fake storage access or file sharing authorization dialogs
      URL Scheme Hijacking: An iOS application may be able to maliciously claim a URL scheme, allowing it to intercept calls that are meant for a different application(Citation: FireEye-Masque2)(Citation: Dhanjani-URLScheme). This technique, for example, could be used to capture OAuth authorization codes(Citation: IETF-PKCE) or to phish user credentials(Citation: MobileIron-XARA).
        ~OAuth Flow Interception: Register malicious app to intercept OAuth authentication flows by claiming legitimate apps' URL schemes, capturing authorization codes and tokens
        ~Login Form Hijacking: Intercept URL schemes used for login flows and present fake login forms to capture credentials
        ~Banking App Impersonation: Register URL schemes used by banking apps to intercept financial credentials and transaction authorizations
        ~SSO Authentication Hijacking: Target Single Sign-On authentication flows by intercepting URL schemes used for identity provider redirects
        ~Deep Link Manipulation: Manipulate deep linking mechanisms to redirect users to malicious versions of legitimate authentication flows
        ~Universal Links Abuse: Exploit universal links functionality to hijack URLs that should be handled by system browsers or legitimate apps
        ~Custom Protocol Handler Spoofing: Register custom protocol handlers that mimic legitimate protocols used for authentication
        ~App-to-App Communication Interception: Intercept inter-app communication channels that use URL schemes for credential exchange
        ~Clipboard Credential Capture: Combine URL scheme hijacking with clipboard monitoring to capture credentials when users copy-paste
        ~System Dialog Manipulation: Exploit system-level URL scheme handling dialogs to trick users into selecting malicious apps
      Keychain: Adversaries may collect the keychain storage data from an iOS device to acquire credentials. Keychains are the built-in way for iOS to keep track of users' passwords and credentials for many services and features such as Wi-Fi passwords, websites, secure notes, certificates, private keys, and VPN credentials.  On the device, the keychain database is stored outside of application sandboxes to prevent unauthorized access to the raw data. Standard iOS APIs allow applications access to their own keychain contained within the database. By utilizing a privilege escalation exploit or existing root access, an adversary can access the entire encrypted database.(Citation: Apple Keychain Services)(Citation: Elcomsoft Decrypt Keychain)
        ~Jailbreak Exploitation: Adversaries exploit iOS jailbreak vulnerabilities to gain root access and extract the keychain database
        ~Backup Interception: Adversaries intercept or access iOS device backups to extract encrypted keychain data
        ~iCloud Keychain Sync: Adversaries target iCloud keychain synchronization to access credentials across devices
        ~Keychain Access API Abuse: Adversaries abuse legitimate keychain access APIs through compromised or malicious apps
        ~Memory Dumping: Adversaries extract keychain credentials by dumping device memory during credential access operations
        ~Keychain Database Manipulation: Adversaries directly modify or query the keychain database through privileged access
        ~Enterprise Profile Abuse: Adversaries leverage managed device profiles to access keychain items
        ~Keychain Decryption Tools: Adversaries use specialized tools to decrypt extracted keychain data
    discovery: The adversary is trying to figure out your environment.  Discovery consists of techniques that allow the adversary to gain knowledge about the characteristics of the mobile device and potentially other networked systems. When adversaries gain access to a new system, they must orient themselves to what they now have control of and what benefits operating from that system give to their current objective or overall goals during the intrusion. The operating system may provide capabilities that aid in this post-compromise information-gathering phase.
      Network Service Scanning: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Methods to acquire this information include port scans and vulnerability scans from the mobile device. This technique may take advantage of the mobile device's access to an internal enterprise network either through local connectivity or through a Virtual Private Network (VPN).
        ~Port Scanning: Using mobile device's network capabilities to perform TCP/UDP port scans to identify running services on remote hosts
        ~Service Version Detection: Probing identified ports to determine specific service versions and potential vulnerabilities
        ~Bluetooth Service Discovery: Scanning for nearby Bluetooth services and devices accessible from the mobile device
        ~WiFi Network Service Enumeration: Scanning services specifically on WiFi networks the mobile device is connected to
        ~VPN-enabled Scanning: Leveraging VPN connections to scan internal network services from the mobile device
        ~Mobile App-based Scanning: Using legitimate or malicious mobile applications to perform network service discovery
        ~Protocol-specific Enumeration: Targeting specific protocols (HTTP, SMB, etc.) to identify services
        ~Passive Service Discovery: Monitoring network traffic to identify services without active scanning
      Device Type Discovery: On Android, device type information is accessible to apps through the android.os.Build class (Citation: Android-Build). Device information could be used to target privilege escalation exploits.
        ~System API Queries: Adversaries may use platform-specific APIs (like android.os.Build or iOS UIDevice) to gather device model, manufacturer, and hardware details
        ~Build Properties Analysis: Adversaries may read Android's build.prop file to extract detailed device specifications and system properties
        ~Filesystem Inspection: Adversaries may examine device-specific directories and files that reveal hardware information
        ~Hardware Feature Detection: Adversaries may probe for specific hardware capabilities (sensors, secure elements, etc.) to fingerprint device types
        ~Kernel Information Gathering: Adversaries may read kernel version and configuration information to determine device characteristics
        ~Device Settings Enumeration: Adversaries may query device settings and configurations that reveal model-specific information
        ~CPU Architecture Detection: Adversaries may determine the processor architecture to target architecture-specific exploits
        ~Vendor-Specific Identifiers: Adversaries may collect vendor-specific device identifiers and serial numbers
      File and Directory Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate files and directories or search in specific device locations for desired information within a filesystem. Adversaries may use the information from [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1420) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including deciding if the adversary should fully infect the target and/or attempt specific actions.   On Android, Linux file permissions and SELinux policies typically stringently restrict what can be accessed by apps without taking advantage of a privilege escalation exploit. The contents of the external storage directory are generally visible, which could present concerns if sensitive data is inappropriately stored there. iOS's security architecture generally restricts the ability to perform any type of [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1420) without use of escalated privileges.
        ~External Storage Access: Adversaries may enumerate files in external storage locations like SD cards or shared storage areas that have fewer access restrictions
        ~App Data Directory Scanning: Adversaries may attempt to scan application-specific directories to locate configuration files, databases, and cached data
        ~Media File Enumeration: Adversaries may specifically target media directories containing photos, videos, and audio files
        ~System Directory Reconnaissance: Adversaries may attempt to enumerate system directories to gather information about device configuration and installed components
        ~Backup File Discovery: Adversaries may search for backup files, logs, or temporary files that might contain sensitive information
        ~Document Storage Scanning: Adversaries may focus on locations where documents, PDFs, and other business files are commonly stored
        ~Database File Location Discovery: Adversaries may search for SQLite databases and other data storage files commonly used by mobile apps
        ~Cache Directory Analysis: Adversaries may enumerate cache directories to find temporary stored data that could contain sensitive information
      Evade Analysis Environment: Malicious applications may attempt to detect their operating environment prior to fully executing their payloads. These checks are often used to ensure the application is not running within an analysis environment such as a sandbox used for application vetting, security research, or reverse engineering.  Adversaries may use many different checks such as physical sensors, location, and system properties to fingerprint emulators and sandbox environments.(Citation: Talos Gustuff Apr 2019)(Citation: ThreatFabric Cerberus)(Citation: Xiao-ZergHelper)(Citation: Cyberscoop Evade Analysis January 2019) Adversaries may access `android.os.SystemProperties` via Java reflection to obtain specific system information.(Citation: Github Anti-emulator) Standard values such as phone number, IMEI, IMSI, device IDs, and device drivers may be checked against default signatures of common sandboxes.(Citation: Sophos Anti-emulation)
        ~System Property Analysis: Checking system properties, build properties, and OS parameters to identify emulator or sandbox environments
        ~Sensor-based Detection: Monitoring physical sensors like accelerometer, gyroscope, or light sensor for anomalies typical of emulated environments
        ~Device ID Verification: Checking device identifiers (IMEI, IMSI, phone numbers) against known sandbox defaults
        ~Hardware Component Detection: Verifying presence and behavior of expected hardware components and drivers
        ~Location Service Analysis: Detecting artificial or static location data typical of analysis environments
        ~Timing Analysis: Measuring execution time or system responses to detect debugging or analysis tools
        ~Network Environment Analysis: Checking network configurations and connectivity patterns typical of analysis setups
        ~API Behavior Analysis: Monitoring API responses and behaviors that differ between real devices and emulators
      System Network Configuration Discovery: Adversaries may look for details about the network configuration and settings, such as IP and/or MAC addresses, of devices they access or through information discovery of remote systems.   Adversaries may use the information from [System Network Configuration Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1422) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including determining certain access within the target network and what actions to do next.   On Android, details of onboard network interfaces are accessible to apps through the `java.net.NetworkInterface` class.(Citation: NetworkInterface) Previously, the Android `TelephonyManager` class could be used to gather telephony-related device identifiers, information such as the IMSI, IMEI, and phone number. However, starting with Android 10, only preloaded, carrier, the default SMS, or device and profile owner applications can access the telephony-related device identifiers.(Citation: TelephonyManager)      On iOS, gathering network configuration information is not possible without root access.      Adversaries may use the information from [System Network Configuration Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1422) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including determining certain access within the target network and what actions to do next.
        Internet Connection Discovery: Adversaries may check for Internet connectivity on compromised systems. This may be performed during automated discovery and can be accomplished in numerous ways such as using `adb shell netstat` for Android.(Citation: adb_commands)  Adversaries may use the results and responses from these requests to determine if the mobile devices are capable of communicating with adversary-owned C2 servers before attempting to connect to them. The results may also be used to identify routes, redirectors, and proxy servers.
          Use 'adb shell netstat' to check network connections and routing tables on Android devices
          Execute 'adb shell ping' to test connectivity to specific hosts or IP addresses
          Query Android ConnectivityManager API to check network state and capabilities
          Use iOS network-info command on jailbroken devices
          Monitor NetworkCallback events through Android NetworkRequest API
          Implement ping sweep using native Android network libraries
          Query Android WifiManager API to gather WiFi connection status
          Use tcpdump through adb to capture and analyze network traffic
          Execute traceroute commands through adb shell to map network paths
          Query Android TelephonyManager for cellular data connection status
          Query system settings provider for proxy configurations
          Check DNS resolution capability through nslookup commands
          Use Android NetworkCapabilities API to determine connection type (VPN/metered)
          Monitor broadcast receivers for connectivity changes
          Query default gateway information through 'ip route' command
          Use socket connections to test specific ports' accessibility
          Check system proxy settings through ProxyInfo API
          Query mobile network type (2G/3G/4G/5G) through telephony APIs
          Monitor NetworkInfo broadcasts for connection state changes
          Use ICMP echo requests through native libraries
        Wi-Fi Discovery: Adversaries may search for information about Wi-Fi networks, such as network names and passwords, on compromised systems. Adversaries may use Wi-Fi information as part of [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0032) or [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0031) activity to support both ongoing and future campaigns.
          Use Android WifiManager API to retrieve saved network configurations and SSID information
          Query Android's /data/misc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf file on rooted devices to extract Wi-Fi passwords
          Access iOS keychain on jailbroken devices to extract stored Wi-Fi credentials
          Exploit Android's WifiManager.getConfiguredNetworks() method to list configured networks
          Leverage Android's ACCESS_WIFI_STATE permission to gather nearby Wi-Fi network information
          Use iOS private APIs on jailbroken devices to access Wi-Fi configuration files
          Monitor Android broadcast receivers for WIFI_STATE_CHANGED events
          Query Android Settings.Secure provider for Wi-Fi configuration data
          Utilize Android's NetworkRequest API to monitor Wi-Fi network availability
          Access Android's WifiInfo class to gather current Wi-Fi connection details
          Use Termux on Android to run wireless network scanning tools like iwlist
          Extract Wi-Fi credentials from Android backup files using ADB
          Leverage MDM configurations to discover enterprise Wi-Fi settings
          Use Android's NetworkSpecifier API to perform targeted Wi-Fi scanning
          Monitor iOS NSNetworkAgent logs for Wi-Fi connection data
          Query Android Enterprise APIs for managed Wi-Fi configurations
          Extract Wi-Fi information from iOS configuration profiles
          Use Android's Network Service Discovery (NSD) API to discover network services
          Analyze iOS wireless diagnostics logs for Wi-Fi information
          Access Android's WifiNetworkSuggestion API to gather suggested networks
          Extract Wi-Fi data from mobile device memory dumps
          Parse cached Wi-Fi information from browser storage
          Access vendor-specific Wi-Fi configuration tools (Samsung, Huawei, etc.)
          Query cloud backup services for stored Wi-Fi configurations
          Analyze mobile device Wi-Fi firmware logs
          Extract Wi-Fi credentials from third-party VPN/security apps
          Access Wi-Fi information through mobile device management (MDM) APIs
          Parse Wi-Fi data from system crash reports and analytics
          Extract Wi-Fi configurations from app-specific storage
          Monitor Wi-Fi related IPC (Inter-Process Communication) traffic
          Monitor power consumption patterns to identify Wi-Fi activity
          Use automation frameworks (e.g., Frida) to hook Wi-Fi related functions
          Extract Wi-Fi information from system update packages
          Analyze shared preferences files from Wi-Fi related system apps
          Query Wi-Fi information through accessibility services
          Monitor Wi-Fi related system events through logcat
          Extract Wi-Fi configurations during device backup/restore processes
          Access Wi-Fi information through debug interfaces (ADB/LLDB)
          Analyze Wi-Fi related temporary files in cache directories
          Monitor Wi-Fi state changes through system content providers
      Process Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get information about running processes on a device. Information obtained could be used to gain an understanding of common software/applications running on devices within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Process Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1424) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.      Recent Android security enhancements have made it more difficult to obtain a list of running processes. On Android 7 and later, there is no way for an application to obtain the process list without abusing elevated privileges. This is due to the Android kernel utilizing the `hidepid` mount feature. Prior to Android 7, applications could utilize the `ps` command or examine the `/proc` directory on the device.(Citation: Android-SELinuxChanges)      In iOS, applications have previously been able to use the `sysctl` command to obtain a list of running processes. This functionality has been removed in later iOS versions.
        ~Android Process Enumeration: Adversaries may attempt to enumerate processes on Android devices using various methods including accessing /proc directory, ps command, or ActivityManager APIs, depending on the Android version and privilege level.
        ~iOS Process Inspection: Adversaries may attempt to gather process information on iOS devices through system APIs, sysctl commands, or other system interfaces, though capabilities are increasingly restricted in newer versions.
        ~Background Service Analysis: Adversaries may focus on discovering background services and daemons running on the device to identify potential security tools or opportunities for persistence.
        ~Memory-Based Process Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to identify running processes by analyzing memory usage patterns and system resource allocation.
        ~API-Based Process Enumeration: Adversaries may utilize legitimate platform APIs and frameworks to query process information while staying within normal application boundaries.
        ~Debug Interface Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse debugging interfaces and developer options to gather process information, particularly on rooted/jailbroken devices.
        ~System Log Analysis: Adversaries may parse system logs and diagnostic information to indirectly identify running processes and their behaviors.
        ~Third-Party App Integration: Adversaries may leverage legitimate third-party apps' capabilities and inter-process communication to gather information about running processes.
        ~Process Snapshot Analysis: Adversaries may attempt to capture and analyze process state information during system state changes or crashes.
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Adversaries may misuse accessibility services to gather information about running applications and processes.
      System Network Connections Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of network connections to or from the compromised device they are currently accessing or from remote systems by querying for information over the network.      This is typically accomplished by utilizing device APIs to collect information about nearby networks, such as Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and cellular tower connections. On Android, this can be done by querying the respective APIs:      * `WifiInfo` for information about the current Wi-Fi connection, as well as nearby Wi-Fi networks. Querying the `WiFiInfo` API requires the application to hold the `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` permission.   * `BluetoothAdapter` for information about Bluetooth devices, which also requires the application to hold several permissions granted by the user at runtime.   * For Android versions prior to Q, applications can use the `TelephonyManager.getNeighboringCellInfo()` method. For Q and later, applications can use the `TelephonyManager.getAllCellInfo()` method. Both methods require the application hold the `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` permission.
        ~WiFi Network Discovery: Adversaries may use WiFi-specific APIs and permissions to enumerate connected and nearby WiFi networks, including SSIDs, signal strength, and security configurations.
        ~Bluetooth Device Discovery: Adversaries may scan for and enumerate nearby Bluetooth devices, including paired devices and available services using Bluetooth-specific APIs.
        ~Cellular Network Discovery: Adversaries may query cellular network information including carrier details, cell tower information, and signal strength using telephony-related APIs.
        ~VPN Connection Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate active VPN connections and configurations to understand the device's network routing.
        ~Network Interface Enumeration: Adversaries may query system APIs to list all available network interfaces and their states.
        ~Tethering Status Discovery: Adversaries may detect if the device is currently sharing its network connection through hotspot or tethering features.
        ~Network Protocol Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate supported network protocols and active network services on the device.
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Adversaries may monitor network traffic patterns and connections using system APIs or network monitoring capabilities.
      System Information Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get detailed information about a device’s operating system and hardware, including versions, patches, and architecture. Adversaries may use the information from [System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1426) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not to fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.      On Android, much of this information is programmatically accessible to applications through the `android.os.Build` class. (Citation: Android-Build) iOS is much more restrictive with what information is visible to applications. Typically, applications will only be able to query the device model and which version of iOS it is running.
        ~API Query Enumeration: Adversaries may use various platform APIs like android.os.Build or iOS UIDevice to gather system information including OS version, device model, and hardware specifications.
        ~System Files Analysis: Adversaries may read system files and directories to gather detailed system information, particularly on rooted/jailbroken devices.
        ~Settings Provider Query: Adversaries may query system settings providers to gather configuration information and system parameters.
        ~Hardware Component Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate hardware components, capabilities, and specifications through system APIs or direct hardware access.
        ~System Configuration Inspection: Adversaries may examine system configuration files and settings to understand device setup and security posture.
        ~Runtime Environment Analysis: Adversaries may gather information about the runtime environment including virtual machine status, debugging mode, and emulator detection.
        ~System Services Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate running system services and daemons to understand system capabilities and potential vulnerabilities.
        ~Package Information Discovery: Adversaries may query package managers and app stores to gather information about installed applications and system components.
        ~Network Configuration Analysis: Adversaries may examine network interfaces, VPN configurations, and proxy settings to understand the device's network setup.
        ~Security Feature Detection: Adversaries may probe for security features like SELinux status, MDM enrollment, or root/jailbreak detection mechanisms.
        ~Storage Information Discovery: Adversaries may enumerate storage details including available space, encryption status, and external storage capabilities.
        ~Device Identifier Collection: Adversaries may gather unique device identifiers such as IMEI, serial numbers, and advertising identifiers.
        ~Sensor Capability Enumeration: Adversaries may probe for available sensors and their capabilities including GPS, biometric sensors, and other hardware features.
      Software Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of applications that are installed on a device. Adversaries may use the information from [Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1418) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not to fully infect the target and/or attempts specific actions.      Adversaries may attempt to enumerate applications for a variety of reasons, such as figuring out what security measures are present or to identify the presence of target applications.
        Security Software Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of security applications and configurations that are installed on a device. This may include things such as mobile security products. Adversaries may use the information from [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1418/001) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not to fully infect the target and/or attempt specific actions.
          Query Android package manager (pm) to list installed security applications
          Check for presence of Mobile Device Management (MDM) profiles on iOS
          Inspect iOS configuration profiles for security settings and restrictions
          Use Android ActivityManager to detect running security services
          Enumerate third-party antivirus applications using system APIs
          Check for root/jailbreak detection mechanisms
          Scan for enterprise security certificates in device keychain
          Query device policy controller status on Android
          Inspect firewall configurations and VPN profiles
          Check for presence of containerization solutions (Knox, Work Profile)
          Query iOS private frameworks to identify security restrictions
          Scan Android manifest files for security-related permissions
          Enumerate background security services using ps command
          Check for security-related system properties in Android
          Monitor system logs for security software signatures
          Query device administrator privileges and assignments
          Inspect network traffic filtering rules and security policies
          Check for secure boot and verified boot configurations
          Enumerate SELinux/sandbox policy configurations
          Scan for endpoint protection signatures in system directories
          Query iOS keychain access control lists for security app entitlements
          Inspect Android security provider configurations
          Enumerate custom security policy handlers
          Check for security-related broadcast receivers
          Scan for security-related dynamic library loads
          Query device attestation and verification states
          Inspect security-related system content providers
          Check for presence of anti-tampering mechanisms
          Enumerate security-related scheduled tasks and jobs
          Scan for custom security framework implementations
      Location Tracking: Adversaries may track a device’s physical location through use of standard operating system APIs via malicious or exploited applications on the compromised device.      On Android, applications holding the `ACCESS_COAURSE_LOCATION` or `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` permissions provide access to the device’s physical location. On Android 10 and up, declaration of the `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` permission in an application’s manifest will allow applications to request location access even when the application is running in the background.(Citation: Android Request Location Permissions) Some adversaries have utilized integration of Baidu map services to retrieve geographical location once the location access permissions had been obtained.(Citation: PaloAlto-SpyDealer)(Citation: Palo Alto HenBox)      On iOS, applications must include the `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`, `NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription`, and/or `NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription` keys in their `Info.plist` file depending on the extent of requested access to location information.(Citation: Apple Requesting Authorization for Location Services) On iOS 8.0 and up, applications call `requestWhenInUseAuthorization()` to request access to location information when the application is in use or `requestAlwaysAuthorization()` to request access to location information regardless of whether the application is in use. With elevated privileges, an adversary may be able to access location data without explicit user consent with the `com.apple.locationd.preauthorized` entitlement key.(Citation: Google Project Zero Insomnia)
        Impersonate SS7 Nodes: Adversaries may exploit the lack of authentication in signaling system network nodes to track the location of mobile devices by impersonating a node.(Citation: Engel-SS7)(Citation: Engel-SS7-2008)(Citation: 3GPP-Security)(Citation: Positive-SS7)(Citation: CSRIC5-WG10-FinalReport)      By providing the victim’s MSISDN (phone number) and impersonating network internal nodes to query subscriber information from other nodes, adversaries may use data collected from each hop to eventually determine the device’s geographical cell area or nearest cell tower.(Citation: Engel-SS7)
          Use SS7 exploitation frameworks like SigPloit to send forged SendRoutingInfoForSM (SRI-SM) messages
          Impersonate Mobile Switching Center (MSC) to query Home Location Register (HLR) for subscriber location
          Send fake AnyTimeInterrogation (ATI) messages to obtain target's serving cell information
          Exploit Mobile Application Part (MAP) protocol to query visitor location registers (VLR)
          Deploy SS7 protocol analyzers to capture and replay legitimate network messages
          Use compromised Global Title (GT) addresses to masquerade as legitimate network elements
          Send fraudulent ProvideSubscriberInfo (PSI) requests to serving networks
          Exploit SIGTRAN protocol stack to access SS7 network through IP-based connections
        Remote Device Management Services: An adversary may use access to cloud services (e.g. Google's Android Device Manager or Apple iCloud's Find my iPhone) or to an enterprise mobility management (EMM)/mobile device management (MDM) server console to track the location of mobile devices managed by the service.(Citation: Krebs-Location)
          Access Android Device Manager via compromised Google account credentials to track device location
          Exploit Find My iPhone through stolen/phished Apple ID credentials to monitor device movements
          Compromise MDM server admin credentials to track enterprise-managed mobile devices
          Use social engineering to add unauthorized devices to legitimate MDM solutions
          Leverage stolen MDM enrollment certificates to register rogue devices
          Exploit vulnerabilities in EMM console authentication to gain unauthorized access
          Use malware to intercept and redirect MDM location reporting to attacker infrastructure
          Abuse MDM API endpoints to extract historical location data
          Create fake MDM enrollment profiles to trick users into granting location access
          Exploit misconfigured MDM group policies to escalate privileges and access location tracking features
          Exploit AirWatch MDM console vulnerabilities to access device location data
          Abuse Microsoft Intune device location tracking features through compromised admin accounts
          Leverage MobileIron MDM misconfigurations to extract location information
          Exploit BlackBerry UEM (Unified Endpoint Management) for unauthorized location tracking
          Use compromised iTunes backup data to access historical device locations
          Exploit Samsung Knox MDM APIs to gather device location information
          Abuse Workspace ONE location services through compromised administrator credentials
          Exploit JAMF Pro MDM solution to track iOS devices in enterprise environments
          Leverage IBM MaaS360 MDM vulnerabilities to access device tracking features
          Exploit Citrix Endpoint Management (formerly XenMobile) location tracking capabilities
          Exploit carrier-based device location services through compromised telecom management portals
          Abuse corporate BYOD enrollment systems to gain unauthorized MDM location access
          Exploit MDM certificate authority services to generate unauthorized device certificates
          Use stolen OAuth tokens to access MDM location services without detection
          Exploit MDM audit log deletion capabilities to hide unauthorized location tracking
          Leverage compromised MDM backup systems to extract historical location data
          Exploit zero-day vulnerabilities in MDM protocol implementations
          Use lateral movement within MDM infrastructure to access location tracking subsystems
          Exploit MDM emergency location services intended for device recovery
          Abuse MDM location data aggregation services used for analytics
    lateral-movement: The adversary is trying to move through your environment.  Lateral movement consists of techniques that enable an adversary to access and control remote systems on a network and could, but does not necessarily, include execution of tools on remote systems. The lateral movement techniques could allow an adversary to gather information from a system without needing additional tools, such as a remote access tool.
      Attack PC via USB Connection: With escalated privileges, an adversary could program the mobile device to impersonate USB devices such as input devices (keyboard and mouse), storage devices, and/or networking devices in order to attack a physically connected PC(Citation: Wang-ExploitingUSB)(Citation: ArsTechnica-PoisonTap) This technique has been demonstrated on Android. We are unaware of any demonstrations on iOS.
        ~USB HID Impersonation: Adversary programs mobile device to appear as a keyboard/mouse to inject keystrokes or commands
        ~USB Storage Masquerading: Device presents itself as a storage device to deliver malicious files or execute autorun attacks
        ~USB Network Adapter Spoofing: Device impersonates network adapter to intercept traffic or create rogue network interfaces
        ~USB Charging Exploitation: Leverages charging connection to execute attacks while appearing as legitimate charging activity
        ~USB Protocol Manipulation: Exploits USB protocol vulnerabilities to execute unauthorized commands or bypass security
        ~USB Composite Device Attack: Presents multiple device interfaces simultaneously to expand attack surface
        ~USB DMA Attack: Exploits Direct Memory Access capabilities through USB connection to read/write system memory
        ~USB Configuration Manipulation: Modifies USB device configurations during enumeration to bypass security controls
        ~USB Zero-Day Exploitation: Leverages unknown USB protocol or driver vulnerabilities
        ~USB Device Class Switching: Dynamically changes device class to evade detection or exploit different attack vectors
      Exploitation of Remote Services: Adversaries may exploit remote services of enterprise servers, workstations, or other resources to gain unauthorized access to internal systems once inside of a network. Adversaries may exploit remote services by taking advantage of a mobile device’s access to an internal enterprise network through local connectivity or through a Virtual Private Network (VPN). Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for lateral movement to enable access to a remote system.   An adversary may need to determine if the remote system is in a vulnerable state, which may be done through [Network Service Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1423) or other Discovery methods. These look for common, vulnerable software that may be deployed in the network, the lack of certain patches that may indicate vulnerabilities, or security software that may be used to detect or contain remote exploitation. Servers are likely a high value target for lateral movement exploitation, but endpoint systems may also be at risk if they provide an advantage or access to additional resources.  Depending on the permissions level of the vulnerable remote service, an adversary may achieve [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1404) as a result of lateral movement exploitation as well.
        ~VPN Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in VPN services or configurations used by mobile devices to gain unauthorized network access
        ~MDM Server Exploitation: Adversaries target vulnerabilities in Mobile Device Management (MDM) servers to compromise managed devices
        ~Mobile App Backend Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in backend services that mobile apps connect to
        ~Enterprise Gateway Exploitation: Adversaries target enterprise mobility gateways and access proxies
        ~Mobile Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in mobile-specific network protocols
        ~Cloud Service API Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in cloud services APIs that mobile devices connect to
        ~Authentication Service Exploitation: Adversaries target mobile-specific authentication services and SSO providers
        ~Debug Interface Exploitation: Adversaries exploit remote debugging interfaces and development features left enabled
        ~Device Sync Service Exploitation: Adversaries target vulnerabilities in services that handle device synchronization
        ~Enterprise Collaboration Service Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in mobile enterprise collaboration platforms
      Replication Through Removable Media: Adversaries may move onto devices by exploiting or copying malware to devices connected via USB. In the case of Lateral Movement, adversaries may utilize the physical connection of a device to a compromised or malicious charging station or PC to bypass application store requirements and install malicious applications directly.(Citation: Lau-Mactans) In the case of Initial Access, adversaries may attempt to exploit the device via the connection to gain access to data stored on the device.(Citation: Krebs-JuiceJacking) Examples of this include:    * Exploiting insecure bootloaders in a Nexus 6 or 6P device over USB and gaining the ability to perform actions including intercepting phone calls, intercepting network traffic, and obtaining the device physical location.(Citation: IBM-NexusUSB)  * Exploiting weakly-enforced security boundaries in Android devices such as the Google Pixel 2 over USB.(Citation: GoogleProjectZero-OATmeal)  * Products from Cellebrite and Grayshift purportedly that can exploit some iOS devices using physical access to the data port to unlock the passcode.(Citation: Computerworld-iPhoneCracking)
        ~Malicious Charging Station Attack: Adversaries may set up compromised charging stations to install malware or extract data when mobile devices are connected for charging
        ~USB Bootloader Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerable bootloaders over USB connections to gain system-level access to mobile devices
        ~Direct Application Installation: Adversaries may bypass app store restrictions by using USB connections to directly install malicious applications
        ~USB Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in USB protocol implementation to gain unauthorized access
        ~Physical Port Access: Adversaries may use specialized hardware tools to exploit device data ports for unlocking or data extraction
        ~USB Debug Interface Abuse: Adversaries may abuse USB debugging interfaces to gain elevated access to device functions
        ~Custom USB Payload Delivery: Adversaries may deliver custom malicious payloads through USB connections to compromise devices
    collection: The adversary is trying to gather data of interest to their goal.  Collection consists of techniques used to identify and gather information, such as sensitive files, from a target network prior to exfiltration. This category also covers locations on a system or network where the adversary may look for information to exfiltrate.
      Protected User Data: Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to collect data from permission-backed data stores on a device, such as the calendar or contact list. These permissions need to be declared ahead of time. On Android, they must be included in the application’s manifest. On iOS, they must be included in the application’s `Info.plist` file.       In almost all cases, the user is required to grant access to the data store that the application is trying to access. In recent OS versions, vendors have introduced additional privacy controls for users, such as the ability to grant permission to an application only while the application is being actively used by the user.      If the device has been jailbroken or rooted, an adversary may be able to access [Protected User Data](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636) without the user’s knowledge or approval.
        Calendar Entries: Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather calendar entry data. On Android, this can be accomplished using the Calendar Content Provider. On iOS, this can be accomplished using the `EventKit` framework.      If the device has been jailbroken or rooted, an adversary may be able to access [Calendar Entries](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/001) without the user’s knowledge or approval.
          Use Android Calendar Content Provider's query() method to access calendar data through content://com.android.calendar
          Leverage EventKit framework's EKEventStore class to fetch calendar events on iOS devices
          Access calendar database directly at /data/data/com.android.providers.calendar/databases/calendar.db on rooted Android devices
          Extract calendar data from iOS backup files on jailbroken devices at /var/mobile/Library/Calendar
          Create malicious calendar provider observer to monitor real-time calendar changes on Android
          Use EventKit's predicateForEvents method to filter and collect specific calendar entries on iOS
          Exploit calendar sync features to exfiltrate data through fake calendar service accounts
          Access shared calendar data through workplace management profiles without user notification
        SMS Messages: Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather SMS messages. On Android, this can be accomplished using the SMS Content Provider. iOS provides no standard API to access SMS messages.   If the device has been jailbroken or rooted, an adversary may be able to access [SMS Messages](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/004) without the user’s knowledge or approval.
