      Network Information Discovery: Adversaries may use device sensors to collect information about nearby networks, such as Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.
        ~WiFi SSID Scanning: Adversaries may scan for available WiFi networks to map nearby wireless infrastructure and identify potential targets.
        ~Bluetooth Device Discovery: Adversaries may use Bluetooth scanning to identify nearby devices, their types, and capabilities.
        ~NFC Proximity Scanning: Adversaries may attempt to detect and read nearby NFC devices or payment terminals.
        ~Cellular Tower Mapping: Adversaries may collect information about nearby cellular towers to track location or identify network infrastructure.
        ~Network Service Enumeration: Adversaries may scan discovered networks for available services and open ports.
        ~Wireless Packet Capture: Adversaries may capture and analyze wireless network traffic to gather information about network configuration.
        ~Location-Based Network Discovery: Adversaries may correlate device GPS location with network scanning results to create network infrastructure maps.
      Stored Application Data: Adversaries may try to access and collect application data resident on the device. Adversaries often target popular applications, such as Facebook, WeChat, and Gmail.(Citation: SWB Exodus March 2019)      Due to mobile OS sandboxing, this technique is only possible in three scenarios:      * An application stores files in unprotected external storage  * An application stores files in its internal storage directory with insecure permissions (e.g. 777)  * The adversary gains root permissions on the device
        ~External Storage Access: Adversaries access application data stored in unprotected external storage locations like SD cards or shared storage areas
        ~Insecure Permission Exploitation: Adversaries target applications with incorrectly configured file permissions to access stored data
        ~Root Access Collection: Adversaries use root/jailbreak privileges to bypass sandbox restrictions and access protected application data
        ~Backup Data Access: Adversaries collect application data from device backups stored locally or in cloud services
        ~Cache Data Collection: Adversaries target temporary cache files and data stored by applications in accessible locations
        ~Credential Storage Access: Adversaries specifically target stored login credentials, tokens, and authentication data
        ~Media File Collection: Adversaries focus on collecting media files (images, videos, audio) stored by applications
        ~Database File Access: Adversaries target application databases (SQLite, Realm) containing user data
        ~Configuration File Collection: Adversaries collect application configuration files containing sensitive settings or data
        ~Shared Storage Access: Adversaries access data in shared storage locations used by multiple applications
        ~Clipboard Data Collection: Adversaries collect sensitive data stored in the device clipboard from application copy/paste operations
        ~App State Extraction: Adversaries target saved application state data including session information and runtime data
        ~Debug Log Access: Adversaries collect application debug logs that may contain sensitive information
        ~Content Provider Access: Adversaries exploit Android content providers to access data shared between applications
        ~Keychain Extraction: Adversaries target iOS keychain data storing application secrets and credentials
        ~WebView Data Collection: Adversaries collect data stored by applications using WebView components (cookies, local storage)
        ~Recovery Data Access: Adversaries collect application auto-save and recovery data
        ~IPC Storage Access: Adversaries target data stored for inter-process communication between application components
        ~Encrypted Storage Exploitation: Adversaries exploit weakly encrypted application storage through key extraction or cryptographic vulnerabilities
        ~Development Data Access: Adversaries collect development-related data left in production applications (test accounts, API keys)
        ~Vendor SDK Data Collection: Adversaries target data stored by third-party SDKs and analytics frameworks
        ~Cross-Platform Storage Access: Adversaries collect data from cross-platform storage solutions (React Native, Flutter storage)
        ~Legacy Storage Access: Adversaries exploit legacy or deprecated storage mechanisms that may have weaker security
        ~Analytics Data Collection: Adversaries target stored analytics and telemetry data containing user behavior information
        ~Framework Cache Access: Adversaries collect data from framework-specific cache locations (Unity, Xamarin)
        ~Sync Data Collection: Adversaries target locally stored synchronization data used for offline capabilities
        ~Temporary File Access: Adversaries collect sensitive data from temporary files created during application operations
        ~Plugin Data Access: Adversaries target data stored by application plugins and extensions
        ~Update Cache Access: Adversaries collect data from application update caches and delta storage
        ~Preference Storage Access: Adversaries target application preference storage containing user settings and configurations
        ~Enterprise Container Access: Adversaries target data stored within enterprise containerization solutions (Knox, Work Profile)
        ~Virtual Space Access: Adversaries collect data from application clones and parallel spaces features
        ~Device Encryption Bypass: Adversaries exploit device encryption implementation flaws to access protected application storage
        ~Alternative Storage Access: Adversaries target non-standard storage locations used by modified or customized applications
        ~Profile Data Collection: Adversaries collect data stored in device management profiles and enterprise configurations
        ~Secure Element Access: Adversaries target data stored in hardware secure elements or trusted execution environments
        ~App Bundle Data Access: Adversaries collect sensitive data stored within application bundles and resource files
        ~Storage Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in storage access protocols and APIs
      Input Capture: Adversaries may use methods of capturing user input to obtain credentials or collect information. During normal device usage, users often provide credentials to various locations, such as login pages/portals or system dialog boxes. Input capture mechanisms may be transparent to the user (e.g. [Keylogging](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417/001)) or rely on deceiving the user into providing input into what they believe to be a genuine application prompt (e.g. [GUI Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417/002)).
        Keylogging: Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials or other information from the user as the user types them.  Some methods of keylogging include:  * Masquerading as a legitimate third-party keyboard to record user keystrokes.(Citation: Zeltser-Keyboard) On both Android and iOS, users must explicitly authorize the use of third-party keyboard apps. Users should be advised to use extreme caution before granting this authorization when it is requested. * Abusing accessibility features. On Android, adversaries may abuse accessibility features to record keystrokes by registering an `AccessibilityService` class, overriding the `onAccessibilityEvent` method, and listening for the `AccessibilityEvent.TYPE_VIEW_TEXT_CHANGED` event type. The event object passed into the function will contain the data that the user typed.  *Additional methods of keylogging may be possible if root access is available.
          Deploy malicious keyboard app disguised as popular third-party keyboard (e.g., SwiftKey clone) that requests keyboard permissions
          Create AccessibilityService implementation that monitors TYPE_VIEW_TEXT_CHANGED events and logs text input
          Install rootkit-based keylogger that hooks into system input methods
          Modify system keyboard cache files to capture keystroke data
          Implement clipboard monitoring service to capture copied credentials
          Create overlay attack that presents fake keyboard while logging real keystrokes
          Exploit custom ROM or jailbreak to install system-level keylogger
          Hook into IME (Input Method Editor) framework to intercept keystrokes
          Use Android Debug Bridge (ADB) with root access to install keylogging service
          Implement background service that monitors input method switching to detect keyboard usage
          Abuse Mobile Device Management (MDM) profiles to deploy keylogging capabilities
          Exploit cloud keyboard sync features to capture keystrokes during backup
          Monitor input method state changes through ContentObserver
          Implement screen recording service focused on keyboard area
          Hook into gesture typing prediction engine to capture swipe patterns
          Exploit input method switching animations to capture keyboard state
          Use accessibility features to monitor password autofill events
          Create malicious custom keyboard theme that logs keystrokes
          Implement keyboard prediction service that captures input patterns
          Hook into keyboard haptic feedback service to track keypress events
          Monitor shared memory segments used by keyboard processes
          Implement side-channel attack using motion sensors to infer keystrokes
          Create malicious keyboard dictionary/language pack with logging capability
          Exploit keyboard cache in shared storage
          Use accessibility API to capture secure keyboard input in banking apps
          Monitor keyboard process memory usage patterns
          Implement acoustic side-channel attack using device microphone
          Hook into keyboard auto-correction database
          Exploit keyboard layout switching mechanism
          Create malicious keyboard extension that logs specialized input methods (emoji, special characters)
        GUI Input Capture: Adversaries may mimic common operating system GUI components to prompt users for sensitive information with a seemingly legitimate prompt. The operating system and installed applications often have legitimate needs to prompt the user for sensitive information such as account credentials, bank account information, or Personally Identifiable Information (PII). Compared to traditional PCs, the constrained display size of mobile devices may impair the ability to provide users with contextual information, making users more susceptible to this technique’s use.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)  There are several approaches adversaries may use to mimic this functionality. Adversaries may impersonate the identity of a legitimate application (e.g. use the same application name and/or icon) and, when installed on the device, may prompt the user for sensitive information.(Citation: eset-finance) Adversaries may also send fake device notifications to the user that may trigger the display of an input prompt when clicked.(Citation: Group IB Gustuff Mar 2019)   Additionally, adversaries may display a prompt on top of a running, legitimate application to trick users into entering sensitive information into a malicious application rather than the legitimate application. Typically, adversaries need to know when the targeted application and the individual activity within the targeted application is running in the foreground to display the prompt at the proper time. Adversaries can abuse Android’s accessibility features to determine which application is currently in the foreground.(Citation: ThreatFabric Cerberus) Two known approaches to displaying a prompt include:  * Adversaries start a new activity on top of a running legitimate application.(Citation: Felt-PhishingOnMobileDevices)(Citation: Hassell-ExploitingAndroid) Android 10 places new restrictions on the ability for an application to start a new activity on top of another application, which may make it more difficult for adversaries to utilize this technique.(Citation: Android Background) * Adversaries create an application overlay window on top of a running legitimate application. Applications must hold the `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission to create overlay windows. This permission is handled differently than typical Android permissions and, at least under certain conditions, is automatically granted to applications installed from the Google Play Store.(Citation: Cloak and Dagger)(Citation: NowSecure Android Overlay)(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility) The `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` permission and its associated ability to create application overlay windows are expected to be deprecated in a future release of Android in favor of a new API.(Citation: XDA Bubbles)
          Create a fake banking app with identical UI elements and app icon as a legitimate bank app to capture login credentials
          Deploy notification-based phishing that mimics system security update prompts
          Use Android's SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW permission to create transparent overlay on top of cryptocurrency wallet apps
          Implement accessibility service abuse to detect when target apps are in foreground
          Create malicious app that launches fake login activity when legitimate social media apps are opened
          Use overlay attacks targeting mobile payment apps during transaction processes
          Deploy fake system permission dialogs requesting sensitive permissions
          Create counterfeit two-factor authentication prompts mimicking legitimate 2FA apps
          Implement screen capture when users enter sensitive data in targeted apps
          Use fake app update prompts to capture existing app credentials
          Create fake mobile carrier billing prompts to capture payment details
          Deploy corporate VPN login screen overlays to harvest enterprise credentials
          Implement time-delayed overlay attacks that activate during specific user actions
          Use accessibility services to detect and overlay keyboard input in targeted messaging apps
          Create region-specific payment app overlays (e.g., WeChat Pay, Alipay, Paytm)
          Deploy fake biometric authentication prompts to capture fingerprint/face data
          Create overlays mimicking mobile device management (MDM) enrollment screens
          Implement fake app store purchase confirmation dialogs
          Use screen recording permission prompts to capture PIN entry
          Deploy fake password manager autofill prompts
          Create fake lock screen overlays when device is in sleep mode
          Deploy context-aware overlays based on user's physical location (e.g., near banks)
          Implement multi-language phishing overlays based on device locale
          Create fake system cleanup/storage management prompts during low storage states
          Deploy overlays that activate during specific app installation processes
          Use fake battery optimization prompts to request additional permissions
          Create overlays mimicking device backup/restore interfaces
          Implement fake security scanner interfaces during malware removal attempts
          Deploy overlays during app switching/multitasking operations
          Create fake emergency alert system prompts
          Create fake smart home device pairing interfaces to capture WiFi credentials
          Deploy overlays during QR code scanning operations
          Implement fake cross-device authentication prompts (e.g., phone-to-laptop sync)
          Create overlays mimicking cloud storage access permissions
          Deploy fake mobile wallet NFC payment confirmation screens
          Create overlays for health data sharing prompts
          Implement fake app cloning/parallel space authentication screens
          Deploy overlays during mobile device backup encryption processes
          Create fake mobile printing service authentication prompts
          Implement overlays during mobile payment card scanning operations
      Malicious SMS Message: Test
        ~SMS Phishing Links: Sending SMS messages containing malicious links that lead to credential harvesting or data collection pages
        ~SMS Command Messages: Sending specially crafted SMS messages that trigger malware or spyware already installed on the device to collect data
        ~Premium Rate SMS Fraud: Tricking users into sending SMS to premium rate numbers while collecting response data
        ~SMS-based C2 Collection: Using SMS as a command and control channel to exfiltrate collected data
        ~Contact List Harvesting: Using malicious SMS to trigger unauthorized access to contact lists
        ~Location Data Collection: Using SMS to trigger location service access and collect GPS coordinates
        ~SMS Backup Interception: Intercepting SMS backup data through malicious messages triggering backup functions
        ~SMS Gateway Exploitation: Exploiting SMS gateway vulnerabilities to collect message content and metadata
        ~Silent SMS Collection: Sending silent SMS messages to track device location and status without user awareness
        ~SMS Database Extraction: Using malicious SMS payloads to extract stored SMS databases
        ~SMS Binary Payload Injection: Sending specially crafted binary SMS messages to exploit SMS processing vulnerabilities and collect data
        ~SMS Flash Message Exploitation: Using flash SMS messages to trigger information disclosure
        ~SMS Concatenation Attacks: Exploiting message reassembly to inject malicious content and collect data
        ~SMS Protocol Abuse: Exploiting SMS protocol features to gather device information
        ~Dual-Tone Collection: Using SMS to trigger DTMF-based data collection
        ~SMS Metadata Harvesting: Collecting SMS metadata through malicious messages
        ~SIM Card Data Collection: Using SMS to trigger SIM card data extraction
        ~SMS Forwarding Setup: Configuring message forwarding through malicious SMS commands
        ~Multi-SIM Collection: Exploiting dual-SIM functionality to collect data across multiple carrier networks
        ~SMS Service Center Spoofing: Impersonating SMSC to intercept and collect messages
        ~SMS Timing Analysis: Using SMS timing patterns to collect side-channel information
        ~Carrier-Specific SMS Exploits: Exploiting carrier-specific SMS features for data collection
        ~SMS Encryption Bypass: Bypassing SMS encryption to collect message content
        ~SMS App API Abuse: Exploiting SMS app APIs to collect additional data
        ~Cross-Platform SMS Collection: Leveraging SMS synchronization features to collect data across multiple devices
      Network Traffic Capture or Redirection: An adversary may capture network traffic to and from the device to obtain credentials or other sensitive data, or redirect network traffic to flow through an adversary-controlled gateway to do the same.  A malicious app could register itself as a VPN client on Android or iOS to gain access to network packets. However, on both platforms, the user must grant consent to the app to act as a VPN client, and on iOS the app requires a special entitlement that must be granted by Apple.  Alternatively, if a malicious app is able to escalate operating system privileges, it may be able to use those privileges to gain access to network traffic.  An adversary could redirect network traffic to an adversary-controlled gateway by establishing a VPN connection or by manipulating the device's proxy settings. For example, Skycure (Citation: Skycure-Profiles) describes the ability to redirect network traffic by installing a malicious iOS Configuration Profile.  If applications encrypt their network traffic, sensitive data may not be accessible to an adversary, depending on the point of capture.
        ~VPN Client Registration: Adversary registers malicious app as VPN client on Android/iOS to capture network traffic, requiring user consent
        ~Privilege Escalation Capture: Adversary exploits elevated OS privileges to capture network traffic directly
        ~Malicious Profile Installation: Adversary installs configuration profiles (e.g., iOS profiles) to redirect traffic through attacker-controlled gateway
        ~Proxy Settings Manipulation: Adversary modifies device proxy settings to redirect traffic through malicious proxy server
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Gateway: Adversary sets up malicious gateway to intercept and analyze redirected traffic
      Audio Capture: Adversaries may capture audio to collect information by leveraging standard operating system APIs of a mobile device. Examples of audio information adversaries may target include user conversations, surroundings, phone calls, or other sensitive information.      Android and iOS, by default, require that applications request device microphone access from the user.       On Android devices, applications must hold the `RECORD_AUDIO` permission to access the microphone or the `CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT` permission to access audio output. Because Android does not allow third-party applications to hold the `CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT` permission by default, only privileged applications, such as those distributed by Google or the device vendor, can access audio output.(Citation: Android Permissions) However, adversaries may be able to gain this access after successfully elevating their privileges. With the `CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT` permission, adversaries may pass the `MediaRecorder.AudioSource.VOICE_CALL` constant to `MediaRecorder.setAudioOutput`, allowing capture of both voice call uplink and downlink.(Citation: Manifest.permission)      On iOS devices, applications must include the `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` key in their `Info.plist` file to access the microphone.(Citation: Requesting Auth-Media Capture)
        ~Continuous Background Recording: Adversaries maintain persistent microphone access to record all ambient audio continuously
        ~Call Audio Interception: Specifically targeting phone call audio streams through privileged access
        ~Voice Command Trigger: Malware activates recording when specific voice commands or keywords are detected
        ~Scheduled Recording: Adversaries schedule audio capture during specific times or at specific locations
        ~Voice Note Hijacking: Intercepting and capturing audio when legitimate voice note or voice message features are used
        ~Microphone Access Exploitation: Exploiting microphone permissions or vulnerabilities to bypass user consent
        ~Audio Buffer Manipulation: Capturing audio by manipulating system audio buffers or memory
        ~Context-Aware Recording: Triggering audio capture based on context (location, app usage, user activity)
        ~VoIP Call Interception: Specifically targeting Voice over IP calls through app manipulation
        ~System API Abuse: Misusing legitimate system APIs intended for audio processing to capture audio
        ~Hardware Driver Exploitation: Targeting audio hardware drivers to bypass system-level controls
        ~Audio Sensor Fusion: Combining audio from multiple device sensors (main mic, noise cancellation mic, etc.)
        ~Social Engineering Triggers: Using social engineering to trick users into granting audio permissions
        ~Compressed Audio Capture: Using audio compression techniques to store long-term recordings
        ~Audio Stream Hijacking: Intercepting audio streams between apps and the audio subsystem
        ~Silent Recording Mode: Disabling audio capture indicators and notifications
        ~Audio Cache Exploitation: Accessing cached audio data from legitimate apps
        ~Remote Trigger Recording: Activating audio capture through remote commands or signals
      Access Notifications: Adversaries may collect data within notifications sent by the operating system or other applications. Notifications may contain sensitive data such as one-time authentication codes sent over SMS, email, or other mediums. In the case of Credential Access, adversaries may attempt to intercept one-time code sent to the device. Adversaries can also dismiss notifications to prevent the user from noticing that the notification has arrived and can trigger action buttons contained within notifications.(Citation: ESET 2FA Bypass)
        ~SMS Notification Interception: Adversaries intercept SMS notifications by exploiting SMS handling vulnerabilities or using malicious apps with SMS permissions
        ~Push Notification Hijacking: Adversaries capture push notifications by manipulating notification listeners or exploiting notification service vulnerabilities
        ~Notification Database Access: Adversaries directly access system notification databases to retrieve stored notification content
        ~Notification Service Impersonation: Adversaries create fake notification services to intercept notifications meant for legitimate apps
        ~Notification UI Overlay: Adversaries use screen overlays to capture notification content when displayed to users
        ~Background Notification Monitoring: Adversaries use persistent background processes to monitor and collect incoming notifications
        ~Notification Permission Abuse: Adversaries exploit notification permission models to gain unauthorized access to notifications
        ~Notification API Exploitation: Adversaries leverage vulnerabilities in notification APIs to intercept or modify notification data
      Call Control: Adversaries may make, forward, or block phone calls without user authorization. This could be used for adversary goals such as audio surveillance, blocking or forwarding calls from the device owner, or C2 communication.  Several permissions may be used to programmatically control phone calls, including:  * `ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS` - Allows the application to answer incoming phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions) * `CALL_PHONE` - Allows the application to initiate a phone call without going through the Dialer interface(Citation: Android Permissions) * `PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS` - Allows the application to see the number being dialed during an outgoing call with the option to redirect the call to a different number or abort the call altogether(Citation: Android Permissions) * `MANAGE_OWN_CALLS` - Allows a calling application which manages its own calls through the self-managed `ConnectionService` APIs(Citation: Android Permissions) * `BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE` - Required permission when using a `ConnectionService`(Citation: Android Permissions) * `WRITE_CALL_LOG` - Allows an application to write to the device call log, potentially to hide malicious phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions)  When granted some of these permissions, an application can make a phone call without opening the dialer first. However, if an application desires to simply redirect the user to the dialer with a phone number filled in, it can launch an Intent using `Intent.ACTION_DIAL`, which requires no specific permissions. This then requires the user to explicitly initiate the call or use some form of [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) to programmatically initiate it.
        ~Call Interception: Adversaries may intercept and monitor phone calls by exploiting telecom connection services and call management permissions
        ~Call Redirection: Adversaries may redirect outgoing calls to different numbers by abusing PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS permission
        ~Call Blocking: Adversaries may selectively block incoming calls to prevent communication or isolate the target
        ~Silent Call Initiation: Adversaries may initiate calls without user awareness using CALL_PHONE permission
        ~Call Log Manipulation: Adversaries may modify call logs to hide malicious activity using WRITE_CALL_LOG permission
        ~Automated Call Answering: Adversaries may automatically answer incoming calls using ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS permission for surveillance
        ~Call Service Hijacking: Adversaries may hijack ConnectionService APIs to take control of call management
        ~Premium Number Abuse: Adversaries may initiate calls to premium numbers for financial fraud
        ~Call Conference Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate conference calls to eavesdrop or inject audio
        ~Voicemail Access: Adversaries may access or manipulate voicemail systems through call control abuse
        ~Emergency Call Manipulation: Adversaries may abuse emergency call handling to bypass restrictions or gain elevated privileges
        ~Call Forwarding Chain: Adversaries may create complex call forwarding chains to obscure the final destination
        ~Protocol-Specific Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit specific telephony protocols (VoLTE, VoWiFi) for call manipulation
        ~Call Recording Abuse: Adversaries may exploit call recording capabilities for surveillance
        ~Caller ID Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate caller ID information to impersonate legitimate callers
        ~Call State Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate call states (hold, mute, etc.) for denial of service or surveillance
        ~SMS-Call Integration Abuse: Adversaries may exploit interactions between call handling and SMS systems
        ~Background Call Services: Adversaries may abuse background call services to maintain persistence
        ~Call Quality Degradation: Adversaries may intentionally degrade call quality for denial of service
        ~Call Duration Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate call duration tracking for billing fraud
      Video Capture: An adversary can leverage a device’s cameras to gather information by capturing video recordings. Images may also be captured, potentially in specified intervals, in lieu of video files.       Malware or scripts may interact with the device cameras through an available API provided by the operating system. Video or image files may be written to disk and exfiltrated later. This technique differs from [Screen Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1513) due to use of the device’s cameras for video recording rather than capturing the victim’s screen.      In Android, an application must hold the `android.permission.CAMERA` permission to access the cameras. In iOS, applications must include the `NSCameraUsageDescription` key in the `Info.plist` file. In both cases, the user must grant permission to the requesting application to use the camera. If the device has been rooted or jailbroken, an adversary may be able to access the camera without knowledge of the user.
        ~Front Camera Exploitation: Specifically targeting the front-facing camera to capture user's face, surroundings, or sensitive information visible in front of the device
        ~Background Recording: Capturing video while the app appears to be in background or device is locked
        ~Trigger-based Recording: Initiating video capture based on specific events or conditions like location, time, or user actions
        ~Permission Abuse: Exploiting legitimately granted camera permissions for malicious purposes beyond stated app functionality
        ~Silent Capture: Recording without showing camera preview or UI indicators
        ~Root/Jailbreak Exploitation: Leveraging elevated privileges on compromised devices to bypass camera access restrictions
        ~Camera API Manipulation: Exploiting vulnerabilities or misconfigurations in camera APIs
        ~Interval-based Capture: Taking periodic photos or short video clips at predetermined intervals
      Capture SMS Messages: A malicious application could capture sensitive data sent via SMS, including authentication credentials. SMS is frequently used to transmit codes used for multi-factor authentication.  On Android, a malicious application must request and obtain permission (either at app install time or run time) in order to receive SMS messages. Alternatively, a malicious application could attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to bypass the permission requirement.  On iOS, applications cannot access SMS messages in normal operation, so an adversary would need to attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to potentially be able to access SMS messages.
        ~SMS Permission Abuse: Exploiting legitimate SMS permissions granted to apps during installation or runtime to intercept messages
        ~SMS Database Access: Directly accessing SMS storage through system database queries after gaining necessary privileges
        ~SMS Broadcast Interception: Intercepting SMS broadcast intents in Android before they reach the default messaging app
        ~SMS Backup Exploitation: Accessing SMS messages through backup files or cloud sync features
        ~SMS Service Provider Impersonation: Masquerading as system SMS service to intercept messages
        ~Privilege Escalation for SMS Access: Exploiting OS vulnerabilities to gain SMS access without proper permissions
        ~SMS Forwarding Configuration: Modifying system settings to forward SMS messages to attacker-controlled destinations
        ~SMS Content Provider Manipulation: Exploiting content provider vulnerabilities to access SMS data
      Access Sensitive Data in Device Logs: On versions of Android prior to 4.1, an adversary may use a malicious application that holds the READ_LOGS permission to obtain private keys, passwords, other credentials, or other sensitive data stored in the device's system log. On Android 4.1 and later, an adversary would need to attempt to perform an operating system privilege escalation attack to be able to access the log.
        ~Legacy READ_LOGS Permission Abuse: Exploiting the READ_LOGS permission on Android versions prior to 4.1 to directly access system logs containing sensitive data
        ~Root Privilege Escalation: Gaining root access on newer Android versions to bypass logging restrictions
        ~ADB Log Access: Using Android Debug Bridge (ADB) connection to extract and analyze system logs
        ~Logcat Command Injection: Injecting malicious commands through the logcat utility to extract sensitive information
        ~Custom Log Parser Exploitation: Using specialized parsing tools to extract specific patterns of sensitive data from logs
        ~System Service Log Interception: Intercepting logs from specific system services that might contain credentials
        ~Crash Log Analysis: Analyzing application crash logs that might contain sensitive information
        ~Debug Log Exploitation: Targeting debug logs that might contain development credentials or sensitive test data
      Access Calendar Entries: An adversary could call standard operating system APIs from a malicious application to gather calendar entry data, or with escalated privileges could directly access files containing calendar data.
        ~API-Based Access: Adversaries may use public calendar provider APIs or content provider interfaces to read calendar data with normal app permissions
        ~Direct File System Access: Adversaries may directly read calendar database files after obtaining root/jailbreak access
        ~Backup Extraction: Adversaries may extract calendar data from device backups stored locally or in cloud services
        ~Sync Service Abuse: Adversaries may compromise calendar sync services or accounts to obtain calendar data
        ~Enterprise MDM Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Mobile Device Management capabilities to access calendar data across managed devices
        ~IPC Interception: Adversaries may intercept or hijack inter-process communication between calendar apps and system services
        ~Calendar Permission Spoofing: Adversaries may trick users into granting calendar access through fake system dialogs or UI overlays
        ~Third-Party Calendar App Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in third-party calendar applications to access calendar data
        ~Calendar Service Worker Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate or hijack calendar service workers to intercept calendar data
      Access Call Log: On Android, an adversary could call standard operating system APIs from a malicious application to gather call log data, or with escalated privileges could directly access files containing call log data.  On iOS, applications do not have access to the call log, so privilege escalation would be required in order to access the data.
        ~Android API Access: Using Android's CallLog.Calls API with READ_CALL_LOG permission to access call history through legitimate API channels
        ~Android Direct File Access: Accessing call log database files directly at /data/data/com.android.providers.contacts/databases/ after gaining root access
        ~iOS Jailbreak Access: Accessing call logs through direct file system access after jailbreaking the device
        ~iOS Private Framework Access: Using private frameworks to access call history after gaining elevated privileges
        ~Historical Call Log Access: Accessing archived or backup call log data stored in device backups or sync locations
        ~Real-time Call Monitoring: Intercepting and logging calls as they occur through hooks or system modifications
        ~Deleted Call Log Recovery: Recovering deleted call log entries from device storage
        ~Cross-App Call Log Access: Exploiting vulnerabilities in other apps with call log permissions to gain indirect access
      Clipboard Data: Adversaries may abuse clipboard manager APIs to obtain sensitive information copied to the device clipboard. For example, passwords being copied and pasted from a password manager application could be captured by a malicious application installed on the device.(Citation: Fahl-Clipboard)      On Android, applications can use the `ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener()` API to register as a listener and monitor the clipboard for changes. However, starting in Android 10, this can only be used if the application is in the foreground, or is set as the device’s default input method editor (IME).(Citation: Github Capture Clipboard 2019)(Citation: Android 10 Privacy Changes)      On iOS, this can be accomplished by accessing the `UIPasteboard.general.string` field. However, starting in iOS 14, upon accessing the clipboard, the user will be shown a system notification if the accessed text originated in a different application. For example, if the user copies the text of an iMessage from the Messages application, the notification will read “application_name has pasted from Messages” when the text was pasted in a different application.(Citation: UIPPasteboard)
        ~Background Clipboard Monitoring: Abuse device permissions or system vulnerabilities to monitor clipboard changes even when the malicious app is not in foreground
        ~Input Method Editor (IME) Exploitation: Masquerade as or compromise legitimate keyboard apps to gain clipboard access privileges
        ~Cross-App Clipboard Interception: Target clipboard data transfers between specific apps, particularly during sensitive operations
        ~Rich Content Clipboard Capture: Target non-text clipboard content including images, files, and structured data
        ~Clipboard History Collection: Access and exfiltrate historical clipboard data stored by the operating system
        ~Clipboard Protection Bypass: Techniques to bypass clipboard access restrictions and notifications in newer OS versions
        ~Induced Clipboard Usage: Trick users into copying sensitive data to clipboard through social engineering
        ~Cloud Clipboard Interception: Target synchronized clipboard data across devices through cloud services
        ~Enterprise Clipboard Exploitation: Target enterprise mobile device management (MDM) clipboard policies and controls
        ~Specialized Format Monitoring: Target specific clipboard format types like cryptocurrency addresses or authentication tokens
      Data from Local System: Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems or local databases, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to exfiltration.       Access to local system data, which includes information stored by the operating system, often requires escalated privileges. Examples of local system data include authentication tokens, the device keyboard cache, Wi-Fi passwords, and photos. On Android, adversaries may also attempt to access files from external storage which may require additional storage-related permissions.
        ~Keyboard Cache Access: Adversaries may access the keyboard cache to collect previously typed text, including passwords and sensitive information
        ~Authentication Token Collection: Adversaries may target stored authentication tokens, cookies, and session data
        ~External Storage Access: Adversaries may access data stored on external storage like SD cards or shared storage areas
        ~Photo Library Access: Adversaries may collect photos, screenshots, and other media files stored on the device
        ~Configuration File Collection: Adversaries may gather system and app configuration files containing sensitive data
        ~Credential Storage Access: Adversaries may target built-in credential stores containing saved passwords and certificates
        ~Browser Data Collection: Adversaries may collect browser history, bookmarks, and cached web content
        ~App Cache Access: Adversaries may collect cached data from installed applications containing sensitive information
        ~System Logs Collection: Adversaries may gather system logs containing usage patterns and sensitive data
        ~Backup File Access: Adversaries may target local backup files containing consolidated sensitive data
        ~SMS/MMS Storage Access: Adversaries may collect stored text messages and multimedia messages
        ~Call Log Collection: Adversaries may gather call history and contact information
        ~Calendar Data Access: Adversaries may collect calendar entries and scheduled events
        ~Location History Collection: Adversaries may gather stored GPS coordinates and location data
        ~Clipboard Data Access: Adversaries may collect data copied to the device clipboard
        ~Device Health Data: Adversaries may collect stored health and fitness information
        ~Biometric Data Storage Access: Adversaries may target stored biometric templates and related data
        ~Device Management Data Collection: Adversaries may gather enterprise management and policy data
      Abuse Accessibility Features: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417), [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), and [Input Prompt](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1411) where appropriate.**  A malicious app could abuse Android's accessibility features to capture sensitive data or perform other malicious actions.(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android to emulate a user's clicks, for example to steal money from a user's bank account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: banking-trojans-google-play)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android devices to evade defenses by repeatedly clicking the "Back" button when a targeted app manager or mobile security app is launched, or when strings suggesting uninstallation are detected in the foreground. This effectively prevents the malicious application from being uninstalled.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)
        ~Screen Reader Abuse: Abuse of screen reader accessibility services to capture screen content and sensitive information displayed on the device
        ~Touch Event Simulation: Using accessibility services to programmatically simulate touch events and gestures
        ~Back Button Manipulation: Automated triggering of back button events to prevent security app access or uninstallation
        ~Accessibility Service Persistence: Exploiting accessibility services to maintain persistent access and prevent removal
        ~UI Automation Abuse: Abuse of UI automation features to perform unauthorized actions on behalf of the user
        ~Keyboard Event Interception: Capturing keyboard inputs through accessibility services to steal credentials
        ~Dialog Dismissal: Automatically dismissing security warnings or consent dialogs using accessibility features
        ~App Switcher Manipulation: Abusing accessibility features to monitor or manipulate app switching behavior
      Screen Capture: Adversaries may use screen capture to collect additional information about a target device, such as applications running in the foreground, user data, credentials, or other sensitive information. Applications running in the background can capture screenshots or videos of another application running in the foreground by using the Android `MediaProjectionManager` (generally requires the device user to grant consent).(Citation: Fortinet screencap July 2019)(Citation: Android ScreenCap1 2019) Background applications can also use Android accessibility services to capture screen contents being displayed by a foreground application.(Citation: Lookout-Monokle) An adversary with root access or Android Debug Bridge (adb) access could call the Android `screencap` or `screenrecord` commands.(Citation: Android ScreenCap2 2019)(Citation: Trend Micro ScreenCap July 2015)
        ~Media Projection API: Use of Android MediaProjectionManager to capture screen content, requiring user consent but providing full screen capture capabilities
        ~Accessibility Services Abuse: Exploitation of Android accessibility services to capture screen content without explicit screen capture permissions
        ~Root-level Commands: Direct use of screencap/screenrecord commands through root or ADB access
        ~Foreground App Targeting: Selective capture of specific foreground applications or activities
        ~Automated Capture: Periodic or event-triggered screen captures based on specific user actions or application states
        ~Credential Field Targeting: Specific focus on capturing screens containing login forms or credential input
        ~Silent Capture: Screen capture without user notification using various OS vulnerabilities
        ~Background Service Capture: Continuous monitoring and capture through persistent background services
      Access Contact List: An adversary could call standard operating system APIs from a malicious application to gather contact list (i.e., address book) data, or with escalated privileges could directly access files containing contact list data.
        ~API Access: Using standard mobile OS APIs to read contact information through legitimate permissions
        ~Direct File System Access: Exploiting elevated privileges to directly read contact database files
        ~Backup Extraction: Accessing contact information from device backups
        ~Content Provider Abuse: Exploiting Android's content provider mechanisms
        ~Contact Sync Services: Intercepting or abusing contact synchronization services
        ~Social Media Integration: Exploiting contact list access through social media app permissions
        ~MDM Abuse: Leveraging Mobile Device Management systems to access contact data
        ~Cross-App Data Access: Exploiting shared storage or IPC mechanisms to access contacts
      Archive Collected Data: Adversaries may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing data can help to obfuscate its contents and minimize use of network resources. Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender.      Both compression and encryption are done prior to exfiltration, and can be performed using a utility, programming library, or custom algorithm.
        ~Local Storage Compression: Use of mobile device's built-in compression APIs or libraries to compress collected data locally
        ~Container-Based Archive: Packaging collected data into encrypted containers using mobile app sandboxing
        ~Custom Encryption Implementation: Using custom encryption algorithms or implementations specific to mobile malware
        ~Third-Party Archive Utilities: Leveraging legitimate mobile archiving apps or libraries
        ~Native OS Archive Features: Using built-in mobile OS compression and encryption capabilities
        ~Memory-Based Compression: Using RAM-based compression to avoid writing to disk
        ~Cloud Service Integration: Leveraging cloud storage APIs for temporary compressed storage
        ~Cross-Platform Archive Format: Using compression formats compatible across mobile/desktop platforms
        ~Hardware-Accelerated Compression: Utilizing mobile device hardware compression capabilities
        ~Temporary Cache Archive: Using app cache directories for temporary compressed storage
      Adversary-in-the-Middle: Adversaries may attempt to position themselves between two or more networked devices to support follow-on behaviors such as [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002) or [Endpoint Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1642).       [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1638) can be achieved through several mechanisms. For example, a malicious application may register itself as a VPN client, effectively redirecting device traffic to adversary-owned resources. Registering as a VPN client requires user consent on both Android and iOS; additionally, a special entitlement granted by Apple is needed for iOS devices. Alternatively, a malicious application with escalation privileges may utilize those privileges to gain access to network traffic.       Specific to Android devices, adversary-in-the-disk is a type of AiTM attack where adversaries monitor and manipulate data that is exchanged between applications and external storage.(Citation: mitd_kaspersky)(Citation: mitd_checkpoint)(Citation: mitd_checkpoint_research) To accomplish this, a malicious application firsts requests for access to multimedia files on the device (`READ_EXTERNAL STORAGE` and `WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE`), then the application reads data on the device and/or writes malware to the device. Though the request for access is common, when used maliciously, adversaries may access files and other sensitive data due to abusing the permission. Multiple applications were shown to be vulnerable against this attack; however, scrutiny of permissions and input validations may mitigate this attack.      Outside of a mobile device, adversaries may be able to capture traffic by employing a rogue base station or Wi-Fi access point. These devices will allow adversaries to capture network traffic after it has left the device, while it is flowing to its destination. On a local network, enterprise techniques could be used, such as [ARP Cache Poisoning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/002) or [DHCP Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/003).       If applications properly encrypt their network traffic, sensitive data may not be accessible to adversaries, depending on the point of capture. For example, properly implementing Apple’s Application Transport Security (ATS) and Android’s Network Security Configuration (NSC) may prevent sensitive data leaks.(Citation: NSC_Android)
        ~Malicious VPN Application: Adversaries register malicious apps as VPN clients to intercept network traffic
        ~Storage Access Manipulation: Adversaries exploit storage permissions to intercept data between apps and external storage
        ~Rogue Base Station: Adversaries deploy fake cellular base stations to intercept mobile device communications
        ~Evil Twin Wi-Fi: Adversaries create fraudulent Wi-Fi access points that mimic legitimate ones
        ~Certificate Manipulation: Adversaries install malicious certificates to intercept encrypted traffic
        ~Network Protocol Abuse: Adversaries exploit weaknesses in mobile network protocols to intercept traffic
        ~Bluetooth Proxy: Adversaries intercept Bluetooth communications between mobile devices and peripherals
        ~NFC Relay: Adversaries position themselves between NFC transactions to intercept or manipulate data
        ~Mobile Proxy Manipulation: Adversaries configure malicious proxy settings through mobile device management or configuration profiles
        ~Inter-App Communication Interception: Adversaries exploit mobile inter-process communication mechanisms to intercept data between apps
      Location Tracking: Adversaries may track a device’s physical location through use of standard operating system APIs via malicious or exploited applications on the compromised device.      On Android, applications holding the `ACCESS_COAURSE_LOCATION` or `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` permissions provide access to the device’s physical location. On Android 10 and up, declaration of the `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` permission in an application’s manifest will allow applications to request location access even when the application is running in the background.(Citation: Android Request Location Permissions) Some adversaries have utilized integration of Baidu map services to retrieve geographical location once the location access permissions had been obtained.(Citation: PaloAlto-SpyDealer)(Citation: Palo Alto HenBox)      On iOS, applications must include the `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`, `NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription`, and/or `NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription` keys in their `Info.plist` file depending on the extent of requested access to location information.(Citation: Apple Requesting Authorization for Location Services) On iOS 8.0 and up, applications call `requestWhenInUseAuthorization()` to request access to location information when the application is in use or `requestAlwaysAuthorization()` to request access to location information regardless of whether the application is in use. With elevated privileges, an adversary may be able to access location data without explicit user consent with the `com.apple.locationd.preauthorized` entitlement key.(Citation: Google Project Zero Insomnia)
        Impersonate SS7 Nodes: Adversaries may exploit the lack of authentication in signaling system network nodes to track the location of mobile devices by impersonating a node.(Citation: Engel-SS7)(Citation: Engel-SS7-2008)(Citation: 3GPP-Security)(Citation: Positive-SS7)(Citation: CSRIC5-WG10-FinalReport)      By providing the victim’s MSISDN (phone number) and impersonating network internal nodes to query subscriber information from other nodes, adversaries may use data collected from each hop to eventually determine the device’s geographical cell area or nearest cell tower.(Citation: Engel-SS7)
          Use SS7 exploitation frameworks like SigPloit to send forged MAP_SEND_ROUTING_INFO_FOR_SM messages
          Impersonate HLR (Home Location Register) nodes to query VLR (Visitor Location Register) for subscriber location
          Send fraudulent ATI (Any Time Interrogation) queries to obtain MSC/VLR addresses
          Exploit SCCP Global Title Translation to route malicious SS7 messages
          Use SS7 protocol analyzers to capture and replay legitimate network messages
          Deploy rogue MSC (Mobile Switching Center) nodes to intercept location updates
          Send PSI (Provide Subscriber Info) messages to gather cell ID information
          Exploit MAP (Mobile Application Part) protocol to query subscriber routing information
          Use compromised STP (Signal Transfer Point) nodes to redirect legitimate queries
          Deploy SS7 message generators to flood network with location tracking requests
          Exploit TCAP (Transaction Capabilities Application Part) to forge location update messages
          Use SS7 fuzzing tools to discover vulnerable network nodes
          Impersonate SMSC (Short Message Service Center) to trigger location updates
          Deploy fake BSC (Base Station Controller) nodes to gather cell information
          Exploit MAP_UPDATE_LOCATION messages to track subscriber movement
          Use SS7 packet crafting tools to create custom tracking messages
          Impersonate GMLC (Gateway Mobile Location Centre) to request precise location data
          Exploit E.214/E.164 address translation for cross-network tracking
          Deploy SS7 sniffers at strategic network points to intercept location data
          Use compromised MSC/VLR pairs to create location tracking mesh
          Exploit SS7/SIGTRAN interworking nodes for hybrid attacks
          Use SS7 message correlation to track devices across network boundaries
          Exploit roaming interfaces to track international movement
          Deploy timing-based SS7 attacks to correlate subscriber presence
          Abuse SS7 backup routes for stealthy tracking
          Exploit MAP version differences between networks for tracking
          Use SS7 over IP (SIGTRAN) to bypass traditional SS7 security
          Exploit inter-carrier gateway vulnerabilities for cross-operator tracking
          Use SS7 protocol state manipulation for persistent tracking
          Deploy distributed SS7 probing for large-scale location tracking
        Remote Device Management Services: An adversary may use access to cloud services (e.g. Google's Android Device Manager or Apple iCloud's Find my iPhone) or to an enterprise mobility management (EMM)/mobile device management (MDM) server console to track the location of mobile devices managed by the service.(Citation: Krebs-Location)
          Access Android Device Manager through compromised Google account credentials to track device location in real-time
          Exploit Apple iCloud Find My service using phished Apple ID credentials to monitor device movements
          Compromise Microsoft Intune MDM console to track enterprise-enrolled devices
          Access VMware Workspace ONE (AirWatch) admin console to retrieve location data of managed devices
          Use MobileIron MDM administrative access to pull GPS coordinates of enrolled devices
          Leverage compromised IBM MaaS360 MDM portal to track corporate device fleet locations
          Access BlackBerry UEM console with stolen admin credentials to monitor device locations
          Exploit Citrix Endpoint Management (formerly XenMobile) to track managed devices
          Use compromised Jamf Pro MDM to monitor locations of enrolled Apple devices
          Access Samsung Knox Manage portal to track location of Samsung enterprise devices
          Exploit Sophos Mobile admin console to track managed device locations
          Access ManageEngine Mobile Device Manager Plus to monitor device GPS data
          Use compromised SOTI MobiControl dashboard for device location tracking
          Leverage carrier/ISP device management portals to obtain location data
          Access Hexnode MDM console to track enterprise device locations
          Exploit Cisco Meraki Systems Manager to monitor managed device positions
          Use compromised Miradore MDM portal for location tracking
          Access 42Gears SureMDM console to retrieve device location information
          Leverage compromised Scalefusion (formerly MobiLock) MDM for location monitoring
          Exploit Matrix42 EMM solution to track enterprise device locations
          Access Samsung SmartThings Find through compromised Samsung account
          Exploit Huawei Mobile Cloud device locator using stolen credentials
          Use compromised Apple Business Manager/School Manager for institutional device tracking
          Access Google Workspace (G Suite) admin console to track organization devices
          Exploit Lenovo ThinkShield device management for location tracking
          Use compromised FileWave MDM in educational environments
          Access LightSpeed Mobile Manager used in educational institutions
          Exploit HP TechPulse device management system for location data
          Use compromised Mosyle Business/Manager MDM for Apple device tracking
          Access Dell Mobile Management for enterprise device location monitoring
          Access T-Mobile/Sprint Device Management Portal for enterprise customer locations
          Exploit Verizon Enterprise Device Management system for fleet tracking
          Use compromised AT&T Business Direct Portal for device location monitoring
          Access Vodafone Business Mobile Device Management console
          Exploit Orange Business Services device management platform
          Use compromised Telefónica Mobile Device Management portal
          Access Deutsche Telekom Mobile Device Services console
          Exploit NTT Mobile Device Management platform
          Use compromised China Mobile Enterprise Management system
          Access Telstra Enterprise Mobile Management platform
    command-and-control: The adversary is trying to communicate with compromised devices to control them.  The command and control tactic represents how adversaries communicate with systems under their control within a target network. There are many ways an adversary can establish command and control with various levels of covertness, depending on system configuration and network topology. Due to the wide degree of variation available to the adversary at the network level, only the most common factors were used to describe the differences in command and control. There are still a great many specific techniques within the documented methods, largely due to how easy it is to define new protocols and use existing, legitimate protocols and network services for communication.   The resulting breakdown should help convey the concept that detecting intrusion through command and control protocols without prior knowledge is a difficult proposition over the long term. Adversaries' main constraints in network-level defense avoidance are testing and deployment of tools to rapidly change their protocols, awareness of existing defensive technologies, and access to legitimate Web services that, when used appropriately, make their tools difficult to distinguish from benign traffic.  Additionally, in the mobile environment, mobile devices are frequently connected to networks outside enterprise control such as cellular networks or public Wi-Fi networks. Adversaries could attempt to evade detection by communicating on these networks, and potentially even by using non-Internet Protocol mechanisms such as Short Message Service (SMS). However, cellular networks often have data caps and/or extra data charges that could increase the potential for adversarial communication to be detected.
      Domain Generation Algorithms: Adversaries may use [Domain Generation Algorithms](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1520) (DGAs) to procedurally generate domain names for command and control communication, and other uses such as malicious application distribution.(Citation: securelist rotexy 2018)  DGAs increase the difficulty for defenders to block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions.
        ~Time-seeded Generation: Uses system time or online time servers as seed for generating domain names
        ~Word-based Generation: Combines dictionary words or syllables to create domain-like strings
        ~Cryptographic Hash-based: Uses cryptographic hash functions to generate pseudo-random domain names
        ~Permutation-based: Creates variations by permuting fixed character sets or patterns
        ~Multi-TLD Generation: Generates domains across multiple top-level domains
        ~Character Set Mutation: Modifies character sets using substitution or transformation rules
        ~Length-varying Generation: Creates domains of varying lengths to avoid pattern detection
        ~Seed-sharing Generation: Uses shared seeds between malware instances for coordinated generation
        ~Locale-specific Generation: Generates domains that match linguistic patterns of target regions
        ~Hybrid Generation: Combines multiple generation methods to increase complexity
        ~Backup Seed Generation: Uses alternative seed sources when primary source is unavailable
        ~Domain-blending Generation: Creates domains that mimic patterns of legitimate domains
        ~Base Encoding Generation: Uses various base encoding schemes (base32/64) for domain string generation
        ~Service-based Generation: Incorporates legitimate service names or patterns into generated domains
        ~Context-aware Generation: Adapts generation based on device or network context
      Encrypted Channel: Adversaries may explicitly employ a known encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if necessary secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files.
        SSL Pinning: Adversaries may use [SSL Pinning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/003)  to protect the C2 traffic from being intercepted and analyzed.  [SSL Pinning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/003)  is a technique commonly utilized by legitimate websites to ensure that encrypted communications are only allowed with a pre-defined certificate. If another certificate is presented, it could indicate device compromise, traffic interception, or another upstream issue. While benign usages are common, it is also possible for adversaries to abuse this technology to protect malicious C2 traffic.  In normal, not pinned SSL validation, when a client connects to a server using HTTPS, it typically checks whether the server’s SSL/TLS certificate is signed by a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) in the device’s trust store. If the certificate is valid and signed by a trusted CA, the connection is established. However, with [SSL Pinning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/003) , the client is configured to trust a specific SSL/TLS certificate or public key, rather than relying on the device’s trust store. This means that even if the server’s certificate is signed by a trusted CA, the client will only establish the connection of the certificate or key is pinned.  There are two types of [SSL Pinning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/003) :  1.	Certificate Pinning: The client stores a copy of the server’s certificate and compares it with the certificate received during the SSL handshake. If the certificates match, then the client proceeds with the connection. This approach also works with self-signed certificates.  2.	Public Key Pinning: Instead of pinning the entire certificate, the client pins just the public key extracted from the certificate. This is often more flexible, as it allows the server to renew its certificate without having to update the pinned certificate or breaking the SSL connection.
          Implement certificate pinning in Android malware using Network Security Configuration XML file to specify allowed certificates
          Use OkHttp's CertificatePinner class in Android malware to programmatically pin certificates
          Implement SSL pinning in iOS malware using NSURLSession delegate methods and certificate validation
          Use AFNetworking library in iOS malware to implement public key pinning
          Embed self-signed certificates in mobile malware and implement custom certificate validation logic
          Use TrustKit library to implement SSL pinning across both iOS and Android malware
          Implement certificate pinning through native code (JNI/NDK) to avoid easy detection and bypass
          Use certificate pinning with domain fronting to mask C2 traffic through legitimate CDNs
          Implement public key pinning with backup pins to maintain C2 connection if primary certificate expires
          Use SSL pinning in combination with certificate rotation to periodically change trusted certificates
          Implement SSL pinning using certificate hashing with SHA-256 to store certificate fingerprints
          Use dynamic code loading to inject SSL pinning logic at runtime to evade static analysis
          Implement SSL pinning with multiple fallback certificates stored in encrypted form
