      Commonly Used Port: Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend with normal network activity to avoid more detailed inspection.   They may use commonly open ports such as  * TCP:80 (HTTP) * TCP:443 (HTTPS) * TCP:25 (SMTP) * TCP/UDP:53 (DNS)  They may use the protocol associated with the port or a completely different protocol.
        ~HTTP Traffic: Adversaries may use HTTP traffic over port 80 to blend C2 communications with normal web traffic, making detection more difficult
        ~HTTPS Traffic: Adversaries may leverage HTTPS (port 443) to encrypt C2 traffic and make it appear as legitimate secure web traffic
        ~DNS Tunneling: Adversaries may tunnel C2 communications through DNS queries and responses on port 53
        ~Email Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may abuse SMTP (port 25) to send commands and receive responses through email-like traffic
        ~Protocol Spoofing: Adversaries may use a different protocol than what is normally associated with the port to evade protocol-based detection
        ~Web Service APIs: Adversaries may abuse legitimate web service APIs over standard HTTP/HTTPS ports to disguise C2 traffic
        ~Mobile App Traffic: Adversaries may disguise C2 traffic as legitimate mobile app communication over common ports
        ~Cloud Service Protocols: Adversaries may leverage common cloud service ports and protocols to blend C2 traffic with legitimate cloud communications
      Non-Standard Port: Adversaries may generate network traffic using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088 or port 587 as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data.
        ~HTTP Traffic Redirection: Using non-standard HTTP ports (8080, 8000, etc.) for C2 communication while masquerading as normal web traffic
        ~DNS Over Non-53: Using alternative ports for DNS tunneling to avoid standard DNS monitoring
        ~Mobile App Protocol Abuse: Exploiting mobile app protocols over non-standard ports to blend with legitimate traffic
        ~SSL/TLS Port Variation: Using encrypted communication over uncommon ports while maintaining SSL/TLS functionality
        ~Custom Protocol Ports: Implementing custom protocols over arbitrary ports to avoid protocol-based detection
        ~SMS Protocol Redirection: Abusing SMS gateway services over non-standard ports to tunnel C2 traffic
        ~Push Notification Abuse: Using non-standard ports for push notification services to maintain persistent C2
        ~VoIP Protocol Manipulation: Utilizing non-standard SIP/RTP ports for covert communication
        ~Cellular Data Protocol Abuse: Exploiting cellular data protocols over non-standard ports to bypass carrier monitoring
        ~Mobile API Port Hopping: Dynamically changing ports for mobile API communications to avoid detection
      Call Control: Adversaries may make, forward, or block phone calls without user authorization. This could be used for adversary goals such as audio surveillance, blocking or forwarding calls from the device owner, or C2 communication.  Several permissions may be used to programmatically control phone calls, including:  * `ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS` - Allows the application to answer incoming phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions) * `CALL_PHONE` - Allows the application to initiate a phone call without going through the Dialer interface(Citation: Android Permissions) * `PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS` - Allows the application to see the number being dialed during an outgoing call with the option to redirect the call to a different number or abort the call altogether(Citation: Android Permissions) * `MANAGE_OWN_CALLS` - Allows a calling application which manages its own calls through the self-managed `ConnectionService` APIs(Citation: Android Permissions) * `BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE` - Required permission when using a `ConnectionService`(Citation: Android Permissions) * `WRITE_CALL_LOG` - Allows an application to write to the device call log, potentially to hide malicious phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions)  When granted some of these permissions, an application can make a phone call without opening the dialer first. However, if an application desires to simply redirect the user to the dialer with a phone number filled in, it can launch an Intent using `Intent.ACTION_DIAL`, which requires no specific permissions. This then requires the user to explicitly initiate the call or use some form of [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) to programmatically initiate it.
        ~Automated Call Answering: Adversary uses ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS permission to automatically answer incoming calls for surveillance or data collection
        ~Unauthorized Call Initiation: Adversary uses CALL_PHONE permission to make calls without user interaction
        ~Call Redirection: Adversary uses PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS to redirect outgoing calls to different numbers
        ~Call Blocking: Adversary prevents specific incoming calls from reaching the device
        ~Call Log Manipulation: Adversary uses WRITE_CALL_LOG permission to modify or delete call history entries
        ~Silent Call Initiation: Adversary initiates calls without user interface indicators
        ~Call Service Manipulation: Adversary exploits ConnectionService APIs to manage calls
        ~Programmatic Call Control: Adversary uses Intent.ACTION_DIAL with input injection to automate calls
        ~Call Forwarding Configuration: Adversary modifies call forwarding settings to redirect calls
        ~Background Call Services: Adversary runs persistent background services to monitor and control calls
        ~Conference Call Manipulation: Adversary manipulates conference call features to add unauthorized participants
        ~VoIP Call Interception: Adversary specifically targets Voice over IP calls for manipulation
        ~Emergency Call Exploitation: Adversary manipulates emergency call handling for privilege escalation
        ~Carrier Signal Manipulation: Adversary exploits carrier-specific call control features
        ~Call Audio Manipulation: Adversary modifies call audio in real-time during transmission
      Remote Access Software: Adversaries may use legitimate remote access software, such as `VNC`, `TeamViewer`, `AirDroid`, `AirMirror`, etc., to establish an interactive command and control channel to target mobile devices.    Remote access applications may be installed and used post-compromise as an alternate communication channel for redundant access or as a way to establish an interactive remote session with the target device. They may also be used as a component of malware to establish a reverse connection to an adversary-controlled system or service. Installation of remote access tools may also include persistence.
        ~Commercial Remote Access Tools: Use of legitimate commercial remote access applications like TeamViewer Mobile or AnyDesk Mobile for C2
        ~Custom Mobile RAT Development: Creation and deployment of custom remote access trojans specifically designed for mobile platforms
        ~Screen Sharing Applications: Exploitation of legitimate screen sharing and remote support apps like AirDroid or AirMirror
        ~Mobile Device Management (MDM) Software: Abuse of enterprise MDM solutions for unauthorized remote access
        ~VNC-based Solutions: Implementation of Virtual Network Computing protocols for mobile device control
        ~Remote Debugging Tools: Misuse of developer tools and debugging interfaces like Android Debug Bridge (ADB)
        ~Remote Support Applications: Leveraging built-in or third-party remote support features
        ~Cloud-Based Remote Access: Using cloud services and APIs for remote device control
        ~Peer-to-Peer Remote Control: Direct device-to-device remote access implementations
        ~Mobile Remote Desktop Protocols: Implementation of remote desktop protocols adapted for mobile environments
        ~SMS-Based Remote Control: Using SMS commands and responses for basic remote control functionality
        ~Mobile App Store Abuse: Distributing remote access capabilities through legitimate app stores
        ~Browser-Based Remote Access: Using web-based remote access interfaces optimized for mobile devices
        ~Remote Access via Mobile Accessibility Features: Exploiting built-in accessibility services for remote control
        ~Instant Messaging App Abuse: Leveraging messaging apps' remote control features
        ~Mobile Game Remote Access: Hiding remote access functionality within mobile games
        ~Mobile Backup Software: Exploiting backup tools' remote access capabilities
        ~Remote File Management Tools: Using file management apps with remote access features
        ~Mobile Remote Printing Tools: Abusing remote printing applications for device access
        ~Mobile Device Synchronization Tools: Exploiting sync tools for remote control
        ~IoT Device Bridging: Using IoT device connections and their management apps for remote access
        ~Mobile Payment App Exploitation: Leveraging payment apps' remote access features for device control
        ~Cross-Platform Remote Access: Utilizing cross-platform frameworks for remote control
        ~Wearable Device Bridge: Using smartwatch or fitness device connections for remote access
        ~Mobile Virtualization Tools: Exploiting mobile virtualization platforms for remote access
        ~Remote Camera Control Apps: Abusing camera control apps for device access
        ~Mobile Voice Assistant Exploitation: Using voice assistant features for remote control
        ~Mobile Device Emulator Abuse: Leveraging emulator connections for remote access
        ~Mobile Health App Features: Exploiting health monitoring apps' remote capabilities
        ~Car Control App Abuse: Using vehicle control apps for device access
      Web Service: Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for relaying data to/from a compromised system. Popular websites and social media, acting as a mechanism for C2, may give a significant amount of cover. This is due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise. Web service providers commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.      Use of Web services may also protect back-end C2 infrastructure from discovery through malware binary analysis, or enable operational resiliency (since this infrastructure may be dynamically changed).
        Dead Drop Resolver: Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to host information that points to additional command and control (C2) infrastructure. Adversaries may post content, known as a dead drop resolver, on Web services with embedded (and often obfuscated/encoded) domains or IP addresses. Once infected, victims will reach out to and be redirected by these resolvers.      Popular websites and social media, acting as a mechanism for C2, may give a significant amount of cover. This is due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise. Web service providers commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.      Use of a dead drop resolver may also protect back-end C2 infrastructure from discovery through malware binary analysis, or enable operational resiliency (since this infrastructure may be dynamically changed).
