      Exfiltration Over Other Network Medium: Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over a different network medium than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a standard Internet connection, the exfiltration may occur, for example, via Bluetooth, or another radio frequency (RF) channel.   Adversaries may choose to do this if they have sufficient access or proximity, and the connection might not be secured or defended as well as the primary Internet-connected channel because it is not routed through the same enterprise network.
        ~Bluetooth Channel: Adversaries may leverage Bluetooth connections to exfiltrate data from compromised mobile devices to nearby receivers
        ~NFC Communication: Adversaries may use Near Field Communication (NFC) to transfer data when in close physical proximity
        ~Cellular Data Channel: Adversaries may switch to cellular data networks (3G/4G/5G) for exfiltration to bypass enterprise network monitoring
        ~SMS/MMS Channel: Adversaries may encode and transmit data through text or multimedia messages
        ~IR Communication: Adversaries may use infrared communication capabilities for short-range data transfer
        ~Wi-Fi Direct: Adversaries may establish peer-to-peer Wi-Fi connections outside of enterprise networks
        ~Audio Signals: Adversaries may encode data in audio signals transmitted through the device's speakers
        ~QR Code Display: Adversaries may display sensitive data as QR codes for optical capture by nearby devices
      Ingress Tool Transfer: Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from an external system onto a compromised device to facilitate follow-on actions. Files may be copied from an external adversary-controlled system through the command and control channel  or through alternate protocols with another tool such as FTP.
        ~Application Store Download: Transfer tools by publishing malicious apps to official or third-party app stores
        ~SMS/MMS Attachment Transfer: Transfer tools through SMS or MMS message attachments
        ~Direct USB Connection: Transfer tools through direct physical connection to device
        ~Web Browser Download: Transfer tools through malicious website downloads
        ~Email Attachment: Transfer tools through email attachments
        ~Bluetooth Transfer: Transfer tools using Bluetooth connections
        ~Cloud Storage Download: Transfer tools through cloud storage services
        ~Instant Messaging Transfer: Transfer tools through messaging app attachments
        ~QR Code Download: Transfer tools through scanning malicious QR codes
        ~Peer-to-Peer Transfer: Transfer tools using P2P file sharing networks
        ~NFC Transfer: Transfer tools using Near Field Communication capabilities
        ~Mobile Device Management Push: Transfer tools by compromising MDM systems
        ~Backup Restoration: Transfer tools through compromised device backup/restore processes
        ~AirDrop-like Services: Transfer tools using platform-specific proximity sharing services
        ~Mobile Sync Software: Transfer tools through desktop sync applications
        ~Wi-Fi Direct Transfer: Transfer tools using direct Wi-Fi connections between devices
        ~SD Card Transfer: Transfer tools via removable storage media
        ~Cross-Device Sync: Transfer tools through platform ecosystem sync features
        ~Developer Mode Transfer: Transfer tools using developer/debug modes
        ~RCS Message Transfer: Transfer tools through Rich Communication Services
        ~VoIP App Transfer: Transfer tools through Voice-over-IP applications
        ~Enterprise App Deployment: Transfer tools through enterprise app distribution systems
        ~Carrier Update Channel: Transfer tools by compromising carrier update mechanisms
      Out of Band Data: Adversaries may communicate with compromised devices using out of band data streams. This could be done for a variety of reasons, including evading network traffic monitoring, as a backup method of command and control, or for data exfiltration if the device is not connected to any Internet-providing networks (i.e. cellular or Wi-Fi). Several out of band data streams exist, such as SMS messages, NFC, and Bluetooth.      On Android, applications can read push notifications to capture content from SMS messages, or other out of band data streams. This requires that the user manually grant notification access to the application via the settings menu. However, the application could launch an Intent to take the user directly there.      On iOS, there is no way to programmatically read push notifications.
        ~SMS-Based Communication: Adversaries may use SMS messages to send commands and receive data from compromised mobile devices
        ~Bluetooth Communication: Adversaries may leverage Bluetooth connections to communicate with nearby compromised devices
        ~NFC Communication: Adversaries may use Near Field Communication for short-range command and control
        ~Audio Signals: Adversaries may use high-frequency or ultrasonic audio signals to transmit data between devices
        ~QR Code Communication: Adversaries may use visual QR codes to transmit commands or receive data
        ~Infrared Communication: Adversaries may utilize infrared signals for short-range device communication
        ~Light-Based Communication: Adversaries may use visible light communication (VLC) through device screens or cameras
        ~Cellular Signaling: Adversaries may exploit cellular control channels outside normal data communication
      Application Layer Protocol: Adversaries may communicate using application layer protocols to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the mobile device, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the mobile device and server.   Adversaries may utilize many different protocols, including those used for web browsing, transferring files, electronic mail, or DNS.
        Web Protocols: Adversaries may communicate using application layer protocols associated with web protocols traffic to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to remote mobile devices, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the mobile client and server.   Web protocols such as HTTP and HTTPS are used for web traffic as well as well as notification services native to mobile messaging services such as Google Cloud Messaging (GCM) and newly, Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM), (GCM/FCM: two-way communication) and Apple Push Notification Service (APNS; one-way server-to-device).  Such notification services leverage HTTP/S via the respective API and are commonly abused on Android and iOS respectively in order blend in with routine device traffic making it difficult for enterprises to inspect.
          Use HTTP POST requests with encrypted payloads to send commands to compromised mobile devices
          Configure malware to use FCM for C2 communications by registering malicious app with Firebase
          Abuse APNS for iOS device control by exploiting compromised developer certificates
          Implement custom HTTP headers to disguise C2 traffic as legitimate API calls
          Use web sockets over HTTPS for real-time bidirectional communication
          Leverage URL parameters in HTTP GET requests to encode commands
          Exploit enterprise MDM configurations to bypass notification service restrictions
          Create malicious proxy servers to relay C2 traffic through legitimate web services
          Implement domain generation algorithms (DGA) for dynamic C2 endpoints
          Use legitimate CDN services as proxies for C2 communication
          Abuse Twitter API for C2 by encoding commands in tweets
          Use Microsoft Push Notification Service (MPNS) for Windows mobile C2
          Implement steganography in HTTP image requests for covert C2
          Abuse mobile ad network APIs for command distribution
          Exploit WebView components for executing C2 commands
          Use public cloud storage APIs (Dropbox/Google Drive) as C2 channels
          Implement HTTP/2 protocol features to avoid detection
          Abuse progressive web app (PWA) service workers for persistent C2
          Use webhook services for automated C2 communication
          Exploit mobile browser push notifications for C2
          Abuse enterprise single sign-on (SSO) tokens for persistent C2 access
          Exploit app store CDN infrastructure for command distribution
          Tunnel C2 traffic through mobile VPN configurations
          Use mobile device management (MDM) APIs for unauthorized C2
          Exploit mobile browser extension APIs for C2 communication
          Abuse cross-device sync features in mobile browsers for C2
          Implement WebRTC data channels for peer-to-peer C2
          Exploit mobile payment gateway APIs for covert C2 traffic
          Use mobile game leaderboard/achievement APIs for C2
          Abuse mobile analytics services for command distribution
          Abuse mobile instant messaging APIs (WhatsApp, Telegram) for C2
          Exploit smart home device APIs to relay C2 traffic
          Abuse mobile app deep linking mechanisms for C2
          Exploit cloud backup service APIs for delayed C2 communication
          Use compromised React Native bridge for cross-platform C2
          Abuse mobile contact sync APIs for command distribution
          Exploit mobile calendar invitation system for C2
          Use mobile device health/fitness APIs for covert C2
          Abuse mobile keyboard extension APIs for keylogging and C2
          Exploit mobile app content provider vulnerabilities for lateral C2
    exfiltration: The adversary is trying to steal data.  Exfiltration refers to techniques and attributes that result or aid in the adversary removing files and information from the targeted mobile device.  In the mobile environment, mobile devices are frequently connected to networks outside enterprise control such as cellular networks or public Wi-Fi networks. Adversaries could attempt to evade detection by communicating on these networks, and potentially even by using non-Internet Protocol mechanisms such as Short Message Service (SMS). However, cellular networks often have data caps and/or extra data charges that could increase the potential for adversarial communication to be detected.
      Commonly Used Port: Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend with normal network activity to avoid more detailed inspection.   They may use commonly open ports such as  * TCP:80 (HTTP) * TCP:443 (HTTPS) * TCP:25 (SMTP) * TCP/UDP:53 (DNS)  They may use the protocol associated with the port or a completely different protocol.
        ~HTTP/HTTPS Communication: Using standard web ports 80/443 to blend malicious traffic with normal mobile web browsing
        ~DNS Tunneling: Encapsulating data in DNS queries and responses over port 53
        ~Email Protocol Abuse: Utilizing SMTP/IMAP/POP3 ports for data exfiltration
        ~Mobile App Protocol Masquerading: Disguising exfiltration traffic as legitimate mobile app communications
        ~Push Notification Channel Abuse: Using ports associated with mobile push notification services
        ~VoIP Protocol Abuse: Exploiting ports used for voice and video communications
        ~Instant Messaging Port Abuse: Using ports commonly associated with messaging apps
        ~Cloud Service Port Abuse: Exploiting ports used by common cloud storage and sync services
        ~Mobile Game Protocol Abuse: Using ports associated with mobile game traffic
        ~Mobile Ad Network Abuse: Exploiting ports used by mobile advertising networks
        ~NTP Protocol Abuse: Exploiting port 123 used for time synchronization
        ~Mobile Device Management Port Abuse: Using ports associated with MDM solutions
        ~Certificate Validation Port Abuse: Exploiting ports used for certificate checking and validation
        ~Mobile Authentication Protocol Abuse: Using ports associated with OAuth and SSO services
        ~Mobile Backup Service Abuse: Exploiting ports used by backup and sync services
        ~Mobile Analytics Port Abuse: Using ports associated with mobile analytics services
        ~Mobile Payment Protocol Abuse: Exploiting ports used by payment processing services
        ~Mobile Health App Port Abuse: Using ports associated with health monitoring apps
        ~Mobile Print Service Port Abuse: Exploiting ports used for mobile printing protocols
        ~Bluetooth Service Port Abuse: Using ports associated with Bluetooth services and tethering
        ~Mobile Location Service Abuse: Exploiting ports used by location and mapping services
        ~Mobile Calendar Sync Port Abuse: Using ports associated with calendar synchronization
        ~Mobile Contact Sync Port Abuse: Exploiting ports used for contact synchronization
        ~Mobile File Sharing Port Abuse: Using ports associated with peer-to-peer file sharing
        ~Mobile Remote Control Port Abuse: Exploiting ports used for remote access applications
      Exfiltration Over C2 Channel: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an existing command and control channel. Stolen data is encoded into the normal communications channel using the same protocol as command and control communications.
        ~SMS-Based Exfiltration: Adversaries encode stolen data into SMS messages sent through the existing C2 channel, leveraging cellular networks for data transfer
        ~Push Notification Exfiltration: Adversaries leverage mobile push notification services to exfiltrate data through existing C2 infrastructure
        ~Mobile App Protocol Abuse: Adversaries hide exfiltrated data within legitimate mobile app protocol traffic
        ~Cellular Data Channel Exfiltration: Adversaries use mobile device's cellular data connection for data exfiltration through C2
        ~Bluetooth C2 Exfiltration: Adversaries leverage Bluetooth connections as C2 channels for data exfiltration
        ~Mobile VPN Tunneling: Adversaries tunnel exfiltrated data through VPN connections established for C2
        ~Mobile Messaging App Abuse: Adversaries leverage legitimate messaging apps' protocols for C2 and data exfiltration
        ~Near Field Communication (NFC) Channel: Adversaries use NFC capabilities for short-range data exfiltration
      Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over a different protocol than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.   Alternate protocols include FTP, SMTP, HTTP/S, DNS, SMB, or any other network protocol not being used as the main command and control channel. Different protocol channels could also include Web services such as cloud storage. Adversaries may opt to also encrypt and/or obfuscate these alternate channels.
        Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol: Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an un-encrypted network protocol other than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.  Adversaries may opt to obfuscate this data, without the use of encryption, within network protocols that are natively unencrypted (such as HTTP, FTP, or DNS). Adversaries may employ custom or publicly available encoding/compression algorithms (such as base64) or embed data within protocol headers and fields.
          Use DNS TXT records with base64-encoded data chunks to exfiltrate data via DNS queries
          Embed data in HTTP GET request parameters using URL encoding
          Upload data to public FTP servers using anonymous login credentials
          Hide data in HTTP custom headers with custom encoding schemes
          Embed data in DNS query names using hex encoding
          Use HTTP POST requests with data hidden in form fields
          Utilize ICMP echo requests with data embedded in the payload
          Transfer data through SMTP by embedding in email headers
          Use HTTP cookies to store and transmit encoded data
          Embed data in DNS AAAA records using custom binary-to-IPv6 encoding
          Split data into small chunks and embed in NetBIOS name queries
          Use SNMP community strings to carry encoded data
          Transmit data through NTP server pool requests with data in optional fields
          Embed data in malformed DHCP requests using vendor-specific options
          Use modified TFTP requests with data hidden in filename fields
          Transmit data through broadcast UDP packets with custom headers
          Hide data in malformed ARP requests using padding fields
          Use multicast packets with data embedded in unused protocol fields
          Embed data in SSDP discovery messages
          Use modified LLMNR queries with encoded data in subdomain fields
          Embed data in malformed LDAP queries using attribute fields
          Use Finger protocol with encoded data in username requests
          Transmit data through RIP routing updates in unused metric fields
          Hide data in Gopher protocol requests using selector strings
          Use modified WHOIS queries with data in domain registration fields
          Embed data in IRC NICK/JOIN commands using nickname fields
          Use modified POP3 commands with data in mailbox names
          Transmit through NNTP newsgroup posts with data in message headers
          Hide data in modified Telnet option negotiations
          Use XDMCP queries with data embedded in display parameters
          Use modified RADIUS accounting packets with data in attribute fields
          Embed data in RTP protocol padding fields of dummy VoIP streams
          Hide data in CUPS printer protocol job attributes
          Use modified IPMI BMC messages with data in custom OEM fields
          Transmit through MQTT publish messages with data in topic fields
          Hide data in mDNS service discovery responses
          Use modified UPnP device discovery messages
          Embed data in TACACS+ authorization requests
          Use NFS file attributes for data transmission
          Hide data in IMAP folder names and flags
          Hide data in Modbus TCP register read/write requests
          Use BACnet device property fields for data transmission
          Embed data in RTSP session parameters
          Transmit through DICOM medical imaging metadata fields
          Use CoAP observe messages with data in token fields
          Hide data in XMPP presence status messages
          Use SIP registration messages with data in contact fields
          Embed data in SOAP service discovery requests
          Use PPTP control messages with data in call ID fields
          Hide data in SCTP chunk parameters
          Use LDAP extended operations with data in controls field
          Embed data in BGP community strings
          Hide data in EIGRP TLV fields
          Use OCSP status requests with data in nonce fields
          Transmit through STUN binding requests
          Hide data in DTLS cookie exchange
          Use QUIC transport parameters for data transmission
          Embed data in TURN channel data messages
          Use SCCP (Skinny) device registration messages
          Hide data in CAPWAP control messages
    impact: The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, or destroy your devices and data.  The impact tactic consists of techniques used by the adversary to execute his or her mission objectives but that do not cleanly fit into another category such as Collection. Mission objectives vary based on each adversary's goals, but examples include toll fraud, destruction of device data, or locking the user out of his or her device until a ransom is paid.
      Manipulate App Store Rankings or Ratings: An adversary could use access to a compromised device's credentials to attempt to manipulate app store rankings or ratings by triggering application downloads or posting fake reviews of applications. This technique likely requires privileged access (a rooted or jailbroken device).
        ~Automated Review Generation: Use automated scripts or bots to generate and post fake reviews at scale using compromised device credentials
        ~Mass Download Automation: Deploy networks of compromised devices to artificially inflate download counts through automated installations
        ~Rating Manipulation via API: Exploit app store APIs using compromised credentials to directly modify ratings
        ~Review Bombing: Coordinate mass negative reviews to intentionally lower competitor app ratings
        ~Credential Harvesting for Reviews: Collect and leverage legitimate user credentials specifically for posting fraudulent reviews
        ~Emulator-Based Downloads: Use device emulators to simulate multiple unique devices downloading target apps
        ~Click Farming: Employ networks of human operators to manually post fake reviews and ratings
        ~In-App Purchase Manipulation: Artificially inflate app revenue metrics through fraudulent in-app purchases
      Generate Fraudulent Advertising Revenue: An adversary could seek to generate fraudulent advertising revenue from mobile devices, for example by triggering automatic clicks of advertising links without user involvement.
        ~Click Fraud: Automatically generating fake clicks on advertisements to simulate user engagement and trigger payment from ad networks
        ~Ad Stacking: Loading multiple ads in a single ad slot where only one is visible to generate impressions for hidden ads
        ~Ad Injection: Inserting unauthorized advertisements into legitimate apps or webpages
        ~Background Ad Loading: Loading advertisements while the app is running in the background without user awareness
        ~Bot Traffic Generation: Using automated scripts or bots to simulate real user traffic and trigger ad views
        ~SDK Manipulation: Exploiting advertising SDK vulnerabilities to generate false impressions or clicks
        ~Location Spoofing: Faking device location to trigger higher-paying geographic-targeted advertisements
        ~View Fraud: Manipulating viewability metrics to falsely report that ads were viewed by users
        ~Attribution Fraud: Falsifying install attribution to claim credit for app installations
        ~Ad Refresh Manipulation: Artificially increasing ad refresh rates to generate more impressions
        ~In-App Purchase Fraud: Manipulating in-app purchase systems to generate fraudulent revenue through fake transactions
        ~Ad Format Spoofing: Misrepresenting ad format types to claim higher rates (e.g., video as interactive)
        ~Device ID Rotation: Cycling through fake device identifiers to bypass fraud detection systems
        ~Hidden Ad Elements: Placing invisible or microscopic ad elements to generate impressions without detection
        ~Organic Traffic Simulation: Creating sophisticated patterns of behavior to mimic legitimate user traffic
        ~Cross-App Fraud: Exploiting multiple apps simultaneously to generate layered fraudulent revenue
        ~Ad Network Arbitrage: Exploiting price differences between ad networks for fraudulent profit
        ~Malvertising Integration: Combining ad fraud with malware distribution for multiple revenue streams
        ~Cookie Stuffing: Dropping third-party cookies to falsely claim commission on future purchases
        ~Premium Traffic Spoofing: Masquerading low-quality traffic as premium publisher inventory
        ~Reward App Manipulation: Exploiting reward-based advertising apps through automated task completion
        ~Engagement Metric Fraud: Falsifying user engagement metrics like time-spent or interaction rates
        ~Rich Media Ad Exploitation: Manipulating interactive or rich media ad units for higher payouts
        ~Ad Placement Manipulation: Modifying ad placement data to claim premium positioning revenue
        ~Affiliate Link Manipulation: Exploiting mobile affiliate marketing systems through fake referrals
        ~App Store Optimization Fraud: Gaming app store algorithms to increase visibility and ad exposure
        ~Social Ad Engagement Fraud: Generating fake social proof and engagement for social media ads
        ~Dynamic Ad Unit Exploitation: Manipulating responsive ad units to trigger higher-paying formats
        ~Push Notification Ad Fraud: Exploiting push notification advertising systems for fraudulent revenue
        ~Ad Mediation Exploitation: Gaming ad mediation platforms to force higher-paying ad network selection
      Call Control: Adversaries may make, forward, or block phone calls without user authorization. This could be used for adversary goals such as audio surveillance, blocking or forwarding calls from the device owner, or C2 communication.  Several permissions may be used to programmatically control phone calls, including:  * `ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS` - Allows the application to answer incoming phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions) * `CALL_PHONE` - Allows the application to initiate a phone call without going through the Dialer interface(Citation: Android Permissions) * `PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS` - Allows the application to see the number being dialed during an outgoing call with the option to redirect the call to a different number or abort the call altogether(Citation: Android Permissions) * `MANAGE_OWN_CALLS` - Allows a calling application which manages its own calls through the self-managed `ConnectionService` APIs(Citation: Android Permissions) * `BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE` - Required permission when using a `ConnectionService`(Citation: Android Permissions) * `WRITE_CALL_LOG` - Allows an application to write to the device call log, potentially to hide malicious phone calls(Citation: Android Permissions)  When granted some of these permissions, an application can make a phone call without opening the dialer first. However, if an application desires to simply redirect the user to the dialer with a phone number filled in, it can launch an Intent using `Intent.ACTION_DIAL`, which requires no specific permissions. This then requires the user to explicitly initiate the call or use some form of [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) to programmatically initiate it.
        ~Direct Call Initiation: Adversaries may use CALL_PHONE permission to make unauthorized calls directly without user interaction or the dialer interface
        ~Call Redirection: Adversaries may use PROCESS_OUTGOING_CALLS permission to intercept and redirect outgoing calls to different numbers
        ~Call Blocking: Adversaries may prevent incoming calls from reaching the device by programmatically ending or rejecting calls
        ~Call Log Manipulation: Adversaries may use WRITE_CALL_LOG permission to modify call history to hide malicious activity
        ~Automated Call Management: Adversaries may use ConnectionService APIs to programmatically manage calls for surveillance or C2 purposes
        ~Silent Call Answering: Adversaries may use ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS permission to automatically answer incoming calls without user awareness
        ~Call Forwarding Configuration: Adversaries may modify call forwarding settings to redirect calls to attacker-controlled numbers
        ~Programmatic USSD Code Execution: Adversaries may execute USSD codes through calls to modify network settings or perform unauthorized actions
      Clipboard Modification: Adversaries may abuse clipboard functionality to intercept and replace information in the Android device clipboard.(Citation: ESET Clipboard Modification February 2019)(Citation: Welivesecurity Clipboard Modification February 2019)(Citation: Syracuse Clipboard Modification 2014) Malicious applications may monitor the clipboard activity through the <code>ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener</code> interface on Android to determine when the clipboard contents have changed.(Citation: Dr.Webb Clipboard Modification origin2 August 2018)(Citation: Dr.Webb Clipboard Modification origin August 2018) Listening to clipboard activity, reading the clipboard contents, and modifying the clipboard contents requires no explicit application permissions and can be performed by applications running in the background, however, this behavior has changed with the release of Android 10.(Citation: Android 10 Privacy Changes)  Adversaries may use [Clipboard Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1510) to replace text prior to being pasted, for example, replacing a copied Bitcoin wallet address with a wallet address that is under adversarial control.  [Clipboard Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1510) had been seen within the Android/Clipper.C trojan. This sample had been detected by ESET in an application distributed through the Google Play Store targeting cryptocurrency wallet numbers.(Citation: ESET Clipboard Modification February 2019)
        ~Cryptocurrency Address Replacement: Adversaries specifically monitor and replace cryptocurrency wallet addresses in clipboard to redirect transactions
        ~Password Interception: Malware monitors clipboard for password patterns and captures or modifies them before pasting
        ~Background Service Monitoring: Continuous clipboard monitoring through persistent background services
        ~URL Modification: Detection and modification of URLs in clipboard to redirect users to malicious sites
        ~Selective Content Filtering: Monitoring for specific patterns or content types and selectively modifying matching data
        ~Cross-App Clipboard Exploitation: Exploiting clipboard sharing between different apps to capture sensitive data
        ~Delayed Modification: Implementing time-delayed modifications to avoid immediate detection
        ~Format-Preserving Modification: Maintaining the original format while replacing content to avoid suspicion
      Data Destruction: Adversaries may destroy data and files on specific devices or in large numbers to interrupt availability to systems, services, and network resources. Data destruction is likely to render stored data irrecoverable by forensic techniques through overwriting files or data on local and remote drives.    To achieve data destruction, adversaries may use the `pm uninstall` command to uninstall packages or the `rm` command to remove specific files. For example, adversaries may first use `pm uninstall` to uninstall non-system apps, and then use `rm (-f) <file(s)>` to delete specific files, further hiding malicious activity.(Citation: rootnik_rooting_tool)(Citation: abuse_native_linux_tools)
        ~System App Removal: Adversaries may specifically target and remove system applications to disrupt device functionality and destroy associated data.
        ~Storage Media Wiping: Adversaries may attempt to wipe entire storage partitions or external storage media like SD cards.
        ~File Overwriting: Adversaries may overwrite files with random data or zeros to prevent recovery.
        ~Backup Destruction: Adversaries may target and destroy local and cloud backup files to prevent data recovery.
        ~Configuration File Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt or delete system configuration files to render the device unusable.
        ~Database Destruction: Adversaries may target app databases to destroy structured data storage.
        ~Cache Clearing: Adversaries may clear application caches and temporary storage to remove recoverable data.
        ~Encryption Key Destruction: Adversaries may destroy encryption keys to make encrypted data permanently inaccessible.
      Modify System Partition: If an adversary can escalate privileges, he or she may be able to use those privileges to place malicious code in the device system partition, where it may persist after device resets and may not be easily removed by the device user.  Many Android devices provide the ability to unlock the bootloader for development purposes. An unlocked bootloader may provide the ability for an adversary to modify the system partition. Even if the bootloader is locked, it may be possible for an adversary to escalate privileges and then modify the system partition.
        ~Bootloader Unlock Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit device bootloader unlocking mechanisms to gain write access to system partitions, often leveraging manufacturer-provided unlock methods or vulnerabilities in bootloader security.
        ~System Update Manipulation: Adversaries may compromise or hijack the system update process to inject malicious code into system partitions during legitimate-appearing update operations.
        ~Root Privilege Abuse: After obtaining root privileges through various means, adversaries may directly mount and modify system partitions using root access capabilities.
        ~Recovery Mode Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse recovery mode features to modify system partitions, potentially bypassing normal system protections.
        ~Custom ROM Installation: Adversaries may replace legitimate system software with malicious custom ROMs that contain backdoors or other malicious code.
        ~System Library Injection: Adversaries may modify system libraries to maintain persistence and intercept system calls, replacing legitimate system libraries with malicious versions.
        ~Init Process Modification: Adversaries may modify init scripts or the init process itself in the system partition to ensure malicious code runs during system startup.
        ~System App Manipulation: Adversaries may modify or replace system apps with malicious versions that have elevated privileges and system-level access.
        ~Partition Table Tampering: Adversaries may modify the partition table structure to hide malicious code or redirect system operations to compromised partitions.
        ~Firmware-Level Persistence: Adversaries may modify system firmware components within the system partition to maintain persistence at a low level.
      Abuse Accessibility Features: **This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1417), [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516), and [Input Prompt](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1411) where appropriate.**  A malicious app could abuse Android's accessibility features to capture sensitive data or perform other malicious actions.(Citation: Skycure-Accessibility)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android to emulate a user's clicks, for example to steal money from a user's bank account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)(Citation: banking-trojans-google-play)  Adversaries may abuse accessibility features on Android devices to evade defenses by repeatedly clicking the "Back" button when a targeted app manager or mobile security app is launched, or when strings suggesting uninstallation are detected in the foreground. This effectively prevents the malicious application from being uninstalled.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa)
        ~Screen Reader Exploitation: Abuse of screen reader accessibility services to intercept and capture screen content and user interactions
        ~Touch Gesture Simulation: Misuse of accessibility services to programmatically simulate user touch gestures and interactions
        ~Back Button Automation: Automated triggering of back button events to prevent security app access or uninstallation
        ~Accessibility Service Persistence: Maintaining malicious accessibility service activation to ensure continuous access
        ~UI Component Manipulation: Using accessibility services to programmatically interact with UI elements without user consent
        ~Automated Dialog Dismissal: Automatically dismissing system dialogs and security warnings through accessibility services
        ~Text-to-Speech Interception: Capturing and manipulating text-to-speech output for malicious purposes
        ~Input Method Hijacking: Exploiting accessibility input method services to intercept user input
      Data Manipulation: Adversaries may insert, delete, or alter data in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. By manipulating data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making.  The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the target application, process, and the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system, typically gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign, in order to have the desired impact.
        Transmitted Data Manipulation: Adversaries may alter data en route to storage or other systems in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. By manipulating transmitted data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making.  Manipulation may be possible over a network connection or between system processes where there is an opportunity to deploy a tool that will intercept and change information. The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the target transmission mechanism as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system, typically gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign, in order to have the desired impact.  One method to achieve [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1641/001) is by modifying the contents of the device clipboard. Malicious applications may monitor clipboard activity through the `ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener` interface on Android to determine when clipboard contents have changed. Listening to clipboard activity, reading clipboard contents, and modifying clipboard contents requires no explicit application permissions and can be performed by applications running in the background. However, this behavior has changed with the release of Android 10.  Adversaries may use [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1641/001) to replace text prior to being pasted. For example, replacing a copied Bitcoin wallet address with a wallet address that is under adversarial control.  [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1641/001) was seen within the Android/Clipper.C trojan. This sample was detected by ESET in an application distributed through the Google Play Store targeting cryptocurrency wallet numbers.(Citation: ESET Clipboard Modification February 2019)
          Monitor clipboard content using ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener to intercept and replace cryptocurrency wallet addresses
          Deploy man-in-the-middle proxy to modify HTTP/HTTPS traffic between mobile apps and backend servers
          Intercept and modify SMS messages during transmission using malicious SMS apps
          Manipulate intent data during inter-component communication between Android apps
          Modify shared preferences or content provider data during cross-app data sharing
          Intercept and alter Bluetooth communications between mobile device and peripheral devices
          Manipulate NFC payment data during transmission between phone and payment terminal
          Modify backup data during cloud synchronization
          Tamper with mobile app API responses during client-server communication
          Intercept and modify data during app-to-app file transfers
          Intercept and modify WebView communication between JavaScript and native code
          Manipulate mobile payment QR code data during scanning
          Tamper with mobile app deep link parameters during URL handling
          Modify data during mobile app authentication token transmission
          Intercept and alter push notification payload data
          Manipulate mobile app cache data during synchronization
          Modify data during mobile app state restoration or saved instance transfer
          Intercept and alter mobile app analytics data transmission
          Manipulate data during mobile app certificate pinning verification
          Modify mobile app configuration data during remote config updates
          Manipulate data during mobile device enrollment with MDM systems
          Intercept and modify data during mobile app sideloading process
          Tamper with mobile app telemetry data during crash reporting
          Modify data during mobile app in-app purchase transactions
          Intercept and alter mobile app geolocation data transmission
          Manipulate data during mobile app auto-update processes
          Modify biometric authentication data during transmission to secure enclave
          Intercept and alter mobile app debugging/logging data transmission
          Manipulate data during mobile app backup/restore operations
          Modify mobile app DRM protection data during license verification
      Endpoint Denial of Service: Adversaries may perform Endpoint Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of services to users.  On Android versions prior to 7, apps can abuse Device Administrator access to reset the device lock passcode, preventing the user from unlocking the device. After Android 7, only device or profile owners (e.g. MDMs) can reset the device’s passcode.(Citation: Android resetPassword)  On iOS devices, this technique does not work because mobile device management servers can only remove the screen lock passcode; they cannot set a new passcode. However, on jailbroken devices, malware has been discovered that can lock the user out of the device.(Citation: Xiao-KeyRaider)
        ~Lock Screen Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate device lock screens through administrator privileges or exploits to prevent legitimate user access
        ~Resource Exhaustion: Adversaries may consume device resources (CPU, memory, storage) to make the device unusable
        ~Battery Depletion: Adversaries may execute processes that rapidly drain the device battery
        ~System Service Disruption: Adversaries may interfere with critical system services to cause device malfunction
        ~Storage Space Exhaustion: Adversaries may fill device storage to prevent normal operation
        ~Network Interface Manipulation: Adversaries may disable or interfere with device networking capabilities
        ~Process Overload: Adversaries may spawn numerous processes to overwhelm device resources
        ~Critical App Disruption: Adversaries may target and crash critical system applications
        ~Hardware API Abuse: Adversaries may abuse hardware APIs to cause physical stress on device components
        ~System Settings Manipulation: Adversaries may modify system settings to prevent normal device operation
        ~Input Sensor Blocking: Adversaries may interfere with touch screen, fingerprint readers, or other input sensors
        ~Notification Flooding: Adversaries may overwhelm the notification system to make device unusable
        ~Display Manipulation: Adversaries may force screen settings or overlays that prevent device use
        ~Update System Abuse: Adversaries may trigger continuous system updates or block critical security updates
        ~App Store Access Denial: Adversaries may prevent access to app stores and updates
        ~Background Service Flooding: Adversaries may spawn excessive background services
        ~Permission System Abuse: Adversaries may manipulate permission dialogs to prevent app functionality
        ~Device Administrator Abuse: Adversaries may abuse device admin features beyond lock screen manipulation
        ~SIM Card Interface Blocking: Adversaries may interfere with SIM card operations to prevent cellular service
        ~Emergency Call System Abuse: Adversaries may abuse emergency call systems to prevent normal phone operation
        ~Device Recovery Mode Forcing: Adversaries may force devices into recovery or safe modes
        ~Bluetooth Stack Exhaustion: Adversaries may overwhelm Bluetooth services to prevent device connectivity
        ~Mobile Radio Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate radio states to drain battery or prevent communications
        ~Secure Element Interference: Adversaries may disrupt secure element operations affecting payment and security features
        ~Runtime Environment Disruption: Adversaries may interfere with app runtime environments
      Network Denial of Service: Adversaries may perform Network Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of targeted resources to users. Network DoS can be performed by exhausting the network bandwidth that services rely on, or by jamming the signal going to or coming from devices.   A Network DoS will occur when an adversary is able to jam radio signals (e.g. Wi-Fi, cellular, GPS) around a device to prevent it from communicating. For example, to jam cellular signal, an adversary may use a handheld signal jammer, which jam devices within the jammer’s operational range.(Citation: NIST-SP800187)   Usage of cellular jamming has been documented in several arrests reported in the news.(Citation: CNET-Celljammer)(Citation: NYTimes-Celljam)(Citation: Digitaltrends-Celljam)(Citation: Arstechnica-Celljam)
        ~Cellular Signal Jamming: Use of portable jammers to block cellular frequencies and prevent mobile devices from connecting to cell towers
        ~WiFi Signal Interference: Generating interference in WiFi frequency bands to disrupt wireless connectivity
        ~GPS Signal Spoofing: Broadcasting fake GPS signals to prevent accurate location services
        ~Bluetooth Signal Jamming: Disrupting Bluetooth communications between mobile devices and peripherals
        ~Protocol Flooding: Overwhelming mobile devices with malformed network packets
        ~Bandwidth Exhaustion: Generating excessive traffic to consume available network bandwidth
        ~Resource Depletion: Forcing devices to maintain numerous connections until resources are exhausted
        ~Signal Path Interference: Physical obstruction or interference with signal propagation paths
      Delete Device Data: Adversaries may wipe a device or delete individual files in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. An application must have administrator access to fully wipe the device, while individual files may not require special permissions to delete depending on their storage location. (Citation: Android DevicePolicyManager 2019)  Stored data could include a variety of file formats, such as Office files, databases, stored emails, and custom file formats. The impact file deletion will have depends on the type of data as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary, but can include deleting update files to evade detection or deleting attacker-specified files for impact.
        ~Device Factory Reset: Adversaries may trigger a factory reset through system APIs or admin controls to completely wipe the device
        ~Selective File Deletion: Adversaries may target specific files or directories for deletion while leaving others intact
        ~Storage Encryption Key Deletion: Adversaries may delete or corrupt storage encryption keys to make data inaccessible
        ~System Partition Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt or delete critical system partitions to render the device inoperable
        ~External Storage Wiping: Adversaries may specifically target data stored on external storage like SD cards
        ~Cloud Backup Deletion: Adversaries may delete cloud backups in conjunction with local data deletion
        ~Application Data Clearing: Adversaries may clear application-specific data stores and caches
        ~Secure Data Wiping: Adversaries may use secure wiping techniques to prevent data recovery
      Input Injection: A malicious application can inject input to the user interface to mimic user interaction through the abuse of Android's accessibility APIs.  [Input Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516) can be achieved using any of the following methods:  * Mimicking user clicks on the screen, for example to steal money from a user's PayPal account.(Citation: android-trojan-steals-paypal-2fa) * Injecting global actions, such as `GLOBAL_ACTION_BACK` (programatically mimicking a physical back button press), to trigger actions on behalf of the user.(Citation: Talos Gustuff Apr 2019) * Inserting input into text fields on behalf of the user. This method is used legitimately to auto-fill text fields by applications such as password managers.(Citation: bitwarden autofill logins)
        ~Touch Event Injection: Simulates touch events and gestures on the screen to interact with UI elements
        ~System Button Simulation: Injects system-level button events like back, home, or recent apps
        ~Text Field Manipulation: Programmatically enters or modifies text in input fields
        ~Gesture Injection: Simulates complex touch gestures like swipe, pinch, or long press
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Exploits accessibility services to perform automated UI navigation
        ~Sensor Input Simulation: Injects fake sensor data (accelerometer, gyroscope, etc.) to trigger motion-based actions
        ~Voice Command Injection: Simulates voice input commands through accessibility services
        ~Cross-App Input Relay: Captures and replays input events across different applications
        ~UI Automation Framework Abuse: Exploits testing frameworks like UI Automator for malicious input injection
        ~Intent Injection: Simulates app-to-app communication to trigger unwanted actions
      Device Lockout: An adversary may seek to lock the legitimate user out of the device, for example to inhibit user interaction or to obtain a ransom payment.  On Android versions prior to 7, apps can abuse Device Administrator access to reset the device lock passcode to prevent the user from unlocking the device. After Android 7, only device or profile owners (e.g. MDMs) can reset the device’s passcode.(Citation: Android resetPassword)  On iOS devices, this technique does not work because mobile device management servers can only remove the screen lock passcode, they cannot set a new passcode. However, on jailbroken devices, malware has been discovered that can lock the user out of the device.(Citation: Xiao-KeyRaider)
        ~Device Admin Abuse: Adversaries may abuse Android Device Administrator privileges to forcibly change or reset device passcodes, preventing legitimate user access.
        ~MDM Profile Exploitation: Adversaries may compromise or abuse Mobile Device Management profiles to implement device lockout policies.
        ~Jailbreak Lock Mechanisms: On jailbroken iOS devices, adversaries may implement custom lock mechanisms that bypass normal OS security controls.
        ~Screen Overlay Lock: Adversaries may use screen overlay attacks to create a persistent full-screen overlay that prevents access to device controls.
        ~Bootloader Lock: Adversaries may modify bootloader settings to prevent device access even after restart.
        ~System UI Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate system UI elements to create a persistent state preventing normal device interaction.
        ~PIN Brute Force Counter: Adversaries may intentionally trigger security mechanisms that lock devices after failed PIN attempts.
        ~Settings Modification Lock: Adversaries may modify critical system settings to create a persistent locked state.
        ~Accessibility Service Abuse: Adversaries may abuse accessibility services to implement screen locks or prevent user interaction.
        ~Recovery Mode Prevention: Adversaries may disable or restrict access to recovery mode to maintain device lockout.
        ~Encryption-Based Lock: Adversaries may encrypt critical system files or partitions to create a persistent device lock state.
        ~Hardware Button Disable: Adversaries may disable or interfere with hardware buttons to prevent device recovery or restart.
        ~Account Linkage Lock: Adversaries may exploit device-account linkages to trigger remote lock mechanisms.
        ~Biometric System Tampering: Adversaries may corrupt or modify biometric authentication systems to prevent legitimate access.
        ~Background Service Lock: Adversaries may implement persistent background services that continuously enforce device locks.
        ~Secure Boot Manipulation: Adversaries may modify secure boot parameters to maintain device lockout across reboots.
        ~Time-Based Lock: Adversaries may implement time-based lock mechanisms that activate at specific intervals.
        ~Network-Dependent Lock: Adversaries may implement locks that require specific network conditions to be unlocked.
        ~System Process Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious code into system processes to maintain persistent locks.
        ~Authentication Cache Poisoning: Adversaries may corrupt authentication caches to prevent successful login attempts.
        ~Virtual Environment Lock: Adversaries may manipulate virtual work profiles or containers to implement cross-profile lockouts.
        ~Storage Quota Abuse: Adversaries may fill device storage to create conditions preventing normal device operation.
        ~Memory Exhaustion Lock: Adversaries may implement memory-intensive processes that maintain device in an unusable state.
        ~Permission Chain Lock: Adversaries may create complex permission dependencies that result in device lockout when broken.
        ~Sensor-Based Trigger: Adversaries may use device sensors (gyroscope, proximity, etc.) to trigger or maintain lock conditions.
        ~System Update Block: Adversaries may manipulate system update mechanisms to prevent security patches that could remove locks.
        ~Resource Depletion Lock: Adversaries may implement resource-intensive processes that drain battery or consume CPU to maintain lockout.
        ~App Component Lock: Adversaries may exploit app component interactions to create system-wide lock conditions.
        ~State Synchronization Lock: Adversaries may manipulate state synchronization between device components to maintain lockout.
        ~Kernel Module Lock: Adversaries may implement kernel-level modules that enforce device lockout conditions.
      Account Access Removal: Adversaries may interrupt availability of system and network resources by inhibiting access to accounts utilized by legitimate users. Accounts may be deleted, locked, or manipulated (ex: credentials changed) to remove access to accounts.
        ~Device Lock Manipulation: Adversaries may change device lock settings, PIN codes, or biometric configurations to prevent legitimate users from accessing their devices
        ~Mobile Account Deletion: Adversaries may delete user accounts directly from the mobile device or associated cloud services
        ~Cloud Service Account Lockout: Adversaries may deliberately trigger security mechanisms to lock cloud-based accounts (e.g., Apple ID, Google Account) connected to the mobile device
        ~Password/Credential Modification: Adversaries may change authentication credentials for mobile accounts without user authorization
        ~Mobile Device Management Override: Adversaries may abuse MDM capabilities to lock out legitimate users from their managed devices
        ~Authentication Factor Removal: Adversaries may remove or disable configured authentication methods like fingerprints, face recognition, or trusted devices
        ~Recovery Option Manipulation: Adversaries may modify account recovery options to prevent users from regaining access to their accounts
        ~App-Specific Account Blocking: Adversaries may manipulate individual app accounts to prevent access to specific services or data
      Data Encrypted for Impact: An adversary may encrypt files stored on a mobile device to prevent the user from accessing them. This may be done in order to extract monetary compensation from a victim in exchange for decryption or a decryption key (ransomware) or to render data permanently inaccessible in cases where the key is not saved or transmitted.
        ~System Storage Encryption: Encrypts system storage areas containing OS files and system configurations
        ~External Storage Encryption: Targets data stored on removable storage like SD cards
        ~App Data Encryption: Specifically encrypts application data directories and cached content
        ~Selective File Encryption: Targets specific high-value file types like photos, documents, and credentials
        ~Full Device Encryption: Encrypts all accessible storage on the device
        ~Background Encryption: Gradually encrypts files while device is in use to avoid detection
        ~Boot-time Encryption: Encrypts critical system files during device startup
        ~Cloud Storage Encryption: Encrypts synchronized cloud storage data and backup files
      Carrier Billing Fraud: A malicious app may trigger fraudulent charges on a victim’s carrier billing statement in several different ways, including SMS toll fraud and SMS shortcodes that make purchases.  Performing SMS fraud relies heavily upon the fact that, when making SMS purchases, the carriers perform device verification but not user verification. This allows adversaries to make purchases on behalf of the user, with little or no user interaction.(Citation: Google Bread)  Malicious applications may also perform toll billing, which occurs when carriers provide payment endpoints over a web page. The application connects to the web page over cellular data so the carrier can directly verify the number, or the application must retrieve a code sent via SMS and enter it into the web page.(Citation: Google Bread)  On iOS, apps cannot send SMS messages.  On Android, apps must hold the `SEND_SMS` permission to send SMS messages. Additionally, Android version 4.2 and above has mitigations against this threat by requiring user consent before allowing SMS messages to be sent to premium numbers (Citation: AndroidSecurity2014).
        ~SMS Premium Services: Adversary sends SMS messages to premium-rate numbers without user consent to generate charges
        ~SMS Shortcode Purchases: Adversary automates purchases through SMS shortcodes that bill directly to carrier
        ~Web-based Toll Billing: Adversary exploits carrier payment endpoints through cellular data connection
        ~SMS Verification Code Interception: Adversary intercepts and automatically enters verification codes for billing authorization
        ~Subscription Service Fraud: Adversary signs up victim for recurring premium services through carrier billing
        ~Direct Carrier Billing API Abuse: Adversary exploits carrier billing APIs to make unauthorized charges
        ~WAP Billing Fraud: Adversary uses WAP (Wireless Application Protocol) billing mechanisms to generate charges
      SMS Control: Adversaries may delete, alter, or send SMS messages without user authorization. This could be used to hide C2 SMS messages, spread malware, or various external effects.  This can be accomplished by requesting the `RECEIVE_SMS` or `SEND_SMS` permissions depending on what the malware is attempting to do. If the app is set as the default SMS handler on the device, the `SMS_DELIVER` broadcast intent can be registered, which allows the app to write to the SMS content provider. The content provider directly modifies the messaging database on the device, which could allow malicious applications with this ability to insert, modify, or delete arbitrary messages on the device.(Citation: SMS KitKat)(Citation: Android SmsProvider)
        ~Default SMS Handler Hijacking: Adversaries may set their malicious app as the default SMS handler to gain full control over SMS processing and storage
        ~SMS Permission Abuse: Adversaries may exploit SEND_SMS and RECEIVE_SMS permissions to perform unauthorized SMS operations
        ~SMS Database Manipulation: Adversaries may directly modify the SMS content provider database to alter message content or metadata
        ~SMS Broadcast Intent Interception: Adversaries may register for SMS_DELIVER broadcast intents to intercept incoming messages
        ~Premium SMS Fraud: Adversaries may send SMS messages to premium rate numbers without user consent to generate fraudulent charges
        ~SMS Command Filter: Adversaries may delete incoming SMS messages containing specific C2 commands to hide malicious activity
        ~Bulk SMS Distribution: Adversaries may use compromised devices to send mass SMS messages for malware propagation
        ~SMS Backup Tampering: Adversaries may modify SMS backup files to persist manipulated messages across device resets
      Generate Traffic from Victim: Adversaries may generate outbound traffic from devices. This is typically performed to manipulate external outcomes, such as to achieve carrier billing fraud or to manipulate app store rankings or ratings. Outbound traffic is typically generated as SMS messages or general web traffic, but may take other forms as well.  If done via SMS messages, Android apps must hold the `SEND_SMS` permission. Additionally, sending an SMS message requires user consent if the recipient is a premium number. Applications cannot send SMS messages on iOS
        ~SMS Fraud: Generate unauthorized SMS messages to premium numbers or services to conduct billing fraud
        ~App Store Rating Manipulation: Generate fake traffic to artificially inflate app rankings or ratings
        ~Click Fraud: Generate automated clicks on advertisements to fraudulently earn revenue
        ~Web Request Flooding: Generate excessive HTTP/HTTPS requests to specific websites or services
        ~Background Traffic Generation: Silently generate traffic in the background without user awareness
        ~API Abuse: Generate repeated calls to APIs to manipulate metrics or exhaust resources
        ~Social Media Engagement Fraud: Generate artificial traffic to social media platforms to boost engagement metrics
        ~In-App Purchase Fraud: Generate fraudulent in-app purchase requests
        ~Network Protocol Abuse: Generate traffic using various network protocols to evade detection
        ~Service Usage Manipulation: Generate traffic to manipulate usage statistics of cloud or mobile services
        ~Push Notification Abuse: Generate excessive push notification traffic to manipulate engagement metrics or exhaust resources
        ~Bluetooth Traffic Generation: Generate unauthorized Bluetooth communication traffic to nearby devices
        ~Location Service Abuse: Generate fake location-based traffic to manipulate location-dependent services
        ~Scheduled Traffic Generation: Generate traffic at specific times to mimic human behavior patterns
        ~Cross-App Traffic Generation: Generate traffic across multiple apps to avoid per-app restrictions
        ~Instant Messaging Abuse: Generate automated messages through mobile messaging platforms
        ~VoIP Traffic Generation: Generate Voice over IP traffic to conduct toll fraud or service abuse
        ~Mobile Carrier Signaling Abuse: Generate signaling traffic to exploit carrier network resources
        ~NFC Communication Abuse: Generate unauthorized NFC communication attempts
  MITRE-ICS
    initial-access: The adversary is trying to get into your ICS environment.  Initial Access consists of techniques that adversaries may use as entry vectors to gain an initial foothold within an ICS environment. These techniques include compromising operational technology assets, IT resources in the OT network, and external remote services and websites. They may also target third party entities and users with privileged access. In particular, these initial access footholds may include devices and communication mechanisms with access to and privileges in both the IT and OT environments. IT resources in the OT environment are also potentially vulnerable to the same attacks as enterprise IT systems. Trusted third parties of concern may include vendors, maintenance personnel, engineers, external integrators, and other outside entities involved in expected ICS operations. Vendor maintained assets may include physical devices, software, and operational equipment. Initial access techniques may also leverage outside devices, such as radios, controllers, or removable media, to remotely interfere with and possibly infect OT operations.
      Data Historian Compromise: Adversaries may compromise and gain control of a data historian to gain a foothold into the control system environment. Access to a data historian may be used to learn stored database archival and analysis information on the control system. A dual-homed data historian may provide adversaries an interface from the IT environment to the OT environment.   Dragos has released an updated analysis on CrashOverride that outlines the attack from the ICS network breach to payload delivery and execution.  (Citation: Industroyer - Dragos - 201810) The report summarized that CrashOverride represents a new application of malware, but relied on standard intrusion techniques. In particular, new artifacts include references to a Microsoft Windows Server 2003 host, with a SQL Server. Within the ICS environment, such a database server can act as a data historian. Dragos noted a device with this role should be "expected to have extensive connections" within the ICS environment. Adversary activity leveraged database capabilities to perform reconnaissance, including directory queries and network connectivity checks.  Permissions Required: Administrator  Contributors: Joe Slowik - Dragos
        ~SQL Injection Attacks: Exploiting vulnerable SQL queries in historian databases to gain unauthorized access or execute malicious commands
        ~Credential Theft: Targeting default or weak credentials commonly used in historian deployments
        ~Network Protocol Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in protocols used for historian communication (e.g., OPC, ODBC)
        ~Dual-Network Position Abuse: Leveraging the historian's position between IT and OT networks to bypass security controls
        ~Configuration File Tampering: Modifying historian configuration files to gain persistent access or elevate privileges
        ~Historical Data Manipulation: Corrupting or modifying stored process data to affect operational decisions
        ~API Interface Exploitation: Attacking exposed APIs used for data collection and retrieval
        ~Memory Corruption: Exploiting buffer overflows or other memory vulnerabilities in historian software
        ~Service Account Compromise: Targeting service accounts used by historian applications
        ~Backup System Exploitation: Compromising historian backup systems to gain access to historical data and configurations
        ~Third-Party Integration Exploitation: Attacking interfaces between historians and third-party analytics or reporting tools
        ~Update Channel Compromise: Exploiting software update mechanisms or patches specific to historian systems
        ~Web Interface Attacks: Targeting historian web consoles and dashboards
        ~Real-time Data Interface Exploitation: Attacking interfaces used for real-time data collection from PLCs and RTUs
        ~Remote Access Feature Abuse: Exploiting remote access capabilities built into historian software
        ~Client Application Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in historian client applications used for data visualization
        ~Custom Script Injection: Injecting malicious scripts into historian's custom calculation or aggregation functions
        ~Replication Channel Attacks: Exploiting data replication mechanisms between redundant historian servers
      Exploitation of Remote Services: Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to enable remote service abuse. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for initial access into and lateral movement throughout the ICS environment to enable access to targeted systems. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  ICS asset owners and operators have been affected by ransomware (or disruptive malware masquerading as ransomware) migrating from enterprise IT to ICS environments: WannaCry, NotPetya, and BadRabbit. In each of these cases, self-propagating (wormable) malware initially infected IT networks, but through exploit (particularly the SMBv1-targeting MS17-010 vulnerability) spread to industrial networks, producing significant impacts. (Citation: Joe Slowik April 2019)
        ~ICS Protocol Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in ICS-specific protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or EtherNet/IP
        ~Remote Access Service Exploitation: Exploitation of VPN, remote desktop, or other remote access services used for ICS maintenance
        ~Web Interface Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in web-based HMI or engineering workstation interfaces
        ~OPC Server Exploitation: Exploitation of Object Linking and Embedding for Process Control (OPC) server vulnerabilities
        ~Database Service Exploitation: Exploitation of historian databases or other ICS-related database services
        ~Network Service Exploitation: Exploitation of common network services (SMB, RDP, etc.) present in ICS environments
        ~Engineering Software Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in vendor-specific engineering software services
        ~SCADA Protocol Exploitation: Exploitation of supervisory control protocols and services
        ~Authentication Bypass: Exploitation of authentication mechanisms in remote services
        ~Configuration Management Exploitation: Exploitation of remote configuration management services
        ~Firmware Update Service Exploitation: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in firmware update mechanisms or services
        ~Third-party Integration Service Exploitation: Exploitation of services that enable integration with external systems or vendors
        ~Device Discovery Service Exploitation: Exploitation of service discovery protocols and mechanisms
        ~Diagnostic Service Exploitation: Exploitation of remote diagnostic and monitoring services
        ~Gateway Service Exploitation: Exploitation of protocol gateway services that bridge different networks or protocols
        ~Time Synchronization Service Exploitation: Exploitation of time synchronization services (NTP, PTP) critical for ICS operations
        ~Backup Service Exploitation: Exploitation of automated backup services and mechanisms
        ~Asset Management Service Exploitation: Exploitation of services used for asset tracking and management
        ~Safety System Service Exploitation: Exploitation of safety instrumented system (SIS) remote services
        ~Alarm Management Service Exploitation: Exploitation of centralized alarm management system services
        ~Industrial IoT Service Exploitation: Exploitation of IIoT gateway services and cloud connectors
        ~Reporting Service Exploitation: Exploitation of automated report generation and distribution services
        ~Remote Sensor Service Exploitation: Exploitation of remote sensor monitoring and configuration services
        ~Energy Management Service Exploitation: Exploitation of energy management system services
        ~Building Automation Service Exploitation: Exploitation of building management system (BMS) services
      Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage remote services to move between assets and network segments. These services are often used to allow operators to interact with systems remotely within the network, some examples are RDP, SMB, SSH, and other similar mechanisms. (Citation: Blake Johnson, Dan Caban, Marina Krotofil, Dan Scali, Nathan Brubaker, Christopher Glyer December 2017) (Citation: Dragos December 2017) (Citation: Joe Slowik April 2019)   Remote services could be used to support remote access, data transmission, authentication, name resolution, and other remote functions. Further, remote services may be necessary to allow operators and administrators to configure systems within the network from their engineering or management workstations. An adversary may use this technique to access devices which may be dual-homed (Citation: Blake Johnson, Dan Caban, Marina Krotofil, Dan Scali, Nathan Brubaker, Christopher Glyer December 2017) to multiple network segments, and can be used for [Program Download](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843) or to execute attacks on control devices directly through [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0859).  Specific remote services (RDP & VNC) may be a precursor to enable [Graphical User Interface](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0823) execution on devices such as HMIs or engineering workstation software.  Based on incident data, CISA and FBI assessed that Chinese state-sponsored actors also compromised various authorized remote access channels, including systems designed to transfer data and/or allow access between corporate and ICS networks.  (Citation: CISA AA21-201A Pipeline Intrusion July 2021)
        ~VPN Access: Exploitation of virtual private network services to gain unauthorized access to ICS networks
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP): Targeting RDP services for unauthorized remote access to HMIs and engineering workstations
        ~Vendor Remote Access: Exploitation of vendor-specific remote access solutions used for maintenance and support
        ~SSH/Telnet Services: Targeting command-line remote access protocols commonly used in ICS environments
        ~OPC/DCOM Services: Exploitation of OPC servers and DCOM services used for remote data exchange
        ~Web-Based Remote Access: Targeting web-based remote access portals and interfaces for ICS components
        ~Remote Monitoring Services: Exploitation of remote monitoring and diagnostic services used in ICS
        ~Remote File Services: Targeting file transfer and sharing services between IT and OT networks
        ~Remote Authentication Services: Exploitation of authentication mechanisms used for remote access
        ~Industrial Protocol Remote Access: Targeting remote access capabilities built into industrial protocols
        ~Remote Engineering Workstation Access: Exploitation of remote engineering software interfaces used for PLC programming
        ~Cloud-Based Remote Services: Targeting cloud-based remote access solutions integrated with ICS environments
        ~Mobile Remote Access: Exploitation of mobile applications and interfaces used for remote ICS monitoring
        ~Remote Historian Access: Targeting historian servers' remote access capabilities
        ~Remote Asset Management Services: Exploitation of asset management and configuration tools' remote interfaces
        ~Remote Backup Services: Targeting backup system interfaces that connect to ICS components
        ~Remote Diagnostic Services: Exploitation of vendor-provided diagnostic and troubleshooting interfaces
        ~Third-Party Integration Services: Targeting remote access mechanisms used for third-party system integration
      Wireless Compromise: Adversaries may perform wireless compromise as a method of gaining communications and unauthorized access to a wireless network. Access to a wireless network may be gained through the compromise of a wireless device. (Citation: Alexander Bolshev, Gleb Cherbov July 2014) (Citation: Alexander Bolshev March 2014) Adversaries may also utilize radios and other wireless communication devices on the same frequency as the wireless network. Wireless compromise can be done as an initial access vector from a remote distance.   A Polish student used a modified TV remote controller to gain access to and control over the Lodz city tram system in Poland. (Citation: John Bill May 2017) (Citation: Shelley Smith February 2008) The remote controller device allowed the student to interface with the trams network to modify track settings and override operator control. The adversary may have accomplished this by aligning the controller to the frequency and amplitude of IR control protocol signals. (Citation: Bruce Schneier January 2008) The controller then enabled initial access to the network, allowing the capture and replay of tram signals. (Citation: John Bill May 2017)
        ~Radio Frequency Jamming: Disruption of wireless communications through RF interference to force systems into failsafe modes or alternate configurations
        ~Signal Replay Attacks: Capturing and replaying legitimate wireless control signals to manipulate ICS devices
        ~Protocol Spoofing: Impersonating legitimate wireless devices by exploiting weaknesses in wireless protocols
        ~Rogue Access Point Deployment: Installing unauthorized wireless access points to intercept or manipulate ICS communications
        ~Wireless Device Impersonation: Cloning or spoofing legitimate wireless devices' identifiers to gain network access
        ~Remote Controller Modification: Altering consumer electronics to interface with industrial wireless systems
        ~Frequency Hopping Exploitation: Taking advantage of predictable frequency hopping patterns in wireless systems
        ~Wireless Sensor Network Compromise: Targeting wireless sensors and their mesh networks to gain access
        ~Bluetooth Protocol Attacks: Exploiting Bluetooth-enabled ICS devices and interfaces
        ~Near-Field Communication (NFC) Exploitation: Compromising NFC-enabled industrial devices and access controls
        ~ZigBee Protocol Exploitation: Targeting ZigBee-based industrial wireless networks and devices
        ~WirelessHART Attacks: Compromising industrial wireless sensor networks using WirelessHART protocol vulnerabilities
        ~ISA100 Protocol Manipulation: Exploiting wireless automation standards in industrial settings
        ~SDR-Based Signal Manipulation: Using software-defined radio to analyze and manipulate industrial wireless protocols
        ~Cellular Network Exploitation: Targeting cellular-based ICS communications and remote access systems
        ~RFID System Compromise: Attacking RFID-based industrial identification and access control systems
        ~Wireless Configuration Tampering: Exploiting insecure wireless device configuration interfaces
        ~Long-Range Wide Area Network (LoRaWAN) Attacks: Compromising industrial IoT networks using LoRaWAN vulnerabilities
        ~SCADA Radio Terminal Unit (RTU) Attacks: Exploiting wireless SCADA RTU communications
        ~Emergency Band Interference: Compromising emergency and backup wireless communications systems
        ~Wi-Fi Direct Exploitation: Targeting peer-to-peer wireless connections in industrial devices
        ~Wireless Bridge Compromise: Attacking wireless bridges used to connect separated industrial networks
        ~Proprietary RF Protocol Attacks: Exploiting vendor-specific wireless protocols in industrial equipment
        ~Cross-Protocol Wireless Attacks: Leveraging interactions between different wireless protocols to gain access
      Transient Cyber Asset: Adversaries may target devices that are transient across ICS networks and external networks. Normally, transient assets are brought into an environment by authorized personnel and do not remain in that environment on a permanent basis. (Citation: North American Electric Reliability Corporation June 2021) Transient assets are commonly needed to support management functions and may be more common in systems where a remotely managed asset is not feasible, external connections for remote access do not exist, or 3rd party contractor/vendor access is required.   Adversaries may take advantage of transient assets in different ways. For instance, adversaries may target a transient asset when it is connected to an external network and then leverage its trusted access in another environment to launch an attack. They may also take advantage of installed applications and libraries that are used by legitimate end-users to interact with control system devices.   Transient assets, in some cases, may not be deployed with a secure configuration leading to weaknesses that could allow an adversary to propagate malicious executable code, e.g., the transient asset may be infected by malware and when connected to an ICS environment the malware propagates onto other systems.
        ~Engineering Workstation Compromise: Targeting laptops and devices used by engineers for system configuration and maintenance that connect to both corporate and ICS networks
        ~Vendor Tool Exploitation: Exploiting vendor-specific software tools and applications installed on transient devices used for diagnostics and maintenance
        ~USB Device Infection: Compromising USB drives and removable media used to transfer data between isolated networks
        ~Misconfigured Security Controls: Exploiting weak or misconfigured security settings on transient devices that don't meet security baselines
        ~Malicious Software Update: Compromising legitimate software updates or patches stored on transient devices
        ~Remote Access Tool Abuse: Exploiting remote access tools and applications installed on transient devices
        ~Maintenance Device Hijacking: Targeting specialized maintenance equipment and diagnostic tools that connect temporarily to ICS systems
        ~Firmware Manipulation: Modifying or corrupting device firmware during external network connections
        ~Credential Harvesting: Collecting authentication credentials stored on transient devices
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Compromising transient devices before they enter the ICS environment through supply chain attacks
        ~Network Interface Manipulation: Exploiting multiple network interfaces on transient devices to bridge isolated networks
        ~Temporary Connection Exploitation: Taking advantage of brief connection windows when transient devices are connected to ICS networks
        ~Protocol State Manipulation: Exploiting protocol states and cached configurations from previous connections
        ~Shadow Copy Abuse: Leveraging system restore points and backup copies stored on transient devices
        ~Configuration Template Poisoning: Modifying device configuration templates stored on transient devices
        ~Asset Management Bypass: Circumventing asset management and tracking systems for transient devices
        ~Offline Cache Exploitation: Accessing and manipulating cached credentials and configurations stored for offline use
        ~Multi-Site Propagation: Using transient devices to spread malware across multiple industrial sites
        ~Contractor Access Abuse: Exploiting contractor-specific access patterns and procedures for transient devices
        ~Emergency Access Exploitation: Taking advantage of emergency procedures that bypass normal security controls for transient devices
        ~Training Environment Compromise: Compromising training and simulation environments used to test transient devices
        ~Device Retirement Exploitation: Targeting transient devices during decommissioning or replacement phases
        ~Documentation Poisoning: Modifying device documentation and procedures to introduce vulnerable configurations
        ~Shared Resource Abuse: Exploiting shared resources and common facilities used by multiple transient devices
        ~Change Window Exploitation: Taking advantage of maintenance windows when security controls might be relaxed
      Rogue Master: Adversaries may setup a rogue master to leverage control server functions to communicate with outstations. A rogue master can be used to send legitimate control messages to other control system devices, affecting processes in unintended ways. It may also be used to disrupt network communications by capturing and receiving the network traffic meant for the actual master. Impersonating a master may also allow an adversary to avoid detection.   In the case of the 2017 Dallas Siren incident, adversaries used a rogue master to send command messages to the 156 distributed sirens across the city, either through a single rogue transmitter with a strong signal, or using many distributed repeaters. (Citation: Bastille April 2017) (Citation: Zack Whittaker April 2017)
        ~Protocol-Specific Master Spoofing: Adversaries may implement protocol-specific master capabilities (e.g., Modbus TCP master, DNP3 master) to communicate with field devices
        ~Radio Frequency Master Emulation: Adversaries may use radio transmitters to emulate wireless master stations and send unauthorized commands
        ~Network Traffic Interception: Adversaries may position rogue masters to intercept and manipulate legitimate traffic between real masters and slaves
        ~Master Configuration Cloning: Adversaries may clone configurations from legitimate master devices to create convincing rogue masters
        ~Multi-Protocol Master Implementation: Adversaries may implement multiple industrial protocols in a single rogue master to target diverse systems
        ~Serial Communication Hijacking: Adversaries may tap into serial communication lines to insert rogue master commands
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Master Spoofing: Adversaries may specifically target and impersonate RTU master stations
        ~Distributed Rogue Master Network: Adversaries may deploy multiple coordinated rogue masters across a facility
      Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries may perform supply chain compromise to gain control systems environment access by means of infected products, software, and workflows. Supply chain compromise is the manipulation of products, such as devices or software, or their delivery mechanisms before receipt by the end consumer. Adversary compromise of these products and mechanisms is done for the goal of data or system compromise, once infected products are introduced to the target environment.   Supply chain compromise can occur at all stages of the supply chain, from manipulation of development tools and environments to manipulation of developed products and tools distribution mechanisms. This may involve the compromise and replacement of legitimate software and patches, such as on third party or vendor websites. Targeting of supply chain compromise can be done in attempts to infiltrate the environments of a specific audience. In control systems environments with assets in both the IT and OT networks, it is possible a supply chain compromise affecting the IT environment could enable further access to the OT environment.     Counterfeit devices may be introduced to the global supply chain posing safety and cyber risks to asset owners and operators. These devices may not meet the safety, engineering and manufacturing requirements of regulatory bodies but may feature tagging indicating conformance with industry standards. Due to the lack of adherence to standards and overall lesser quality, the counterfeit products may pose a serious safety and operational risk. (Citation: Control Global May 2019)   Yokogawa identified instances in which their customers received counterfeit differential pressure transmitters using the Yokogawa logo. The counterfeit transmitters were nearly indistinguishable with a semblance of functionality and interface that mimics the genuine product. (Citation: Control Global May 2019)   F-Secure Labs analyzed the approach the adversary used to compromise victim systems with Havex. (Citation: Daavid Hentunen, Antti Tikkanen June 2014) The adversary planted trojanized software installers available on legitimate ICS/SCADA vendor websites. After being downloaded, this software infected the host computer with a Remote Access Trojan (RAT).
        ~Software Development Environment Compromise: Adversaries compromise development environments to inject malicious code during software building process
        ~Counterfeit Hardware Distribution: Adversaries introduce counterfeit ICS components that mimic legitimate vendor products
        ~Compromised Software Dependencies: Adversaries inject malicious code into third-party libraries or components used in ICS software
        ~Firmware Manipulation: Adversaries modify device firmware before distribution to include backdoors or malicious functionality
        ~Update Infrastructure Compromise: Adversaries compromise software update servers or mechanisms to distribute malicious updates
        ~Vendor Website Compromise: Adversaries compromise vendor websites to host modified versions of legitimate software
        ~Hardware Component Tampering: Adversaries modify legitimate hardware components during manufacturing or distribution
        ~Third-Party Service Provider Compromise: Adversaries compromise service providers who have privileged access to ICS environments
        ~Development Tool Compromise: Adversaries compromise tools used in ICS software development to inject malicious code
        ~Certificate Authority Compromise: Adversaries compromise certificate authorities to sign malicious software as legitimate
      Spearphishing Attachment: Adversaries may use a spearphishing attachment, a variant of spearphishing, as a form of a social engineering attack against specific targets. Spearphishing attachments are different from other forms of spearphishing in that they employ malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered and target a specific individual, company, or industry. In this scenario, adversaries attach a file to the spearphishing email and usually rely upon [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0863) to gain execution and access. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK October 2019)   A Chinese spearphishing campaign running from December 9, 2011 through February 29, 2012, targeted ONG organizations and their employees. The emails were constructed with a high level of sophistication to convince employees to open the malicious file attachments. (Citation: CISA AA21-201A Pipeline Intrusion July 2021)
        ~Engineering Documentation Lure: Malicious files disguised as industrial system specifications, PLC programming files, or equipment manuals
        ~Firmware Update Attachment: Malicious files masquerading as legitimate firmware updates for ICS equipment
        ~Safety Report Lure: Malicious files disguised as safety incident reports or compliance documentation
        ~Vendor Communication Lure: Malicious attachments appearing to be from legitimate ICS vendors or suppliers
        ~Maintenance Schedule Lure: Malicious files masquerading as equipment maintenance schedules or procedures
        ~Certificate Attachment: Malicious files disguised as industrial certification documents or compliance certificates
        ~Control System Configuration Lure: Malicious attachments appearing to be PLC or SCADA system configuration files
        ~Work Order Lure: Malicious files disguised as work orders or service requests for ICS equipment
        ~Training Material Lure: Malicious attachments appearing as ICS training documents or operational procedures
        ~Inspection Report Lure: Malicious files masquerading as regulatory inspection reports or audit findings
        ~Equipment Quotation Lure: Malicious files disguised as price quotes or procurement documents for ICS equipment
        ~Network Diagram Lure: Malicious attachments appearing to be industrial network architecture documents
        ~Production Schedule Lure: Malicious files masquerading as manufacturing or production scheduling documents
        ~Calibration Report Lure: Malicious attachments disguised as equipment calibration or testing reports
        ~Emergency Response Lure: Malicious files appearing as emergency procedures or incident response protocols
        ~Quality Control Lure: Malicious attachments masquerading as quality assurance reports or test results
        ~Environmental Compliance Lure: Malicious files disguised as environmental monitoring or compliance reports
        ~Project Proposal Lure: Malicious attachments appearing as industrial project documentation or proposals
        ~Asset Inventory Lure: Malicious files masquerading as ICS asset management or inventory documents
        ~Standard Operating Procedure Lure: Malicious attachments disguised as updated SOPs or operational guidelines
        ~Third-Party Audit Lure: Malicious files disguised as external audit reports or findings
        ~Insurance Documentation Lure: Malicious attachments appearing as industrial insurance or risk assessment documents
        ~Component Specification Lure: Malicious files masquerading as detailed technical specifications for ICS components
        ~Contractor Agreement Lure: Malicious attachments disguised as contractor or service provider documentation
        ~Regulatory Update Lure: Malicious files appearing as updates to industry regulations or compliance requirements
        ~Remote Access Request Lure: Malicious attachments masquerading as remote access or support documentation
        ~Security Assessment Lure: Malicious files disguised as security audit reports or vulnerability assessments
        ~Industry Alert Lure: Malicious attachments appearing as urgent industry security bulletins or alerts
        ~Change Management Lure: Malicious files masquerading as change request forms or approval documents
        ~Commissioning Document Lure: Malicious attachments disguised as system commissioning or validation reports
      Drive-by Compromise: Adversaries may gain access to a system during a drive-by compromise, when a user visits a website as part of a regular browsing session. With this technique, the user's web browser is targeted and exploited simply by visiting the compromised website.   The adversary may target a specific community, such as trusted third party suppliers or other industry specific groups, which often visit the target website. This kind of targeted attack relies on a common interest, and is known as a strategic web compromise or watering hole attack.   The National Cyber Awareness System (NCAS) has issued a Technical Alert (TA) regarding Russian government cyber activity targeting critical infrastructure sectors. (Citation: Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency March 2018) Analysis by DHS and FBI has noted two distinct categories of victims in the Dragonfly campaign on the Western energy sector: staging and intended targets. The adversary targeted the less secure networks of staging targets, including trusted third-party suppliers and related peripheral organizations. Initial access to the intended targets used watering hole attacks to target process control, ICS, and critical infrastructure related trade publications and informational websites.
        ~Watering Hole Attack: Compromise of ICS vendor websites, industry forums, or trade publications specifically targeted at ICS professionals
        ~JavaScript Injection: Injection of malicious JavaScript code into compromised ICS-related websites to exploit visitor browsers
        ~Browser Exploit Pack: Use of exploit kits specifically designed to target vulnerabilities in browsers commonly used in ICS environments
        ~Supply Chain Portal Compromise: Targeting of vendor portals and software download sites used for ICS components
        ~Man-in-the-Browser Attack: Browser-based attacks that intercept and modify ICS web applications traffic
        ~Browser Extension Exploitation: Compromise through malicious or compromised browser extensions used in ICS environments
        ~HTML5 API Exploitation: Abuse of HTML5 features to compromise browsers in ICS networks
        ~Certificate Spoofing: Use of fake or compromised certificates to make malicious sites appear legitimate to ICS users
        ~Cross-Origin Resource Sharing Abuse: Exploitation of CORS misconfigurations in ICS web applications
        ~Web Protocol Handler Exploitation: Abuse of custom protocol handlers in browsers used in ICS environments
        ~PDF Document Exploitation: Compromise through malicious PDF documentation commonly used in ICS environments
        ~CAD File Attack Vector: Exploitation through compromised CAD files or viewers accessed via web browsers
        ~Industrial Protocol Gateway Attack: Compromise through web-based HMI or SCADA protocol gateways
        ~Trusted Partner Portal Hijacking: Compromise of legitimate vendor/contractor portals used for ICS maintenance
        ~Web-based Engineering Workstation Attack: Targeting browser-based engineering workstation interfaces
        ~Remote Access Portal Exploitation: Attacks through compromised remote access or VPN portals
        ~Software Update Site Compromise: Targeting of ICS software update sites and package repositories
        ~Web-based Configuration Tool Attack: Exploitation through browser-based configuration tools for ICS devices
      Internet Accessible Device: Adversaries may gain access into industrial environments through systems exposed directly to the internet for remote access rather than through [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0822). Internet Accessible Devices are exposed to the internet unintentionally or intentionally without adequate protections. This may allow for adversaries to move directly into the control system network. Access onto these devices is accomplished without the use of exploits, these would be represented within the [Exploit Public-Facing Application](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0819) technique.  Adversaries may leverage built in functions for remote access which may not be protected or utilize minimal legacy protections that may be targeted. (Citation: NCCIC January 2014) These services may be discoverable through the use of online scanning tools.   In the case of the Bowman dam incident, adversaries leveraged access to the dam control network through a cellular modem. Access to the device was protected by password authentication, although the application was vulnerable to brute forcing. (Citation: NCCIC January 2014) (Citation: Danny Yadron December 2015) (Citation: Mark Thompson March 2016)  In Trend Micros manufacturing deception operations adversaries were detected leveraging direct internet access to an ICS environment through the exposure of operational protocols such as Siemens S7, Omron FINS, and EtherNet/IP, in addition to misconfigured VNC access. (Citation: Stephen Hilt, Federico Maggi, Charles Perine, Lord Remorin, Martin Rsler, and Rainer Vosseler)
        ~Industrial Protocol Exposure: Direct access through exposed industrial protocols like Siemens S7, Modbus TCP, or EtherNet/IP without proper security controls
        ~Remote HMI Access: Unauthorized access through exposed Human Machine Interface (HMI) systems or SCADA interfaces
        ~Cellular Modem Access: Access through poorly secured cellular modems and gateways used for remote monitoring
        ~VNC/RDP Exposure: Access through misconfigured remote desktop or VNC services exposed to the internet
        ~Web Interface Access: Access through exposed web-based management interfaces of industrial devices
        ~Default Credential Usage: Access using default or commonly used credentials on internet-exposed industrial devices
        ~OPC Server Access: Direct access through exposed OPC servers and interfaces
        ~Engineering Workstation Access: Access through exposed engineering workstations used for system configuration
        ~IoT Gateway Access: Access through exposed IoT gateways and data collectors
        ~Historian Server Access: Access through exposed historian servers or data logging systems
        ~PLC Programming Port Access: Access through exposed programming ports used for PLC configuration
        ~Industrial Router Access: Access through poorly configured industrial routers and switches with management interfaces exposed
        ~Remote Diagnostic Interface: Access through vendor-supplied remote diagnostic and maintenance interfaces
        ~Building Management System Access: Access through exposed building automation and management systems
        ~Vendor Portal Access: Access through vendor-maintained remote access portals for support
        ~Time Synchronization Service: Access through exposed NTP or PTP services used for industrial time synchronization
        ~Remote Telemetry Unit Access: Access through exposed RTUs used for remote monitoring and control
        ~Protocol Gateway Access: Access through protocol conversion gateways between different industrial networks
        ~Safety System Interface Access: Access through exposed safety system monitoring and configuration interfaces
        ~Environmental Monitoring System: Access through exposed environmental monitoring and control systems
        ~Asset Management System Access: Access through exposed asset management and condition monitoring platforms
        ~Backup System Interface: Access through exposed backup and recovery system interfaces
        ~Power Management Interface: Access through exposed power management and monitoring systems
        ~Industrial Firewall Management: Access through exposed industrial firewall configuration interfaces
      Replication Through Removable Media: Adversaries may move onto systems, such as those separated from the enterprise network, by copying malware to removable media which is inserted into the control systems environment. The adversary may rely on unknowing trusted third parties, such as suppliers or contractors with access privileges, to introduce the removable media. This technique enables initial access to target devices that never connect to untrusted networks, but are physically accessible.       Operators of the German nuclear power plant, Gundremmingen, discovered malware on a facility computer not connected to the internet. (Citation: Kernkraftwerk Gundremmingen April 2016) (Citation: Trend Micro April 2016) The malware included Conficker and W32.Ramnit, which were also found on eighteen removable disk drives in the facility. (Citation: Christoph Steitz, Eric Auchard April 2016) (Citation: Catalin Cimpanu April 2016) (Citation: Peter Dockrill April 2016) (Citation: Lee Mathews April 2016) (Citation: Sean Gallagher April 2016) (Citation: Dark Reading Staff April 2016) The plant has since checked for infection and cleaned up more than 1,000 computers. (Citation: BBC April 2016) An ESET researcher commented that internet disconnection does not guarantee system safety from infection or payload execution. (Citation: ESET April 2016)
        ~USB Drive Infection: Use of infected USB drives to spread malware, particularly targeting air-gapped systems through deliberate or accidental insertion
        ~Maintenance Device Compromise: Exploitation of legitimate maintenance devices, diagnostic tools, and programming terminals that connect to ICS systems
        ~Supply Chain Media Manipulation: Compromising removable media during manufacturing or distribution before it reaches the target facility
        ~Engineering Workstation Transfer: Using infected removable media to bridge between engineering workstations and control system components
        ~Firmware Update Media: Compromising legitimate firmware update media or creating malicious firmware update packages
        ~Automated Run Commands: Leveraging autorun features and Windows shortcuts to execute malware when media is inserted
        ~Hidden Partition Exploitation: Using hidden partitions on removable media to conceal malicious code
        ~Multi-Stage Payload Delivery: Using removable media as the first stage in a multi-stage attack chain
        ~External Hard Drive Compromise: Using larger capacity external drives to deploy more sophisticated malware or to exfiltrate data while introducing malicious code
        ~Memory Card Manipulation: Exploiting industrial cameras, PLCs, and other devices that use SD/CF cards for configuration and updates
        ~Social Engineering Drop: Strategically placing infected media in locations where employees are likely to find and use them
        ~Vendor Package Masquerading: Disguising malicious media as legitimate vendor-supplied updates or documentation
        ~Multi-Protocol Device Abuse: Using devices that support multiple protocols (e.g., USB/Ethernet converters) to bypass security controls
      External Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage external remote services as a point of initial access into your network. These services allow users to connect to internal network resources from external locations. Examples are VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms. Remote service gateways often manage connections and credential authentication for these services. (Citation: Daniel Oakley, Travis Smith, Tripwire)  External remote services allow administration of a control system from outside the system. Often, vendors and internal engineering groups have access to external remote services to control system networks via the corporate network. In some cases, this access is enabled directly from the internet. While remote access enables ease of maintenance when a control system is in a remote area, compromise of remote access solutions is a liability. The adversary may use these services to gain access to and execute attacks against a control system network. Access to valid accounts is often a requirement.   As they look for an entry point into the control system network, adversaries may begin searching for existing point-to-point VPN implementations at trusted third party networks or through remote support employee connections where split tunneling is enabled. (Citation: Electricity Information Sharing and Analysis Center; SANS Industrial Control Systems March 2016)
        ~VPN Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in VPN appliances and software used for remote access to ICS networks, including exploitation of known CVEs in popular VPN solutions
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) Abuse: Exploiting exposed RDP services, often used by vendors and maintenance personnel for remote system access
        ~Vendor Remote Access Systems: Compromising dedicated vendor remote access solutions and portals specifically designed for ICS maintenance
        ~Split Tunneling Abuse: Exploiting split tunnel VPN configurations that allow simultaneous access to both Internet and ICS networks
        ~Remote Access Gateway Compromise: Targeting centralized remote access management systems and authentication gateways
        ~Third-Party Remote Support Tools: Exploiting remote support and troubleshooting tools used by vendors and contractors
        ~Web-Based Management Interfaces: Targeting web-based remote access interfaces for ICS components and SCADA systems
        ~Remote Engineering Workstation Access: Compromising remote access to engineering workstations used for ICS configuration and maintenance
        ~Cloud-Based Remote Access: Exploiting cloud-based remote access solutions increasingly used in modern ICS environments
        ~Mobile Remote Access: Targeting mobile applications and interfaces designed for remote ICS monitoring and control
        ~Remote Diagnostic Services: Exploiting remote diagnostic and troubleshooting services that provide direct access to ICS equipment
        ~Legacy Serial Remote Access: Targeting dial-up modems and serial-based remote access systems still common in older ICS installations
        ~Emergency Remote Access: Exploiting emergency remote access systems designed for critical situations but often less secured
        ~Wireless Remote Terminal Units (RTUs): Compromising wireless RTUs and their remote access capabilities
        ~OEM Remote Support Infrastructure: Targeting Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) specific remote support infrastructure and backdoors
        ~Remote Historian Access: Exploiting remote access to historian servers and data collection systems
        ~Remote Asset Management Systems: Compromising centralized asset management systems used for remote equipment monitoring
        ~Remote Backup Systems: Targeting backup system interfaces that provide remote access for data backup and recovery
        ~Industrial Protocol Gateways: Exploiting remote access capabilities in industrial protocol gateways (e.g., Modbus, DNP3, OPC UA)
        ~Temporary Contractor Access: Targeting temporary remote access solutions set up for contractors and maintenance work
        ~Remote Firmware Update Channels: Exploiting remote firmware update mechanisms and their associated access paths
        ~Remote Safety Systems Access: Compromising remote access to safety instrumented systems (SIS) and emergency shutdown systems
        ~Remote Calibration Systems: Targeting remote calibration and configuration interfaces for industrial instruments
        ~Remote Time Synchronization Services: Exploiting remote access for time synchronization services critical to ICS operations
        ~Remote License Management Systems: Compromising remote license verification and management systems
      Exploit Public-Facing Application: Adversaries may leverage weaknesses to exploit internet-facing software for initial access into an industrial network. Internet-facing software may be user applications, underlying networking implementations, an assets operating system, weak defenses, etc. Targets of this technique may be intentionally exposed for the purpose of remote management and visibility.  An adversary may seek to target public-facing applications as they may provide direct access into an ICS environment or the ability to move into the ICS network. Publicly exposed applications may be found through online tools that scan the internet for open ports and services. Version numbers for the exposed application may provide adversaries an ability to target specific known vulnerabilities. Exposed control protocol or remote access ports found in Commonly Used Port may be of interest by adversaries.
        ~HMI Interface Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in web-based Human Machine Interface (HMI) systems that provide control and monitoring capabilities
        ~Protocol Gateway Attacks: Exploiting vulnerabilities in public-facing protocol conversion gateways that bridge IT and OT networks
        ~Remote Access Portal Compromise: Attacking vulnerabilities in remote access solutions specifically designed for ICS maintenance and operations
        ~Vendor Management Interface Exploitation: Targeting vendor-specific web interfaces used for equipment configuration and management
        ~Engineering Workstation Interface Attacks: Exploiting web-based interfaces of engineering workstations used for system configuration and programming
        ~Historian Server Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in public-facing historian servers that collect and store operational data
        ~Building Management System Attacks: Exploiting web interfaces of building automation and control systems
        ~IIoT Gateway Compromise: Attacking vulnerabilities in Industrial Internet of Things gateways and cloud connectors
        ~Field Device Web Interface Exploitation: Targeting embedded web servers in PLCs, RTUs, and other field devices
        ~Security Appliance Management Interface Attacks: Exploiting vulnerabilities in industrial firewall or security device administration interfaces
      Engineering Workstation Compromise: Adversaries will compromise and gain control of an engineering workstation for Initial Access into the control system environment. Access to an engineering workstation may occur through or physical means, such as a Valid Accounts with privileged access or infection by removable media. A dual-homed engineering workstation may allow the adversary access into multiple networks. For example, unsegregated process control, safety system, or information system networks. An Engineering Workstation is designed as a reliable computing platform that configures, maintains, and diagnoses control system equipment and applications. Compromise of an engineering workstation may provide access to, and control of, other control system applications and equipment. In the Maroochy attack, the adversary utilized a computer, possibly stolen, with proprietary engineering software to communicate with a wastewater system.
        ~Remote Access Software Abuse: Exploitation of remote access tools and software commonly installed on engineering workstations for maintenance and support
        ~Engineering Software Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in proprietary engineering and configuration software packages
        ~Dual-Network Configuration Abuse: Leveraging dual-homed configurations to pivot between IT and OT networks
        ~Physical Access Exploitation: Unauthorized physical access to engineering workstations through stolen hardware or direct interaction
        ~Privileged Account Compromise: Using stolen or compromised valid credentials with administrative access
        ~Removable Media Infection: Using infected USB drives or other removable media to compromise workstations
        ~Configuration Software Manipulation: Exploiting engineering workstation's configuration management capabilities
        ~Maintenance Session Hijacking: Intercepting and hijacking legitimate maintenance sessions
        ~Software License Server Attack: Compromising software license servers to gain access to engineering applications
        ~Diagnostic Tool Abuse: Exploiting diagnostic and troubleshooting tools present on engineering workstations
        ~Project File Tampering: Modifying or corrupting engineering project files and backups to gain persistent access
        ~Vendor Support Portal Abuse: Exploiting vendor support portals and remote access mechanisms
        ~Social Engineering of Engineers: Targeted phishing and social engineering attacks against engineering personnel
        ~Engineering Backup System Compromise: Exploiting backup and recovery systems of engineering workstations
        ~Third-Party Software Dependencies: Compromising third-party software components and libraries used in engineering applications
        ~Engineering Documentation Exploitation: Leveraging stolen or compromised engineering documentation to aid in workstation compromise
        ~Virtual Machine Escape: Exploiting virtualized engineering workstation environments
        ~Update Server Compromise: Compromising update mechanisms for engineering software
    execution: The adversary is trying to run code or manipulate system functions, parameters, and data in an unauthorized way.  Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a local or remote system, device, or other asset. This execution may also rely on unknowing end users or the manipulation of device operating modes to run. Adversaries may infect remote targets with programmed executables or malicious project files that operate according to specified behavior and may alter expected device behavior in subtle ways. Commands for execution may also be issued from command-line interfaces, APIs, GUIs, or other available interfaces. Techniques that run malicious code may also be paired with techniques from other tactics, particularly to aid network [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0102) and [Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0100), impact operations, and inhibit response functions.
      Scripting: Adversaries may use scripting languages to execute arbitrary code in the form of a pre-written script or in the form of user-supplied code to an interpreter. Scripting languages are programming languages that differ from compiled languages, in that scripting languages use an interpreter, instead of a compiler. These interpreters read and compile part of the source code just before it is executed, as opposed to compilers, which compile each and every line of code to an executable file. Scripting allows software developers to run their code on any system where the interpreter exists. This way, they can distribute one package, instead of precompiling executables for many different systems. Scripting languages, such as Python, have their interpreters shipped as a default with many Linux distributions.   In addition to being a useful tool for developers and administrators, scripting language interpreters may be abused by the adversary to execute code in the target environment. Due to the nature of scripting languages, this allows for weaponized code to be deployed to a target easily, and leaves open the possibility of on-the-fly scripting to perform a task.
        ~Engineering Workstation Scripts: Adversaries leverage automation scripts on engineering workstations to modify PLC programs or configurations
        ~HMI Scripting: Exploitation of built-in scripting capabilities in HMI/SCADA systems to execute malicious code
        ~Protocol-Specific Scripts: Using scripting to manipulate industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or OPC
        ~Maintenance Script Modification: Tampering with legitimate maintenance and backup scripts used in ICS environments
        ~Runtime Script Injection: Inserting malicious scripts into running processes or applications during system operation
        ~Configuration Script Abuse: Manipulation of device configuration scripts to alter system behavior
        ~Data Logging Scripts: Modifying or creating scripts that handle data logging to manipulate operational data
        ~Automation Server Scripts: Exploiting script execution capabilities in automation servers or controllers
        ~Diagnostic Script Manipulation: Abusing diagnostic and troubleshooting scripts to execute unauthorized commands
        ~Library Script Modification: Altering shared script libraries used across ICS components
        ~Historian Script Abuse: Exploiting scripting capabilities in historian software to manipulate historical data or execution flow
        ~Safety System Scripts: Manipulating scripts in safety instrumented systems (SIS) to bypass safety controls
        ~Batch Process Scripts: Modifying recipe or batch processing scripts to alter manufacturing processes
        ~Remote Terminal Scripts: Exploiting script execution in remote terminal units (RTUs) or field devices
        ~Vendor Tool Scripts: Abusing vendor-specific configuration and maintenance tool scripting capabilities
        ~Integration Gateway Scripts: Manipulating scripts in integration gateways between IT and OT networks
        ~Reporting System Scripts: Modifying automated report generation scripts to hide system changes
        ~Backup System Scripts: Exploiting backup and recovery scripts to maintain persistence or alter system states
      Execution through API: Adversaries may attempt to leverage Application Program Interfaces (APIs) used for communication between control software and the hardware. Specific functionality is often coded into APIs which can be called by software to engage specific functions on a device or other software.
        ~Native API Manipulation: Adversaries abuse native APIs built into ICS components to execute unauthorized commands or modify system behavior
        ~Protocol-Specific API Abuse: Exploitation of standard industrial protocol APIs (OPC, Modbus, DNP3) to execute malicious commands
        ~Engineering Software API Exploitation: Misuse of APIs exposed by engineering workstation software to manipulate control logic
        ~Vendor-Specific API Abuse: Targeting proprietary APIs unique to specific ICS vendors or products
        ~Remote API Access: Leveraging remote access capabilities in APIs to execute commands from external locations
        ~Configuration API Manipulation: Abuse of APIs used for device configuration and parameter settings
        ~Diagnostic API Exploitation: Misuse of diagnostic and maintenance APIs to execute unauthorized operations
        ~HMI API Abuse: Manipulation of Human Machine Interface APIs to execute commands or modify display data
        ~Historian API Exploitation: Abuse of data historian APIs to execute commands or manipulate historical data
        ~Safety System API Manipulation: Targeting safety system APIs to modify safety parameters or bypass protections
        ~Real-time Data Exchange API Abuse: Exploitation of APIs used for real-time data exchange between different ICS components
        ~Device Management API Manipulation: Abuse of APIs used for device discovery, registration, and management
        ~Batch Processing API Exploitation: Targeting APIs used in batch process control and recipe management
        ~Custom Script API Abuse: Manipulation of APIs that allow custom script execution or automation
        ~Field Device API Manipulation: Exploitation of APIs used to communicate with field devices like sensors and actuators
        ~Integration Gateway API Abuse: Targeting APIs used in integration gateways between IT and OT systems
        ~Alarm Management API Exploitation: Abuse of APIs handling alarm configuration and notification systems
        ~Asset Management API Manipulation: Misuse of APIs used for asset tracking and management functions
      User Execution: Adversaries may rely on a targeted organizations user interaction for the execution of malicious code. User interaction may consist of installing applications, opening email attachments, or granting higher permissions to documents.   Adversaries may embed malicious code or visual basic code into files such as Microsoft Word and Excel documents or software installers. (Citation: Booz Allen Hamilton) Execution of this code requires that the user enable scripting or write access within the document. Embedded code may not always be noticeable to the user especially in cases of trojanized software. (Citation: Daavid Hentunen, Antti Tikkanen June 2014)   A Chinese spearphishing campaign running from December 9, 2011 through February 29, 2012 delivered malware through spearphishing attachments which required user action to achieve execution. (Citation: CISA AA21-201A Pipeline Intrusion July 2021)
        ~Malicious Engineering Project Files: Users execute modified project files for PLCs, DCS, or SCADA systems containing malicious logic or configurations
        ~HMI Script Execution: Users unknowingly execute malicious scripts embedded in Human Machine Interface (HMI) applications or configurations
        ~Control System Software Add-ins: Users install or enable malicious add-ins or extensions for industrial control system software
        ~Engineering Workstation Applications: Users execute trojanized versions of legitimate engineering tools or software updates
        ~Remote Access Tool Installation: Users install seemingly legitimate remote access tools that contain hidden malicious functionality
        ~Configuration File Manipulation: Users import compromised configuration files for industrial devices or systems
        ~Firmware Update Packages: Users apply malicious firmware updates disguised as legitimate vendor updates
        ~Industrial Protocol Libraries: Users install compromised protocol libraries or drivers for industrial communication
      Program Organization Units: Program Organizational Units (POUs) are block structures used within PLC programming to create programs and projects. (Citation: Guidance - IEC61131) POUs can be used to hold user programs written in IEC 61131-3 languages: Structured text, Instruction list, Function block, and Ladder logic. (Citation: Guidance - IEC61131) Application - 201203 They can also provide additional functionality, such as establishing connections between the PLC and other devices using TCON. (Citation: PLCBlaster - Spenneberg)    Stuxnet uses a simple code-prepending infection technique to infect Organization Blocks (OB). For example, the following sequence of actions is performed when OB1 is infected  (Citation: Stuxnet - Symantec - 201102): *Increase the size of the original block. *Write malicious code to the beginning of the block. *Insert the original OB1 code after the malicious code.
        ~Organization Block Manipulation: Modification of OB blocks that control program execution flow and interrupt handling in PLCs
        ~Function Block Infection: Targeting function blocks to alter specific control logic or add malicious functionality
        ~Structured Text Modification: Manipulation of structured text programs to insert malicious logic or alter existing functionality
        ~Ladder Logic Tampering: Modification of ladder logic programs to alter control system behavior or inject malicious routines
        ~Network Connection Block Abuse: Exploitation of POUs responsible for network communications (e.g., TCON blocks)
        ~Code Prepending: Insertion of malicious code at the beginning of POU blocks while preserving original functionality
        ~POU Size Manipulation: Altering block sizes to accommodate malicious code or cause buffer overflows
        ~Interrupt Handler Modification: Targeting interrupt-handling POUs to alter system response to events
        ~Cross-Reference Tampering: Manipulation of POU cross-references to alter program flow or bypass security checks
        ~Runtime Block Modification: Dynamic modification of POUs during PLC runtime to alter behavior
        ~Data Block Corruption: Manipulation of data blocks associated with POUs to affect process variables and system state
        ~Vendor-Specific Block Exploitation: Targeting vendor-specific POU implementations and special function blocks
        ~Project File Tampering: Modification of POU definitions in project files before download to PLC
        ~Memory Block Reallocation: Manipulating POU memory allocation to create execution vulnerabilities
        ~Backup Block Manipulation: Tampering with POU backup mechanisms to persist malicious code
        ~Synchronization Block Tampering: Manipulation of POUs handling synchronization between redundant PLCs
        ~Library Block Injection: Compromising shared library POUs to affect multiple program instances
        ~Debug Block Abuse: Exploitation of POUs used for debugging and monitoring functions
        ~Configuration Block Modification: Tampering with POUs that handle system configuration and initialization
      Change Program State: Adversaries may attempt to change the state of the current program on a control device. Program state changes may be used to allow for another program to take over control or be loaded onto the device.
        ~Stop Program: Adversaries may forcibly stop the currently running program to allow loading of malicious code
        ~Program Download Mode: Adversaries may switch the device into program download mode to enable unauthorized code loading
        ~Memory Manipulation: Adversaries may modify program memory states to alter execution flow
        ~Runtime State Modification: Adversaries may modify program variables and states during execution
        ~Mode Switch Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit mode switching capabilities to change program execution states
        ~Program Counter Manipulation: Adversaries may modify program counters to redirect execution
        ~Watchdog Timer Disable: Adversaries may disable watchdog timers to prevent program state protection mechanisms
        ~State Table Modification: Adversaries may modify state transition tables to alter program behavior
        ~Program Buffer Overflow: Adversaries may exploit buffer overflows to modify program state
        ~Initialization State Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate program initialization states to inject malicious code
        ~Program Lock Override: Adversaries may bypass program lock mechanisms to enable state changes
        ~Redundancy State Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate redundant program states to force failover
        ~Maintenance Mode Exploitation: Adversaries may force devices into maintenance mode to enable program changes
        ~State Preservation Bypass: Adversaries may bypass state preservation mechanisms during power cycles
        ~Program Version Rollback: Adversaries may force program version states to revert to vulnerable versions
        ~Real-time Clock Manipulation: Adversaries may modify system time to trigger state changes
        ~Program Checksum Bypass: Adversaries may bypass checksum verification to enable unauthorized state changes
        ~Diagnostic Mode Exploitation: Adversaries may abuse diagnostic modes to modify program states
        ~Safety Mode Override: Adversaries may manipulate safety system program states to disable protective functions
        ~Program Parameter Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt critical program parameters to force state changes
        ~Sequence Logic Manipulation: Adversaries may modify sequential program logic states to disrupt operations
        ~Backup Program Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate backup program states to inject malicious code
        ~Emergency Mode Trigger: Adversaries may force emergency operation modes to enable program state changes
        ~Program State Synchronization Attack: Adversaries may disrupt state synchronization between redundant systems
      Native API: Adversaries may directly interact with the native OS application programming interface (API) to access system functions. Native APIs provide a controlled means of calling low-level OS services within the kernel, such as those involving hardware/devices, memory, and processes. (Citation: The MITRE Corporation May 2017) These native APIs are leveraged by the OS during system boot (when other system components are not yet initialized) as well as carrying out tasks and requests during routine operations.   Functionality provided by native APIs are often also exposed to user-mode applications via interfaces and libraries. For example, functions such as memcpy and direct operations on memory registers can be used to modify user and system memory space.
        ~Device Driver Manipulation: Adversaries may use native APIs to directly interact with device drivers controlling industrial equipment, potentially altering their behavior or bypassing safety controls
        ~Memory Register Operations: Adversaries may use native memory manipulation APIs to directly modify system memory registers that control industrial process parameters
        ~Process Control Interface: Adversaries may leverage native process management APIs to manipulate industrial control processes, including starting, stopping, or modifying critical system processes
        ~System Configuration Access: Adversaries may use native APIs to read or modify system configuration parameters that affect ICS operations
        ~Protocol Stack Interaction: Adversaries may utilize native networking APIs to directly interact with industrial protocol stacks like Modbus, DNP3, or OPC
        ~Real-time System Interface: Adversaries may abuse native APIs that handle real-time operations and scheduling to disrupt timing-critical industrial processes
        ~Firmware Communication Interface: Adversaries may leverage native APIs to interact with firmware update mechanisms or directly communicate with device firmware
        ~Diagnostic Interface Access: Adversaries may use native APIs to manipulate system diagnostic and monitoring functions, potentially hiding malicious activities
        ~Resource Management Interface: Adversaries may abuse native resource management APIs to affect system performance or cause resource exhaustion
        ~Authentication Bypass Interface: Adversaries may exploit native authentication APIs to bypass access controls or escalate privileges in ICS environments
      Hooking: Adversaries may hook into application programming interface (API) functions used by processes to redirect calls for execution and privilege escalation means. Windows processes often leverage these API functions to perform tasks that require reusable system resources. Windows API functions are typically stored in dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) as exported functions. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  One type of hooking seen in ICS involves redirecting calls to these functions via import address table (IAT) hooking. IAT hooking uses modifications to a process IAT, where pointers to imported API functions are stored. (Citation: Nicolas Falliere, Liam O Murchu, Eric Chien February 2011)
        ~Import Address Table (IAT) Hooking: Modifying the IAT to redirect API calls to malicious functions, particularly targeting ICS-specific DLLs and runtime libraries
        ~Inline Function Hooking: Directly modifying the function prologue to redirect execution flow in ICS applications
        ~Protocol Handler Hooking: Intercepting industrial protocol handlers like Modbus, DNP3, or OPC UA communications
        ~Driver Function Hooking: Targeting device driver interfaces used for PLC or RTU communications
        ~HMI Interface Hooking: Intercepting Human-Machine Interface API calls to manipulate displayed information
        ~Runtime Library Hooking: Targeting common runtime libraries used by ICS applications
        ~Message Queue Hooking: Intercepting message queues used for inter-process communication in ICS environments
        ~System Call Hooking: Low-level interception of system calls in ICS operating systems
        ~Memory-Mapped I/O Hooking: Intercepting direct memory access operations to field devices
        ~Event Handler Hooking: Modifying event handling mechanisms in SCADA systems
        ~Safety System API Hooking: Intercepting safety instrumented system (SIS) function calls
        ~Automation Protocol Stack Hooking: Targeting industrial automation protocol stacks like EtherCAT or PROFINET
        ~Engineering Software Hooking: Intercepting API calls in ICS engineering and configuration tools
        ~Real-time OS API Hooking: Targeting real-time operating system APIs used in ICS environments
        ~Historian Interface Hooking: Intercepting data logging and historical data access functions
        ~Fieldbus Communication Hooking: Targeting fieldbus protocol interfaces like Foundation Fieldbus or HART
        ~Remote Access Gateway Hooking: Intercepting remote access and maintenance interface calls
        ~Process Control Network Hooking: Targeting network stack APIs specific to process control networks
        ~Embedded Device API Hooking: Targeting embedded device firmware interfaces and system calls
        ~Vendor Protocol Hooking: Intercepting vendor-specific proprietary protocols and interfaces
        ~Diagnostic Interface Hooking: Targeting maintenance and troubleshooting interfaces
        ~Time Synchronization Hooking: Intercepting time synchronization protocol interfaces like PTP or NTP
        ~Asset Management Hooking: Targeting asset management and device configuration interfaces
        ~Alarm Management Hooking: Intercepting alarm handling and notification system APIs
      Change Operating Mode: Adversaries may change the operating mode of a controller to gain additional access to engineering functions such as Program Download.   Programmable controllers typically have several modes of operation that control the state of the user program and control access to the controllers API. Operating modes can be physically selected using a key switch on the face of the controller but may also be selected with calls to the controllers API. Operating modes and the mechanisms by which they are selected often vary by vendor and product line. Some commonly implemented operating modes are described below:    * Program - This mode must be enabled before changes can be made to a devices program. This allows program uploads and downloads between the device and an engineering workstation. Often the PLCs logic Is halted, and all outputs may be forced off. (Citation: N.A. October 2017)   * Run - Execution of the devices program occurs in this mode. Input and output (values, points, tags, elements, etc.) are monitored and used according to the programs logic. [Program Upload](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0845) and [Program Download](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843) are disabled while in this mode. (Citation: Omron) (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)  (Citation: N.A. October 2017) (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)    * Remote - Allows for remote changes to a PLCs operation mode. (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)     * Stop - The PLC and program is stopped, while in this mode, outputs are forced off. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Reset - Conditions on the PLC are reset to their original states. Warm resets may retain some memory while cold resets will reset all I/O and data registers. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Test / Monitor mode - Similar to run mode, I/O is processed, although this mode allows for monitoring, force set, resets, and more generally tuning or debugging of the system. Often monitor mode may be used as a trial for initialization. (Citation: Omron)
        ~Physical Key Switch Manipulation: Adversaries physically tamper with or bypass the key switch mechanism to change operating modes without authorization
        ~Remote API Exploitation: Adversaries exploit vulnerabilities in the controller's API to remotely change operating modes
        ~Engineering Workstation Compromise: Adversaries compromise engineering workstations to issue legitimate-looking mode change commands
        ~Mode Change During Operation: Adversaries change modes during critical operations to cause disruption
        ~Maintenance Mode Abuse: Adversaries exploit maintenance windows to make unauthorized mode changes
        ~Mode Change Sequencing: Adversaries use specific sequences of mode changes to achieve unintended effects
        ~Mode Change Through Protocol Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate industrial protocols to force mode changes
        ~Memory Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate memory locations controlling mode states
        ~Firmware-Level Mode Control: Adversaries exploit firmware vulnerabilities to override mode controls
        ~Mode Change Through Configuration Files: Adversaries modify configuration files to alter mode behavior
        ~Time-Based Mode Switching: Adversaries exploit timing windows between mode transitions to inject unauthorized commands
        ~Social Engineering for Mode Access: Adversaries manipulate operators into performing mode changes through social engineering
        ~Supply Chain Mode Manipulation: Adversaries compromise PLC hardware/software during manufacturing to enable unauthorized mode changes
        ~Mode State Spoofing: Adversaries forge mode state indicators to hide unauthorized mode changes
        ~Distributed Mode Attack: Adversaries coordinate mode changes across multiple PLCs to amplify impact
        ~Mode Change Through Backup Systems: Adversaries exploit backup or redundant system switchovers to trigger mode changes
        ~Mode Authentication Bypass: Adversaries bypass authentication mechanisms specifically designed for mode changes
        ~Mode Change Through Safety Systems: Adversaries trigger safety systems to force mode changes
      Autorun Image: Adversaries may leverage AutoRun functionality or scripts to execute malicious code. Devices configured to enable AutoRun functionality or legacy operating systems may be susceptible to abuse of these features to run malicious code stored on various forms of removeable media (i.e., USB, Disk Images [.ISO]). Commonly, AutoRun or AutoPlay are disabled in many operating systems configurations to mitigate against this technique. If a device is configured to enable AutoRun or AutoPlay, adversaries may execute code on the device by mounting the removable media to the device, either through physical or virtual means. This may be especially relevant for virtual machine environments where disk images may be dynamically mapped to a guest system on a hypervisor.    An example could include an adversary gaining access to a hypervisor through the management interface to modify a virtual machine’s hardware configuration. They could then deploy an iso image with a malicious AutoRun script to cause the virtual machine to automatically execute the code contained on the disk image. This would enable the execution of malicious code within a virtual machine without needing any prior remote access to that system.
        ~Physical USB Media: Adversaries may use physical USB devices with malicious AutoRun configurations to execute code when connected to ICS systems
        ~Virtual Disk Images: Adversaries may create and mount malicious ISO or other virtual disk images containing AutoRun scripts
        ~Network Shares: Adversaries may abuse AutoRun functionality through mapped network drives or shares
        ~Hypervisor Management: Adversaries may manipulate VM configurations to force AutoRun execution of mounted disk images
        ~Legacy AutoRun Abuse: Adversaries may specifically target older ICS systems with AutoRun enabled by default
        ~Removable Drive Emulation: Adversaries may use USB device emulators to trick systems into treating malicious payloads as legitimate removable media
        ~AutoRun.inf Manipulation: Adversaries may modify AutoRun configuration files to execute arbitrary commands
        ~System Backup Images: Adversaries may compromise system backup images to include malicious AutoRun configurations
      Command-Line Interface: Adversaries may utilize command-line interfaces (CLIs) to interact with systems and execute commands. CLIs provide a means of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many types of platforms and devices within control systems environments. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018) Adversaries may also use CLIs to install and run new software, including malicious tools that may be installed over the course of an operation.  CLIs are typically accessed locally, but can also be exposed via services, such as SSH, Telnet, and RDP.  Commands that are executed in the CLI execute with the current permissions level of the process running the terminal emulator, unless the command specifies a change in permissions context. Many controllers have CLI interfaces for management purposes.
        ~Serial Console Access: Adversaries may connect directly to device serial ports to access CLI functions, bypassing network security controls
        ~Engineering Workstation CLI: Adversaries may use engineering software's built-in CLI capabilities to send commands to controllers
        ~Protocol-Specific CLI: Adversaries may utilize industrial protocol-specific command line tools (like Modbus, DNP3 clients) to interact with devices
        ~Remote Terminal Access: Adversaries may exploit remote terminal services (SSH, Telnet) exposed on ICS devices to gain CLI access
        ~Vendor-Specific Tools: Adversaries may leverage vendor-provided CLI utilities designed for device configuration and maintenance
        ~Script Execution: Adversaries may create and execute scripts through CLI to automate malicious actions on ICS devices
        ~Diagnostic Interface: Adversaries may access diagnostic or debug CLI interfaces meant for troubleshooting
        ~Configuration Management: Adversaries may use CLI to modify device configurations, parameters, and operating modes
      Modify Controller Tasking: Adversaries may modify the tasking of a controller to allow for the execution of their own programs. This can allow an adversary to manipulate the execution flow and behavior of a controller.   According to 61131-3, the association of a Task with a Program Organization Unit (POU) defines a task association. (Citation: IEC February 2013) An adversary may modify these associations or create new ones to manipulate the execution flow of a controller. Modification of controller tasking can be accomplished using a Program Download in addition to other types of program modification such as online edit and program append.  Tasks have properties, such as interval, frequency and priority to meet the requirements of program execution. Some controller vendors implement tasks with implicit, pre-defined properties whereas others allow for these properties to be formulated explicitly. An adversary may associate their program with tasks that have a higher priority or execute associated programs more frequently. For instance, to ensure cyclic execution of their program on a Siemens controller, an adversary may add their program to the task, Organization Block 1 (OB1).
        ~Program Download Modification: Adversary modifies task associations by downloading modified program configurations to the controller
        ~Online Task Edit: Real-time modification of task properties and associations while the controller is running
        ~Priority Level Manipulation: Changing task priority levels to ensure malicious code executes before legitimate tasks
        ~Task Interval Modification: Altering the frequency or timing of task execution to affect controller behavior
        ~Program Append Injection: Adding malicious code to existing task structures without replacing the original program
        ~Task Association Redirection: Redirecting existing task associations to point to malicious program units
        ~Cyclic Task Manipulation: Modifying cyclic execution blocks (like Siemens OB1) to include unauthorized code
        ~Task Property Override: Overriding pre-defined task properties to change execution behavior
        ~Background Task Injection: Installing malicious programs as background tasks to avoid detection
        ~Vendor-Specific Task Exploitation: Exploiting vendor-specific task implementation features for unauthorized execution
        ~Task Synchronization Manipulation: Modifying how multiple tasks synchronize and communicate to create race conditions or timing attacks
        ~Interrupt Task Hijacking: Modifying interrupt service routines and their associated tasks
        ~Event-Triggered Task Modification: Altering tasks that respond to specific events or conditions
        ~Memory-Resident Task Injection: Creating new tasks that reside in volatile memory to avoid persistent changes
        ~Task Resource Allocation Manipulation: Modifying how system resources are allocated to different tasks
      Graphical User Interface: Adversaries may attempt to gain access to a machine via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) to enhance execution capabilities. Access to a GUI allows a user to interact with a computer in a more visual manner than a CLI. A GUI allows users to move a cursor and click on interface objects, with a mouse and keyboard as the main input devices, as opposed to just using the keyboard.  If physical access is not an option, then access might be possible via protocols such as VNC on Linux-based and Unix-based operating systems, and RDP on Windows operating systems. An adversary can use this access to execute programs and applications on the target machine.
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP): Adversaries may use RDP to gain remote GUI access to engineering workstations and control system interfaces
        ~Virtual Network Computing (VNC): Adversaries may leverage VNC protocols to remotely access and manipulate ICS GUI environments
        ~Human Machine Interface (HMI) Manipulation: Adversaries may interact with industrial HMIs to modify process controls and parameters
        ~Engineering Software GUI Access: Adversaries may utilize vendor-specific engineering software interfaces to modify control logic and configurations
        ~Operator Console Access: Adversaries may target operator workstation GUIs to execute unauthorized commands or modify system settings
        ~Screen Sharing Services: Adversaries may exploit screen sharing capabilities built into ICS software to gain unauthorized GUI access
        ~Remote Access Tools (RAT) with GUI Control: Adversaries may deploy RATs that provide GUI control capabilities specific to ICS environments
        ~Touch Screen Interface Manipulation: Adversaries may exploit touch screen interfaces common in industrial environments to execute unauthorized actions
    persistence: The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold in your ICS environment.  Persistence consists of techniques that adversaries use to maintain access to ICS systems and devices across restarts, changed credentials, and other interruptions that could cut off their access. Techniques used for persistence include any access, action, or configuration changes that allow them to secure their ongoing activity and keep their foothold on systems. This may include replacing or hijacking legitimate code, firmware, and other project files, or adding startup code and downloading programs onto devices.
      Hardcoded Credentials: Adversaries may leverage credentials that are hardcoded in software or firmware to gain an unauthorized interactive user session to an asset. Examples credentials that may be hardcoded in an asset include:  * Username/Passwords * Cryptographic keys/Certificates * API tokens  Unlike [Default Credentials](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0812), these credentials are built into the system in a way that they either cannot be changed by the asset owner, or may be infeasible to change because of the impact it would cause to the control system operation. These credentials may be reused across whole product lines or device models and are often not published or known to the owner and operators of the asset.   Adversaries may utilize these hardcoded credentials to move throughout the control system environment or provide reliable access for their tools to interact with industrial assets.
        ~Firmware Authentication Keys: Exploitation of cryptographic keys embedded in device firmware for authentication
        ~Engineering Software Credentials: Hardcoded credentials in engineering workstation software and configuration tools
        ~Protocol Authentication Tokens: Built-in authentication tokens used in industrial protocols
        ~Maintenance Backdoor Accounts: Hidden service accounts embedded by manufacturers
        ~Component Authentication Keys: Hardcoded keys used for component-to-component authentication
        ~Web Interface Credentials: Built-in credentials for web-based management interfaces
        ~API Access Keys: Permanent API keys embedded in control system applications
        ~Debug Interface Credentials: Hardcoded credentials for debugging and diagnostic ports
        ~Communication Module Keys: Pre-shared keys in communication modules
        ~Backup System Credentials: Hardcoded credentials in backup and recovery systems
        ~SCADA Client Credentials: Hardcoded authentication in SCADA client applications
        ~PLC Program Credentials: Authentication keys embedded in PLC logic programs
        ~OEM Configuration Tools: Vendor-specific hardcoded credentials in configuration utilities
        ~Remote Terminal Unit Keys: Embedded authentication in RTU firmware
        ~Historian Database Credentials: Hardcoded access credentials in historian systems
        ~Field Device Authentication: Pre-programmed authentication in field devices
        ~Network Equipment Backdoors: Hardcoded credentials in industrial network equipment
        ~HMI Default Access: Built-in operator access credentials in HMI systems
        ~Safety System Credentials: Hardcoded authentication in safety instrumented systems (SIS)
        ~Building Management Credentials: Embedded credentials in building automation systems
        ~Proprietary Protocol Keys: Hardcoded keys in vendor-specific industrial protocols
        ~Cloud Gateway Credentials: Built-in authentication for ICS cloud connectivity
        ~Integration Middleware Credentials: Hardcoded credentials in multi-vendor integration platforms
        ~Device Discovery Credentials: Built-in authentication for asset discovery protocols
      Valid Accounts: Adversaries may steal the credentials of a specific user or service account using credential access techniques. In some cases, default credentials for control system devices may be publicly available. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on hosts and within the network, and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems. Compromised and default credentials may also grant an adversary increased privilege to specific systems and devices or access to restricted areas of the network. Adversaries may choose not to use malware or tools, in conjunction with the legitimate access those credentials provide, to make it harder to detect their presence or to control devices and send legitimate commands in an unintended way.   Adversaries may also create accounts, sometimes using predefined account names and passwords, to provide a means of backup access for persistence. (Citation: Booz Allen Hamilton)   The overlap of credentials and permissions across a network of systems is of concern because the adversary may be able to pivot across accounts and systems to reach a high level of access (i.e., domain or enterprise administrator)  and possibly between the enterprise and operational technology environments. Adversaries may be able to leverage valid credentials from one system to gain access to another system.
        ~Default Accounts: Use of default vendor-supplied accounts and passwords in ICS devices and software
        ~Local Accounts: Exploitation of local user and service accounts on ICS systems
        ~Domain Accounts: Leverage of enterprise domain accounts with access to ICS systems
        ~Application Accounts: Use of valid accounts in ICS applications and engineering workstations
        ~Controller Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for PLC and RTU access
        ~HMI Accounts: Use of human-machine interface operator and administrator accounts
        ~Backup Accounts: Creation or use of backup/emergency access accounts
        ~Shared Accounts: Exploitation of shared operator or maintenance accounts
        ~Service Accounts: Use of automated service and process accounts
        ~Remote Access Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for remote maintenance and support
        ~Vendor Management Accounts: Exploitation of vendor-specific maintenance and support accounts
        ~Project File Accounts: Use of credentials embedded in project files and configurations
        ~Database Accounts: Leverage of historian and process database account credentials
        ~Third-Party Integration Accounts: Use of accounts that connect ICS to external systems
        ~Cloud Service Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for cloud-based ICS services
        ~Temporary Contractor Accounts: Use of time-limited accounts created for contractors
        ~Protocol-Specific Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for specific industrial protocols (e.g., OPC, Modbus)
        ~Legacy System Accounts: Use of accounts on older systems that cannot be patched or updated
        ~Emergency Override Accounts: Exploitation of emergency access or override account credentials
      Modify Program: Adversaries may modify or add a program on a controller to affect how it interacts with the physical process, peripheral devices and other hosts on the network. Modification to controller programs can be accomplished using a Program Download in addition to other types of program modification such as online edit and program append.   Program modification encompasses the addition and modification of instructions and logic contained in Program Organization Units (POU)  (Citation: IEC February 2013) and similar programming elements found on controllers. This can include, for example, adding new functions to a controller, modifying the logic in existing functions and making new calls from one function to another.   Some programs may allow an adversary to interact directly with the native API of the controller to take advantage of obscure features or vulnerabilities.
        ~Program Download: Adversaries may download modified programs directly to controllers using engineering software or other tools that support program transfer functionality
        ~Online Edit: Adversaries may modify program logic while the controller is running using online editing features available in engineering software
        ~Program Append: Adversaries may append additional code or functions to existing programs without replacing the entire program
        ~Function Block Modification: Adversaries may modify existing function blocks to alter control logic or add malicious functionality
        ~Native API Manipulation: Adversaries may interact directly with controller APIs to modify program behavior in ways not intended by the manufacturer
        ~Ladder Logic Manipulation: Adversaries may modify ladder logic programs to alter control system behavior and industrial processes
        ~Instruction List Modification: Adversaries may modify instruction list (IL) programs to change controller behavior
        ~Structured Text Alteration: Adversaries may modify structured text (ST) programs to implement malicious logic
        ~Sequential Function Chart Tampering: Adversaries may modify sequential function charts to alter process sequences and timing
        ~Custom Function Implementation: Adversaries may implement custom functions to extend or replace existing program functionality
        ~Timer Manipulation: Adversaries may modify timer values and logic to disrupt process timing and create race conditions
        ~Memory Block Modification: Adversaries may modify memory blocks and data areas used by programs to affect their behavior
        ~I/O Configuration Changes: Adversaries may modify I/O configurations within programs to alter how the controller interacts with field devices
        ~Program State Manipulation: Adversaries may modify program state transitions and conditions to affect how programs execute
        ~Interrupt Routine Modification: Adversaries may modify interrupt service routines to inject malicious code that executes during specific events
        ~Runtime Environment Modification: Adversaries may modify the runtime environment settings to affect how programs execute
        ~Program Backup Tampering: Adversaries may modify program backups to ensure persistence of malicious changes across system restores
        ~Communication Block Modification: Adversaries may modify communication blocks within programs to alter network interactions
        ~Program Validation Bypass: Adversaries may modify program validation mechanisms to bypass security checks
        ~Watchdog Timer Modification: Adversaries may modify watchdog timer logic to prevent detection of malicious program behavior
      Module Firmware: Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment.     This technique is similar to [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0857), but is conducted on other system components that may not have the same capabilities or level of integrity checking. Although it results in a device re-image, malicious device firmware may provide persistent access to remaining devices. (Citation: Daniel Peck,  Dale Peterson January 2009)    An easy point of access for an adversary is the Ethernet card, which may have its own CPU, RAM, and operating system. The adversary may attack and likely exploit the computer on an Ethernet card. Exploitation of the Ethernet card computer may enable the adversary to accomplish additional attacks, such as the following: (Citation: Daniel Peck,  Dale Peterson January 2009)    * Delayed Attack - The adversary may stage an attack in advance and choose when to launch it, such as at a particularly damaging time.   * Brick the Ethernet Card - Malicious firmware may be programmed to result in an Ethernet card failure, requiring a factory return.   * Random Attack or Failure - The adversary may load malicious firmware onto multiple field devices. Execution of an attack and the time it occurs is generated by a pseudo-random number generator.    * A Field Device Worm - The adversary may choose to identify all field devices of the same model, with the end goal of performing a device-wide compromise.   * Attack Other Cards on the Field Device - Although it is not the most important module in a field device, the Ethernet card is most accessible to the adversary and malware. Compromise of the Ethernet card may provide a more direct route to compromising other modules, such as the CPU module.
        ~Ethernet Card Firmware Modification: Adversaries modify firmware of network interface cards to establish persistence and enable further attacks
        ~CPU Module Firmware Tampering: Adversaries target the main processing unit's firmware to maintain control over device operations
        ~I/O Module Firmware Compromise: Adversaries modify firmware of input/output modules to manipulate sensor readings or control signals
        ~Memory Module Firmware Alteration: Adversaries modify firmware of memory modules to maintain persistent access or corrupt device operation
        ~Communication Module Exploitation: Adversaries target firmware of communication modules like serial or fieldbus interfaces
        ~Power Supply Module Firmware Attack: Adversaries modify firmware of power management modules to affect device reliability
        ~Backup Module Firmware Manipulation: Adversaries compromise firmware of backup or redundancy modules to affect failover capabilities
        ~Remote Access Module Tampering: Adversaries modify firmware of remote access cards or modules to maintain unauthorized access
        ~Protocol Converter Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of protocol conversion modules to manipulate industrial protocol communications
        ~Expansion Card Firmware Modification: Adversaries target firmware of various expansion cards or add-on modules
        ~Diagnostics Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of diagnostic or monitoring modules to hide malicious activity or system state
        ~Safety Module Firmware: Adversaries compromise firmware of safety instrumented system (SIS) modules to bypass safety controls
        ~Motion Control Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of motion control or servo drive modules to manipulate physical operations
        ~Human Machine Interface (HMI) Module Firmware: Adversaries target firmware of display or operator interface modules
        ~Real-Time Clock Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of timing modules to disrupt time-sensitive operations
        ~Analog Signal Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of analog input/output processing modules to manipulate sensor readings or control signals
        ~Redundancy Control Module Firmware: Adversaries target firmware of modules managing failover and redundancy switching
        ~Field Device Interface Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of modules that interface with field devices like sensors and actuators
        ~Data Logging Module Firmware: Adversaries compromise firmware of modules responsible for historical data collection and storage
        ~Process Control Module Firmware: Adversaries modify firmware of specialized process control modules that manage specific industrial processes
      System Firmware: System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network.   An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers. (Citation: Basnight, Zachry, et al.)
        ~Malicious Firmware Update Package: Creating and distributing malicious firmware updates disguised as legitimate vendor updates
        ~Direct Memory Modification: Directly modifying firmware in device memory without using update mechanisms
        ~Firmware Downgrade Attack: Installing older, vulnerable versions of firmware to exploit known vulnerabilities
        ~Remote Firmware Update Exploitation: Exploiting remote firmware update capabilities over network protocols
        ~Boot Firmware Modification: Targeting and modifying boot firmware to maintain persistence across device restarts
        ~Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA) Manipulation: Modifying programmable hardware firmware
        ~Embedded Controller Firmware: Targeting firmware of embedded controllers in ICS devices
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Installing malicious firmware during manufacturing or distribution
        ~Firmware Configuration Tampering: Modifying firmware configuration settings without replacing the entire firmware
        ~Recovery Mode Exploitation: Exploiting device recovery/emergency modes to install unauthorized firmware
        ~Peripheral Device Firmware: Modifying firmware of connected peripheral devices or sensors
        ~Real-Time Operating System (RTOS) Firmware: Targeting the firmware of RTOS systems common in ICS
        ~Protocol Stack Firmware: Modifying communication protocol firmware implementations
        ~Backup Firmware Manipulation: Tampering with backup firmware images stored on devices
        ~Firmware Encryption Bypass: Exploiting weak or absent firmware encryption
        ~Hardware Security Module (HSM) Firmware: Targeting security module firmware in ICS devices
        ~Watchdog Timer Firmware: Modifying watchdog timer firmware to prevent detection
        ~Firmware Update Verification Bypass: Exploiting weak signature verification in firmware updates
        ~Safety Instrumented System Firmware: Targeting firmware of safety-critical systems
        ~PLC Firmware Modification: Specifically targeting Programmable Logic Controller firmware
        ~Remote Terminal Unit Firmware: Modifying firmware of RTU devices
        ~Industrial Network Switch Firmware: Targeting network infrastructure firmware in ICS environments
        ~Human Machine Interface Firmware: Modifying HMI device firmware
        ~Intelligent Electronic Device Firmware: Targeting IED firmware in power systems
        ~Device Configuration Unit Firmware: Modifying firmware of configuration and programming units
        ~Redundant Control System Firmware: Targeting firmware in redundant/backup control systems
        ~Process Historian Firmware: Modifying firmware of historical data collection systems
        ~Legacy Device Firmware: Specifically targeting older generation device firmware
        ~Industrial Gateway Firmware: Targeting protocol conversion and gateway device firmware
        ~Industrial Firewall Firmware: Modifying firmware of ICS-specific firewall devices
        ~Environmental Monitoring System Firmware: Targeting environmental control and monitoring system firmware
      Project File Infection: Adversaries may attempt to infect project files with malicious code. These project files may consist of objects, program organization units, variables such as tags, documentation, and other configurations needed for PLC programs to function. (Citation: Beckhoff) Using built in functions of the engineering software, adversaries may be able to download an infected program to a PLC in the operating environment enabling further [Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0104) and [Persistence](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0110) techniques. (Citation: PLCdev)   Adversaries may export their own code into project files with conditions to execute at specific intervals. (Citation: Nicolas Falliere, Liam O Murchu, Eric Chien February 2011) Malicious programs allow adversaries control of all aspects of the process enabled by the PLC. Once the project file is downloaded to a PLC the workstation device may be disconnected with the infected project file still executing. (Citation: PLCdev)
        ~Function Block Manipulation: Adversaries may modify existing function blocks or insert malicious function blocks within project files to execute unauthorized operations while appearing legitimate.
        ~Tag Variable Modification: Adversaries may alter tag definitions and variable configurations to manipulate process control parameters or create hidden control channels.
        ~Startup Routine Infection: Adversaries may inject malicious code into PLC startup routines to ensure persistence across device reboots.
        ~Timer-Based Execution: Adversaries may implement time-based triggers in project files to execute malicious functions at specific intervals or timestamps.
        ~Condition-Based Execution: Adversaries may add malicious logic that executes based on specific process conditions or sensor values.
        ~Configuration File Tampering: Adversaries may modify configuration files within the project to alter device settings and enable unauthorized operations.
        ~Library Module Infection: Adversaries may compromise reusable library modules to spread malicious code across multiple projects.
        ~Documentation Element Abuse: Adversaries may hide malicious code within project documentation elements that are parsed by the engineering software.
    privilege-escalation: The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions.  Privilege Escalation consists of techniques that adversaries use to gain higher-level permissions on a system or network. Adversaries can often enter and explore a network with unprivileged access but require elevated permissions to follow through on their objectives. Common approaches are to take advantage of system weaknesses, misconfigurations, and vulnerabilities.
      Exploitation for Privilege Escalation: Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Security constructs such as permission levels will often hinder access to information and use of certain techniques, so adversaries will likely need to perform privilege escalation to include use of software exploitation to circumvent those restrictions. (Citation: The MITRE Corporation)   When initially gaining access to a system, an adversary may be operating within a lower privileged process which will prevent them from accessing certain resources on the system. Vulnerabilities may exist, usually in operating system components and software commonly running at higher permissions, that can be exploited to gain higher levels of access on the system. This could enable someone to move from unprivileged or user level permissions to SYSTEM or root permissions depending on the component that is vulnerable. This may be a necessary step for an adversary compromising an endpoint system that has been properly configured and limits other privilege escalation methods. (Citation: The MITRE Corporation)
        ~Control System Buffer Overflow: Exploitation of buffer overflow vulnerabilities in control system software components to gain elevated privileges
        ~HMI Software Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Human-Machine Interface software to escalate privileges on operator workstations
        ~Protocol Stack Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in industrial protocol implementations to gain elevated access
        ~Engineering Software Vulnerabilities: Targeting vulnerabilities in engineering and configuration software used to program PLCs
        ~Firmware Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in device firmware to gain elevated privileges on industrial devices
        ~Legacy System Vulnerabilities: Targeting known vulnerabilities in older, unpatched ICS components
        ~Service Configuration Exploitation: Exploiting misconfigurations in ICS service accounts and permissions
        ~Memory Corruption: Exploiting memory corruption vulnerabilities in ICS software components
        ~Command Injection: Exploiting command injection vulnerabilities in ICS software interfaces
        ~Privilege Boundary Violation: Exploiting vulnerabilities that allow crossing privilege boundaries in ICS applications
        ~Real-time OS Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in real-time operating systems used in ICS devices
        ~Historian Database Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in historian databases to escalate privileges
        ~Remote Access Gateway Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in remote access components and VPN gateways
        ~Third-party Library Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in common libraries used across ICS applications
        ~Network Stack Component Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in network stack implementations specific to ICS devices
        ~OPC Server Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in OPC servers and interfaces
        ~Device Driver Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in device drivers for ICS hardware
        ~Time Sync Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in time synchronization services
        ~Authentication Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities that allow bypassing authentication mechanisms
        ~Web Interface Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in web-based ICS management interfaces
        ~Safety System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in safety instrumented systems (SIS) and emergency shutdown systems
        ~Virtualization Layer Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in ICS virtualization platforms
        ~Industrial Middleware Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in middleware that connects different ICS components
        ~Field Device Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities specific to field devices like sensors and actuators
        ~Backup System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in backup and recovery systems
        ~Asset Management System Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in asset management and inventory systems
        ~Alarm Management System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in alarm management and notification systems
        ~Configuration Management Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in configuration management databases and tools
        ~Reporting System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in reporting and analytics systems
        ~License Management System Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in license management systems
        ~Custom Protocol Stack Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in proprietary or custom industrial protocol implementations
        ~Monitoring System Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in performance monitoring and diagnostics systems
        ~Edge Computing Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in edge computing devices and gateways
        ~IIoT Device Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in Industrial Internet of Things devices
        ~Integration Layer Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in systems that integrate IT and OT networks
        ~Simulation System Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in process simulation and modeling systems
        ~Data Diode Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in unidirectional security gateways
        ~Industrial Firewall Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in industrial firewalls and security appliances
        ~Wireless Gateway Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in industrial wireless communication gateways
        ~Patch Management System Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in patch management and deployment systems
      Hooking: Adversaries may hook into application programming interface (API) functions used by processes to redirect calls for execution and privilege escalation means. Windows processes often leverage these API functions to perform tasks that require reusable system resources. Windows API functions are typically stored in dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) as exported functions. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  One type of hooking seen in ICS involves redirecting calls to these functions via import address table (IAT) hooking. IAT hooking uses modifications to a process IAT, where pointers to imported API functions are stored. (Citation: Nicolas Falliere, Liam O Murchu, Eric Chien February 2011)
        ~Import Address Table (IAT) Modification: Adversaries modify the IAT to redirect API calls to malicious functions, particularly targeting ICS-specific DLLs
        ~Runtime API Hooking: Attackers inject code during runtime to intercept API calls from ICS applications to system libraries
        ~DLL Injection Hooking: Malicious code is injected into legitimate ICS processes to intercept API calls
        ~Kernel API Hooking: Adversaries hook into kernel-level APIs to intercept system calls from ICS applications
        ~Device Driver Hooking: Attackers target device driver interfaces to intercept communications between ICS hardware and software
        ~Protocol Stack Hooking: Modification of industrial protocol stacks to intercept and modify communication between ICS components
        ~Memory-Based API Hooking: Direct modification of process memory to redirect API calls in ICS applications
        ~Service Function Hooking: Targeting Windows service functions used by ICS applications
    evasion: The adversary is trying to avoid security defenses.  Evasion consists of techniques that adversaries use to avoid technical defenses throughout their campaign. Techniques used for evasion include removal of indicators of compromise, spoofing communications, and exploiting software vulnerabilities. Adversaries may also leverage and abuse trusted devices and processes to hide their activity, possibly by masquerading as master devices or native software. Methods of defense evasion for this purpose are often more passive in nature.
      Exploitation for Evasion: Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to evade detection. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to disable or circumvent security features.    Adversaries may have prior knowledge through [Remote System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0888) about security features implemented on control devices. These device security features will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of firmware RAM/ROM consistency checks on control devices being targeted by adversaries to enable the installation of malicious [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0857).
        ~Firmware Integrity Check Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in firmware verification mechanisms to bypass integrity checks and load unauthorized firmware
        ~Authentication Mechanism Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in authentication systems to bypass access controls without generating alerts
        ~Security Monitor Tampering: Exploiting vulnerabilities in security monitoring tools to prevent alert generation or logging
        ~Protocol Security Feature Bypass: Exploiting industrial protocol security features to avoid detection while maintaining malicious communications
        ~Memory Protection Bypass: Exploiting vulnerabilities in memory protection mechanisms to execute unauthorized code without detection
        ~Access Control Validation Bypass: Targeting flaws in access control validation to gain unauthorized access while avoiding logging
        ~Trust Relationship Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in trust mechanisms between ICS components to evade detection
        ~Security Feature Deactivation: Exploiting vulnerabilities to disable security features without generating alerts or logs
      Indicator Removal on Host: Adversaries may attempt to remove indicators of their presence on a system in an effort to cover their tracks. In cases where an adversary may feel detection is imminent, they may try to overwrite, delete, or cover up changes they have made to the device.
        ~Clear System Logs: Adversaries may clear or delete system logs, alarm logs, and event logs to remove evidence of their activities on ICS systems
        ~Remove Configuration Changes: Adversaries may restore previous configuration settings or remove evidence of unauthorized changes to PLC programs, HMI configurations, and control logic
        ~Delete Malware Components: Adversaries may remove malicious files, scripts, or programs they deployed on engineering workstations and control system components
        ~Clear Diagnostic Data: Adversaries may delete or manipulate diagnostic data and troubleshooting logs that could reveal system anomalies or unauthorized operations
        ~Restore Process Variables: Adversaries may reset process variables and parameters to hide evidence of tampering with industrial processes
        ~Remove Custom Programs: Adversaries may delete unauthorized programs or routines added to PLCs and other industrial controllers
        ~Clear Historical Data: Adversaries may delete or manipulate historical process data and trending information that could reveal their activities
        ~Remove Network Artifacts: Adversaries may clear network capture files, protocol analysis logs, and other network monitoring data
        ~Delete Backup Files: Adversaries may remove backup copies of modified control logic and configuration files to prevent recovery and analysis
        ~Clear HMI Cache: Adversaries may clear cached data and temporary files from Human Machine Interface systems that could reveal unauthorized access
        ~Clear Firmware Update Logs: Adversaries may remove evidence of unauthorized firmware modifications or updates on ICS devices
        ~Remove Remote Access Traces: Adversaries may clear remote access logs and connection history from engineering software and remote terminal units
        ~Delete Historian Records: Adversaries may manipulate or delete industrial historian databases to remove evidence of process anomalies
        ~Clear Safety System Logs: Adversaries may remove evidence of safety system bypasses or modifications
        ~Remove OPC Server Logs: Adversaries may clear logs from OPC servers that could reveal unauthorized data access or system interactions
        ~Delete Controller State Data: Adversaries may remove stored state information and diagnostic data from programmable logic controllers
        ~Clear Asset Management Logs: Adversaries may remove evidence from asset management and inventory systems that track ICS device configurations
        ~Remove SCADA Alert History: Adversaries may delete historical alert data from SCADA systems to hide evidence of suspicious activities
        ~Clear Protocol Conversion Logs: Adversaries may remove evidence from protocol gateways and converters that handle industrial protocol translations
        ~Remove Field Device Diagnostics: Adversaries may clear diagnostic data from field devices like sensors, actuators, and intelligent electronic devices (IEDs)
        ~Delete Change Management Records: Adversaries may remove entries from change management and version control systems used for ICS configurations
        ~Clear Real-time Database Logs: Adversaries may remove traces from real-time databases that store process variables and system states
        ~Remove Device Authentication Logs: Adversaries may clear logs of device authentication attempts and key exchanges in industrial networks
        ~Delete Batch Processing Records: Adversaries may remove evidence from batch processing systems and recipe management platforms
        ~Clear Industrial Firewall Logs: Adversaries may remove logs from industrial firewalls and security appliances specific to OT networks
      Rootkit: Adversaries may deploy rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting and modifying operating-system API calls that supply system information. Rootkits or rootkit-enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system, or lower. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018)     Firmware rootkits that affect the operating system yield nearly full control of the system. While firmware rootkits are normally developed for the main processing board, they can also be developed for the I/O that is attached to an asset. Compromise of this firmware allows the modification of all of the process variables and functions the module engages in. This may result in commands being disregarded and false information being fed to the main device. By tampering with device processes, an adversary may inhibit its expected response functions and possibly enable [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105).
        ~Firmware Rootkit for PLCs: Malicious code embedded in programmable logic controller firmware to hide malicious activities and maintain persistence
        ~Kernel-Level Control System Rootkit: Rootkits that operate at the operating system kernel level of ICS systems to intercept and modify system calls
        ~Driver Manipulation Rootkit: Rootkits that target industrial device drivers to hide malicious activities from monitoring systems
        ~I/O Module Firmware Rootkit: Specialized rootkits targeting I/O modules to manipulate sensor readings and control signals
        ~Communication Protocol Rootkit: Rootkits that intercept and modify industrial protocol communications to hide malicious traffic
        ~HMI Application Rootkit: Rootkits that modify human-machine interface applications to display false information
        ~Historian Database Rootkit: Rootkits that manipulate historical process data storage systems
        ~Engineering Workstation Rootkit: Rootkits specifically designed to compromise engineering workstations used for ICS configuration
        ~Remote Terminal Unit Rootkit: Rootkits targeting RTU firmware to manipulate field device communications
        ~Safety System Rootkit: Rootkits designed to compromise safety instrumented systems while hiding malicious modifications
        ~DCS Controller Rootkit: Rootkits targeting distributed control system controllers to hide malicious modifications
        ~Industrial Network Switch Rootkit: Rootkits that compromise industrial network switches to hide traffic and maintain persistence
        ~OPC Server Rootkit: Rootkits targeting OPC servers to manipulate data exchange between different control systems
        ~Industrial Gateway Rootkit: Rootkits designed for industrial protocol gateways to hide malicious traffic between different networks
        ~Field Device Interface Rootkit: Rootkits targeting field device interfaces to manipulate sensor and actuator communications
        ~Embedded HMI Rootkit: Rootkits specifically designed for embedded HMI panels commonly used in industrial environments
        ~Industrial Firewall Rootkit: Rootkits that compromise industrial firewalls to allow hidden malicious traffic
        ~Time Synchronization Rootkit: Rootkits targeting precision time protocol (PTP) or NTP servers in industrial networks
        ~Redundancy Controller Rootkit: Rootkits targeting redundancy control systems to hide malicious activities during failover scenarios
        ~Industrial Data Diode Rootkit: Rootkits specifically designed to compromise unidirectional security gateways
        ~Asset Management System Rootkit: Rootkits targeting industrial asset management and inventory systems
        ~Condition Monitoring Rootkit: Rootkits that compromise equipment condition monitoring systems to hide system state changes
        ~Industrial Protocol Analyzer Rootkit: Rootkits targeting network monitoring and protocol analysis tools
        ~Backup System Rootkit: Rootkits designed to compromise backup and recovery systems while hiding malicious modifications
        ~Industrial UPS Rootkit: Rootkits targeting uninterruptible power supply systems and their management interfaces
      Masquerading: Adversaries may use masquerading to disguise a malicious application or executable as another file, to avoid operator and engineer suspicion. Possible disguises of these masquerading files can include commonly found programs, expected vendor executables and configuration files, and other commonplace application and naming conventions. By impersonating expected and vendor-relevant files and applications, operators and engineers may not notice the presence of the underlying malicious content and possibly end up running those masquerading as legitimate functions.   Applications and other files commonly found on Windows systems or in engineering workstations have been impersonated before. This can be as simple as renaming a file to effectively disguise it in the ICS environment.
        ~Match Legitimate File Names: Adversaries rename malicious files to match legitimate ICS application names, configuration files, or system utilities commonly found in the environment
        ~Vendor Application Spoofing: Malicious code masquerades as trusted vendor-specific applications, such as PLC programming software or HMI applications
        ~Protocol Implementation Masquerading: Malware disguises itself as legitimate industrial protocol implementations or drivers
        ~Configuration File Masquerading: Malicious content is hidden within what appears to be legitimate control system configuration files
        ~Engineering Workstation Tool Impersonation: Malware poses as common engineering tools used for system configuration and maintenance
        ~System Service Masquerading: Malware masquerades as legitimate ICS system services or daemons
        ~Device Firmware Masquerading: Malicious code disguises itself as legitimate device firmware updates or packages
        ~Backup File Masquerading: Adversaries hide malicious content within what appears to be system backup files
        ~Documentation Masquerading: Malware is disguised as technical documentation files commonly found in ICS environments
        ~Network Protocol Binary Masquerading: Malicious executables pose as industrial network protocol binaries or utilities
        ~Historical Data Masquerading: Malware disguises itself as historical process data files or trending data exports
        ~Maintenance Script Masquerading: Malicious scripts pose as routine maintenance or calibration scripts
        ~Safety System Component Masquerading: Malware masquerades as safety system components or configurations
        ~Remote Access Tool Masquerading: Malicious remote access tools disguise themselves as legitimate vendor remote support tools
        ~Diagnostic Utility Masquerading: Malware poses as system diagnostic or troubleshooting utilities
      Spoof Reporting Message: Adversaries may spoof reporting messages in control system environments for evasion and to impair process control. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. Reporting messages are important for monitoring the normal operation of a system or identifying important events such as deviations from expected values.   If an adversary has the ability to Spoof Reporting Messages, they can impact the control system in many ways. The adversary can Spoof Reporting Messages that state that the process is operating normally, as a form of evasion. The adversary could also Spoof Reporting Messages to make the defenders and operators think that other errors are occurring in order to distract them from the actual source of a problem. (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)
        ~Protocol-Specific Message Spoofing: Adversaries may forge messages using specific industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, DNP3, EtherNet/IP) to send false data while maintaining protocol compliance
        ~Sensor Data Manipulation: Adversaries may specifically target and manipulate sensor readings and measurements to mask real process conditions
        ~Status Update Forgery: Adversaries may create false equipment status updates to hide malfunctions or create false alarms
        ~Historical Data Tampering: Adversaries may modify historical process data and logs to hide evidence of manipulation
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Spoofing: Adversaries may intercept and modify legitimate reporting messages in transit
        ~Replay Attack Spoofing: Adversaries may capture and replay old legitimate messages to mask current conditions
        ~Time Synchronization Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate time stamps in reporting messages to create confusion or hide activities
        ~Alarm System Spoofing: Adversaries may specifically target alarm systems by sending false normal state messages during attacks
      Change Operating Mode: Adversaries may change the operating mode of a controller to gain additional access to engineering functions such as Program Download.   Programmable controllers typically have several modes of operation that control the state of the user program and control access to the controllers API. Operating modes can be physically selected using a key switch on the face of the controller but may also be selected with calls to the controllers API. Operating modes and the mechanisms by which they are selected often vary by vendor and product line. Some commonly implemented operating modes are described below:    * Program - This mode must be enabled before changes can be made to a devices program. This allows program uploads and downloads between the device and an engineering workstation. Often the PLCs logic Is halted, and all outputs may be forced off. (Citation: N.A. October 2017)   * Run - Execution of the devices program occurs in this mode. Input and output (values, points, tags, elements, etc.) are monitored and used according to the programs logic. [Program Upload](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0845) and [Program Download](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843) are disabled while in this mode. (Citation: Omron) (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)  (Citation: N.A. October 2017) (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)    * Remote - Allows for remote changes to a PLCs operation mode. (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)     * Stop - The PLC and program is stopped, while in this mode, outputs are forced off. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Reset - Conditions on the PLC are reset to their original states. Warm resets may retain some memory while cold resets will reset all I/O and data registers. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Test / Monitor mode - Similar to run mode, I/O is processed, although this mode allows for monitoring, force set, resets, and more generally tuning or debugging of the system. Often monitor mode may be used as a trial for initialization. (Citation: Omron)
        ~Physical Key Switch Manipulation: Adversaries may physically manipulate the key switch on controllers to change operating modes, bypassing remote access controls
        ~Remote Mode Change: Adversaries may exploit remote access capabilities to change operating modes without physical access
        ~Unauthorized Mode Transitions: Adversaries may force unauthorized transitions between operating modes to bypass safety interlocks
        ~Memory Retention Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate mode changes to exploit how different modes retain or reset memory
        ~Debug Mode Abuse: Adversaries may abuse test/monitor modes to gather system information and manipulate I/O
        ~Mode Change Sequencing: Adversaries may sequence multiple mode changes to achieve specific effects or bypass protections
        ~Vendor-Specific Mode Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit unique features or vulnerabilities in vendor-specific operating modes
        ~Mode Change During Operation: Adversaries may time mode changes to coincide with critical operations to maximize impact
      System Binary Proxy Execution: Adversaries may bypass process and/or signature-based defenses by proxying execution of malicious content with signed, or otherwise trusted, binaries. Binaries used in this technique are often Microsoft-signed files, indicating that they have been either downloaded from Microsoft or are already native in the operating system. (Citation: LOLBAS Project) Binaries signed with trusted digital certificates can typically execute on Windows systems protected by digital signature validation. Several Microsoft signed binaries that are default on Windows installations can be used to proxy execution of other files or commands. Similarly, on Linux systems adversaries may abuse trusted binaries such as split to proxy execution of malicious commands. (Citation: split man page)(Citation: GTFO split)  Adversaries may abuse application binaries installed on a system for proxy execution of malicious code or domain-specific commands. These commands could be used to target local resources on the device or networked devices within the environment through defined APIs ([Execution through API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0871)) or application-specific programming languages (e.g., MicroSCADA SCIL). Application binaries may be signed by the developer or generally trusted by the operators, analysts, and monitoring tools accustomed to the environment. These applications may be developed and/or directly provided by the device vendor to enable configuration, management, and operation of their devices without many alternatives.   Adversaries may seek to target these trusted application binaries to execute or send commands without the development of custom malware. For example, adversaries may target a SCADA server binary which has the existing ability to send commands to substation devices, such as through IEC 104 command messages. Proxy execution may still require the development of custom tools to hook into the application binary’s execution.
        ~SCADA Application Abuse: Adversaries may abuse legitimate SCADA application binaries to execute malicious commands or scripts, leveraging their built-in functionality and trusted status.
        ~Engineering Workstation Tools: Adversaries may misuse vendor-provided engineering workstation tools and utilities that are signed and trusted within the ICS environment.
        ~Device Configuration Utilities: Adversaries may exploit trusted device configuration and management utilities to proxy the execution of malicious commands.
        ~Protocol Gateway Applications: Adversaries may abuse protocol conversion applications and gateways that are trusted to facilitate communication between different industrial protocols.
        ~Historian Software Components: Adversaries may leverage trusted historian software components to execute unauthorized commands while appearing legitimate.
        ~HMI Application Abuse: Adversaries may exploit Human Machine Interface (HMI) application binaries to proxy execution of malicious commands to field devices.
        ~OPC Server Applications: Adversaries may abuse OPC server applications that are trusted to handle data exchange between different industrial devices and systems.
        ~PLC Programming Software: Adversaries may misuse vendor-specific PLC programming software that has privileged access to industrial devices.
        ~Asset Management Tools: Adversaries may exploit trusted asset management and inventory tools to execute unauthorized commands.
        ~Diagnostic Utility Abuse: Adversaries may leverage trusted diagnostic and troubleshooting utilities to proxy execution of malicious commands.
        ~Firmware Update Utilities: Adversaries may abuse legitimate firmware update utilities that are trusted to modify device software.
        ~Industrial Database Tools: Adversaries may exploit trusted industrial database management tools that have privileged access to process data and configurations.
        ~Remote Access Tools: Adversaries may abuse vendor-provided remote access utilities that are trusted for maintenance and support.
        ~Backup System Components: Adversaries may leverage trusted backup and recovery applications to execute unauthorized commands.
        ~Time Synchronization Services: Adversaries may exploit trusted time synchronization utilities that are essential for ICS operations.
        ~License Management Tools: Adversaries may abuse software license management utilities that are trusted across the ICS environment.
        ~System Health Monitoring Tools: Adversaries may leverage trusted system monitoring and health check utilities to proxy malicious commands.
        ~Safety System Utilities: Adversaries may abuse trusted safety system configuration and management utilities that have privileged access to critical safety functions.
        ~Compliance Monitoring Tools: Adversaries may exploit trusted compliance and audit tools that have broad system access rights.
        ~Industrial Protocol Analyzers: Adversaries may leverage legitimate protocol analysis tools that are trusted for network diagnostics and troubleshooting.
        ~Device Driver Utilities: Adversaries may abuse trusted device driver installation and management utilities specific to industrial hardware.
        ~Change Management Tools: Adversaries may exploit trusted change management and version control systems used in ICS environments.
        ~Calibration Utilities: Adversaries may abuse trusted calibration tools used for industrial sensor and instrument calibration.
        ~Simulation Software: Adversaries may exploit trusted process simulation and modeling tools that have privileged access to system configurations.
        ~Industrial Reporting Tools: Adversaries may leverage trusted reporting and analytics applications that have broad data access.
        ~Equipment Testing Utilities: Adversaries may abuse trusted testing and validation tools used for industrial equipment verification.
        ~Energy Management Systems: Adversaries may exploit trusted energy management system components that have control over power distribution and monitoring.
        ~Documentation Management Systems: Adversaries may abuse trusted documentation and drawing management systems that have access to sensitive system configurations and specifications.
        ~Training Simulator Applications: Adversaries may exploit trusted operator training simulators that mirror production system functionality.
        ~System Integration Tools: Adversaries may leverage trusted system integration utilities used to connect different industrial systems and components.
        ~Industrial Cloud Connectors: Adversaries may abuse trusted cloud connectivity tools designed for ICS-cloud integration.
        ~Batch Management Systems: Adversaries may exploit trusted batch process management applications that control sequential operations.
    discovery: The adversary is locating information to assess and identify their targets in your environment.  Discovery consists of techniques that adversaries use to survey your ICS environment and gain knowledge about the internal network, control system devices, and how their processes interact. These techniques help adversaries observe the environment and determine next steps for target selection and Lateral Movement. They also allow adversaries to explore what they can control and gain insight on interactions between various control system processes. Discovery techniques are often an act of progression into the environment which enable the adversary to orient themselves before deciding how to act. Adversaries may use Discovery techniques that result in Collection, to help determine how available resources benefit their current objective. A combination of native device communications and functions, and custom tools are often used toward this post-compromise information-gathering objective.
      Serial Connection Enumeration: Adversaries may perform serial connection enumeration to gather situational awareness after gaining access to devices in the OT network. Control systems devices often communicate to each other via various types of serial communication mediums. These serial communications are used to facilitate informational communication, as well as commands.  Serial Connection Enumeration differs from I/O Module Discovery, as I/O modules are auxiliary systems to the main system, and devices that are connected via serial connection are normally discrete systems.  While IT and OT networks may work in tandem, the exact structure of the OT network may not be discernible from the IT network alone. After gaining access to a device on the OT network, an adversary may be able to enumerate the serial connections. From this perspective, the adversary can see the specific physical devices to which the compromised device is connected to. This gives the adversary greater situational awareness and can influence the actions that the adversary can take in an attack.
        ~RS-232/485 Port Scanning: Adversaries may scan for RS-232 or RS-485 serial ports to identify potential communication channels between ICS devices
        ~Modbus Serial Discovery: Adversaries may specifically target Modbus serial connections to enumerate slave devices and their functions
        ~Protocol-Specific Enumeration: Adversaries may use protocol-specific tools to identify and enumerate devices using protocols like DNP3, Profibus, or DeviceNet over serial connections
        ~Physical Port Mapping: Adversaries may physically inspect and map out serial port connections on compromised devices
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Adversaries may analyze device configuration files to identify configured serial connections and parameters
        ~Serial Traffic Monitoring: Adversaries may passively monitor serial traffic to identify active connections and device relationships
        ~Serial Port Parameter Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to determine serial port parameters like baud rate, parity, and stop bits
        ~Legacy Protocol Discovery: Adversaries may specifically target legacy serial protocols used in older ICS equipment
      Remote System Discovery: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of other systems by IP address, hostname, or other logical identifier on a network that may be used for subsequent Lateral Movement or Discovery techniques. Functionality could exist within adversary tools to enable this, but utilities available on the operating system or vendor software could also be used. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018)
        ~Network Service Scanning: Using industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, S7comm) to identify control system devices and their services
        ~Active Controller Discovery: Probing programmable logic controllers (PLCs) and remote terminal units (RTUs) for identification
        ~HMI System Enumeration: Identifying human-machine interface systems and their connections
        ~Engineering Workstation Discovery: Locating engineering workstations used for ICS configuration and programming
        ~Historian Server Detection: Identifying historian servers that collect and store process data
        ~Protocol-Specific Discovery: Using industrial protocol-specific commands to enumerate devices
        ~Asset Management System Query: Querying asset management systems for device information
        ~Control Network Mapping: Creating maps of control network topology and relationships
        ~Field Device Discovery: Identifying field devices like sensors and actuators
        ~OPC Server Discovery: Locating OPC servers that facilitate data exchange between industrial devices
        ~DCS Component Discovery: Identifying distributed control system components and their relationships
        ~Safety System Enumeration: Discovering safety instrumented systems (SIS) and emergency shutdown systems
        ~SCADA Terminal Discovery: Locating SCADA terminals and remote access points
        ~Fieldbus Network Discovery: Identifying devices on fieldbus networks (Foundation Fieldbus, Profibus)
        ~Building Management System Discovery: Locating building automation and control systems
        ~Telemetry System Discovery: Identifying remote telemetry units and SCADA telemetry systems
        ~Industrial Gateway Discovery: Locating protocol converters and industrial gateway devices
        ~Vendor-Specific Tool Discovery: Using vendor-specific tools and protocols for system enumeration
        ~Process Instrumentation Discovery: Identifying smart instruments and process measurement devices
        ~Backup System Discovery: Locating backup and redundant control systems
        ~Batch System Discovery: Identifying batch processing systems and recipe management controllers
        ~Motion Control System Discovery: Locating servo drives, motion controllers, and CNC systems
        ~Power Management Discovery: Identifying power distribution units and energy management systems
        ~Industrial Wireless Discovery: Detecting wireless sensors, actuators, and industrial wireless networks
        ~Time Synchronization System Discovery: Locating precision time protocol (PTP) servers and sync devices
        ~Environmental Control Discovery: Identifying HVAC and environmental monitoring systems
        ~Material Handling System Discovery: Locating automated storage, conveyor, and robotics systems
        ~Industrial IoT Gateway Discovery: Identifying IIoT edge devices and gateways
        ~Process Analyzer Discovery: Locating online analyzers and quality control systems
        ~Redundancy Control Discovery: Identifying hot-standby and failover control systems
        ~Digital Twin System Discovery: Identifying digital twin platforms and their connected physical assets
        ~Manufacturing Execution System Discovery: Locating MES components and interfaces
        ~Industrial Cloud Gateway Discovery: Identifying cloud-connected industrial gateways and edge devices
        ~Production Planning System Discovery: Locating production scheduling and resource planning systems
        ~Laboratory Information System Discovery: Identifying LIMS and quality management systems
        ~Condition Monitoring System Discovery: Locating predictive maintenance and asset health monitoring systems
        ~Industrial Network Storage Discovery: Identifying industrial data historians and storage systems
        ~Process Optimization System Discovery: Locating advanced process control and optimization platforms
        ~Industrial Mobile Device Discovery: Identifying mobile HMIs and maintenance terminals
        ~Supply Chain System Discovery: Locating logistics and inventory management systems integrated with ICS
        ~Legacy Protocol System Discovery: Identifying systems using obsolete or proprietary industrial protocols
        ~Packaging System Discovery: Locating automated packaging and labeling control systems
        ~Industrial Database Discovery: Identifying specialized industrial databases and data lakes
        ~Custom Protocol Discovery: Detecting systems using organization-specific protocols
        ~Machine Vision System Discovery: Locating quality inspection and machine vision controllers
        ~Industrial Firewall Discovery: Identifying industrial firewalls and protocol filters
        ~Remote Maintenance System Discovery: Locating vendor remote access and maintenance systems
        ~Industrial VPN Discovery: Identifying VPN concentrators and secure remote access systems
        ~Sequence Control Discovery: Locating sequential control systems and state machines
        ~Regulatory Compliance System Discovery: Identifying systems handling regulatory compliance and reporting
      Control Device Identification: Adversaries may perform control device identification to determine the make and model of a target device. Management software and device APIs may be utilized by the adversary to gain this information. By identifying and obtaining device specifics, the adversary may be able to determine device vulnerabilities. This device information can also be used to understand device functionality and inform the decision to target the environment.
        ~Protocol Scanning: Use of industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, S7comm, EtherNet/IP) to query device information and identify device types
        ~Management Software Queries: Utilizing vendor-specific management tools to gather device information
        ~Network Service Enumeration: Scanning for device-specific ports and services to identify control system components
        ~Physical Label Collection: Gathering information from physical device labels, nameplates, and markings
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Examining configuration files and backups to identify device types and models
        ~Vendor Protocol Analysis: Using vendor-specific protocols to identify device characteristics
        ~Device API Interrogation: Direct querying of device APIs to gather device information
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Passive monitoring of control system traffic to identify device types
        ~Web Interface Fingerprinting: Identifying devices through their web management interfaces
        ~Serial Communication Analysis: Using serial connections to identify device characteristics
        ~Firmware Version Querying: Extracting device information through firmware version requests and responses
        ~Device Discovery Broadcasting: Using broadcast messages and protocols specific to industrial environments
        ~Asset Management System Access: Leveraging existing asset management and inventory systems
        ~Engineering Workstation Analysis: Examining engineering workstations and their project files
        ~OPC Server Enumeration: Querying OPC servers to identify connected devices and their characteristics
        ~PLC Project File Analysis: Examining PLC project files and backups for device information
        ~Device Documentation Collection: Gathering information from technical documentation and manuals
        ~SNMP Polling: Using SNMP queries to gather device information
        ~Registry and Installation Analysis: Examining software installations and registry entries for device information
        ~Vendor Support Tool Analysis: Using vendor diagnostic and support tools
        ~Remote Access Gateway Enumeration: Identifying devices through remote access and maintenance connections
        ~Historian Database Analysis: Querying historian databases to identify connected control devices
        ~BACnet Device Discovery: Using building automation protocols to identify control systems
        ~Safety System Enumeration: Identifying safety instrumented systems and their components
        ~HMI Project Analysis: Examining HMI projects and runtime files for device information
        ~DCS Configuration Analysis: Analyzing distributed control system configurations
        ~Time Sync Protocol Analysis: Using time synchronization protocols to identify network devices
        ~Field Device Configuration Tool Analysis: Examining field device configuration tools and their data
        ~Backup Server Enumeration: Analyzing backup systems for device configuration information
        ~Device Certificate Analysis: Examining digital certificates used by control devices
      Remote System Information Discovery: An adversary may attempt to get detailed information about remote systems and their peripherals, such as make/model, role, and configuration. Adversaries may use information from Remote System Information Discovery to aid in targeting and shaping follow-on behaviors. For example, the system's operational role and model information can dictate whether it is a relevant target for the adversary's operational objectives. In addition, the system's configuration may be used to scope subsequent technique usage.   Requests for system information are typically implemented using automation and management protocols and are often automatically requested by vendor software during normal operation. This information may be used to tailor management actions, such as program download and system or module firmware. An adversary may leverage this same information by issuing calls directly to the system's API.
        ~Protocol-Specific Scanning: Using industrial protocols like Modbus, S7comm, or EtherNet/IP to query device information and capabilities
        ~Engineering Software Queries: Utilizing vendor-specific engineering software to discover system information and configurations
        ~Management Protocol Abuse: Exploiting management protocols like SNMP, HTTP/HTTPS, or Telnet to gather system details
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Extracting system information from configuration files and backups
        ~Asset Management System Query: Accessing asset management or inventory systems to gather device information
        ~OPC Server Enumeration: Querying OPC servers to discover available tags, devices, and system hierarchy
        ~Device Discovery Broadcasting: Using broadcast messages to identify and enumerate industrial devices on the network
        ~Firmware Version Polling: Specifically querying devices for firmware versions and update status
        ~Component Capability Discovery: Gathering information about specific capabilities and features of system components
        ~Historical Data Mining: Analyzing historical data and logs to understand system configuration and components
        ~Vendor Documentation Analysis: Gathering system information by analyzing publicly available vendor documentation, manuals, and support materials
        ~Physical Label Reconnaissance: Collecting information from physical labels, displays, and HMI screens showing system details
        ~Device Profile Enumeration: Querying devices for their electronic device profiles or EDS files
        ~Real-time Data Monitoring: Observing real-time process data to infer system components and configurations
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Passively monitoring network traffic to identify system characteristics and relationships
        ~Remote Diagnostic Tool Usage: Utilizing built-in diagnostic and maintenance tools to gather system information
        ~Time Synchronization Query: Exploiting time synchronization protocols to discover system components and relationships
        ~Backup Server Enumeration: Identifying and querying backup servers for system configuration information
        ~Safety System Interrogation: Specifically querying safety instrumented systems (SIS) and safety PLCs for configuration and status
        ~Remote Access Infrastructure Discovery: Identifying and gathering information about remote access servers, gateways, and VPN concentrators
        ~Third-party Integration Mapping: Discovering system information through third-party system integration points and interfaces
        ~Supply Chain System Query: Gathering information from supply chain management systems that interface with ICS
        ~Redundancy Configuration Discovery: Specifically querying redundant systems and failover configurations
        ~Historian Server Enumeration: Identifying and querying historian servers for system architecture information
      Network Service Scanning: Network Service Scanning is the process of discovering services on networked systems.  This can be achieved through a technique called port scanning or probing.  Port scanning interacts with the TCP/IP ports on a target system to determine whether ports are open, closed, or filtered by a firewall.  This does not reveal the service that is running behind the port, but since many common services are run on [https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers/service-names-port-numbers.xhtml specific port numbers], the type of service can be assumed.  More in-depth testing includes interaction with the actual service to determine the service type and specific version.  One of the most-popular tools to use for Network Service Scanning is [https://nmap.org/ Nmap].  An adversary may attempt to gain information about a target device and its role on the network via Network Service Scanning techniques, such as port scanning.  Network Service Scanning is useful for determining potential vulnerabilities in services on target devices.  Network Service Scanning is closely tied to .  Scanning ports can be noisy on a network. In some attacks, adversaries probe for specific ports using custom tools. This was specifically seen in the Triton and PLC-Blaster attacks.
        ~Common Port Scanning: Scanning commonly used ICS protocol ports (e.g., Modbus TCP 502, S7comm 102)
        ~Protocol-Specific Probing: Detailed inspection of ICS-specific protocols to identify device types and versions
        ~Service Banner Grabbing: Collecting service banners and response data to identify ICS equipment and versions
        ~Passive Network Monitoring: Observing network traffic to identify services without active probing
        ~Sequential Port Enumeration: Systematically scanning all ports on ICS devices to discover services
        ~Device-Specific Scanning: Targeted scanning of known ICS device types (PLCs, RTUs, HMIs)
        ~Broadcast Protocol Discovery: Using broadcast messages to identify responsive ICS services
        ~Vendor-Specific Service Detection: Scanning for vendor-specific services and management interfaces
      Network Connection Enumeration: Adversaries may perform network connection enumeration to discover information about device communication patterns. If an adversary can inspect the state of a network connection with tools, such as Netstat(Citation: Netstat), in conjunction with [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0857), then they can determine the role of certain devices on the network  (Citation: MITRE). The adversary can also use [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0842) to watch network traffic for details about the source, destination, protocol, and content.
        ~Protocol-Specific Connection Analysis: Adversaries may analyze ICS-specific protocols (like Modbus, DNP3, EtherNet/IP) to understand device relationships and roles
        ~Connection State Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor the state of connections (established, listening, closed) to understand communication patterns
        ~Port Scanning Analysis: Adversaries may enumerate open ports and services to identify ICS components and their functions
        ~Traffic Flow Mapping: Adversaries may map traffic flows between devices to understand operational relationships
        ~Service Discovery Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate services running on ICS devices to identify their roles and capabilities
        ~Temporal Pattern Analysis: Adversaries may analyze connection patterns over time to identify periodic communications and operational cycles
        ~Network Topology Mapping: Adversaries may use connection information to map the physical and logical topology of the ICS network
        ~Device Role Classification: Adversaries may analyze connection patterns to classify devices as controllers, sensors, actuators, or other ICS components
      Wireless Sniffing: Adversaries may seek to capture radio frequency (RF) communication used for remote control and reporting in distributed environments. RF communication frequencies vary between 3 kHz to 300 GHz, although are commonly between 300 MHz to 6 GHz. (Citation: Candell, R., Hany, M., Lee, K. B., Liu,Y., Quimby, J., Remley, K. April 2018)  The wavelength and frequency of the signal affect how the signal propagates through open air, obstacles (e.g. walls and trees) and the type of radio required to capture them. These characteristics are often standardized in the protocol and hardware and may have an effect on how the signal is captured. Some examples of wireless protocols that may be found in cyber-physical environments are: WirelessHART, Zigbee, WIA-FA, and 700 MHz Public Safety Spectrum.   Adversaries may capture RF communications by using specialized hardware, such as software defined radio (SDR), handheld radio, or a computer with radio demodulator tuned to the communication frequency. (Citation: Bastille April 2017) Information transmitted over a wireless medium may be captured in-transit whether the sniffing device is the intended destination or not. This technique may be particularly useful to an adversary when the communications are not encrypted. (Citation: Gallagher, S. April 2017)   In the 2017 Dallas Siren incident, it is suspected that adversaries likely captured wireless command message broadcasts on a 700 MHz frequency during a regular test of the system. These messages were later replayed to trigger the alarm systems. (Citation: Gallagher, S. April 2017)
        ~WirelessHART Protocol Sniffing: Targeting industrial wireless sensor networks using WirelessHART protocol (2.4 GHz) to capture process control and monitoring data
        ~ZigBee Protocol Interception: Capturing ZigBee-based automation and control signals commonly used in industrial environments
        ~Public Safety Band Monitoring: Targeting emergency and safety system communications in the 700 MHz band
        ~Industrial WiFi Capture: Intercepting industrial WiFi communications used for process control and monitoring
        ~Bluetooth Protocol Sniffing: Targeting Bluetooth-based industrial sensors and control devices
        ~Mobile Radio Communication Capture: Intercepting mobile radio communications used by maintenance and operations staff
        ~Passive RF Energy Analysis: Monitoring RF energy emissions to identify operational patterns without active interception
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Signal Capture: Targeting wireless communications between RTUs and control centers
        ~Sensor Network Traffic Analysis: Capturing wireless sensor network traffic to understand process parameters and control flows
        ~Emergency System Signal Monitoring: Targeting emergency broadcast and alert system wireless communications
        ~ISA100.11a Protocol Capture: Targeting wireless industrial automation protocol communications used in process control
        ~Time-Synchronized Channel Hopping Interception: Capturing communications that use frequency hopping for enhanced reliability
        ~Modbus Wireless Monitoring: Targeting wireless Modbus protocol implementations in industrial networks
        ~Low-Power Wide-Area Network (LPWAN) Sniffing: Intercepting long-range, low-power industrial communications
        ~Process Control Radio Capture: Targeting proprietary radio protocols used in specific industrial control systems
        ~Near-Field Communication (NFC) Sniffing: Capturing short-range wireless communications used for maintenance and configuration
        ~Ultra-Wideband Signal Capture: Monitoring ultra-wideband communications used in precision location and sensing applications
        ~Industrial IoT Protocol Interception: Targeting wireless protocols specific to IIoT devices and networks
      Network Sniffing: Network sniffing is the practice of using a network interface on a computer system to monitor or capture information (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018) regardless of whether it is the specified destination for the information.   An adversary may attempt to sniff the traffic to gain information about the target. This information can vary in the level of importance. Relatively unimportant information is general communications to and from machines.  Relatively important information would be login information. User credentials may be sent over an unencrypted protocol, such as Telnet, that can be captured and obtained through network packet analysis.   In addition, ARP and Domain Name Service (DNS) poisoning can be used to capture credentials to websites, proxies, and internal systems by redirecting traffic to an adversary.
        ~Control System Protocol Sniffing: Capturing industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, EtherNet/IP, and PROFINET to understand control system communications and operations
        ~Engineering Workstation Traffic Monitoring: Intercepting traffic between engineering workstations and PLCs/RTUs during programming and configuration activities
        ~Operator Console Monitoring: Capturing HMI/SCADA traffic to understand operator actions and system state information
        ~Field Device Communication Sniffing: Monitoring communications between field devices like sensors, actuators, and controllers
        ~Maintenance Network Monitoring: Capturing traffic during maintenance activities when temporary connections are established
        ~Remote Access Channel Sniffing: Monitoring VPN and remote access connections used for vendor support and remote operations
        ~Safety System Communication Monitoring: Capturing traffic related to safety instrumented systems (SIS) and emergency shutdown systems
        ~Process Network Boundary Sniffing: Monitoring traffic at the boundaries between IT and OT networks, including demilitarized zones (DMZ)
      I/O Module Discovery: Adversaries may use input/output (I/O) module discovery to gather key information about a control system device. An I/O module is a device that allows the control system device to either receive or send signals to other devices. These signals can be analog or digital, and may support a number of different protocols. Devices are often able to use attachable I/O modules to increase the number of inputs and outputs that it can utilize. An adversary with access to a device can use native device functions to enumerate I/O modules that are connected to the device. Information regarding the I/O modules can aid the adversary in understanding related control processes.
        ~Protocol-Based Discovery: Using industrial protocols like Modbus, Profinet, or EtherNet/IP to query and identify I/O modules and their configurations
        ~Engineering Software Queries: Utilizing vendor-specific engineering software tools to enumerate I/O module configurations and settings
        ~Device Configuration File Analysis: Examining configuration files and backups to identify I/O module information
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Passively monitoring industrial network traffic to identify I/O module communications
        ~Web Interface Enumeration: Using device web interfaces to discover I/O module configurations
        ~Native Function Calls: Directly querying device APIs and native functions to enumerate I/O modules
        ~Physical Interface Scanning: Direct physical inspection or scanning of I/O module interfaces and connections
        ~Memory Analysis: Reading device memory locations containing I/O module configuration data
        ~Diagnostic Tool Usage: Leveraging built-in diagnostic and maintenance tools to gather I/O module information
        ~Runtime State Analysis: Monitoring runtime state information to identify active I/O modules and their configurations
    lateral-movement: The adversary is trying to move through your ICS environment.  Lateral Movement consists of techniques that adversaries use to enter and control remote systems on a network. These techniques abuse default credentials, known accounts, and vulnerable services, and may also leverage dual-homed devices and systems that reside on both the IT and OT networks. The adversary uses these techniques to pivot to their next point in the environment, positioning themselves to where they want to be or think they should be. Following through on their primary objective often requires [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0102) of the network and [Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0100) to develop awareness of unique ICS devices and processes, in order to find their target and subsequently gain access to it. Reaching this objective often involves pivoting through multiple systems, devices, and accounts. Adversaries may install their own remote tools to accomplish Lateral Movement or leverage default tools, programs, and manufacturer set or other legitimate credentials native to the network, which may be stealthier.
      Program Download: Adversaries may perform a program download to transfer a user program to a controller.   Variations of program download, such as online edit and program append, allow a controller to continue running during the transfer and reconfiguration process without interruption to process control. However, before starting a full program download (i.e., download all) a controller may need to go into a stop state. This can have negative consequences on the physical process, especially if the controller is not able to fulfill a time-sensitive action. Adversaries may choose to avoid a download all in favor of an online edit or program append to avoid disrupting the physical process. An adversary may need to use the technique Detect Operating Mode or Change Operating Mode to make sure the controller is in the proper mode to accept a program download.  The granularity of control to transfer a user program in whole or parts is dictated by the management protocol (e.g., S7CommPlus, TriStation) and underlying controller API. Thus, program download is a high-level term for the suite of vendor-specific API calls used to configure a controllers user program memory space.    [Modify Controller Tasking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0821) and [Modify Program](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0889) represent the configuration changes that are transferred to a controller via a program download.
        ~Full Program Download: Complete replacement of controller program requiring stop state
        ~Online Edit: Partial program modifications while controller remains running
        ~Program Append: Addition of new program segments without full replacement
        ~Protocol-Specific Transfer: Using specific industrial protocols (S7CommPlus, TriStation) for program transfer
        ~Staged Download: Breaking down program transfer into smaller segments to avoid detection
        ~Memory Block Transfer: Direct manipulation of controller memory blocks
        ~Firmware Update Channel: Abuse of firmware update mechanisms for program download
        ~Remote Access Service: Utilizing remote access services to perform program downloads
        ~Engineering Workstation Impersonation: Masquerading as legitimate engineering station
        ~Backup Channel Download: Using backup/restore functionality for program transfer
        ~Recovery Mode Download: Exploiting controller recovery/maintenance modes
        ~Time-Synchronized Download: Coordinating downloads across multiple controllers
        ~Vendor Debug Interface: Using vendor-specific debug ports/interfaces
        ~External Media Transfer: Using removable media interfaces for program loading
        ~Bootstrap Mode Download: Exploiting controller boot process
        ~Cross-Controller Transfer: Using intermediary controller as download proxy
        ~Legacy Protocol Download: Exploiting older/deprecated programming interfaces
        ~Simulation Mode Transfer: Using simulation/test modes for program transfer
      Exploitation of Remote Services: Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to enable remote service abuse. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for initial access into and lateral movement throughout the ICS environment to enable access to targeted systems. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  ICS asset owners and operators have been affected by ransomware (or disruptive malware masquerading as ransomware) migrating from enterprise IT to ICS environments: WannaCry, NotPetya, and BadRabbit. In each of these cases, self-propagating (wormable) malware initially infected IT networks, but through exploit (particularly the SMBv1-targeting MS17-010 vulnerability) spread to industrial networks, producing significant impacts. (Citation: Joe Slowik April 2019)
        ~OPC Classic Vulnerabilities: Exploitation of vulnerabilities in OPC Classic protocols and services commonly used in ICS environments for data exchange and communication
        ~HMI Remote Access Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in Human Machine Interface remote access capabilities and services
        ~Engineering Workstation Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in engineering workstation software and associated remote services
        ~PLC Programming Software Vulnerabilities: Targeting remote access capabilities and vulnerabilities in PLC programming and configuration software
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in RTU communication services and protocols
        ~Historian Database Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in historian database remote access and query services
        ~Protocol Gateway Vulnerabilities: Exploiting vulnerabilities in protocol gateways that bridge different industrial protocols or IT/OT networks
        ~Remote Diagnostic Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in vendor-provided remote diagnostic and maintenance services
        ~SCADA Server Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in SCADA server remote access and control capabilities
        ~Industrial Protocol Vulnerabilities: Targeting implementation flaws in industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or EtherNet/IP
        ~Remote Asset Management System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in centralized asset management and monitoring systems used in ICS environments
        ~Safety System Remote Access Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in safety instrumented systems (SIS) remote access capabilities
        ~Industrial Firewall Management Interface Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in industrial firewall remote management interfaces
        ~Remote Firmware Update Service Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in remote firmware update mechanisms for ICS devices
        ~Building Management System Service Exploitation: Targeting remote access vulnerabilities in building automation and management systems
        ~Remote Telemetry Unit Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in telemetry systems used for remote monitoring and data collection
        ~Industrial IoT Gateway Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in IIoT gateways that connect industrial devices to cloud platforms
        ~Energy Management System Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in energy management system remote interfaces
        ~Industrial Cloud Connector Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in cloud connectivity modules and services designed for ICS
        ~Process Optimization Service Exploitation: Targeting vulnerabilities in remote services used for process optimization and advanced control
      Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage remote services to move between assets and network segments. These services are often used to allow operators to interact with systems remotely within the network, some examples are RDP, SMB, SSH, and other similar mechanisms. (Citation: Blake Johnson, Dan Caban, Marina Krotofil, Dan Scali, Nathan Brubaker, Christopher Glyer December 2017) (Citation: Dragos December 2017) (Citation: Joe Slowik April 2019)   Remote services could be used to support remote access, data transmission, authentication, name resolution, and other remote functions. Further, remote services may be necessary to allow operators and administrators to configure systems within the network from their engineering or management workstations. An adversary may use this technique to access devices which may be dual-homed (Citation: Blake Johnson, Dan Caban, Marina Krotofil, Dan Scali, Nathan Brubaker, Christopher Glyer December 2017) to multiple network segments, and can be used for [Program Download](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843) or to execute attacks on control devices directly through [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0859).  Specific remote services (RDP & VNC) may be a precursor to enable [Graphical User Interface](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0823) execution on devices such as HMIs or engineering workstation software.  Based on incident data, CISA and FBI assessed that Chinese state-sponsored actors also compromised various authorized remote access channels, including systems designed to transfer data and/or allow access between corporate and ICS networks.  (Citation: CISA AA21-201A Pipeline Intrusion July 2021)
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP): Adversaries may use RDP to remotely access and control HMIs, engineering workstations, and other ICS components that support graphical interfaces
        ~Virtual Network Computing (VNC): Adversaries may leverage VNC services for remote visualization and control of ICS systems, particularly for accessing operator interfaces
        ~Secure Shell (SSH): Adversaries may utilize SSH connections for remote command-line access to ICS devices, particularly in Linux-based control systems
        ~Vendor Remote Access Tools: Adversaries may exploit vendor-specific remote access solutions designed for maintenance and support of ICS equipment
        ~OPC UA Remote Services: Adversaries may leverage OPC UA remote services for moving between different components of industrial automation systems
        ~Web-based Remote Access: Adversaries may exploit web-based remote access interfaces commonly used in modern ICS/SCADA systems
        ~Remote Engineering Software: Adversaries may utilize vendor engineering software's remote access capabilities to connect to PLCs and other control devices
        ~Remote Diagnostic Services: Adversaries may exploit remote diagnostic and maintenance services built into ICS equipment
      Default Credentials: Adversaries may leverage manufacturer or supplier set default credentials on control system devices. These default credentials may have administrative permissions and may be necessary for initial configuration of the device. It is general best practice to change the passwords for these accounts as soon as possible, but some manufacturers may have devices that have passwords or usernames that cannot be changed. (Citation: Keith Stouffer May 2015)  Default credentials are normally documented in an instruction manual that is either packaged with the device, published online through official means, or published online through unofficial means. Adversaries may leverage default credentials that have not been properly modified or disabled.
        ~Vendor Documentation Credentials: Exploitation of credentials published in vendor manuals, datasheets, or official documentation
        ~Factory Reset Credentials: Use of default credentials that appear after device factory reset
        ~Hardcoded Credentials: Exploitation of unchangeable credentials embedded in device firmware
        ~Service Account Credentials: Default credentials for built-in service or maintenance accounts
        ~Web Interface Credentials: Default login credentials for device web management interfaces
        ~Protocol-Specific Credentials: Default credentials used in industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or BACnet
        ~Engineering Workstation Credentials: Default credentials for engineering software and configuration tools
        ~Backup System Credentials: Default credentials in backup or redundant systems
        ~Remote Access Credentials: Default credentials in remote access or VPN systems
        ~Default PLC Credentials: Standard credentials used across programmable logic controller product lines
        ~HMI Default Credentials: Standard credentials used in Human Machine Interface systems
        ~Historian Database Credentials: Default access credentials for industrial historian databases
        ~OEM Component Credentials: Default credentials in Original Equipment Manufacturer components
        ~Network Device Credentials: Default credentials in industrial network switches, routers, and gateways
        ~Third-Party Integration Credentials: Default credentials used in third-party system integrations
        ~Temporary Commissioning Credentials: Default credentials used during system commissioning
        ~Emergency Access Credentials: Built-in emergency or backdoor access credentials
        ~Diagnostic Tool Credentials: Default credentials in diagnostic and monitoring tools
        ~SCADA Software Credentials: Default credentials in SCADA application suites
        ~Firmware Update Credentials: Default credentials used for firmware update processes
        ~DCS Default Credentials: Default credentials in Distributed Control System components
        ~RTU Access Credentials: Default credentials in Remote Terminal Units
        ~Building Automation Credentials: Default credentials in building management and automation systems
        ~Safety System Credentials: Default credentials in safety instrumented systems (SIS)
        ~Legacy System Credentials: Default credentials in obsolete or unsupported legacy systems
        ~Telemetry System Credentials: Default credentials in industrial telemetry and monitoring systems
        ~Industrial IoT Credentials: Default credentials in IIoT devices and gateways
        ~Batch Management Credentials: Default credentials in batch process management systems
        ~Field Device Credentials: Default credentials in field instruments and sensors
        ~Backup Controller Credentials: Default credentials in redundant or backup controllers
      Hardcoded Credentials: Adversaries may leverage credentials that are hardcoded in software or firmware to gain an unauthorized interactive user session to an asset. Examples credentials that may be hardcoded in an asset include:  * Username/Passwords * Cryptographic keys/Certificates * API tokens  Unlike [Default Credentials](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0812), these credentials are built into the system in a way that they either cannot be changed by the asset owner, or may be infeasible to change because of the impact it would cause to the control system operation. These credentials may be reused across whole product lines or device models and are often not published or known to the owner and operators of the asset.   Adversaries may utilize these hardcoded credentials to move throughout the control system environment or provide reliable access for their tools to interact with industrial assets.
        ~Firmware Authentication Keys: Exploitation of hardcoded cryptographic keys embedded in device firmware for authentication or secure communication
        ~Engineering Software Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in engineering workstation software or configuration tools
        ~Protocol Authentication Tokens: Exploitation of hardcoded authentication tokens used in industrial protocols
        ~Backup/Recovery Credentials: Access using hardcoded emergency access or recovery credentials
        ~Service Account Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded maintenance or service account credentials
        ~Inter-device Authentication: Use of hardcoded credentials used for communication between ICS devices
        ~Debug Interface Access: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in debug or diagnostic interfaces
        ~Remote Access Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in remote access/support functionality
        ~Configuration File Credentials: Exploitation of credentials embedded in device configuration files
        ~Web Interface Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in device web management interfaces
        ~OEM Development Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials left from device development/testing phases
        ~Third-party Component Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in third-party libraries or components
        ~Legacy Protocol Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in legacy industrial protocols
        ~Embedded Database Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials for accessing embedded databases
        ~Bootloader Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in device bootloader or initialization sequences
        ~Hardware Security Module Access: Use of hardcoded credentials for accessing HSM or security modules
        ~Update System Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in firmware/software update mechanisms
        ~Safety System Bypass Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in safety instrumented systems
        ~Network Equipment Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in industrial network infrastructure
        ~Cross-vendor Gateway Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in protocol conversion gateways
        ~Historian Access Credentials: Use of hardcoded credentials in data historian systems
        ~Redundancy System Credentials: Exploitation of hardcoded credentials in failover/redundancy mechanisms
      Valid Accounts: Adversaries may steal the credentials of a specific user or service account using credential access techniques. In some cases, default credentials for control system devices may be publicly available. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on hosts and within the network, and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems. Compromised and default credentials may also grant an adversary increased privilege to specific systems and devices or access to restricted areas of the network. Adversaries may choose not to use malware or tools, in conjunction with the legitimate access those credentials provide, to make it harder to detect their presence or to control devices and send legitimate commands in an unintended way.   Adversaries may also create accounts, sometimes using predefined account names and passwords, to provide a means of backup access for persistence. (Citation: Booz Allen Hamilton)   The overlap of credentials and permissions across a network of systems is of concern because the adversary may be able to pivot across accounts and systems to reach a high level of access (i.e., domain or enterprise administrator)  and possibly between the enterprise and operational technology environments. Adversaries may be able to leverage valid credentials from one system to gain access to another system.
        ~Default Vendor Accounts: Exploitation of default credentials provided by ICS vendors that were never changed
        ~Local Accounts: Use of compromised local system or device accounts
        ~Domain Accounts: Leveraging compromised enterprise domain accounts for ICS access
        ~Service Accounts: Abuse of automated service or process accounts with elevated privileges
        ~Remote Access Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for remote maintenance and support
        ~Operator Accounts: Use of compromised operator-level accounts with control system access
        ~Engineering Workstation Accounts: Abuse of accounts with access to engineering workstations
        ~Shared Accounts: Exploitation of shared accounts used across multiple systems or operators
        ~Emergency Access Accounts: Abuse of emergency/break-glass accounts with elevated privileges
        ~Cross-boundary Accounts: Use of accounts with access to both IT and OT environments
        ~Third-party Integration Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used for integration with third-party systems or vendors
        ~Historian Accounts: Abuse of accounts with access to historical data systems
        ~Temporary Maintenance Accounts: Use of contractor or temporary maintenance account credentials
        ~Control System Application Accounts: Exploitation of accounts specific to control system applications
        ~Backup System Accounts: Abuse of accounts used for backup and recovery systems
        ~Safety System Accounts: Exploitation of accounts with access to safety instrumented systems (SIS)
        ~Configuration Management Accounts: Abuse of accounts used for ICS configuration and change management
        ~Monitoring System Accounts: Use of accounts designated for system monitoring and alerting
        ~Asset Management Accounts: Exploitation of accounts used in industrial asset management systems
      Program Organization Units: Program Organizational Units (POUs) are block structures used within PLC programming to create programs and projects. (Citation: Guidance - IEC61131) POUs can be used to hold user programs written in IEC 61131-3 languages: Structured text, Instruction list, Function block, and Ladder logic. (Citation: Guidance - IEC61131) Application - 201203 They can also provide additional functionality, such as establishing connections between the PLC and other devices using TCON. (Citation: PLCBlaster - Spenneberg)    Stuxnet uses a simple code-prepending infection technique to infect Organization Blocks (OB). For example, the following sequence of actions is performed when OB1 is infected  (Citation: Stuxnet - Symantec - 201102): *Increase the size of the original block. *Write malicious code to the beginning of the block. *Insert the original OB1 code after the malicious code.
        ~Organization Block Manipulation: Modifying OBs like OB1 (main program block) to inject malicious code while preserving original functionality
        ~Function Block Infection: Targeting Function Blocks to insert malicious logic that can affect multiple PLCs using the same function
        ~Communication Block Abuse: Exploiting TCON and other communication blocks to establish unauthorized connections
        ~Ladder Logic Injection: Inserting malicious ladder logic code into existing POU structures
        ~Structured Text Modification: Altering structured text programs within POUs to implement unauthorized functionality
        ~Cross-PLC Propagation: Using shared or common POUs to spread across multiple PLCs
        ~POU Library Compromise: Infecting commonly used POU libraries to affect multiple systems
        ~Runtime Block Manipulation: Modifying POU behavior during runtime without changing stored programs
      Lateral Tool Transfer: Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from one system to another to stage adversary tools or other files over the course of an operation. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK) Copying of files may also be performed laterally between internal victim systems to support Lateral Movement with remote Execution using inherent file sharing protocols such as file sharing over SMB to connected network shares. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  In control systems environments, malware may use SMB and other file sharing protocols to move laterally through industrial networks.
        ~SMB Protocol Transfer: Using Server Message Block protocol to transfer files between networked industrial systems
        ~USB Drive Transfer: Moving tools via removable media between air-gapped systems
        ~Engineering Workstation File Transfer: Utilizing engineering software's built-in file transfer capabilities
        ~OPC Protocol Transfer: Leveraging OPC interfaces for file movement between systems
        ~FTP/TFTP Transfer: Using standard file transfer protocols common in industrial networks
        ~Historian Database Transfer: Moving files through historian database connections
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol Transfer: Transferring files through RDP sessions
        ~Network Share Mounting: Mounting network shares to transfer files between systems
        ~SCADA Protocol Transfer: Using industrial protocol capabilities for file transfer
        ~Web Interface Transfer: Using web-based interfaces of ICS components for file transfer
        ~PLC Program Transfer: Using PLC programming software's transfer capabilities to move files
        ~DCS Configuration Transfer: Leveraging distributed control system configuration tools
        ~Backup System Transfer: Using backup and recovery systems to move files
        ~Remote Maintenance Tool Transfer: Utilizing vendor remote access and maintenance tools
        ~Serial Protocol Transfer: Using serial communications (Modbus, DNP3) for file transfer
        ~HMI Project Transfer: Moving files through HMI project deployment mechanisms
        ~Firmware Update Channel: Exploiting firmware update mechanisms to transfer files
        ~RTU Configuration Transfer: Using remote terminal unit configuration channels
        ~Safety System Transfer: Using safety instrumented system configuration channels
        ~Time Synchronization Channel: Exploiting NTP or PTP infrastructure for transfers
        ~Asset Management Tool Transfer: Using asset management and inventory systems
        ~Test Environment Transfer: Leveraging test/staging environment synchronization
        ~Third-party Integration Transfer: Using third-party system integration channels
        ~Redundancy Channel Transfer: Exploiting system redundancy mechanisms
    collection: The adversary is trying to gather data of interest and domain knowledge on your ICS environment to inform their goal.  Collection consists of techniques adversaries use to gather domain knowledge and obtain contextual feedback in an ICS environment. This tactic is often performed as part of [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0102), to compile data on control systems and targets of interest that may be used to follow through on the adversary’s objective. Examples of these techniques include observing operation states, capturing screenshots, identifying unique device roles, and gathering system and diagram schematics. Collection of this data can play a key role in planning, executing, and even revising an ICS-targeted attack. Methods of collection depend on the categories of data being targeted, which can include protocol specific, device specific, and process specific configurations and functionality. Information collected may pertain to a combination of system, supervisory, device, and network related data, which conceptually fall under high, medium, and low levels of plan operations. For example, information repositories on plant data at a high level or device specific programs at a low level. Sensitive floor plans, vendor device manuals, and other references may also be at risk and exposed on the internet or otherwise publicly accessible.
      Data from Local System: Adversaries may target and collect data from local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, or local databases. This can include sensitive data such as specifications, schematics, or diagrams of control system layouts, devices, and processes.  Adversaries may do this using [Command-Line Interface](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0807) or [Scripting](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0853) techniques to interact with the file system to gather information. Adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0802) on the local system.
        ~Configuration Files Collection: Adversaries may specifically target configuration files containing system parameters, setpoints, and control logic for ICS components
        ~Engineering Project Files: Adversaries may collect engineering project files containing PLC programs, HMI configurations, and control system logic
        ~Historical Data Collection: Adversaries may gather historical process data, trends, and logs stored locally on operator workstations or engineering stations
        ~Device Backup Files: Adversaries may target backup files containing device configurations, programs, and settings for ICS devices
        ~System Documentation: Adversaries may collect locally stored technical documentation, manuals, and standard operating procedures
        ~Database Files: Adversaries may extract local databases containing process parameters, alarm configurations, and user settings
        ~Network Configuration Data: Adversaries may gather locally stored network configuration files containing IP addresses, protocols, and communication paths
        ~Security Configuration Files: Adversaries may collect files containing security settings, access control lists, and authentication configurations
      Role Identification: Adversaries may perform role identification of devices involved with physical processes of interest in a target control system. Control systems devices often work in concert to control a physical process. Each device can have one or more roles that it performs within that control process. By collecting this role-based data, an adversary can construct a more targeted attack.  For example, a power generation plant may have unique devices such as one that monitors power output of a generator and another that controls the speed of a turbine. Examining devices roles allows the adversary to observe how the two devices work together to monitor and control a physical process. Understanding the role of a target device can inform the adversary's decision on what action to take, in order to cause Impact and influence or disrupt the integrity of operations. Furthermore, an adversary may be able to capture control system protocol traffic. By studying this traffic, the adversary may be able to determine which devices are outstations, and which are masters. Understanding of master devices and their role within control processes can enable the use of Rogue Master Device
        ~Protocol Analysis: Adversaries analyze industrial protocols (like Modbus, DNP3, EtherNet/IP) to identify master/slave relationships and device roles based on command patterns and data flows
        ~Device Configuration Mining: Adversaries examine device configuration files and settings to determine operational roles and responsibilities within the control system
        ~Process Variable Monitoring: Adversaries observe process variables and data points to understand how different devices interact and their roles in controlling physical processes
        ~Human Machine Interface Analysis: Adversaries study HMI screens and configurations to map device roles and their relationships in process control
        ~Engineering Workstation Reconnaissance: Adversaries access engineering workstations to gather information about device programming and operational roles
        ~Network Topology Analysis: Adversaries map network connections and communication patterns to identify critical control devices and their roles
        ~Historical Data Analysis: Adversaries examine historical process data and logs to understand device functions and responsibilities
        ~Device Documentation Review: Adversaries collect and analyze device manuals, documentation, and configuration guides to understand intended roles
      Detect Program State: Adversaries may seek to gather information about the current state of a program on a PLC. State information reveals information about the program, including whether it's running, halted, stopped, or has generated an exception. This information may be leveraged as a verification of malicious program execution or to determine if a PLC is ready to download a new program.
        ~Program Status Register Polling: Adversaries may repeatedly query specific memory registers that contain program status information using industrial protocols
        ~Engineering Software Monitoring: Adversaries may use vendor-specific engineering software to view program state information through legitimate programming interfaces
        ~Runtime Error Analysis: Adversaries may monitor error logs and exception handlers to determine program state and execution status
        ~Physical LED Status Monitoring: Adversaries may observe physical LED indicators on PLCs that show program run states
        ~Network Protocol State Queries: Adversaries may use industrial protocol commands (like Modbus function codes) to query program status
        ~Diagnostic Port Monitoring: Adversaries may connect to diagnostic ports to gather program state information
        ~Memory State Analysis: Adversaries may read memory areas containing program execution flags and status indicators
        ~Watchdog Timer Monitoring: Adversaries may observe watchdog timer states to determine program execution status
      Detect Operating Mode: Adversaries may gather information about a PLCs or controllers current operating mode. Operating modes dictate what change or maintenance functions can be manipulated and are often controlled by a key switch on the PLC (e.g.,  run, prog [program], and remote). Knowledge of these states may be valuable to an adversary to determine if they are able to reprogram the PLC. Operating modes and the mechanisms by which they are selected often vary by vendor and product line. Some commonly implemented operating modes are described below:    * Program - This mode must be enabled before changes can be made to a devices program. This allows program uploads and downloads between the device and an engineering workstation. Often the PLCs logic Is halted, and all outputs may be forced off. (Citation: N.A. October 2017)   * Run - Execution of the devices program occurs in this mode. Input and output (values, points, tags, elements, etc.) are monitored and used according to the programs logic.[Program Upload](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0845) and [Program Download](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843) are disabled while in this mode. (Citation: Omron) (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)  (Citation: N.A. October 2017) (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)    * Remote - Allows for remote changes to a PLCs operation mode. (Citation: PLCgurus 2021)     * Stop - The PLC and program is stopped, while in this mode, outputs are forced off. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Reset - Conditions on the PLC are reset to their original states. Warm resets may retain some memory while cold resets will reset all I/O and data registers. (Citation: Machine Information Systems 2007)    * Test / Monitor mode - Similar to run mode, I/O is processed, although this mode allows for monitoring, force set, resets, and more generally tuning or debugging of the system. Often monitor mode may be used as a trial for initialization. (Citation: Omron)
        ~Key Switch Position Monitoring: Adversaries may physically observe or monitor the position of key switches on PLCs to determine their current operating mode.
        ~Network Protocol Analysis: Adversaries may analyze industrial network protocols to detect operating mode indicators in communication patterns and command responses.
        ~Engineering Software Queries: Adversaries may use vendor-specific engineering software to query PLC status and determine current operating modes.
        ~Status Register Reading: Adversaries may directly read status registers or memory locations that indicate the current operating mode of the PLC.
        ~Mode Change Attempt Detection: Adversaries may attempt to initiate mode changes and observe success/failure responses to determine current operating state.
        ~HMI Interface Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor Human Machine Interface (HMI) screens and indicators that display PLC operating modes.
        ~Diagnostic Data Analysis: Adversaries may analyze diagnostic data and logs to determine operating mode changes and current states.
        ~Remote Access Interface Probing: Adversaries may probe remote access interfaces to determine if remote mode changes are possible and current mode status.
      Location Identification: Adversaries may perform location identification using device data to inform operations and targeted impact for attacks. Location identification data can come in a number of forms, including geographic location, location relative to other control system devices, time zone, and current time. An adversary may use an embedded global positioning system (GPS) module in a device to figure out the physical coordinates of a device. NIST SP800-82 recommends that devices utilize GPS or another location determining mechanism to attach appropriate timestamps to log entries (Citation: Guidance - NIST SP800-82). While this assists in logging and event tracking, an adversary could use the underlying positioning mechanism to determine the general location of a device. An adversary can also infer the physical location of serially connected devices by using serial connection enumeration.   An adversary attempt to attack and cause Impact could potentially affect other control system devices in close proximity. Device local-time and time-zone settings can also provide adversaries a rough indicator of device location, when specific geographic identifiers cannot be determined from the system.
        ~GPS Module Analysis: Exploitation of embedded GPS modules in ICS devices to determine precise geographic coordinates
        ~Serial Connection Mapping: Using serial connection enumeration to determine physical device relationships and proximity
        ~Time Zone Analysis: Leveraging device time zone settings and local time configurations to infer geographic locations
        ~Network Topology Discovery: Analyzing network connections and routing to determine relative device locations
        ~Device Naming Convention Analysis: Extracting location information from device names and identifiers that may contain geographic or physical location references
        ~Physical Infrastructure Documentation: Gathering and analyzing facility diagrams, floor plans, and equipment layout documents
        ~Environmental Sensor Data Analysis: Using temperature, humidity, and other environmental sensor data to infer device locations
        ~Radio Signal Analysis: Measuring radio signal strength and characteristics to determine device proximity and location
        ~Asset Management System Reconnaissance: Extracting location information from asset management and inventory systems
        ~Visual Identification: Using camera feeds, security systems, or physical observation to confirm device locations
      Adversary-in-the-Middle: Adversaries with privileged network access may seek to modify network traffic in real time using adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) attacks. (Citation: Gabriel Sanchez October 2017) This type of attack allows the adversary to intercept traffic to and/or from a particular device on the network. If a AiTM attack is established, then the adversary has the ability to block, log, modify, or inject traffic into the communication stream. There are several ways to accomplish this attack, but some of the most-common are Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) poisoning and the use of a proxy. (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)    An AiTM attack may allow an adversary to perform the following attacks:   [Block Reporting Message](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0804), [Spoof Reporting Message](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0856), [Modify Parameter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0836), [Unauthorized Command Message](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0855)
        ~ARP Cache Poisoning: Adversary manipulates Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) tables to redirect traffic through their system
        ~DNS Spoofing: Adversary intercepts and modifies DNS queries to redirect traffic to malicious endpoints
        ~SSL/TLS Interception: Adversary uses fake certificates or downgrades encryption to intercept secure communications
        ~Protocol-Specific Proxy: Adversary sets up specialized proxies for industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or OPC
        ~Physical Tap Installation: Adversary installs physical network taps or hardware devices to intercept traffic
        ~Radio Frequency Jamming: Adversary interferes with wireless industrial protocols to force failover to compromised channels
        ~Switch Port Stealing: Adversary manipulates switch port assignments to redirect traffic
        ~VLAN Hopping: Adversary exploits VLAN configurations to intercept traffic from other network segments
        ~Gateway Impersonation: Adversary impersonates legitimate network gateways or routers
        ~Protocol Downgrade: Adversary forces communications to use less secure protocol versions
        ~Serial Protocol Interception: Adversary intercepts serial communications between field devices and controllers
        ~Remote Access Gateway Exploitation: Adversary compromises remote access pathways used for maintenance
        ~Engineering Workstation Proxy: Adversary intercepts traffic between engineering workstations and control systems
        ~Protocol Translation Attack: Adversary exploits protocol converters or gateways between different industrial networks
        ~Fieldbus Interception: Adversary taps into fieldbus networks to intercept device-level communications
        ~Time Synchronization Manipulation: Adversary interferes with time synchronization protocols to disrupt coordinated operations
        ~OPC Classic Interception: Adversary exploits vulnerabilities in OPC Classic communications
        ~Historian Communication Interception: Adversary intercepts data flows between field devices and historian servers
      Data from Information Repositories: Adversaries may target and collect data from information repositories. This can include sensitive data such as specifications, schematics, or diagrams of control system layouts, devices, and processes. Examples of information repositories include reference databases in the process environment, as well as databases in the corporate network that might contain information about the ICS.(Citation: Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency March 2018)  Information collected from these systems may provide the adversary with a better understanding of the operational environment, vendors used, processes, or procedures of the ICS.  In a campaign between 2011 and 2013 against ONG organizations, Chinese state-sponsored actors searched document repositories for specific information such as, system manuals, remote terminal unit (RTU) sites, personnel lists, documents that included the string SCAD*, user credentials, and remote dial-up access information. (Citation: CISA AA21-201A Pipeline Intrusion July 2021)
        ~Engineering Documentation Access: Collection of engineering drawings, P&IDs, network diagrams, and system specifications from engineering document management systems
        ~Maintenance Records Collection: Targeting maintenance logs, equipment specifications, and repair histories from maintenance management systems
        ~Operational Database Mining: Extraction of process parameters, setpoints, and operational data from historian databases and SCADA servers
        ~Cloud Repository Access: Accessing ICS-related information stored in cloud-based document management systems or backup repositories
        ~Network Share Enumeration: Scanning and collecting data from network file shares containing ICS documentation and configurations
        ~Control System Backup Collection: Targeting backup repositories containing PLC programs, HMI configurations, and system settings
        ~Vendor Documentation Access: Gathering vendor manuals, firmware specifications, and technical documentation from manufacturer portals
        ~Employee Portal Exploitation: Accessing internal wikis, knowledge bases, and collaboration platforms containing ICS information
      Program Upload: Adversaries may attempt to upload a program from a PLC to gather information about an industrial process. Uploading a program may allow them to acquire and study the underlying logic. Methods of program upload include vendor software, which enables the user to upload and read a program running on a PLC. This software can be used to upload the target program to a workstation, jump box, or an interfacing device.
        ~Vendor Engineering Software Upload: Using vendor-provided engineering software like RSLogix, Step7, or Unity Pro to upload PLC programs through legitimate functionality
        ~Memory Upload via Protocol: Directly uploading program memory blocks using industrial protocols like S7comm, Modbus, or EtherNet/IP
        ~Online Program Analysis: Uploading and analyzing program logic while PLC is running without interrupting operations
        ~Offline Program Extraction: Physical access to PLC memory or storage media to extract program data
        ~Project File Recovery: Recovering project files and program logic from engineering workstation backups or temporary files
        ~Firmware Analysis Upload: Extracting program logic embedded within firmware updates or backups
        ~Network Capture Analysis: Capturing program uploads during legitimate maintenance activities through network monitoring
        ~Third-Party Tool Upload: Using specialized third-party or open-source tools designed for PLC program extraction
        ~Remote Access Software Upload: Leveraging remote access and maintenance tools to perform program uploads
        ~Configuration Backup Analysis: Extracting program logic from automated backup systems and configuration management tools
        ~Version Control System Analysis: Extracting program logic from engineering version control systems or source code repositories
        ~Cloud Backup Analysis: Accessing PLC programs stored in cloud-based backup or disaster recovery systems
        ~Change Management System Upload: Obtaining programs through automated change management and deployment systems
        ~Development Environment Recovery: Extracting program logic from development and testing environments
        ~Service Provider System Access: Accessing program copies stored in vendor or system integrator maintenance systems
      Automated Collection: Adversaries may automate collection of industrial environment information using tools or scripts. This automated collection may leverage native control protocols and tools available in the control systems environment. For example, the OPC protocol may be used to enumerate and gather information. Access to a system or interface with these native protocols may allow collection and enumeration of other attached, communicating servers and devices.
        ~OPC Protocol Enumeration: Automated scanning and enumeration using OPC protocols (DA, UA, etc.) to discover and collect data from OPC servers and connected devices
        ~PLC Program Collection: Automated extraction of ladder logic, function blocks, and program code from PLCs using vendor-specific protocols
        ~SCADA HMI Data Harvesting: Automated collection of process data, alarms, and historical trends through HMI interfaces and historian databases
        ~Industrial Protocol Scanning: Systematic scanning using industrial protocols (Modbus, Profinet, EtherNet/IP) to gather device information and process data
        ~Configuration File Extraction: Automated extraction of device configurations, parameters, and setpoints from control system components
        ~Telemetry Data Collection: Automated gathering of real-time telemetry data from sensors, actuators, and field devices
        ~Network Share Enumeration: Automated discovery and collection of engineering files, documentation, and diagrams from network shares
        ~Device Discovery Scanning: Automated scanning to identify and enumerate ICS devices, their roles, and capabilities across the network
        ~Historical Data Mining: Automated extraction of historical process data and trends from historian databases and archives
        ~Asset Management System Query: Automated collection of asset information through queries to asset management and maintenance systems
        ~Engineering Workstation Scanning: Automated collection of project files, configurations, and documentation from engineering workstations
        ~Backup System Collection: Automated extraction of system backups containing configurations, programs, and historical data
        ~Safety System Data Collection: Automated gathering of safety system configurations, limits, and operational parameters
        ~Building Management System Collection: Automated collection of building automation system data, including HVAC, access control, and energy management systems
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Data Collection: Automated gathering of data from RTUs including status, measurements, and configurations
        ~Firmware Version Collection: Automated extraction of firmware versions and update files from ICS devices
        ~Recipe Management Collection: Automated extraction of production recipes, formulas, and batch processing parameters
        ~Industrial IoT Device Enumeration: Automated discovery and data collection from IIoT sensors and edge devices
        ~Industrial Wireless Network Collection: Automated gathering of data from wireless sensors, mesh networks, and industrial WiFi systems
        ~Supply Chain System Collection: Automated extraction of data from manufacturing execution systems (MES) and supply chain integration points
        ~Quality Control System Collection: Automated collection of quality control parameters, test results, and inspection data
        ~Mobile HMI Collection: Automated data gathering from industrial mobile devices and portable operator interfaces
        ~Proprietary Protocol Collection: Automated collection using vendor-specific or custom industrial protocols
        ~Time Series Database Collection: Automated extraction from specialized industrial time series databases and data lakes
        ~Industrial Network Traffic Collection: Automated capture and analysis of industrial network traffic patterns and communications
      Wireless Sniffing: Adversaries may seek to capture radio frequency (RF) communication used for remote control and reporting in distributed environments. RF communication frequencies vary between 3 kHz to 300 GHz, although are commonly between 300 MHz to 6 GHz. (Citation: Candell, R., Hany, M., Lee, K. B., Liu,Y., Quimby, J., Remley, K. April 2018)  The wavelength and frequency of the signal affect how the signal propagates through open air, obstacles (e.g. walls and trees) and the type of radio required to capture them. These characteristics are often standardized in the protocol and hardware and may have an effect on how the signal is captured. Some examples of wireless protocols that may be found in cyber-physical environments are: WirelessHART, Zigbee, WIA-FA, and 700 MHz Public Safety Spectrum.   Adversaries may capture RF communications by using specialized hardware, such as software defined radio (SDR), handheld radio, or a computer with radio demodulator tuned to the communication frequency. (Citation: Bastille April 2017) Information transmitted over a wireless medium may be captured in-transit whether the sniffing device is the intended destination or not. This technique may be particularly useful to an adversary when the communications are not encrypted. (Citation: Gallagher, S. April 2017)   In the 2017 Dallas Siren incident, it is suspected that adversaries likely captured wireless command message broadcasts on a 700 MHz frequency during a regular test of the system. These messages were later replayed to trigger the alarm systems. (Citation: Gallagher, S. April 2017)
        ~Protocol-Specific Capture: Targeting specific industrial wireless protocols like WirelessHART, Zigbee, or WIA-FA using specialized equipment
        ~Public Safety Band Monitoring: Focusing on capturing communications in public safety bands (e.g., 700 MHz) used for emergency and control systems
        ~Short-Range Industrial Communication: Targeting short-range industrial wireless communications typically used in factory automation
        ~Long-Range Industrial Communication: Focusing on long-range wireless communications used in remote monitoring and SCADA systems
        ~Passive Signal Collection: Collecting RF signals without actively transmitting, using directional antennas and sensitive receivers
        ~Mobile Collection: Using mobile or vehicle-mounted equipment to collect wireless signals while moving through industrial areas
        ~Test Window Exploitation: Targeting scheduled system tests or maintenance windows when wireless communications are more frequent
        ~Environmental Adaptation: Adjusting collection methods based on physical obstacles and environmental conditions
        ~Multi-Band Capture: Simultaneously monitoring multiple frequency bands to capture different types of industrial control communications
        ~Automated Signal Analysis: Using automated tools to identify and classify industrial control system communications in captured RF data
        ~Control Signal Isolation: Focusing specifically on isolating and capturing control signals from general industrial wireless traffic
        ~Interference Pattern Analysis: Monitoring and analyzing interference patterns to identify specific industrial equipment operations
        ~Maintenance Channel Monitoring: Targeting wireless maintenance and diagnostic channels used for equipment configuration
        ~Emergency Override Channel Capture: Focusing on emergency override and safety system wireless communications
        ~Process-Specific Collection: Targeting wireless communications related to specific industrial processes or equipment types
        ~Relay Communication Capture: Monitoring wireless relay and repeater stations used in industrial networks
        ~Time-Synchronized Collection: Capturing wireless signals with precise timing to correlate with industrial process cycles
        ~Cross-Protocol Analysis: Simultaneously monitoring multiple protocols to understand system interactions
        ~Backup System Communication Capture: Targeting wireless communications between primary and backup control systems
        ~Start-up/Shutdown Sequence Monitoring: Focusing on wireless communications during critical operational state changes
        ~Cross-Site Communication Capture: Targeting wireless communications between different industrial sites or facilities
        ~Vendor-Specific Protocol Collection: Focusing on proprietary wireless protocols used by specific ICS vendors
        ~Remote Sensor Network Monitoring: Targeting wireless communications in distributed sensor networks
        ~Redundant Channel Collection: Monitoring redundant wireless channels used for critical system communications
      Point & Tag Identification: Adversaries may collect point and tag values to gain a more comprehensive understanding of the process environment. Points may be values such as inputs, memory locations, outputs or other process specific variables. (Citation: Dennis L. Sloatman September 2016) Tags are the identifiers given to points for operator convenience.   Collecting such tags provides valuable context to environmental points and enables an adversary to map inputs, outputs, and other values to their control processes. Understanding the points being collected may inform an adversary on which processes and values to keep track of over the course of an operation.
        ~Protocol Scanning: Adversaries may scan industrial protocols (like Modbus, S7comm) to identify and collect tag information from PLCs and other control devices
        ~HMI Interface Analysis: Adversaries may observe and collect point/tag information through Human Machine Interface screens and configurations
        ~Engineering Workstation Access: Adversaries may access engineering workstations to extract point and tag configurations from project files
        ~Historical Data Collection: Adversaries may gather historical data from historians or data loggers to identify important process points and tags
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Adversaries may analyze configuration files and databases to extract point and tag mappings
        ~Memory Dump Analysis: Adversaries may perform memory dumps of control systems to extract point and tag information
        ~Network Traffic Monitoring: Adversaries may passively monitor industrial network traffic to identify points and tags in communication
        ~Database Query: Adversaries may query industrial databases (like tag databases) to collect point and tag information
        ~OPC Server Enumeration: Adversaries may enumerate OPC servers to discover available points and tags
        ~Runtime Process Analysis: Adversaries may analyze running processes to identify active points and tags in real-time
        ~Physical Panel Inspection: Adversaries may physically observe control panels, displays, and operator stations to collect point and tag information
        ~Documentation Analysis: Adversaries may gather and analyze system documentation, manuals, and specifications to identify points and tags
        ~Vendor Tool Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vendor-specific configuration tools to extract point and tag information
        ~Backup System Analysis: Adversaries may access backup systems and archives to collect historical point and tag configurations
        ~Third-party Integration Points: Adversaries may target third-party systems integrated with ICS to gather point and tag information
        ~Social Engineering Collection: Adversaries may use social engineering to obtain point and tag information from operators or maintenance personnel
        ~Remote Access Gateway Analysis: Adversaries may analyze remote access gateways and jump servers to identify point and tag configurations
        ~Change Management System Access: Adversaries may access change management systems to collect point and tag modification history
        ~Cloud SCADA Analysis: Adversaries may target cloud-based SCADA systems to collect point and tag information
        ~Mobile HMI Application Analysis: Adversaries may extract point and tag information from mobile applications used for ICS monitoring
        ~Test Environment Access: Adversaries may access development or testing environments to gather point and tag configurations
        ~Automation Script Analysis: Adversaries may analyze automation scripts and scheduled tasks to identify referenced points and tags
        ~Supply Chain System Access: Adversaries may collect point and tag information through compromised vendor systems or update mechanisms
        ~Asset Management System Analysis: Adversaries may extract point and tag information from asset management and inventory systems
      Monitor Process State: Adversaries may gather information about the physical process state. This information may be used to gain more information about the process itself or used as a trigger for malicious actions. The sources of process state information may vary such as, OPC tags, historian data, specific PLC block information, or network traffic.
        ~OPC Tag Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor OPC (OLE for Process Control) tags to gather real-time process data and state information from industrial devices
        ~Historian Database Query: Adversaries may query historian databases to collect historical process data and trend information
        ~PLC Program Block Reading: Adversaries may read specific program blocks from PLCs to understand process logic and current state values
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Adversaries may capture and analyze industrial protocol traffic to extract process state information
        ~HMI Screen Capture: Adversaries may capture HMI screen data to observe process visualization and state information
        ~Direct I/O Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor I/O values directly from field devices to gather process state information
        ~Process Parameter Trending: Adversaries may collect and analyze process parameter trends over time to understand normal operations
        ~Alarm System Monitoring: Adversaries may monitor alarm systems to gather information about process deviations and operational states
      Screen Capture: Adversaries may attempt to perform screen capture of devices in the control system environment. Screenshots may be taken of workstations, HMIs, or other devices that display environment-relevant process, device, reporting, alarm, or related data. These device displays may reveal information regarding the ICS process, layout, control, and related schematics. In particular, an HMI can provide a lot of important industrial process information. (Citation: ICS-CERT October 2017) Analysis of screen captures may provide the adversary with an understanding of intended operations and interactions between critical devices.
        ~HMI Workstation Capture: Capturing screens from Human Machine Interface workstations showing real-time process control data, alarms, and system status
        ~Engineering Workstation Capture: Targeting screens from engineering workstations containing PLC programming, system configurations, and network architecture
        ~Automated Periodic Capture: Systematic capture of screens at regular intervals to monitor operational patterns and changes
        ~Event-Triggered Capture: Screen captures triggered by specific events like alarms, process changes, or operator actions
        ~Remote Console Capture: Capturing screens from remote access and monitoring consoles used for system administration
        ~Mobile HMI Capture: Targeting screens from mobile devices used for ICS monitoring and control
        ~Historian Interface Capture: Capturing screens from historian interfaces showing historical process data and trends
        ~SCADA Dashboard Capture: Targeting comprehensive SCADA system dashboards showing multiple process views
      I/O Image: Adversaries may seek to capture process values related to the inputs and outputs of a PLC. During the scan cycle, a PLC reads the status of all inputs and stores them in an image table. (Citation: Nanjundaiah, Vaidyanath) The image table is the PLCs internal storage location where values of inputs/outputs for one scan are stored while it executes the user program. After the PLC has solved the entire logic program, it updates the output image table. The contents of this output image table are written to the corresponding output points in I/O Modules.  The Input and Output Image tables described above make up the I/O Image on a PLC. This image is used by the user program instead of directly interacting with physical I/O. (Citation: Spenneberg, Ralf 2016)   Adversaries may collect the I/O Image state of a PLC by utilizing a devices [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0834) to access the memory regions directly. The collection of the PLCs I/O state could be used to replace values or inform future stages of an attack.
        ~Memory Region Direct Access: Adversaries may directly access PLC memory regions containing I/O image tables using native memory read functions
        ~Real-time Monitoring: Adversaries may continuously monitor I/O image updates to capture changes in process values over time
        ~Input Image Collection: Adversaries may specifically target input image tables to understand sensor readings and field device states
        ~Output Image Collection: Adversaries may specifically target output image tables to understand control commands and actuator states
        ~Scan Cycle Synchronization: Adversaries may synchronize their collection with PLC scan cycles to capture complete I/O states
        ~Historical I/O Logging: Adversaries may collect and store I/O image data over time to analyze patterns and process behavior
        ~Cross-Reference Collection: Adversaries may collect both input and output images simultaneously to understand cause-effect relationships
        ~Selective I/O Monitoring: Adversaries may target specific I/O points of interest rather than the complete I/O image
    command-and-control: The adversary is trying to communicate with and control compromised systems, controllers, and platforms with access to your ICS environment.  Command and Control consists of techniques that adversaries use to communicate with and send commands to compromised systems, devices, controllers, and platforms with specialized applications used in ICS environments. Examples of these specialized communication devices include human machine interfaces (HMIs), data historians, SCADA servers, and engineering workstations (EWS). Adversaries often seek to use commonly available resources and mimic expected network traffic to avoid detection and suspicion. For instance, commonly used ports and protocols in ICS environments, and even expected IT resources, depending on the target network. Command and Control may be established to varying degrees of stealth, often depending on the victim’s network structure and defenses.
      Connection Proxy: Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications.  The definition of a proxy can also be expanded to encompass trust relationships between networks in peer-to-peer, mesh, or trusted connections between networks consisting of hosts or systems that regularly communicate with each other.  The network may be within a single organization or across multiple organizations with trust relationships. Adversaries could use these types of relationships to manage command and control communications, to reduce the number of simultaneous outbound network connections, to provide resiliency in the face of connection loss, or to ride over existing trusted communications paths between victims to avoid suspicion. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018)
        ~Protocol Tunneling: Using legitimate ICS protocols to tunnel malicious traffic through firewalls and other security controls
        ~Vendor Remote Access: Exploiting existing vendor remote access systems and VPNs used for maintenance
        ~Engineering Workstation Proxy: Using compromised engineering workstations as proxy nodes to reach deeper into ICS networks
        ~Trust Relationship Abuse: Exploiting trusted relationships between different ICS zones or between IT and OT networks
        ~Historian Server Proxy: Leveraging data historian servers that typically have connectivity to both IT and OT networks
        ~DMZ Server Relay: Using compromised servers in the industrial DMZ as communication relays
        ~HMI System Proxy: Utilizing compromised HMI systems as intermediaries for command and control
        ~Multi-hop Proxy Chain: Creating chains of proxy connections through multiple ICS components
      Standard Application Layer Protocol: Adversaries may establish command and control capabilities over commonly used application layer protocols such as HTTP(S), OPC, RDP, telnet, DNP3, and modbus. These protocols may be used to disguise adversary actions as benign network traffic. Standard protocols may be seen on their associated port or in some cases over a non-standard port.  Adversaries may use these protocols to reach out of the network for command and control, or in some cases to other infected devices within the network.
        ~Modbus Command and Control: Adversaries may abuse Modbus protocol for command and control by hiding malicious traffic within legitimate industrial protocol communications
        ~DNP3 Protocol Abuse: Adversaries may leverage DNP3 (Distributed Network Protocol) communications for command and control in power systems and utilities
        ~OPC Classic Protocol: Adversaries may exploit OPC Classic (DA, AE, HDA) protocols for command and control between industrial systems
        ~OPC UA Protocol: Adversaries may leverage OPC UA protocol's sophisticated capabilities for maintaining persistent command and control
        ~HTTP/HTTPS Communications: Adversaries may use standard web protocols to blend C2 traffic with legitimate web traffic in ICS networks
        ~Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP): Adversaries may abuse RDP for command and control of engineering workstations and HMIs
        ~Telnet Protocol: Adversaries may utilize telnet for command and control of legacy ICS devices that still support this protocol
        ~S7comm Protocol: Adversaries may abuse Siemens S7 communication protocol for command and control of Siemens PLCs
        ~BACnet Protocol: Adversaries may leverage Building Automation and Control Network protocol for C2 in building management systems
        ~EtherNet/IP Protocol: Adversaries may abuse EtherNet/IP protocol for command and control in industrial automation systems
        ~ICCP/TASE.2 Protocol: Adversaries may abuse Inter-Control Center Communications Protocol for command and control between control centers
        ~MQTT Protocol: Adversaries may leverage Message Queuing Telemetry Transport protocol for C2 in IoT and ICS environments
        ~Profinet Protocol: Adversaries may exploit Profinet industrial ethernet protocol for command and control in manufacturing systems
        ~CIP Protocol: Adversaries may abuse Common Industrial Protocol for C2 across different industrial network types
        ~IEC 60870-5-104: Adversaries may leverage this protocol for command and control in power system automation
        ~FINS Protocol: Adversaries may abuse Omron's Factory Interface Network Service protocol for C2 in Omron devices
        ~SNMP Protocol: Adversaries may use Simple Network Management Protocol for C2 in industrial network monitoring systems
        ~SOAP/XML Protocol: Adversaries may leverage web services protocols for C2 in modern ICS applications
        ~FTP/SFTP Protocol: Adversaries may abuse file transfer protocols for C2 in ICS data transfer operations
      Commonly Used Port: Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend in with normal network activity, to avoid more detailed inspection. They may use the protocol associated with the port, or a completely different protocol. They may use commonly open ports, such as the examples provided below.     * TCP:80 (HTTP)   * TCP:443 (HTTPS)   * TCP/UDP:53 (DNS)   * TCP:1024-4999 (OPC on XP/Win2k3)   * TCP:49152-65535 (OPC on Vista and later)   * TCP:23 (TELNET)   * UDP:161 (SNMP)   * TCP:502 (MODBUS)   * TCP:102 (S7comm/ISO-TSAP)   * TCP:20000 (DNP3)   * TCP:44818 (Ethernet/IP)
        ~Protocol Abuse: Adversaries tunnel non-standard protocols through commonly allowed ports (e.g., tunneling Modbus through HTTP port 80)
        ~Industrial Protocol Masquerading: Adversaries use legitimate ICS protocol ports but with malicious payloads
        ~Cross-Domain Protocol Misuse: Adversaries leverage IT protocols to carry OT traffic
        ~Dynamic Port Allocation: Adversaries exploit dynamic port ranges used by OPC and similar protocols
        ~Legacy Protocol Exploitation: Adversaries target older, insecure protocols still common in ICS (e.g., Telnet)
        ~Protocol Multiplexing: Adversaries combine multiple protocols over single ports to obfuscate traffic
        ~Standard IT Protocol Abuse: Adversaries leverage common IT protocols (HTTP/HTTPS) to blend with normal traffic
        ~Management Protocol Abuse: Adversaries exploit management protocols like SNMP for command and control
    inhibit-response-function: The adversary is trying to prevent your safety, protection, quality assurance, and operator intervention functions from responding to a failure, hazard, or unsafe state.  Inhibit Response Function consists of techniques that adversaries use to hinder the safeguards put in place for processes and products. This may involve the inhibition of safety, protection, quality assurance, or operator intervention functions to disrupt safeguards that aim to prevent the loss of life, destruction of equipment, and disruption of production. These techniques aim to actively deter and prevent expected alarms and responses that arise due to statuses in the ICS environment. Adversaries may modify or update system logic, or even outright prevent responses with a denial-of-service. They may result in the prevention, destruction, manipulation, or modification of programs, logic, devices, and communications. As prevention functions are generally dormant, reporting and processing functions can appear fine, but may have been altered to prevent failure responses in dangerous scenarios. Unlike [Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0103), Inhibit Response Function techniques may be more intrusive, such as actively preventing responses to a known dangerous scenario. Adversaries may use these techniques to follow through with or provide cover for [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105) techniques.
      Block Reporting Message: Adversaries may block or prevent a reporting message from reaching its intended target. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. By blocking these reporting messages, an adversary can potentially hide their actions from an operator.  Blocking reporting messages in control systems that manage physical processes may contribute to system impact, causing inhibition of a response function. A control system may not be able to respond in a proper or timely manner to an event, such as a dangerous fault, if its corresponding reporting message is blocked. (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)  (Citation: Electricity Information Sharing and Analysis Center; SANS Industrial Control Systems March 2016)
        ~Protocol Message Dropping: Block specific protocol messages (e.g., Modbus, DNP3, or EtherNet/IP) by intercepting and dropping them before reaching their destination
        ~Network Segmentation Abuse: Manipulate network routing or firewall rules to prevent reporting messages from traversing network segments
        ~Message Queue Manipulation: Interfere with message queues or buffers to prevent delivery of reporting messages
        ~Communication Link Disruption: Disrupt physical communication links or interfaces used for reporting
        ~Selective Telemetry Filtering: Filter out specific types of telemetry data or status updates while allowing others to pass
        ~Time Synchronization Tampering: Manipulate time synchronization to cause message drops due to timing violations
        ~Protocol Session Hijacking: Take over communication sessions to control which messages are delivered
        ~Buffer Overflow Exploitation: Exploit buffer limitations to prevent new messages from being processed
      Denial of Service: Adversaries may perform Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks to disrupt expected device functionality. Examples of DoS attacks include overwhelming the target device with a high volume of requests in a short time period and sending the target device a request it does not know how to handle. Disrupting device state may temporarily render it unresponsive, possibly lasting until a reboot can occur. When placed in this state, devices may be unable to send and receive requests, and may not perform expected response functions in reaction to other events in the environment.   Some ICS devices are particularly sensitive to DoS events, and may become unresponsive in reaction to even a simple ping sweep. Adversaries may also attempt to execute a Permanent Denial-of-Service (PDoS) against certain devices, such as in the case of the BrickerBot malware. (Citation: ICS-CERT April 2017)   Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to cause a denial of service by taking advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to cause a denial of service condition.   Adversaries may have prior knowledge about industrial protocols or control devices used in the environment through [Remote System Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0888). There are examples of adversaries remotely causing a [Device Restart/Shutdown](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0816) by exploiting a vulnerability that induces uncontrolled resource consumption. (Citation: ICS-CERT August 2018) (Citation: Common Weakness Enumeration January 2019) (Citation: MITRE March 2018)
        ~Network Flooding: Overwhelming target devices with high volumes of network traffic, including ping floods, TCP SYN floods, or UDP floods targeting ICS protocols
        ~Protocol Exploitation: Sending malformed or unexpected protocol requests that cause devices to enter failure states
        ~Resource Exhaustion: Consuming system resources like CPU, memory, or disk space until the device becomes unresponsive
        ~Permanent DoS: Executing attacks that cause permanent damage to device firmware or configuration
        ~Memory Corruption: Exploiting memory-related vulnerabilities to crash device services or the entire system
        ~Connection Exhaustion: Consuming all available network connections or sessions to prevent legitimate communications
        ~Timing Attack: Manipulating timing-sensitive operations in ICS devices to cause operational disruptions
        ~Buffer Overflow DoS: Exploiting buffer overflow vulnerabilities specifically to cause system crashes
        ~Configuration Corruption: Modifying device configurations in ways that cause operational failures
        ~Process Exhaustion: Creating excessive processes or threads until system resources are depleted
        ~Application Layer DoS: Targeting specific ICS applications with requests that cause them to hang or crash
        ~Hardware Interface Flooding: Overwhelming physical I/O interfaces with excessive signals or requests
        ~Protocol State Manipulation: Forcing protocols into invalid states that cause devices to stop responding
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Interfering with watchdog timer mechanisms to cause device resets
        ~Storage Device Flooding: Filling storage devices with excessive logging or data until system failure
        ~Control Loop Interference: Causing DoS by disrupting PID and other control loop calculations
        ~Safety System Flooding: Overwhelming safety systems with false alarms to trigger shutdown conditions
        ~Sensor Signal Flooding: Bombarding sensor inputs with excessive or invalid signals
        ~Field Device Command Flooding: Overwhelming field devices with rapid command sequences
        ~HMI Session Flooding: Exhausting HMI connection resources to prevent operator control
        ~Time Sync Disruption: Interfering with time synchronization services to cause device coordination failures
        ~Historian Database Flooding: Overwhelming historical data systems with excessive data points
        ~Engineering Software DoS: Targeting engineering workstation software to prevent configuration changes
        ~Backplane Communication Flooding: Overwhelming internal chassis communication in modular systems
        ~Remote IO Link Flooding: Saturating remote IO links to cause device isolation
      Modify Control Logic: Adversaries may place malicious code in a system, which can cause the system to malfunction by modifying its control logic. Control system devices use programming languages (e.g. relay ladder logic) to control physical processes by affecting actuators, which cause machines to operate, based on environment sensor readings. These devices often include the ability to perform remote control logic updates.   Program code is normally edited in a vendor-specific Integrated Development Environment (IDE) that relies on proprietary tools and features. These IDEs allow an engineer to perform host target development and may have the ability to run the code on the machine it is programmed for. The IDE will transmit the control logic to the testing device, and will perform the required device-specific functions to apply the changes and make them active.  An adversary may attempt to use this host target IDE to modify device control logic. Even though proprietary tools are often used to edit and update control logic, the process can usually be reverse-engineered and reproduced with open-source tools.  An adversary can de-calibrate a sensor by removing functions in control logic that account for sensor error. This can be used to change a control process without actually spoofing command messages to a controller or device.   It is believed this process happened in the lesser known over-pressurizer attacks build into Stuxnet. Pressure sensors are not perfect at translating pressure into an analog output signal, but their errors can be corrected by calibration. The pressure controller can be told what the “real” pressure is for given analog signals and then automatically linearize the measurement to what would be the “real” pressure. If the linearization is overwritten by malicious code on the S7-417 controller, analog pressure readings will be “corrected” during the attack by the pressure controller, which then interprets all analog pressure readings as perfectly normal pressure no matter how high or low their analog values are. The pressure controller then acts accordingly by never opening the stage exhaust valves. In the meantime, actual pressure keeps rising. (Citation: Stuxnet - Langner - 201311)  In the Maroochy Attack, Vitek Boden gained remote computer access to the control system and altered data so that whatever function should have occurred at affected pumping stations did not occur or occurred in a different way. The software program installed in the laptop was one developed by Hunter Watertech for its use in changing configurations in the PDS computers. This ultimately led to 800,000 liters of raw sewage being spilled out into the community. (Citation: Maroochy - MITRE - 200808)
        ~Sensor Calibration Manipulation: Adversaries modify control logic responsible for sensor calibration and error correction, causing systems to misinterpret sensor readings
        ~Logic Download Manipulation: Adversaries intercept and modify control logic during legitimate download processes to PLCs or control systems
        ~Runtime Logic Alteration: Adversaries modify control logic while system is operating, changing behavior without stopping operations
        ~Safety Interlock Bypass: Adversaries modify interlocking logic designed to prevent dangerous operating conditions
        ~Timer/Counter Manipulation: Adversaries alter timing and counting functions in control logic to disrupt process synchronization
        ~Ladder Logic Injection: Adversaries insert malicious ladder logic code into existing programs
        ~Function Block Modification: Adversaries modify function blocks that control critical operations
        ~Sequence Logic Tampering: Adversaries alter sequential logic controlling process steps or state machines
        ~Variable Range Manipulation: Adversaries modify acceptable ranges for process variables in control logic
        ~Backup Logic Corruption: Adversaries modify backup/failover logic to prevent proper system recovery
        ~Protocol Command Injection: Adversaries inject malicious commands into industrial protocols to modify control logic
        ~Engineering Workstation Exploitation: Adversaries compromise engineering workstations to deploy unauthorized logic changes
        ~Configuration File Tampering: Adversaries modify configuration files that define control logic parameters
        ~Firmware Logic Modification: Adversaries alter firmware-level logic components
        ~Historical Data Manipulation: Adversaries modify logic handling historical process data to mask changes
        ~Remote Access Tool Abuse: Adversaries exploit legitimate remote access tools to deploy logic modifications
        ~Library Function Replacement: Adversaries replace standard logic library functions with malicious versions
        ~State Logic Corruption: Adversaries modify state transition logic to create undefined states
        ~Vendor Update Mechanism Abuse: Adversaries exploit legitimate vendor update mechanisms to deploy malicious logic
        ~Logic Validation Bypass: Adversaries circumvent logic validation and verification mechanisms
        ~Cross-Controller Logic Tampering: Adversaries modify logic that coordinates multiple controllers
        ~Emergency Response Logic Modification: Adversaries alter logic controlling emergency response procedures
        ~Logic Version Control Exploitation: Adversaries manipulate version control systems to inject malicious logic
        ~Logic Backup Restoration Tampering: Adversaries modify logic during backup restoration processes
      Modify Alarm Settings: Adversaries may modify alarm settings to prevent alerts that may inform operators of their presence or to prevent responses to dangerous and unintended scenarios. Reporting messages are a standard part of data acquisition in control systems. Reporting messages are used as a way to transmit system state information and acknowledgements that specific actions have occurred. These messages provide vital information for the management of a physical process, and keep operators, engineers, and administrators aware of the state of system devices and physical processes.   If an adversary is able to change the reporting settings, certain events could be prevented from being reported. This type of modification can also prevent operators or devices from performing actions to keep the system in a safe state. If critical reporting messages cannot trigger these actions then a [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105) could occur.   In ICS environments, the adversary may have to use [Alarm Suppression](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0878) or contend with multiple alarms and/or alarm propagation to achieve a specific goal to evade detection or prevent intended responses from occurring. (Citation: Jos Wetzels, Marina Krotofil 2019)  Methods of suppression often rely on modification of alarm settings, such as modifying in memory code to fixed values or tampering with assembly level instruction code.
        ~Modify Alarm Thresholds: Altering threshold values that trigger alarms to prevent alerts during abnormal conditions
        ~Disable Alarm Notifications: Completely disabling the transmission of alarm notifications to operators or other systems
        ~Modify Alarm Priority Levels: Changing the priority/severity levels of alarms to downgrade critical alerts
        ~Tamper with Alarm Logic: Modifying the underlying logic that determines when alarms should trigger
        ~Block Alarm Propagation: Preventing alarms from being propagated to other connected systems or backup monitoring stations
        ~Modify Alarm Delay Timers: Adjusting the time delays between alarm detection and notification to create windows of opportunity
        ~Corrupt Alarm Configuration Files: Modifying or corrupting configuration files that define alarm behaviors
        ~Modify Alarm Acknowledgment Requirements: Changing settings related to how alarms must be acknowledged or cleared
      Data Destruction: Adversaries may perform data destruction over the course of an operation. The adversary may drop or create malware, tools, or other non-native files on a target system to accomplish this, potentially leaving behind traces of malicious activities. Such non-native files and other data may be removed over the course of an intrusion to maintain a small footprint or as a standard part of the post-intrusion cleanup process. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018)  Data destruction may also be used to render operator interfaces unable to respond and to disrupt response functions from occurring as expected. An adversary may also destroy data backups that are vital to recovery after an incident.  Standard file deletion commands are available on most operating system and device interfaces to perform cleanup, but adversaries may use other tools as well. Two examples are Windows Sysinternals SDelete and Active@ Killdisk.
        ~Runtime Data Deletion: Deletion of operational data during system runtime to prevent proper system response
        ~Configuration File Destruction: Targeted destruction of system configuration files to disable safety mechanisms
        ~Historian Database Corruption: Corrupting or destroying historical data used for trend analysis and incident response
        ~Backup System Targeting: Specifically destroying backup data and recovery systems
        ~Memory Manipulation: Direct manipulation and corruption of system memory to prevent proper function
        ~Firmware Corruption: Destroying or corrupting device firmware to prevent proper operation
        ~Safety Logic Deletion: Removing or corrupting safety-related logic and programming
        ~HMI Data Destruction: Destroying data required for proper HMI operation and visualization
        ~Sensor Data Corruption: Destroying or manipulating sensor data storage to prevent proper system monitoring
        ~Audit Log Destruction: Removing audit logs to prevent incident investigation and response
        ~Communication Buffer Clearing: Destroying data in communication buffers between ICS components
        ~Time-Delayed Destruction: Implementing scheduled or triggered data destruction based on specific conditions
        ~Storage Media Corruption: Physical corruption of storage devices through repeated write operations
        ~Protocol Configuration Destruction: Destroying protocol-specific configuration data and mappings
        ~Redundancy Synchronization Corruption: Destroying data synchronization between redundant systems
      Rootkit: Adversaries may deploy rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting and modifying operating-system API calls that supply system information. Rootkits or rootkit-enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system, or lower. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK January 2018)     Firmware rootkits that affect the operating system yield nearly full control of the system. While firmware rootkits are normally developed for the main processing board, they can also be developed for the I/O that is attached to an asset. Compromise of this firmware allows the modification of all of the process variables and functions the module engages in. This may result in commands being disregarded and false information being fed to the main device. By tampering with device processes, an adversary may inhibit its expected response functions and possibly enable [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105).
        ~Firmware Rootkit: Malicious code embedded in device firmware to modify core functionality and hide malicious activity at the lowest level
        ~Kernel-Level Rootkit: Rootkits that operate at the operating system kernel level to intercept and modify system calls
        ~Driver Rootkit: Malicious code masquerading as or modifying legitimate device drivers to control hardware interfaces
        ~I/O Module Rootkit: Specialized rootkits targeting industrial I/O modules to manipulate process signals and measurements
        ~PLC Rootkit: Rootkits specifically designed to compromise and hide malicious activity in programmable logic controllers
        ~Engineering Workstation Rootkit: Rootkits targeting engineering workstations to hide malicious configuration changes
        ~Protocol-Level Rootkit: Rootkits that manipulate industrial protocol communications to hide malicious traffic
        ~Memory-Based Rootkit: Rootkits that operate entirely in memory to avoid detection and persist across system reboots
      Service Stop: Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)  Services may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services in order to conduct Data Destruction. (Citation: Enterprise ATT&CK)
        ~Emergency Response System Shutdown: Stopping emergency response and safety monitoring services that handle critical alarms and notifications
        ~HMI Service Termination: Disabling Human Machine Interface services to prevent operator intervention and monitoring
        ~Safety Instrumented System (SIS) Deactivation: Stopping services related to safety instrumented systems that provide automated safety responses
        ~Historian Service Disruption: Terminating historian services that record process data and safety events
        ~Protection Relay Service Stop: Disabling protective relay services that prevent equipment damage
        ~Watchdog Timer Disruption: Stopping watchdog timer services that monitor system health and trigger safety responses
        ~Alarm Management System Shutdown: Terminating services responsible for processing and displaying critical alarms
        ~Backup System Service Stop: Disabling backup and failover services that provide redundancy during failures
        ~Quality Control Service Disruption: Stopping services that monitor and maintain product quality parameters
        ~Process Control Loop Service Stop: Disabling control loop services that maintain safe operating conditions
        ~Protocol Gateway Service Disruption: Stopping services that translate between different industrial protocols
        ~OPC Server Service Stop: Disabling OPC server services that facilitate data exchange between control systems
        ~Diagnostic Service Termination: Stopping system diagnostic services that monitor equipment health
        ~Time Synchronization Service Stop: Disabling services that maintain accurate timing across the ICS network
        ~Remote Access Service Disruption: Terminating services that enable remote monitoring and control
        ~Asset Management Service Stop: Disabling services that track and manage industrial assets
        ~Environmental Monitoring Service Stop: Terminating services that monitor facility conditions
        ~Load Balancing Service Disruption: Stopping services that distribute processing loads
        ~Redundancy Management Service Stop: Disabling services that manage failover and redundancy
        ~Configuration Management Service Stop: Terminating services that maintain device configurations
        ~Batch Management Service Stop: Disabling services that control batch processing operations
        ~Recipe Management Service Disruption: Stopping services that manage production recipes and formulas
        ~Material Handling Service Stop: Terminating automated material handling and conveyor control services
        ~Energy Management Service Disruption: Stopping services that optimize energy consumption
        ~Packaging Line Service Stop: Disabling services controlling automated packaging systems
        ~Laboratory Information System Stop: Terminating services that manage quality testing and lab data
        ~Supply Chain Integration Service Stop: Disabling services that coordinate with external supply systems
        ~Maintenance Management Service Stop: Terminating predictive and preventive maintenance services
        ~Industrial Network Management Stop: Disabling services that monitor and manage industrial network
        ~Regulatory Compliance Service Stop: Terminating services that ensure regulatory compliance monitoring
      Alarm Suppression: Adversaries may target protection function alarms to prevent them from notifying operators of critical conditions. Alarm messages may be a part of an overall reporting system and of particular interest for adversaries. Disruption of the alarm system does not imply the disruption of the reporting system as a whole.  A Secura presentation on targeting OT notes a dual fold goal for adversaries attempting alarm suppression: prevent outgoing alarms from being raised and prevent incoming alarms from being responded to. (Citation: Jos Wetzels, Marina Krotofil 2019) The method of suppression may greatly depend on the type of alarm in question:    * An alarm raised by a protocol message  * An alarm signaled with I/O  * An alarm bit set in a flag (and read)   In ICS environments, the adversary may have to suppress or contend with multiple alarms and/or alarm propagation to achieve a specific goal to evade detection or prevent intended responses from occurring. (Citation: Jos Wetzels, Marina Krotofil 2019)  Methods of suppression may involve tampering or altering device displays and logs, modifying in memory code to fixed values, or even tampering with assembly level instruction code.
        ~Protocol Message Manipulation: Adversaries modify or intercept protocol-level alarm messages to prevent them from reaching their intended recipients
        ~I/O Signal Tampering: Adversaries interfere with physical I/O signals used for alarm indication by modifying signal values or disrupting signal paths
        ~Flag Bit Modification: Adversaries alter memory locations or registers containing alarm status flags to prevent alarm conditions from being detected
        ~Display Interface Tampering: Adversaries modify HMI or other display interfaces to prevent alarm information from being shown to operators
        ~Memory Value Freezing: Adversaries force alarm-related memory values to remain at non-alarm states regardless of actual conditions
        ~Alarm Configuration Modification: Adversaries alter alarm thresholds or conditions in system configuration to prevent alarms from triggering
        ~Alarm Log Manipulation: Adversaries modify or delete alarm logs to hide evidence of alarm occurrences
        ~Communication Path Disruption: Adversaries block or redirect alarm communication paths to prevent alarm propagation
      Block Command Message: Adversaries may block a command message from reaching its intended target to prevent command execution. In OT networks, command messages are sent to provide instructions to control system devices. A blocked command message can inhibit response functions from correcting a disruption or unsafe condition. (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)  (Citation: Electricity Information Sharing and Analysis Center; SANS Industrial Control Systems March 2016)
        ~Network Traffic Filtering: Adversaries may implement network filtering rules to block specific command messages from reaching control devices
        ~Protocol Command Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious protocol commands to override or cancel legitimate command messages
        ~Communication Path Disruption: Adversaries may physically or logically disrupt communication paths between control systems and field devices
        ~Command Validation Tampering: Adversaries may modify command validation mechanisms to reject legitimate commands
        ~Message Queue Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate message queues to prevent command processing
        ~Command Timing Manipulation: Adversaries may alter timing parameters to cause commands to be ignored or dropped
        ~Gateway/Proxy Interference: Adversaries may compromise gateway devices to selectively block command messages
        ~Command Authentication Bypass: Adversaries may bypass authentication mechanisms to prevent command verification and execution
      Manipulate I/O Image: Adversaries may manipulate the I/O image of PLCs through various means to prevent them from functioning as expected. Methods of I/O image manipulation may include overriding the I/O table via direct memory manipulation or using the override function used for testing PLC programs. (Citation: Dr. Kelvin T. Erickson December 2010) During the scan cycle, a PLC reads the status of all inputs and stores them in an image table. (Citation: Nanjundaiah, Vaidyanath) The image table is the PLCs internal storage location where values of inputs/outputs for one scan are stored while it executes the user program. After the PLC has solved the entire logic program, it updates the output image table. The contents of this output image table are written to the corresponding output points in I/O Modules.   One of the unique characteristics of PLCs is their ability to override the status of a physical discrete input or to override the logic driving a physical output coil and force the output to a desired status.
        ~Direct Memory Override: Adversaries directly modify memory locations containing I/O image tables using memory manipulation techniques
        ~Force I/O Function Abuse: Adversaries exploit built-in PLC force I/O functions typically used for testing and maintenance
        ~Scan Cycle Injection: Adversaries inject malicious modifications during specific phases of the PLC scan cycle
        ~Input Status Manipulation: Adversaries specifically target and modify input status values in the image table
        ~Output Coil Override: Adversaries manipulate output coil status values to force specific output states
        ~Program Logic Modification: Adversaries modify PLC program logic to alter how I/O values are processed
        ~Communication Buffer Manipulation: Adversaries intercept and modify I/O values in communication buffers
        ~Runtime Table Modification: Adversaries alter runtime I/O tables during program execution
      Change Credential: Adversaries may modify software and device credentials to prevent operator and responder access. Depending on the device, the modification or addition of this password could prevent any device configuration actions from being accomplished and may require a factory reset or replacement of hardware. These credentials are often built-in features provided by the device vendors as a means to restrict access to management interfaces.  An adversary with access to valid or hardcoded credentials could change the credential to prevent future authorized device access. Change Credential may be especially damaging when paired with other techniques such as Modify Program, Data Destruction, or Modify Controller Tasking. In these cases, a device’s configuration may be destroyed or include malicious actions for the process environment, which cannot not be removed through normal device configuration actions.   Additionally, recovery of the device and original configuration may be difficult depending on the features provided by the device. In some cases, these passwords cannot be removed onsite and may require that the device be sent back to the vendor for additional recovery steps.   A chain of incidents occurred in Germany, where adversaries locked operators out of their building automation system (BAS) controllers by enabling a previously unset BCU key. (Citation: German BAS Lockout Dec 2021)
        ~Default Credential Modification: Modify default vendor-supplied credentials to prevent access to device configuration interfaces
        ~Authentication Key Manipulation: Enable or modify authentication keys (like BCU keys) to lock out legitimate users
        ~Password Hash Tampering: Modify stored password hashes in device firmware or configuration
        ~Multi-Factor Authentication Disruption: Alter MFA settings or tokens to prevent authorized access
        ~Emergency Access Credential Alteration: Modify emergency/backdoor access credentials used for recovery
        ~Configuration Interface Lockout: Change credentials specifically for configuration and management interfaces
        ~Remote Access Credential Modification: Target remote access authentication mechanisms
        ~Backup Configuration Credential Alteration: Modify credentials in backup configurations to prevent restore operations
        ~Firmware Update Authentication Tampering: Change credentials used for firmware update verification
        ~Service Account Credential Modification: Alter maintenance and service account credentials
        ~Protocol Authentication Parameter Modification: Alter credentials used in industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or Profinet
        ~Time-based Authentication Disruption: Modify time-synchronized credentials or token generation parameters
        ~Hardware Key Modification: Alter hardware-based authentication mechanisms like physical keys or dongles
        ~Role-Based Access Control Manipulation: Change credentials for specific operational roles or access levels
        ~Controller-to-Controller Authentication Tampering: Modify credentials used for inter-controller communication
        ~Historian Credential Modification: Alter authentication parameters for historical data access
        ~Engineering Workstation Access Control: Change credentials specific to engineering workstation access
        ~Safety System Authentication Tampering: Modify credentials for safety system access and configuration
      System Firmware: System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network.   An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers. (Citation: Basnight, Zachry, et al.)
        ~Malicious Firmware Update Package: Creating and distributing malicious firmware updates that appear legitimate
        ~Firmware Downgrade Attack: Force installing older, vulnerable versions of firmware to exploit known vulnerabilities
        ~Direct Memory Modification: Directly modifying firmware in memory without going through normal update procedures
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Intercepting and modifying legitimate firmware updates during distribution
        ~Remote Firmware Update Exploitation: Exploiting vulnerabilities in remote firmware update mechanisms
        ~Physical Firmware Reprogramming: Using specialized equipment to manually reprogram firmware through physical access
        ~Bootloader Manipulation: Modifying bootloader firmware to maintain persistence and control device startup
        ~Firmware Configuration Tampering: Altering firmware configuration parameters without full replacement
        ~Firmware Encryption Bypass: Circumventing firmware encryption mechanisms to inject malicious code
        ~Component-Specific Firmware Attack: Targeting specific system components' firmware (e.g., PLCs, RTUs, sensors)
        ~Firmware Signature Verification Bypass: Circumventing digital signature checks during firmware updates
        ~Hidden Firmware Partition Manipulation: Creating or modifying hidden firmware partitions to store malicious code
        ~Firmware Recovery Mode Abuse: Exploiting device recovery modes to install unauthorized firmware
        ~Cross-Device Firmware Injection: Using firmware intended for different device models to exploit compatibility checks
        ~Firmware Update Protocol Abuse: Exploiting weaknesses in firmware update protocols
        ~Partial Firmware Update: Modifying only specific portions of firmware to avoid detection
        ~Firmware Rollback Protection Bypass: Circumventing mechanisms that prevent unauthorized firmware downgrades
        ~Real-time Firmware Modification: Modifying firmware during device operation without requiring reboot
        ~Firmware Update Storage Manipulation: Tampering with stored firmware updates before installation
        ~External Device Firmware Attack: Using compromised external devices to deliver malicious firmware
        ~Firmware Backup Manipulation: Tampering with firmware backup mechanisms to inject malicious code during restore
        ~Hardware Security Module Bypass: Circumventing HSM-based firmware protection mechanisms
        ~Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA) Bitstream Manipulation: Modifying FPGA configurations through firmware
        ~Firmware Debug Interface Abuse: Exploiting debugging interfaces to modify firmware
        ~Vendor-Specific Update Tool Exploitation: Compromising vendor-provided firmware update tools
        ~Serial Flash Memory Manipulation: Directly modifying firmware stored in serial flash memory chips
        ~Firmware Load Address Manipulation: Modifying firmware load addresses to execute malicious code
        ~Industrial Protocol Firmware Update Exploitation: Abusing industrial protocols (e.g., HART, Profibus) for firmware updates
        ~Firmware Authentication Cache Poisoning: Manipulating cached firmware authentication results
        ~Out-of-Band Firmware Modification: Using alternative communication channels to modify firmware
        ~Firmware Version Spoofing: Masquerading malicious firmware as legitimate by manipulating version information
        ~Dual-Bank Firmware Attack: Exploiting dual-bank firmware update mechanisms in redundant systems
        ~Firmware Parameter Injection: Injecting malicious parameters during firmware initialization
        ~Network Boot Firmware Manipulation: Compromising network-boot firmware loading processes
        ~Time-of-Check-Time-of-Use Firmware Attack: Exploiting race conditions in firmware validation
        ~Cross-Firmware Component Attack: Exploiting interactions between different firmware components
        ~Firmware Update State Machine Manipulation: Interfering with the firmware update state machine
        ~Firmware Memory Layout Manipulation: Modifying firmware memory layout to execute unauthorized code
        ~Firmware Watchdog Bypass: Circumventing firmware watchdog mechanisms
        ~Distributed Firmware Attack: Coordinating firmware modifications across multiple system components
        ~Safety System Firmware Override: Manipulating firmware of safety instrumented systems (SIS)
        ~Redundant Controller Firmware Attack: Exploiting firmware update mechanisms in redundant controller setups
        ~Emergency Shutdown Firmware Manipulation: Modifying firmware handling emergency shutdown procedures
        ~Cross-Vendor Firmware Exploitation: Exploiting firmware compatibility between different vendor systems
        ~Firmware Recovery Mechanism Abuse: Exploiting automated firmware recovery features
        ~Real-time Operating System Firmware Attack: Targeting RTOS-specific firmware components
        ~Firmware Diagnostic Mode Exploitation: Abusing diagnostic modes to modify firmware
        ~Historical Data Firmware Manipulation: Modifying firmware components handling historical data storage
        ~Firmware Synchronization Attack: Exploiting firmware synchronization in redundant systems
        ~Failsafe Firmware Override: Manipulating firmware failsafe mechanisms
      Activate Firmware Update Mode: Adversaries may activate firmware update mode on devices to prevent expected response functions from engaging in reaction to an emergency or process malfunction. For example, devices such as protection relays may have an operation mode designed for firmware installation. This mode may halt process monitoring and related functions to allow new firmware to be loaded. A device left in update mode may be placed in an inactive holding state if no firmware is provided to it. By entering and leaving a device in this mode, the adversary may deny its usual functionalities.
        ~Protection Relay Update Mode: Targeting protection relay devices by activating their firmware update modes to prevent protective functions from operating
        ~PLC Update State: Exploiting programmable logic controller firmware update states to disable normal control operations
        ~Remote Terminal Unit Maintenance: Activating maintenance/update modes in RTUs to prevent normal monitoring and control functions
        ~Safety Controller Update: Targeting safety instrumented system controllers through their firmware update mechanisms
        ~Network Device Update Mode: Exploiting firmware update modes in industrial network devices like switches and gateways
        ~HMI System Update State: Triggering update modes in Human Machine Interface systems to prevent operator control
        ~Sensor Calibration Mode: Exploiting sensor calibration and update modes to prevent accurate readings and responses
        ~Drive Controller Update: Targeting variable frequency drives and motor controllers through their update mechanisms
        ~Device Configuration Port: Exploiting physical configuration ports used for firmware updates
        ~Update Mode Persistence: Techniques to maintain devices in update mode without completing the update process
        ~Bootloader Mode Exploitation: Forcing devices into bootloader mode to prevent normal operation and response functions
        ~Wireless Update Interface: Exploiting wireless firmware update capabilities in modern ICS devices
        ~Supply Chain Update Mode: Leveraging legitimate firmware update channels to activate update modes
        ~Update Authentication Bypass: Exploiting authentication mechanisms in firmware update processes
        ~Serial Interface Update: Utilizing serial/maintenance ports to trigger firmware update modes
        ~Multiple Device Update: Coordinated activation of update modes across multiple devices
        ~Update Mode Timer Manipulation: Exploiting timeout mechanisms in firmware update processes
        ~Emergency Update Override: Exploiting emergency firmware update features that bypass normal safeguards
        ~Recovery Mode Trigger: Exploiting device recovery modes that are designed for firmware recovery but can prevent normal operation
        ~Redundancy System Update: Exploiting firmware update modes in redundant systems to prevent failover capabilities
        ~Remote Update Service: Exploiting remote firmware update services and mechanisms
        ~Protocol-Specific Update Mode: Leveraging industrial protocol-specific firmware update features (e.g., PROFINET, EtherNet/IP)
        ~Backup Configuration Update: Exploiting backup configuration loading mechanisms that trigger update-like states
      Block Serial COM: Adversaries may block access to serial COM to prevent instructions or configurations from reaching target devices. Serial Communication ports (COM) allow communication with control system devices. Devices can receive command and configuration messages over such serial COM. Devices also use serial COM to send command and reporting messages. Blocking device serial COM may also block command messages and block reporting messages.   A serial to Ethernet converter is often connected to a serial COM to facilitate communication between serial and Ethernet devices. One approach to blocking a serial COM would be to create and hold open a TCP session with the Ethernet side of the converter. A serial to Ethernet converter may have a few ports open to facilitate multiple communications. For example, if there are three serial COM available -- 1, 2 and 3 --, the converter might be listening on the corresponding ports 20001, 20002, and 20003. If a TCP/IP connection is opened with one of these ports and held open, then the port will be unavailable for use by another party. One way the adversary could achieve this would be to initiate a TCP session with the serial to Ethernet converter at 10.0.0.1 via Telnet on serial port 1 with the following command: telnet 10.0.0.1 20001.
        ~TCP Session Holding: Create and maintain TCP connections to serial-to-Ethernet converter ports to prevent legitimate access
        ~Serial Port Flooding: Continuously send malformed or excessive data to saturate serial COM buffers
        ~Protocol Manipulation: Modify serial communication protocols to prevent proper message handling
        ~Physical Port Blocking: Physically disconnect or damage serial COM ports
        ~Driver Manipulation: Modify or disable device drivers handling serial communications
        ~Configuration Override: Change serial port configurations to prevent proper communication
        ~Message Queue Saturation: Fill device message queues to prevent processing of legitimate commands
        ~Timing Attack: Exploit timing-sensitive aspects of serial communications to cause failures
        ~Port Resource Exhaustion: Consume all available serial port resources
        ~Communication Rate Manipulation: Alter baud rates or timing parameters to disrupt communication
        ~Serial-to-Wireless Gateway Interference: Disrupt wireless bridges used for serial communication
        ~Multiplexer Manipulation: Tamper with serial port multiplexers to prevent proper routing
        ~Signal Line Interference: Generate electromagnetic interference to corrupt serial communications
        ~Terminal Server Exploitation: Compromise terminal servers that manage serial connections
        ~Address Resolution Spoofing: Manipulate address resolution for serial-to-network conversions
        ~Redundant Link Disruption: Block backup serial communication paths
        ~Protocol Conversion Gateway Attack: Interfere with protocol converters between different serial standards
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Modify watchdog timers that monitor serial communication health
        ~Serial Diagnostic Port Blocking: Prevent access to diagnostic and maintenance serial ports
      Device Restart/Shutdown: Adversaries may forcibly restart or shutdown a device in an ICS environment to disrupt and potentially negatively impact physical processes. Methods of device restart and shutdown exist in some devices as built-in, standard functionalities. These functionalities can be executed using interactive device web interfaces, CLIs, and network protocol commands.  Unexpected restart or shutdown of control system devices may prevent expected response functions happening during critical states.  A device restart can also be a sign of malicious device modifications, as many updates require a shutdown in order to take effect.
        ~Web Interface Manipulation: Adversaries exploit device web management interfaces to initiate unauthorized restarts or shutdowns
        ~CLI Command Injection: Adversaries use command-line interface access to execute restart/shutdown commands
        ~Protocol-Specific Commands: Adversaries leverage industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, DNP3) to send restart/shutdown commands
        ~Physical Button Simulation: Adversaries simulate physical button presses through software/firmware manipulation
        ~Scheduled Task Abuse: Adversaries manipulate device maintenance schedules to trigger unauthorized restarts
        ~Power Management Interface Exploitation: Adversaries exploit power management features to force device shutdowns
        ~Firmware Update Trigger: Adversaries initiate fake firmware updates to force device restarts
        ~Remote Management Protocol Abuse: Adversaries exploit remote management protocols to execute restart commands
        ~Safety System Override: Adversaries bypass safety interlocks to force emergency shutdowns
        ~Resource Exhaustion: Adversaries deliberately exhaust system resources to trigger automatic restarts
        ~Hypervisor Control Channel Abuse: Adversaries exploit virtualization management interfaces to restart virtual ICS components
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Adversaries tamper with watchdog timer settings to trigger automatic restarts
        ~Configuration Change Trigger: Adversaries make malicious configuration changes requiring device restarts
        ~Network Stack Manipulation: Adversaries exploit network stack vulnerabilities to force device reboots
        ~Temperature Control Tampering: Adversaries manipulate temperature monitoring systems to trigger thermal shutdowns
        ~Vendor-Specific Tool Abuse: Adversaries misuse legitimate vendor maintenance tools to initiate restarts
        ~Battery Management System Exploitation: Adversaries manipulate UPS/battery systems to force shutdowns
        ~Memory Management Exploitation: Adversaries trigger memory-related issues forcing system restarts
        ~Time Synchronization Attack: Adversaries manipulate time synchronization to trigger time-based restarts
    impair-process-control: The adversary is trying to manipulate, disable, or damage physical control processes.  Impair Process Control consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt control logic and cause determinantal effects to processes being controlled in the target environment. Targets of interest may include active procedures or parameters that manipulate the physical environment. These techniques can also include prevention or manipulation of reporting elements and control logic. If an adversary has modified process functionality, then they may also obfuscate the results, which are often self-revealing in their impact on the outcome of a product or the environment. The direct physical control these techniques exert may also threaten the safety of operators and downstream users, which can prompt response mechanisms. Adversaries may follow up with or use [Inhibit Response Function](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0107) techniques in tandem, to assist with the successful abuse of control processes to result in [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105).
      Unauthorized Command Message: Adversaries may send unauthorized command messages to instruct control system assets to perform actions outside of their intended functionality, or without the logical preconditions to trigger their expected function. Command messages are used in ICS networks to give direct instructions to control systems devices. If an adversary can send an unauthorized command message to a control system, then it can instruct the control systems device to perform an action outside the normal bounds of the device's actions. An adversary could potentially instruct a control systems device to perform an action that will cause an [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105). (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)  In the Dallas Siren incident, adversaries were able to send command messages to activate tornado alarm systems across the city without an impending tornado or other disaster. (Citation: Zack Whittaker April 2017) (Citation: Benjamin Freed March 2019)
        ~Protocol Command Injection: Adversaries may inject unauthorized commands using specific industrial protocols like Modbus, DNP3, or EtherNet/IP to manipulate control system behavior
        ~Command Message Replay: Adversaries may capture legitimate command messages and replay them at inappropriate times to cause unintended system actions
        ~Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may modify parameters within otherwise legitimate command messages to cause systems to operate outside safe boundaries
        ~Authentication Bypass: Adversaries may bypass command authentication mechanisms to send unauthorized commands to control systems
        ~Remote Command Injection: Adversaries may exploit remote access capabilities to send unauthorized commands from external networks
        ~Malformed Command Messages: Adversaries may send deliberately malformed command messages to exploit parsing vulnerabilities in control system devices
        ~Physical Interface Command Injection: Adversaries may connect directly to physical interfaces like serial ports or USB connections to send unauthorized commands
        ~Man-in-the-Middle Command Manipulation: Adversaries may intercept and modify legitimate command messages in transit between control systems
        ~Time-Based Command Injection: Adversaries may exploit timing windows or race conditions to inject unauthorized commands between legitimate control operations
        ~Engineering Workstation Exploitation: Adversaries may compromise engineering workstations to send unauthorized commands using legitimate software tools
      Modify Control Logic: Adversaries may place malicious code in a system, which can cause the system to malfunction by modifying its control logic. Control system devices use programming languages (e.g. relay ladder logic) to control physical processes by affecting actuators, which cause machines to operate, based on environment sensor readings. These devices often include the ability to perform remote control logic updates.   Program code is normally edited in a vendor-specific Integrated Development Environment (IDE) that relies on proprietary tools and features. These IDEs allow an engineer to perform host target development and may have the ability to run the code on the machine it is programmed for. The IDE will transmit the control logic to the testing device, and will perform the required device-specific functions to apply the changes and make them active.  An adversary may attempt to use this host target IDE to modify device control logic. Even though proprietary tools are often used to edit and update control logic, the process can usually be reverse-engineered and reproduced with open-source tools.  An adversary can de-calibrate a sensor by removing functions in control logic that account for sensor error. This can be used to change a control process without actually spoofing command messages to a controller or device.   It is believed this process happened in the lesser known over-pressurizer attacks build into Stuxnet. Pressure sensors are not perfect at translating pressure into an analog output signal, but their errors can be corrected by calibration. The pressure controller can be told what the “real” pressure is for given analog signals and then automatically linearize the measurement to what would be the “real” pressure. If the linearization is overwritten by malicious code on the S7-417 controller, analog pressure readings will be “corrected” during the attack by the pressure controller, which then interprets all analog pressure readings as perfectly normal pressure no matter how high or low their analog values are. The pressure controller then acts accordingly by never opening the stage exhaust valves. In the meantime, actual pressure keeps rising. (Citation: Stuxnet - Langner - 201311)  In the Maroochy Attack, Vitek Boden gained remote computer access to the control system and altered data so that whatever function should have occurred at affected pumping stations did not occur or occurred in a different way. The software program installed in the laptop was one developed by Hunter Watertech for its use in changing configurations in the PDS computers. This ultimately led to 800,000 liters of raw sewage being spilled out into the community. (Citation: Maroochy - MITRE - 200808)
        ~Sensor Calibration Manipulation: Modify control logic functions that handle sensor calibration and error correction to cause misreadings
        ~Logic Program Replacement: Complete replacement of existing control logic programs with malicious versions
        ~Function Block Modification: Alter specific function blocks within control logic to change system behavior
        ~Remote Logic Update Exploitation: Abuse legitimate remote update capabilities to deploy malicious logic
        ~IDE/Engineering Software Abuse: Leverage vendor-specific development environments to modify control logic
        ~Runtime Logic Manipulation: Modify control logic during system runtime without full program replacement
        ~Ladder Logic Injection: Insert malicious ladder logic code into existing programs
        ~Control Parameter Modification: Change critical control parameters within logic programs
        ~Safety Function Bypass: Modify logic responsible for safety interlocks and protective functions
        ~Timing Logic Manipulation: Alter timing-related logic to disrupt synchronized processes
        ~Protocol Command Modification: Alter logic handling of industrial protocol commands
        ~State Machine Manipulation: Modify state transition logic in programmable controllers
        ~Feedback Loop Interference: Modify PID controller logic and feedback mechanisms
        ~Cross-Controller Logic Tampering: Modify logic controlling interactions between multiple controllers
        ~Sequence Logic Corruption: Alter sequential logic patterns in batch processes
        ~Memory Buffer Manipulation: Modify control logic by manipulating memory buffers used for process variables
        ~Firmware Logic Injection: Insert malicious logic during firmware update processes
        ~Equipment-Specific Logic Tampering: Target logic specific to specialized industrial equipment types
        ~Variable Table Manipulation: Modify variable tables and data blocks used by control logic
      Brute Force I/O: Adversaries may repetitively or successively change I/O point values to perform an action. Brute Force I/O may be achieved by changing either a range of I/O point values or a single point value repeatedly to manipulate a process function. The adversary's goal and the information they have about the target environment will influence which of the options they choose. In the case of brute forcing a range of point values, the adversary may be able to achieve an impact without targeting a specific point. In the case where a single point is targeted, the adversary may be able to generate instability on the process function associated with that particular point.   Adversaries may use Brute Force I/O to cause failures within various industrial processes. These failures could be the result of wear on equipment or damage to downstream equipment.
        ~Range Scanning: Systematically modify values across a range of I/O points to identify vulnerable process controls or cause system instability
        ~Single Point Flooding: Repeatedly modify a specific I/O point value at high frequency to cause process disruption
        ~Threshold Testing: Incrementally adjust I/O values until reaching operational thresholds that could trigger system failures
        ~Timing-based Manipulation: Modify I/O points with specific timing patterns to exploit process control timing dependencies
        ~Sequential Point Testing: Systematically test combinations of multiple I/O points to identify critical control interactions
        ~Boundary Value Attack: Target I/O points with values at or beyond their specified operational limits
        ~Process Variable Oscillation: Create rapid oscillations in process variables through I/O manipulation
        ~Control Loop Interference: Repeatedly modify I/O values to disrupt PID and other control loop functions
        ~Sensor Input Flooding: Overwhelm sensor inputs with rapid value changes to cause erratic system behavior
        ~State Transition Forcing: Repeatedly trigger state changes through I/O manipulation to wear down equipment
        ~Batch Process Disruption: Repeatedly modify I/O values during batch processing operations to corrupt product quality
        ~Feedback Loop Saturation: Continuously inject extreme values to saturate feedback control mechanisms
        ~Dead Band Exploitation: Manipulate I/O values within system dead bands to accumulate subtle process deviations
        ~Multi-Point Correlation: Simultaneously manipulate multiple related I/O points to exploit process dependencies
        ~Ramp Rate Abuse: Exploit rate-of-change limitations in control systems through rapid value modifications
        ~Historical Data Poisoning: Repeatedly trigger I/O changes to corrupt historical process data collection
        ~Safety Interlock Testing: Systematically probe safety interlock I/O points to identify bypass opportunities
        ~Calibration Drift Forcing: Induce calibration errors through repeated boundary value modifications
        ~Process Hold Manipulation: Exploit process hold states through repeated I/O modifications
        ~Signal Quality Degradation: Repeatedly modify I/O values to degrade signal quality and sensor readings
        ~Redundancy Switch Forcing: Repeatedly trigger I/O changes to force failover between redundant systems
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Systematically interfere with watchdog timer I/O signals
        ~Remote I/O Flooding: Overwhelm remote I/O modules with high-frequency value changes
        ~Analog-Digital Conversion Stress: Exploit A/D converter limitations through rapid value changes
        ~Field Device Reset Triggering: Force field device resets through excessive I/O operations
        ~Communication Buffer Overflow: Overwhelm I/O communication buffers with rapid value changes
        ~Engineering Unit Conversion Attack: Exploit unit conversion calculations through boundary value manipulation
        ~Time Synchronization Disruption: Interfere with time-stamped I/O operations to cause synchronization issues
        ~Alarm System Flooding: Overwhelm alarm systems through mass I/O value modifications
        ~Network Segment Saturation: Saturate network segments with excessive I/O requests
      Modify Parameter: Adversaries may modify parameters used to instruct industrial control system devices. These devices operate via programs that dictate how and when to perform actions based on such parameters. Such parameters can determine the extent to which an action is performed and may specify additional options. For example, a program on a control system device dictating motor processes may take a parameter defining the total number of seconds to run that motor.        An adversary can potentially modify these parameters to produce an outcome outside of what was intended by the operators. By modifying system and process critical parameters, the adversary may cause [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0105) to equipment and/or control processes. Modified parameters may be turned into dangerous, out-of-bounds, or unexpected values from typical operations. For example, specifying that a process run for more or less time than it should, or dictating an unusually high, low, or invalid value as a parameter.
        ~Timing Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify time-based parameters like cycle times, delays, or execution intervals to disrupt process timing and synchronization
        ~Threshold Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter safety or operational thresholds that trigger alarms or protective actions
        ~Setpoint Manipulation: Adversaries change process setpoints that control operating conditions like temperature, pressure, or flow rates
        ~Configuration Parameter Tampering: Adversaries modify device configuration parameters that affect how equipment interprets or responds to control signals
        ~Calibration Value Modification: Adversaries alter sensor calibration parameters to cause misreading of process conditions
        ~Control Loop Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries modify PID controller parameters to destabilize control loops
        ~Range Limit Modification: Adversaries change acceptable operating ranges to allow dangerous operating conditions
        ~State Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters that define equipment states or operating modes
        ~Communication Parameter Tampering: Adversaries modify parameters affecting how devices communicate on the control network
        ~Safety Interlock Parameter Modification: Adversaries change parameters that control safety interlocks and protective functions
        ~Batch Process Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters that control batch processing sequences and recipes
        ~Motion Control Parameter Tampering: Adversaries alter parameters related to servo motors, actuators, and motion control systems
        ~Historical Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify stored or archived parameter values to affect future operations
        ~Parameter Modification via Engineering Workstation: Adversaries exploit engineering software to modify parameters
        ~Remote Access Parameter Modification: Adversaries leverage remote access capabilities to modify parameters
        ~Firmware Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters stored in device firmware
        ~Temporary Parameter Override: Adversaries temporarily modify parameters during specific operations
        ~Sequence Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters that control sequential logic and process steps
        ~Environmental Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters related to environmental controls like HVAC
        ~Quality Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters that affect product quality measurements
        ~Chemical Process Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters specific to chemical processing like reaction rates and chemical feed rates
        ~Power Generation Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling power generation equipment like turbines and generators
        ~Cascading Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify multiple related parameters to create compound effects
        ~Maintenance Mode Parameter Modification: Adversaries exploit maintenance mode to modify normally locked parameters
        ~Load-Based Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters that adjust based on system load or demand
        ~Redundancy Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling failover and redundant systems
        ~Startup/Shutdown Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters specific to system startup or shutdown sequences
        ~Emergency Response Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters that control emergency response systems
        ~Product Specification Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters that define product specifications and tolerances
        ~Feedback Loop Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters that control system feedback mechanisms
        ~Water Treatment Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling water treatment processes like filtration and chemical dosing
        ~Manufacturing Line Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling assembly line operations and robotics
        ~Gradual Parameter Modification: Adversaries slowly modify parameters over time to avoid immediate detection
        ~Oil and Gas Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling pipeline operations and processing equipment
        ~Multi-Site Parameter Modification: Adversaries coordinate parameter modifications across multiple facilities
        ~Material Handling Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling conveyor systems and material transport
        ~Building Management System Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling facility infrastructure
        ~Energy Distribution Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling power distribution and switching
        ~Resource Allocation Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling resource distribution
        ~Alarm Management Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling alarm systems and notifications
        ~Asset Management Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling equipment health monitoring and maintenance scheduling
        ~Advanced Process Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters in model predictive control systems
        ~Supply Chain Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters affecting supply chain and logistics systems integration
        ~Custom Protocol Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters in proprietary or custom industrial protocols
        ~Laboratory Information System Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling quality testing and lab analysis
        ~Emissions Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling environmental monitoring and emissions
        ~Inventory Control Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters affecting automated inventory and storage systems
        ~Process Optimization Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters controlling efficiency and optimization algorithms
        ~Scheduling Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify parameters controlling production scheduling and planning
        ~Compliance Monitoring Parameter Modification: Adversaries alter parameters related to regulatory compliance monitoring
      Change Program State: Adversaries may attempt to change the state of the current program on a control device. Program state changes may be used to allow for another program to take over control or be loaded onto the device.
        ~Modify Runtime Configuration: Adversary changes configuration parameters during program execution to alter behavior
        ~Force Program Stop: Adversary forces the running program to terminate or enter a stopped state
        ~Program Download Manipulation: Adversary interferes with program download processes to inject malicious code
        ~State Transition Manipulation: Adversary exploits state transition mechanisms to force unintended program states
        ~Memory Manipulation: Adversary directly modifies program memory to alter execution state
        ~Program Mode Change: Adversary switches between different operational modes (e.g., run, program, test)
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Adversary interferes with watchdog timers to affect program state monitoring
        ~Backup Program Manipulation: Adversary modifies backup program states to affect failover behavior
        ~Program Counter Manipulation: Adversary alters program counter to redirect execution flow
        ~State Variable Modification: Adversary changes critical state variables to affect program behavior
        ~Program Initialization Manipulation: Adversary interferes with program startup sequences or initialization routines
        ~State Preservation Attack: Adversary corrupts or modifies program state save/restore mechanisms
        ~Program Version Manipulation: Adversary forces program to run in incompatible or vulnerable version states
        ~State Synchronization Interference: Adversary disrupts synchronization between redundant program instances
        ~Forced Program Reset: Adversary triggers unauthorized program resets to achieve desired state
        ~Program State Feedback Loop: Adversary manipulates feedback mechanisms that maintain program state
        ~State Retention Memory Attack: Adversary modifies non-volatile memory storing program states
        ~Real-time Clock Manipulation: Adversary alters system time to affect time-dependent program states
        ~Protocol State Manipulation: Adversary exploits industrial protocol state machines to force unintended states
        ~Operator Interface State Attack: Adversary manipulates HMI/SCADA interface states to affect program behavior
        ~Emergency State Exploitation: Adversary forces program into or out of emergency operational states
        ~Maintenance Mode Exploitation: Adversary manipulates maintenance/diagnostic state transitions
        ~Interlock State Bypass: Adversary modifies program states to bypass safety interlocks
        ~Process Sequence Manipulation: Adversary alters sequential program states in batch processes
        ~State Recovery Prevention: Adversary blocks program from returning to normal operational state
      Spoof Reporting Message: Adversaries may spoof reporting messages in control system environments for evasion and to impair process control. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. Reporting messages are important for monitoring the normal operation of a system or identifying important events such as deviations from expected values.   If an adversary has the ability to Spoof Reporting Messages, they can impact the control system in many ways. The adversary can Spoof Reporting Messages that state that the process is operating normally, as a form of evasion. The adversary could also Spoof Reporting Messages to make the defenders and operators think that other errors are occurring in order to distract them from the actual source of a problem. (Citation: Bonnie Zhu, Anthony Joseph, Shankar Sastry 2011)
        ~Protocol-Specific Spoofing: Adversaries may exploit specific industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, DNP3, OPC) to inject false reporting messages
        ~Historian Data Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate historical data records to present false trends or process states
        ~HMI Display Spoofing: Adversaries may alter Human Machine Interface displays to show incorrect process values while masking real conditions
        ~Sensor Data Manipulation: Adversaries may intercept and modify sensor readings before they reach monitoring systems
        ~Alarm System Spoofing: Adversaries may suppress legitimate alarms or generate false alarms to confuse operators
        ~Real-time Data Stream Injection: Adversaries may inject false data into real-time monitoring streams
        ~Report File Manipulation: Adversaries may modify stored report files to hide evidence of system manipulation
        ~Network Traffic Replay: Adversaries may capture and replay normal operation traffic to mask malicious activities
        ~Gateway/Proxy Manipulation: Adversaries may compromise communication gateways to alter messages between different network segments
        ~Time Synchronization Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate time stamps in reporting messages to create confusion or hide activities
        ~Configuration File Tampering: Adversaries may modify configuration files that define reporting parameters and thresholds
        ~Message Queue Manipulation: Adversaries may intercept and modify messages in industrial protocol message queues
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Spoofing: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate RTUs to send false field data
        ~Engineering Workstation Spoofing: Adversaries may compromise engineering workstations to generate false maintenance reports
        ~Backup System Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate backup system reports to mask system state changes
        ~Cross-Protocol Translation Tampering: Adversaries may exploit protocol conversion points to inject false data
        ~Aggregation Point Manipulation: Adversaries may compromise data aggregation points to alter multiple data streams simultaneously
        ~Field Device Emulation: Adversaries may emulate field devices to generate false telemetry data
        ~Safety System Report Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate safety system status reports to mask unsafe conditions
        ~Compliance Report Manipulation: Adversaries may alter automated regulatory compliance reporting data
        ~Batch Process Report Spoofing: Adversaries may modify batch processing reports to hide quality issues
        ~Equipment Health Monitoring Spoofing: Adversaries may falsify equipment condition monitoring reports
        ~Environmental Monitoring System Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate environmental compliance and monitoring reports
        ~Supply Chain Integration Spoofing: Adversaries may alter automated reporting between integrated supply chain systems
        ~Quality Control System Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate quality control testing and verification reports
      Module Firmware: Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment.     This technique is similar to [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0857), but is conducted on other system components that may not have the same capabilities or level of integrity checking. Although it results in a device re-image, malicious device firmware may provide persistent access to remaining devices. (Citation: Daniel Peck,  Dale Peterson January 2009)    An easy point of access for an adversary is the Ethernet card, which may have its own CPU, RAM, and operating system. The adversary may attack and likely exploit the computer on an Ethernet card. Exploitation of the Ethernet card computer may enable the adversary to accomplish additional attacks, such as the following: (Citation: Daniel Peck,  Dale Peterson January 2009)    * Delayed Attack - The adversary may stage an attack in advance and choose when to launch it, such as at a particularly damaging time.   * Brick the Ethernet Card - Malicious firmware may be programmed to result in an Ethernet card failure, requiring a factory return.   * Random Attack or Failure - The adversary may load malicious firmware onto multiple field devices. Execution of an attack and the time it occurs is generated by a pseudo-random number generator.    * A Field Device Worm - The adversary may choose to identify all field devices of the same model, with the end goal of performing a device-wide compromise.   * Attack Other Cards on the Field Device - Although it is not the most important module in a field device, the Ethernet card is most accessible to the adversary and malware. Compromise of the Ethernet card may provide a more direct route to compromising other modules, such as the CPU module.
        ~Ethernet Card Firmware Manipulation: Targeting the network interface card's firmware to establish persistence and enable further attacks
        ~CPU Module Firmware Compromise: Directly targeting the main processing unit's firmware to control device operations
        ~Memory Module Manipulation: Modifying firmware of memory modules to enable data manipulation or persistence
        ~I/O Module Firmware Attack: Compromising input/output module firmware to manipulate sensor data or control signals
        ~Communication Module Exploitation: Targeting communication module firmware to intercept or modify industrial protocols
        ~Power Supply Module Firmware: Manipulating power management firmware to cause physical damage or operational disruption
        ~Field Device Controller Firmware: Targeting programmable logic controller (PLC) or remote terminal unit (RTU) specific firmware
        ~Backup Module Compromise: Attacking backup or redundancy module firmware to prevent failover mechanisms
        ~Human Machine Interface (HMI) Module: Compromising display or interface module firmware to mislead operators
        ~Firmware Update Mechanism Abuse: Exploiting legitimate firmware update processes to install malicious firmware
        ~Safety Instrumented System Firmware: Targeting firmware of safety-critical modules to disable or bypass protection mechanisms
        ~Protocol Translation Module: Compromising firmware of protocol gateway modules that bridge different industrial networks
        ~Time Synchronization Module: Attacking firmware of modules responsible for precision timing and synchronization
        ~Diagnostic Module Firmware: Targeting maintenance and diagnostic module firmware to hide system state
        ~Remote Access Module: Compromising firmware of remote access or management modules
        ~Redundancy Control Module: Targeting firmware of modules managing failover and redundancy operations
        ~Field Bus Interface Module: Attacking firmware of modules handling field bus communications
        ~Motion Control Module: Compromising firmware of modules controlling precise mechanical movements
        ~Analog/Digital Conversion Module: Targeting firmware of modules responsible for signal conversion and processing
        ~Environmental Monitoring Module: Compromising firmware of modules that monitor temperature, humidity, or other environmental factors
        ~Data Acquisition Module: Attacking firmware of modules dedicated to gathering and processing sensor data
        ~Custom Protocol Module: Targeting firmware of modules implementing proprietary or custom industrial protocols
        ~Process Control Module: Compromising firmware of modules specifically designed for process control algorithms
    impact: The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, or destroy your ICS systems, data, and their surrounding environment.  Impact consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt, compromise, destroy, and manipulate the integrity and availability of control system operations, processes, devices, and data. These techniques encompass the influence and effects resulting from adversarial efforts to attack the ICS environment or that tangentially impact it. Impact techniques can result in more instantaneous disruption to control processes and the operator, or may result in more long term damage or loss to the ICS environment and related operations. The adversary may leverage [Impair Process Control](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0106) techniques, which often manifest in more self-revealing impacts on operations, or [Impair Process Control](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0106) techniques to hinder safeguards and alarms in order to follow through with and provide cover for Impact. In some scenarios, control system processes can appear to function as expected, but may have been altered to benefit the adversary’s goal over the course of a longer duration. These techniques might be used by adversaries to follow through on their end goal or to provide cover for a confidentiality breach.  [Loss of Productivity and Revenue](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0828), [Theft of Operational Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0882), and [Damage to Property](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0879) are meant to encompass some of the more granular goals of adversaries in targeted and untargeted attacks. These techniques in and of themselves are not necessarily detectable, but the associated adversary behavior can potentially be mitigated and/or detected.
      Loss of Availability: Adversaries may attempt to disrupt essential components or systems to prevent owner and operator from delivering products or services. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)   Adversaries may leverage malware to delete or encrypt critical data on HMIs, workstations, or databases.  In the 2021 Colonial Pipeline ransomware incident, pipeline operations were temporally halted on May 7th and were not fully restarted until May 12th. (Citation: Colonial Pipeline Company May 2021)
        ~Data Destruction: Adversaries may delete or corrupt critical operational data, configurations, and programs on ICS systems to cause loss of availability
        ~Network Denial of Service: Adversaries may overwhelm network infrastructure or services to disrupt communications between ICS components
        ~Resource Consumption: Adversaries may execute processes that consume excessive system resources to degrade or prevent normal system operations
        ~Service Stop: Adversaries may stop or disable critical ICS services to prevent system functionality
        ~System Shutdown/Reboot: Adversaries may force unauthorized shutdowns or reboots of ICS components
        ~Hardware Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate hardware settings or configurations to cause system failures
        ~Database Manipulation: Adversaries may corrupt or modify operational databases to prevent access to critical data
        ~Firmware Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt device firmware to render ICS components inoperable
        ~Communication Interface Disruption: Adversaries may interfere with communication interfaces between ICS components
        ~Process Manipulation: Adversaries may alter process parameters or states to cause system unavailability
        ~Time Source Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate system time sources to disrupt time-sensitive operations and cause loss of availability
        ~Supply Chain Component Manipulation: Adversaries may compromise supply chain components to cause system unavailability when deployed
        ~Physical Component Interference: Adversaries may cause physical interference to ICS components through environmental controls
        ~Backup Prevention: Adversaries may disable or corrupt backup systems to prevent recovery from availability losses
        ~Authentication System Disruption: Adversaries may manipulate authentication systems to prevent legitimate access to ICS components
        ~Safety System Impairment: Adversaries may disable or modify safety systems to force operational shutdowns
        ~Remote Access Disruption: Adversaries may block remote access capabilities to prevent operators from managing systems
        ~Historical Data Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt historical data stores to prevent trend analysis and system optimization
        ~Redundancy System Interference: Adversaries may prevent failover to redundant systems by manipulating redundancy mechanisms
        ~HMI Application Disruption: Adversaries may specifically target HMI applications to prevent operators from monitoring and controlling processes
        ~Monitoring System Unavailability: Adversaries may disable or corrupt monitoring systems to prevent detection of process anomalies
        ~Engineering Workstation Lockout: Adversaries may prevent access to engineering workstations to block system modifications and maintenance
        ~Control Logic Unavailability: Adversaries may make control logic inaccessible to prevent process control modifications
      Loss of Protection: Adversaries may compromise protective system functions designed to prevent the effects of faults and abnormal conditions. This can result in equipment damage, prolonged process disruptions and hazards to personnel.   Many faults and abnormal conditions in process control happen too quickly for a human operator to react to. Speed is critical in correcting these conditions to limit serious impacts such as Loss of Control and Property Damage.   Adversaries may target and disable protective system functions as a prerequisite to subsequent attack execution or to allow for future faults and abnormal conditions to go unchecked. Detection of a Loss of Protection by operators can result in the shutdown of a process due to strict policies regarding protection systems. This can cause a Loss of Productivity and Revenue and may meet the technical goals of adversaries seeking to cause process disruptions.
        ~Safety Instrumented System Manipulation: Adversaries may modify SIS logic, parameters, or configurations to prevent proper safety responses during hazardous conditions
        ~Protection Relay Tampering: Adversaries may alter settings or disable electrical protection relays that guard against overcurrent, overvoltage, or other electrical faults
        ~Emergency Shutdown Override: Adversaries may compromise emergency shutdown systems to prevent automatic shutdown during dangerous conditions
        ~Interlock Bypass: Adversaries may disable or modify equipment interlocking systems that prevent dangerous equipment interactions or sequences
        ~Alarm System Suppression: Adversaries may suppress or disable alarm systems that alert operators to abnormal or dangerous conditions
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Adversaries may disable or modify watchdog timers that monitor and reset failed system components
        ~Safety Parameter Modification: Adversaries may alter critical safety parameters like pressure limits, temperature thresholds, or flow restrictions
        ~Redundancy System Compromise: Adversaries may disable redundant safety systems to remove backup protection mechanisms
        ~Safety Logic Deletion: Adversaries may delete or corrupt safety logic programs in safety PLCs or controllers
        ~Protection Device Communication Disruption: Adversaries may interrupt communications between protection devices and control systems
        ~Physical Barrier Control Defeat: Adversaries may disable electronic locks, gates, or other physical protection systems that prevent unauthorized access
        ~Fire Suppression System Compromise: Adversaries may disable or modify fire detection and suppression systems
        ~Environmental Control Bypass: Adversaries may compromise HVAC or atmospheric control systems that protect sensitive equipment
        ~Personnel Safety Interlock Defeat: Adversaries may disable light curtains, pressure mats, or other personnel safety devices
        ~Quality Control System Tampering: Adversaries may modify systems that protect against out-of-specification products or materials
      Loss of View: Adversaries may cause a sustained or permanent loss of view where the ICS equipment will require local, hands-on operator intervention; for instance, a restart or manual operation. By causing a sustained reporting or visibility loss, the adversary can effectively hide the present state of operations. This loss of view can occur without affecting the physical processes themselves. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)
        ~Network Protocol Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate industrial network protocols to prevent data from reaching monitoring systems while allowing control commands to pass through
        ~Sensor Data Tampering: Adversaries may modify or block sensor data transmission while leaving the sensors physically functional
        ~HMI Display Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt or freeze Human Machine Interface displays while leaving underlying systems operational
        ~Historian Database Disruption: Adversaries may interrupt or corrupt the data collection and storage in historian databases
        ~Communication Channel Flooding: Adversaries may overwhelm communication channels with noise to prevent legitimate monitoring data from being transmitted
        ~Process Data Buffer Overflow: Adversaries may cause buffer overflows in data collection systems to disrupt monitoring capabilities
        ~Time Synchronization Attack: Adversaries may manipulate time synchronization to cause monitoring systems to miss or misreport events
        ~Configuration File Manipulation: Adversaries may modify configuration files to prevent systems from properly reporting status and alerts
        ~Telemetry System Isolation: Adversaries may isolate telemetry systems from the network while leaving control systems connected
        ~Monitoring Service Termination: Adversaries may selectively terminate monitoring services while leaving control services running
        ~Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) Reporting Disruption: Adversaries may manipulate RTU configurations to stop reporting while continuing to execute control commands
        ~Monitoring System Memory Manipulation: Adversaries may exploit memory management to prevent data updates while maintaining process execution
        ~Data Aggregator Isolation: Adversaries may selectively block data aggregation points while allowing individual control systems to operate
        ~Backup System Deactivation: Adversaries may disable backup monitoring systems while leaving primary control systems operational
        ~Engineering Workstation Display Manipulation: Adversaries may alter engineering workstation displays while maintaining field device communication
        ~Log Collection Prevention: Adversaries may block or corrupt log collection mechanisms while allowing normal operations to continue
        ~Report Generation Disruption: Adversaries may prevent automated report generation while maintaining underlying data collection
        ~Redundant System Desynchronization: Adversaries may cause monitoring system redundancy to fail while keeping control redundancy intact
      Denial of Control: Adversaries may cause a denial of control to temporarily prevent operators and engineers from interacting with process controls. An adversary may attempt to deny process control access to cause a temporary loss of communication with the control device or to prevent operator adjustment of process controls. An affected process may still be operating during the period of control loss, but not necessarily in a desired state. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)  In the 2017 Dallas Siren incident operators were unable to disable the false alarms from the Office of Emergency Management headquarters. (Citation: Mark Loveless April 2017)
        ~Network Flooding: Overwhelming control system networks with excessive traffic to prevent legitimate control commands from reaching devices
        ~Command Interface Blocking: Preventing access to HMI, engineering workstations, or other control interfaces through system lockouts or crashes
        ~Protocol Communication Disruption: Interfering with industrial protocols (e.g., Modbus, DNP3) to prevent control messages from reaching devices
        ~Authentication System Manipulation: Modifying authentication mechanisms to prevent legitimate operator login to control systems
        ~Control Session Hijacking: Taking over legitimate control sessions to prevent operator access while maintaining adversary control
        ~Timing Attack: Manipulating network timing or introducing delays to prevent proper control system operation
        ~Configuration Change: Altering device configurations to ignore legitimate control commands
        ~Backup Control Disruption: Disabling or corrupting backup control systems to prevent failover capabilities
        ~Remote Access Blocking: Preventing remote access capabilities used for system control and maintenance
        ~Safety System Interface Disruption: Blocking access to safety system controls and interfaces
        ~Physical Control Override: Disabling or overriding physical control mechanisms like emergency stop buttons or local control panels
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Interfering with watchdog timers to cause automated system resets or failsafe modes
        ~Control Signal Jamming: Using RF or electromagnetic interference to block wireless control signals
        ~Redundant Controller Isolation: Preventing switchover to redundant controllers by isolating backup systems
        ~Operator Display Manipulation: Providing false feedback on HMIs while preventing actual control actions
        ~Field Device Communication Block: Specifically targeting communication with field devices like PLCs, RTUs, or IEDs
        ~Historical Data Access Prevention: Blocking access to historical data needed for process control decisions
        ~Engineering Software Lockout: Preventing access to engineering software used for control system configuration
      Denial of View: Adversaries may cause a denial of view in attempt to disrupt and prevent operator oversight on the status of an ICS environment. This may manifest itself as a temporary communication failure between a device and its control source, where the interface recovers and becomes available once the interference ceases. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)   An adversary may attempt to deny operator visibility by preventing them from receiving status and reporting messages. Denying this view may temporarily block and prevent operators from noticing a change in state or anomalous behavior. The environment's data and processes may still be operational, but functioning in an unintended or adversarial manner.
        ~Network Traffic Manipulation: Adversaries may intercept and modify network traffic between ICS devices and monitoring systems to prevent accurate status updates from reaching operators.
        ~HMI Screen Freezing: Adversaries may cause Human Machine Interface (HMI) screens to freeze or display stale data while system states change.
        ~Sensor Data Spoofing: Adversaries may manipulate sensor readings to show normal operations while actual conditions are different.
        ~Log File Tampering: Adversaries may modify or delete system logs to prevent operators from detecting unauthorized changes or anomalies.
        ~Protocol Communication Disruption: Adversaries may interfere with industrial protocols (like Modbus, DNP3) to block status updates.
        ~Alarm System Suppression: Adversaries may disable or suppress alarm systems that would normally alert operators to abnormal conditions.
        ~Time Synchronization Attack: Adversaries may manipulate time synchronization to cause monitoring systems to display incorrect temporal data.
        ~Historian Database Interference: Adversaries may corrupt or block access to historian databases that store operational data.
      Loss of Control: Adversaries may seek to achieve a sustained loss of control or a runaway condition in which operators cannot issue any commands even if the malicious interference has subsided. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)  The German Federal Office for Information Security (BSI) reported a targeted attack on a steel mill in its 2014 IT Security Report.(Citation: BSI State of IT Security 2014) These targeted attacks affected industrial operations and resulted in breakdowns of control system components and even entire installations. As a result of these breakdowns, massive impact resulted in damage and unsafe conditions from the uncontrolled shutdown of a blast furnace.
        ~Control Loop Interference: Manipulation of feedback loops and control algorithms to prevent proper system response to operator commands
        ~Command Path Blocking: Disruption of communication channels between operators and control systems to prevent command transmission
        ~Safety System Override: Disabling or bypassing safety interlocks and emergency shutdown systems
        ~Controller State Manipulation: Altering the state of PLCs or RTUs to ignore valid operator commands
        ~Network Protocol Exploitation: Exploiting industrial protocols to prevent legitimate control messages from reaching devices
        ~Human Machine Interface (HMI) Lock-out: Preventing operator access to control systems through HMI manipulation
        ~Firmware Corruption: Corrupting device firmware to create persistent loss of control
        ~Timing Attack: Manipulating system timing to prevent synchronization between control components
        ~False Data Injection: Injecting false sensor data to trigger unwanted control responses
        ~Watchdog Timer Manipulation: Disabling or manipulating watchdog timers that monitor system health
        ~Physical Override Blocking: Disabling or interfering with local physical control mechanisms and emergency overrides
        ~Backup System Neutralization: Preventing failover to backup control systems or redundant controllers
        ~Configuration Corruption: Altering system configuration files to prevent proper control responses
        ~State Machine Manipulation: Forcing control systems into invalid states that reject operator commands
        ~Recovery Mode Prevention: Blocking system recovery mechanisms and maintenance modes
        ~Control Logic Flooding: Overwhelming control logic with excessive commands to prevent legitimate control
        ~Historical Data Manipulation: Altering historical data to prevent proper system restoration
        ~Remote Access Lockout: Permanently disabling remote access capabilities for operators
        ~Control System Time Manipulation: Desynchronizing system time to disrupt coordinated control operations
        ~Distributed Control Disruption: Breaking communication between distributed control system components
        ~Resource Exhaustion Attack: Depleting system resources to prevent control operations
        ~Sensor Calibration Tampering: Manipulating sensor calibration data to force incorrect control responses
        ~Authentication System Poisoning: Corrupting operator authentication databases to prevent legitimate access
        ~Dependencies Chain Attack: Disrupting supporting systems required for proper control operations
        ~Industrial Protocol State Confusion: Exploiting protocol state machines to create persistent control issues
        ~Control Loop Parameter Corruption: Modifying PID and other control loop parameters permanently
        ~Operator Session Hijacking: Taking over legitimate operator sessions to prevent proper control
        ~System Library Corruption: Modifying system libraries critical for control operations
        ~Field Device Configuration Lock: Permanently altering field device configurations to reject changes
        ~Engineering Workstation Isolation: Cutting off engineering workstations from control systems
      Manipulation of Control: Adversaries may manipulate physical process control within the industrial environment. Methods of manipulating control can include changes to set point values, tags, or other parameters. Adversaries may manipulate control systems devices or possibly leverage their own, to communicate with and command physical control processes. The duration of manipulation may be temporary or longer sustained, depending on operator detection.     Methods of Manipulation of Control include:   * Man-in-the-middle   * Spoof command message  * Changing setpoints    A Polish student used a remote controller device to interface with the Lodz city tram system in Poland. (Citation: John Bill May 2017) (Citation: Shelley Smith February 2008) (Citation: Bruce Schneier January 2008) Using this remote, the student was able to capture and replay legitimate tram signals. As a consequence, four trams were derailed and twelve people injured due to resulting emergency stops. (Citation: Shelley Smith February 2008) The track controlling commands issued may have also resulted in tram collisions, a further risk to those on board and nearby the areas of impact. (Citation: Bruce Schneier January 2008)
        ~Parameter Modification: Adversaries modify control parameters like setpoints, thresholds, and alarm limits to affect process behavior while maintaining system operation
        ~Command Message Injection: Adversaries insert malicious control commands into legitimate communication channels between controllers and field devices
        ~Signal Replay: Adversaries capture legitimate control signals and replay them later to cause unintended system behavior
        ~Protocol Manipulation: Adversaries exploit industrial protocols to send unauthorized commands or modify process variables
        ~Controller Reprogramming: Adversaries modify the logic or firmware of programmable logic controllers (PLCs) to alter control behavior
        ~Sensor Data Manipulation: Adversaries tamper with sensor inputs to cause controllers to make incorrect control decisions
        ~Safety System Override: Adversaries bypass or disable safety interlocks and protection systems to enable dangerous process conditions
        ~Remote Access Abuse: Adversaries exploit remote access capabilities intended for maintenance to directly manipulate control systems
        ~Communication Channel Hijacking: Adversaries take control of communication paths between control system components
        ~Time Synchronization Manipulation: Adversaries disrupt time synchronization to affect coordinated control actions
        ~Field Device Manipulation: Adversaries physically tamper with field devices like actuators, valves, or switches to alter their behavior or response to commands
        ~Process Variable Spoofing: Adversaries inject false process variables into the control system to trigger unwanted control actions
        ~Control Loop Interference: Adversaries manipulate feedback loops by introducing delays or modifications to disrupt process stability
        ~Sequence Logic Manipulation: Adversaries modify sequential control logic to alter the order or timing of process steps
        ~Batch Process Manipulation: Adversaries alter batch control parameters or recipes to affect product quality or safety
        ~Distributed Control Manipulation: Adversaries exploit distributed control system architectures to manipulate multiple control loops simultaneously
        ~Historical Data Manipulation: Adversaries modify historical process data to hide their actions or cause incorrect operator decisions
        ~Human Machine Interface (HMI) Manipulation: Adversaries alter HMI displays to show false information while executing malicious control actions
      Loss of Safety: Adversaries may compromise safety system functions designed to maintain safe operation of a process when unacceptable or dangerous conditions occur. Safety systems are often composed of the same elements as control systems but have the sole purpose of ensuring the process fails in a predetermined safe manner.   Many unsafe conditions in process control happen too quickly for a human operator to react to. Speed is critical in correcting these conditions to limit serious impacts such as Loss of Control and Property Damage.   Adversaries may target and disable safety system functions as a prerequisite to subsequent attack execution or to allow for future unsafe conditionals to go unchecked. Detection of a Loss of Safety by operators can result in the shutdown of a process due to strict policies regarding safety systems. This can cause a Loss of Productivity and Revenue and may meet the technical goals of adversaries seeking to cause process disruptions.
        ~Safety Controller Modification: Adversaries may modify safety controller logic or parameters to prevent proper emergency responses
        ~Safety Sensor Tampering: Adversaries may manipulate or disable sensors that trigger safety responses
        ~Emergency Shutdown Override: Adversaries may bypass or disable emergency shutdown mechanisms
        ~Safety Interlock Bypass: Adversaries may circumvent safety interlocks designed to prevent dangerous operations
        ~Alarm System Suppression: Adversaries may suppress or disable safety-related alarms and notifications
        ~Safety Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may alter safety thresholds to allow dangerous operating conditions
        ~Safety Communication Disruption: Adversaries may interfere with communications between safety systems and control systems
        ~Safety Backup System Disable: Adversaries may compromise redundant safety systems to remove fallback protection
        ~Safety Test Function Manipulation: Adversaries may tamper with safety system testing and validation functions
        ~Safety Documentation Alteration: Adversaries may modify safety system documentation to hide malicious changes
        ~Physical Safety Lock Bypass: Adversaries may disable mechanical safety locks, barriers, or physical interlocks
        ~Safety Training System Compromise: Adversaries may tamper with operator safety training simulators and systems
        ~Environmental Monitor Disable: Adversaries may compromise systems monitoring hazardous conditions like gas leaks or radiation
        ~Personnel Protection Override: Adversaries may disable systems designed to protect personnel in hazardous areas
        ~Safety Certification Bypass: Adversaries may forge or bypass safety certification checks and validation processes
        ~Safety Maintenance Lock: Adversaries may prevent safety system updates or maintenance to keep known vulnerabilities
        ~Safety Log Manipulation: Adversaries may alter safety incident logs to hide malicious activities
        ~Safety Zone Control Bypass: Adversaries may disable systems enforcing safety zones or restricted areas
      Damage to Property: Adversaries may cause damage and destruction of property to infrastructure, equipment, and the surrounding environment when attacking control systems. This technique may result in device and operational equipment breakdown, or represent tangential damage from other techniques used in an attack. Depending on the severity of physical damage and disruption caused to control processes and systems, this technique may result in [Loss of Safety](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0880). Operations that result in [Loss of Control](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0827) may also cause damage to property, which may be directly or indirectly motivated by an adversary seeking to cause impact in the form of [Loss of Productivity and Revenue](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0828).    The German Federal Office for Information Security (BSI) reported a targeted attack on a steel mill under an incidents affecting business section of its 2014 IT Security Report. (Citation: BSI State of IT Security 2014)  These targeted attacks affected industrial operations and resulted in breakdowns of control system components and even entire installations. As a result of these breakdowns, massive impact and damage resulted from the uncontrolled shutdown of a blast furnace.   A Polish student used a remote controller device to interface with the Lodz city tram system in Poland. (Citation: John Bill May 2017) (Citation: Shelley Smith February 2008) (Citation: Bruce Schneier January 2008) Using this remote, the student was able to capture and replay legitimate tram signals. This resulted in damage to impacted trams, people, and the surrounding property. Reportedly, four trams were derailed and were forced to make emergency stops. (Citation: Shelley Smith February 2008) Commands issued by the student may have also resulted in tram collisions, causing harm to those on board and the environment outside. (Citation: Bruce Schneier January 2008)
        ~Equipment Overload: Manipulate control systems to operate equipment beyond design specifications, causing mechanical failure or breakdown
        ~Process Parameter Manipulation: Alter critical process parameters to cause equipment stress and physical damage
        ~Safety System Override: Disable or bypass safety mechanisms to allow dangerous operating conditions
        ~Component Misconfiguration: Deliberately misconfigure system components to cause mechanical stress and failure
        ~Signal Replay Attacks: Capture and replay control signals to cause erratic equipment behavior and physical damage
        ~Cooling System Disruption: Interfere with cooling systems to cause overheating and equipment damage
        ~Pressure Control Tampering: Manipulate pressure controls to cause vessel or pipe ruptures
        ~Emergency Stop Abuse: Trigger emergency stops at critical moments to cause mechanical stress
        ~Actuator Override: Force physical actuators beyond safe operating limits
        ~Environmental Control Disruption: Disable environmental controls leading to equipment damage from temperature/humidity
        ~Chemical Feed Manipulation: Alter chemical dosing or mixing ratios to cause corrosive damage
        ~Synchronization Disruption: Interfere with synchronized processes to cause mechanical collisions
        ~Valve Sequencing Attacks: Manipulate valve sequences to cause water hammer effects or fluid mixing issues
        ~Power Cycling Abuse: Rapidly cycle power to equipment causing electrical and mechanical stress
        ~Mechanical Resonance Inducement: Force equipment to operate at resonant frequencies causing structural damage
        ~Material Flow Disruption: Alter material handling parameters to cause jams or equipment damage
        ~Calibration System Tampering: Modify calibration parameters to cause gradual equipment wear
        ~Lubrication System Interference: Disable or modify lubrication systems to increase mechanical wear
        ~Batch Process Sabotage: Manipulate batch processing sequences to cause material waste and equipment damage
        ~Thermal Cycling Abuse: Force rapid temperature changes to cause material stress and structural damage
        ~Utility Distribution Attack: Manipulate utility distribution systems (steam, air, water) to cause infrastructure damage
        ~Sensor Data Poisoning: Feed false sensor data to cause automated systems to damage themselves
        ~Maintenance Override: Override maintenance schedules and limits to cause equipment deterioration
        ~Process Chain Reaction: Trigger cascading process failures that cause widespread equipment damage
        ~Feedback Loop Manipulation: Create unstable feedback loops in control systems leading to equipment damage
        ~Transport System Abuse: Manipulate automated transport systems to cause collision or derailment
        ~Pressure Wave Generation: Create pressure waves in fluid systems to damage pipes and vessels
        ~Equipment Isolation Tampering: Manipulate isolation systems to cause incompatible process interactions
        ~Rotational Speed Manipulation: Alter rotation speeds beyond mechanical limits causing bearing/shaft damage
        ~Waste System Overload: Overwhelm waste handling systems causing containment failures
        ~Multi-System Desynchronization: Cause damage through desynchronizing multiple interconnected systems
        ~Byproduct Accumulation: Manipulate processes to accumulate harmful byproducts causing corrosion/damage
        ~Backup System Interference: Disable or corrupt backup systems leading to primary system damage
        ~Preventive Control Deactivation: Systematically disable preventive controls leading to accelerated wear
        ~Emergency Response Disruption: Interfere with emergency response systems amplifying damage from incidents
        ~Process Deadlock Creation: Create process deadlocks causing mechanical stress at interaction points
        ~Material Property Alteration: Change process conditions to alter material properties causing equipment damage
        ~Diagnostic System Corruption: Manipulate diagnostic systems to hide developing equipment damage
        ~Energy Transfer Disruption: Interfere with energy transfer processes causing thermal/mechanical stress
        ~Component Lifetime Reduction: Manipulate operating conditions to accelerate component aging and failure
      Theft of Operational Information: Adversaries may steal operational information on a production environment as a direct mission outcome for personal gain or to inform future operations. This information may include design documents, schedules, rotational data, or similar artifacts that provide insight on operations.    In the Bowman Dam incident, adversaries probed systems for operational data. (Citation: Mark Thompson March 2016) (Citation: Danny Yadron December 2015)
        ~Process Documentation Theft: Theft of detailed process documentation including standard operating procedures, maintenance schedules, and emergency response plans
        ~Engineering Drawing Exfiltration: Theft of CAD files, P&IDs, and technical drawings of industrial processes and equipment
        ~Production Schedule Theft: Extraction of production schedules, batch processing information, and manufacturing timelines
        ~Operational Parameter Collection: Collection of setpoints, control limits, and other operational parameters used in industrial processes
        ~Equipment Configuration Data Theft: Theft of device configurations, firmware versions, and calibration data
        ~Maintenance Record Extraction: Collection of maintenance logs, repair histories, and equipment performance data
        ~Real-time Process Data Capture: Intercepting and collecting real-time process data, sensor readings, and control signals
        ~Historical Data Theft: Extraction of historical operational data, trend information, and performance metrics
        ~Vendor Documentation Theft: Collection of vendor manuals, specifications, and proprietary operational documentation
        ~Safety System Configuration Theft: Extraction of safety system settings, alarm thresholds, and emergency shutdown parameters
        ~Personnel Roster Theft: Collection of operator schedules, shift patterns, and staffing information
        ~Supply Chain Data Extraction: Theft of supplier information, material specifications, and logistics planning data
        ~Network Architecture Documentation Theft: Collection of network diagrams, communication paths, and protocol configurations
        ~Quality Control Data Theft: Extraction of quality assurance parameters, testing procedures, and compliance documentation
        ~Business System Integration Data Theft: Collection of data exchange configurations between ICS and business systems
        ~Chemical Process Formula Theft: Extraction of proprietary formulas, recipes, and process parameters for chemical processes
        ~Environmental Compliance Data Theft: Collection of environmental monitoring data, compliance reports, and permit information
        ~Asset Management Data Theft: Extraction of asset inventories, lifecycle information, and equipment specifications
        ~Production Capacity Data Theft: Collection of throughput data, equipment capabilities, and capacity planning information
        ~Incident Response Procedure Theft: Extraction of emergency procedures, incident handling protocols, and recovery plans
        ~Product Specification Theft: Collection of detailed product specifications, tolerances, and manufacturing requirements
        ~Energy Consumption Pattern Theft: Extraction of energy usage patterns, peak load data, and power management configurations
        ~Research Project Data Theft: Collection of experimental process data, prototype configurations, and test results
        ~Regulatory Compliance Record Theft: Extraction of audit trails, compliance verification data, and regulatory documentation
        ~Custom Tool Configuration Theft: Collection of specialized tooling parameters, custom equipment settings, and calibration data
        ~Production Cost Data Theft: Extraction of operational cost data, resource utilization metrics, and efficiency parameters
        ~Facility Layout Information Theft: Collection of physical security layouts, access control configurations, and facility maps
        ~Waste Management Data Theft: Extraction of waste handling procedures, environmental impact data, and disposal records
        ~Raw Material Specification Theft: Collection of material handling requirements, storage parameters, and quality specifications
        ~Process Optimization Data Theft: Extraction of performance optimization parameters, tuning configurations, and efficiency metrics
      Manipulation of View: Adversaries may attempt to manipulate the information reported back to operators or controllers. This manipulation may be short term or sustained. During this time the process itself could be in a much different state than what is reported. (Citation: Corero) (Citation: Michael J. Assante and Robert M. Lee) (Citation: Tyson Macaulay)   Operators may be fooled into doing something that is harmful to the system in a loss of view situation. With a manipulated view into the systems, operators may issue inappropriate control sequences that introduce faults or catastrophic failures into the system. Business analysis systems can also be provided with inaccurate data leading to bad management decisions.
        ~HMI Screen Manipulation: Altering the visual representation of process data on Human Machine Interface screens to deceive operators
        ~Historian Data Tampering: Modifying historical data records to mask abnormal conditions or create false trends
        ~Sensor Value Manipulation: Intercepting and altering sensor readings before they reach monitoring systems
        ~Alarm System Deception: Suppressing or generating false alarms to confuse operators
        ~Network Traffic Manipulation: Modifying network packets containing process data between field devices and control systems
        ~Time Synchronization Tampering: Manipulating timestamps to create confusion about event sequences
        ~Controller Status Spoofing: Falsifying controller state information to hide malicious activities
        ~Process Variable Manipulation: Altering specific process variables while maintaining others to avoid detection
        ~Calibration Parameter Alteration: Modifying calibration settings to cause subtle but persistent measurement errors
        ~Real-time Data Stream Injection: Injecting false data into real-time monitoring streams
        ~Report Generation Tampering: Manipulating automated reports to mask system anomalies or create false operational narratives
        ~Remote Terminal Unit Spoofing: Falsifying data from RTUs to central monitoring systems
        ~Engineering Station Display Manipulation: Altering data displayed on engineering workstations used for system configuration
        ~Redundant System Deception: Manipulating backup monitoring systems to prevent detection through comparison
        ~Field Device Interface Manipulation: Altering local display values on field devices to deceive on-site operators
        ~Data Aggregation Point Tampering: Manipulating data at collection and aggregation points before distribution
        ~Diagnostic Tool Deception: Falsifying information shown in system diagnostic and troubleshooting tools
        ~Control Loop Feedback Manipulation: Altering feedback signals while maintaining apparent normal operation
        ~Safety System Display Manipulation: Modifying safety system status indicators to hide dangerous conditions
        ~Mobile HMI Manipulation: Tampering with data displayed on mobile/tablet interfaces used by operators
        ~Third-party Monitoring System Deception: Manipulating data feeds to external monitoring services or vendors
        ~Cross-system Correlation Tampering: Altering data across multiple systems to maintain consistent false readings
        ~Physical Gauge Spoofing: Manipulating electronic signals to physical gauges and indicators
        ~Maintenance Dashboard Deception: Falsifying equipment health and maintenance status displays
        ~Asset Management System Manipulation: Altering equipment status and health data in asset management platforms
        ~Environmental Monitoring System Deception: Manipulating environmental parameter displays like temperature, pressure, or emissions
        ~Quality Control System Manipulation: Falsifying product quality monitoring and testing system displays
      Loss of Productivity and Revenue: Adversaries may cause loss of productivity and revenue through disruption and even damage to the availability and integrity of control system operations, devices, and related processes. This technique may manifest as a direct effect of an ICS-targeting attack or tangentially, due to an IT-targeting attack against non-segregated environments.   In cases where these operations or services are brought to a halt, the loss of productivity may eventually present an impact for the end-users or consumers of products and services. The disrupted supply-chain may result in supply shortages and increased prices, among other consequences.   A ransomware attack on an Australian beverage company resulted in the shutdown of some manufacturing sites, including precautionary halts to protect key systems. (Citation: Paganini, Pierluigi June 2020) The company announced the potential for temporary shortages of their products following the attack. (Citation: Paganini, Pierluigi June 2020) (Citation: Lion Corporation June 2020)   In the 2021 Colonial Pipeline ransomware incident, the pipeline was unable to transport approximately 2.5 million barrels of fuel per day to the East Coast.  (Citation: Colonial Pipeline Company May 2021)
        ~Production Line Shutdown: Adversaries may force production lines to halt by manipulating control systems, causing direct revenue loss and productivity impact
        ~Supply Chain Disruption: Adversaries may target logistics and inventory management systems to create supply chain bottlenecks
        ~Quality Control Manipulation: Adversaries may alter quality control parameters causing product waste and rework
        ~Resource Overconsumption: Adversaries may manipulate systems to waste raw materials or energy, increasing operational costs
        ~Equipment Lifecycle Impact: Adversaries may cause premature wear or damage to equipment through manipulation of operational parameters
        ~Process Timing Disruption: Adversaries may alter process timing causing inefficiencies and reduced throughput
        ~Order Management Interference: Adversaries may disrupt order processing and fulfillment systems
        ~Maintenance Schedule Manipulation: Adversaries may force unnecessary downtime through false maintenance triggers
        ~Production Rate Manipulation: Adversaries may alter production speeds causing inefficiencies or quality issues
        ~Utility System Disruption: Adversaries may target supporting utility systems to impact overall production capability
        ~Safety System Triggering: Adversaries may trigger safety shutdowns through false sensor readings or alarm manipulation
        ~Regulatory Compliance Disruption: Adversaries may cause violations of regulatory requirements forcing operational stoppage
        ~Customer Interface Disruption: Adversaries may impact customer-facing systems affecting order processing and delivery
        ~Production Data Manipulation: Adversaries may alter business intelligence data leading to poor operational decisions
        ~Market Timing Impact: Adversaries may cause disruptions timed to maximize market impact and financial losses
        ~Batch Process Corruption: Adversaries may corrupt batch processing parameters causing product loss
        ~Environmental Control Disruption: Adversaries may manipulate environmental controls causing production environment issues
        ~Quality Certification Interference: Adversaries may disrupt quality certification processes blocking product shipment
  MITRE-ATLAS
    Reconnaissance: The adversary is trying to gather information about the AI system they can use to plan future operations.  Reconnaissance consists of techniques that involve adversaries actively or passively gathering information that can be used to support targeting. Such information may include details of the victim organizations' AI capabilities and research efforts. This information can be leveraged by the adversary to aid in other phases of the adversary lifecycle, such as using gathered information to obtain relevant AI artifacts, targeting AI capabilities used by the victim, tailoring attacks to the particular models used by the victim, or to drive and lead further Reconnaissance efforts.
      Search Open Technical Databases: Adversaries may search for publicly available research and technical documentation to learn how and where AI is used within a victim organization. The adversary can use this information to identify targets for attack, or to tailor an existing attack to make it more effective. Organizations often use open source model architectures trained on additional proprietary data in production. Knowledge of this underlying architecture allows the adversary to craft more realistic proxy models ([Create Proxy AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0005)). An adversary can search these resources for publications for authors employed at the victim organization.  Research and technical materials may exist as academic papers published in [Journals and Conference Proceedings](/techniques/AML.T0000.000), or stored in [Pre-Print Repositories](/techniques/AML.T0000.001), as well as [Technical Blogs](/techniques/AML.T0000.002).
        Journals and Conference Proceedings: Many of the publications accepted at premier artificial intelligence conferences and journals come from commercial labs. Some journals and conferences are open access, others may require paying for access or a membership. These publications will often describe in detail all aspects of a particular approach for reproducibility. This information can be used by adversaries to implement the paper.
          Use academic search engines like Google Scholar, Microsoft Academic to search for papers from specific organizations or authors
          Monitor conference proceedings of major AI conferences (NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, AAAI) for papers from target organizations
          Create fake academic credentials or use credential sharing sites to bypass journal paywalls
          Use reference mining tools to track citation networks and identify related papers from target organizations
          Set up automated alerts for new publications from specific authors or organizations
          Leverage institutional access through compromised university accounts to access restricted papers
          Use paper sharing platforms like ResearchGate or Academia.edu to find author preprints
          Extract and analyze code repositories linked in academic papers' supplementary materials
          Use web scraping tools to bulk download conference proceedings
          Cross-reference author affiliations with LinkedIn/professional networks to map organizational structure
          Use natural language processing tools to automatically analyze paper content for technical details and architectures
          Register for conferences as an attendee to access proceedings and network with target organization members
          Create scripts to monitor conference submission/acceptance lists for specific organizations
          Use PDF metadata extraction tools to gather additional information about authors and organizations
          Cross-reference acknowledgments sections to identify funding sources and collaborations
          Contact authors directly posing as fellow researchers to request additional details
          Monitor conference presentation videos and slides for technical details not in papers
          Use Internet Archive/Wayback Machine to access older versions of conference websites and proceedings
          Analyze author contribution statements to identify key technical personnel
          Track organization's conference sponsorship and exhibition presence to determine AI priorities
          Analyze peer review comments on open review platforms (e.g., OpenReview) for additional technical details
          Monitor special interest group (SIG) workshops and focused symposia proceedings
          Track changes in paper versions between preprint and final publication to identify sensitive redactions
          Use language translation tools to access papers from international conferences in different languages
          Collect and analyze supplementary materials from conference poster sessions
          Monitor conference panel discussions and round-table summaries for strategic insights
          Track organization's paper submission patterns across different conference tiers
          Analyze conference demo sessions and technical demonstrations
          Extract information from conference tutorial materials and workshop notes
          Monitor conference award nominations and winners to identify high-impact work
        Pre-Print Repositories: Pre-Print repositories, such as arXiv, contain the latest academic research papers that haven't been peer reviewed. They may contain research notes, or technical reports that aren't typically published in journals or conference proceedings. Pre-print repositories also serve as a central location to share papers that have been accepted to journals. Searching pre-print repositories  provide adversaries with a relatively up-to-date view of what researchers in the victim organization are working on.
          Use arXiv API to automatically scrape papers from specific organizations or authors
          Create alerts for new submissions from target organizations/researchers on arXiv
          Use web scraping tools to extract author affiliations and contact information from papers
          Cross-reference author names with LinkedIn/professional networks to map organizational structure
          Analyze code repositories linked in papers to identify AI frameworks and architectures used
          Track citation patterns to identify collaborations and research dependencies
          Use natural language processing tools to analyze paper abstracts for specific AI capabilities
          Monitor paper submission dates to determine research timeline and development cycles
          Extract model architectures and hyperparameters from methodology sections
          Search for supplementary materials and datasets referenced in papers
          Use semantic search engines (e.g., Semantic Scholar) to find related papers and research threads
          Monitor paper withdrawal patterns to identify potentially sensitive research
          Analyze funding acknowledgments to map financial relationships and project scope
          Compare pre-print versions with final publications to identify redacted or sensitive information
          Use document metadata analysis to identify software tools used in research
          Track conference submission patterns to predict upcoming research presentations
          Search for technical appendices and supplementary files that may contain implementation details
          Use language translation tools to analyze papers from international branches of the organization
          Create knowledge graphs of research topics and evolution over time
          Monitor paper comments and revisions for technical discussions and corrections
          Compare submission timestamps across different pre-print platforms (arXiv, bioRxiv, etc.) to identify research priorities
          Extract and analyze computational resource requirements mentioned in experiments
          Track author order patterns to identify key researchers and research leads
          Monitor supplementary code releases on platforms mentioned in pre-prints
          Analyze acknowledgments sections to map laboratory equipment and computing infrastructure
          Use PDF metadata extraction tools to identify internal document naming conventions
          Track shared email domains in author affiliations to map organizational structure
          Monitor paper categories and subject classifications to identify shifts in research focus
          Create timeline visualizations of organization's research progression
          Analyze author contributions sections to identify technical capabilities of team members
          Use bibliometric analysis tools to identify critical research dependencies and tools
          Cross-reference paper submission times with organization's public announcements or product releases
          Analyze author institution changes to track talent movement and knowledge transfer
          Compare experimental results across papers to identify shared datasets or model architectures
          Monitor paper rejection patterns through transitions from pre-prints to publications
          Track changes in author collaboration networks over time to identify emerging research teams
          Analyze hardware specifications in experiments to map computing infrastructure evolution
          Compare pre-print versions with patents to identify commercialization strategies
          Monitor citation patterns to identify dependencies on specific AI frameworks or tools
          Analyze acknowledgment sections for cloud service provider usage patterns
          Use machine learning to cluster similar research approaches across different papers
          Analyze LaTeX template usage patterns to identify internal documentation standards
          Track shared GitHub repository references across different papers to map code dependencies
          Monitor paper submission timing patterns to identify organizational review processes
          Analyze image and diagram similarities across papers to identify shared tools/platforms
          Create knowledge bases of specific AI model performance claims for benchmarking
          Track changes in technical terminology usage to identify emerging research directions
          Analyze author email response patterns to public queries about their pre-prints
          Monitor pre-print comment sections for insights into technical limitations or challenges
          Create automated alerts for specific technical keywords in new submissions
        Technical Blogs: Research labs at academic institutions and Company R&D divisions often have blogs that highlight their use of artificial intelligence and its application to the organizations unique problems. Individual researchers also frequently document their work in blogposts. An adversary may search for posts made by the target victim organization or its employees. In comparison to [Journals and Conference Proceedings](/techniques/AML.T0000.000) and [Pre-Print Repositories](/techniques/AML.T0000.001) this material will often contain more practical aspects of the AI system. This could include underlying technologies and frameworks used, and possibly some information about the API access and use case. This will help the adversary better understand how that organization is using AI internally and the details of their approach that could aid in tailoring an attack.
          Use RSS feed aggregators to monitor updates on target organization's engineering blogs
          Create automated alerts for new blog posts from identified employee personal blogs using Google Alerts
          Use web scraping tools like BeautifulSoup to extract technical details from blog posts
          Search Medium.com tags and publications related to target organization's AI initiatives
          Monitor organization's dev.to team page and individual developer accounts
          Use the Wayback Machine to access historical versions of technical blog posts
          Deploy sentiment analysis tools to identify posts discussing technical challenges or limitations
          Cross-reference LinkedIn profiles with personal blog URLs to identify employee technical writings
          Use GitHub profile README.md to find linked personal blog sites of organization members
          Deploy content aggregation platforms like Feedly to track multiple AI research blogs simultaneously
          Monitor organization's Twitter/X accounts for links to technical blog posts
          Analyze blog comments for additional technical details or corrections from team members
          Search Substack newsletters for AI-related technical content from target organization
          Use code extraction tools to collect code snippets and implementation details from blog posts
          Cross-reference blog posts with organization's public GitHub repositories for detailed implementations
          Monitor AI-specific blogging platforms like Neptune.ai and Weights & Biases
          Use SEO tools to find technical blogs linking to target organization's content
          Deploy natural language processing to identify technical specifications and parameters in blog posts
          Monitor SlideShare for published presentation materials related to blog content
          Track organization's technical blog presence on platforms like HashNode and Analytics Vidhya
          Monitor technical Q&A platforms like Stack Overflow for links to explanatory blog posts
          Use Google Scholar profile's "Cited by" feature to find related technical blog discussions
          Extract metadata from blog images and diagrams for additional technical insights
          Monitor regional blogging platforms (e.g., Qiita for Japanese content)
          Track organization's conference presentation blogs and workshop materials
          Analyze blog post revision history when available for technical detail changes
          Use language translation tools to access technical blogs in different languages
          Monitor podcast show notes and transcripts that reference technical blog content
          Track technical meetup group blogs and presentation materials
          Analyze blog author profiles across different platforms for comprehensive technical background
          Monitor cloud provider blogs (AWS, GCP, Azure) for customer case studies
          Search Internet Archive for deleted or modified technical blog content
          Analyze technical documentation accidentally exposed in blog screenshots
          Monitor partner organizations' integration stories and technical case studies
          Track industry-specific blogging networks (e.g., AIMultiple, KDnuggets)
          Analyze job posting technical requirements cross-referenced with blog content
          Monitor technical webinar and workshop blog summaries
          Track organization's product changelog and release notes blogs
          Analyze technical marketing whitepapers linked from blog posts
          Monitor employee contributions to third-party vendor technical blogs
      Search Open AI Vulnerability Analysis: Much like the [Search Open Technical Databases](/techniques/AML.T0000), there is often ample research available on the vulnerabilities of common AI models. Once a target has been identified, an adversary will likely try to identify any pre-existing work that has been done for this class of models. This will include not only reading academic papers that may identify the particulars of a successful attack, but also identifying pre-existing implementations of those attacks. The adversary may obtain [Adversarial AI Attack Implementations](/techniques/AML.T0016.000) or develop their own [Adversarial AI Attacks](/techniques/AML.T0017.000) if necessary.
        ~Academic Paper Analysis: Search and analyze academic papers, conference proceedings, and preprints for published vulnerabilities and attack methods against AI systems
        ~Public Repository Mining: Search through code repositories like GitHub for implementations of AI attacks and vulnerability demonstrations
        ~CVE Database Search: Monitor and search Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) databases for AI-specific vulnerabilities
        ~AI Security Forum Monitoring: Monitor AI security forums, discussion boards, and social media for discussions about vulnerabilities
        ~Model Zoo Analysis: Search through public model repositories and documentation for known vulnerabilities in common AI models
        ~Security Conference Monitoring: Track AI security conferences, workshops, and presentations for new vulnerability discoveries
        ~Vendor Documentation Analysis: Review AI framework and platform documentation for security considerations and known limitations
        ~Bug Bounty Program Review: Search through public bug bounty programs and disclosed vulnerabilities related to AI systems
        ~Research Lab Monitoring: Track publications and announcements from prominent AI security research labs
        ~Technical Blog Analysis: Monitor technical blogs and articles discussing AI security vulnerabilities and attack techniques
        ~AI Benchmark Analysis: Study AI model benchmark platforms and leaderboards to identify potential weaknesses in model performance
        ~Patent Database Search: Search through patent databases for defensive AI security measures that might reveal system vulnerabilities
        ~Security Incident Analysis: Review public security incident reports and post-mortems involving AI systems
        ~Standards Documentation Review: Analyze AI security standards and compliance documents for security considerations
        ~AI Competition Monitoring: Track AI security competitions and challenges for novel attack techniques
        ~Security Tool Analysis: Review AI security testing and auditing tool documentation for vulnerability detection methods
        ~Industry Report Analysis: Study industry analysis reports and whitepapers on AI security trends
        ~Regulatory Filing Review: Search through public regulatory filings and documentation that might reveal AI system details
        ~AI Supply Chain Analysis: Research vulnerabilities in common AI development tools, frameworks, and dependencies
        ~Historical Attack Pattern Analysis: Study historical patterns and trends in AI vulnerabilities and attacks
        ~Cross-Domain Vulnerability Mapping: Analyze vulnerabilities from related domains (like traditional software security) that might apply to AI systems
        ~AI Threat Intelligence Monitoring: Track AI-specific threat intelligence feeds and reports
        ~Model Architecture Analysis: Study common model architectures and their known security weaknesses
        ~AI Development Pipeline Review: Research vulnerabilities in AI development and deployment pipelines
        ~Hardware Vulnerability Analysis: Research hardware-specific vulnerabilities that could affect AI system security
      Search Victim-Owned Websites: Adversaries may search websites owned by the victim for information that can be used during targeting. Victim-owned websites may contain technical details about their AI-enabled products or services. Victim-owned websites may contain a variety of details, including names of departments/divisions, physical locations, and data about key employees such as names, roles, and contact info. These sites may also have details highlighting business operations and relationships.  Adversaries may search victim-owned websites to gather actionable information. This information may help adversaries tailor their attacks (e.g. [Adversarial AI Attacks](/techniques/AML.T0017.000) or [Manual Modification](/techniques/AML.T0043.003)). Information from these sources may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (e.g. [Search Open Technical Databases](/techniques/AML.T0000) or [Search Open AI Vulnerability Analysis](/techniques/AML.T0001))
        ~Model Documentation Search: Searching for technical documentation, API references, or model cards that describe the victim's AI models, including architecture details, training data sources, and performance metrics
        ~Job Posting Analysis: Analyzing job postings and career pages for information about AI technologies, frameworks, and infrastructure used by the organization
        ~Blog/Technical Content Mining: Examining technical blog posts, case studies, and whitepapers that detail the organization's AI implementation and research
        ~Product Feature Analysis: Investigating product documentation and feature descriptions to identify AI capabilities and underlying technologies
        ~Research Publication Review: Analyzing published research papers and technical reports from the organization's AI research teams
        ~Infrastructure Indicators: Searching for references to AI-specific infrastructure, computing resources, or cloud services
        ~Team Structure Analysis: Mapping AI team structure and expertise through organizational charts and employee profiles
        ~Partner Ecosystem Review: Examining references to AI technology partners, vendors, and service providers
        ~Automated Web Scraping: Using automated tools to systematically collect and analyze AI-related content across victim-owned properties
        ~Historical Analysis: Reviewing archived versions of websites to track the evolution of AI capabilities and infrastructure
        ~Developer Portal Analysis: Examining developer portals, SDKs, and API documentation for details about AI service integration and capabilities
        ~Support Forum Mining: Analyzing customer support forums and knowledge bases for technical details revealed in troubleshooting discussions
        ~Community Platform Investigation: Reviewing organization-managed developer communities, Discord servers, or Slack channels for AI implementation details
        ~Training Material Analysis: Examining customer training materials, tutorials, and workshops that reveal AI system capabilities and configurations
        ~Code Repository Review: Analyzing public code repositories, documentation, and issues on organization-managed GitHub/GitLab instances
        ~Demo Environment Analysis: Investigating demo environments, sandboxes, and testing platforms that showcase AI capabilities
        ~Event Content Analysis: Reviewing presentations, workshops, and technical content from organization-hosted events
        ~Integration Guide Analysis: Examining integration guides and partner documentation that reveal AI system interfaces and requirements
        ~Investor Relations Analysis: Examining annual reports, SEC filings, and investor presentations for AI investment and strategy details
        ~Regional Site Comparison: Analyzing differences in AI capabilities and documentation across regional/localized versions of websites
        ~Compliance Documentation Review: Investigating AI ethics statements, bias reports, and regulatory compliance documentation
        ~Sales Collateral Analysis: Examining sales presentations, competitive comparisons, and ROI calculators that detail AI capabilities
        ~Performance Benchmark Review: Analyzing published performance metrics, benchmarks, and comparison studies
        ~Service Level Agreement Analysis: Reviewing SLAs and terms of service for AI-powered services to understand operational parameters
        ~Press Release Mining: Systematically analyzing press releases and news announcements for AI capability details
      Search Application Repositories: Adversaries may search open application repositories during targeting. Examples of these include Google Play, the iOS App store, the macOS App Store, and the Microsoft Store.  Adversaries may craft search queries seeking applications that contain a AI-enabled components. Frequently, the next step is to [Acquire Public AI Artifacts](/techniques/AML.T0002).
        ~Mobile App Store Analysis: Adversaries systematically search mobile app stores (Google Play, iOS App Store) for applications advertising AI/ML capabilities or containing keywords related to AI frameworks.
        ~Desktop Store Reconnaissance: Adversaries search desktop application marketplaces (Microsoft Store, macOS App Store) for software containing AI components or machine learning features.
        ~Package Repository Scanning: Adversaries scan package managers and code repositories (npm, PyPI, Maven) for dependencies and libraries related to popular AI frameworks.
        ~Metadata Analysis: Adversaries analyze application metadata, descriptions, and documentation for indicators of AI capabilities or machine learning features.
        ~Automated Store Crawling: Adversaries use automated tools and scripts to systematically crawl app stores and extract information about AI-enabled applications.
        ~Version History Analysis: Adversaries examine application update histories and changelogs for introduction of AI features or machine learning capabilities.
        ~Developer Profile Analysis: Adversaries investigate developer/company profiles in app stores to identify organizations working with AI technologies.
      Active Scanning: An adversary may probe or scan the victim system to gather information for targeting. This is distinct from other reconnaissance techniques that do not involve direct interaction with the victim system.
        ~Model API Scanning: Probing exposed model APIs to identify endpoints, supported model types, and input/output formats
        ~ML Infrastructure Discovery: Scanning for common ML development and training infrastructure components like Jupyter notebooks, MLflow servers, or model repositories
        ~Model Behavior Probing: Systematically testing model inputs to understand model behavior, limitations, and potential vulnerabilities
        ~Training Pipeline Scanning: Identifying and probing components of ML training pipelines including data preprocessing, model training, and deployment infrastructure
        ~Model Parameter Extraction: Actively querying models to extract information about model architecture, parameters, or hyperparameters
        ~Framework Detection: Scanning to identify ML frameworks, libraries, and versions in use
        ~GPU Resource Discovery: Probing for GPU/TPU resources and ML-specific hardware infrastructure
        ~Model Registry Scanning: Scanning for model registry services and attempting to enumerate stored models
        ~Data Pipeline Discovery: Identifying and mapping data ingestion, preprocessing, and augmentation pipelines
        ~Distributed Training Detection: Scanning for distributed training infrastructure and communication patterns
        ~Model Serving Scanner: Scanning for common model serving platforms like TensorFlow Serving, TorchServe, or custom serving solutions
        ~Model Monitoring Probe: Identifying and probing model monitoring systems, performance metrics collection, and alerting infrastructure
        ~Edge Device Discovery: Scanning for AI models deployed on edge devices or IoT infrastructure
        ~Security Control Probe: Active scanning to identify AI-specific security controls like input validation, rate limiting, or access controls
        ~Version Control Scanning: Probing for model versioning systems and artifact storage
        ~Container Registry Scanning: Identifying and enumerating AI/ML-specific container registries and images
        ~AutoML Infrastructure Discovery: Scanning for automated machine learning platforms and their components
        ~Feature Store Detection: Probing for feature stores and feature engineering infrastructure
        ~Model Optimization Infrastructure: Scanning for model optimization and compression infrastructure
        ~Experiment Tracking Discovery: Identifying experiment tracking systems and their exposed information
        ~Collaborative Platform Scanning: Probing for collaborative AI development platforms and shared workspaces
        ~Cloud AI Service Discovery: Scanning for managed AI services and cloud-based ML platforms
        ~Model Marketplace Probe: Scanning for connections to AI/ML model marketplaces and external model repositories
        ~Development Environment Scanner: Identifying AI/ML-specific IDE configurations and development environments
        ~Data Annotation Infrastructure: Scanning for data labeling and annotation infrastructure
        ~Model Validation Scanner: Probing model validation and testing infrastructure
        ~CI/CD Pipeline Discovery: Scanning for AI-specific continuous integration and deployment pipelines
        ~Transfer Learning Infrastructure: Identifying transfer learning and model fine-tuning infrastructure
        ~Model Benchmark Infrastructure: Scanning for model benchmarking and evaluation systems
        ~Knowledge Distillation Scanner: Probing for model compression and knowledge distillation infrastructure
      Gather RAG-Indexed Targets: Adversaries may identify data sources used in retrieval augmented generation (RAG) systems for targeting purposes. By pinpointing these sources, attackers can focus on poisoning or otherwise manipulating the external data repositories the AI relies on.  RAG-indexed data may be identified in public documentation about the system, or by interacting with the system directly and observing any indications of or references to external data sources.
        ~Documentation Mining: Adversaries analyze public documentation, technical papers, and marketing materials to identify data sources explicitly mentioned as part of the RAG system's knowledge base.
        ~System Response Analysis: Adversaries systematically query the RAG system and analyze responses for citations, references, or metadata that reveal underlying data sources.
        ~Network Traffic Monitoring: Adversaries observe network communications between the RAG system and external data repositories during query processing.
        ~API Endpoint Discovery: Adversaries examine exposed APIs and interfaces to identify connections to external data stores or knowledge bases.
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Adversaries search for exposed configuration files or environment variables that might reveal indexed data sources.
        ~Source Code Review: Adversaries analyze any available source code or deployment scripts for references to external data repositories.
        ~Cache Inspection: Adversaries examine system caches or temporary storage to identify frequently accessed external data sources.
        ~Web Crawling Detection: Adversaries monitor web crawlers and indexing activities that might be associated with the target RAG system.
        ~Vendor Analysis: Adversaries research third-party vendors and services integrated with the target system to identify potential data sources.
        ~Historical Version Analysis: Adversaries examine previous versions or snapshots of the system to track changes in data source usage.
        ~Employee Social Engineering: Adversaries target employees with access to RAG system information through phishing, pretexting, or other social engineering techniques to gain insights about data sources.
        ~Metadata Extraction: Adversaries analyze metadata from RAG system outputs to identify patterns in source attribution or data origin.
        ~Public Repository Correlation: Adversaries cross-reference public data repositories with RAG system responses to identify matching content sources.
        ~Error Message Analysis: Adversaries deliberately trigger system errors to expose information about underlying data sources in error messages or logs.
        ~Job Posting Analysis: Adversaries examine organization job postings and requirements for hints about data management and RAG system architecture.
        ~Conference Presentation Mining: Adversaries analyze technical presentations, demos, and conference materials where organizations discuss their RAG implementations.
        ~Open Source Intelligence (OSINT): Adversaries gather information from public forums, social media, and technical communities where RAG system details might be discussed.
        ~Dependency Analysis: Adversaries examine software dependencies and libraries used by the RAG system to identify potential data source integrations.
        ~Latency Pattern Analysis: Adversaries measure response times across different queries to identify patterns that might reveal different data source access patterns.
        ~Resource Usage Monitoring: Adversaries observe system resource utilization patterns to infer information about external data source access and caching strategies.
        ~Model Behavior Comparison: Adversaries compare responses between different instances or versions of the RAG system to identify changes in underlying data sources.
        ~Infrastructure Mapping: Adversaries analyze the target's cloud infrastructure and network topology to identify potential data storage and indexing services.
        ~Update Pattern Analysis: Adversaries monitor system update patterns and maintenance windows to identify when new data sources might be added or indexed.
        ~Search Result Fingerprinting: Adversaries create unique fingerprints of search results to identify specific data sources based on response patterns.
    Resource Development: The adversary is trying to establish resources they can use to support operations.  Resource Development consists of techniques that involve adversaries creating, purchasing, or compromising/stealing resources that can be used to support targeting. Such resources include AI artifacts, infrastructure, accounts, or capabilities. These resources can be leveraged by the adversary to aid in other phases of the adversary lifecycle, such as [AI Attack Staging](/tactics/AML.TA0001).
      Acquire Public AI Artifacts: Adversaries may search public sources, including cloud storage, public-facing services, and software or data repositories, to identify AI artifacts. These AI artifacts may include the software stack used to train and deploy models, training and testing data, model configurations and parameters. An adversary will be particularly interested in artifacts hosted by or associated with the victim organization as they may represent what that organization uses in a production environment. Adversaries may identify artifact repositories via other resources associated with the victim organization (e.g. [Search Victim-Owned Websites](/techniques/AML.T0003) or [Search Open Technical Databases](/techniques/AML.T0000)). These AI artifacts often provide adversaries with details of the AI task and approach.  AI artifacts can aid in an adversary's ability to [Create Proxy AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0005). If these artifacts include pieces of the actual model in production, they can be used to directly [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043). Acquiring some artifacts requires registration (providing user details such email/name), AWS keys, or written requests, and may require the adversary to [Establish Accounts](/techniques/AML.T0021).  Artifacts might be hosted on victim-controlled infrastructure, providing the victim with some information on who has accessed that data.
        Datasets: Adversaries may collect public datasets to use in their operations. Datasets used by the victim organization or datasets that are representative of the data used by the victim organization may be valuable to adversaries. Datasets can be stored in cloud storage, or on victim-owned websites. Some datasets require the adversary to [Establish Accounts](/techniques/AML.T0021) for access.  Acquired datasets help the adversary advance their operations, stage attacks,  and tailor attacks to the victim organization.
