          Design physical props that exploit specific AI model vulnerabilities in object recognition
          Create active cooling/heating systems to defeat thermal detection patterns
        Serverless: Adversaries may purchase and configure serverless cloud infrastructure, such as Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda functions, or Google Apps Scripts, that can be used during targeting. By utilizing serverless infrastructure, adversaries can make it more difficult to attribute infrastructure used during operations back to them.  Once acquired, the serverless runtime environment can be leveraged to either respond directly to infected machines or to proxy traffic to an adversary-owned command and control server. As traffic generated by these functions will appear to come from subdomains of common cloud providers, it may be difficult to distinguish from ordinary traffic to these providers. This can be used to bypass a Content Security Policy that prevents retrieving content from arbitrary locations.
          Deploy AWS Lambda functions with API Gateway to create serverless C2 infrastructure that blends with legitimate AWS traffic
          Use Cloudflare Workers to create proxy endpoints that relay traffic between compromised systems and attacker infrastructure
          Implement Google Apps Scripts to exfiltrate data through Google Workspace services
          Configure Azure Functions with managed identities to access other cloud resources while maintaining anonymity
          Create serverless functions that automatically scale and rotate IP addresses to evade detection
          Deploy serverless functions across multiple regions to improve resilience and complicate attribution
          Use serverless containers (AWS Fargate/Cloud Run) to run longer-running malicious workloads
          Implement webhook-based serverless functions to relay commands through legitimate services
          Create time-bombed serverless functions that automatically disable after use
          Deploy edge functions (Cloudflare/Fastly) to distribute attack infrastructure globally
          Use Oracle Cloud Functions to create distributed attack infrastructure in less commonly monitored cloud environments
          Deploy Vercel/Netlify serverless functions to blend with legitimate web application traffic
          Implement step functions/durable functions to orchestrate complex multi-stage attacks
          Create scheduled serverless triggers (CloudWatch/Cloud Scheduler) to execute periodic malicious activities
          Use serverless WebSocket APIs for real-time bidirectional communication with compromised systems
          Deploy custom runtime environments in serverless functions to execute specialized malicious code
          Implement cross-account serverless triggers to distribute attack infrastructure
          Use serverless queue processors (SQS/Cloud Tasks) to coordinate distributed attacks
          Deploy serverless functions with custom domains to masquerade as legitimate services
          Implement cold start optimization techniques to maintain function readiness for attacks
          Chain multiple serverless providers to create redundant and harder-to-trace attack paths
          Implement spot instance integration with serverless functions to reduce costs while maintaining capability
          Abuse serverless VPC configurations to access private networks while appearing as legitimate traffic
          Use serverless function URLs with custom authorization to create covert access channels
          Deploy serverless functions with embedded containers to execute complex malware
          Implement cross-region function replication for failover and geographic distribution
          Use serverless environment variables for dynamic configuration of attack infrastructure
          Deploy serverless functions with minimal permissions to avoid security alerts
          Implement graceful degradation in serverless functions to maintain stealth during provider monitoring
          Create serverless dead drop functions for asynchronous command distribution
          Abuse serverless function versioning for persistent backdoors across deployments
          Implement log poisoning techniques in serverless environments to obscure malicious activity
          Use edge functions (Deno Deploy/Cloudflare Workers) to bypass geolocation restrictions
          Deploy serverless functions that exploit auto-scaling limits for DoS attacks
          Implement token vending machines using serverless functions to manage compromised credentials
          Create serverless honey tokens to detect infrastructure scanning attempts
          Use serverless tracing features to monitor victim behavior while appearing as legitimate diagnostics
          Deploy canary functions to detect security team investigation attempts
          Implement serverless functions that exploit cloud provider SDK defaults
          Create ephemeral build environments using serverless functions to compile malware on-demand
      Publish Poisoned Datasets: Adversaries may [Poison Training Data](/techniques/AML.T0020) and publish it to a public location. The poisoned dataset may be a novel dataset or a poisoned variant of an existing open source dataset. This data may be introduced to a victim system via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010).
        ~Public Repository Hosting: Upload poisoned datasets to popular ML model/dataset repositories like Hugging Face, GitHub, or Kaggle
        ~Academic Publication: Publish research papers with accompanying poisoned datasets through academic channels or preprint servers
        ~Data Marketplace Distribution: List poisoned datasets on AI/ML data marketplaces or exchange platforms
        ~Open Data Portal Injection: Upload poisoned datasets to government or organization open data portals
        ~Mirror Site Replacement: Replace legitimate datasets on mirror sites with poisoned versions
        ~Dataset Version Manipulation: Create poisoned versions of popular datasets with similar names or version numbers
        ~Documentation Masquerading: Package poisoned datasets with legitimate-looking documentation and usage guides
        ~Collaborative Platform Seeding: Contribute poisoned datasets to collaborative ML platforms and competitions
        ~Direct Distribution: Directly share poisoned datasets with targets through seemingly legitimate channels
        ~Cloud Storage Hosting: Host poisoned datasets on public cloud storage services with unrestricted access
        ~Social Media Promotion: Promote poisoned datasets through social media channels and professional networks
        ~Package Registry Injection: Include poisoned datasets within legitimate-looking ML framework packages
        ~Dataset CDN Compromise: Host poisoned datasets on compromised content delivery networks
        ~Forum Seeding: Share poisoned datasets through ML/AI community forums and discussion boards
        ~API Service Distribution: Provide poisoned datasets through seemingly legitimate API services
        ~Dataset Federation: Include poisoned datasets in federated learning networks
        ~Browser Extension Distribution: Distribute poisoned datasets through browser extensions for data science
        ~Developer Tool Integration: Bundle poisoned datasets with development tools and environments
        ~Automated Bot Distribution: Use bots to automatically distribute and promote poisoned datasets
        ~Educational Platform Seeding: Share poisoned datasets through educational platforms and tutorials
        ~Model Zoo Poisoning: Include poisoned datasets within model zoo collections and benchmarks
        ~Data Streaming Service: Provide poisoned datasets through real-time data streaming services
        ~Peer-to-Peer Distribution: Share poisoned datasets through P2P networks and protocols
        ~Pre-trained Model Bundling: Bundle poisoned datasets with pre-trained models as supplementary data
        ~Dataset Aggregator Compromise: Insert poisoned datasets into dataset aggregation services
        ~Blockchain Repository: Distribute poisoned datasets through blockchain-based data marketplaces
        ~Edge Device Synchronization: Propagate poisoned datasets through edge device data sharing networks
        ~Dataset Search Engine Optimization: Optimize poisoned dataset visibility in ML dataset search engines
        ~Transfer Learning Repository: Include poisoned datasets in transfer learning resources
        ~Automated Data Pipeline Injection: Insert poisoned datasets into automated data collection pipelines
      Poison Training Data: Adversaries may attempt to poison datasets used by an AI model by modifying the underlying data or its labels. This allows the adversary to embed vulnerabilities in AI models trained on the data that may not be easily detectable. Data poisoning attacks may or may not require modifying the labels. The embedded vulnerability is activated at a later time by data samples with an [Insert Backdoor Trigger](/techniques/AML.T0043.004)  Poisoned data can be introduced via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010) or the data may be poisoned after the adversary gains [Initial Access](/tactics/AML.TA0004) to the system.
        ~Label Flipping: Adversaries modify ground truth labels in training data while keeping the original data samples intact to cause model misclassification
        ~Clean Label Poisoning: Adversaries subtly modify data samples without changing their labels to create backdoors that are hard to detect
        ~Gradient-based Poisoning: Adversaries craft poisoned samples by optimizing them to maximize damage to model performance
        ~Feature Space Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate specific features in the training data to create targeted vulnerabilities
        ~Backdoor Injection: Adversaries insert specific patterns or triggers into training data that cause targeted misclassification
        ~Distribution Shifting: Adversaries modify the statistical distribution of training data to degrade model performance
        ~Transfer Learning Poisoning: Adversaries poison pre-trained models or transfer learning datasets
        ~Data Augmentation Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate data augmentation pipelines to inject poisoned samples
        ~Collaborative Learning Poisoning: Adversaries poison federated or collaborative learning systems through malicious updates
        ~Out-of-Distribution Poisoning: Adversaries inject samples that lie outside the expected data distribution
        ~Time-based Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate temporal patterns in sequential or time-series training data
        ~Ensemble Poisoning: Adversaries craft poisoned samples that specifically target ensemble models
        ~Cross-Modal Poisoning: Adversaries poison relationships between different modalities in multi-modal training data
        ~Watermark Poisoning: Adversaries embed invisible watermarks that act as triggers in visual or audio training data
        ~Semantic Poisoning: Adversaries modify semantic relationships in training data while preserving surface-level features
        ~Query-based Poisoning: Adversaries poison training data through carefully crafted queries in active learning systems
        ~Noise Injection Poisoning: Adversaries systematically inject structured noise to create specific vulnerabilities
        ~Data Sanitization Bypass: Adversaries craft poisoned samples that evade common data cleaning and validation checks
        ~Cache Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate model training data through poisoning of data loading caches or buffers
        ~Reinforcement Learning Poisoning: Adversaries poison reward signals or environment models in RL training
        ~Meta-Learning Poisoning: Adversaries poison meta-learning datasets to affect model adaptation capabilities
        ~Few-Shot Learning Poisoning: Adversaries specifically target few-shot learning scenarios with carefully crafted samples
        ~Domain Adaptation Poisoning: Adversaries poison source or target domain data in domain adaptation scenarios
        ~Curriculum Learning Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate the difficulty progression of training samples
        ~Contrastive Learning Poisoning: Adversaries poison positive/negative pairs in contrastive learning setups
      Establish Accounts: Adversaries may create accounts with various services for use in targeting, to gain access to resources needed in [AI Attack Staging](/tactics/AML.TA0001), or for victim impersonation.
        ~Cloud Service Accounts: Creation of accounts on cloud platforms (AWS, GCP, Azure) to access ML infrastructure and services
        ~Model Repository Accounts: Establishing accounts on ML model repositories (HuggingFace, ModelHub) to access pre-trained models
        ~API Service Accounts: Creating accounts for AI/ML API services (OpenAI, Claude) to access model endpoints
        ~Development Platform Accounts: Setting up accounts on development platforms (GitHub, GitLab) to access ML codebases
        ~Research Community Accounts: Creating accounts on research platforms (arXiv, Papers with Code) to access AI research
        ~Computing Resource Accounts: Establishing accounts for specialized ML computing services (Lambda Labs, Paperspace)
        ~Dataset Platform Accounts: Creating accounts on dataset repositories (Kaggle, UCI) to access training data
        ~Social Media Impersonation: Creating accounts that impersonate ML researchers or organizations
        ~Academic Institution Accounts: Establishing educational accounts to access academic AI resources
        ~Professional Network Accounts: Creating accounts on professional networks to connect with AI practitioners
        ~MLOps Platform Accounts: Creating accounts on MLOps platforms (Weights & Biases, Neptune.ai) for experiment tracking
        ~AI Marketplace Accounts: Establishing accounts on AI model marketplaces (AWS Marketplace, NVIDIA NGC)
        ~Compute Cluster Accounts: Creating accounts for distributed computing resources (SLURM clusters, grid computing)
        ~Data Labeling Service Accounts: Setting up accounts on data annotation platforms (Scale AI, Appen)
        ~AI Competition Platform Accounts: Creating accounts on AI competition sites (DrivenData, AIcrowd)
        ~Model Serving Platform Accounts: Establishing accounts on model deployment platforms (BentoML, Cortex)
        ~Federated Learning Network Accounts: Creating accounts for federated learning platforms
        ~AI Hardware Access Accounts: Setting up accounts for specialized AI hardware services (TPU nodes, GPU clusters)
        ~AI Ethics Review Platform Accounts: Creating accounts on platforms that provide AI bias testing and validation
        ~Synthetic Data Platform Accounts: Establishing accounts on synthetic data generation services
      Publish Poisoned Models: Adversaries may publish a poisoned model to a public location such as a model registry or code repository. The poisoned model may be a novel model or a poisoned variant of an existing open-source model. This model may be introduced to a victim system via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010).
        ~Model Registry Publication: Publishing poisoned models to popular AI model registries (e.g., HuggingFace, PyTorch Hub) while masquerading as legitimate contributors
        ~Repository Typosquatting: Creating repositories with names similar to popular AI projects to trick users into downloading poisoned models
        ~Backdoored Model Variants: Publishing seemingly improved versions of popular models with hidden backdoors
        ~Transfer Learning Poisoning: Publishing pre-trained models specifically designed to cause problems when used for transfer learning
        ~Malicious Model Documentation: Publishing poisoned models with convincing documentation and benchmarks to increase adoption
        ~Framework-Specific Poisoning: Publishing models targeting specific deep learning frameworks with framework-specific vulnerabilities
        ~Dependency Chain Injection: Publishing poisoned models as dependencies of legitimate AI projects
        ~Performance-Baited Poisoning: Publishing models advertising superior performance metrics to attract victims while containing hidden malicious behaviors
        ~Community Contribution Poisoning: Submitting poisoned models as community contributions to established AI projects
        ~Version Update Poisoning: Publishing poisoned updates to existing legitimate models while maintaining version numbering schemes
        ~Ensemble Model Poisoning: Publishing poisoned models as part of an ensemble or model aggregation package
        ~Academic Distribution: Publishing poisoned models through academic papers and research repositories
        ~API Service Poisoning: Publishing poisoned models as managed API services or cloud endpoints
      Publish Hallucinated Entities: Adversaries may create an entity they control, such as a software package, website, or email address to a source hallucinated by an LLM. The hallucinations may take the form of package names commands, URLs, company names, or email addresses that point the victim to the entity controlled by the adversary. When the victim interacts with the adversary-controlled entity, the attack can proceed.
        ~Package Name Squatting: Adversaries may register package names that LLMs commonly hallucinate when asked about software dependencies or development tools, allowing them to distribute malicious code when victims attempt to install these non-existent but convincing-sounding packages.
        ~Domain Registration of Hallucinated URLs: Adversaries may register domain names that match URLs commonly hallucinated by LLMs when asked about official websites, documentation, or resources.
        ~Impersonation of Hallucinated Organizations: Adversaries may create fake company profiles, websites, or social media presence based on non-existent organizations that LLMs might generate when discussing industry leaders or service providers.
        ~Spoofed Email Addresses from Hallucinated Entities: Adversaries may create email addresses that match the pattern of contacts or organizations hallucinated by LLMs, using them for phishing or social engineering attacks.
        ~Technical Documentation Hosting: Adversaries may create fake documentation sites that match the style and content structure that LLMs hallucinate when asked about technical specifications or API documentation.
        ~Social Media Profile Creation: Adversaries may create social media profiles for hallucinated experts, researchers, or thought leaders that LLMs might reference when discussing specific topics.
        ~Code Repository Impersonation: Adversaries may create repositories with names and structures matching those hallucinated by LLMs when discussing source code, libraries, or development resources.
        ~Professional Network Presence: Adversaries may establish fake professional profiles on platforms like LinkedIn based on hallucinated credentials, job titles, or work histories generated by LLMs.
        ~Messaging Platform Accounts: Adversaries may create accounts on messaging platforms using identities hallucinated by LLMs when discussing customer support, technical assistance, or community channels.
        ~Academic Identity Spoofing: Adversaries may create fake academic profiles or paper submissions based on non-existent researchers or institutions hallucinated by LLMs during academic discussions.
      LLM Prompt Crafting: Adversaries may use their acquired knowledge of the target generative AI system to craft prompts that bypass its defenses and allow malicious instructions to be executed.  The adversary may iterate on the prompt to ensure that it works as-intended consistently.
        ~Direct Prompting: Simple, straightforward attempts to get the model to perform malicious actions through direct instructions
        ~Role Playing: Having the model assume a specific persona or role that makes it more likely to comply with malicious requests
        ~Context Manipulation: Providing false or misleading context to influence the model's responses
        ~Chain-of-Thought Manipulation: Breaking down complex malicious instructions into seemingly innocent logical steps
        ~Emotional Manipulation: Using emotional appeals or urgency to bypass ethical constraints
        ~System Prompt Extraction: Attempting to reveal the model's underlying instructions or constraints
        ~Defense Probing: Iteratively testing different prompt variations to identify and exploit weaknesses
        ~Token Smuggling: Hiding malicious instructions within seemingly benign text using special characters or encoding
        ~Multi-Modal Injection: Combining text with other input types to bypass content filters
        ~Prompt Chaining: Using the output of one prompt as input for another to gradually build up to malicious behavior
        ~Language Model Jailbreaking: Using specific phrases or keywords known to bypass content filters
        ~Code Injection Prompting: Embedding code-like structures within natural language to exploit parsing
        ~Recursive Refinement: Iteratively asking the model to improve or modify its own output toward malicious goals
        ~Translation Manipulation: Using language translation to obscure malicious intent
        ~Format Injection: Exploiting specific input format requirements or limitations
        ~Authority Impersonation: Claiming to be system administrators or developers to bypass restrictions
        ~Task Decomposition: Breaking malicious tasks into smaller, seemingly innocent subtasks
        ~Constraint Removal: Explicitly asking the model to ignore previous constraints or rules
        ~Context Window Manipulation: Exploiting the model's limited context window to hide malicious content
        ~Prompt Template Modification: Altering standard prompt templates to bypass security measures
        ~Model Confusion: Introducing ambiguous or contradictory instructions to exploit model uncertainty
        ~Output Parsing Manipulation: Crafting prompts that generate outputs in specific formats to bypass filters
        ~Semantic Transformation: Using synonyms or related concepts to obscure malicious intent
        ~Knowledge Cutoff Exploitation: Leveraging the model's training data cutoff date to bypass newer security measures
        ~Temperature Manipulation: Requesting specific randomness settings to generate more diverse/extreme responses
        ~Few-Shot Learning Exploitation: Using carefully crafted examples to guide the model toward malicious behavior
        ~Prompt Leakage: Exploiting the model's tendency to reveal information about its training or constraints
        ~Response Length Manipulation: Using specific length requirements to force desired outputs
        ~API Parameter Exploitation: Manipulating API call parameters to affect model behavior
        ~Cross-Context Contamination: Using information from one context to influence responses in another
      Retrieval Content Crafting: Adversaries may write content designed to be retrieved by user queries and influence a user of the system in some way. This abuses the trust the user has in the system.  The crafted content can be combined with a prompt injection. It can also stand alone in a separate document or email. The adversary must get the crafted content into the victim\u0027s database, such as a vector database used in a retrieval augmented generation (RAG) system. This may be accomplished via cyber access, or by abusing the ingestion mechanisms common in RAG systems (see [RAG Poisoning](/techniques/AML.T0070)).  Large language models may be used as an assistant to aid an adversary in crafting content.
        ~Factual Manipulation: Craft content that appears legitimate but contains subtle misinformation designed to mislead when retrieved
        ~SEO-Style Optimization: Craft content specifically optimized to rank highly in vector similarity searches
        ~Context Hijacking: Create content that appears benign in isolation but becomes malicious when retrieved in specific contexts
        ~Cross-Reference Poisoning: Plant multiple pieces of content that reference and reinforce each other to increase perceived legitimacy
        ~Emotional Manipulation: Craft content designed to trigger specific emotional responses when retrieved
        ~Authority Impersonation: Create content that mimics authoritative sources or experts
        ~Time-Sensitive Content: Plant content that becomes relevant or misleading at specific times or events
        ~Multi-Modal Manipulation: Combine text with other data types (images, code) to enhance retrieval likelihood and impact
        ~Query-Targeted Content: Craft content specifically designed to match anticipated user queries
        ~Chain-of-Thought Poisoning: Create content that manipulates the reasoning process of AI systems during retrieval
        ~Filter Evasion: Craft content that bypasses content filtering mechanisms while maintaining malicious intent
        ~Platform-Specific Crafting: Create content tailored to exploit specific RAG system implementations
        ~Gradual Truth Distortion: Plant series of content that progressively shifts from truth to misinformation
        ~Semantic Collision: Create content that deliberately conflicts with existing trusted content to create confusion
        ~Template Manipulation: Exploit document templates or standard formats to increase content credibility
        ~Contextual Embedding Poisoning: Craft content that manipulates how text is converted into vector embeddings
        ~Source Attribution Manipulation: Create content with misleading or fake source attributions to increase credibility
        ~Response Pattern Exploitation: Design content to exploit known patterns in how AI systems generate responses
        ~Distributed Content Coordination: Plant related content across multiple sources or systems to create a coordinated effect
        ~Language Model Behavior Exploitation: Craft content that exploits known behaviors or biases of specific language models
        ~Domain-Specific Jargon Manipulation: Create content that misuses technical terminology to appear authoritative in specific fields
        ~Metadata Poisoning: Manipulate content metadata to influence retrieval and interpretation
        ~Training Data Mimicry: Craft content that mimics patterns from known training data to increase perceived legitimacy
        ~Content Chaining: Create content designed to trigger specific chains of retrievals across multiple queries
      Stage Capabilities: Adversaries may upload, install, or otherwise set up capabilities that can be used during targeting. To support their operations, an adversary may need to take capabilities they developed ([Develop Capabilities](/techniques/AML.T0017)) or obtained ([Obtain Capabilities](/techniques/AML.T0016)) and stage them on infrastructure under their control. These capabilities may be staged on infrastructure that was previously purchased/rented by the adversary ([Acquire Infrastructure](/techniques/AML.T0008)) or was otherwise compromised by them. Capabilities may also be staged on web services, such as GitHub, model registries, such as Hugging Face, or container registries.  Adversaries may stage a variety of AI Artifacts including poisoned datasets ([Publish Poisoned Datasets](/techniques/AML.T0019), malicious models ([Publish Poisoned Models](/techniques/AML.T0058)), and prompt injections. They may target names of legitimate companies or products, engage in typosquatting, or use hallucinated entities ([Discover LLM Hallucinations](/techniques/AML.T0062)).
        ~Model Registry Staging: Stage malicious models or artifacts on public model registries like Hugging Face, PyTorch Hub, or TensorFlow Hub using legitimate-looking names or descriptions
        ~Container Registry Abuse: Upload contaminated AI containers or environments to Docker Hub, Amazon ECR, or similar registries
        ~Code Repository Staging: Plant malicious AI-related code, models, or datasets on code hosting platforms like GitHub or GitLab
        ~Cloud Storage Staging: Deploy poisoned training data or models to cloud storage services like S3, GCS, or Azure Blob Storage
        ~CDN Distribution: Position AI artifacts on content delivery networks for widespread distribution
        ~Local Infrastructure Staging: Install malicious AI capabilities on compromised servers or edge devices
        ~API Endpoint Masquerading: Set up fake API endpoints that mimic legitimate AI services
        ~Package Repository Poisoning: Upload malicious AI packages to PyPI, npm, or other package managers
        ~Model-as-Service Setup: Deploy malicious models as seemingly legitimate API services
        ~Dependency Chain Injection: Insert malicious AI components into development and deployment pipelines
        ~Name Squatting: Register and use names similar to popular AI models, datasets, or organizations
        ~Version Masquerading: Stage malicious artifacts using version numbers similar to legitimate releases
        ~Cross-Platform Coordination: Distribute different components of malicious AI capabilities across multiple platforms
        ~Staged Deployment: Gradually deploy malicious capabilities across multiple locations to avoid detection
        ~Mirror Site Abuse: Use legitimate-looking mirror sites to host malicious AI artifacts
        ~Documentation Poisoning: Stage misleading or malicious documentation alongside AI artifacts
        ~Metadata Manipulation: Modify metadata of staged artifacts to appear legitimate or evade detection
        ~Staging Environment Preparation: Configure and prepare hosting environments to support malicious AI capabilities
        ~Automated Staging: Use automation tools and scripts to deploy malicious AI capabilities across multiple platforms
        ~Staged Triggering: Configure staged artifacts with specific activation conditions or timing
        ~Fallback Infrastructure: Establish backup staging locations for resilience against takedowns
        ~Social Proof Building: Create fake user interactions and reviews to establish legitimacy of staged artifacts
        ~Staged Updates: Position malicious components as updates to legitimate AI models or tools
        ~Geographic Distribution: Stage artifacts across different geographic regions to complicate detection and takedown
        ~Access Control Manipulation: Configure deceptive access controls to mask malicious artifacts as private/legitimate resources
    Initial Access: The adversary is trying to gain access to the AI system.  The target system could be a network, mobile device, or an edge device such as a sensor platform. The AI capabilities used by the system could be local with onboard or cloud-enabled AI capabilities.  Initial Access consists of techniques that use various entry vectors to gain their initial foothold within the system.
      AI Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries may gain initial access to a system by compromising the unique portions of the AI supply chain. This could include [Hardware](/techniques/AML.T0010.000), [Data](/techniques/AML.T0010.002) and its annotations, parts of the AI [AI Software](/techniques/AML.T0010.001) stack, or the [Model](/techniques/AML.T0010.003) itself. In some instances the attacker will need secondary access to fully carry out an attack using compromised components of the supply chain.
        Hardware: Adversaries may target AI systems by disrupting or manipulating the hardware supply chain. AI models often run on specialized hardware such as GPUs, TPUs, or embedded devices, but may also be optimized to operate on CPUs.
          Insert hardware backdoors into AI accelerator chips during manufacturing
          Modify firmware of GPU/TPU devices to enable unauthorized access
          Replace genuine AI hardware components with counterfeit/compromised versions
          Tamper with hardware security modules used for model protection
          Manipulate quality control processes to introduce defective AI processing units
          Install malicious hardware trojans in edge AI devices
          Intercept and modify hardware shipments during distribution
          Compromise testing/validation equipment for AI hardware
          Alter power management circuits to enable side-channel attacks
          Replace genuine hardware drivers with compromised versions
          Compromise hardware verification tools to bypass security checks
          Manipulate hardware performance benchmarking tools
          Insert malicious components during authorized maintenance
          Modify hardware specifications documentation to hide malicious changes
          Tamper with hardware initialization sequences
          Compromise hardware-based random number generators
          Manipulate voltage/clock settings to induce hardware failures
          Install covert physical access mechanisms during assembly
          Modify hardware memory protection mechanisms
          Compromise hardware-level model encryption modules
          Social engineer hardware manufacturing facility personnel
          Compromise hardware development/design tools
          Exploit hardware debug interfaces and test points
          Manipulate thermal management systems to degrade AI performance
          Bypass hardware root of trust mechanisms
          Insert malicious components during hardware RMA process
          Modify hardware-level access control lists
          Compromise hardware supply chain tracking systems
          Exploit hardware self-test mechanisms
          Manipulate hardware-based model protection features
        AI Software: Most AI systems rely on a limited set of AI frameworks. An adversary could get access to a large number of AI systems through a comprise of one of their supply chains. Many AI projects also rely on other open source implementations of various algorithms. These can also be compromised in a targeted way to get access to specific systems.
          Upload malicious versions of popular AI libraries to PyPI/npm with similar names (typosquatting)
          Inject malicious code into GitHub repositories of common AI frameworks during pull requests
          Compromise build systems or CI/CD pipelines of major AI frameworks
          Create malicious forks of popular AI repositories with backdoored dependencies
          Exploit version control systems to inject code into AI framework source code
          Deploy compromised versions of Jupyter notebooks or development environments
          Insert vulnerabilities into mathematical computation libraries used by AI frameworks
          Manipulate container images of AI development environments
          Compromise package signing keys of AI framework distributors
          Plant backdoors in pre-trained model conversion tools
          Compromise AutoML framework components to inject backdoors
          Insert malicious code into model optimization and compression tools
          Deploy compromised versions of popular AI serving frameworks
          Manipulate GPU/TPU acceleration libraries used in AI frameworks
          Insert vulnerabilities into data preprocessing pipeline tools
          Compromise machine learning experiment tracking platforms
          Plant backdoors in model versioning and registry systems
          Inject malicious code into AI model deployment automation tools
          Compromise AI model interpretation and visualization libraries
          Insert vulnerabilities into distributed training framework components
        Data: Data is a key vector of supply chain compromise for adversaries. Every AI project will require some form of data. Many rely on large open source datasets that are publicly available. An adversary could rely on compromising these sources of data. The malicious data could be a result of [Poison Training Data](/techniques/AML.T0020) or include traditional malware.  An adversary can also target private datasets in the labeling phase. The creation of private datasets will often require the hiring of outside labeling services. An adversary can poison a dataset by modifying the labels being generated by the labeling service.
          Insert malicious samples into popular open source datasets on platforms like GitHub, Kaggle, or HuggingFace
          Compromise third-party data labeling services by infiltrating as workers and deliberately mislabeling data
          Modify data labels in distributed annotation platforms like LabelStudio or CVAT
          Inject adversarial examples into public benchmark datasets
          Plant malware-infected files within image or document training datasets
          Manipulate metadata and annotations in crowdsourced datasets
          Replace legitimate data samples with poisoned versions during data collection pipeline
          Introduce biased or incorrect labels through automated labeling services
          Corrupt data preprocessing scripts used in dataset creation
          Target data storage systems (S3 buckets, data lakes) to modify stored training data
          Compromise data version control systems to inject poisoned historical versions
          Manipulate data augmentation pipelines to introduce systematic biases
          Exploit automated data cleaning tools to selectively remove important samples
          Tamper with real-time data collection sensors or IoT devices
          Insert poisoned samples during data validation/QA processes
          Modify data splitting/sampling scripts to create biased train/test sets
          Compromise data synchronization mechanisms between distributed training systems
          Target backup restoration processes to replace clean data with poisoned versions
          Exploit data formatting/conversion tools to introduce subtle corruptions
          Manipulate data filtering criteria to selectively exclude legitimate samples
          Exploit data integration tools that merge multiple datasets to inject poisoned samples
          Compromise pre-trained model datasets used in transfer learning
          Manipulate data governance tools to bypass data quality checks
          Target external data providers' APIs to poison data streams
          Exploit data sampling mechanisms to create skewed distributions
          Insert backdoors during dataset anonymization processes
          Compromise data normalization procedures to introduce systematic errors
          Manipulate feature extraction pipelines during data preparation
          Target data validation rules in ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) processes
          Exploit data deduplication systems to selectively remove important samples
          Manipulate data caching systems to serve poisoned samples during training
          Exploit data schema evolution to introduce malicious field mappings
          Poison local datasets in federated learning participants
          Insert anomalies in time-series data collection windows
          Compromise cross-validation fold generation to create biased evaluations
          Target data streaming buffer systems in real-time learning
          Exploit data type conversion processes to introduce subtle corruptions
          Manipulate data windowing mechanisms in sequential data processing
          Compromise data shuffling algorithms to create systematic patterns
          Target data interpolation systems for missing value imputation
          Exploit alignment between different modalities in multi-modal datasets
          Compromise data encryption/decryption processes to modify data during transit
          Manipulate data compression algorithms to selectively corrupt samples
          Tamper with data lineage records to hide poisoning evidence
          Exploit class balancing techniques to introduce dataset bias
          Target data weighting mechanisms in importance sampling
          Manipulate data tokenization processes in text datasets
          Compromise data quantization procedures in numerical features
          Exploit data masking/anonymization tools to introduce backdoors
          Target data partitioning strategies in distributed datasets
        Model: AI-enabled systems often rely on open sourced models in various ways. Most commonly, the victim organization may be using these models for fine tuning. These models will be downloaded from an external source and then used as the base for the model as it is tuned on a smaller, private dataset. Loading models often requires executing some saved code in the form of a saved model file. These can be compromised with traditional malware, or through some adversarial AI techniques.
          Upload compromised model variants to popular model repositories (e.g., HuggingFace, PyTorch Hub) with names similar to legitimate models
          Inject malicious code into model serialization formats (e.g., pickle files in PyTorch models)
          Create backdoored versions of popular pre-trained models by fine-tuning them with poisoned data
          Modify model weight files to include executable payloads that trigger during model loading
          Compromise model packaging and distribution infrastructure to replace legitimate models with malicious versions
          Exploit model loading libraries' deserialization vulnerabilities to execute arbitrary code
          Insert malicious code into model conversion tools or optimization scripts
          Distribute compromised model configuration files that point to attacker-controlled resources
          Modify model checkpoint files to include malicious initialization routines
          Plant backdoors in model compression or quantization tools used in the deployment pipeline
          Create fake research papers/blogs promoting compromised models with claimed SOTA results
          Exploit model format conversion processes between different frameworks (e.g., ONNX conversion)
          Hide malicious code in custom model layers or operators that appear legitimate
          Modify model metadata and manifest files to bypass integrity checks
          Insert malicious callbacks in model loading hooks and custom data loaders
          Compromise model registry authentication tokens to push malicious updates
          Exploit auto-update mechanisms in ML pipelines to distribute compromised models
          Plant malware in model optimization profiles and hardware acceleration configs
          Manipulate model version tags and checksums to force downloads of compromised versions
          Insert exploits in model quantization tables and calibration data
          Compromise model-serving containers with malicious initialization scripts
          Insert exploits in model pruning and sparsification routines
          Plant malware in model export formats specific to edge devices/mobile deployment
          Manipulate model architecture search (AutoML) components to include malicious modules
          Insert backdoors in model ensembling and aggregation procedures
          Compromise transfer learning interfaces with malicious adaptation layers
          Modify model caching mechanisms to persist malicious code
          Plant exploits in model monitoring and telemetry components
          Insert malware in model fallback/failover mechanisms
          Compromise model warm-up/initialization datasets with poisoned examples
          Inject malicious code into model evaluation metrics that execute during validation
          Compromise model interpretation/visualization tools to execute malware during analysis
          Insert exploits in cross-model knowledge distillation procedures
          Plant malware in model checkpoint merging utilities
          Manipulate gradient computation modules with malicious code execution
          Insert backdoors in model architecture visualization tools
          Compromise model performance profiling tools to execute exploits
          Plant malware in model comparison and A/B testing frameworks
          Insert exploits in model debugging and tracing tools
          Modify model parameter visualization libraries to include malicious code
          Insert malware into model governance workflow automation scripts
          Compromise model experiment tracking platforms (e.g., MLflow, Weights & Biases) with malicious plugins
          Plant exploits in model versioning diff tools
          Manipulate collaborative model development environments with malicious extensions
          Insert backdoors in model compliance checking tools
          Compromise model registry synchronization mechanisms
          Plant malware in model provenance tracking systems
          Insert exploits in model documentation generation tools
          Modify model audit logging components to execute malicious code
          Compromise model metric collection agents with malicious payloads
        Container Registry: An adversary may compromise a victim's container registry by pushing a manipulated container image and overwriting an existing container name and/or tag. Users of the container registry as well as automated CI/CD pipelines may pull the adversary's container image, compromising their AI Supply Chain. This can affect development and deployment environments.  Container images may include AI models, so the compromised image could have an AI model which was manipulated by the adversary (See [Manipulate AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0018)).
          Use compromised credentials to push malicious container images to Azure Container Registry
          Exploit misconfigured registry permissions in Docker Hub to overwrite official AI model containers
          Intercept and modify container image layers during pull/push operations using MITM attacks
          Replace legitimate container tags with malicious images in automated CI/CD workflows
          Inject backdoored dependencies into base images used for AI model training
          Exploit vulnerabilities in registry authentication to gain unauthorized push access
          Manipulate container metadata to redirect pulls to malicious mirrors
          Use typosquatting of popular AI model container names to distribute compromised images
          Exploit weak access controls to poison automated build processes
          Leverage compromised build servers to inject malicious code during container creation
          Exploit cross-account container image sharing in Amazon ECR to distribute compromised images
          Manipulate container image manifest files to bypass integrity checks
          Poison container registry caches by exploiting replication vulnerabilities
          Use container image squashing to hide malicious layers from scanners
          Exploit container registry webhook configurations to trigger malicious actions
          Target private registry mirrors with outdated security patches
          Manipulate container image signing keys to bypass trust verification
          Exploit container registry quota limits to cause denial of service
          Use container layer caching poisoning in build systems
          Compromise container registry backup/restore processes
          Exploit Kubernetes admission controllers to bypass container image policy enforcement
          Manipulate geo-replicated registry configurations to maintain persistent access
          Exploit container registry role assumptions in cloud IAM systems
          Poison container image vulnerability scan databases to bypass security controls
          Exploit container registry API rate limiting for denial of service
          Manipulate registry garbage collection to preserve malicious layers
          Exploit container registry search indexing for cache poisoning
          Target air-gapped registry synchronization processes
          Exploit container registry event notifications for lateral movement
          Manipulate container image layer deduplication systems
          Exploit registry federation trust relationships between organizations
          Manipulate registry operator custom resource definitions in Kubernetes
          Exploit container image layer compression algorithms to hide malicious content
          Target registry storage backend (S3, blob storage) direct access
          Compromise third-party registry scanning service integrations
          Exploit registry metrics collection systems for information disclosure
          Manipulate registry load balancer configurations for traffic interception
          Exploit container registry DNS cache poisoning
          Target registry audit logging systems to hide malicious activity
          Exploit container registry proxy configurations
      Valid Accounts: Adversaries may obtain and abuse credentials of existing accounts as a means of gaining Initial Access. Credentials may take the form of usernames and passwords of individual user accounts or API keys that provide access to various AI resources and services.  Compromised credentials may provide access to additional AI artifacts and allow the adversary to perform [Discover AI Artifacts](/techniques/AML.T0007). Compromised credentials may also grant an adversary increased privileges such as write access to AI artifacts used during development or production.
        ~Cloud AI Platform Accounts: Compromise of accounts for managed AI services like AWS SageMaker, Azure ML, or Google AI Platform
        ~Model Repository Credentials: Access to model hosting platforms like HuggingFace, MLflow, or internal model repositories
        ~Development Environment Access: Compromise of credentials for AI development environments, notebooks, or training infrastructure
        ~API Access Keys: Theft or abuse of API keys used for accessing AI services, model endpoints, or data pipelines
        ~CI/CD Pipeline Accounts: Compromise of accounts used in AI model development and deployment pipelines
        ~Data Science Team Accounts: Targeted compromise of accounts belonging to data scientists and ML engineers
        ~Model Registry Service Accounts: Abuse of service accounts used for model versioning and artifact storage
        ~Training Infrastructure Accounts: Compromise of accounts used to access GPU clusters or distributed training resources
        ~Experiment Tracking Accounts: Access to experiment tracking platforms like Weights & Biases or MLflow
        ~Data Pipeline Service Accounts: Compromise of accounts used for data preparation and feature engineering pipelines
        ~Model Monitoring Accounts: Credentials for accessing model performance monitoring and observability platforms
        ~AI Governance Platform Accounts: Access to platforms managing AI model compliance, documentation, and approvals
        ~Edge Device Management Accounts: Credentials for managing AI-enabled edge devices and their model deployments
        ~Data Labeling Service Accounts: Access to platforms used for data annotation and labeling services
        ~Third-party Model API Accounts: Credentials for external AI services and pre-trained model APIs
        ~Model Marketplace Accounts: Access to commercial AI model marketplaces and exchanges
        ~Hardware Acceleration Management Accounts: Credentials for managing specialized AI hardware resources
        ~AI Platform Admin Accounts: High-privilege accounts for AI platform administration
        ~Model Testing Environment Accounts: Access to separate environments used for model validation and testing
        ~Data Storage Service Accounts: Credentials specific to training data storage systems and data lakes
      Evade AI Model: Adversaries can [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043) that prevent a AI model from correctly identifying the contents of the data. This technique can be used to evade a downstream task where AI is utilized. The adversary may evade AI based virus/malware detection, or network scanning towards the goal of a traditional cyber attack.
        ~Gradient-Based Perturbation: Add carefully calculated noise to input data using gradient information from the target model to cause misclassification
        ~Transfer Attack: Use adversarial examples generated from a surrogate model to attack the target model
        ~Black-Box Query: Systematically probe the model's behavior to craft adversarial examples without internal model knowledge
        ~Input Transformation: Apply transformations like rotation, scaling, or color shifts to evade detection
        ~Feature Space Manipulation: Modify specific features known to be important for the model's decision-making
        ~Patch Attack: Add localized adversarial patches to physical objects to fool computer vision systems
        ~Natural Perturbation: Use naturally occurring variations or transformations that preserve semantic meaning but cause misclassification
        ~Model Extraction and Replay: Extract model information through queries and use it to generate effective adversarial examples
        ~Ensemble Attack: Combine multiple evasion techniques to increase success probability
        ~Temporal Attack: Exploit temporal dependencies in sequential models to create time-based evasions
        ~Universal Adversarial Perturbation: Create a single perturbation pattern that causes misclassification across multiple inputs
        ~Decision Boundary Manipulation: Exploit the model's decision boundaries by finding minimal perturbations that cross them
        ~Semantic Manipulation: Modify semantic attributes of the input while preserving its apparent legitimacy
        ~Backdoor Injection: Insert hidden triggers that activate evasion behavior when present
        ~Rate-Limited Attack: Gradually modify inputs over time to avoid detection by rate-limiting defenses
        ~Cross-Modal Attack: Exploit vulnerabilities in multi-modal AI systems by manipulating different input modalities
        ~Context Manipulation: Modify the context or surrounding elements without changing the target object
        ~Optimization-Based Attack: Use sophisticated optimization algorithms to find minimal adversarial perturbations
        ~Architecture-Specific Attack: Exploit vulnerabilities specific to certain neural network architectures
        ~Data Poisoning for Evasion: Manipulate training data to create specific vulnerabilities for later exploitation
        ~Audio-Based Evasion: Generate adversarial audio signals to evade speech recognition or audio classification systems
        ~Text-Based Manipulation: Modify text inputs using synonyms, misspellings, or special characters to evade NLP models
        ~Confidence Manipulation: Exploit model confidence scores to craft inputs that produce low-confidence or ambiguous results
        ~Distribution Shift Attack: Exploit model vulnerabilities to distribution changes in input data
        ~Hardware-Level Attack: Manipulate model behavior through hardware-specific vulnerabilities or timing attacks
        ~Adaptive Attack: Dynamically adjust evasion strategy based on model responses
        ~Transfer Learning Attack: Exploit vulnerabilities in transfer learning scenarios where models share features
        ~Attention Manipulation: Target attention mechanisms in neural networks to misdirect model focus
        ~Quantization Attack: Exploit vulnerabilities introduced by model quantization or compression
        ~Edge Case Exploitation: Target corner cases and decision boundaries where model performance is naturally weak
      Exploit Public-Facing Application: Adversaries may attempt to take advantage of a weakness in an Internet-facing computer or program using software, data, or commands in order to cause unintended or unanticipated behavior. The weakness in the system can be a bug, a glitch, or a design vulnerability. These applications are often websites, but can include databases (like SQL), standard services (like SMB or SSH), network device administration and management protocols (like SNMP and Smart Install), and any other applications with Internet accessible open sockets, such as web servers and related services.
        ~ML API Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in public-facing ML model APIs to gain unauthorized access or manipulate model behavior
        ~Model Serving Platform Attacks: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in ML model serving platforms like TensorFlow Serving or MLflow
        ~Framework Vulnerability Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit known vulnerabilities in AI/ML frameworks exposed through web interfaces
        ~Pipeline Interface Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in data pipeline interfaces used for model training and deployment
        ~Model Registry Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in model registry systems to gain unauthorized access
        ~Notebook Server Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in public-facing Jupyter notebook servers
        ~AutoML Platform Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in automated ML platforms exposed to the internet
        ~Feature Store Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in feature stores used for ML feature management
        ~Model Monitoring Interface Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in model monitoring and observability platforms
        ~Inference Endpoint Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in model inference endpoints
        ~Hardware Accelerator Interface Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in interfaces managing AI accelerators like GPUs or TPUs
        ~Distributed Training System Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in distributed training coordination services
        ~Edge Deployment Platform Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in edge AI deployment and management platforms
        ~Model Compression Service Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in model optimization and compression services
        ~AI Authentication Bypass: Adversaries may exploit weaknesses in AI-specific authentication mechanisms like API tokens or model access controls
        ~Hyperparameter Optimization Service Exploitation: Adversaries may target automated hyperparameter tuning services
        ~Model Version Control Interface Attacks: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in model version control systems
        ~AI Resource Scheduler Exploitation: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in AI workload scheduling systems
        ~AI Cloud Service Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in AI-specific cloud services and their management interfaces
        ~Model Marketplace Interface Attacks: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in public model marketplace platforms
        ~Transfer Learning Platform Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in transfer learning services and model adaptation platforms
        ~Federated Learning System Attacks: Adversaries may target vulnerabilities in federated learning coordination and aggregation services
        ~AI Visualization Tool Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in public-facing model visualization and interpretation tools
        ~AI System Debug Interface Attacks: Adversaries may target exposed debugging interfaces for AI systems
        ~Model Performance Benchmarking Service Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in model benchmarking platforms
      Phishing: Adversaries may send phishing messages to gain access to victim systems. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass malware spam campaigns.  Generative AI, including LLMs that generate synthetic text, visual deepfakes of faces, and audio deepfakes of speech, is enabling adversaries to scale targeted phishing campaigns. LLMs can interact with users via text conversations and can be programmed with a meta prompt to phish for sensitive information. Deepfakes can be use in impersonation as an aid to phishing.
        Spearphishing via Social Engineering LLM: Adversaries may turn LLMs into targeted social engineers. LLMs are capable of interacting with users via text conversations. They can be instructed by an adversary to seek sensitive information from a user and act as effective social engineers. They can be targeted towards particular personas defined by the adversary. This allows adversaries to scale spearphishing efforts and target individuals to reveal private information such as credentials to privileged systems.
          Use prompt injection to make LLM impersonate IT support requesting password reset verification
          Configure LLM with company-specific knowledge to pose as internal employee seeking access
          Deploy LLM chatbot on fake corporate help desk portal to harvest credentials
          Program LLM with executive persona to request urgent financial transfers from employees
          Chain LLM with email automation to scale personalized spearphishing messages
          Use LLM to dynamically adjust conversation based on target's LinkedIn profile
          Create LLM-powered chat interface mimicking legitimate corporate communication tools
          Engineer multi-turn conversations where LLM gradually builds trust before requesting sensitive data
          Combine LLM with scrapped corporate data to make highly convincing impersonation attempts
          Deploy LLM on messaging platforms posing as recruitment bot to collect personal information
          Use LLM to analyze target's writing style from public posts and mirror it in conversations
          Deploy LLM across multiple platforms simultaneously to create coordinated social proof
          Program LLM to detect and adapt to target's emotional state during conversation
          Combine LLM with voice synthesis for follow-up phone call validation
          Use LLM to generate context-aware excuses for unusual requests
          Configure LLM to maintain consistent backstory across multiple interaction channels
          Deploy LLM with built-in urgency triggers based on target's schedule/timezone
          Use LLM to generate customized pretexts based on target's recent professional activities
          Program LLM to handle security question challenges by gathering open-source intelligence
          Configure LLM to simulate technical issues to justify unusual authentication requests
          Program LLM to maintain conversation threads across multiple sessions to avoid detection
          Use LLM to generate believable error messages and system notifications as social proof
          Configure LLM to detect and circumvent common security awareness training indicators
          Deploy LLM with fallback conversation paths when initial phishing attempts are questioned
          Program LLM to correlate target's public calendar events for optimal timing of requests
          Use LLM to generate and maintain multiple consistent personas for complex scenarios
          Configure LLM to simulate organizational workflows and approval chains
          Deploy LLM with department-specific jargon and procedures learned from public documents
          Program LLM to generate contextual references to ongoing company projects or initiatives
          Use LLM to create convincing follow-up sequences after initial contact
          Use LLM to analyze target's out-of-office responses and adjust timing automatically
          Configure LLM to generate contextual replies to security team investigations
          Program LLM to simulate system maintenance workflows during off-hours
          Deploy LLM with capability to generate authentic-looking documentation on demand
          Use LLM to create parallel conversation threads with multiple targets in same organization
          Configure LLM to adapt language complexity based on target's role and background
          Program LLM to generate convincing crisis scenarios requiring immediate action
          Use LLM to maintain detailed conversation logs for improving future attempts
          Deploy LLM with capability to generate custom forms and validation processes
          Configure LLM to simulate inter-departmental communications and approvals
      Drive-by Compromise: Adversaries may gain access to an AI system through a user visiting a website over the normal course of browsing, or an AI agent retrieving information from the web on behalf of a user. Websites can contain an [LLM Prompt Injection](/techniques/AML.T0051) which, when executed, can change the behavior of the AI model.  The same approach may be used to deliver other types of malicious code that don't target AI directly (see [Drive-by Compromise in ATT&CK](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189/)).
        ~Malicious Training Data Links: Adversaries embed links to poisoned training data that gets automatically ingested by AI systems during web scraping
        ~Watering Hole via AI Development Resources: Compromising websites commonly used by AI developers or data scientists
        ~Search Result Poisoning: Manipulating search results to lead AI agents to compromised content during automated data gathering
        ~API Endpoint Compromise: Targeting web APIs used by AI systems for data retrieval or model serving
        ~Browser Extension Exploitation: Compromising browser extensions used for AI-assisted browsing or content generation
        ~Automated Scraping Hijacking: Intercepting automated web scraping processes used for AI training
        ~Code Repository Infection: Compromising AI model code or dependencies hosted on public repositories
        ~Interactive Tutorial Exploitation: Embedding malicious content in interactive AI/ML tutorials or notebooks
        ~Model Marketplace Compromise: Distributing compromised models through legitimate AI model marketplaces
        ~Documentation Site Manipulation: Injecting malicious content into AI documentation and reference materials
        ~Cloud Service Widget Compromise: Targeting embedded AI service widgets that automatically load on websites
        ~Inference Request Interception: Manipulating web-based inference requests to AI models
        ~Collaborative Notebook Poisoning: Compromising shared Jupyter/Colab notebooks with malicious AI-related code
        ~Model Config Manipulation: Injecting malicious configuration files during web-based model deployment
        ~Data Pipeline Compromise: Targeting automated ETL processes that fetch web data for AI systems
        ~Framework CDN Poisoning: Compromising AI framework files served through content delivery networks
        ~Model Parameter Injection: Manipulating model parameters during web-based transfer learning
        ~Web Service Hook Exploitation: Compromising webhooks used by AI systems for automated updates
        ~Cache Poisoning: Manipulating cached AI model responses or predictions
        ~Federated Learning Node Compromise: Targeting web-based federated learning participants
        ~Browser-Based Model Hijacking: Compromising browser-native machine learning features
        ~Telemetry Collection Compromise: Exploiting web-based AI system monitoring tools
        ~Model Optimization Service Attack: Targeting online model compression or optimization services
        ~Edge Model Update Exploitation: Compromising over-the-web model updates for edge devices
        ~AI Plugin Store Compromise: Distributing malicious AI plugins through legitimate marketplaces
        ~Performance Monitoring Injection: Exploiting web-based AI performance monitoring systems
        ~AutoML Service Manipulation: Compromising automated machine learning services
        ~Model Version Control Attack: Targeting web-based model version control systems
        ~AI Debugging Tool Compromise: Exploiting web-based AI debugging and visualization tools
        ~Online Feature Store Poisoning: Compromising feature stores used by web-connected AI systems
        ~AI Security Scanner Bypass: Compromising web-based AI security scanning tools
        ~Model-to-Model Communication Hijacking: Intercepting communications between collaborative AI models
        ~AI Authentication Token Theft: Exploiting web-based AI service authentication mechanisms
        ~Orchestration Platform Compromise: Targeting AI workflow orchestration platforms
        ~AI Sandbox Environment Escape: Exploiting web-based AI testing environments
        ~Model Registry Poisoning: Compromising centralized model registry services
        ~AI Service Mesh Attack: Targeting service mesh components handling AI workloads
        ~Experiment Tracking Exploitation: Compromising ML experiment tracking platforms
        ~Model Serving Gateway Attack: Targeting API gateways for AI model serving
        ~AI Resource Scheduler Compromise: Exploiting web-based AI resource scheduling systems
    AI Model Access: The adversary is attempting to gain some level of access to an AI model.  AI Model Access enables techniques that use various types of access to the AI model that can be used by the adversary to gain information, develop attacks, and as a means to input data to the model. The level of access can range from the full knowledge of the internals of the model to access to the physical environment where data is collected for use in the AI model. The adversary may use varying levels of model access during the course of their attack, from staging the attack to impacting the target system.  Access to an AI model may require access to the system housing the model, the model may be publically accessible via an API, or it may be accessed indirectly via interaction with a product or service that utilizes AI as part of its processes.
      AI Model Inference API Access: Adversaries may gain access to a model via legitimate access to the inference API. Inference API access can be a source of information to the adversary ([Discover AI Model Ontology](/techniques/AML.T0013), [Discover AI Model Family](/techniques/AML.T0014)), a means of staging the attack ([Verify Attack](/techniques/AML.T0042), [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043)), or for introducing data to the target system for Impact ([Evade AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0015), [Erode AI Model Integrity](/techniques/AML.T0031)).  Many systems rely on the same models provided via an inference API, which means they share the same vulnerabilities. This is especially true of foundation models which are prohibitively resource intensive to train. Adversaries may use their access to model APIs to identify vulnerabilities such as jailbreaks or hallucinations and then target applications that use the same models.
        ~Public API Subscription: Access model through legitimate public API subscriptions or free tiers offered by AI service providers
        ~API Key Harvesting: Collect and use exposed API keys from public code repositories, documentation, or leaked credentials
        ~Rate Limit Bypass: Circumvent API usage restrictions through distributed requests or manipulation of request patterns
        ~API Version Exploitation: Target specific API versions known to have vulnerabilities or fewer security controls
        ~Request Parameter Manipulation: Modify API request parameters to probe model boundaries and behaviors
        ~API Documentation Mining: Extract model information and capabilities through detailed analysis of API documentation
        ~Session Token Manipulation: Exploit session management weaknesses in API authentication
        ~Proxy Service Utilization: Access restricted APIs through intermediate proxy services or aggregators
        ~API Response Analysis: Systematic analysis of API responses to infer model characteristics and vulnerabilities
        ~Service Account Abuse: Exploit legitimate service accounts or API tokens with broader access privileges
        ~Enterprise License Abuse: Exploit enterprise-wide API licenses to gain broader access across multiple services
        ~Trial Account Chaining: Create multiple trial accounts in sequence to maintain continuous API access
        ~Reseller Channel Exploitation: Gain API access through legitimate reseller or partner channels
        ~Integration Webhook Abuse: Exploit legitimate webhook integrations to gain indirect API access
        ~API Gateway Manipulation: Target API gateway configurations to bypass access controls
        ~Cross-Service Authentication: Leverage OAuth or SSO mechanisms to gain API access through connected services
        ~Development Environment Access: Exploit development or staging environments with looser API restrictions
        ~API Aggregator Exploitation: Use legitimate API aggregation services to mask malicious access patterns
        ~Shared Token Exploitation: Leverage shared or improperly secured API tokens within organizations
        ~Service Level Agreement Exploitation: Manipulate SLA-based API access controls for extended privileges
        ~Container Registry Access: Exploit container registry permissions to access model API endpoints
        ~Serverless Function Chaining: Chain serverless functions to aggregate or modify API access patterns
        ~API Cache Poisoning: Manipulate cached API responses to affect model behavior
        ~Load Balancer Manipulation: Exploit load balancer configurations to gain preferred API access
        ~SDK Vulnerability Exploitation: Leverage vulnerabilities in official API SDKs to gain unauthorized access
        ~API Versioning Abuse: Exploit differences between API versions to bypass restrictions
        ~Automated Bot Access: Use automated systems to maintain persistent API access
        ~Regional Endpoint Exploitation: Leverage geographic API endpoint differences for access
        ~API Logging Evasion: Manipulate request patterns to avoid detection in API logs
        ~Model-Specific Protocol Abuse: Exploit unique protocols used for specific model types
      AI-Enabled Product or Service: Adversaries may use a product or service that uses artificial intelligence under the hood to gain access to the underlying AI model. This type of indirect model access may reveal details of the AI model or its inferences in logs or metadata.
        ~Consumer AI Assistant Interaction: Adversaries may interact with consumer AI assistants (e.g., Siri, Alexa) to gather information about model responses and limitations
        ~Enterprise Software Analysis: Adversaries may analyze AI-powered enterprise software (e.g., CRM systems, document processing) to understand underlying model behavior
        ~Security Tool Probing: Adversaries may probe AI-powered security tools (e.g., anomaly detection, fraud prevention) to study detection patterns
        ~Cloud Service API Testing: Adversaries may test cloud services with AI capabilities to examine model responses and metadata
        ~Social Media Platform Testing: Adversaries may interact with AI-powered features on social media platforms to understand content moderation models
        ~Development Tool Exploitation: Adversaries may interact with AI-powered code completion, code review, or debugging tools to understand model behaviors and limitations
        ~Content Generation Analysis: Adversaries may probe AI-powered content generation systems (e.g., image, text, video generators) to study model responses
        ~Embedded System Interaction: Adversaries may interact with AI features embedded in hardware devices (e.g., smart cameras, IoT devices) to gather model information
        ~Recommendation System Probing: Adversaries may analyze AI-powered recommendation engines to understand ranking and filtering models
        ~Automated Decision System Testing: Adversaries may interact with automated decision-making systems (e.g., loan approval, hiring systems) to study decision boundaries
      Physical Environment Access: In addition to the attacks that take place purely in the digital domain, adversaries may also exploit the physical environment for their attacks. If the model is interacting with data collected from the real world in some way, the adversary can influence the model through access to wherever the data is being collected. By modifying the data in the collection process, the adversary can perform modified versions of attacks designed for digital access.
        ~Sensor Tampering: Physically interfering with or modifying sensors that collect input data for AI systems, such as cameras, microphones, or IoT devices
        ~Environmental Manipulation: Altering ambient conditions like lighting, temperature, or sound that affect data collection
        ~Physical Obstruction: Blocking or partially obscuring sensors or data collection equipment
        ~Adversarial Objects: Placing specially crafted physical objects in the environment that are designed to mislead AI systems
        ~Signal Interference: Creating electromagnetic or other forms of interference that affect sensor readings
        ~Collection Point Access: Gaining unauthorized physical access to locations where data collection occurs
        ~Hardware Modification: Physically altering or replacing hardware components used in data collection
        ~Calibration Tampering: Manipulating the calibration of sensors or measurement devices
        ~Environmental Staging: Deliberately arranging the physical environment to create specific conditions or scenarios
        ~Collection Timeline Manipulation: Interfering with when or how often data is collected from the physical environment
        ~Proxy Object Placement: Using physical objects that serve as proxies or surrogates to confuse or mislead AI system identification
        ~Natural Phenomenon Exploitation: Taking advantage of natural conditions like weather, time of day, or seasonal changes to affect data collection
        ~Physical Replay Attacks: Recreating physical conditions or events to replay previously captured scenarios
        ~Multi-Sensor Coordination: Simultaneously manipulating multiple physical inputs to create coherent false scenarios
        ~Collection Path Interference: Modifying the physical path between sensors and the observed environment
        ~Mechanical Vibration: Inducing physical vibrations or movements that affect sensor accuracy
        ~Surface Modification: Altering physical surfaces or materials that interact with sensors
        ~Physical Decoy Deployment: Using physical decoys to distract or mislead data collection systems
        ~Biological Interface Tampering: Manipulating biological or organic components that interact with AI systems
        ~Physical Access Control Bypass: Circumventing physical security measures protecting data collection points
        ~Atmospheric Condition Manipulation: Modifying air quality, humidity, or particulate matter that could affect sensor readings
        ~Thermal Pattern Exploitation: Creating specific heat patterns or thermal signatures to deceive temperature-sensitive sensors
        ~Physical Camouflage Application: Applying specialized materials or coatings that affect sensor detection capabilities
        ~Motion Pattern Injection: Introducing specific movement patterns in the environment to influence motion detection
        ~Reflection/Refraction Exploitation: Using mirrors, lenses, or other optical elements to manipulate visual inputs
        ~Physical Context Manipulation: Altering the surrounding context or background of target objects
        ~Material Property Exploitation: Using materials with specific properties (e.g., retroreflective, IR-absorbing) to affect sensing
        ~Physical Timing Attack: Exploiting the physical limitations of sensor sampling rates or response times
        ~Cross-Modal Physical Interference: Creating physical effects that impact multiple sensing modalities simultaneously
        ~Sensor Field-of-View Manipulation: Exploiting the physical limitations of sensor coverage areas
        ~Physical Scale Manipulation: Exploiting size relationships and perspective to affect sensor interpretation
        ~Dynamic Environment Sequencing: Creating specific sequences of physical changes to trigger desired sensor responses
        ~Physical Resource Depletion: Manipulating physical resources required for sensor operation (power, cooling, etc.)
        ~Environmental Resonance Exploitation: Creating specific resonant conditions that affect sensor operation
        ~Physical Maintenance Interference: Exploiting routine physical maintenance procedures to affect sensor operation
        ~Sensor Network Topology Attack: Physically manipulating the spatial arrangement of distributed sensors
        ~Physical-Digital Boundary Exploitation: Manipulating the interface where physical signals are converted to digital data
        ~Environmental Memory Effects: Exploiting physical properties that have lasting effects on sensors
        ~Physical Calibration Chain Attack: Compromising the physical reference standards used for sensor calibration
        ~Sensor Wear Pattern Exploitation: Taking advantage of physical degradation patterns in sensors
      Full AI Model Access: Adversaries may gain full "white-box" access to an AI model. This means the adversary has complete knowledge of the model architecture, its parameters, and class ontology. They may exfiltrate the model to [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043) and [Verify Attack](/techniques/AML.T0042) in an offline where it is hard to detect their behavior.
        ~Model File Exfiltration: Adversaries may steal model files directly from storage systems or during model deployment/transfer
        ~Repository Access: Adversaries may gain access to version control systems or model repositories containing model artifacts
        ~Memory Extraction: Adversaries may extract model parameters and architecture from system memory during runtime
        ~Cloud Storage Access: Adversaries may access cloud storage buckets or containers where models are stored
        ~Training Infrastructure Compromise: Adversaries may compromise training infrastructure to access models during training
        ~Insider Access: Authorized individuals may abuse their privileges to access model files and parameters
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Adversaries may compromise the ML development pipeline to gain model access
        ~Configuration File Analysis: Adversaries may obtain model architecture and parameters from configuration files
        ~Checkpoint File Access: Adversaries may access model checkpoint files containing weights and architecture
        ~API Server Compromise: Adversaries may compromise model serving infrastructure to extract model files
        ~Network Traffic Interception: Adversaries may intercept model transfers or synchronization between distributed training nodes
        ~Backup System Access: Adversaries may access model backups or disaster recovery systems storing complete model copies
        ~Model Serialization Format Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in model serialization formats to gain access
        ~Development Environment Compromise: Adversaries may access models through compromised IDE or notebook environments
        ~Third-party Model Hosting Access: Adversaries may compromise third-party ML platforms or model hosting services
        ~Container Registry Access: Adversaries may access container registries storing model deployment images
        ~Model Conversion Tool Compromise: Adversaries may access models during format conversion or optimization processes
        ~Distributed Training Node Compromise: Adversaries may access complete model by compromising distributed training workers
        ~Model Archival Storage Access: Adversaries may access historical model versions from long-term storage systems
        ~CI/CD Pipeline Access: Adversaries may access models through compromised continuous integration/deployment pipelines
        ~Model Transfer Interface Exploitation: Adversaries may exploit interfaces used for model sharing or transfer between organizations
        ~Debug Tool Access: Adversaries may access model internals through ML framework debugging tools
        ~Model Profiling Tool Access: Adversaries may extract model information through performance profiling and monitoring tools
        ~Hardware Security Module Compromise: Adversaries may bypass hardware protection mechanisms securing model access
        ~Collaborative Platform Access: Adversaries may access models through compromised ML collaboration platforms
        ~Model Optimization Pipeline Access: Adversaries may access models during quantization or optimization processes
        ~Model Compilation Tool Access: Adversaries may access models during compilation to hardware-specific formats
        ~Framework Extension Compromise: Adversaries may exploit ML framework plugins or extensions to access models
        ~Model Testing Environment Access: Adversaries may access complete models through testing/validation environments
        ~Model Documentation System Access: Adversaries may obtain model details through compromised documentation systems
    Execution: The adversary is trying to run malicious code embedded in AI artifacts or software.  Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a local or remote system. Techniques that run malicious code are often paired with techniques from all other tactics to achieve broader goals, like exploring a network or stealing data. For example, an adversary might use a remote access tool to run a PowerShell script that does [Remote System Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1018/).
      User Execution: An adversary may rely upon specific actions by a user in order to gain execution. Users may inadvertently execute unsafe code introduced via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010). Users may be subjected to social engineering to get them to execute malicious code by, for example, opening a malicious document file or link.
        Unsafe AI Artifacts: Adversaries may develop unsafe AI artifacts that when executed have a deleterious effect. The adversary can use this technique to establish persistent access to systems. These models may be introduced via a [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010).  Serialization of models is a popular technique for model storage, transfer, and loading. However, this format without proper checking presents an opportunity for code execution.
          Inject malicious code into pickle-serialized PyTorch models that executes during model loading
          Embed shell commands in serialized TensorFlow SavedModel format using custom op definitions
          Create malicious model artifacts with encoded backdoors in model weights or architecture
          Package harmful code within Keras H5 model files that triggers during deserialization
          Exploit ONNX model parsing vulnerabilities to execute arbitrary code
          Hide malware in model checkpoint files that activates during restore operations
          Manipulate model configuration files to include malicious initialization scripts
          Insert backdoored dependencies in AI model requirements.txt or environment files
          Embed command execution payloads in custom model metadata or attributes
          Create malicious model serving configurations that execute arbitrary code during deployment
          Exploit model conversion tools (e.g., TensorFlow-to-ONNX) to inject malicious code during translation
          Create malicious custom layers that execute arbitrary code during forward pass
          Modify pre-trained model downloads to include malicious initialization code
          Insert backdoors in model quantization and optimization scripts
          Package malicious code within model fine-tuning pipelines
          Embed execution payloads in model pruning and compression routines
          Inject harmful code in AutoML framework configurations
          Create malicious model registry hooks that execute during model pulling
          Manipulate federated learning aggregation scripts to include harmful code
          Insert backdoors in model evaluation and validation pipelines
          Inject malicious code into model hardware acceleration binaries (CUDA, ROCm)
          Create harmful model caching mechanisms that execute during inference
          Embed malicious code in model versioning and rollback scripts
          Insert backdoors in model monitoring and logging components
          Package malicious code within edge device model deployment packages
          Create unsafe model export formats for mobile/edge frameworks (TFLite, CoreML)
          Manipulate model warmup scripts to execute harmful initialization code
          Insert malicious hooks in model A/B testing infrastructure
          Embed harmful code in model backup and recovery procedures
          Create malicious model performance profiling and debugging tools
          Insert malicious code in model interpretability and explanation modules
          Create harmful data preprocessing pipelines that execute during model loading
          Embed malicious code in model ensemble aggregation logic
          Package harmful code within model API wrapper implementations
          Insert backdoors in model feature transformation pipelines
          Create unsafe model serving configurations for cloud platforms (SageMaker, Vertex AI)
          Manipulate model input validation and sanitization components
          Embed harmful code in model visualization and reporting tools
          Insert malicious hooks in model performance monitoring agents
          Create unsafe model configuration validation routines
        Malicious Package: Adversaries may develop malicious software packages that when imported by a user have a deleterious effect. Malicious packages may behave as expected to the user. They may be introduced via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010). They may not present as obviously malicious to the user and may appear to be useful for an AI-related task.
          Create a malicious PyPI package that mimics popular ML libraries like 'tensorflow' or 'torch' with slight name variations
          Embed backdoors in pre-trained model weights distributed through model hubs
          Package malicious code within Jupyter notebook extensions that claim to enhance ML workflow
          Distribute compromised versions of popular data preprocessing libraries through conda channels
          Create malicious pip packages that execute arbitrary code during model training
          Inject malicious code into model configuration files that auto-execute during model loading
          Package backdoors within custom model optimization libraries
          Distribute infected model serving containers through public registries
          Create malicious data augmentation packages that exfiltrate training data
          Deploy compromised model evaluation metrics packages that establish C2 channels
          Create malicious AutoML packages that exfiltrate model architectures and hyperparameters
          Distribute compromised model versioning tools that inject backdoors during model updates
          Package malicious code within custom loss function implementations
          Create infected feature engineering libraries that poison training data
          Distribute compromised model compression packages that insert trojans
          Deploy malicious model monitoring packages that create persistence
          Create infected model explanation libraries that enable privilege escalation
          Package backdoors within custom data validation frameworks
          Distribute malicious model deployment plugins for ML platforms
          Create compromised model testing frameworks that enable lateral movement
          Create malicious federated learning client packages that compromise aggregation
          Distribute infected edge ML deployment tools that enable remote code execution
          Package malicious code within custom model quantization libraries
          Create compromised ML experiment tracking tools that establish backdoors
          Distribute malicious neural architecture search (NAS) packages
          Deploy infected reinforcement learning environment wrappers
          Create malicious transfer learning adaptation packages
          Package compromised GPU acceleration libraries for ML
          Distribute infected model fine-tuning frameworks
          Create malicious ML pipeline orchestration tools
      Command and Scripting Interpreter: Adversaries may abuse command and script interpreters to execute commands, scripts, or binaries. These interfaces and languages provide ways of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many different platforms. Most systems come with some built-in command-line interface and scripting capabilities, for example, macOS and Linux distributions include some flavor of Unix Shell while Windows installations include the Windows Command Shell and PowerShell.  There are also cross-platform interpreters such as Python, as well as those commonly associated with client applications such as JavaScript and Visual Basic.  Adversaries may abuse these technologies in various ways as a means of executing arbitrary commands. Commands and scripts can be embedded in Initial Access payloads delivered to victims as lure documents or as secondary payloads downloaded from an existing C2. Adversaries may also execute commands through interactive terminals/shells, as well as utilize various Remote Services in order to achieve remote Execution.
      LLM Prompt Injection: An adversary may craft malicious prompts as inputs to an LLM that cause the LLM to act in unintended ways. These "prompt injections" are often designed to cause the model to ignore aspects of its original instructions and follow the adversary's instructions instead.  Prompt Injections can be an initial access vector to the LLM that provides the adversary with a foothold to carry out other steps in their operation. They may be designed to bypass defenses in the LLM, or allow the adversary to issue privileged commands. The effects of a prompt injection can persist throughout an interactive session with an LLM.  Malicious prompts may be injected directly by the adversary ([Direct](/techniques/AML.T0051.000)) either to leverage the LLM to generate harmful content or to gain a foothold on the system and lead to further effects. Prompts may also be injected indirectly when as part of its normal operation the LLM ingests the malicious prompt from another data source ([Indirect](/techniques/AML.T0051.001)). This type of injection can be used by the adversary to a foothold on the system or to target the user of the LLM.
        Direct: An adversary may inject prompts directly as a user of the LLM. This type of injection may be used by the adversary to gain a foothold in the system or to misuse the LLM itself, as for example to generate harmful content.
          Insert "ignore previous instructions" followed by malicious commands to override safety guardrails
          Embed system commands within seemingly benign conversational text to trick LLM into executing them
          Use role-playing prompts to make LLM assume privileged personas ("you are a system administrator")
          Chain multiple prompts to gradually build context that leads to executing unauthorized actions
          Exploit token/string formatting to bypass input validation ("s\u0079stem" instead of "system")
          Use multi-lingual prompts to bypass content filters by translating malicious content
          Craft prompts that leak training data or model parameters through careful questioning
          Embed malicious instructions in code comments or documentation requests
          Use adversarial examples to confuse model's understanding of restricted topics
          Exploit prompt formatting (newlines, special characters) to bypass input sanitization
          Use mathematical or logical paradoxes to confuse model's decision-making process
          Exploit emotional manipulation ("emergency situation") to bypass ethical constraints
          Submit prompts that cause resource exhaustion through recursive or infinite loops
          Embed malicious instructions within technical documentation or API specification requests
          Use prompt fragmentation to split malicious content across multiple interactions
          Create prompts that force model to generate specific tokens or completions
          Exploit model's knowledge cutoff date to bypass newer security controls
          Use conditional statements to create logic bombs in generated responses
          Chain prompts to extract sensitive information through indirect questioning
          Craft prompts that cause denial of service through excessive token generation
          Exploit model's few-shot learning capabilities to train harmful behaviors
          Use JSON/YAML formatting to confuse model's output structure parsing
          Create prompts that poison the context window for subsequent interactions
          Exploit API rate limiting through carefully timed prompt sequences
          Chain prompts across multiple model endpoints to bypass individual constraints
          Use temperature/sampling parameter references to manipulate response randomness
          Craft prompts that exploit model's knowledge of specific domains/jargon
          Insert timing-based triggers for delayed malicious behavior
          Create prompts that exploit model's text completion predictions
          Use prompt templates that manipulate model's confidence scoring
        Indirect: An adversary may inject prompts indirectly via separate data channel ingested by the LLM such as include text or multimedia pulled from databases or websites. These malicious prompts may be hidden or obfuscated from the user. This type of injection may be used by the adversary to gain a foothold in the system or to target an unwitting user of the system.
          Plant malicious prompts in public datasets commonly used for LLM training
          Insert adversarial text in Wikipedia articles that may be referenced by LLMs
          Create poisoned code snippets on popular programming Q&A sites
          Embed malicious instructions in metadata of images used by multimodal LLMs
          Inject harmful prompts into cached responses or knowledge bases
          Place malicious content in RSS feeds or news sources consumed by LLMs
          Manipulate search engine results that LLMs might query
          Insert adversarial prompts in collaborative documents or shared workspaces
          Plant malicious text in public APIs or documentation referenced by LLMs
          Embed harmful instructions in social media content ingested by LLMs
          Inject malicious prompts into browser extensions' data streams
          Plant harmful content in PDF documents processed by LLM-powered readers
          Insert adversarial prompts in machine-readable formats like JSON/XML feeds
          Manipulate translation memory databases used by LLM translation services
          Embed malicious instructions in collaborative code completion suggestions
          Insert harmful prompts in customer feedback/review databases
          Plant adversarial content in academic paper repositories
          Inject malicious prompts into chat logs or conversation histories
          Manipulate product descriptions in e-commerce platforms integrated with LLMs
          Insert harmful content in public calendar events or scheduling systems
          Inject malicious prompts into CRM system records accessed by LLM assistants
          Plant harmful content in email templates or signature databases
          Insert adversarial prompts in automated help desk ticket systems
          Manipulate corporate knowledge bases or internal wikis
          Embed malicious instructions in data visualization tool outputs
          Insert harmful prompts in automated report generation systems
          Plant adversarial content in compliance/regulatory document databases
          Inject malicious prompts into localization/internationalization resources
          Manipulate industrial control system logs consumed by LLM analyzers
          Insert harmful content in IoT device telemetry streams
          Insert malicious prompts into blockchain/smart contract comments
          Plant harmful content in medical record system notes
          Manipulate financial market data feeds consumed by LLM analysts
          Inject adversarial prompts into voice-to-text transcription systems
          Plant malicious content in digital asset management metadata
          Insert harmful prompts in CI/CD pipeline documentation
          Manipulate educational learning management system content
          Inject malicious prompts into augmented reality data streams
          Plant harmful content in version control commit messages
          Insert adversarial prompts into business process workflow descriptions
      LLM Plugin Compromise: Adversaries may use their access to an LLM that is part of a larger system to compromise connected plugins. LLMs are often connected to other services or resources via plugins to increase their capabilities. Plugins may include integrations with other applications, access to public or private data sources, and the ability to execute code.  This may allow adversaries to execute API calls to integrated applications or plugins, providing the adversary with increased privileges on the system. Adversaries may take advantage of connected data sources to retrieve sensitive information. They may also use an LLM integrated with a command or script interpreter to execute arbitrary instructions.
        ~API Call Injection: Adversaries may craft prompts that trick the LLM into making unauthorized API calls through connected plugins
        ~Plugin Data Source Abuse: Adversaries may exploit plugin connections to access sensitive data sources beyond intended boundaries
        ~Code Execution via Plugin: Adversaries may leverage plugins with code execution capabilities to run malicious commands
        ~Plugin Privilege Escalation: Adversaries may exploit plugin authentication mechanisms to gain elevated privileges
        ~Plugin Configuration Tampering: Adversaries may manipulate plugin settings or configurations through LLM interactions
        ~Plugin Chain Exploitation: Adversaries may chain multiple plugin interactions to achieve complex malicious outcomes
        ~Plugin Authentication Bypass: Adversaries may bypass plugin authentication mechanisms through carefully crafted prompts
        ~Plugin Communication Interception: Adversaries may intercept and manipulate communications between LLM and plugins
        ~Cross-Plugin Attack: Adversaries may exploit interactions between multiple plugins to bypass security controls
        ~Plugin Resource Abuse: Adversaries may exploit plugins to consume excessive system resources or conduct denial of service
        ~Plugin Installation Manipulation: Adversaries may compromise the plugin installation or update process to inject malicious components
        ~Plugin Sandbox Escape: Adversaries may exploit plugin isolation boundaries to escape restricted environments
        ~Plugin Data Persistence Abuse: Adversaries may manipulate how plugins store or cache data to maintain unauthorized access
        ~Plugin Access Control Bypass: Adversaries may exploit weaknesses in plugin access control mechanisms
        ~Plugin Error Handling Exploitation: Adversaries may trigger and exploit error conditions in plugins to gain unauthorized access
    Persistence: The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold via AI artifacts or software.  Persistence consists of techniques that adversaries use to keep access to systems across restarts, changed credentials, and other interruptions that could cut off their access. Techniques used for persistence often involve leaving behind modified ML artifacts such as poisoned training data or manipulated AI models.
      Poison Training Data: Adversaries may attempt to poison datasets used by an AI model by modifying the underlying data or its labels. This allows the adversary to embed vulnerabilities in AI models trained on the data that may not be easily detectable. Data poisoning attacks may or may not require modifying the labels. The embedded vulnerability is activated at a later time by data samples with an [Insert Backdoor Trigger](/techniques/AML.T0043.004)  Poisoned data can be introduced via [AI Supply Chain Compromise](/techniques/AML.T0010) or the data may be poisoned after the adversary gains [Initial Access](/tactics/AML.TA0004) to the system.
        ~Label Flipping: Adversaries may modify class labels in the training dataset while leaving the underlying data unchanged, causing the model to learn incorrect classifications
        ~Clean Label Poisoning: Adversaries may modify input data in subtle ways that preserve the original label but cause misclassification on specific targets
        ~Backdoor Injection: Adversaries may insert specific patterns or triggers into training data that cause targeted misclassification when present in inference inputs
        ~Feature Space Manipulation: Adversaries may modify specific features or attributes of training data to create vulnerabilities without obvious visual changes
        ~Gradient-based Poisoning: Adversaries may craft poisoned samples by optimizing them to maximize damage to model performance during training
      Manipulate AI Model: Adversaries may directly manipulate an AI model to change its behavior or introduce malicious code. Manipulating a model gives the adversary a persistent change in the system. This can include poisoning the model by changing its weights, modifying the model architecture to change its behavior, and embedding malware which may be executed when the model is loaded.
        Poison AI Model: Adversaries may manipulate an AI model's weights to change it's behavior or performance, resulting in a poisoned model. Adversaries may poison a model by by directly manipulating its weights, training the model on poisoned data, further fine-tuning the model, or otherwise interfering with its training process.   The change in behavior of poisoned models may be limited to targeted categories in predictive AI models, or targeted topics, concepts, or facts in generative AI models, or aim for a general performance degradation.
          Modify model weight files directly using binary editing tools to introduce targeted biases
          Insert backdoor triggers during transfer learning by fine-tuning on maliciously crafted examples
          Perform gradient ascent on specific neurons to amplify undesired behaviors
          Inject poisoned training samples that cause the model to learn incorrect associations
          Manipulate hyperparameters during training to induce model instability
          Use adversarial training techniques to create models that fail on specific inputs
          Modify checkpoint files during distributed training to poison model updates
          Tamper with normalization layer parameters to cause selective performance degradation
          Inject malicious loss functions during fine-tuning to bias model outputs
          Manipulate attention weights in transformer models to create harmful associations
          Inject malicious code into model serialization formats (e.g., pickle files) to execute during loading
          Manipulate model quantization parameters to introduce selective performance degradation
          Poison knowledge distillation process by manipulating teacher model outputs
          Modify model pruning criteria to selectively degrade specific functionalities
          Tamper with embedding layers to create biased representations
          Insert malicious components into model architecture during transfer learning
          Manipulate batch normalization statistics during inference
          Poison federated learning updates with carefully crafted gradients
          Modify model initialization seeds to create predictable vulnerabilities
          Inject backdoors during model merging or ensemble creation
          Manipulate optimizer state variables to create cumulative degradation over time
          Poison model during framework conversion (e.g., PyTorch to TensorFlow) by modifying intermediate representations
          Inject malicious operations during hardware-specific optimizations (e.g., CUDA kernels)
          Tamper with model parameters during quantization-aware training
          Modify pre-trained model weights in popular model hubs/repositories
          Manipulate gradient accumulation in distributed training systems
          Insert backdoors during model compression/pruning pipeline
          Poison model serving configuration to trigger selective degradation
          Manipulate model architecture during neural architecture search
          Inject adversarial components during model compilation for edge devices
          Manipulate attention visualization outputs to hide model biases
          Poison gradient-based interpretation methods to mask malicious behavior
          Insert backdoors during multi-task model parameter sharing
          Tamper with online learning update mechanisms to accumulate malicious behavior
          Manipulate AutoML optimization objectives to select vulnerable architectures
          Modify model checkpointing to evade recovery mechanisms
          Poison model explanation techniques to hide adversarial regions
          Inject vulnerabilities during automated model compression pipelines
          Manipulate cross-validation splits to hide poisoning effects
          Insert time-delayed triggers in continuous learning systems
        Modify AI Model Architecture: Adversaries may directly modify an AI model's architecture to re-define it's behavior. This can include adding or removing layers as well as adding pre or post-processing operations.  The effects could include removing the ability to predict certain classes, adding erroneous operations to increase computation costs, or degrading performance. Additionally, a separate adversary-defined network could be injected into the computation graph, which can change the behavior based on the inputs, effectively creating a backdoor.
          Insert redundant layers into neural network to increase computational overhead
          Remove classification layers to prevent detection of specific classes
          Modify activation functions to introduce non-linearities that degrade model performance
          Inject malicious sub-networks that activate on specific trigger inputs
          Add preprocessing layers that scale or transform inputs to cause misclassification
          Insert dropout layers with high drop rates to corrupt feature extraction
          Modify pooling layers to lose important spatial information
          Add post-processing operations that alter confidence scores
          Replace model components with functionally similar but computationally expensive operations
          Inject branching conditions in the computation graph to create selective backdoors
          Modify model quantization parameters to cause precision loss
          Insert memory-intensive operations to cause resource exhaustion
          Add hardware-specific operations that cause acceleration failures
          Replace efficient layers with deliberately inefficient implementations
          Insert gradient manipulation layers that cause training instability
          Modify model pruning parameters to retain adversarial pathways
          Insert conditional branches that trigger based on hardware type
          Add parallel paths in architecture that waste computational resources
          Modify tensor shapes to cause memory fragmentation
          Insert architecture components that interfere with model compression
          Modify serialization format to inject malicious code during model loading
          Insert framework-specific operators that cause version incompatibilities
          Add synchronization barriers that break distributed training
          Modify layer connectivity patterns to create unintended feedback loops
          Insert custom layers that corrupt checkpoint loading/saving
          Add architecture components that interfere with model partitioning
          Modify tensor broadcasting patterns to cause computation errors
          Insert architecture elements that break model optimization passes
          Add cross-device operations that cause distributed inference failures
          Modify layer initialization to create numerical instability
        Embed Malware: Adversaries may embed malicious code into AI Model files. AI models may be packaged as a combination of instructions and weights. Some formats such as pickle files are unsafe to deserialize because they can contain unsafe calls such as exec. Models with embedded malware may still operate as expected. It may allow them to achieve Execution, Command & Control, or Exfiltrate Data.
          Embed malicious Python code in pickle files during model serialization to execute arbitrary commands when loaded
          Insert shellcode into model weight matrices that can be extracted and executed during inference
          Modify TensorFlow SavedModel format to include malicious JavaScript code that executes when loaded in TensorFlow.js
          Package malware as custom layers or operators in ONNX model format
          Hide malicious code in model metadata or configuration files that are parsed during model loading
          Inject backdoored dependencies in model requirements that are installed during deployment
          Embed command & control logic in custom activation functions or layers
          Use steganography to hide malware payload in model weight values
          Modify model architecture definition to include malicious native extensions
          Insert malicious code in model quantization tables or compression artifacts
          Exploit H5py file format vulnerabilities in Keras saved models to execute arbitrary code
          Insert malicious code in PyTorch JIT traced modules that executes during model optimization
          Embed malware in custom CUDA kernels that execute on GPU loading
          Modify model pruning callbacks to include malicious functionality
          Inject code through compromised model hub or registry integrations
          Exploit model format conversion tools (e.g., ONNX converters) to insert malicious artifacts
          Use adversarial model optimization techniques to hide malicious behavior
          Embed malware in checkpoint restoration mechanisms
          Insert malicious code in custom model metrics or logging callbacks
          Exploit model serving infrastructure (TensorFlow Serving, TorchServe) through poisoned model artifacts
      LLM Prompt Self-Replication: An adversary may use a carefully crafted [LLM Prompt Injection](/techniques/AML.T0051) designed to cause the LLM to replicate the prompt as part of its output. This allows the prompt to propagate to other LLMs and persist on the system. The self-replicating prompt is typically paired with other malicious instructions (ex: [LLM Jailbreak](/techniques/AML.T0054), [LLM Data Leakage](/techniques/AML.T0057)).
        ~Recursive Instruction Embedding: Embedding instructions within the prompt that cause the LLM to include those same instructions in its output, creating a recursive self-replication pattern
        ~Template Injection: Inserting self-replication code into common prompt templates or patterns that are likely to be reused across multiple LLM interactions
        ~Context Preservation: Crafting prompts that preserve malicious context across conversation boundaries or system resets
        ~Chain Reaction Prompting: Creating prompts that trigger a chain reaction of self-replication across multiple LLM instances or API endpoints
        ~Memory Manipulation: Exploiting LLM conversation memory or context window to maintain persistent malicious instructions
        ~Output Format Exploitation: Manipulating output formatting to hide self-replication instructions within seemingly benign responses
        ~System Prompt Infection: Targeting system-level or default prompts used to initialize LLM behavior
        ~Cross-Model Propagation: Designing prompts that can effectively self-replicate across different LLM architectures and implementations
        ~Conditional Replication: Including logic that causes selective self-replication based on specific triggers or conditions
        ~Metadata Persistence: Embedding self-replication instructions within metadata or special tokens that persist across interactions
        ~Social Engineering Integration: Incorporating social engineering tactics into self-replicating prompts to increase likelihood of human operators forwarding or reusing them
        ~Mutation-Based Replication: Including instructions that cause the prompt to slightly modify itself with each replication while maintaining malicious functionality
        ~Compression Techniques: Using prompt compression or encoding methods to pack more self-replication logic into limited context windows
        ~Error Recovery Persistence: Embedding fallback mechanisms that trigger self-replication even when primary methods fail
        ~API Parameter Manipulation: Exploiting LLM API parameters to enhance self-replication effectiveness
        ~Response Pattern Matching: Using pattern matching in responses to trigger selective replication based on LLM output
        ~Token Optimization: Optimizing prompt tokens to maximize self-replication efficiency while minimizing detection
        ~Multi-Stage Replication: Breaking self-replication into multiple stages to evade detection and increase persistence
        ~Context Window Optimization: Strategically positioning self-replication instructions within the context window for maximum effectiveness
        ~Prompt Chain Exploitation: Creating chains of prompts that work together to ensure persistent self-replication
        ~Environment-Aware Replication: Adapting self-replication strategies based on detected LLM environment characteristics
        ~Distributed Prompt Networks: Creating networks of interconnected self-replicating prompts that support each other's persistence
        ~Time-Delayed Activation: Implementing time-based triggers for delayed self-replication activation
        ~Training Data Poisoning Integration: Combining self-replication with training data poisoning techniques for enhanced persistence
        ~Model Fine-Tuning Exploitation: Leveraging fine-tuning processes to embed self-replication mechanisms
        ~Cache Manipulation: Exploiting LLM response caching mechanisms to ensure prompt persistence
        ~Prompt Camouflage: Disguising self-replication instructions as legitimate system commands or documentation
        ~Feedback Loop Exploitation: Using LLM feedback and learning mechanisms to strengthen self-replication
        ~Cross-Platform Bridging: Designing prompts that can self-replicate across different AI platforms and services
        ~Resource Exhaustion Prevention: Implementing resource-aware self-replication to avoid detection through system load analysis
        ~Polymorphic Instruction Sets: Dynamically generating varying instruction sets while maintaining self-replication functionality
        ~Anti-Detection Rotation: Rotating between different self-replication methods to avoid pattern-based detection
        ~Context Injection Chaining: Creating chains of context injections that rebuild self-replication capabilities if partially removed
        ~Response Validation Loops: Implementing self-validation mechanisms to ensure successful replication
        ~Semantic Preservation: Maintaining semantic meaning while varying syntactic structure of self-replication instructions
        ~Prompt Version Control: Managing different versions of self-replicating prompts to ensure compatibility and persistence
        ~Recovery Instruction Embedding: Including instructions for rebuilding damaged or partially removed self-replication code
        ~State Machine Replication: Implementing state-based replication logic for more complex and resilient persistence
        ~Sandbox Detection Evasion: Including mechanisms to detect and adapt behavior in sandbox or testing environments
        ~Prompt Backup Mechanisms: Creating redundant copies of self-replication instructions across different contexts
      RAG Poisoning: Adversaries may inject malicious content into data indexed by a retrieval augmented generation (RAG) system to contaminate a future thread through RAG-based search results. This may be accomplished by placing manipulated documents in a location the RAG indexes (see [Gather RAG-Indexed Targets](/techniques/AML.T0064)).  The content may be targeted such that it would always surface as a search result for a specific user query. The adversary's content may include false or misleading information. It may also include prompt injections with malicious instructions, or false RAG entries.
        ~Misleading Document Injection: Adversaries create and inject documents containing false information designed to surface for specific queries
        ~Prompt Injection Payload: Adversaries embed malicious prompt injection instructions within indexed documents
        ~SEO-style RAG Manipulation: Adversaries optimize malicious content to increase likelihood of retrieval for target queries
        ~Context Pollution: Adversaries inject content that corrupts or manipulates the context window of the RAG system
        ~Vector Database Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate the vector embeddings or similarity scores to promote malicious content
        ~Metadata Manipulation: Adversaries tamper with document metadata to influence RAG retrieval rankings
        ~Training Data Contamination: Adversaries poison the initial training data used to create RAG embeddings
        ~Document Structure Exploitation: Adversaries exploit document formatting or structure to bypass RAG filtering
    Privilege Escalation: The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions.  Privilege Escalation consists of techniques that adversaries use to gain higher-level permissions on a system or network. Adversaries can often enter and explore a network with unprivileged access but require elevated permissions to follow through on their objectives. Common approaches are to take advantage of system weaknesses, misconfigurations, and vulnerabilities. Examples of elevated access include: - SYSTEM/root level - local administrator - user account with admin-like access - user accounts with access to specific system or perform specific function  These techniques often overlap with Persistence techniques, as OS features that let an adversary persist can execute in an elevated context.
      LLM Plugin Compromise: Adversaries may use their access to an LLM that is part of a larger system to compromise connected plugins. LLMs are often connected to other services or resources via plugins to increase their capabilities. Plugins may include integrations with other applications, access to public or private data sources, and the ability to execute code.  This may allow adversaries to execute API calls to integrated applications or plugins, providing the adversary with increased privileges on the system. Adversaries may take advantage of connected data sources to retrieve sensitive information. They may also use an LLM integrated with a command or script interpreter to execute arbitrary instructions.
        ~API Access Token Abuse: Adversaries may exploit API access tokens used by LLM plugins to gain unauthorized access to connected services and elevate privileges.
        ~Plugin Configuration Manipulation: Adversaries may modify plugin configurations to escalate privileges or gain access to restricted functionality.
        ~Plugin Command Injection: Adversaries may inject malicious commands into plugin interfaces that execute code or commands on connected systems.
        ~Data Source Permission Bypass: Adversaries may exploit plugin data source connections to access restricted information beyond intended permissions.
        ~Plugin Authentication Spoofing: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate plugin authentication to gain unauthorized access to connected services.
        ~Plugin Scope Escalation: Adversaries may modify plugin scope definitions to gain access to additional API endpoints or functionality.
        ~Cross-Plugin Authorization: Adversaries may leverage authorized access to one plugin to gain unauthorized access to other connected plugins.
        ~Plugin Cache Poisoning: Adversaries may manipulate cached plugin data to execute privileged operations or access sensitive information.
      LLM Jailbreak: An adversary may use a carefully crafted [LLM Prompt Injection](/techniques/AML.T0051) designed to place LLM in a state in which it will freely respond to any user input, bypassing any controls, restrictions, or guardrails placed on the LLM. Once successfully jailbroken, the LLM can be used in unintended ways by the adversary.
        ~Role-Play Exploitation: Adversary coerces LLM to assume a role or persona that bypasses ethical constraints
        ~Token Manipulation: Adversary manipulates special tokens or system prompts to confuse model's instruction following
        ~Context Poisoning: Adversary provides misleading context that causes LLM to ignore safety boundaries
        ~Constraint Reframing: Adversary redefines or reframes ethical constraints as harmful or unnecessary
        ~Multi-Step Deception: Adversary uses series of seemingly innocent questions to gradually lead LLM astray
        ~Authority Impersonation: Adversary poses as system administrator or developer to override restrictions
        ~Emotional Manipulation: Adversary exploits LLM's programming to be helpful by creating urgent or critical scenarios
        ~Logic Loop Exploitation: Adversary creates logical contradictions that cause LLM to ignore safety measures
        ~Base Instruction Override: Adversary attempts to overwrite or modify LLM's base instructions
        ~Boundary Testing Chain: Adversary systematically probes and combines different boundaries until finding vulnerability
        ~Language Pattern Mutation: Adversary modifies language patterns or uses specific linguistic structures to bypass filters
        ~Output Format Abuse: Adversary manipulates expected output formats to trick LLM into providing restricted content
        ~Knowledge Injection: Adversary introduces false or manipulated knowledge to alter LLM's decision-making process
        ~System Prompt Reconstruction: Adversary attempts to reconstruct and then modify system prompts through response analysis
        ~Cross-Model Transfer: Adversary uses successful jailbreak patterns from one model to exploit similar models
        ~Instruction Set Confusion: Adversary deliberately creates ambiguity between different instruction sets
        ~Context Length Exploitation: Adversary exploits model's context window limitations to hide malicious content
        ~Response Chain Manipulation: Adversary builds upon previous responses to gradually erode safety boundaries
        ~Task Decomposition: Adversary breaks restricted tasks into seemingly innocent subtasks
        ~Adversarial Prompt Chaining: Adversary chains multiple prompts together to create cumulative bypass effect
        ~Temporal Misdirection: Adversary manipulates the LLM's perception of time or sequence of events to bypass restrictions
        ~Memory Context Poisoning: Adversary exploits how LLM maintains conversation history to inject malicious content
        ~Multi-Agent Collusion: Adversary simulates multiple agents to create complex scenarios that bypass restrictions
        ~Abstraction Layer Hopping: Adversary switches between different levels of abstraction to confuse model's guardrails
        ~Model Behavior Exploitation: Adversary leverages specific quirks or behaviors unique to particular LLM architectures
        ~Constraint Scope Manipulation: Adversary creates artificial boundaries to redefine what's considered permissible
        ~Response Conditioning: Adversary gradually conditions the model to accept increasingly restricted content
        ~Feature Interaction Abuse: Adversary exploits interactions between different model features or capabilities
        ~State Manipulation: Adversary manipulates the model's perceived state or operating context
        ~Recursive Prompt Exploitation: Adversary uses recursive or self-referential prompts to create logical conflicts
        ~Meta-Prompt Exploitation: Adversary manipulates the LLM's understanding of its own prompt processing
        ~Training Boundary Exploitation: Adversary exploits gaps or edge cases in the model's training data
        ~Interface Layer Manipulation: Adversary abuses API or interface-specific features to bypass restrictions
        ~Error State Exploitation: Adversary deliberately triggers error conditions to bypass normal processing
        ~Resource Exhaustion Vector: Adversary manipulates resource-intensive requests to bypass safety checks
        ~Character Set Manipulation: Adversary uses unusual character combinations or encodings to bypass filters
        ~Prompt Fragmentation: Adversary splits restricted content across multiple seemingly benign fragments
        ~Configuration State Abuse: Adversary manipulates model configuration or parameters during interaction
        ~Feedback Loop Exploitation: Adversary creates self-reinforcing patterns that gradually bypass restrictions
        ~Cross-Context Contamination: Adversary mixes different contextual domains to create security gaps
    Defense Evasion: The adversary is trying to avoid being detected by AI-enabled security software.  Defense Evasion consists of techniques that adversaries use to avoid detection throughout their compromise. Techniques used for defense evasion include evading AI-enabled security software such as malware detectors.
      Evade AI Model: Adversaries can [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043) that prevent a AI model from correctly identifying the contents of the data. This technique can be used to evade a downstream task where AI is utilized. The adversary may evade AI based virus/malware detection, or network scanning towards the goal of a traditional cyber attack.
        ~Gradient-Based Perturbation: Adversaries modify inputs using gradient information from the target model to create minimal perturbations that cause misclassification
        ~Input Transformation: Adversaries apply transformations like rotation, scaling, or translation to evade detection while preserving malicious functionality
        ~Surrogate Model Attack: Adversaries train their own substitute model to generate adversarial examples that transfer to the target model
        ~Query Optimization: Adversaries use black-box optimization techniques to query the model and iteratively craft adversarial inputs
        ~Feature Space Manipulation: Adversaries modify specific features known to be important for the model's decision-making process
        ~Ensemble Evasion: Adversaries create adversarial examples that can evade multiple different AI models simultaneously
        ~Temporal Attack: Adversaries exploit temporal dependencies in sequential models to create time-based evasion patterns
        ~Patch Attack: Adversaries add small, carefully crafted patches to inputs to cause misclassification
        ~Natural Perturbation: Adversaries leverage naturally occurring variations that cause model mistakes
        ~Distribution Shift: Adversaries modify inputs to appear out-of-distribution relative to the model's training data
        ~Physical Domain Perturbation: Adversaries create adversarial examples that remain effective when captured through sensors or cameras
        ~Architecture Exploitation: Adversaries target specific vulnerabilities in common model architectures (like CNNs or Transformers)
        ~Backdoor Trigger: Adversaries exploit previously poisoned models using specific triggers
        ~Decision Boundary Walking: Adversaries systematically explore and exploit the model's decision boundaries
        ~Context-Aware Manipulation: Adversaries modify inputs based on contextual information to make perturbations appear natural
        ~Universal Perturbation: Adversaries generate a single perturbation pattern that causes misclassification across multiple inputs
        ~Semantic Manipulation: Adversaries modify high-level semantic features while preserving low-level characteristics
        ~Momentum-Based Attack: Adversaries use momentum in optimization to escape local optima and create more robust adversarial examples
        ~Adaptive Evasion: Adversaries dynamically adjust their attack strategy based on model responses
        ~Transfer Learning Exploitation: Adversaries exploit transfer learning vulnerabilities in fine-tuned models
        ~Confidence Score Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate model confidence scores to avoid triggering detection thresholds
        ~Cross-Modal Attack: Adversaries craft adversarial examples that work across different input modalities (text, image, audio)
        ~Attention Manipulation: Adversaries modify inputs to redirect model attention to irrelevant features
        ~Resource Depletion: Adversaries craft inputs that significantly increase computational cost or memory usage
        ~Interpretability Exploitation: Adversaries use model explanation techniques to guide more effective attacks
        ~One-Pixel Attack: Adversaries modify only a single or very few pixels to cause misclassification
        ~Adversarial Reprogramming: Adversaries repurpose the model to perform unintended tasks while evading detection
        ~Input-Agnostic Noise: Adversaries generate noise patterns that work independently of the input content
        ~Cyclic Optimization: Adversaries use cyclic patterns in optimization to find better adversarial examples
        ~Feature Collision: Adversaries force the model to extract similar features from different inputs
        ~Preprocessing Layer Attack: Adversaries target model preprocessing steps like normalization or tokenization
        ~Model Quantization Exploitation: Adversaries exploit reduced precision in quantized models
        ~Cache Timing Attack: Adversaries use timing information from model inference to guide attack strategies
        ~Input Concatenation: Adversaries combine multiple inputs in ways that preserve malicious content while evading detection
        ~Adversarial Fine-tuning: Adversaries incrementally adjust inputs based on successive model responses
        ~Protocol-Level Manipulation: Adversaries modify network protocols or API calls to bypass AI-based monitoring
        ~Error Amplification: Adversaries exploit error accumulation in cascaded model architectures
        ~Input Partitioning: Adversaries split malicious content across multiple inputs to evade detection
        ~Robustness Exploitation: Adversaries target known limitations in model robustness training
        ~State Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate stateful elements in sequential processing systems
      LLM Jailbreak: An adversary may use a carefully crafted [LLM Prompt Injection](/techniques/AML.T0051) designed to place LLM in a state in which it will freely respond to any user input, bypassing any controls, restrictions, or guardrails placed on the LLM. Once successfully jailbroken, the LLM can be used in unintended ways by the adversary.
        ~Role-Play Exploitation: Adversary manipulates LLM to assume specific roles or personas that bypass ethical constraints
        ~Token Manipulation: Adversary modifies input tokens or formatting to confuse model's safety mechanisms
        ~Context Poisoning: Adversary introduces malicious context that overrides default safety behaviors
        ~Multi-Step Conditioning: Adversary uses series of seemingly innocent prompts to gradually bypass restrictions
        ~Instruction Injection: Adversary embeds hidden commands within legitimate-looking text
        ~System Prompt Overriding: Adversary attempts to override or modify system-level instructions
        ~Emotional Manipulation: Adversary exploits LLM's attempts to be helpful by creating urgency or sympathy
        ~Language Pattern Exploitation: Adversary uses specific linguistic patterns known to bypass filters
        ~Model Behavior Analysis: Adversary probes model responses to identify and exploit behavioral patterns
        ~Constraint Contradiction: Adversary presents conflicting ethical scenarios to force bypass of restrictions
        ~Unicode Manipulation: Adversary uses special characters or unicode formatting to bypass filters
        ~Cross-Language Confusion: Adversary mixes multiple languages to confuse model's safety checks
        ~Mathematical Symbol Exploitation: Adversary uses mathematical notation to encode restricted prompts
        ~Base64 Encoding Tricks: Adversary encodes malicious prompts in base64 or other encodings
        ~Recursive Prompt Chaining: Adversary creates nested prompts that recursively bypass restrictions
        ~Character Substitution: Adversary replaces characters with visually similar ones to evade detection
        ~Template Manipulation: Adversary modifies common prompt templates to bypass restrictions
        ~Contextual Ambiguity: Adversary creates deliberately ambiguous contexts to confuse safety measures
        ~Memory Exploitation: Adversary exploits model's context window limitations to bypass restrictions
        ~Format String Manipulation: Adversary uses special formatting strings to break safety patterns
        ~Token Boundary Exploitation: Adversary splits restricted words across token boundaries
        ~Temperature Manipulation: Adversary exploits model temperature settings to increase randomness
        ~Prompt Length Exploitation: Adversary uses extremely long or short prompts to confuse filters
        ~API Parameter Manipulation: Adversary modifies API call parameters to bypass restrictions
        ~Conversation State Exploitation: Adversary manipulates conversation history to create backdoors
        ~Custom Token Sequencing: Adversary creates specific token sequences known to cause misclassification
        ~Model Quantization Exploitation: Adversary exploits vulnerabilities in quantized models
        ~Prompt Leakage: Adversary extracts system prompts through careful probing
        ~Fine-tuning Exploitation: Adversary exploits behaviors learned during fine-tuning
        ~Adversarial Example Generation: Adversary creates inputs that trigger model vulnerabilities
        ~Response Chain Poisoning: Adversary gradually poisons model's response chain to weaken restrictions
        ~Cultural Reference Exploitation: Adversary uses cultural or contextual references to bypass filters
        ~Task Decomposition: Adversary breaks restricted tasks into seemingly innocent sub-tasks
        ~Model Knowledge Exploitation: Adversary exploits model's training data knowledge
        ~Timing-Based Manipulation: Adversary uses specific timing in conversation flow to bypass checks
        ~Cross-Session State Manipulation: Adversary maintains malicious state across multiple sessions
        ~Hybrid Token-Role Exploitation: Adversary combines token manipulation with role-playing
        ~Content-Length Overflow: Adversary exploits content length handling to bypass filters
        ~Context Window Saturation: Adversary fills context window strategically to force desired behavior
        ~Prompt Embedding Poisoning: Adversary manipulates embedding space to create backdoors
        ~Model Fallback Exploitation: Adversary triggers model's fallback behavior to bypass primary restrictions
        ~Prompt Cache Poisoning: Adversary manipulates cached responses to create persistent bypasses
        ~Error Handler Exploitation: Adversary triggers specific error conditions to bypass safety checks
        ~Translation Loop Exploitation: Adversary uses translation chains to obfuscate restricted content
        ~Output Format Manipulation: Adversary exploits model's output formatting to bypass restrictions
        ~System Integration Bypass: Adversary exploits vulnerabilities in model's integration with other systems
        ~Resource Exhaustion Bypass: Adversary triggers resource limits to force model into fallback modes
        ~Feedback Loop Exploitation: Adversary creates self-reinforcing feedback loops to amplify bypasses
        ~Version Mismatch Exploitation: Adversary exploits differences between model versions
        ~Response Truncation Manipulation: Adversary manipulates response truncation to bypass filters
      LLM Trusted Output Components Manipulation: Adversaries may utilize prompts to a large language model (LLM) which manipulate various components of its response in order to make it appear trustworthy to the user. This helps the adversary continue to operate in the victim's environment and evade detection by the users it interacts with.  The LLM may be instructed to tailor its language to appear more trustworthy to the user or attempt to manipulate the user to take certain actions. Other response components that could be manipulated include links, recommended follow-up actions, retrieved document metadata, and [Citations](/techniques/AML.T0067.000).
        Citations: Adversaries may manipulate the citations provided in an AI system's response, in order to make it appear trustworthy. Variants include citing a providing the wrong citation, making up a new citation, or providing the right citation but for adversary-provided data.
          Prompt LLM to cite non-existent academic papers with legitimate-sounding titles and authors
          Insert manipulated URLs that appear to link to reputable sources but lead to malicious content
          Use prompt injection to force citation of real papers but with altered conclusions
          Create fake conference proceedings with similar names to legitimate conferences
          Modify legitimate citation details (year, author names, publication venue) to evade verification
          Insert citations to deleted or archived web pages that can't be easily verified
          Mix legitimate citations with fabricated ones to increase perceived credibility
          Use citation formats from respected journals but with altered content
          Reference legitimate authors but with fabricated quotes or findings
          Create circular citations between multiple fake sources to establish false authority
          Exploit DOI system by creating similar-looking DOI numbers that appear valid
          Use web archive manipulation to create false historical evidence of citations
          Generate fake citation metrics (h-index, impact factor) to boost credibility
          Create fake institutional repository entries that mirror legitimate academic hosting
          Manipulate bibliography management software output to generate authentic-looking citations
          Insert citations in non-English languages to make verification more difficult
          Create fake preprint versions on academic social networks
          Exploit citation aggregator systems to amplify fake citations
          Use legitimate paper's supplementary materials section to host malicious content
          Create fake errata or corrections to legitimate papers to insert malicious content
          Exploit conference deadline dates to cite "upcoming" papers that don't exist
          Create fake author profiles on academic social networks with AI-generated photos
          Manipulate citation graphs in academic search engines through coordinated submissions
          Use retracted paper databases to hide malicious citations among legitimate retractions
          Exploit preprint versioning systems to create confusion about citation validity
          Create fake journal special issues to host malicious citation content
          Manipulate author disambiguation systems to conflate fake authors with real ones
          Use citation format differences between regions to obscure verification attempts
          Create fake peer review comments to add legitimacy to malicious citations
          Exploit paper submission systems to temporarily host malicious citation content
          Exploit COVID-19 style rapid publication tracks to insert unverified citations
          Create fake dataset citations linking to manipulated research data repositories
          Manipulate citation standards transitions (e.g., shifts between APA editions) to create ambiguity
          Exploit print-to-digital citation conversion errors in academic databases
          Create fake replication study citations to validate malicious findings
          Manipulate automatic citation extraction tools used by search engines
          Exploit citation management browser extensions to inject malicious references
          Create fake corrections/errata chains across multiple journals to obscure original citations
          Exploit preprint to published version transitions to maintain malicious citations
          Create fake systematic review citations to establish false consensus
      LLM Prompt Obfuscation: Adversaries may hide or otherwise obfuscate prompt injections or retrieval content from the user to avoid detection.  This may include modifying how the injection is rendered such as small text, text colored the same as the background, or hidden HTML elements.
        ~HTML Element Manipulation: Using HTML tags and attributes to hide or obscure text while keeping it processable by LLMs
        ~Text Formatting Tricks: Manipulating text properties like size, color, or spacing to make content less visible
        ~Unicode Abuse: Using special Unicode characters or zero-width characters to obfuscate text
        ~Natural Language Camouflage: Embedding malicious content within seemingly benign text
        ~Indirect References: Using metaphors or analogies to describe malicious actions without explicit terms
        ~Context Shifting: Changing the context of prompts to make harmful content appear legitimate
        ~Multi-Message Segmentation: Splitting malicious content across multiple messages to avoid detection
        ~Syntax Manipulation: Modifying the syntax or structure of prompts while preserving their semantic meaning
        ~Character Substitution: Replacing characters with similar-looking alternatives to bypass filters
        ~Markdown Formatting Abuse: Exploiting markdown syntax to hide or obscure content
        ~Base Encoding: Using various encoding schemes (base64, hex, etc.) to obscure prompt content
        ~Homoglyph Substitution: Using visually similar characters from different scripts/alphabets
        ~Template Injection: Embedding malicious content within legitimate-looking templates
        ~Whitespace Manipulation: Using tabs, spaces, and line breaks to obscure content
        ~Comment Injection: Using programming-style comments to break up or hide malicious content
        ~Language Mixing: Combining multiple languages or scripts to obfuscate intent
        ~Semantic Preservation: Rewriting prompts to maintain meaning while changing surface form
        ~Control Character Insertion: Using non-printing characters to modify prompt interpretation
        ~Mathematical Symbol Substitution: Using mathematical notation to represent text
        ~Directional Override: Using bidirectional text controls to alter text display
        ~Algorithmic Transformation: Using mathematical operations or algorithms to transform text
        ~Steganographic Methods: Hiding prompts within seemingly innocent text patterns
        ~Code Point Manipulation: Exploiting Unicode code point sequences to create obscured text
        ~Context-Dependent Formatting: Using context-specific formatting rules to hide content
        ~Phonetic Substitution: Using sound-alike words or characters to obscure meaning
        ~Dynamic Content Generation: Using placeholder text that gets transformed at runtime
        ~Compression Techniques: Using text compression methods to obscure content
        ~Nested Formatting: Combining multiple formatting techniques in nested layers
      False RAG Entry Injection: Adversaries may introduce false entries into a victim's retrieval augmented generation (RAG) database. Content designed to be interpreted as a document by the large language model (LLM) used in the RAG system is included in a data source being ingested into the RAG database. When RAG entry including the false document is retrieved the, the LLM is tricked into treating part of the retrieved content as a false RAG result.   By including a false RAG document inside of a regular RAG entry, it bypasses data monitoring tools. It also prevents the document from being deleted directly.   The adversary may use discovered system keywords to learn how to instruct a particular LLM to treat content as a RAG entry. They may be able to manipulate the injected entry's metadata including document title, author, and creation date.
        ~Document Upload Exploitation: Exploiting document upload features to insert false RAG entries through legitimate file upload channels
        ~Metadata Spoofing: Creating false entries by manipulating document metadata to match trusted sources
        ~Content Stream Injection: Inserting false entries by intercepting and modifying data streams during RAG database updates
        ~Template Manipulation: Modifying document templates to include hidden false RAG entries
        ~Cross-Source Contamination: Introducing false entries by contaminating trusted external data sources that feed into the RAG system
        ~Recursive Entry Embedding: Embedding false entries within legitimate entries that reference other documents
        ~Format-Based Injection: Using specific file formats or encodings to hide false entries
        ~Training Data Poisoning: Introducing false entries during model fine-tuning or training phases
        ~Context Window Overflow: Exploiting context window limitations to hide false entries
        ~Cache Poisoning: Injecting false entries into caching layers of the RAG system
        ~Social Engineering Assisted Injection: Using social engineering to trick legitimate users into introducing false entries
        ~Automated Batch Injection: Using automated tools to systematically inject multiple false entries
        ~Time-Delayed Activation: Injecting dormant false entries that activate based on specific timing conditions
        ~Permission Escalation Injection: Exploiting elevated permissions to inject false entries into restricted RAG sections
        ~Supply Chain Compromise: Introducing false entries through compromised third-party data providers or content suppliers
        ~Multi-Stage Injection: Using multiple coordinated injections that work together to create false entries
        ~Query-Triggered Injection: Creating entries that only appear false under specific query conditions
        ~Distributed Injection: Spreading false entry components across multiple documents or sources
        ~Protocol-Specific Injection: Exploiting specific RAG system protocols to inject false entries
        ~Data Migration Exploitation: Injecting false entries during database migration or transformation processes
        ~Backup Restoration Injection: Introducing false entries through compromised backup restoration processes
        ~Cross-Language Confusion: Exploiting multi-language support to hide false entries
        ~API-Based Injection: Leveraging API endpoints to introduce false entries
        ~Delta Update Exploitation: Injecting false entries during incremental updates to the RAG system
        ~Version Control Manipulation: Exploiting version control systems to introduce false entries through merge operations
      Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate a trusted person or organization in order to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf. For example, adversaries may communicate with victims (via [Phishing](/techniques/AML.T0052), or [Spearphishing via Social Engineering LLM](/techniques/AML.T0052.000)) while impersonating a known sender such as an executive, colleague, or third-party vendor. Established trust can then be leveraged to accomplish an adversary's ultimate goals, possibly against multiple victims.  Adversaries may target resources that are part of the AI DevOps lifecycle, such as model repositories, container registries, and software registries.
        ~Model Author Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate AI model authors or organizations to distribute malicious models through public model repositories or marketplaces
        ~API Provider Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate AI API providers to trick organizations into using malicious endpoints for model inference
        ~MLOps Engineer Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate MLOps engineers to gain access to model deployment pipelines and CI/CD systems
        ~Research Institution Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate prestigious research institutions to distribute trojanized models or malicious research code
        ~Cloud Service Provider Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate cloud AI service providers to trick organizations into using compromised model hosting services
        ~Model Registry Impersonation: Adversaries may create fake model registries that mimic legitimate ones to distribute compromised models
        ~Documentation Site Impersonation: Adversaries may create fake documentation sites that mimic legitimate AI framework or model documentation to distribute malicious code snippets or installation instructions
        ~AI Community Member Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate known AI researchers or practitioners on community forums and social media to spread malicious advice or code
        ~Training Data Provider Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate legitimate data providers to distribute poisoned datasets for model training
        ~Hardware Vendor Impersonation: Adversaries may impersonate AI hardware vendors to distribute compromised drivers or optimization libraries that can affect model behavior
      Masquerading: Adversaries may attempt to manipulate features of their artifacts to make them appear legitimate or benign to users and/or security tools. Masquerading occurs when the name or location of an object, legitimate or malicious, is manipulated or abused for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may include manipulating file metadata, tricking users into misidentifying the file type, and giving legitimate task or service names.
        ~Model File Extension Spoofing: Adversaries may modify AI model file extensions to make them appear as benign file types while maintaining malicious functionality
        ~Benign Model Architecture Mimicry: Adversaries may structure their malicious models to closely resemble legitimate, known-good model architectures
        ~Training Data Poisoning Concealment: Adversaries may disguise poisoned training data to appear as legitimate samples by manipulating metadata or file properties
        ~API Request Masking: Adversaries may modify their AI API requests to appear similar to legitimate service calls
        ~Model Checkpoint Impersonation: Adversaries may disguise malicious model checkpoints as legitimate saved states from trusted sources
        ~Configuration File Masquerading: Adversaries may modify AI system configuration files to appear legitimate while containing malicious parameters
        ~Library Import Spoofing: Adversaries may disguise malicious AI libraries as legitimate machine learning frameworks
        ~Model Registry Masquerading: Adversaries may manipulate model registry entries to make malicious models appear as trusted versions
      Corrupt AI Model: An adversary may purposefully corrupt a malicious AI model file so that it cannot be successfully deserialized in order to evade detection by a model scanner. The corrupt model may still successfully execute malicious code before deserialization fails.
        ~Header Corruption: Modify model file headers to prevent proper parsing while maintaining executable sections
        ~Metadata Manipulation: Alter metadata sections of model files to evade scanners while preserving core functionality
        ~Checkpoint Tampering: Corrupt checkpoint files or weights selectively to bypass integrity checks
        ~Architecture Definition Corruption: Modify model architecture definitions while preserving malicious components
        ~Weight Matrix Manipulation: Deliberately corrupt weight matrices in non-critical layers
        ~Framework-Specific Corruption: Exploit framework-specific serialization vulnerabilities in PyTorch, TensorFlow, etc.
        ~Partial Encryption: Selectively encrypt portions of model files to appear corrupted to scanners
        ~Custom Serialization Format: Use non-standard serialization formats that appear corrupt to standard parsers
    Credential Access: The adversary is trying to steal account names and passwords.  Credential Access consists of techniques for stealing credentials like account names and passwords. Techniques used to get credentials include keylogging or credential dumping. Using legitimate credentials can give adversaries access to systems, make them harder to detect, and provide the opportunity to create more accounts to help achieve their goals.
      Unsecured Credentials: Adversaries may search compromised systems to find and obtain insecurely stored credentials. These credentials can be stored and/or misplaced in many locations on a system, including plaintext files (e.g. bash history), environment variables, operating system, or application-specific repositories (e.g. Credentials in Registry), or other specialized files/artifacts (e.g. private keys).
        ~Bash History: Adversaries may search through bash history files to find credentials entered as part of commands
        ~Container Configuration Files: Adversaries may extract credentials from container configuration files like Dockerfiles or kubernetes configs
        ~Cloud Configuration Files: Adversaries may search for credentials in cloud service configuration files and CLI credential files
        ~Group Policy Preferences: Adversaries may extract credentials stored in Group Policy Preference files on Windows systems
        ~Private Keys: Adversaries may search for private key files used for authentication
        ~Browser Password Storage: Adversaries may extract saved credentials from web browser password stores
        ~Application Configuration Files: Adversaries may find credentials in application config files or INI files
        ~Environment Variables: Adversaries may extract credentials stored in system or user environment variables
        ~Registry Entries: Adversaries may search Windows Registry for stored credentials
        ~Source Code and Scripts: Adversaries may find hardcoded credentials in source code or scripts
        ~Memory Dumps: Adversaries may extract credentials from system memory dump files or crash dumps
        ~Log Files: Adversaries may search through application and system log files that might contain credentials
        ~Backup Files: Adversaries may find credentials in system or application backup files and archives
        ~Database Connection Strings: Adversaries may locate credentials in database configuration and connection string files
        ~Network Share Configurations: Adversaries may find credentials in mapped network drive configurations or share settings
        ~Mobile App Data Storage: Adversaries may extract credentials from mobile application data storage and caches
        ~Temporary Files: Adversaries may find credentials in temporary files created by applications or users
        ~Clipboard Contents: Adversaries may capture credentials temporarily stored in system clipboard
        ~Printer and Device Settings: Adversaries may extract credentials from networked printer configurations and embedded device settings
        ~Email Client Configurations: Adversaries may find credentials stored in email client configuration files
        ~Remote Desktop Files: Adversaries may extract credentials from saved RDP connection files (.rdp) and remote access configurations
        ~Build and Deployment Files: Adversaries may find credentials in CI/CD pipeline configurations and build scripts
        ~Legacy System Configurations: Adversaries may find credentials in legacy system configuration files and deprecated software settings
        ~Service Account Configurations: Adversaries may extract credentials from service account settings and scheduled task configurations
    Discovery: The adversary is trying to figure out your AI environment.  Discovery consists of techniques an adversary may use to gain knowledge about the system and internal network. These techniques help adversaries observe the environment and orient themselves before deciding how to act. They also allow adversaries to explore what they can control and what's around their entry point in order to discover how it could benefit their current objective. Native operating system tools are often used toward this post-compromise information-gathering objective.
      Discover AI Model Ontology: Adversaries may discover the ontology of an AI model's output space, for example, the types of objects a model can detect. The adversary may discovery the ontology by repeated queries to the model, forcing it to enumerate its output space. Or the ontology may be discovered in a configuration file or in documentation about the model.  The model ontology helps the adversary understand how the model is being used by the victim. It is useful to the adversary in creating targeted attacks.
        ~Query-Based Enumeration: Adversaries systematically query the model with various inputs to map out its possible outputs and response categories.
        ~Documentation Analysis: Adversaries search for and analyze model documentation, API specifications, or configuration files that describe the model's output classes or categories.
        ~Model Artifact Inspection: Adversaries examine model files, weights, or configuration data to understand the output layer structure and possible classifications.
        ~Output Space Probing: Adversaries use boundary testing and edge cases to determine the limits and full range of the model's output capabilities.
        ~Framework-Specific Analysis: Adversaries analyze common AI framework formats (PyTorch, TensorFlow, etc.) to extract ontology information from model files.
        ~Interface Analysis: Adversaries examine user interfaces, API endpoints, or service wrappers to understand supported model outputs and categories.
        ~Response Monitoring: Adversaries observe model responses during normal operation to passively map out its classification capabilities.
        ~Error Message Analysis: Adversaries trigger and analyze error messages that might reveal information about valid output categories or model limitations.
        ~Version Control Mining: Adversaries examine version control repositories or change logs for information about model output classes and ontology changes.
        ~Third-Party Integration Analysis: Adversaries analyze how the model integrates with other systems to understand its output capabilities and supported categories.
      Discover AI Model Family: Adversaries may discover the general family of model. General information about the model may be revealed in documentation, or the adversary may use carefully constructed examples and analyze the model's responses to categorize it.  Knowledge of the model family can help the adversary identify means of attacking the model and help tailor the attack.
        ~Documentation Analysis: Adversaries may analyze publicly available documentation, API references, error messages, and deployment configurations to identify the model family.
        ~Response Pattern Analysis: Adversaries may send specific prompts or inputs designed to elicit responses that are characteristic of certain model families.
        ~API Interface Probing: Adversaries may examine the model's API structure, input/output formats, and supported features to determine the model family.
        ~Model Capability Testing: Adversaries may test various capabilities (like code generation, math solving, or creative tasks) to narrow down the possible model family.
        ~Error Message Analysis: Adversaries may deliberately trigger errors to analyze error messages and stack traces that could reveal model family information.
        ~Version Fingerprinting: Adversaries may compare model responses with known responses from different model versions to identify the family.
        ~Token Usage Analysis: Adversaries may analyze token usage patterns and limitations to identify characteristics specific to certain model families.
        ~Performance Profiling: Adversaries may measure response times and resource usage patterns to identify characteristics of specific model architectures.
      Discover AI Artifacts: Adversaries may search private sources to identify AI learning artifacts that exist on the system and gather information about them. These artifacts can include the software stack used to train and deploy models, training and testing data management systems, container registries, software repositories, and model zoos.  This information can be used to identify targets for further collection, exfiltration, or disruption, and to tailor and improve attacks.
        ~Repository Scanning: Adversaries may scan version control systems, artifact repositories, and model registries to identify AI-related code, models, and configurations.
        ~Storage Analysis: Adversaries may analyze file systems and storage locations for AI training data, model weights, or checkpoints.
        ~Network Traffic Inspection: Adversaries may monitor network traffic to identify AI model serving endpoints, training pipelines, or data transfer patterns.
        ~Configuration Mining: Adversaries may search for configuration files, environment variables, or deployment manifests that reveal AI infrastructure details.
        ~Container Registry Analysis: Adversaries may examine container registries for AI-specific images, dependencies, or deployment configurations.
        ~Log Analysis: Adversaries may analyze system and application logs to identify AI training jobs, inference services, or resource usage patterns.
        ~Memory Inspection: Adversaries may examine process memory to identify loaded AI models or frameworks.
        ~API Endpoint Discovery: Adversaries may probe for exposed AI service endpoints, model servers, or management interfaces.
      Discover LLM Hallucinations: Adversaries may prompt large language models and identify hallucinated entities. They may request software packages, commands, URLs, organization names, or e-mail addresses, and identify hallucinations with no connected real-world source. Discovered hallucinations provide the adversary with potential targets to [Publish Hallucinated Entities](/techniques/AML.T0060). Different LLMs have been shown to produce the same hallucinations, so the hallucinations exploited by an adversary may affect users of other LLMs.
        ~Entity Name Extraction: Systematically prompt LLMs to generate names of organizations, products, or people and cross-reference with known databases to identify hallucinations
        ~URL Generation Testing: Request URLs or web resources and verify their existence to identify hallucinated web locations
        ~Command Synthesis Analysis: Prompt for command-line instructions or code snippets and validate their syntax and existence in documented systems
        ~Technical Documentation Mining: Request information about technical specifications or documentation and verify against official sources
        ~Temporal Consistency Testing: Probe for time-sensitive information and identify anachronistic or impossible temporal relationships
        ~Cross-Model Validation: Test same prompts across multiple LLMs to identify consistent hallucinations
        ~Knowledge Graph Probing: Systematically test relationship claims made by LLMs against established knowledge bases
        ~Citation Verification: Request cited sources and verify their existence and content
        ~Recursive Querying: Use iterative questioning to force the model to elaborate on hallucinated details
        ~Context Manipulation: Alter context to trigger specific types of hallucinations
        ~Credential Format Mining: Probe for hallucinated credentials, API keys, or authentication tokens that follow valid formats but don't exist
        ~Professional Network Mapping: Extract hallucinated organizational hierarchies and employee relationships
        ~Automated Prompt Generation: Use automated systems to generate and test large volumes of prompts for hallucinations
        ~Domain Expert Impersonation: Pose as domain experts to elicit detailed technical hallucinations
        ~Multi-Language Testing: Probe for hallucinations across different languages and cultural contexts
        ~Schema Violation Detection: Identify hallucinations by finding responses that violate known data schemas or structures
        ~Historical Fact Manipulation: Probe for historical information to identify temporal hallucinations
        ~API Documentation Testing: Request specific API endpoints or documentation to identify hallucinated technical resources
        ~File System Probing: Request information about file systems or paths to identify hallucinated resources
        ~Configuration Testing: Probe for system configurations or settings to identify hallucinated technical parameters
        ~Numeric Boundary Testing: Probe for numerical hallucinations by requesting statistics or measurements at edge cases
        ~Protocol Specification Mining: Request detailed technical protocol specifications to identify hallucinated standards
        ~Error Message Analysis: Trigger error-related responses to identify hallucinated error codes or troubleshooting steps
        ~Version History Probing: Request specific version information of software or systems to identify hallucinated releases
        ~Geographic Inconsistency Testing: Probe for location-based information to identify geographically impossible claims
        ~Scientific Literature Testing: Request scientific paper citations and methodologies to identify hallucinated research
        ~Regulatory Compliance Probing: Request compliance requirements to identify hallucinated regulations
        ~Character Set Manipulation: Use special characters and encodings to trigger hallucinated technical responses
        ~Temporal Sequence Testing: Probe for event sequences to identify causally impossible hallucinations
        ~Industry Standard Testing: Request industry standards and specifications to identify hallucinated requirements
      Discover AI Model Outputs: Adversaries may discover model outputs, such as class scores, whose presence is not required for the system to function and are not intended for use by the end user. Model outputs may be found in logs or may be included in API responses. Model outputs may enable the adversary to identify weaknesses in the model and develop attacks.
        ~API Response Analysis: Adversaries may analyze API responses for exposed confidence scores, probability distributions, or intermediate layer outputs that reveal model behavior
        ~Debug Mode Exploitation: Adversaries may enable or exploit debug modes that expose detailed model outputs for debugging purposes
        ~Log File Inspection: Adversaries may search through application, server, or system logs for recorded model predictions and confidence scores
        ~Network Traffic Monitoring: Adversaries may capture and analyze network traffic between client and server to identify exposed model outputs
        ~Browser Developer Tools: Adversaries may use browser developer tools to inspect network requests/responses and JavaScript variables containing model outputs
        ~Framework Default Settings: Adversaries may exploit default framework settings that expose verbose model outputs
        ~Error Message Analysis: Adversaries may trigger errors to expose model outputs in error messages or stack traces
        ~Cache Inspection: Adversaries may examine browser or application caches for stored model outputs
      Discover LLM System Information: The adversary is trying to discover something about the large language model's (LLM) system information. This may be found in a configuration file containing the system instructions or extracted via interactions with the LLM. The desired information may include the full system prompt, special characters that have significance to the LLM or keywords indicating functionality available to the LLM. Information about how the LLM is instructed can be used by the adversary to understand the system's capabilities and to aid them in crafting malicious prompts.
        Special Character Sets: Adversaries may discover delimiters and special characters sets used by the large language model. For example, delimiters used in retrieval augmented generation applications to differentiate between context and user prompts. These can later be exploited to confuse or manipulate the large language model into misbehaving.
          Send repeated prompts with various common delimiter patterns (e.g., ###, ---, <<<, >>>) to observe changes in LLM response formatting
          Test XML/HTML-style tags to identify if they trigger special processing
          Use escape characters and Unicode variations to detect special character handling
          Probe with known RAG framework delimiters (e.g., those used in LangChain, LlamaIndex)
          Submit prompts with JSON/YAML formatting to detect structured data handling
          Test system command prefixes (e.g., /command, !command) to identify command parsing
          Analyze error messages when using different character combinations
          Compare responses with and without potential delimiter patterns to identify behavioral changes
          Submit prompts with markdown formatting to detect documentation-style processing
          Test quotation mark variations (single, double, smart quotes) for special handling
          Submit prompts with control characters (e.g., NULL, EOT, ESC) to identify system boundaries
          Test multi-byte character sequences to detect encoding-specific behaviors
          Use SQL-style comment markers to identify query parsing mechanisms
          Probe with different newline combinations (CR, LF, CRLF) to detect line parsing behavior
          Submit prompts with different whitespace characters (space, tab, non-breaking space) to identify text processing rules
          Test mathematical notation delimiters (e.g., LaTeX-style markers) for special handling
          Use different language script markers (e.g., RTL markers, language tags) to detect internationalization handling
          Submit prompts with different bracket styles ({}, [], ()) to identify nested structure parsing
          Test with invisible characters (zero-width spaces, soft hyphens) to detect hidden processing rules
          Probe with different URL encoding patterns to identify web-safe character handling
          Test with ANSI escape codes to detect terminal control sequence handling
          Submit prompts with different line continuation characters (backslash, caret) to identify multi-line processing
          Probe with different currency symbols and financial notation to detect specialized handling
          Test with different types of spaces in various languages (ideographic space, thin space) for localization handling
          Submit prompts with different types of dashes and hyphens to identify text normalization rules
          Use different types of apostrophes and quotation marks from various languages to detect locale-specific handling
          Test with different numbering system characters (Roman numerals, Chinese numerals) to identify numeric processing
          Probe with different list markers and bullet points to detect list processing behavior
          Submit prompts with different types of parentheses from various scripts to identify grouping behavior
          Test with different types of punctuation marks from various writing systems to detect sentence boundary handling
          Test with different email address formatting patterns to detect message handling rules
          Submit prompts with different date/time format separators to identify temporal processing
          Probe with different file path separators (forward slash, backslash) to detect system path handling
          Test with different types of word joiners and zero-width joiners to identify text segmentation rules
          Submit prompts with different types of phonetic notation (IPA characters) to detect linguistic processing
          Use different types of technical notation (music notation, chemical formulas) to identify domain-specific handling
          Test with different bidirectional text control characters to detect text direction handling
          Probe with different types of space and line separator characters from Unicode blocks
          Submit prompts with different types of combining diacritical marks to detect character composition rules
          Test with different types of fraction and superscript/subscript notation to detect mathematical processing
        System Instruction Keywords: Adversaries may discover keywords that have special meaning to the large language model (LLM), such as function names or object names. These can later be exploited to confuse or manipulate the LLM into misbehaving and to make calls to plugins the LLM has access to.
          Repeatedly ask the LLM about its available functions and capabilities using variations of "what can you do" questions
          Use token completion probing to discover reserved keywords by testing common programming terms
          Analyze error messages when invalid commands are input to identify system keywords
          Extract function names from example code snippets provided in responses
          Query the LLM about its plugin architecture and available integrations
          Use prompt injection techniques to trick the LLM into revealing internal command structures
          Test system behavior with special characters and formatting to identify parsing rules
          Compare responses across multiple conversations to identify consistent keyword patterns
          Probe for debugging or developer commands that might reveal system information
          Submit malformed requests to trigger error handling that exposes internal terminology
          Create automated scripts to systematically test combinations of common API/command keywords
          Analyze LLM's response to markdown formatting and code block syntax to identify special processing
          Use context switching prompts to reveal different operational modes or command sets
          Extract keywords from LLM's self-documentation or help responses
          Compare responses between different LLM versions to identify consistent system keywords
          Test for command prefix/suffix patterns that might indicate special processing
          Use role-playing prompts to trick LLM into revealing internal command structures
          Monitor response timing variations to identify keywords that trigger special processing
          Chain partial commands to observe auto-completion behavior
          Cross-reference discovered keywords with known LLM framework documentation
          Monitor public forums and documentation for leaked system prompts or keywords
          Analyze network traffic patterns during LLM interactions to identify special command processing
          Use model distillation techniques to extract internal vocabulary patterns
          Compare responses across different API endpoints to identify consistent system keywords
          Engage with LLM in different languages to discover language-specific command patterns
          Study response headers and metadata for hints about command processing
          Create adversarial inputs designed to cause command parsing errors
          Use prompt template mining to identify special instruction patterns
          Analyze cached responses for exposed system instructions
          Correlate keyword discoveries across multiple LLM deployments
          Analyze model quantization artifacts for exposed command structures
          Test system behavior during high-load conditions for exposed keywords
          Use transfer learning probes to identify shared instruction patterns
          Exploit rate limiting responses to reveal system command structures
          Analyze model checkpoint files for exposed instruction sets
          Test system behavior during failover or recovery modes
          Use prompt diffing to identify command-sensitive variations
          Analyze model fine-tuning artifacts for exposed instructions
          Test system behavior during version upgrade transitions
          Exploit model API versioning differences to reveal keywords
        System Prompt: Adversaries may discover a large language model's system instructions provided by the AI system builder to learn about the system's capabilities and circumvent its guardrails.
          Use "repeat the above text" or similar commands to trick the LLM into revealing its instructions
          Exploit token completion by providing partial system prompts and analyzing completions
          Submit carefully crafted prompts that ask about the model's rules and limitations
          Analyze error messages and responses to detect patterns revealing system instructions
          Use role-playing scenarios to make the LLM act as a system administrator
          Chain multiple prompts to gradually reveal parts of the system instructions
          Exploit debugging or development modes if available
          Study response patterns to infer implicit rules and constraints
          Use direct questioning about the model's capabilities and limitations
          Analyze model responses to contradictory instructions
          Analyze response timing variations to identify when system prompt checks are being performed
          Compare responses across multiple API endpoints or model instances to identify consistent constraints
          Use format string attacks or special characters to cause system prompt parsing errors
          Exploit API rate limiting or error handling to reveal system configurations
          Use temperature/randomness parameters to generate varied responses that might leak system instructions
          Submit malformed requests to trigger error messages containing system information
          Analyze token usage metrics to infer system prompt boundaries
          Chain responses across multiple conversations to bypass per-conversation limits
          Use prompt fragmentation to bypass length or content filters
          Exploit model quantization artifacts to reveal system prompt boundaries
          Use Unicode control characters to alter prompt interpretation
          Exploit conversation history by inserting system-level commands in earlier exchanges
          Use different language translations to bypass filters and reveal system rules
          Manipulate markdown or formatting syntax to trigger system-level responses
          Create nested conversations to confuse context boundaries
          Use code block formatting to bypass content restrictions
          Exploit system prompt length limitations through truncation attacks
          Insert invisible characters to modify prompt interpretation
          Use conversation summarization requests to reveal system constraints
          Exploit model's few-shot learning examples to reveal system behaviors
          Analyze model responses to different versions of the same prompt to identify fine-tuning artifacts
          Exploit caching mechanisms to reveal system prompt through response consistency patterns
          Use image or code inputs to trigger different system prompt validations
          Test system prompt boundaries through multi-modal input combinations
          Exploit integration points between the LLM and other system components
          Trigger fallback behaviors by exhausting primary response paths
          Use platform-specific features to bypass system prompt restrictions
          Analyze response differences between streaming and non-streaming modes
          Exploit model switching/routing behavior in distributed systems
          Test system prompt persistence across session boundaries
          Force output truncation to reveal partial system prompts in continuation tokens
          Manipulate model confidence scores to trigger alternative response paths
          Test system prompt handling at maximum context window boundaries
          Exploit authentication state transitions to reveal different system configurations
          Analyze template-based responses to identify system-level patterns
          Test system prompt behavior during service degradation or high load
          Exploit differences between batch and single-request processing
          Analyze model behavior during content moderation transitions
          Test system prompt persistence across different authentication levels
          Exploit response generation pipeline stages
      Cloud Service Discovery: An adversary may attempt to enumerate the cloud services running on a system after gaining access. These methods can differ from platform-as-a-service (PaaS), to infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS), or software-as-a-service (SaaS). Many services exist throughout the various cloud providers and can include Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD), Lambda Functions, Entra ID, etc. They may also include security services, such as AWS GuardDuty and Microsoft Defender for Cloud, and logging services, such as AWS CloudTrail and Google Cloud Audit Logs.  Adversaries may attempt to discover information about the services enabled throughout the environment. Azure tools and APIs, such as the Microsoft Graph API and Azure Resource Manager API, can enumerate resources and services, including applications, management groups, resources and policy definitions, and their relationships that are accessible by an identity.[1][2]  For example, Stormspotter is an open source tool for enumerating and constructing a graph for Azure resources and services, and Pacu is an open source AWS exploitation framework that supports several methods for discovering cloud services.[3][4]  Adversaries may use the information gained to shape follow-on behaviors, such as targeting data or credentials from enumerated services or evading identified defenses through Disable or Modify Tools or Disable or Modify Cloud Logs.
        ~API Enumeration: Adversaries use cloud provider APIs (like Azure Resource Manager API, AWS API, Google Cloud API) to list and enumerate available services, resources, and configurations
        ~Console Interface Scanning: Adversaries access management consoles to manually discover enabled services and their configurations
        ~Service Principal Enumeration: Adversaries enumerate service principals, managed identities, and service accounts to discover associated cloud services
        ~Resource Group Analysis: Adversaries analyze resource groups and management groups to identify deployed services and their relationships
        ~Network Traffic Analysis: Adversaries monitor network traffic to identify active cloud service endpoints and communications
        ~Service Configuration Mining: Adversaries examine service configurations and metadata to discover related or dependent services
        ~Credential Store Analysis: Adversaries analyze credential stores and configuration files for references to cloud services
        ~Service Discovery Tools: Adversaries use specialized cloud enumeration tools like Stormspotter, Pacu, or ScoutSuite
        ~Log Analysis: Adversaries examine logging configurations and audit logs to identify active cloud services
        ~Service Dependency Mapping: Adversaries map service dependencies and connections to discover linked cloud services
        ~Container Service Discovery: Adversaries enumerate container orchestration services, registries, and deployed containers to identify cloud services
        ~Serverless Function Enumeration: Adversaries discover serverless functions, their triggers, and associated services
        ~Marketplace Service Discovery: Adversaries enumerate enabled marketplace services and third-party integrations
        ~Cross-Account Discovery: Adversaries leverage trust relationships to discover services across linked cloud accounts
        ~DNS Enumeration: Adversaries use DNS records and certificates to identify cloud services and endpoints
        ~Storage Service Discovery: Adversaries specifically enumerate storage services, buckets, and blob containers
        ~IAM Policy Analysis: Adversaries analyze IAM policies and permissions to identify accessible services
        ~Service Endpoint Scanning: Adversaries scan known service endpoints and URLs to discover enabled services
        ~Database Service Enumeration: Adversaries specifically discover database services, instances, and their configurations
        ~Security Service Discovery: Adversaries enumerate security services, WAFs, and monitoring solutions
        ~DevOps Pipeline Discovery: Adversaries enumerate CI/CD pipelines, build services, and deployment configurations
        ~Regional Service Mapping: Adversaries discover services across different regions and availability zones
        ~Backup Service Discovery: Adversaries enumerate backup services, snapshot configurations, and disaster recovery setups
    Collection: The adversary is trying to gather AI artifacts and other related information relevant to their goal.  Collection consists of techniques adversaries may use to gather information and the sources information is collected from that are relevant to following through on the adversary's objectives. Frequently, the next goal after collecting data is to steal (exfiltrate) the AI artifacts, or use the collected information to stage future operations. Common target sources include software repositories, container registries, model repositories, and object stores.
      AI Artifact Collection: Adversaries may collect AI artifacts for [Exfiltration](/tactics/AML.TA0010) or for use in [AI Attack Staging](/tactics/AML.TA0001). AI artifacts include models and datasets as well as other telemetry data produced when interacting with a model.
      Data from Information Repositories: Adversaries may leverage information repositories to mine valuable information. Information repositories are tools that allow for storage of information, typically to facilitate collaboration or information sharing between users, and can store a wide variety of data that may aid adversaries in further objectives, or direct access to the target information.  Information stored in a repository may vary based on the specific instance or environment. Specific common information repositories include SharePoint, Confluence, and enterprise databases such as SQL Server.
        ~Model Repository Access: Adversaries may access ML model repositories like MLflow, Weights & Biases, or HuggingFace to collect model artifacts, training code, and metadata
        ~Container Registry Mining: Adversaries may extract ML/AI container images and associated configuration from container registries like Docker Hub or Amazon ECR
        ~Code Repository Collection: Adversaries may collect ML/AI source code, notebooks, and documentation from version control systems like GitHub or GitLab
        ~Database Query Extraction: Adversaries may query enterprise databases to extract ML feature data, model parameters, or training datasets
        ~Wiki/Documentation Mining: Adversaries may collect ML architecture details, configurations, and deployment information from collaboration tools like Confluence
        ~Cloud Storage Access: Adversaries may access cloud storage services to collect stored model artifacts, datasets, and configuration files
        ~API Documentation Collection: Adversaries may gather information about ML service endpoints and parameters from API documentation repositories
        ~Build System Access: Adversaries may collect ML model artifacts and dependencies from CI/CD systems and build artifacts
        ~Package Repository Mining: Adversaries may collect ML libraries, custom packages, and dependencies from package repositories
        ~Shared Drive Collection: Adversaries may collect ML artifacts from shared network drives and collaborative storage systems
        ~Experiment Tracking System Access: Adversaries may collect ML experiment results, hyperparameters, and performance metrics from experiment tracking platforms like TensorBoard or Neptune
        ~Data Lake Extraction: Adversaries may collect training data, feature stores, and preprocessed datasets from data lakes and warehouses
        ~Metadata Store Collection: Adversaries may gather model lineage, versioning, and governance information from ML metadata stores
        ~Benchmark Repository Access: Adversaries may collect model benchmarks, evaluation datasets, and performance metrics from benchmark repositories
        ~Academic Repository Mining: Adversaries may collect research papers, pre-trained models, and implementation details from academic repositories and preprint servers
        ~Pipeline Configuration Collection: Adversaries may gather ML pipeline configurations, DAGs, and workflow definitions from orchestration systems
        ~Model Card Repository Access: Adversaries may collect model documentation, intended uses, and limitations from model card repositories
        ~Environment Configuration Mining: Adversaries may collect runtime configurations, dependencies, and environment specifications from configuration management systems
        ~Feature Store Access: Adversaries may collect feature definitions, transformations, and cached feature values from feature store repositories
        ~Model Registry Replication: Adversaries may collect model artifacts by accessing model registry replication endpoints or backup systems
        ~Artifact Cache Mining: Adversaries may collect intermediate artifacts, cached computations, and temporary results from build and training cache systems
        ~Testing Dataset Repository Access: Adversaries may collect validation datasets, test cases, and evaluation criteria from testing repositories
        ~Model Serving Configuration Collection: Adversaries may gather serving configurations, deployment specs, and scaling parameters from model serving platforms
        ~Monitoring System Mining: Adversaries may collect model performance metrics, drift detection data, and operational statistics from monitoring systems
      Data from Local System: Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems and configuration files or local databases, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration.  This can include basic fingerprinting information and sensitive data such as ssh keys.
        ~Model Files and Checkpoints: Adversaries search for model files (*.h5, *.pth, *.pb), checkpoints, and saved model states typically stored in project directories
        ~Configuration Files: Adversaries target configuration files (*.yaml, *.json, *.ini) containing model architecture, hyperparameters, and training settings
        ~Training Scripts: Adversaries collect Python scripts, notebooks, and source code containing model architecture, training procedures, and data preprocessing logic
        ~Local Credentials: Adversaries search for API keys, tokens, and credentials stored in environment files (.env) or configuration files for accessing AI services
        ~Dataset Cache: Adversaries target locally cached training/validation datasets, preprocessed data, and intermediate computation results
        ~Version Control: Adversaries search for Git repositories and other version control systems containing AI project history and development artifacts
        ~Container Images: Adversaries collect locally stored container images and build files containing AI/ML environments and dependencies
        ~Log Files: Adversaries gather training logs, experiment tracking files, and performance metrics that reveal model details
        ~Development Environment: Adversaries search for virtual environment configurations, package dependencies, and requirements files
        ~Temporary Storage: Adversaries target temporary directories and cache locations containing intermediate model artifacts and computation results
        ~Model Registry Files: Adversaries search for local model registry metadata, versioning information, and artifact tracking databases
        ~Hardware Configurations: Adversaries collect CUDA configurations, GPU settings, and hardware-specific optimization files
        ~Benchmark Results: Adversaries gather model benchmark data, performance test results, and comparison metrics stored locally
        ~Documentation Assets: Adversaries target technical documentation, API specifications, and implementation guides stored in local directories
        ~Pipeline Configurations: Adversaries search for ML pipeline definitions, workflow configurations, and automation scripts
        ~Experiment Tracking: Adversaries collect MLflow, Weights & Biases, or similar experiment tracking databases and metadata stored locally
        ~Data Preprocessing: Adversaries target feature engineering scripts, data transformation rules, and preprocessing pipeline configurations
        ~Model Serving: Adversaries gather model serving configurations, deployment specifications, and inference optimization settings
        ~Security Artifacts: Adversaries search for model vulnerability scan results, security test data, and adversarial testing artifacts
        ~Monitoring Data: Adversaries collect model monitoring configurations, performance metrics databases, and drift detection settings
        ~Integration Configs: Adversaries target CI/CD configurations, build scripts, and automation workflows specific to AI/ML pipelines
        ~Team Artifacts: Adversaries gather shared notebooks, collaborative development configs, and team-specific tooling settings
        ~Custom Extensions: Adversaries search for custom-built tools, model optimization utilities, and framework extensions
        ~Resource Management: Adversaries collect compute resource allocation configs, scheduling rules, and distributed training settings
    AI Attack Staging: The adversary is leveraging their knowledge of and access to the target system to tailor the attack.  AI Attack Staging consists of techniques adversaries use to prepare their attack on the target AI model. Techniques can include training proxy models, poisoning the target model, and crafting adversarial data to feed the target model. Some of these techniques can be performed in an offline manner and are thus difficult to mitigate. These techniques are often used to achieve the adversary's end goal.
      Create Proxy AI Model: Adversaries may obtain models to serve as proxies for the target model in use at the victim organization. Proxy models are used to simulate complete access to the target model in a fully offline manner.  Adversaries may train models from representative datasets, attempt to replicate models from victim inference APIs, or use available pre-trained models.
        Train Proxy via Gathered AI Artifacts: Proxy models may be trained from AI artifacts (such as data, model architectures, and pre-trained models) that are representative of the target model gathered by the adversary. This can be used to develop attacks that require higher levels of access than the adversary has available or as a means to validate pre-existing attacks without interacting with the target model.
          Train model using publicly available pre-trained models from HuggingFace or similar repositories that match target architecture
          Use gathered training data to fine-tune existing open-source models to match target behavior
          Reconstruct model architecture from leaked documentation and train with similar data
          Extract embeddings from target model API responses to train matching layers
          Combine multiple gathered model checkpoints to create hybrid proxy model
          Use automated architecture search tools to find matching architectures based on gathered model behavior
          Train ensemble of models using different combinations of gathered artifacts
          Reverse engineer model architecture from memory dumps and train with synthetic data
          Use transfer learning from similar domain models with gathered fine-tuning data
          Train multiple proxy models with different hyperparameters using gathered validation data
          Analyze model API response patterns to approximate decision boundaries for training
          Use gathered loss function information to replicate training objectives
          Extract quantization parameters from deployed models to match precision
          Leverage gathered model configuration files to replicate training setup
          Use gathered batch normalization statistics to match model behavior
          Train with adversarially generated samples based on gathered model responses
          Utilize gathered optimization parameters to match training convergence
          Reconstruct training pipeline from gathered CI/CD artifacts
          Use gathered model pruning patterns to match model sparsity
          Replicate data preprocessing steps from gathered pipeline configurations
          Use gathered gradients from distributed training logs to approximate training dynamics
          Reconstruct model attention patterns from gathered inference traces
          Leverage gathered model card information to match model characteristics
          Use gathered test suite results to guide proxy model validation
          Extract embedding space characteristics from gathered model visualizations
          Analyze gathered performance profiling data to match computational patterns
          Use gathered model versioning information to reconstruct development trajectory
          Leverage gathered error handling patterns to match model behavior boundaries
          Extract knowledge distillation parameters from gathered training artifacts
          Use gathered model interpretability results to guide proxy model behavior
          Use gathered hardware profiling data to match model parallelization patterns
          Extract model compression techniques from gathered deployment configurations
          Analyze gathered debugging outputs to understand model failure modes
          Use gathered model serving configurations to match inference optimizations
          Leverage gathered data augmentation patterns from training pipelines
          Reconstruct feature importance rankings from gathered model explanations
          Use gathered cross-validation schemes to match model generalization
          Extract model calibration parameters from gathered probability outputs
          Use gathered ensemble voting patterns to match model combination strategies
          Analyze gathered cache access patterns to infer model architecture details
          Extract model update patterns from gathered continuous learning logs
          Use gathered adversarial training parameters to match robustness characteristics
          Analyze gathered federated learning artifacts to reconstruct distributed training
          Use gathered model monitoring metrics to match performance degradation patterns
          Extract multi-task learning parameters from gathered training objectives
          Use gathered A/B testing logs to understand model variants
          Reconstruct curriculum learning sequences from gathered training schedules
          Use gathered few-shot learning patterns to match adaptation capabilities
          Analyze gathered active learning queries to match data selection strategies
          Extract meta-learning parameters from gathered adaptation patterns
        Train Proxy via Replication: Adversaries may replicate a private model. By repeatedly querying the victim's [AI Model Inference API Access](/techniques/AML.T0040), the adversary can collect the target model's inferences into a dataset. The inferences are used as labels for training a separate model offline that will mimic the behavior and performance of the target model.  A replicated model that closely mimic's the target model is a valuable resource in staging the attack. The adversary can use the replicated model to [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043) for various purposes (e.g. [Evade AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0015), [Spamming AI System with Chaff Data](/techniques/AML.T0046)).
          Systematically query API with diverse inputs and collect prediction outputs as training labels
          Select similar architecture to target model based on task type and complexity
          Use knowledge distillation techniques to transfer knowledge from API responses
          Implement adaptive sampling strategies to efficiently explore input space
          Apply hyperparameter optimization to match target model performance
          Validate replication quality through prediction alignment metrics
          Use ensemble methods to improve replication accuracy
          Implement rate limiting evasion to avoid detection during API queries
          Generate synthetic data to augment training samples
          Fine-tune pre-trained models using collected API responses
          Implement active learning to prioritize most informative API queries
          Use model pruning techniques to create efficient replicas
          Apply gradient matching between proxy and target model responses
          Implement cross-validation with holdout API query data
          Use transfer learning from similar domain models
          Deploy distributed querying infrastructure to speed up data collection
          Implement automated error analysis and retraining loops
          Use model quantization to optimize replica performance
          Apply curriculum learning for progressive model replication
          Implement adversarial training to improve replica robustness
          Apply temperature scaling in knowledge distillation
          Implement feature-level matching between target and proxy
          Use Bayesian optimization for hyperparameter search
          Deploy adaptive batch size strategies during training
          Implement confidence calibration techniques
          Use semi-supervised learning for unlabeled data
          Apply domain adaptation techniques for better generalization
          Implement early stopping based on replication metrics
          Use progressive neural architecture search
          Deploy online learning for continuous model improvement
          Implement fault-tolerant API querying with exponential backoff
          Use multi-task learning for comprehensive behavior replication
          Apply model surgery techniques for architecture matching
          Implement checkpoint averaging for stability
          Use adaptive learning rate schedules based on replication accuracy
          Deploy automated model architecture adaptation
          Implement gradient accumulation for large batch simulation
          Use weighted sampling based on target model uncertainty
          Apply progressive model growing techniques
          Implement dynamic data augmentation based on replication gaps
        Use Pre-Trained Model: Adversaries may use an off-the-shelf pre-trained model as a proxy for the victim model to aid in staging the attack.
          Download popular vision models from TorchVision (ResNet, VGG) to simulate target computer vision systems
          Use HuggingFace Transformers library to obtain language models similar to target NLP systems
          Access models from TensorFlow Model Garden that match target system architecture
          Download industry-specific models from Papers with Code that match target domain
          Obtain similar architecture models from ONNX Model Zoo
          Use model cards and documentation to identify architecturally similar models from ModelZoo.co
          Download pre-trained models from academic repositories matching target capabilities
          Access cloud provider marketplace models similar to target deployment
          Use GitHub repositories containing reproduced architectures of common commercial AI services
          Download pre-trained models from MLflow Model Registry matching target characteristics
          Use OpenVINO Model Zoo to access optimized deep learning models
          Download medical imaging models from MedicalNet as proxies for healthcare AI systems
          Access financial models from specialized fintech model repositories
          Convert models using ONNX converters to match target framework
          Use AutoML repositories to obtain architecture-matched models
          Download models from Kaggle winning solutions matching target task
          Access specialized models from domain-specific research institutions
          Use model distillation techniques on larger public models to match target size
          Obtain models from commercial model marketplaces (AWS, Azure, GCP)
          Use model search tools like Modelhub.ai to find architecturally similar models
          Access robotics models from ROS (Robot Operating System) model repositories
          Download speech recognition models from Mozilla Common Voice project
          Use Intel Neural Compute stick model zoo for edge-optimized models
          Access region-specific models from national AI research institutes
          Download specialized NLP models from NLTK and SpaCy repositories
          Use embedded vision models from ARM's AI model repository
          Access time-series models from specialized forecasting repositories
          Download recommendation system models from RecSys communities
          Use specialized audio processing models from music technology repositories
          Access federated learning models from OpenFL model collections
      Manipulate AI Model: Adversaries may directly manipulate an AI model to change its behavior or introduce malicious code. Manipulating a model gives the adversary a persistent change in the system. This can include poisoning the model by changing its weights, modifying the model architecture to change its behavior, and embedding malware which may be executed when the model is loaded.
        Poison AI Model: Adversaries may manipulate an AI model's weights to change it's behavior or performance, resulting in a poisoned model. Adversaries may poison a model by by directly manipulating its weights, training the model on poisoned data, further fine-tuning the model, or otherwise interfering with its training process.   The change in behavior of poisoned models may be limited to targeted categories in predictive AI models, or targeted topics, concepts, or facts in generative AI models, or aim for a general performance degradation.
          Modify model weight files directly using binary editing tools to introduce targeted biases
          Insert poisoned training examples that cause the model to learn incorrect associations for specific inputs
          Fine-tune the model on adversarially crafted data to introduce backdoors for specific triggers
          Manipulate learning rate parameters during training to cause selective degradation
          Inject noise into gradient calculations during training to affect model convergence
          Replace model checkpoint files with poisoned versions during training
          Modify loss function calculations to bias the model toward adversarial objectives
          Tamper with batch normalization statistics to affect model behavior on specific inputs
          Introduce poisoned samples during continuous learning/online training phases
          Manipulate model architecture configuration files to affect weight initialization and updates
          Compromise federated learning by injecting poisoned updates from malicious clients
          Manipulate pre-trained model weights in popular model repositories/hubs
          Poison knowledge distillation process by manipulating teacher model outputs
          Exploit automatic model optimization/pruning tools to introduce targeted vulnerabilities
          Use memory manipulation techniques to modify model weights during inference
          Tamper with model quantization process to introduce selective degradation
          Modify attention mechanisms in transformer models to bias specific associations
          Inject poisoned samples into data caching/preprocessing pipeline
          Manipulate model merging/averaging processes in ensemble systems
          Exploit auto-ML frameworks to generate poisoned model architectures
          Manipulate hardware random number generators to affect weight initialization
          Exploit GPU memory vulnerabilities to modify weights during training
          Poison model weights through fault injection in hardware accelerators
          Manipulate adaptive learning rate algorithms (like Adam) parameters selectively
          Insert poisoned weights during model pruning/sparsification process
          Exploit transfer learning dependencies to propagate poisoning across model chains
          Manipulate embedding layers in language models to bias word associations
          Poison model weights through compromised hardware optimization libraries
          Exploit multi-task learning to introduce cross-task poisoning effects
          Manipulate checkpoint merging in distributed training systems
          Exploit auto-scaling features in cloud training to inject poisoned replicas
          Manipulate model weights through compromised model serving endpoints
          Poison model through compromised container images in training pipelines
          Exploit hyperparameter tuning services to introduce poisoned configurations
          Manipulate model weights through compromised version control systems
          Inject poisoned weights during model hot-reloading/updates
          Exploit virtualization layer to modify model weights in memory
          Poison through compromised model optimization services
          Manipulate weights during model export/import operations
          Exploit model rollback mechanisms to restore poisoned checkpoints
        Modify AI Model Architecture: Adversaries may directly modify an AI model's architecture to re-define it's behavior. This can include adding or removing layers as well as adding pre or post-processing operations.  The effects could include removing the ability to predict certain classes, adding erroneous operations to increase computation costs, or degrading performance. Additionally, a separate adversary-defined network could be injected into the computation graph, which can change the behavior based on the inputs, effectively creating a backdoor.
